window.SEARCH_INDEX = [
{"title":"CNC router: Category 2 in the Woods / Construction lab","page":"Axiom CNC Router","summary":"Match the VCarve job to the router setup, secure every cut piece, and check the first movement against the preview.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/axiom-cnc-router.html#cnc-router-category-2-in-the-woods-construction-lab","text":"CNC router: Category 2 in the Woods / Construction lab The router moves a rotating cutter along programmed paths to make pockets and profiles. See G-code and Toolpaths for programmed motion. Category 2 in the Woods / Construction lab. Use Equipment Categories and Supervision for permission and supervision. This overview follows our lab's Axiom AR4 Basic and VCarve instruction. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure, Personal Protective Equipment and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. In our labs, the router instruction requires hearing protection during operation and prior approval for material, including material brought from outside the lab."},
{"title":"In VCarve: match the material, preview the paths, then post","page":"Axiom CNC Router","summary":"Match the VCarve job to the router setup, secure every cut piece, and check the first movement against the preview.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/axiom-cnc-router.html#in-vcarve-match-the-material-preview-the-paths-then-post","text":"In VCarve: match the material, preview the paths, then post Create or open the.crv job. Measure the material and match its width, height, thickness and units. In the lab's demonstrated setup, XY zero is the bottom-left corner and Z zero is the material surface. Import the DXF vectors, remove unwanted geometry and join the outlines needed for the paths. Leave space for clamps and check the actual cutter path against their positions. Create the pocket and profile toolpaths. Match the selected tool to the installed cutter; check cutting depths, feeds and speeds. Plan tabs or other workholding so the cut pieces remain secured. Preview all toolpaths in their cutting order: pockets first, outside profiles last in this workflow. Check the first cutting location, remaining tabs, cut depth and clearance above clamps. Save the intended toolpaths with the demonstrated Axiom HHC CNC (mm) (*.mmg) post processor and transfer the.MMG file to the supplied USB drive. Confirm the file selected at the machine is the one you previewed. The preview checks the programmed cut; it does not prove that the real clamps are clear. The material position and zero settings at the router must match the job."},
{"title":"Before running: secure the cutter, material and every cut piece","page":"Axiom CNC Router","summary":"Match the VCarve job to the router setup, secure every cut piece, and check the first movement against the preview.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/axiom-cnc-router.html#before-running-secure-the-cutter-material-and-every-cut-piece","text":"Before running: secure the cutter, material and every cut piece With router power off and the machine made safe for tool setup, remove the magnetic dust boot. Check cutter insertion and tighten the collet with the appropriate wrenches, as demonstrated. Use the installed power-up procedure, insert the USB drive, and select All axis home on the controller with the travel area clear. Homing finds the machine's references; it does not locate the workpiece. Clamp the material on the spoilboard, the sacrificial surface above the machine bed. Keep clamps out of the entire tool path, leave the zero corner accessible, and tighten clamps progressively in the demonstrated zig-zag order. Check that the material and the pieces cut from it will remain held throughout cutting. A loose piece can be struck and thrown by the cutter. Set XY zero at the job's datum and Z zero at the material surface, using the demonstrated touch-off or tool-setting puck method. Clear the puck and its cord from the tool's path after setting. Raise the router as demonstrated, refit the dust boot, turn on router power, open the dust-collection gate and start dust collection. Keep clear of the cutter and the moving carriage throughout operation. The dust boot helps collect debris; it does not make the cutting area safe to reach into."},
{"title":"At Run, check the spindle and first movement against the preview","page":"Axiom CNC Router","summary":"Match the VCarve job to the router setup, secure every cut piece, and check the first movement against the preview.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/axiom-cnc-router.html#at-run-check-the-spindle-and-first-movement-against-the-preview","text":"At Run, check the spindle and first movement against the preview In our lab's demonstrated controller workflow, press RUN/PAUSE, choose UDisk File, select the intended.mmg file and review SetWorkParam. Confirm the parameters with the checkout instruction before accepting them. Accepting starts a countdown, followed by spindle startup and travel to the first toolpath location. If the spindle does not start or the first location is wrong, press STOP/CANCEL. Resolve the cause with the instructor before restarting. Do not reach in to change the router switch while the program continues moving. In our labs, watch the process and listen for unusual noises. Keep the controller accessible. Use STOP/CANCEL for a problem with the run or the emergency stop for an emergency, as demonstrated during checkout. Notify the instructor and use Lab Conduct and Emergencies when needed."},
{"title":"After cutting, switch off the router before removing the work","page":"Axiom CNC Router","summary":"Match the VCarve job to the router setup, secure every cut piece, and check the first movement against the preview.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/axiom-cnc-router.html#after-cutting-switch-off-the-router-before-removing-the-work","text":"After cutting, switch off the router before removing the work In our lab's sequence, turn router power off, wait for the cutter to stop, raise it to the demonstrated Z clearance, and remove the dust boot before unclamping and removing the workpiece. Refit the dust boot afterward. Cut the retaining tabs using an approved tool and its own operating procedure; the lab instruction advises against popping the parts out. Stop dust collection when it is no longer needed, return tools and clamps, and complete shutdown and cleanup."},
{"title":"Category 3 in both labs; vertical and horizontal setups differ","page":"Band Saw","summary":"Support the work, keep the exposed blade short and feed without twisting or pinching the band.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/band-saw.html#category-3-in-both-labs-vertical-and-horizontal-setups-differ","text":"Category 3 in both labs; vertical and horizontal setups differ A band saw cuts with a continuous toothed band. A vertical saw can follow a wood outline; a horizontal saw lowers its blade through clamped material. See How Saws Cut Wood and Choosing a Woodworking Machine. Category 3 in the Woods / Construction lab. Both vertical and horizontal band saws are Category 3 in the Metals lab. Use the category requirements for permission and supervision. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment. See Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for guards, entanglement and isolation, and Metal Chips and Swarf when cutting metal."},
{"title":"Before starting: match the blade and stabilize the work","page":"Band Saw","summary":"Support the work, keep the exposed blade short and feed without twisting or pinching the band.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/band-saw.html#before-starting-match-the-blade-and-stabilize-the-work","text":"Before starting: match the blade and stabilize the work Confirm that the installed blade suits the material, thickness and intended curve. Teeth per inch (TPI) alone do not establish suitability. Check that wheel covers are closed. On the vertical saw, in our labs, set the upper blade guard approximately 1/4 inch above the work, with the machine off. In our labs, use stock with a flat supporting surface. On the vertical saw it rests flat on the table; on the horizontal saw it seats against the vise's fixed surface and is firmly clamped. Plan forward exits from the cut and hand positions that stay out of the blade path. Avoid a layout that requires twisting the band or repeatedly backing out."},
{"title":"Feed steadily; do not force a curve or withdraw against a moving band","page":"Band Saw","summary":"Support the work, keep the exposed blade short and feed without twisting or pinching the band.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/band-saw.html#feed-steadily-do-not-force-a-curve-or-withdraw-against-a-moving-band","text":"Feed steadily; do not force a curve or withdraw against a moving band Vertical saw: hold the work flat against the table and feed at a moderate, steady rate the blade can cut. Keep hands away from the line of cut. Do not twist the blade to force a tighter turn. In our labs, stop the machine before backing out of a long cut; wait until the band has stopped. Horizontal saw: keep the work clamped. Where the saw has a hydraulic feed control, use the trained adjustment procedure to set a controlled descent for the material and section. Do not apply the vertical saw's hand-feeding method to this setup. Why thickness matters as well as material A thin section needs enough teeth engaged to avoid snagging; a thick section also needs room between teeth to carry chips away. Select the blade for both conditions rather than assuming all metal needs the same high TPI."},
{"title":"If the blade breaks or pinches: stand clear and stop","page":"Band Saw","summary":"Support the work, keep the exposed blade short and feed without twisting or pinching the band.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/band-saw.html#if-the-blade-breaks-or-pinches-stand-clear-and-stop","text":"If the blade breaks or pinches: stand clear and stop If the band breaks, step clear and use the emergency stop if safely reachable. Do not open wheel covers or try to catch the band. If the blade pinches, stop and get the instructor before freeing the work under the isolation procedure in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. Report a failure or injury through Lab Conduct and Emergencies. Wait for a complete stop before removing offcuts; follow Equipment Operating Procedure for shutdown and cleanup."},
{"title":"What is a Safety Data Sheet (SDS)?","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#what-is-a-safety-data-sheet-sds","text":"What is a Safety Data Sheet (SDS)? A Safety Data Sheet is the manufacturer's written answer to what their product can do to you, what to wear, and what to do when it goes wrong. There are sixteen sections, always in the same order. You do not need to read a SDS front to back but you should read the following three sections before you use a product. Section 2 Hazard(s) identification. What the chemical can do to you, in the manufacturer's own words - a signal word, the hazard statements, the red-bordered pictograms. Section 4 First-aid measures. On skin, in eyes, breathed in, swallowed. Section 8 Exposure controls / personal protection. Gloves, eye protection, ventilation - the PPE answer for that specific chemical, rather than the general one. None of this is a local rule. OSHA requires a sheet for every hazardous chemical, and requires the sixteen sections and the order they come in - 29 CFR 1910.1200(g). All sixteen sections, and the four OSHA does not enforce This is OSHA's own list, from Appendix D to 29 CFR 1910.1200. The numbers and headings are fixed; a manufacturer cannot renumber them or reorder them. Section 1 Identification - product identifier, recommended use and restrictions on use, who made it, emergency phone number. Section 2 Hazard(s) identification - classification, signal word, hazard statement(s), symbol(s) and precautionary statement(s). Section 3 Composition / information on ingredients - chemical name, CAS number, and for a mixture, the ingredients classified as health hazards and how much of each. Section 4 First-aid measures - by route of exposure, plus the symptoms that matter and when to get medical attention. Section 5 Fire-fighting measures - suitable and unsuitable extinguishing media, and what the chemical gives off when it burns. Section 6 Accidental release measures - the spill response. Section 7 Handling and storage - \"conditions for safe storage, including any incompatibilities\". Section 8 Exposure controls / personal protection - the OSHA permissible exposure limit, the ACGIH threshold limit value, engineering controls, PPE. Section 9 Physical and chemical properties - physical state, odor, flash point, auto-ignition temperature, density, pH and the rest. Section 10 Stability and reactivity - reactivity, conditions to avoid, \"incompatible materials\", hazardous decomposition products. Section 11 Toxicological information - the health effects and the data behind them, including any carcinogen listings. Section 12 Ecological information Section 13 Disposal considerations Section 14 Transport information Section 15 Regulatory information Section 16 Other information - \"the date of preparation of the SDS or the last change to it\". Twelve through fifteen are the odd ones out. The headings have to be there: a note to the standard says an SDS \"must also include the headings\" for those four, \"in order\", to stay consistent with the international system the format comes from. What goes under them is a different question. Appendix D says \"Sections 12-15 may be included in the SDS, but are not mandatory\", and a second note to the same paragraph says \"OSHA will not be enforcing information requirements in sections 12 through 15, as these areas are not under its jurisdiction.\" Those four cover the environment, waste, shipping and other agencies' regulations - EPA and DOT ground, not OSHA's. So Section 13 tells you what the manufacturer suggests doing with the leftovers. It does not tell you what happens to them here. Ask. Two more things about the shape of the document. The section order is fixed but the order within a section is not - preparers \"are not required to present the information in any particular order within each section\", so two sheets can look different inside the same numbered box. And a heading with nothing under it is not an oversight: where there is no relevant information, \"the SDS shall clearly indicate that no applicable information is available.\" Read that as nobody has the data - not as nothing to worry about. Signal words, pictograms and H-numbers - how Section 2 is written There are exactly two signal words, and OSHA defines them in 1910.1200(c): a signal word indicates \"the relative level of severity of hazard\", and \"'Danger' is used for the more severe hazards, while 'warning' is used for the less severe.\" Severity is relative to the same kind of hazard, and that is the part that catches people. Flammable liquids in OSHA's Appendix C are the clean example - all three categories carry the same Flame pictogram, and the signal word is the only thing separating them: Category 1 Danger - \"Extremely flammable liquid and vapor\" Category 2 Danger - \"Highly flammable liquid and vapor\" Category 3 Warning - \"Flammable liquid and vapor\" So \"warning\" does not mean mild. A Category 3 flammable liquid still catches fire. The signal word ranks a hazard against others of its own kind; it is not a statement that the chemical is safe. The pictograms are a fixed set of eight - a black symbol in a red-bordered diamond standing on a point. OSHA names them: Flame, Flame Over Circle, Exclamation Mark, Exploding Bomb, Corrosion, Gas Cylinder, Health Hazard, and Skull and Crossbones. The eight OSHA Hazard Communication pictograms GHS Flame pictogram. Flame GHS Flame Over Circle pictogram. Flame Over Circle GHS Exclamation Mark pictogram. Exclamation Mark GHS Exploding Bomb pictogram. Exploding Bomb GHS Corrosion pictogram. Corrosion GHS Gas Cylinder pictogram. Gas Cylinder GHS Health Hazard pictogram. Health Hazard GHS Skull and Crossbones pictogram. Skull and Crossbones The H-numbers are an index, not the warning. Real sheets often print a code such as H225 beside a hazard statement. Those codes come from the international GHS scheme this format is built on, which treats them as reference labels rather than as a substitute for the statement itself. OSHA's Appendix C does not use them at all - it specifies the sentence. Read the sentence."},
{"title":"Where is SDS binder located?","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#where-is-sds-binder-located","text":"Where is SDS binder located? All Safety Data Sheets are organized in a designated binder for easy reference. Review before use - check the sheet for any unfamiliar chemical or liquid. Ask the instructor if you cannot locate the sheet for a material. The binder is one way of satisfying the rule underneath it. 1910.1200(g)(8) requires an employer to keep the sheets \"readily accessible during each work shift to employees when they are in their work area(s)\", and allows electronic copies in place of paper \"as long as no barriers to immediate employee access in each workplace are created by such options.\" Binder or screen, the test is the same: the sheet is something you can reach now, not something you have to go and ask for. Your responsibility: know the hazards of any chemical you are using, and follow the proper handling procedures. Telling a current sheet from an old one Section 16 carries \"the date of preparation of the SDS or the last change to it.\" That is the only date on the document, and it is how you tell a live sheet from one that has sat in a binder for a decade. Keeping it current is the manufacturer's job, not yours. Under 1910.1200(g)(5), when a manufacturer or importer \"becomes newly aware of any significant information regarding the hazards of a chemical, or ways to protect against the hazards, this new information shall be added to the safety data sheet within three months.\""},
{"title":"Material Storage and incompatible materials","page":"Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets","summary":"Every chemical and liquid in the shop has a Safety Data Sheet. Read it before you use the material, not after.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/chemicals-and-sds.html#material-storage-and-incompatible-materials","text":"Material Storage and incompatible materials Flammable liquids must be stored in an approved flammables cabinet when not in use. NEVER store incompatible chemicals together. All containers must be properly labeled. \"Incompatible\" is not a judgement call, and answering it needs no chemistry. It is written on the sheet, by name, in two places: Section 10 Stability and reactivity. One of its required entries is \"incompatible materials\" - what this chemical must not contact. Beside it sit \"conditions to avoid\" and \"hazardous decomposition products\", which is what the two of them would make. Section 7 Handling and storage. Required to give \"conditions for safe storage, including any incompatibilities\" - the same question answered for the shelf rather than for the reaction. So the test before two things share a cabinet: open both sheets and read Section 10 on each. The entry may name a specific substance or a whole class of them - oxidizers, strong acids. If either sheet names the other material, they do not go together. Read both, because the pair may only be called out on one of them. Fumes and dust have a PPE requirement of their own - see respiratory protection. What makes a flammables cabinet \"approved\", and how to spot one OSHA calls it a storage cabinet and defines it by a fire test rather than by how solid it looks. 29 CFR 1910.106(d)(3) requires cabinets to \"limit the internal temperature to not more than 325 &deg;F. when subjected to a 10-minute fire test\", with all joints and seams staying tight and the door staying closed throughout. The same paragraph requires the marking, which is the fastest way to recognise one: \"Cabinets shall be labeled in conspicuous lettering, 'Flammable - Keep Fire Away.'\""},
{"title":"Prohibited behavior","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#prohibited-behavior","text":"Prohibited behavior NEVER operate equipment under the influence of drugs, alcohol, or medication that causes drowsiness. NO horseplay, pranks or roughhousing in any lab area. Do not distract others who are operating equipment. No unauthorized experiments or modifications to equipment."},
{"title":"Staying alert","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#staying-alert","text":"Staying alert Stay focused - maintain concentration while operating equipment. Take breaks when tired or distracted. Ask questions if you are unsure about any procedure. Report unsafe conditions immediately to the instructor."},
{"title":"Emergency procedures","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#emergency-procedures","text":"Emergency procedures Know the location of emergency stops, fire extinguishers, first aid kits and exits. Call 911 for serious injuries or fires. Notify the instructor immediately of any incident, injury, or near-miss. Do not move a seriously injured person unless they are in immediate danger. For electrical shock specifically, the first action is different - see If someone is shocked."},
{"title":"Workspace responsibility","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#workspace-responsibility","text":"Workspace responsibility Clean as you go - do not wait until the end. Report equipment problems before they become safety hazards."},
{"title":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","page":"Lab Conduct and Emergencies","summary":"What is never acceptable in a lab, what alertness actually requires, and what to do when something goes wrong.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/conduct-and-emergency.html#section-5","text":"Injury or emergency - Call 911 - or Murray State University Police at 270-809-2222"},
{"title":"Guided holes: Category 2 in both labs","page":"Drill Press","summary":"Clamp wood or metal securely, remove the chuck key and keep clear of the rotating bit, work and chips.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/drill-press.html#guided-holes-category-2-in-both-labs","text":"Guided holes: Category 2 in both labs A drill press guides a rotating bit into work held on a table. Bit selection determines the hole it can make; see drill-bit choices for wood. Category 2 in the Woods / Construction lab and Category 2 in the Metals lab. Follow category permission and supervision requirements. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment. The exposed chuck and bit are an entanglement hazard (see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping ). Before you start, check the clothing and hair rules: long hair secured back, no long sleeves, hoodies or jackets near the spindle, no jewelry except a silicone wedding ring, and nothing dangling."},
{"title":"Before starting: secure the bit, work and vise","page":"Drill Press","summary":"Clamp wood or metal securely, remove the chuck key and keep clear of the rotating bit, work and chips.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/drill-press.html#before-starting-secure-the-bit-work-and-vise","text":"Before starting: secure the bit, work and vise Select a bit and spindle speed suitable for the material and hole diameter. Have the instructor confirm an unfamiliar setup; a list of bit types is not approval to use every type in this press. With the machine isolated for setup, seat and tighten the bit using the installed chuck's procedure. The chuck key never leaves your hand. It is not released until it is out of the chuck. Do not count on a key ejecting itself (see the chuck-key rule ). Clamp wood and metal to the table, or grip the work in a vise secured to the table. Secure small parts in a suitable vise or fixture; clamp larger parts with adequate support. Handholding is not workholding. Check that the bit's full travel clears the table, vise and clamps. For a through-hole, provide clearance beneath the work or suitable sacrificial backing. Secure the table at the required height and check that the work cannot rotate or lift when the bit catches."},
{"title":"Feed steadily and keep hands away from the rotating work","page":"Drill Press","summary":"Clamp wood or metal securely, remove the chuck key and keep clear of the rotating bit, work and chips.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/drill-press.html#feed-steadily-and-keep-hands-away-from-the-rotating-work","text":"Feed steadily and keep hands away from the rotating work A bit can catch and turn an unsecured part into a spinning lever. That is why a vise must be secured as well as tightened around the work. Start with the bit clear of the work. Feed steadily so it cuts without being forced. Ease the feed as the bit breaks through the underside. Keep hands clear rather than reaching underneath to support the exit. Withdraw the bit under control, then stop the spindle before releasing or moving the work. Stop before clearing chips near the bit. For metal, use the chip-handling and cleanup procedure; do not pull a stringy chip by hand. Why larger drills usually need fewer revolutions per minute At the same spindle speed, a larger bit's outer edge travels faster. Diameter, work material and bit material all affect the speed choice. “Larger or harder means slower” is a starting trend, not a speed setting."},
{"title":"If the bit breaks or the part comes loose: stand clear","page":"Drill Press","summary":"Clamp wood or metal securely, remove the chuck key and keep clear of the rotating bit, work and chips.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/drill-press.html#if-the-bit-breaks-or-the-part-comes-loose-stand-clear","text":"If the bit breaks or the part comes loose: stand clear Do not grab a spinning part. Step clear and stop the drill press using a safely reachable stop control. For a broken bit, use the emergency stop if safely reachable. Wait for motion to cease and notify the instructor; do not restart until the fault and workholding have been checked. For injury, follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies. Finish normal work with shutdown and cleanup."},
{"title":"Electrical Sources","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#electrical-sources","text":"Electrical Sources What hurts you at 5 V is not what hurts you at 240 V; one is a burn hazard while the other is a shock hazard. 5 V / 12 V The power supply on the bench, batteries, and the battery pack out of a cordless drill. Lower shock risk, not a magic cutoff. Not below the burn threshold. 120 V Wall outlets. Dangerous - can cause serious injury or death. 240 V / 480 V Industrial equipment. Extremely dangerous - often fatal. Voltage tells you whether a circuit can push current through you. It does not tell you what that circuit can do to a ring, a dropped tool or a shorted cell. Those are two different hazards with two different thresholds. The next two sections are about how each hazard changes."},
{"title":"Electrical - Shock Hazard","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#electrical-shock-hazard","text":"Electrical - Shock Hazard There is no magic voltage at which shock suddenly becomes possible. A shock can be painful without being life-threatening, and its severity depends on current, path, duration, and your body's resistance. Higher voltage can drive more current through the body, so the risk of serious injury rises with it. In U.S. workplace rules, 50 volts is a common control threshold: at or above it, live parts must be guarded and work near exposed live parts requires specific controls. OSHA requires live parts to be de-energized before you work on or near them - and then says that \"live parts that operate at less than 50 volts to ground need not be deenergized if there will be no increased exposure to electrical burns or to explosion due to electric arcs\" ( 29 CFR 1910.333(a)(1) ). Read that second half again; the rest of this page hangs on it. At or above the line, live parts must be guarded against accidental contact. The rule is written for \"electric equipment operating at 50 volts or more\" ( 29 CFR 1910.303(g)(2)(i) ). That number applies to DC as well as AC. The standard does not distinguish between them, and OSHA has stated it \"considers all voltages of 50 volts or above to be hazardous\" (interpretation letter, 4 September 2015). NFPA 70E draws the same line. Below 50 V you may work live only where the capacity of the source and any overcurrent protection have been considered and found not to add burn or arc exposure (2024 edition, 110.2(B) Exception No. 5). \"It's not the voltage that gets you, it's the current\" The saying is true. What usually gets taken from it - that the voltage does not matter - is not. Current is electricity actually moving through you, and it takes very little of it to do real harm. Volts sitting on a wire nobody is touching do nothing at all. Two things together decide how much current you get: how hard the electricity is being pushed, which is what voltage measures, and how hard your body holds it back, which is called resistance. Harder push, more current. More resistance, less. Your resistance is not a fixed number - wet skin holds current back far less than dry skin does, so the same push moves much more current through you. That half of it cannot be relied on, which is why the line is drawn on the half that can be: the push. The international standards are not one number On datasheets you will meet a different set of figures, and there the AC and DC limits are not the same: 50 V AC / 120 V DC Extra-Low Voltage under IEC 61140 and IEC 60364-4-41 - 50 V AC rms, or 120 V ripple-free DC. \"Ripple-free\" is a real condition, not a figure of speech: past roughly 10 % rms ripple the supply is treated as AC and the lower number applies. 30 V AC / 60 V DC Class ES1 in IEC 62368-1, the standard behind most benchtop and consumer equipment. ES1 is the class that needs no safeguard at all; ES2 runs up to 50 V AC / 120 V DC and requires one. In this lab, use 50 V - AC or DC. It is the number OSHA enforces, it is the most conservative of the figures above, and it assumes nothing about your skin being dry or your hands being clean. If a circuit is at or above 50 V, it is not something you work on live."},
{"title":"Electrical - Burn Hazard","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#electrical-burn-hazard","text":"Electrical - Burn Hazard Below 50 V, the primary concern often shifts from shock to short-circuit current. This is the half of the page people get wrong, because the voltage people assume is harmless is the one nobody is careful with. Look at what the exemption above is actually conditioned on. Not shock - burns and arcs. NFPA 70E names the same condition as the capacity of the source. Both standards are telling you that once you are under that workplace control threshold, the question changes from how many volts to how many amps can this thing deliver into a dead short. Where the current comes from A bench supply set to 5 V will deliver its full rated current into a short without complaint. That is what the current limit is for - and if you have wound the limit up to get a motor turning, you have wound the protection off. A lithium cell or pack has almost no internal resistance. Shorted, a single cell can dump tens of amps within milliseconds. A pack does considerably worse. A lead-acid battery is rated in hundreds of cold-cranking amps - and that is a 30-second load test, not a ceiling. A dead short pulls far more. None of these can push meaningful current through your body at 12 V. All of them can push hundreds of amps through a piece of metal - and metal carrying current it was never sized for gets hot, fast. Metal jewelry is the classic way this happens ALL jewelry is already PROHIBITED here - see clothing and jewelry. That rule was written for rotating machinery. It earns its keep a second time on the bench, and OSHA writes it a second time for exactly this reason: conductive jewelry \"may not be worn if they might contact exposed energized parts\" ( 29 CFR 1910.333(c)(8) ) - a rule that states no voltage floor. The same sentence gives the way out: such articles may be worn if they are \"rendered nonconductive by covering, wrapping, or other insulating means.\" A ring or a metal watch band bridging a low-voltage source is a near-zero-resistance path across something that can supply hundreds of amps. The ring becomes the heating element. This is documented injury, not a story: a mechanic's gold ring touched the positive terminal of a 12 V car battery while the wrench in his hand bridged the ring and the negative terminal, and the result was a deep circumferential burn around the base of the finger that needed a skin graft. A review of nine ring burns at one burn center found the same pattern: the small size of the burn hides how bad it is, because a burn that wraps a finger is far more likely to need grafting and to leave lasting loss of function than a burn of the same area anywhere else. You can drop a wrench. You cannot drop a ring. The silicone wedding ring that the PPE rule allows is also the right answer electrically: silicone does not conduct, so it cannot become the short. A shorted lithium cell does not just get warm An external short is one of the recognized triggers for thermal runaway - the cell generates heat faster than it can shed it, and the outcome is \"extremely high temperatures, violent cell venting, smoke and fire\" (UL Research Institutes). Once it has started, disconnecting it does not stop it. The cell is now its own energy source. NEVER leave loose cells or packs where metal can bridge the terminals - a drawer with tools in it, a pocket with keys in it, a bin with offcuts in it. Federal transport rules require lithium cells and batteries to be packed so their terminals cannot short ( 49 CFR 173.185 ). The reason that rule exists is the reason to do the same thing on your own bench. A cell that has been shorted, dropped, crushed, punctured or has swollen is out of service. Tell the instructor. Do not put it back on charge to find out. See Fire Safety. The sentence to take away: low voltage does not mean low energy. Voltage helps determine whether a circuit can shock you. Current decides whether it can burn you - and current is set by what the source can deliver and what is in the path, not by the number printed on the front of the supply."},
{"title":"Electrical Conductors","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#electrical-conductors","text":"Electrical Conductors Inspect every cord, every time, before it goes into the receptacle. Never run your hand along a cord while it is plugged in; that is a dangerous way to find fraying. It takes about five seconds, and it is the one check that catches most of this page before it happens rather than after. With the cord unplugged, run it through your hand end to end. Look for frayed, cracked, cut or exposed insulation, and for flat spots where something has been driven over it. Check the plug: blades all present, straight, tight in the body, not discolored or pitted. Check the ground pin is still there - a missing ground pin means the cord is out of service, not that you have found a workaround. Check where the cord enters the plug and enters the tool. That is where flexing breaks conductors, and it is where the damage hides. Inspect extension cords for cuts, crushing and wear, and check the receptacle end for scorching or melted plastic. A power cord with its outer jacket split open across the middle, exposing the colored insulation of the conductors inside and the frayed shielding around them. Damage like this means the cord is finished, not that it needs watching. If you find damage, stop, unplug it, and notify the instructor. Not \"just for this one cut\". Tag it or hand it over, so the next person does not find it the hard way. Tape is not a repair. OSHA allows electrical tape over superficial scuffs in the outer jacket, and has stated just as plainly that \"tape repairs of the jacket may not be used to bring a worn or frayed flexible cord into compliance\" (interpretation letter, 16 December 1998). If conductors or their insulation are showing, the cord is done. Connections and plugs Always disconnect power before performing any maintenance or adjustment. NEVER yank or pull a cord - grasp the plug, not the cord. Pulling by the cord wrecks the strain relief where you cannot see it, and it is how a plug ends up half out of the receptacle with the blades exposed. Push a plug fully home, and look at it. A plug that will not seat is a fault to report, not a fit problem to force. Keep electrical connections away from water and wet surfaces. Wet skin has on the order of 1,000 ohms of resistance where dry skin has 100,000 ohms or more, so water moves you roughly a hundredfold closer to the current every rule on this page is trying to keep out of you. Be aware of overhead wiring when you are handling long stock, moving a ladder or lifting. A partly unplugged appliance is a short waiting for something to land on it Plug fully seated A plug is fully seated in a wall receptacle. No metal blades are exposed. 1 FULLY IN NO EXPOSED BLADES Plug pulled partly out The same plug is pulled halfway out of the wall receptacle, leaving both metal blades exposed in the gap. 2 EXPOSED GAP BLADES ARE LIVE Metal chip bridges exposed blades A metal chip falls across the two exposed blades and short-circuits the supply. A flash marks the contact point. 3 CHIP BRIDGE DEAD SHORT The hazard is the gap, and it is there before anything lands in it. Exposed blades sit at full line voltage with nothing between them but air. Anything conductive that falls across them completes the circuit straight across the supply - and in a shop, the thing that falls is a metal chip. What follows is not a shock. It is a dead short: a bang, a hard flash, and enough heat at the contact point to weld and pit the blades. It will very likely trip the breaker and it can wreck the outlet or whatever is plugged into it. The breaker protects the wiring, not your face, and it does not act until after the flash. Plugs are all the way in or all the way out. Nothing in between, and nothing left in between. Chips travel further than you expect and they conduct - see Metal Chips and Swarf. That is the direct line between the housekeeping rules and this one. Lockout/Tagout for machinery with moving or rotating parts is covered in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Cords on the floor - the one most likely to get you","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#cords-on-the-floor-the-one-most-likely-to-get-you","text":"Cords on the floor - the one most likely to get you Nothing else on this page is as likely to actually injure someone as a cord lying across a walkway. It is not a shock hazard at all. It is a trip hazard - and a trip in a shop is not a trip onto carpet. You land on concrete, or into a machine, or on something sharp. The regulation treats this as a floor problem rather than an electrical one: \"walking-working surfaces are maintained free of hazards such as sharp or protruding objects, loose boards, corrosion, leaks, spills, snow, and ice\" ( 29 CFR 1910.22(a)(3) ), and passageways and work areas are kept \"in a clean, orderly, and sanitary condition\" ( 29 CFR 1910.22(a)(1) ). A cord across an aisle is an obstruction in an aisle. A person mid-stumble in a shop, one foot caught in a coiled yellow extension cord run across an open concrete walkway, both hands thrown out to break the fall. A trip in a shop is not a trip onto carpet. You land where the machines are. The correct route is longer and costs nothing. That is the whole argument. What to do, in order of preference Do not cross the walkway. Move the work to the outlet, or use a different outlet. This is almost always available, and it is almost never the first thing anyone tries. Route along the wall or the bench line rather than across open floor. Take the long way around the edge of the room. Go overhead where a drop or a reel is provided. That is what they are for. If it genuinely must cross, cover or anchor it. OSHA's own guidance is to \"ensure that temporary electrical cords that cross aisles are taped or anchored to the floor\". A cord ramp or cord cover does the same job better and protects the cord as well as the person. Use the shortest cord that reaches. Slack on the floor is the hazard, and a coiled surplus in a walkway is the same hazard in a tighter package. Unplug it and put it away when you are done, in the same movement as shutting the tool down. The cord left out after the job is finished is the one nobody is expecting. PROHIBITED - running a flexible cord through a doorway, a window, or a hole in a wall, ceiling or floor, and attaching it to building surfaces. Those are named prohibitions in the electrical standard ( 29 CFR 1910.305(g)(1)(iv) ), along with using flexible cord as a substitute for the fixed wiring of a building. Flexible cord is temporary by definition. A cord treated as permanent gets pinched, crushed and forgotten. NEVER walk past a cord lying across a walkway and leave it because it is not yours. If you can see it, you can move it or say something. The person who trips over it will not have seen it. Read from the cord's side, the rule is the same: portable equipment must be handled so it is not damaged, and every cord set must be inspected before use on any shift for cuts, crushing and a pinched jacket ( 29 CFR 1910.334(a) ). A cord that gets walked on is a cord that fails the inspection above. This section and that one are the same problem twice. Keeping walkways clear is the Set in Order and Shine part of 5S. The safety case and the lean case are the same case."},
{"title":"If someone is shocked","page":"Electrical Safety","summary":"Why 50 V is a workplace control threshold, why 5 V can still burn you, the inspection required before every use, cords on the floor, and what to do when someone is shocked.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/electrical-safety.html#if-someone-is-shocked","text":"If someone is shocked Do NOT touch them directly. If current is still flowing they are part of the circuit, and grabbing them puts you in it as well. Kill the power first - the machine switch, the disconnect, the breaker, or pull the plug. If you cannot kill it, use a dry, non-conductive object to separate them from the source, and stand on something dry while you do it. Then call for help and start first aid. Call 911 and notify the instructor - follow emergency procedure. A small mark is not evidence of a small injury. Electrical and electrothermal burns run deep with very little to see on the surface, and a burn that wraps a finger can cost the use of it. Any shock, and any electrical burn, gets reported and gets looked at - however fine the person says they are."},
{"title":"Three categories, one question","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#three-categories-one-question","text":"Three categories, one question Every piece of equipment in the lab is Category 1, 2 or 3. The category answers one question: who has to be present before you switch it on? Permission from an instructor is required in every case. What changes between categories is when that permission can be given, and who has to be in the room while the machine is running. Category 1 Permission may be given in advance. Work alone once you have it. Category 2 Permission at the time of use. A peer must be present throughout. Category 3 Permission at the time of use. A peer and an instructor must be present throughout. The category never removes a requirement. General safety procedures and PPE apply to every category, to every person present - see Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment."},
{"title":"Category 1","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-1","text":"Category 1 Can be used with instructor permission - may be given in advance or at the time of use. Can be operated independently, without peer or instructor supervision, once permission is granted. You must still follow all general safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Category 2","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-2","text":"Category 2 Can be used with instructor permission - permission must be given at the time of use. Does not require instructor supervision during operation. Requires peer supervision present at all times during use. Both the operator and the peer supervisor must follow all safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Category 3","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#category-3","text":"Category 3 Can be used with instructor permission - permission must be given at the time of use. Requires both peer AND instructor supervision present during operation. The highest-risk equipment, requiring maximum safety oversight. All participants - operator, peer and instructor - must follow safety procedures and PPE requirements."},
{"title":"Which category is my machine?","page":"Equipment Categories and Supervision","summary":"Every machine in every lab falls into one of three categories. The category tells you who has to be standing there before you switch it on.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-categories.html#which-category-is-my-machine","text":"Which category is my machine? The category assignment is per lab. Each lab's roster lists its machines by category: Woods / Construction lab Rapid Prototyping Center Metals lab If a machine is not on your lab's roster, ask the instructor. A machine you cannot place is a machine you do not start."},
{"title":"Before operating any equipment","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#before-operating-any-equipment","text":"Before operating any equipment Obtain permission to operate the equipment from the instructor. Verify training - ensure ALL operators have received proper equipment training. Check PPE - confirm you are wearing all required personal protective equipment. See Personal Protective Equipment. Perform a pre-use inspection - check for damage or required maintenance. Confirm all guards and safety devices are in place and operational. Secure the workpiece - ensure all material is properly clamped or held. Communicate your intentions - tell others in the area what you are about to do."},
{"title":"During equipment operation","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#during-equipment-operation","text":"During equipment operation Stay alert - maintain focus, and never leave equipment unattended while it is running. Follow proper procedures - use equipment only as trained and as intended. Machine-specific hazards - pinch points, measurement, housekeeping - are covered in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"After operating any equipment","page":"Equipment Operating Procedure","summary":"The procedure that applies to every piece of equipment in every lab, every time you use it.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/equipment-operating-procedure.html#after-operating-any-equipment","text":"After operating any equipment Power down safely - turn off and disconnect the equipment from its power source. Report issues immediately - notify the instructor of any broken or malfunctioning equipment. Return and organize - put all equipment and tools back in their designated locations. Restore to shutdown state - return the equipment to its original, safe configuration. Clean thoroughly - remove all dust, chips, liquids and debris from the equipment and the work area. The habit behind the last two is 5S - the same discipline, named and made routine."},
{"title":"Decide before you reach for anything","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#decide-before-you-reach-for-anything","text":"Decide before you reach for anything An extinguisher is for a small fire you caught early. Fight it only if all four are true The fire is small and contained - a bin, a rag, a puddle. Not spreading. You know what is burning, and you have the right extinguisher for it. You have a clear exit behind you and the fire is not between you and it. Someone else has already raised the alarm. Otherwise: get out. Pull the alarm, leave, call 911 from outside, and tell the instructor and the fire crew what was burning. If you try and it does not go out in the first few seconds, stop and leave. A hand extinguisher lasts roughly 10&ndash;20 seconds. That is your whole attempt."},
{"title":"Using an extinguisher - PASS","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#using-an-extinguisher-pass","text":"Using an extinguisher - PASS Remember the acronym: P&ndash;A&ndash;S&ndash;S. P Pull Pull the pin. It breaks the tamper seal and frees the lever. A Aim Aim low, at the base of the fire - not at the flames. Stand back about 6&ndash;8 ft. S Squeeze Squeeze the lever slowly and evenly. Releasing it stops the discharge. S Sweep Sweep side to side across the base until the fire is out. Watch for it reigniting. When it looks out, back away watching the fire, exit behind you. Do not assume it is over - a knocked-down fire can reignite."},
{"title":"Fire classes, and what puts them out","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#fire-classes-and-what-puts-them-out","text":"Fire classes, and what puts them out Fires are classed by what is burning. An extinguisher is rated for the classes it can handle, and using the wrong one ranges from useless to dangerous. Class A Class B Class C Class D Class K Most extinguishers on a wall are ABC dry chemical - one unit covering the first three classes. Check the label; the classes it is rated for are printed on it."},
{"title":"Metal fires, and what it takes to start one","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#metal-fires-and-what-it-takes-to-start-one","text":"Metal fires, and what it takes to start one Metal burns only when it is divided finely enough, and then only when several conditions line up at once. Ordinary aluminum chips and swarf will not do this, and neither will the quantities of fine material a lab space like this one generates. Solid stock does not burn at all. The cases below are the ones that are genuinely different. The form matters more than the metal Magnesium is the real Class D case. Thin sections, chips and turnings ignite readily and burn with intense heat and a brilliant white light, and dull tools alone can produce enough heat during machining to start it. Titanium turnings can ignite, and titanium fines and grinding dust are worse - fine enough that a static discharge is a credible ignition source. Aluminum fines - the dust off sanding, grinding, buffing and polishing - are a genuine hazard, but only through the specific mechanisms below. Aluminum chips and swarf are hard to ignite. Aluminum carries heat away from a hot spot fast, it wears a tough oxide skin that holds together while the metal underneath melts, and a chip has little surface area for its mass. Put a torch to a pile of chips and they melt. Solid stock does not burn. A block of aluminum is not fuel. Magnesium and titanium are not common in student labs. A senior project might bring one in. If you are about to cut, grind or sand either one, ask first - every time. Size is the usual line - shape is the exception The line in the standard is about half a millimeter, measured across a particle's smallest dimension - which different editions write as 500 microns or as 420 microns (fine enough to pass a 40-mesh sieve), not two separate limits. Below it, metal dust can carry an explosion. Above it, generally it cannot - and generally is the word the standard uses, not never. Flakes are the exception, and buffing and polishing make flakes. A flake is wide but thin, so it is too big for the sieve and still burns like dust. Treat anything off a buffing or polishing wheel as fines however big it looks. Machining chips are genuinely coarse - that is the difference between a chip tray and a dust collector. The ways fine metal actually ignites A suspended dust cloud meeting an ignition source. Fines sitting in a tray are not the hazard. Fines thrown into the air, at the right concentration, reaching a flame, a torch, a spark or a static discharge, are - burning travels from one particle to the next with extreme speed. It is why you never blow fine dust around, and why you do not raise a cloud of it cleaning up. Fines heavily mixed with rust or grinding scale. Aluminum plus iron oxide - rust, or the scale off steel grinding - is a thermite reaction: the aluminum strips the oxygen off the iron, so the mixture burns without needing air from outside. That is what makes it a metal fire rather than an ordinary one, and why water and an ABC extinguisher do nothing to it. It takes a real mixture to go - something near three parts iron oxide to one part aluminum by weight, and an ignition source far hotter than a spark. That is a rust-caked pile, not a trace. See Metal Chips and Swarf for how far apart that threshold and the recycling one actually sit. Wet fines. Aluminum fines in contact with water slowly give off hydrogen. That is a reason not to seal damp fines into a closed container, and part of the reason water is the wrong thing to put on a metal fire. If fine metal does ignite Do not fight it. Most general-purpose shops have no Class D extinguisher - assume you do not have one unless somebody has shown you where it is. Pull the alarm, get out, call 911, and tell the fire crew it is a metal fire. That changes how they fight it, and they need to know before they arrive with a hose. PROHIBITED - the ABC extinguisher off the wall. \"Dry powder\" (Class D) and \"dry chemical\" (ABC) are not the same thing. An ABC unit throws an incompatible agent at pressure: it scatters burning fines and spreads the fire. NEVER put water on a metal fire. The stream throws burning material, can raise an explosive dust cloud, and reacts with the metal to release hydrogen. Alarm, evacuate, report. Why dry sand is on the list, and why it is probably not on yours Sand does work. The Aluminum Association's Guidelines for Handling Aluminum Fines (F-1) lists fine dry sand, \"preferably finer than 20 mesh,\" next to approved dry powders as effective for isolating an aluminum fines fire. It also says what has to be true first. Dry. F-1 says dry sand. Damp sand is water, and water \"will react with the aluminum fines to form flammable hydrogen gas.\" Already in the room. \"A plentiful supply should be easily available in the operating area and stored in covered bins or drums.\" Sand fetched after the fire starts is not that. With the right tool waiting beside it. \"Long handled shovels should be available at each bin or drum, and should be constructed of non-sparking metal.\" Laid, not thrown. The shovels \"lay the sand or powder around the edge or perimeter of the fire.\" You are walling it in. Anything thrown at the pile raises the dust cloud that is the actual explosion. Decided before it started, by someone senior. \"Qualified supervisory personnel should decide in advance whether it is better to attempt to fight the fire or to withdraw to a safe location and allow it to burn itself out.\" Those are preconditions, not tips. If you cannot point at the covered sand bin and the non-sparking shovel beside it right now, you do not have this option - and the call to fight it was never yours to make on the spot. Alarm, evacuate, report. What that means day to day Do not let fine dust accumulate. Empty trays, vacuums and collectors rather than letting them fill - see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for housekeeping. Chips and fines are disposed of differently, and the difference matters - see Metal Chips and Swarf. Let hot chips and debris cool before they go anywhere near a bin of paper or rag. Ask before you machine an unfamiliar metal. If you do not know what it is, that is the answer: ask."},
{"title":"Hot work and the things that start fires","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#hot-work-and-the-things-that-start-fires","text":"Hot work and the things that start fires Grinding throws sparks a surprising distance. Clear rag, sawdust, solvent and packaging out of the area before you start, not after it smolders. Oily rags - it depends entirely on which oil. Drying oils - linseed, tung, oil-based stains, varnishes and finishes - cure by reacting with oxygen, and that reaction gives off heat. Bunched in a pile the rags hold the heat in, and the pile can reach ignition on its own with nothing to light it. Those rags NEVER go in a pile: spread them flat to dry, drop them in water, or use the designated container. Petroleum oils do not do that. Way oil, cutting fluid, hydraulic oil and ordinary shop oil do not cure by oxidation, so a rag wiped with them will not self-heat and will not spontaneously combust. It is still combustible - a spark or a hot chip will light it like any oily cloth - so keep it away from hot work and out of a bin of paper or sawdust. If you do not know which oil is on the rag, treat it as a drying oil. Flammable liquids live in a flammables cabinet when they are not in your hand - see Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets. A smoldering fire can restart long after you leave. If something got hot enough to smoke, tell the instructor before you go."},
{"title":"Know these before you need them","page":"Fire Safety","summary":"When to fight a fire and when to leave, and how to use an extinguisher.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/fire-safety.html#know-these-before-you-need-them","text":"Know these before you need them None of the above helps if you are looking for equipment while a fire grows. On your first day in any lab space, locate: The nearest fire extinguisher, and what classes it is rated for. The nearest alarm pull station. Both ways out of the room, and the route that does not pass the machines. Where your class gathers outside, so you can be accounted for. The first aid kit and the emergency stops - see Lab Conduct and Emergencies."},
{"title":"Flat faces and straight edges: Category 3 in the woods lab","page":"Jointer","summary":"Use push blocks, suitable stock and shallow cuts to keep hands clear of the cutterhead while establishing a flat face or straight edge.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/jointer.html#flat-faces-and-straight-edges-category-3-in-the-woods-lab","text":"Flat faces and straight edges: Category 3 in the woods lab A jointer establishes a flat face or a straight edge at a set angle to an adjacent face. It does not by itself make opposite faces parallel. See Milling Rough Lumber Square for the sequence and Choosing a Woodworking Machine for machine selection. Category 3 in the Woods / Construction lab. Follow category permission and supervision requirements. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment; see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for guarding and isolation."},
{"title":"Before starting: check the stock, guard and depth of cut","page":"Jointer","summary":"Use push blocks, suitable stock and shallow cuts to keep hands clear of the cutterhead while establishing a flat face or straight edge.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/jointer.html#before-starting-check-the-stock-guard-and-depth-of-cut","text":"Before starting: check the stock, guard and depth of cut In our labs: Check for nails, screws, loose knots, splits and other defects. Do not feed wood containing metal; ask the instructor about defective or unstable stock. Use stock at least 1/4 inch thick and 10 inches long. These minimums do not make an otherwise unstable setup acceptable. Set the infeed table for no more than 1/16 inch removal per pass, with the machine isolated for setup. Check that the swinging cutterhead guard covers the cutter and returns freely. Do not use a setup that defeats it. Plan the feed with the grain, with a cupped face concave-side down so the board does not roll. Have the instructor check stock that will not sit stably. Have push blocks ready. Turn on dust collection and open the machine's collection gate before cutting."},
{"title":"Keep hands 4 inches away and transfer pressure with push blocks","page":"Jointer","summary":"Use push blocks, suitable stock and shallow cuts to keep hands clear of the cutterhead while establishing a flat face or straight edge.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/jointer.html#keep-hands-4-inches-away-and-transfer-pressure-with-push-blocks","text":"Keep hands 4 inches away and transfer pressure with push blocks In our labs, keep hands at least 4 inches from the cutterhead and never pass hands over it. Do not hook fingers around the end or side of the board. Use push blocks or the trained push-assist device; plan hand transfers before starting. Stand to the side of the board near the cutter, not behind its feed line. Start the jointer and wait for full speed. Feed with slight, even pressure against the table and fence. Keep the board stable rather than forcing it flat. Once the leading portion has passed the cutter by more than the 4-inch margin, use the push block to apply downward pressure on the outfeed side. Transfer hands without passing them over the cutterhead. Complete the pass while maintaining control and clearance. Let the guard return over the cutter before lifting the board away."},
{"title":"If the board kicks back or control is lost: stop the jointer","page":"Jointer","summary":"Use push blocks, suitable stock and shallow cuts to keep hands clear of the cutterhead while establishing a flat face or straight edge.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/jointer.html#if-the-board-kicks-back-or-control-is-lost-stop-the-jointer","text":"If the board kicks back or control is lost: stop the jointer The cutter can throw a board opposite the feed direction. Do not chase it or reach toward the cutter. Stop the jointer from a safe position and notify the instructor before another pass. If injured, move clear and follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies. Wait for the cutterhead to stop before cleanup, then complete Equipment Operating Procedure."},
{"title":"Lifting Rules","page":"Lifting and Carrying","summary":"Team lifting above 40 lbs, and the technique that keeps you in control of the load. What usually defeats a lift is the size of the thing, not its weight.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/lifting-and-carrying.html#lifting-rules","text":"Lifting Rules Get a second person for anything over 40 lbs. A gym weight plate is 45 lbs and nobody lifts one in pairs. Size - not weight - is what makes shop materials hard to carry. Forty pounds of aluminum bar is about four inches square and two feet long: you can hold it against your chest, see over it, and set it down where you meant to. A twenty-foot piece of tube can weigh less than that and still put half its length out in front of you, and a sheet of plywood fills your arms and hides the floor you are walking on. Same weight, different job. OSHA sets no weight limit - no standard says how much a person may lift, only that a workplace be kept free of recognized hazards. The number everyone borrows comes from NIOSH, the federal institute that researches workplace injury: 51 lbs with two hands under ideal conditions, cut for every way a real lift is worse. Hold that same load at arm's length instead of against you and it rates about 20 lbs. Turning a quarter circle as you lift takes off another 30%, and nothing good to grab takes off 10% more. A load much wider than your body is outside what the equation covers at all - used there, it underestimates the hazard. Team lifting is required for Anything over 40 lbs. Items lighter than 40 lbs but awkward in shape, size or balance. Any object that feels unstable or is difficult to grip. The 40 lb line is as much about dropping it as about your back. A load you cannot fully control is a hazard to your feet, the floor, the workpiece and anyone walking past - well before it is a hazard to your spine. If you cannot hold it against your body and keep your back straight, it is too heavy for you to lift alone - whatever the number says. Weight still wins in the end; that point just sits somewhere different for every person and every shape."},
{"title":"Proper lifting technique","page":"Lifting and Carrying","summary":"Team lifting above 40 lbs, and the technique that keeps you in control of the load. What usually defeats a lift is the size of the thing, not its weight.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/lifting-and-carrying.html#proper-lifting-technique","text":"Proper lifting technique Plan the lift - clear your path and know where you are going. Get close to the object before lifting. Squat down using your leg muscles; keep your back straight. Grip firmly with both hands; avoid awkward hand positions. Lift smoothly using your legs, not your back. Keep the load close to your body throughout the lift. Setting it down Use the same technique in reverse - squat down, do not bend over. Place objects gently. Do not drop them."},
{"title":"Rotating machinery","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#rotating-machinery","text":"Rotating machinery Rotating machinery injures you by catching hold of something and pulling it in. It does not have to catch your hand. It catches a sleeve, a glove, a lanyard or a strand of hair, and your hand goes wherever that goes. Gears, pulleys and belts, chains and sprockets are extremely dangerous. Where a rotating part meets another surface there is a wedge that closes to nothing - an in-running nip point, the place the machine feeds things into itself. It is where the belt runs onto the pulley, where the chain runs onto the sprocket, where the gear teeth mesh. The contact and the injury happen in different places. You touch the belt out in the open, where it looks like a moving strap; the belt carries you to the pulley, where there is no gap at all. That distance is why staying clear of the nip point is not enough on its own - anything the machine can grip is a handle it uses to bring you there. What gets caught Loose clothing, jewelry and unsecured hair - see clothing, jewelry and hair. Gloves. Gloves or rags are for handling sharp objects such as cutters, and only once the machine is shut down. NEVER wear gloves at a running machine - a glove catches where bare skin would pull free, and takes your hand with it. See chips and gloves. A long stringy chip still connected to the cut - see Metal Chips and Swarf. It does not take teeth, and it does not take speed. A plain smooth shaft turning slowly will still wind hair or cloth onto itself, and once it has a turn or two it does not let go. Contact happens where the belt is open. The injury happens where it closes. The word for this, and where it comes from \"Nip point\" is not shop slang. It is the term the machine-guarding standard uses. 29 CFR 1910.212(a)(1) requires guarding against \"hazards such as those created by point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, flying chips and sparks.\" OSHA's Machine Guarding eTool puts it plainly: \" In-running nip point hazards are caused by the rotating parts on machinery. \" Its longer treatment, Concepts and Techniques of Machine Safeguarding (OSHA 3067), gives three kinds: \"Parts can rotate in opposite directions while their axes are parallel to each other. These parts may be in contact (producing a nip point) or in close proximity.\" - meshing gears. \"Nip points are also created between rotating and tangentially moving parts. Some examples would be: the point of contact between a power transmission belt and its pulley, a chain and a sprocket, and a rack and pinion.\" \"Nip points can occur between rotating and fixed parts which create a shearing, crushing, or abrading action.\" So belts, chains, sprockets and gears are rotating hazards by name. What makes them dangerous is that they draw material in - not that two parts close on each other. OSHA is just as direct about the plain shaft: \"Rotating motion can be dangerous; even smooth, slowly rotating shafts can grip hair and clothing, and through minor contact force the hand and arm into a dangerous position.\" These parts are meant to be behind covers, and the standard is specific about it - 29 CFR 1910.219(f)(3): \"All sprocket wheels and chains shall be enclosed unless they are more than seven (7) feet above the floor or platform.\" The rotating parts that have no cover Belts, gears and chains belong behind covers. The working end of a machine does not. The spindle - the driven shaft the motor turns - the chuck that grips the tool or the work, and the stock (the raw material) held in it all turn out in the open. Nothing is between you and them, because you have to reach them to do the job. So everything above about what gets caught applies here with nothing in the way - and it applies to every machine that turns, not only the loud ones. A drill press is quiet, it is slow, and it looks like the harmless one. It is still an open chuck spinning, and it will wind hair, a sleeve or a glove onto itself like anything else. Slow is how long it takes, not whether it happens. A cartoon machinist beside a drill press. A torn scrap of his shirt sleeve is knotted around the chuck and spinning with it. The sleeve has been ripped off at his shoulder, leaving the arm bare, with a ragged strand of cloth still trailing from the shoulder toward the chuck. He is pulling away, one hand braced flat on the drill press table. The sleeve tore before the arm did. That is the shirt's doing, not the machine's. The lathe is where this goes furthest, and the reason is what a lathe is. Most machines hold the work still and move a cutter across it; a lathe does the opposite and turns the work. What is spinning in the open is then not a bit the width of a pencil - it is the whole part, as long and as heavy as that part happens to be. That is why the fatality reports for lathes describe something other than a hand injury: a person wound onto their own workpiece. A cartoon machinist at an engine lathe. The front of his shirt has wound several turns onto the workpiece turning in the chuck, and the cloth is stretched taut off his chest. His feet are planted and his free arm is thrown out as the machine hauls him against the bed. Wound on, cloth is a rope. Each revolution takes up another turn, and bracing changes nothing. The question, before anything is switched on: what is turning, and what of mine can reach it? A sleeve, a glove, loose hair and a strip of abrasive cloth held against the work have each pulled somebody into a machine. Every one of them is easier to deal with before the spindle is turning than after. None of this is a ranking. The mechanism is the same on every machine that turns, and the one that can catch you is whichever one you are standing at. What the investigations actually describe Oregon's state fatality-investigation program, summarizing the record for this class of machine: \" The most common causes of death and injury from metal lathes include entanglement of clothing in moving parts, being hit by loose objects on the lathe (e.g., chuck keys, tools, metal shavings) and being struck by a part that has not been adequately secured.\" (OR-FACE 2012-18-1, Experienced Journeyman Machinist Killed While Operating an Engine Lathe, 2014.) Four investigated deaths, all the same event - something attached to a person touched something turning, and the machine did the rest: Smoothing a part while it turned. A 69-year-old machinist with over 30 years' experience \"reached over the work piece to smooth out a radiused transition with emery cloth and a file in hand\" and \"his loose clothing (left arm sleeve) became entangled around the rotating work piece\". A co-worker 30 feet away heard \"a strange &lsquo;winding-up' sound\". Of that same part, the shop foreman had said that \"the rough surface could grab clothing or gloves\". (OR-FACE 2012-18-1.) Stock sticking out the back of the spindle. A 52-year-old machinist was set up on a 103-inch steel bar: \"Due to the length of the material, 24 inches were sticking out the back of the lathe, unguarded, and held in place with a clamping device that rotates as the lathe rotates.\" He reached across the running machine and \"the sleeve of his shirt became entangled\". (Kentucky FACE 21KY002, 2021.) A burr on the end of the bar. A lathe operator in his twenties had a 1 7/8-inch stub of 1-inch bar standing past the tailstock. \"The right pocket of his shirt was caught by the rotating tailstock burr. His shirt wrapped around the stock and tightened at the decedent's neck and chest.\" The listed contributing factor is \"walking to and/or reaching over unguarded rotating bar stock\". OSHA makes the same point in general: the danger \"increases when projections such as set screws, bolts, nicks, abrasions, and projecting keys&hellip; are exposed on rotating parts.\" (Michigan FACE 14MI034, 2014; OSHA Machine Guarding eTool.) A drill press. \"As Employee #1 reached into the unguarded point of operation, his shirt sleeve or gloves became entangled in the drill bit. Employee #1 was pulled toward the machine, entangling Employee #1's long hair. The drill press continued to spin, completely pulling Employee #1 into the operating area.\" She died of her injuries. The same report records that the spindle \"had speeds that ranged from 45 to 1115 rotations per minute\". (OSHA accident 114097.015, 20 February 2019.) Why abrasive cloth keeps appearing. The UK Health and Safety Executive publishes a sheet on this one task: \"Every year there are serious accidents involving the use of emery cloth on metalworking lathes. These accidents result in serious injuries such as broken bones, dislocations, lacerations, amputations and occasionally death.\" The mechanism it names is the one above: \"Accidents occur when hand-held emery cloth is entangled in the rotating parts of the machine or where the emery cloth is snagged on the rotating component dragging the operator into the danger zone.\" Its own rules are that emery cloth \"should NEVER be applied directly by hand on manual metalworking lathes\", and that gloves \"increase the risk of entanglement and\" are \"never acceptable near rotating parts of machinery\". (HSE Engineering Information Sheet No 2, rev 1, 2015 - that is HSE's guidance; ask what the procedure is in the shop you are standing in.) And the drill press is not the exception it looks like. OSHA's own drill-press training script lists, among the ways accidents happen, \"getting dangling jewelry, loose clothing, or hair caught in the drill chuck (rotational hazard)\", and states: \"The primary hazards of drill presses are contact at the point of operation and rotational hazards. An operator can risk serious hand injury when working too close to the drilling area, wearing gloves, loose clothing, loose hair, or jewelry.\""},
{"title":"Moving machinery and pinch points","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#moving-machinery-and-pinch-points","text":"Moving machinery and pinch points Moving machinery injures you without ever catching hold of you. Two surfaces close on each other with you in between. Nothing pulls you anywhere - you run out of room. What makes a pinch point A pinch point is a gap that is closing: two parts, at least one of them moving, with space between them that is on its way to nothing. That is the whole of it, and it is why pinch points are hard to spot. There are no teeth, no edge and often no noise. A wide gap and a narrow gap look the same until the last inch, and the gap does not care what is in it. Two kinds of movement, and both make one: Back and forth, up and down A press ram, a drill head coming down, a saw guide being lowered, a clamp closing. You are caught between the part that moves and the part that does not. Straight along A table, a carriage or a saddle travelling. It closes the gap against whatever waits at the end of its travel - a stop, a column, the machine's own frame, or the hand resting there. Where they hide Between the work and whatever holds it. A vise jaw, a clamp, a fixture: the hand steadying the workpiece is inside the gap that is closing on the workpiece. At the end of a table's travel. Wind it far enough and the space between the table and the frame goes to zero, with whatever was resting there in between. Under anything that comes down. A head, a ram, a guard, a guide. On hinges. Covers, doors and lids close on their own frames - at the free edge, where a hand is holding them, and again right at the hinge, where the gap closes over almost no distance at all. A pinch point needs no power, no speed and no noise. A vise turned by hand multiplies what you put into the handle many times over, and it closes slowly and quietly enough that nothing warns you. Switching a machine off removes the part that would have closed the gap for you. It does not remove the gap. The question to ask Before moving anything - a handwheel, a lever, a clamp, a guard - what closes, and what is in the way? Follow the moving part to the end of its travel and find what it meets there. Then look at where your hands are, and where they will still be once you are watching the cut instead of watching your hands. A pinch point is a gap on its way to zero. It looks safe for most of the trip. Why belts and chains count as rotating, not pinching OSHA sorts machine hazards by the motion that causes them, and \"pinch point\" is the vocabulary it attaches to parts that travel, not to parts that turn. From Concepts and Techniques of Machine Safeguarding (OSHA 3067): Reciprocating motion - \"Reciprocating motions may be hazardous because, during the back-and-forth or up-and-down motion, a worker may be struck by or caught between a moving and a stationary part.\" Transverse motion, which means movement in a straight continuous line - \"Transverse motion (movement in a straight, continuous line) creates a hazard because a worker may be struck or caught in a pinch or shear point by the moving part.\" A belt on a pulley is a rotating hazard that draws material in; a table running up against a column is a moving hazard that closes on it. Both can take a hand, and the vocabulary is worth keeping straight because the two do not have the same tell. You avoid the first by giving it nothing to grip. You avoid the second by knowing where the gap is going."},
{"title":"Guards stay on, and taking one off is not your call","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#guards-stay-on-and-taking-one-off-is-not-your-call","text":"Guards stay on, and taking one off is not your call A guard is the thing standing between you and everything the two sections above describe. It is not an accessory, and it is not in your way - it is doing the one job nothing else on the machine does. No one removes a guard in this lab. A machine that needs a guard off is the instructor's to deal with, out of class. That is the whole rule, and it is deliberately not a judgement call. You do not have to decide whether this particular setup is the exception, whether the cut is short enough, or whether you will be careful. If the guard is in the way, the answer is to stop and say so - not to work out whether you can manage without it. It also means a machine you find with a guard already missing is not ready to use. Say something. You have not done anything wrong by finding it that way, and the person who uses it after you has no way of knowing. Why the rule has no exceptions in it Guarding is not a local preference. 29 CFR 1910.212(a)(1) - quoted already in Rotating machinery above for its definition of a nip point - is a guarding requirement first: one or more methods of machine guarding shall be provided to protect the operator and other employees from hazards including those created by point of operation, ingoing nip points, rotating parts, flying chips and sparks. The reason a rule like this is written without exceptions is that the exceptions are exactly where people get hurt. A guard comes off for a legitimate setup, the setup takes longer than expected, somebody else walks up to a machine that looks normal and is not. The standard's own answer to \"but this task genuinely needs the guard off\" is not a more careful operator - it is Lockout/Tagout, the next card on this page, which is a procedure rather than a permission."},
{"title":"Lockout/Tagout - before anyone works on a machine","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#lockout-tagout-before-anyone-works-on-a-machine","text":"Lockout/Tagout - before anyone works on a machine Everything on a machine that turns or travels does so because something is powering it. Lockout/Tagout is how that power is taken away and kept away while somebody works on the machine. NEVER work on equipment with rotating or moving parts without first disconnecting power. Lockout/Tagout procedures must be followed for maintenance. The two words Lockout A lock on the device that isolates the energy - the disconnect, the breaker, the valve - so it physically cannot be switched back on. Tagout A tag on that same device saying who put it there and why, so nobody removes it wondering. Off is not disconnected, and disconnected is not still Off is not disconnected. A machine's own on/off switch is a control. Someone can press it back on, and either way the machine is still connected to its supply. The disconnect is the thing that gets locked. Disconnected is not still. A spindle coasts. A raised head or ram drops. A spring, a charged capacitor or air still in a line will all move something after the power is gone. Energy already inside the machine is part of what the procedure has to deal with. What the standard actually covers 29 CFR 1910.147(a)(1)(i): \"This standard covers the servicing and maintenance of machines and equipment in which the unexpected energization or start up of the machines or equipment, or release of stored energy could cause injury to employees.\" Two things are worth reading twice. Unexpected is the whole point - the hazard is not that the machine runs, it is that it runs while someone has a hand inside it. And stored energy is named alongside energization, because a machine that has been switched off and unplugged can still contain everything it needs to move once. \"Servicing and maintenance\" is much broader than it sounds. The standard defines it as \" constructing, installing, setting up, adjusting, inspecting, modifying, and maintaining and/or servicing machines or equipment\", and adds: \"These activities include lubrication, cleaning or unjamming of machines or equipment and making adjustments or tool changes, where the employee may be exposed to the unexpected energization or startup of the equipment or release of hazardous energy.\" Setting up, clearing a jam and changing a tool are all on that list. Stored energy gets its own requirement - 1910.147(d)(5)(i): \"Following the application of lockout or tagout devices to energy isolating devices, all potentially hazardous stored or residual energy shall be relieved, disconnected, restrained, and otherwise rendered safe.\" The lock comes first, and then the energy already in the machine is dealt with. Both, in that order."},
{"title":"The environment around the machine","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#the-environment-around-the-machine","text":"The environment around the machine Slip hazards Clean dust, metal chips, water and coolant off the floor immediately. Lighting Ensure lighting is adequate before operating any machine. Walkways Keep aisles and emergency exits unobstructed."},
{"title":"Operating habits","page":"Machine Hazards and Housekeeping","summary":"The two ways a machine injures you - it catches hold and pulls you in, or it closes on you - plus the environment around it and the habits that keep both off you.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/machine-hazards.html#operating-habits","text":"Operating habits The chuck key never leaves your hand. It is not released until it is out of the chuck. There is no moment where the key is in the chuck and not in your grip - not to reach for something, not to answer someone, not for a second. Stop machines completely before taking measurements or making adjustments. Secure all workpieces before operation - use clamps, vises or fixtures. Never leave tools on or near operating machinery. Why the chuck key rule is worded as a grip and not as a reminder A key left in a chuck is launched the instant the machine starts. Written as \"remember to remove the key\" the rule depends on you remembering at the one moment you are busy with something else; written as the key never leaves your hand there is nothing to remember, because a key that is still in the chuck is still in your hand and you cannot start the machine. Do not let a spring be the thing that saves you. Some chuck keys are spring-loaded and eject themselves when you let go - ours are, on the lathe. Not all keys are, and our Jacobs chuck keys are not. So the habit has to be the same on every machine, or it is a habit that works until you walk up to a different chuck. A drill-press chuck key is lighter than a lathe chuck key and carries far less energy. It is still dangerous: it is in the list of things that become projectiles in Rotating machinery above, and \"less mass\" is not the same as \"will not hurt you\" when it leaves a spinning chuck at the distance you are standing. The shutdown and cleaning steps that close every session are in After operating any equipment."},
{"title":"Manual lathe: Category 3 in the Metals lab","page":"Manual Lathe","summary":"Check workholding and material support before starting; keep clear of the rotating work and stop for hazardous chip strings.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/manual-lathe.html#manual-lathe-category-3-in-the-metals-lab","text":"Manual lathe: Category 3 in the Metals lab A lathe rotates the workpiece against a cutting tool. See Lathe Operations for chucks, work support and turning operations. Category 3 in the Metals lab. Follow Equipment Categories and Supervision for permission and supervision. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment before this machine-specific check. The general entanglement and stopping rules are in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. In our labs, nothing that can get caught by the lathe comes near it: no long, untied hair, long sleeves, gloves or rings (see Personal Protective Equipment )."},
{"title":"Check the setup and the material extending through the spindle","page":"Manual Lathe","summary":"Check workholding and material support before starting; keep clear of the rotating work and stop for hazardous chip strings.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/manual-lathe.html#check-the-setup-and-the-material-extending-through-the-spindle","text":"Check the setup and the material extending through the spindle In our labs, check the setup before starting the lathe. Check that the chuck or other workholding grips the part securely and that the cutting tool is secured. The chuck key never leaves your hand. It is not released until it is out of the chuck (see the chuck-key rule ). Check the support for long material, including material extending through the back of the spindle. In our labs, never run long, unsupported material through the spindle. Check the planned movement of the tool and carriage against the chuck, workpiece and supports. Locate the emergency stop and foot brake, and confirm how to operate them before starting. A long unsupported bar can whip as it rotates. Support at the cutting end does not establish that the part behind the spindle is safe."},
{"title":"The whole workpiece rotates; chip strings can catch and cut","page":"Manual Lathe","summary":"Check workholding and material support before starting; keep clear of the rotating work and stop for hazardous chip strings.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/manual-lathe.html#the-whole-workpiece-rotates-chip-strings-can-catch-and-cut","text":"The whole workpiece rotates; chip strings can catch and cut The chuck, workpiece and any material projecting from the spindle are rotating hazards. Apply the entanglement rules to the entire setup, including its back side. In our labs, stop and clear long chip strings when they become a hazard. Never pull a chip string by hand. Use Metal Chips and Swarf for removal and cleanup. If long strings keep forming, have the instructor review the cutting setup before continuing."},
{"title":"If the work shifts or the setup behaves unexpectedly, stop safely","page":"Manual Lathe","summary":"Check workholding and material support before starting; keep clear of the rotating work and stop for hazardous chip strings.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/manual-lathe.html#if-the-work-shifts-or-the-setup-behaves-unexpectedly-stop-safely","text":"If the work shifts or the setup behaves unexpectedly, stop safely Do not try to steady rotating work or catch a loose part. Use the stopping controls you were shown if you can reach them safely, keep clear, and notify the instructor. Use Lab Conduct and Emergencies for an injury or emergency. Finish with the shutdown procedure and chip cleanup. A stopped spindle is required before measuring or changing the setup; maintenance requires the isolation procedure in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Four reasons a chip is dangerous","page":"Metal Chips and Swarf","summary":"Chips come off hot, razor sharp and fast. How to handle them, how to clean them up, and the rules on compressed air.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metal-chips.html#four-reasons-a-chip-is-dangerous","text":"Four reasons a chip is dangerous A chip is not scrap until it has cooled and stopped moving. Straight off the tool it is every one of these at once. Hot Where an aluminum chip forms, the cut runs roughly 250&ndash;650 &deg;C (500&ndash;1200 &deg;F), depending on the alloy and the speed. Chips do not look it. Sharp The leading edge of a chip is always razor sharp. It is a freshly cut edge and it will open a hand. Strong A long continuous chip is a steel ribbon. If it wraps a sleeve or a limb it can pull you into the machine. Slippery Chips on the floor roll underfoot. This is one of the most common ways people get hurt without the machine touching them."},
{"title":"Never clear chips with your hands","page":"Metal Chips and Swarf","summary":"Chips come off hot, razor sharp and fast. How to handle them, how to clean them up, and the rules on compressed air.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metal-chips.html#never-clear-chips-with-your-hands","text":"Never clear chips with your hands Do not reach into a machine to clear chips while it is running, and do not pick up a long stringy chip by hand at any time. Stop the spindle, then deal with it. Gloves Gloves may only be used for cleanup after the machine is shut down. Never wear gloves at a running machine. A glove catches where bare skin would pull free, and takes your hand with it - see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. Brush A brush may be used while the machine is in operation - your hands stay outside the machine. If clearing the chip means reaching in, stop the spindle first. On a CNC machine, take particular care: the tool can move without warning, and the enclosure invites you to reach further in than you should."},
{"title":"Compressed air - there are limits on this","page":"Metal Chips and Swarf","summary":"Chips come off hot, razor sharp and fast. How to handle them, how to clean them up, and the rules on compressed air.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metal-chips.html#compressed-air-there-are-limits-on-this","text":"Compressed air - there are limits on this Compressed air is convenient and it is the option people reach for first. It is also regulated, because a chip in a moving air stream is a projectile and air forced against skin can be driven into the body. The rule OSHA permits compressed air for cleaning only where it is reduced to less than 30 psi, and only then with effective chip guarding and personal protective equipment ( 29 CFR 1910.242(b) ). All three conditions, together - not the pressure alone. Chip guarding is anything that stops a chip reaching eyes or unbroken skin: a protective cone nozzle covers the operator, and a screen or barrier is what covers everyone else. In practice The 30 psi is measured at the nozzle, dead-ended - blocked by the work or a hand - and is met by a limited air nozzle or a regulator, not by the supply pressure upstream of it. Eye protection, every time. Everyone nearby too - you do not get to decide the range. Never at skin, yours or anyone else's. Never toward another person, and check who is behind the machine first. Never into an enclosed space. If air would just relocate the mess into the air or across the shop, use a brush or the vacuum instead."},
{"title":"Cleaning up","page":"Metal Chips and Swarf","summary":"Chips come off hot, razor sharp and fast. How to handle them, how to clean them up, and the rules on compressed air.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metal-chips.html#cleaning-up","text":"Cleaning up Use cleaning equipment or gloves to handle chips for disposal - the sharp edge does not dull because the machine stopped. Clear the machine so only the residue in the bottom tray remains. Sweep the floor, and mop if the floor is wet with coolant. If cleaning equipment such as a vacuum gets wet, open it up and let it dry before it is put away. Chips and fines do not go in the same place Chips and swarf go in the recycling container. That container is for chips only. Fines go in the trash. Dust from sanding, grinding, buffing and polishing, and what comes out of a dust collector - never the recycling container. Why - two reasons, and the second one is the safety reason The recycler pays for clean aluminum. A clean load gets the aluminum price; mix another metal in and the whole load drops to a much cheaper one. Fines pick iron up - off a shared belt or wheel, off steel work at the next bench - so fines do not go in. Compare how often each container actually leaves the building. A chip recycling container fills slowly and can stand most of a semester between pickups; the trash goes out daily. Fines belong in the stream that leaves, not the one that stands in the corner for months quietly collecting more of them. Anything whose problem is accumulation does not go in the container designed to accumulate. Two thresholds, same observation. \"There is iron in the fines\" is a real problem for the recycling price and not a fire problem - and the reason both are true at once is that the two thresholds are orders of magnitude apart. The price threshold is a trace: a few steel chips are enough to drop the whole load to the cheaper price. A thermite reaction needs iron oxide - rust or grinding scale, not bright steel - at something near three parts oxide to one part aluminum by weight, plus an ignition source far hotter than a spark. That is most of the pile, not a trace. Most rules in a shop have a number behind them; knowing which number you are being measured against, and how far you actually are from it, is the difference between following a rule and understanding it. Fine dust burns; chips do not. Aluminum chips and swarf are hard to ignite. The fire hazard is fines - and then only when a cloud of them is thrown into the air and meets an ignition source, or when a great deal of rust or steel grinding scale is mixed into them. Do not let fine dust accumulate, and do not raise a cloud of it cleaning up. If fine metal debris does ignite, do not fight it. The ABC extinguisher on the wall is the wrong tool and will scatter it. Alarm, evacuate, and tell the fire crew it is a metal fire. See Fire Safety."},
{"title":"Find the equipment category","page":"Metals Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Metals lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metals-lab-equipment.html#find-the-equipment-category","text":"Find the equipment category These are the equipment categories for the Metals lab. If an item is not on the list, ask the instructor to place it before you use it."},
{"title":"Category 1","page":"Metals Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Metals lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metals-lab-equipment.html#category-1","text":"Category 1 Hand tools Fixture table Clamps Sanding / polishing Deburring kit Part setup See Category 1 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 2","page":"Metals Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Metals lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metals-lab-equipment.html#category-2","text":"Category 2 CNC mill Drill press Belt/disk sander Grinder Power tools Waterjet See Category 2 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 3","page":"Metals Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Metals lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/metals-lab-equipment.html#category-3","text":"Category 3 Horizontal band saw Vertical band saw Manual mill CNC lathe Manual lathe Desktop injection molder See Category 3 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Crosscuts and angled ends: Category 3 in the woods lab","page":"Miter Saw","summary":"Support the board against the fence, keep clear of the full blade path and use the cutting motion taught for the installed saw.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/miter-saw.html#crosscuts-and-angled-ends-category-3-in-the-woods-lab","text":"Crosscuts and angled ends: Category 3 in the woods lab A miter saw cuts a board to length or cuts an angled end. The miter setting turns the cut across the table; the bevel setting tilts the blade. See How Saws Cut Wood and Choosing a Woodworking Machine. Category 3 in the Woods / Construction lab. Follow category permission and supervision requirements. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure, Personal Protective Equipment and the guard and isolation requirements in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Before starting: set angles, support the board and check clearance","page":"Miter Saw","summary":"Support the board against the fence, keep clear of the full blade path and use the cutting motion taught for the installed saw.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/miter-saw.html#before-starting-set-angles-support-the-board-and-check-clearance","text":"Before starting: set angles, support the board and check clearance With the machine off and isolated for setup, set and secure the miter and bevel angles. Mark the cut and check its alignment with the stationary blade's path; a laser, if fitted, is an alignment aid. Seat the board against the fence and table. Support long ends at the same height as the saw table so the board cannot tip during the cut. Check that the moving guard works and shields the blade. If a dust collector is fitted, check that it is ready for use. Check the entire blade path at the selected angles, including clearance from clamps. In our labs, keep all body parts at least 6 inches from the blade and never cut small pieces. If the piece cannot be supported while maintaining that clearance, stop and ask for another method."},
{"title":"Keep the work still and use the motion taught for this saw","page":"Miter Saw","summary":"Support the board against the fence, keep clear of the full blade path and use the cutting motion taught for the installed saw.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/miter-saw.html#keep-the-work-still-and-use-the-motion-taught-for-this-saw","text":"Keep the work still and use the motion taught for this saw Secure the work against the fence using the trained holding or clamping method. Do not improvise a stacked-board cut; have the instructor establish the setup. With hands outside the blade path and the blade clear of the work, start the saw and let it reach cutting speed. Make the cut with controlled feed using the motion demonstrated for the installed saw. A sliding saw needs its own cutting sequence. Have that demonstrated before using the slide; do not interpret “pull through” as permission to pull a spinning blade toward you through the board. At the end of the cut, release the trigger, keep the head controlled and wait for the blade to stop before lifting it or removing the work and cutoff. Use a blade and material combination approved for the installed saw. Changing the blade alone does not make a woodworking miter saw suitable for metal, masonry or wet cutting."},
{"title":"If the board shifts or the blade binds: release the trigger","page":"Miter Saw","summary":"Support the board against the fence, keep clear of the full blade path and use the cutting motion taught for the installed saw.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/miter-saw.html#if-the-board-shifts-or-the-blade-binds-release-the-trigger","text":"If the board shifts or the blade binds: release the trigger Keep clear of the blade and let it stop. Do not reach in to reposition the work or free a cutoff while it moves. Notify the instructor and follow the isolation procedure before clearing a jam. If injured, move clear and follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies. Normal shutdown and cleanup follow Equipment Operating Procedure."},
{"title":"Waterjet: Category 2 in the Metals lab","page":"OMAX Waterjet","summary":"The lab's CAD-to-cut sequence: prepare the path, verify material settings, follow the setup prompts and dry-run before cutting.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/omax-waterjet.html#waterjet-category-2-in-the-metals-lab","text":"Waterjet: Category 2 in the Metals lab The waterjet cuts a programmed shape with water and abrasive. See Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting for how abrasive waterjet cutting works. Category 2 in the Metals lab. Follow Equipment Categories and Supervision for permission and supervision, and complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment. Keep hands away from the cutting nozzle and clear of its travel. The jet cuts material, and the moving head can trap a hand. See Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for movement, guards and stopping. OMAX safety information is further reading for the installed-machine training."},
{"title":"Prepare the file and toolpath: lab steps 1–10","page":"OMAX Waterjet","summary":"The lab's CAD-to-cut sequence: prepare the path, verify material settings, follow the setup prompts and dry-run before cutting.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/omax-waterjet.html#prepare-the-file-and-toolpath-lab-steps-1-10","text":"Prepare the file and toolpath: lab steps 1–10 This is our lab's OMAX operating sequence. Follow it with the machine-specific instruction you received at checkout. Export the CAD file to DXF or DWG, as for a laser-cut part. Turn on the OMAX machine. Turn on the water: the top water line under the sink. Open ProtoMAX Layout software. Click File → “Open file for other CAD software.” Use the move, rotate and duplicate tools to lay out the parts. One square is equal to one inch. Click Autopath to create toolpaths. Check that the path cuts on the correct side of each line and cuts inside items before outside items, so the part is not cut loose before its interior features are cut. Click Post. Select the starting position, normally the bottom left. Save the post. It saves as an.omax file."},
{"title":"Verify settings, follow the prompts and dry-run: lab steps 11–15","page":"OMAX Waterjet","summary":"The lab's CAD-to-cut sequence: prepare the path, verify material settings, follow the setup prompts and dry-run before cutting.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/omax-waterjet.html#verify-settings-follow-the-prompts-and-dry-run-lab-steps-11-15","text":"Verify settings, follow the prompts and dry-run: lab steps 11–15 Right-click on Post and open the.omax file in ProtoMAX Make. When Make asks for cutting parameters, select the appropriate material and enter its thickness. The lab procedure says to oversize the thickness “by just a bit”; confirm that allowance with the instructor. If the machine was just turned on, Make shows a red homing button at the top of the screen. Make sure nothing obstructs the nozzle's movement before clicking it to home the machine. Follow all the on-screen steps. These include, but are not limited to: Attaching the abrasive hose. Testing the nozzle for abrasive flow. Placing and securing the material. Homing the nozzle to the starting position on the material. Adjusting the water height if necessary. Dry-running the program. Flipping down the splash guard. Run the program. In step 14, “homing” to the material means the job's starting position; it is distinct from the machine-homing step in step 13. Have both positions demonstrated during checkout."},
{"title":"A dropped part or a fault needs a safe stop, not a reach into the tank","page":"OMAX Waterjet","summary":"The lab's CAD-to-cut sequence: prepare the path, verify material settings, follow the setup prompts and dry-run before cutting.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/omax-waterjet.html#a-dropped-part-or-a-fault-needs-a-safe-stop-not-a-reach-into-the-tank","text":"A dropped part or a fault needs a safe stop, not a reach into the tank Do not reach into the cutting area to retrieve a part while the machine is operating. If the material moves or the cut behaves unexpectedly, keep clear and use the stopping procedure demonstrated during checkout. Notify the instructor; follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies for an emergency. The lab sequence above ends at running the program. Have the instructor demonstrate shutdown and safe part recovery before using the machine; a part falling into the tank is not a recovery procedure. Use Equipment Operating Procedure for the general shutdown and reporting requirements."},
{"title":"Personal Protective Equipment","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#personal-protective-equipment","text":"Personal Protective Equipment Five topics. One of them - eye protection - applies the moment you walk in. The rest are set by what you are doing. Eyes Always, in the lab spaces. No exceptions. Hearing Prolonged exposure to loud equipment. Feet Closed-toe shoes, always. Respiratory Any grinding or sanding; hazardous dust or fumes. Clothing & hair Always, near anything that rotates."},
{"title":"Eye protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#eye-protection","text":"Eye protection Safety glasses are MANDATORY in the lab spaces at all times. If you wear prescription glasses Side shields that attach to your existing frames. Oversized safety glasses that fit over corrective glasses. Prescription safety glasses are also acceptable. Lens requirements Safety glasses must be clear or yellow tinted only. Dark or shaded safety glasses are not acceptable for indoor lab use. Must meet ANSI Z87.1 safety standards."},
{"title":"Hearing protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#hearing-protection","text":"Hearing protection Industry standard: shop tools nearly always require hearing protection in professional settings. In a teaching lab the exposure is typically shorter than in production work, but the protection still matters. When to use it Required for prolonged exposure to loud equipment - grinders, sanders, planers and similar. The rule of thumb: if you have to raise your voice to be heard by someone at arm's length, it is loud enough to need protection. You do not need a meter, and most shop tools fail this test the moment they start. The numbers behind it OSHA measures noise as an average across the working day, in A-weighted decibels ( dBA ). Two figures matter: 85 dBA The action level, averaged over 8 hours - the point at which a workplace must run a hearing conservation programme. 90 dBA The permissible exposure limit, averaged over 8 hours. Above 90 dBA the allowed time drops fast - every extra 5 dBA halves it: 90 dBA 8 hours 95 dBA 4 hours 100 dBA 2 hours 105 dBA 1 hour 110 dBA 30 minutes 115 dBA 15 minutes or less Impact noise should not exceed 140 dB peak - a single hammer blow or nail gun can reach it, and that damage is immediate rather than cumulative. These are OSHA workplace limits (29 CFR 1910.95). NIOSH recommends a stricter 85 dBA limit that halves the allowed time every 3 dBA rather than every 5. Hearing loss from noise does not come back, so treat these as ceilings, not targets. Two types available Earplugs Small foam plugs inserted into the ear canal. Disposable or reusable. Available on request from the instructor. Ear muffs Over-ear cups covering the whole ear. Reusable and adjustable. Often preferred for intermittent use. Both types significantly reduce harmful noise levels."},
{"title":"Foot protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#foot-protection","text":"Foot protection Minimum requirement: closed-toe shoes are MANDATORY - no sandals, no flip-flops, no open-toe footwear. Recommended upgrades Slip-resistant soles, for traction on surfaces that may be slippery. Puncture-resistant soles, against sharp metal debris. Steel-toe or composite-toe protection - recommended, not required. Avoid Canvas shoes or thin-soled footwear. High heels or unstable footwear. Anything that does not fully enclose your feet. You will be standing for most of the lab period - choose something supportive and comfortable."},
{"title":"Respiratory protection","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#respiratory-protection","text":"Respiratory protection MANDATORY when using any grinder or sander, and when working with materials that produce hazardous dust. Grinding galvanized material is PROHIBITED. Zinc fumes from galvanized steel are toxic when heated. Health hazards Metal dust and particles cause respiratory irritation and long-term lung damage. Wood dust can cause allergic reactions. Dust and fumes are not the same problem, and they do not take the same protection. The next section is the one that decides which you need."},
{"title":"Respiratory protection - what to wear","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#respiratory-protection-what-to-wear","text":"Respiratory protection - what to wear A dust mask does nothing against fumes or vapours. An N95 is a particle filter. It does not stop gases, welding or brazing fume, solvent vapour, or anything else you can smell. Wearing one while you can smell the air is not protection. For dust - grinding, sanding, sawing N95 mask Filters 95% of airborne particles. The minimum for dusty work. Half- or full-face respirator Fitted with a particulate filter, for heavy or extended dust. For fumes and vapours You need an approved respirator with the right chemical cartridge, not a dust mask. Ask the instructor which cartridge before you start. A respirator only works if it seals. A loose mask, or a beard along the seal, defeats it. If there is no correct cartridge available, the job does not happen until there is. If you can smell fumes or taste particles, your protection is inadequate. Stop work immediately and upgrade it - or stop the job. Chemicals and liquids carry their own handling and first-aid information - see Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Clothing and jewelry","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#clothing-and-jewelry","text":"Clothing and jewelry Loose clothing and accessories catch in rotating machinery. That is how this causes serious injury or death - it is not a dress code. Prohibited Loose-fitting clothing, or garments with dangling features - drawstrings, ties and similar. Long sleeves, hoodies or jackets near rotating equipment. ALL jewelry, except silicone wedding rings. Neckties, scarves and lanyards."},
{"title":"Hair, and what to wear instead","page":"Personal Protective Equipment","summary":"What you wear, and when. Eye protection is mandatory in the lab spaces at all times; the rest depends on the task.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/ppe.html#hair-and-what-to-wear-instead","text":"Hair, and what to wear instead Hair Long hair must be secured back and kept away from equipment. Use hair ties, clips or caps to prevent entanglement. Recommended Cotton or fire-retardant materials - preferred, not required. Close-fitting, short-sleeved shirts. Pants without excessive loose material."},
{"title":"3D printers: Category 1 in the Rapid Prototyping Center","page":"Prusa 3D Printers","summary":"Obtain project approval, match the sliced file to the printer and filament, and physically check the machine before setting it ready.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/prusa-3d-printers.html#3d-printers-category-1-in-the-rapid-prototyping-center","text":"3D printers: Category 1 in the Rapid Prototyping Center These printers build parts from filament, layer by layer. See 3D Printing from CAD for preparing a model and FDM 3D Printing for the printing process. Category 1 in the Rapid Prototyping Center. Use Equipment Categories and Supervision for permission and supervision. General checks and protective equipment are in Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment."},
{"title":"Get the project and material approved before printing","page":"Prusa 3D Printers","summary":"Obtain project approval, match the sliced file to the printer and filament, and physically check the machine before setting it ready.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/prusa-3d-printers.html#get-the-project-and-material-approved-before-printing","text":"Get the project and material approved before printing In our labs, a DET faculty member must approve a 3D-printing project before it starts. A project assigned by DET faculty needs no separate project approval, but must stay within its own guidelines and all RPC rules. For approval, explain what the item is, where its design came from, how it will be made, the material amount and source, and its intended use; supply the digital file through the instructor's designated channel. No personal material without prior approval. No weapons, weapon-like items, weapon components, ammunition, dangerous materials or drug paraphernalia. No commercial use. Class projects take priority over personal projects."},
{"title":"Slice for the selected printer, then check it in person","page":"Prusa 3D Printers","summary":"Obtain project approval, match the sliced file to the printer and filament, and physically check the machine before setting it ready.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/prusa-3d-printers.html#slice-for-the-selected-printer-then-check-it-in-person","text":"Slice for the selected printer, then check it in person In PrusaSlicer, arrange and orient the model. Select the printer and filament, and set supports, infill and brim as needed. Slice, review the preview, material use and estimated time, then export the.gcode or.bgcode file. Use the lab's private job-identification instructions to name the file. In Prusa Connect, select a printer matching the type used when slicing. Check that printer's queue before uploading the file. In our labs, talk with the uploader before deleting a named job you think is no longer needed. Upload the file to the selected printer's dashboard, or use the guide's Send to Connect workflow from PrusaSlicer. Before choosing Set Ready and Print Now, go to the machine and check: The build plate is clear. The loaded filament type matches the sliced file. There is enough filament for the job. If you are unsure the machine is ready, choose Add to print queue and check it before setting it ready later."},
{"title":"Hot surfaces and moving parts remain hazards during remote printing","page":"Prusa 3D Printers","summary":"Obtain project approval, match the sliced file to the printer and filament, and physically check the machine before setting it ready.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/prusa-3d-printers.html#hot-surfaces-and-moving-parts-remain-hazards-during-remote-printing","text":"Hot surfaces and moving parts remain hazards during remote printing The nozzle and heated bed can burn, including after motion stops. Let them cool before handling the finished print or build plate. Keep hands and tools out of the printer's moving area; use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for the general movement and isolation rules. See Prusa's hot-part precautions for further reading, not permission to service lab equipment. The lab guide uses Prusa Connect or its app to monitor progress. A remote status display does not replace the physical readiness check. Set Ready can release the next queued job, so use it only after checking the machine and clearing the work area. When your print is finished and safe to handle, remove it, check the plate and material again, and set the printer ready for the next job. Remove scrap and purged filament, return tools and leave the work area tidy."},
{"title":"Report jams and faults; do not repair the printer yourself","page":"Prusa 3D Printers","summary":"Obtain project approval, match the sliced file to the printer and filament, and physically check the machine before setting it ready.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/prusa-3d-printers.html#report-jams-and-faults-do-not-repair-the-printer-yourself","text":"Report jams and faults; do not repair the printer yourself In our labs, report jams, malfunctions and other technical problems to DET faculty. Do not try to fix equipment yourself. Stop a failing print using the controls covered in your training, and do not set a faulty printer ready for another job. The student guide permits resetting an offline printer only when it is not currently in use. If you cannot establish that, or if a fault remains, report it rather than repeatedly resetting. Use Lab Conduct and Emergencies for an injury or emergency."},
{"title":"Find the equipment category","page":"Rapid Prototyping Center Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Rapid Prototyping Center.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/rpc-lab-equipment.html#find-the-equipment-category","text":"Find the equipment category These are the equipment categories for the Rapid Prototyping Center. If an item is not on the list, ask the instructor to place it before you use it."},
{"title":"Category 1","page":"Rapid Prototyping Center Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Rapid Prototyping Center.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/rpc-lab-equipment.html#category-1","text":"Category 1 3D printers Hand tools (excluding knives) See Category 1 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 2","page":"Rapid Prototyping Center Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Rapid Prototyping Center.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/rpc-lab-equipment.html#category-2","text":"Category 2 Laser cutter Hand tools (knives) See Category 2 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 3","page":"Rapid Prototyping Center Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Rapid Prototyping Center.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/rpc-lab-equipment.html#category-3","text":"Category 3 None. See Category 3 permission and supervision requirements."},
{"title":"Straight cuts: Category 3 in the woods lab","page":"Table Saw","summary":"Guide the work, keep hands out of the blade path and prevent material from binding between the blade and fence.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/table-saw.html#straight-cuts-category-3-in-the-woods-lab","text":"Straight cuts: Category 3 in the woods lab A table saw makes straight cuts: ripping follows the grain; crosscutting goes across it. See How Saws Cut Wood and Choosing a Woodworking Machine for blade and machine selection. Category 3 in the Woods / Construction lab. Follow category permission and supervision requirements. Complete Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment before using this reminder. Guarding, entanglement and power isolation are covered in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Before starting: check the blade, guide and feed path","page":"Table Saw","summary":"Guide the work, keep hands out of the blade path and prevent material from binding between the blade and fence.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/table-saw.html#before-starting-check-the-blade-guide-and-feed-path","text":"Before starting: check the blade, guide and feed path Confirm that the blade suits the material and cut. With the machine stopped and isolated for setup, expose only the blade height needed for the cut, following the installed saw's instructions. Check the blade guard and the saw's anti-kickback equipment. A missing or malfunctioning device means the setup is not ready. No one removes a guard in this lab. A machine that needs a guard off is the instructor's to deal with, out of class (see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping ). Choose the guide for the cut: the rip fence for ripping, or the approved crosscut setup. In our labs, do not use the sled with the rip fence. Plan support for the board throughout the cut and place the push stick within reach before starting. Check where both hands will go as the trailing end approaches the blade."},
{"title":"Control blade contact and kickback throughout the cut","page":"Table Saw","summary":"Guide the work, keep hands out of the blade path and prevent material from binding between the blade and fence.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/table-saw.html#control-blade-contact-and-kickback-throughout-the-cut","text":"Control blade contact and kickback throughout the cut Kickback is material thrown back toward the feed side by the blade. Binding between the blade and fence is one cause. Do not make a cut that traps a loose cutoff there. Use the approved guide to keep the board aligned; do not steer it freehand. For a rip cut, push the part between the fence and blade. Use the push stick to keep hands out of the blade path, including at the end of the cut. Feed steadily at a rate the blade can cut. Ease the feed through harder areas; do not force a struggling cut. Keep clear of the blade while it coasts. Stop it completely before removing scraps or reaching for a finished piece near it. Why one feed rate does not fit every cut Thickness, wood, blade and available power change how readily material cuts. Pushing harder does not fix binding or a poorly chosen setup; it can make control worse."},
{"title":"If material binds or kicks back: stop and report it","page":"Table Saw","summary":"Guide the work, keep hands out of the blade path and prevent material from binding between the blade and fence.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/table-saw.html#if-material-binds-or-kicks-back-stop-and-report-it","text":"If material binds or kicks back: stop and report it Do not reach after a thrown piece or try to free material beside a moving blade. Stop the saw from a safe position and notify the instructor. Have the setup checked before restarting; clearing a jam follows the isolation procedure in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. If injured, move clear and stop the machine only if you can do so safely. Follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies. Finish normal work with the shutdown and cleanup steps in Equipment Operating Procedure."},
{"title":"Manual mill: Category 3; CNC mill: Category 2 in the Metals lab","page":"Vertical Mill","summary":"Check workholding, cutter clearance and cutting settings; recognize the different categories for manual and CNC mills.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/vertical-mill.html#manual-mill-category-3-cnc-mill-category-2-in-the-metals-lab","text":"Manual mill: Category 3; CNC mill: Category 2 in the Metals lab A vertical mill uses a rotating cutter to remove material held in a vise or clamps. See Vertical Mill Anatomy and Motion for the machine's parts. The manual mill is Category 3 in the Metals lab; the CNC mill is Category 2 there. Use Equipment Categories and Supervision for the corresponding permission and supervision requirements. Start with Equipment Operating Procedure, Personal Protective Equipment, Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Metal Chips and Swarf. For the Tormach workflow, use Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown; its controller sequence is not a startup sequence for a manual mill."},
{"title":"Check clamping, cutter travel and cutting settings before the cut","page":"Vertical Mill","summary":"Check workholding, cutter clearance and cutting settings; recognize the different categories for manual and CNC mills.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/vertical-mill.html#check-clamping-cutter-travel-and-cutting-settings-before-the-cut","text":"Check clamping, cutter travel and cutting settings before the cut In our labs, check and recheck the workpiece clamping before beginning an operation. Check the vise or clamps and the cutter's retention. Confirm the tool can follow the planned path without striking the workholding. Identify the closing gaps between the moving table, workholding and machine frame. Keep the planned travel clear. Check spindle speed, feed, depth of cut and width of cut for the planned operation. Check coolant and slide lubrication as directed for the installed mill. Report low levels, damage or a maintenance need rather than adjusting the machine's sliding fits yourself. Complete the installed mill's guard and emergency-stop checks from Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"A rotating cutter and a moving table create different hazards","page":"Vertical Mill","summary":"Check workholding, cutter clearance and cutting settings; recognize the different categories for manual and CNC mills.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/vertical-mill.html#a-rotating-cutter-and-a-moving-table-create-different-hazards","text":"A rotating cutter and a moving table create different hazards The cutter can cut or catch what touches it; the table can close a gap against the column, vise or clamps. Keep clear of both the cutting area and the table's travel. CNC motion follows the program, so a pause in motion is not permission to reach in. Use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for stopping before measurements and for isolation before adjustments or maintenance. Use Metal Chips and Swarf for chip removal, including the required controls if compressed air is used."},
{"title":"If a tool breaks or the work comes loose, step away","page":"Vertical Mill","summary":"Check workholding, cutter clearance and cutting settings; recognize the different categories for manual and CNC mills.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/vertical-mill.html#if-a-tool-breaks-or-the-work-comes-loose-step-away","text":"If a tool breaks or the work comes loose, step away In our labs, step away if a tool breaks or the workpiece becomes loose. Stop the mill if it is safe to do so; do not endanger yourself to reach a control. Notify the instructor in every case. If injured, ask for help and follow Lab Conduct and Emergencies. For normal shutdown use the installed mill's procedure and Equipment Operating Procedure; the Tormach sequence is in Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown."},
{"title":"Find the equipment category","page":"Woods / Construction Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Woods / Construction lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/woods-lab-equipment.html#find-the-equipment-category","text":"Find the equipment category These are the equipment categories for the Woods / Construction lab. If an item is not on the list, ask the instructor to place it before you use it."},
{"title":"Category 1","page":"Woods / Construction Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Woods / Construction lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/woods-lab-equipment.html#category-1","text":"Category 1 Hand tools (excluding saws) Paint / stain Sandpaper Glue Clamps See Category 1 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 2","page":"Woods / Construction Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Woods / Construction lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/woods-lab-equipment.html#category-2","text":"Category 2 Hand tools (saws) Power tools Belt/disk sander Drill press CNC router See Category 2 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"Category 3","page":"Woods / Construction Lab Equipment","summary":"Equipment categories for the Woods / Construction lab.","collection":"Safety","url":"safety/woods-lab-equipment.html#category-3","text":"Category 3 Table saw Band saw Miter saw Planer Jointer See Category 3 permission and supervision requirements before use."},
{"title":"DC flows one way; AC reverses direction","page":"AC and DC","summary":"Current direction, sine-wave voltage, transmission losses, and conversion between AC and DC.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/ac-and-dc.html#dc-flows-one-way-ac-reverses-direction","text":"DC flows one way; AC reverses direction Direct current (DC) flows in one direction. Its magnitude can change without making it AC. Alternating current (AC) reverses direction repeatedly. A battery supplies DC; a wall receptacle supplies AC. Electronics generally run from a DC supply. That does not mean a transistor cannot handle an AC signal: transistors can amplify changing signals and act as switches. The supply and the signal are different parts of the story. Start with Current, Voltage, and Resistance for the quantities and equations used here. For measurement, see Measuring Electric Circuits."},
{"title":"A 120 V sine wave peaks near positive and negative 170 V","page":"AC and DC","summary":"Current direction, sine-wave voltage, transmission losses, and conversion between AC and DC.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/ac-and-dc.html#a-120-v-sine-wave-peaks-near-positive-and-negative-170-v","text":"A 120 V sine wave peaks near positive and negative 170 V The nominal 120 V of a US receptacle is an RMS value: root mean square. For a sine wave, V peak = V RMS &times; &radic;2, and V RMS = V peak / &radic;2. Thus 120 &times; &radic;2 = 169.7 V. The instantaneous voltage swings between about +170 V and &minus;170 V, or about 340 V peak to peak. One cycle of a sine wave: positive peak 169.7 V, negative peak minus 169.7 V, and RMS reference 120 V. RMS is not the waveform's average voltage. Frequency counts complete cycles per second, in hertz (Hz). A 60 Hz waveform completes 60 whole cycles per second; a 50 Hz waveform completes 50. A cycle returns to the same phase, rather than ending at the opposite peak. US household mains is 60 Hz; many other countries use 50 Hz ( DOE, Electricity 101 ). Why use RMS instead of the average voltage? A symmetrical sine wave averages to zero over a whole cycle, yet it still heats a resistor. RMS is the DC voltage that would produce the same average heating in that resistance. Square the instantaneous voltage, average those squares over a cycle, then take the square root. The square-root-of-two conversion applies to a sine wave; do not apply it to an arbitrary inverter waveform."},
{"title":"Higher transmission voltage reduces current and line loss","page":"AC and DC","summary":"Current direction, sine-wave voltage, transmission losses, and conversion between AC and DC.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/ac-and-dc.html#higher-transmission-voltage-reduces-current-and-line-loss","text":"Higher transmission voltage reduces current and line loss For the same transmitted power, a higher voltage allows a lower current. The line's resistive heating is P loss = I 2 R line. Halve the current and the loss becomes one quarter, if line resistance stays the same. Here R line includes the complete current path. Transformers made it practical to step AC voltage up for transmission and down for use. That advantage helped the AC systems associated with Westinghouse and Tesla prevail over Edison's early DC distribution system in the “war of the currents.” It was not one inventor winning every part of electrical engineering. A comparison with the same power and the same line In an ideal DC or unity-power-factor comparison, transmitting 8,000 W at 400 V requires 20 A. At 4,000 V it requires 2 A. If the complete line resistance is 0.5 &Omega;, the losses are 200 W and 2 W. Ten times the voltage gives one hundredth the resistive loss. This simplified comparison omits converter losses and voltage regulation; it is not a design for a real transmission line. For AC loads with a phase shift, see Power and Efficiency. The historical advantage belongs to the equipment available at the time. Modern converters also make high-voltage DC transmission practical."},
{"title":"A rectifier is one stage of an AC-to-DC power supply","page":"AC and DC","summary":"Current direction, sine-wave voltage, transmission losses, and conversion between AC and DC.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/ac-and-dc.html#a-rectifier-is-one-stage-of-an-ac-to-dc-power-supply","text":"A rectifier is one stage of an AC-to-DC power supply A rectifier makes current flow in one direction from an AC input. Its output can still pulsate. Filtering smooths the output, and regulation controls the output voltage. A complete power supply may include all these functions as well as isolation. Linear supply: commonly uses a line-frequency transformer, rectifier, filter, and linear regulator. The regulator dissipates excess voltage as heat. Switch-mode supply: switches current rapidly and uses energy-storage components to convert voltage efficiently. An AC-input version also rectifies its input. Not every switch-mode converter provides isolation. Power-supply manufacturer ARCH gives illustrative typical efficiency categories: linear supplies about 50–60%, conventional switching supplies 75–85%, and modern high-efficiency designs 90–95%. Both lose some input energy as heat. Efficiency depends on the design, input voltage and load; compare published efficiency curves at the intended operating point rather than assuming one percentage for a whole category. See Power and Efficiency for the input/output calculation."},
{"title":"An inverter makes AC from DC; the waveform matters","page":"AC and DC","summary":"Current direction, sine-wave voltage, transmission losses, and conversion between AC and DC.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/ac-and-dc.html#an-inverter-makes-ac-from-dc-the-waveform-matters","text":"An inverter makes AC from DC; the waveform matters A pure-sine inverter produces an output close to a sine wave. A modified-sine inverter produces a stepped approximation. Equipment may run differently, make more noise or heat, or fail to operate on a stepped waveform. Check the equipment manufacturer's compatibility guidance; equal RMS voltages do not make the waveforms interchangeable. For grid-tied PV inverters, efficiency is typically above 95% under most operating conditions, according to Sandia PVPMC’s CEC Inverter Test Protocol. Efficiency depends on AC output power, DC voltage and sometimes temperature. Every real conversion loses some energy. Several conversions in a row multiply their efficiencies rather than adding them. Much of what we use today runs on DC internally, even when it plugs into AC. Phones, computers, and microcontroller electronics bring the instructor's closing question into focus: who gets the last laugh? Motors, generation, and energy storage make use of both forms of electrical power."},
{"title":"Acceleration is change in velocity over time","page":"Acceleration and Squared Time","summary":"Calculate change in velocity over time, distinguish average from instantaneous acceleration, and convert squared time units correctly.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/acceleration-and-squared-time.html#acceleration-is-change-in-velocity-over-time","text":"Acceleration is change in velocity over time Acceleration is not simply \"speeding up.\" It is a change in velocity, so it can describe speeding up, slowing down, or turning. a avg = &Delta;v / &Delta;t = (v f &minus; v i ) / (t f &minus; t i ) &Delta;v Change in velocity. With one chosen positive direction, &Delta;v = v f &minus; v i. m/s 2 Meters per second per second. Each second, velocity changes by the stated number of meters per second. ft/s 2 Feet per second per second. The coherent foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second system uses this acceleration unit with slugs for mass. Write the sign. A final velocity lower than the initial velocity produces negative acceleration in the selected direction; it is not an error just because the answer is negative."},
{"title":"Average is an interval; instantaneous is a moment","page":"Acceleration and Squared Time","summary":"Calculate change in velocity over time, distinguish average from instantaneous acceleration, and convert squared time units correctly.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/acceleration-and-squared-time.html#average-is-an-interval-instantaneous-is-a-moment","text":"Average is an interval; instantaneous is a moment Average acceleration is calculated over stated start and end conditions. An accelerometer or control system can report instantaneous acceleration at one moment. They match at every moment only when the acceleration stays constant through the interval. Machine arm example A machine arm starts from rest and reaches 3 m/s in 4 s: a avg = (3 m/s &minus; 0 m/s) / 4 s = 0.75 m/s 2. This is the average acceleration for the four-second move. If the drive ramps gently at the start and end, its instantaneous acceleration changes during the move even though the average remains 0.75 m/s 2. Robot gripper example A SolidWorks motion study programs a gripper from 0 to 5 ft/s in 0.5 s to pick an electronic component. Its average acceleration is 5 ft/s / 0.5 s = 10 ft/s 2. In SI, 10 ft/s 2 &times; (0.3048 m / 1 ft) = 3.048 m/s 2."},
{"title":"Squared time needs a squared factor","page":"Acceleration and Squared Time","summary":"Calculate change in velocity over time, distinguish average from instantaneous acceleration, and convert squared time units correctly.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/acceleration-and-squared-time.html#squared-time-needs-a-squared-factor","text":"Squared time needs a squared factor Acceleration has time in the denominator twice. When converting an acceleration unit, convert the entire squared time relationship, not just one s. 1 min = 60 s means 1 min 2 = (60 s) 2 = 3,600 s 2. Use the inverse relationship in the correct direction. 1 ft/s 2 = 0.3048 m/s 2, not 0.3048 2 m/s 2. The foot is the only unit being changed in that expression; seconds are already seconds in both units. By contrast, converting m/min 2 to m/s 2 requires the full squared time factor. The unit cancellation shows why: 1 m/min 2 &times; (1 min / 60 s) 2 = 1/3,600 m/s 2."},
{"title":"Acceleration lab","page":"Acceleration and Squared Time","summary":"Calculate change in velocity over time, distinguish average from instantaneous acceleration, and convert squared time units correctly.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/acceleration-and-squared-time.html#acceleration-lab","text":"Acceleration lab Change the initial and final velocities and the elapsed time. The last result converts the calculated foot-per-second-squared value to SI. Velocity change Initial velocity (ft/s) Final velocity (ft/s) Elapsed time (s) Change in velocity 5 ft/s Average acceleration 10 ft/s² SI equivalent 3.048 m/s² JavaScript is off. The shown gripper case is solved above: 0 to 5 ft/s in 0.5 s gives 10 ft/s², or 3.048 m/s²."},
{"title":"Practice and sources","page":"Acceleration and Squared Time","summary":"Calculate change in velocity over time, distinguish average from instantaneous acceleration, and convert squared time units correctly.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/acceleration-and-squared-time.html#practice-and-sources","text":"Practice and sources Use the practice bank to apply average acceleration over a stated interval. Add to practice Source note NASA's Newton's second-law resource connects force, mass, and acceleration for grades 9&ndash;12. The calculation here uses the average velocity-change form first; a real machine profile may have changing instantaneous acceleration within the same interval."},
{"title":"Ten bits give 1024 codes, numbered 0 through 1023","page":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","summary":"Relate an Uno R3's 10-bit ADC reading to its reference voltage, step size and approximate sampling time.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/analog-to-digital-conversion.html#ten-bits-give-1024-codes-numbered-0-through-1023","text":"Ten bits give 1024 codes, numbered 0 through 1023 An analog-to-digital converter (ADC) samples a voltage and reports a number. The classic Uno R3 has a 10-bit ADC: 2 10 = 1024 possible codes. analogRead(A0) reads analog input A0 and returns an integer from 0 to 1023. The reference voltage sets the scale. With an assumed exact 5.000 V reference, one nominal step is 5/1024 = 0.0048828125 V = 4.883 mV, or about 4.9 mV. Resolution describes the step size; it does not guarantee that a real voltage measurement is accurate to that amount. Arduino's resolution and timing figures The official analogRead reference source gives “5 volts / 1024 units” and “about 100 microseconds (0.0001 s)”. That is roughly 10,000 reads per second on ATmega-based boards using the usual API, not an absolute ADC hardware limit or a guaranteed loop rate. Computation, communication and delays take additional time. The published analogRead reference is the learner's API lookup."},
{"title":"Use 1024 for the nominal voltage step","page":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","summary":"Relate an Uno R3's 10-bit ADC reading to its reference voltage, step size and approximate sampling time.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/analog-to-digital-conversion.html#use-1024-for-the-nominal-voltage-step","text":"Use 1024 for the nominal voltage step For these exercises, use a clearly defined ideal bin model: divide the 0–5 V span into 1024 equal bins, round down to the bin number, and limit the top code to 1023. code = min(1023, floor(V in × 1024 / V ref )). Here floor means round down. At 3.000 V with a 5.000 V reference: floor(614.4) = 614. To report the nominal voltage represented by a code, use its lower bin edge: V = code × V ref /1024. Code 800 represents 800 × 5/1024 = 3.90625 V. In this model its bin extends up to, but not including, 3.9111328125 V. Why dividing by 1023 answers a different question There are 1024 codes but only 1023 intervals between code labels 0 and 1023. Multiplying by 5/1023 stretches those endpoints to exactly 0 and 5 V; that is endpoint scaling, not the nominal ADC step. The explorer and practice consistently use 1024. Real converters have transition offsets, noise and reference error; their exact thresholds are not predicted by this classroom bin model. A code cannot recover the original voltage exactly."},
{"title":"Explore the steps and check the units","page":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","summary":"Relate an Uno R3's 10-bit ADC reading to its reference voltage, step size and approximate sampling time.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/analog-to-digital-conversion.html#explore-the-steps-and-check-the-units","text":"Explore the steps and check the units The two-way ADC controls in the PWM and ADC explorer let you enter a voltage or a code. Try nearby voltages within one bin, then cross its boundary. In reverse, code 1023 gives 4.9951171875 V as its lower edge, not proof that the input equals that value. Practice converting a reading to volts"},
{"title":"Rotation is measured three ways","page":"Angular Motion","summary":"Measure rotation in degrees, radians, and revolutions, then use angular velocity and angular acceleration to describe a turning shaft.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/angular-motion.html#rotation-is-measured-three-ways","text":"Rotation is measured three ways Angular displacement is how far something has turned. Its symbol is &theta; (theta), and there are three units in everyday use for it. They measure the same rotation; they differ only in how much turning counts as one unit. Revolution (r) One complete turn. This is what a machine nameplate counts. Degree (&deg;) One revolution cut into 360 parts. This is what a drawing and a protractor use. Radian (rad) The angle whose arc is exactly as long as the radius. This is the SI unit, and it is the one formulas such as v = &omega;r expect. One radian A sector drawn from the centre of a circle. Two radii of equal length open out from the centre, and the curved arc between their tips is drawn the same length as one radius. The angle enclosed at the centre is labelled one radian. radius r radius r arc length = r 1 rad A radian is defined by the circle itself, not by a count somebody picked. Swing the radius around the rim until the arc it traces is as long as the radius, and the angle you have opened is one radian. The three units, describing one and the same rotation. In revolutions In degrees In radians 1 r 360&deg; 2&pi; rad = 6.283185 rad 0.5 r 180&deg; &pi; rad = 3.141593 rad 0.25 r 90&deg; &pi;/2 rad = 1.570796 rad &asymp; 0.159 r &asymp; 57.29578&deg; 1 rad The two factors worth keeping to hand: 1&deg; = 0.01745329 rad and 1 rad = 180&deg;/&pi; = 57.29578&deg;. The degree sign sits tight against the number - 90&deg;, not 90 &deg;. Every other unit symbol takes a space: 1.57 rad, 45 r/min. The degree, minute, and second of plane angle are the only exceptions to that rule. Why a radian is barely a unit at all A radian is an arc length divided by a radius - a length over a length. The two cancel, so NIST gives the base-unit expression for the radian as m/m and its value as simply 1: the radian and steradian are \"special names for the number one that may be used to convey information about the quantity concerned.\" That is not trivia. It is why a torque in N-m multiplied by an angle in rad comes out as energy in joules with no conversion factor anywhere, and why a formula that mixes rotation with straight-line motion works only when the angle is in radians. Degrees and revolutions are counts somebody picked; the radian is the ratio the geometry already contains."},
{"title":"Angular velocity is how fast it turns","page":"Angular Motion","summary":"Measure rotation in degrees, radians, and revolutions, then use angular velocity and angular acceleration to describe a turning shaft.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/angular-motion.html#angular-velocity-is-how-fast-it-turns","text":"Angular velocity is how fast it turns Angular velocity uses the symbol &omega; (omega), and it is directed angle change per time interval for rotation about a fixed axis: &omega; avg = &Delta;&theta; / &Delta;t. Instantaneous angular velocity is the rate at one moment; it equals this average throughout the interval only for constant angular velocity. rad/s Radian per second. The SI unit, and the form every formula on Rotation and Linear Speed needs. r/min, or rpm Revolutions per minute. What a motor nameplate, a spindle display, and a speed chart are marked in. Nameplates speak rpm; formulas speak rad/s. Convert before you calculate, and convert back before you set a machine. The conversion pair, rounded, either way round: 1 r/min = 0.1047198 rad/s - 1 rad/s = 9.549297 r/min Worked example Convert 12.5 rpm to rad/s. Put revolutions per minute on the bottom so they cancel: 12.5 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s 1 r/min = 1.309 rad/s. Where 0.1047198 comes from One revolution is 2&pi; radians and one minute is 60 seconds, so one revolution per minute is 2&pi;/60 = 0.104719755&hellip; radians per second. It is not a measured constant; it falls straight out of the two definitions. Turning it over gives 60/(2&pi;) = 9.5492966&hellip; revolutions per minute in one radian per second."},
{"title":"Angular acceleration is how fast the turning changes","page":"Angular Motion","summary":"Measure rotation in degrees, radians, and revolutions, then use angular velocity and angular acceleration to describe a turning shaft.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/angular-motion.html#angular-acceleration-is-how-fast-the-turning-changes","text":"Angular acceleration is how fast the turning changes Angular acceleration uses the symbol &alpha; (alpha), and it is change in angular velocity divided by the time it took: &alpha; avg = &Delta;&omega; / &Delta;t Its SI unit is rad/s 2. Everything true of straight-line acceleration on Acceleration and Squared Time is true here as well: it is a change, so a shaft slowing down has angular acceleration opposite its angular velocity (negative if it is turning in the positive direction), and the squared second means a squared factor whenever the time unit changes. Worked example: a motor speeding up A motor shaft is turning at 17 rpm. Power to the motor is increased, and 1.1 s later it is turning 8 rpm faster. What is the angular acceleration in rad/s 2? Only the change matters, so convert the 8 rpm increase: 8 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 0.837758 rad/s. Divide by the time: 0.837758 rad/s &divide; 1.1 s = 0.7616 rad/s 2. 0.76 rad/s 2, as an average over those 1.1 s. The starting 17 rpm never enters the calculation - acceleration is built out of the change, not out of the speed. Worked example: a spindle running up A machining-centre spindle goes from rest to 8,000 rpm in 2.5 s. 8,000 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 837.758 rad/s. (837.758 rad/s &minus; 0 rad/s) &divide; 2.5 s = 335.10 rad/s 2. That is the average over the run-up. A drive that ramps gently at each end has a changing instantaneous angular acceleration inside the same interval, exactly as a linear drive does."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Angular Motion","summary":"Measure rotation in degrees, radians, and revolutions, then use angular velocity and angular acceleration to describe a turning shaft.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/angular-motion.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked NIST SP 811, Appendix B gives revolution per minute to radian per second as 1.047 198 E&minus;01, revolution to radian as 6.283 185 E+00, and degree to radian as 1.745 329 E&minus;02. The 9.549297 r/min in one rad/s is the reciprocal of the first of those. NIST SP 811, Chapter 4, Table 3, lists the radian with symbol rad, value 1, and base-unit expression m/m. The rule that every unit symbol takes a space except the degree, minute, and second of plane angle is NIST SP 811, section 7.2."},
{"title":"Ampere-hours measure charge; watt-hours measure energy","page":"Battery Capacity and Packs","summary":"Convert Ah to Wh, use C-rate and current to estimate performance, and predict the effect of series and parallel cells.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-capacity-and-packs.html#ampere-hours-measure-charge-watt-hours-measure-energy","text":"Ampere-hours measure charge; watt-hours measure energy A 2 Ah cell and a 2 Ah battery can store very different amounts of energy if their voltages differ. Ah tells you charge. To estimate energy, you also need voltage. Energy (Wh) ≈ capacity (Ah) × nominal voltage (V) Energy (J) = energy (Wh) × 3600 For an ideal 2.4 Ah, 3.6 V cell: 2.4 × 3.6 = 8.64 Wh, or 8.64 × 3600 = 31,104 J. Convert mAh to Ah by dividing by 1000 before multiplying by voltage. Why the battery estimate uses an approximate sign Wh = Ah × V is exact for constant voltage. A real cell's voltage changes during discharge, so nominal voltage gives an energy estimate. Usable charge also changes with load, temperature, age and the cutoff voltage. These calculations assume the stated capacity is available. See Work and Energy for energy units and Current, Voltage, and Resistance for voltage and charge. Practise battery energy"},
{"title":"C-rate sets a current scale; load current sets ideal runtime","page":"Battery Capacity and Packs","summary":"Convert Ah to Wh, use C-rate and current to estimate performance, and predict the effect of series and parallel cells.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-capacity-and-packs.html#c-rate-sets-a-current-scale-load-current-sets-ideal-runtime","text":"C-rate sets a current scale; load current sets ideal runtime A discharge rate of 1C means a current numerically equal to the capacity in Ah divided by one hour. The rate has units of h −1. Here C is a rate notation, not the coulomb symbol. Rated maximum current (A) ≈ maximum discharge C-rate (h⁻¹) × capacity (Ah) Ideal runtime (h) = capacity (Ah) ÷ load current (A) A 2.4 Ah cell rated for continuous 3C discharge has a rated current estimate of 3 × 2.4 = 7.2 A. At a constant 1.2 A load, ideal runtime is 2.4 ÷ 1.2 = 2 h. At 3C, the ideal duration is one-third of an hour, or 20 minutes. A C-rate may describe a test rate, a charge rate, a continuous limit or a short pulse limit. Only a specified maximum discharge rating belongs in the maximum-current equation. Actual pack limits may be lower because of wiring, temperature or protection hardware. A constant-power load also changes current as voltage changes. Practise ideal runtime"},
{"title":"Series adds voltage; parallel adds charge capacity","page":"Battery Capacity and Packs","summary":"Convert Ah to Wh, use C-rate and current to estimate performance, and predict the effect of series and parallel cells.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-capacity-and-packs.html#series-adds-voltage-parallel-adds-charge-capacity","text":"Series adds voltage; parallel adds charge capacity For matched cells, connect cells in series to increase voltage. Put identical series strings in parallel to increase Ah at the same voltage. 3s2p means three cells per series string and two strings in parallel: six cells altogether. Two identical 3.6 V 2.4 Ah cells in series give 7.2 V and 2.4 Ah; the same two in parallel give 3.6 V and 4.8 Ah. Each arrangement stores an ideal 17.28 Wh. Pack V = series count × cell V Pack Ah = parallel count × cell Ah Pack Wh ≈ pack V × pack Ah With 3.6 V, 2.4 Ah cells, a 3s2p pack is 10.8 V, 4.8 Ah and 51.84 Wh. At 3C it has an ideal rated-current estimate of 14.4 A. A 1.2 A pack load gives 4 h ideal runtime. This is an electrical model, not a pack assembly procedure. It assumes matched cells, balanced series operation and equal current sharing between parallel strings. Real packs need appropriate cell matching, interconnections and protection. See Series and Parallel Circuits."},
{"title":"Explore a pack by changing one input at a time","page":"Battery Capacity and Packs","summary":"Convert Ah to Wh, use C-rate and current to estimate performance, and predict the effect of series and parallel cells.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-capacity-and-packs.html#explore-a-pack-by-changing-one-input-at-a-time","text":"Explore a pack by changing one input at a time First double the series count: voltage and energy double, but Ah and runtime at the same pack current do not. Reset it, then double the parallel count: Ah, energy, rated current and ideal runtime double. The load current below is measured at the pack terminals. Paper example: 3.6 V, 2.4 Ah cells; 3s2p; continuous discharge rating 3C; load 1.2 A. V = 3 × 3.6 = 10.8 V; Ah = 2 × 2.4 = 4.8 Ah; Wh = 10.8 × 4.8 = 51.84 Wh; Imax = 3 × 4.8 = 14.4 A; t = 4.8 ÷ 1.2 = 4 h. With no JavaScript, use these equations to explore other packs. This example remains in the print view. The model excludes discharge curves, conversion losses, reserve capacity and aging. It reports when the requested current exceeds the entered rating; a result below that rating is not a safety certification. Practise series and parallel packs"},
{"title":"Capacity, self-discharge and aging are different","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#capacity-self-discharge-and-aging-are-different","text":"Capacity, self-discharge and aging are different A cell is one electrochemical unit. A battery may contain one cell or several. Its chemistry helps determine voltage, mass, charging needs and how well it holds charge. Self-discharge is loss of stored charge while no load is connected. Aging is loss of the ability to hold or deliver charge, even after recharging. Both depend on the cell design, temperature and storage conditions, so a chemistry name is not a shelf-life specification. A capacity rating also depends on discharge current, temperature and the voltage at which the test stops. Use the particular cell's data, not a single capacity assigned to every AA or D cell."},
{"title":"Primary alkaline cells suit replaceable, modest loads","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#primary-alkaline-cells-suit-replaceable-modest-loads","text":"Primary alkaline cells suit replaceable, modest loads Common primary alkaline cells serve devices such as clocks, remote controls and flashlights. They are widely available and retain charge relatively well in storage. Their useful capacity and terminal voltage fall more under heavy loads and cold conditions. They are not intended to be recharged. Leakage can damage equipment, especially when cells are exhausted or left in storage. Rechargeable-battery memory effect is not a useful concern for a primary cell."},
{"title":"NiCd and NiMH are rechargeable nickel batteries","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#nicd-and-nimh-are-rechargeable-nickel-batteries","text":"NiCd and NiMH are rechargeable nickel batteries Nickel-cadmium (NiCd) and nickel-metal hydride (NiMH) use alkaline electrolytes, but they should not be confused with primary alkaline cells. Two rechargeable nickel families. Family Uses and strengths Limits NiCd Legacy cordless tools and industrial backup equipment; capable of substantial discharge currents in suitable designs. Cadmium is toxic and requires appropriate battery recycling. Repeated partial cycling can cause memory-related voltage depression. Self-discharge matters during storage. NiMH Rechargeable consumer cells and hybrid-vehicle packs; generally more capacity than comparable NiCd cells and no cadmium. Conventional cells can self-discharge noticeably; low-self-discharge versions improve charge retention. Voltage depression can occur, but memory concerns are generally less pronounced than with NiCd. Use a charger designed for the chemistry and cell. Fast charging is appropriate only when the cell and charger support it. Do not deliberately drive a pack to zero volts to prevent memory effect: a weaker cell can be forced into reverse polarity by the others. Why a remote-control recommendation needs more than a chemistry name A low average load makes charge retention important, but it does not by itself select NiCd over NiMH. Compare the actual cells' self-discharge, usable capacity, voltage and charging requirements. A low-self-discharge NiMH cell behaves differently from an older conventional NiMH cell."},
{"title":"Lead-acid trades low mass efficiency for useful power and cost","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#lead-acid-trades-low-mass-efficiency-for-useful-power-and-cost","text":"Lead-acid trades low mass efficiency for useful power and cost Lead-acid batteries appear in engine starting and stationary backup supplies. They can deliver high starting currents and are relatively inexpensive, but they are heavy for the energy stored. They self-discharge during storage. Prolonged storage while discharged promotes sulfation, which can reduce capacity; this is not the NiCd memory effect. Starting and deep-cycle designs serve different loads. Use the specified charging method: the electrolyte is acidic, lead is toxic, and charging can release gas. Flooded, absorbed-glass-mat and gel designs all exist; modern lead-acid does not always mean gel."},
{"title":"Lithium-ion needs protection; LiPo is not automatically better","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#lithium-ion-needs-protection-lipo-is-not-automatically-better","text":"Lithium-ion needs protection; LiPo is not automatically better Lithium-ion batteries power portable electronics, tools and electric vehicles. Their energy per unit mass is generally considerably higher than NiMH, with relatively slow self-discharge and no routine need for full discharge to prevent memory effect. They still lose capacity through calendar aging and cycling. Protection and charge control must keep voltage, current and temperature within the cell's limits. A bare cell may not contain its own protection circuit. The pack design must provide the required protection; a protection circuit is not a substitute for a compatible charger. Lithium-ion polymer, often called LiPo, commonly appears as a light pouch cell in portable devices and model aircraft. The packaging allows thin shapes; some designs support high discharge rates. It does not guarantee higher energy density, lower cost or better resistance to physical damage. Pouches need protection against puncture and crushing. Self-discharge and memory behaviour are broadly those of lithium-ion. Nominal voltage and charging limits depend on the particular chemistry and cell. Do not use a single voltage for every lithium-ion battery, and do not confuse rechargeable lithium-ion with primary lithium-metal cells."},
{"title":"Battery history moves from a pile to portable rechargeable cells","page":"Battery Chemistries","summary":"Compare primary alkaline, rechargeable nickel, lead-acid and lithium-ion batteries, and the short history behind them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/battery-chemistries.html#battery-history-moves-from-a-pile-to-portable-rechargeable-cells","text":"Battery history moves from a pile to portable rechargeable cells The important sequence is the change in what batteries made possible. Volta, around 1800: the voltaic pile made a sustained electrochemical source practical to demonstrate. Gaston Planté, 1850s: lead-acid introduced a rechargeable battery. Carl Gassner, 1880s: dry-cell development made the electrolyte easier to contain for portable use. “Dry” means immobilized electrolyte, not absence of moisture. Waldemar Jungner, 1890s: nickel-cadmium added another rechargeable chemistry. Lewis Urry, 1950s: work on the modern alkaline cell improved portable primary batteries. Lithium-ion, 1990s: commercialization enabled rechargeable products with high energy per unit mass, building on earlier work by several researchers. Scope of this short chronology This overview uses the historical sequence in the course generation-and-storage notes, with dates rounded to eras. Invention, patent and market introduction are different milestones; the dates are not intended as a priority claim. The development of lithium-ion was a multi-researcher effort, not solely the work of the company that first sold it."},
{"title":"A bearing allows useful motion and restrains unwanted motion","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#a-bearing-allows-useful-motion-and-restrains-unwanted-motion","text":"A bearing allows useful motion and restrains unwanted motion A loose rigid part can move in six independent ways: translate along three axes and rotate about those three axes. These are its degrees of freedom. A bearing transfers load to a support while allowing the motion the machine needs, with low friction. For a rotating shaft, the goal is usually one remaining freedom: rotation about its own axis. Two spaced radial supports resist sideways movement and tilting; axial location stops the shaft walking lengthwise. A single short bearing does not automatically provide that whole constraint system. Six free-body motions become one intended shaft rotation when spaced radial supports and axial location are provided. Let x run along the shaft. Translation T means movement along an axis; rotation R means turning about it. This is an ideal rigid-body model; real supports deflect and have clearance. Bearings guide motion; motors and drives supply it. Belt, chain and gear drives load their supporting shafts. See Torque for turning effort and Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment for the complete support arrangement."},
{"title":"Radial load crosses the shaft; thrust acts along it","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#radial-load-crosses-the-shaft-thrust-acts-along-it","text":"Radial load crosses the shaft; thrust acts along it Radial load acts perpendicular to the shaft axis. Axial load, or thrust, acts parallel to it. Combined load has both components. A bearing's orientation and internal geometry determine which it can carry. Three horizontal shafts show perpendicular radial force, parallel axial force, and both force components together. Load direction is relative to the shaft, not to the room: a vertical shaft can have a horizontal radial load. First selection filter, not a load rating. Check the exact catalog design. Type Radial load Axial load Combined load Plain journal, without thrust faces Yes No intended capacity Needs a thrust bearing Plain footstep or thrust collar face Needs a journal guide Yes, against the loaded face With a radial guide Deep-groove ball Yes Both directions, within rating Yes, within rating Angular-contact ball or tapered roller, single row Yes Primarily one direction Yes; opposing bearing needed for reverse thrust Cylindrical roller, NU or N design Yes No intended locating duty Use another bearing for thrust Pure thrust ball or cylindrical thrust roller No Yes; direction depends on arrangement No Do not turn “roller” into “radial only,” or “thrust” into a rule for every product bearing that name. Tapered rollers carry combined loads; some spherical roller thrust designs also accept radial load under specified conditions."},
{"title":"Plain and rolling bearings trade friction, upkeep and cost","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#plain-and-rolling-bearings-trade-friction-upkeep-and-cost","text":"Plain and rolling bearings trade friction, upkeep and cost A plain bearing supports sliding surfaces, often with a replaceable sleeve called a bushing. A rolling-element bearing places balls or rollers between rings. “Anti-friction” is a traditional name, not a promise of zero friction. Compare the operating conditions as well as the purchase price. Question Plain bearing Rolling-element bearing Starting versus running resistance? Breakaway friction can exceed running friction. A hydrodynamic film is not fully established at rest. Often low starting resistance and a smaller start/run difference; seals, grease and preload still matter. Lubrication and maintenance? Dry grades may need no added lubricant; oil-fed designs require the specified supply and cleanliness. Usually grease or oil lubricated. A sealed, prelubricated bearing still has a finite service life. Speed? Dry bushings have material-specific speed and pressure limits. Fluid-film bearings can serve high-speed machinery. Many suit moderate to high speeds; type, size, lubrication and heat limit speed. Load and cost? Simple bushings often cost little initially. Capacity depends on size, material, speed and heat removal. Precision rings and elements add cost. Roller designs often carry more load than ball designs of similar overall size. Rolling contact generally reduces resistance compared with sliding contact. It does not guarantee higher efficiency than every fluid-film bearing, nor does a cheap bushing guarantee lower lifetime cost. Include lubrication equipment, replacement, downtime and power loss. Bearing losses contribute to Power and Efficiency and the stage efficiencies discussed in Gear Ratios."},
{"title":"Plain bearings differ in both lubrication and load direction","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#plain-bearings-differ-in-both-lubrication-and-load-direction","text":"Plain bearings differ in both lubrication and load direction Dry or self-lubricating A suitable polymer, composite or lubricant-containing material supplies a low-friction sliding surface. Follow that material's instructions; adding grease is not automatically an improvement. Hydrodynamic Relative motion draws lubricant into a converging gap, forming a pressure-supported film. Starts and stops require attention because the running film depends on motion. Hydrostatic An external pressure supply supports the load on a fluid film, including at rest while the supply operates. The pump, flow control and loss-of-pressure behavior are part of the system. Those are lubrication descriptions. Shape answers a different question: a journal surrounds a shaft to carry radial load; a footstep supports axial load at a shaft end; a thrust collar presents an annular face to carry axial load. An assembly can combine a journal and a collar to handle both components."},
{"title":"Rings carry the load; the cage spaces the rolling elements","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#rings-carry-the-load-the-cage-spaces-the-rolling-elements","text":"Rings carry the load; the cage spaces the rolling elements Front view of a ball bearing identifying the inner ring, outer ring, balls, cage and ring raceway surfaces. Schematic with seals omitted. A raceway is the track on a ring that the rolling elements contact; ring and raceway are not quite the same thing. The inner ring fits around the shaft; the outer ring fits in the housing. The rolling elements transfer load between their raceways. The cage maintains spacing and guides the elements. In a common arrangement the inner ring turns with the shaft while the outer ring stays still. The balls spin about their own axes and orbit around the shaft. The rings do not have to rotate in opposite directions. Balls have idealized point contact; rollers have idealized line contact. Under load these become finite contact patches. The longer roller contact generally supports more load in a similar overall size. Contact angle, not just element shape, determines thrust capability. Thrust ball and roller bearings place their contact geometry to support axial force. Manufacturer reading for bearing selection Use the exact product's current catalog to check its load, speed and lubrication limits. These references introduce the design choices. NSK: Introduction to Bearings identifies rings, elements and cage. NSK: Roller Bearings compares line and point contact. Kingsbury: Hydrodynamic Bearings describes oil-film support and high-speed applications. igus: self-lubricating plain bearings describes embedded solid lubricants. SKF: Rolling Bearings, bearing type and arrangement, for product-specific selection. Practice identifying the load"},
{"title":"Double a ball bearing's load and its basic rating life falls to one eighth","page":"Bearing Types and Loads","summary":"Choose the motion to allow, identify the load, then compare plain and rolling-element bearings.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/bearing-types-and-loads.html#double-a-ball-bearing-s-load-and-its-basic-rating-life-falls-to-one-eighth","text":"Double a ball bearing's load and its basic rating life falls to one eighth L10 is the life that 90% of a large group of identical bearings are expected to reach or exceed. It is neither a guaranteed life nor an average. Change one input below and watch the life curve. Example bearing: C = 14.0 kN, given for this exercise. A real bearing's C comes from its manufacturer's catalog; this example does not represent a catalog part. Basic rating life: L10 = (C/P) p in millions of revolutions, with p = 3 for balls and p = 10/3 for rollers. C is the basic dynamic load rating; P is the equivalent dynamic load, taken as given here (for pure radial loading in this exercise, P = F r ). Use the same force units for C and P. At constant speed n in r/min, L10h = L10 × 10⁶/(60 × n) hours. The years conversion assumes a duty schedule you can change; it does not predict calendar aging. Static worked example (also used for printing). Example ball bearing: C = 14 kN (given), P = 2 kN, n = 1500 r/min C/P 7 L10 (million revolutions) 343 L10h (hours) 3811 Years at assumed duty 1.906 Duty assumption: 8 h/day × 250 days/year. Years = hours ÷ annual operating hours. L10h versus P: logarithmic vertical scale. Equal vertical steps represent equal life ratios. C and speed stay fixed. L10h (h), log scale 30490 1 3811 2 476.4 4 P (kN) Marked points: current 2 kN → 3811 h; double load 4 kN → 476.4 h. Double the load → 1/8 the life. C/P = 14/2 = 7; L10 = 7³ = 343 million revolutions. L10h = 343 × 10⁶/(60 × 1500) = 3811 h, or 1.906 years at 2000 h/year. At P = 4 kN, L10 = 42.875 million revolutions and L10h = 476.4 h. The static worked table and chart above work without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to change the inputs. Adjusted life factors (a1, aISO), contamination, lubrication and combined-load X/Y factors are beyond this page. Practice: Ball rating life · Life in hours · Double the load · Roller rating life · Required dynamic rating ·"},
{"title":"Three ways to carry rotation from one shaft to another","page":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","summary":"Choose a drive by shaft spacing, timing, speed and environment, then check its load rating and maintenance needs.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-chain-and-gear-drives.html#three-ways-to-carry-rotation-from-one-shaft-to-another","text":"Three ways to carry rotation from one shaft to another A drive transfers motion and turning effort from a driver to a driven shaft. Belts and chains bridge a gap. Gears engage directly and fit into a compact housing. Start with the job the drive must do, then choose the parts. Belt pulleys joined by a flexible band, chain sprockets joined by articulated links, and two directly meshing gears. Dashed lines mark shaft centers. Three transmission paths, drawn schematically. A belt or chain lets the shafts sit farther apart without adding an intermediate shaft. Gears, chains and timing belts are positive drives: engagement maintains a tooth-count ratio without ordinary frictional slip. Flat and V-belts are friction drives: their grip depends on friction and tension, so slip can change the speed ratio. Positive engagement does not mean perfect positioning. Tooth clearance, elastic deflection and chain speed ripple still matter. For ratios, compound trains and losses, use Gear Ratios. For turning effort and power, use Torque and Power and Efficiency."},
{"title":"Explore which drives fit the job","page":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","summary":"Choose a drive by shaft spacing, timing, speed and environment, then check its load rating and maintenance needs.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-chain-and-gear-drives.html#explore-which-drives-fit-the-job","text":"Explore which drives fit the job Choose a shaft arrangement, a timing requirement, a speed range and an environment. Change one requirement at a time and watch which reason changes. “Close” allows a compact gear mesh; “separated” needs a flexible span. These are qualitative screening rules, not a component sizing tool. Try this on paper: two separated parallel shafts must stay synchronized in a clean, lubricant-sensitive area. A timing belt is a useful starting candidate: its teeth maintain timing and the belt mesh needs no oil. Flat and V-belts cannot guarantee timing. An ordinary chain needs lubrication; a single gear pair cannot bridge the chosen gap without making the gears much larger. Now remove the timing requirement: flat or V-belts become candidates. Bring the shafts close together inside a lubricated housing: gears become candidates. Each change removes a different constraint. The controls need JavaScript. Use the example above and the comparison below to make the same decisions."},
{"title":"Compare timing, space and upkeep side by side","page":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","summary":"Choose a drive by shaft spacing, timing, speed and environment, then check its load rating and maintenance needs.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-chain-and-gear-drives.html#compare-timing-space-and-upkeep-side-by-side","text":"Compare timing, space and upkeep side by side Typical tendencies for correctly selected industrial drives; actual ratings come from the chosen product. Question Flat / V-belt Timing belt Roller chain Gear pair Center distance Useful across separated shafts Useful across separated shafts; check available lengths Useful across separated shafts; manage slack Close shafts; size and mesh set spacing Slip and timing Can slip; unsuitable for fixed timing Positive; matched teeth engage Positive average ratio; clearance and speed ripple remain Positive ratio; backlash matters on reversal Noise Usually quiet; absorbs vibration Often quiet, but tooth engagement can make noise Link engagement becomes noisier at speed Spur teeth can whine; helical engagement is smoother Lubrication Belt contact runs dry Belt contact runs dry Ordinary chain needs lubricant at its joints Most loaded metal gears need lubrication Cost Often low initial cost Matched toothed parts usually cost more than basic friction belts Include sprockets, lubrication and replacement Precision gears, bearings and housing can raise initial cost Maintenance Inspect wear, alignment and tension Inspect teeth, tracking and specified tension Lubricate; measure wear elongation; inspect sprockets Check oil/grease, seals, tooth condition and alignment Belt contact running dry does not mean the shaft bearings need no lubrication. A sealed gearbox can keep lubricant away from a product; an exposed chain generally cannot. Special materials and lubrication-free products need their own ratings. Read the parts in detail: Belt Drives, Chain Drives and Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry."},
{"title":"Turn a shortlist into a rated drive","page":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","summary":"Choose a drive by shaft spacing, timing, speed and environment, then check its load rating and maintenance needs.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-chain-and-gear-drives.html#turn-a-shortlist-into-a-rated-drive","text":"Turn a shortlist into a rated drive Record shaft directions, center distance, available space and adjustment travel. Specify input speed, required output speed, power and torque, including starts and shock loads. Decide whether the job needs synchronized motion, low noise or accurate reversal. Check temperature, abrasive dust, water and chemicals against the material and lubricant ratings. Use the manufacturer's selection procedure to check size, service factor, wrap or tooth engagement, shaft loads and bearings. Plan inspection access, replacement parts, lubrication and the total cost over the drive's life. A bigger pitch is not a complete strength rating. Width, material, speed, tooth count and duty also matter. A drive that fits the space can still fail the load check. For safe access to a drive, use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Equipment Operating Procedure. Screw-driven motion is covered separately in Screw Threads. Practice choosing a drive What the explorer assumes, and where to check a selection The explorer compares ordinary drives on parallel shafts. “Separated” means a belt or chain span is practical but a single compact gear pair cannot reach. Speed labels are relative to the chosen component's rating, with no universal rpm cutoff. It excludes load sizing, life prediction, efficiency percentages and automatic cost rankings. “Candidate” means worth sizing, not approved for use. Its rules synthesize Gates' belt maintenance manual, Renold's transmission-chain installation and maintenance guide, and KHK's helical gear selection notes. Each manufacturer requires details beyond the four classroom choices."},
{"title":"Flat and V-belts grip; timing belts engage teeth","page":"Belt Drives","summary":"Distinguish friction belts from timing belts, read belt and pulley terminology, and recognize common wear patterns.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-drives.html#flat-and-v-belts-grip-timing-belts-engage-teeth","text":"Flat and V-belts grip; timing belts engage teeth Flat belt A broad, flat band transmits force through friction against a pulley. Useful for smooth, quiet motion and long spans when the drive is designed for them. Tracking and tension matter. V-belt A trapezoidal section runs in a matching V-groove. Contact at the sides gives a wedging action that increases grip. The belt should not rely on rubbing the bottom of the groove. Timing / synchronous belt Teeth on the belt engage matching grooves in the pulleys. Load is carried by this engagement, so the drive keeps its tooth-count ratio without frictional slip in normal operation. Flat belt touching a flat pulley surface, V-belt contacting groove sides with bottom clearance, and timing-belt teeth fitting matching pulley grooves. Contact shape determines how force crosses from pulley to belt. Sketches show the principle, not a manufacturing profile. Timing does not come from extra tension. It comes from matching teeth. An undertensioned or overloaded timing belt can jump teeth or strip them; that is a fault, not its normal way of transmitting power. A notched V-belt remains a friction belt: relief notches for bending do not make it a timing belt."},
{"title":"Read length, tension and wrap before ordering","page":"Belt Drives","summary":"Distinguish friction belts from timing belts, read belt and pulley terminology, and recognize common wear patterns.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-drives.html#read-length-tension-and-wrap-before-ordering","text":"Read length, tension and wrap before ordering Pulley / sheave The wheel that carries a belt. “Sheave” is commonly used for a grooved belt pulley. A timing pulley has matching tooth grooves. Pitch and pitch length Timing-belt pitch is the spacing between adjacent tooth centers along the pitch line. Pitch length is the loop length along that reference line, near the tensile cords. It is not the outside circumference. Match pitch and tooth profile to the pulley. Tension The pulling force along the belt. The difference between the loaded and return spans transmits torque. Installation tension keeps the belt seated; too little encourages slip or tooth jumping, and too much loads belts and bearings. Wrap angle The angle through which the belt contacts a pulley. Less wrap gives a friction belt less contact and a timing belt fewer teeth in engagement. Check the smaller pulley in particular. Catalogs may specify inside, outside, datum, effective or pitch lengths. Use the length convention and measuring method for that belt family; do not substitute a tape measurement of the outside for the catalog value. One-stage reminder: n out = n in × D driver / D driven using matching effective/pitch diameters and assuming no slip. For timing belts, use tooth counts N in place of diameters. Write the reduction as i:1, where i = input speed / output speed. Follow Gear Ratios for the ratio method and Rotation and Linear Speed for belt linear speed. Here N means teeth and n means rotational speed. Some class slides use n for teeth and ω for angular speed; check the quantity, not just the letter."},
{"title":"Choose a belt for the load and environment","page":"Belt Drives","summary":"Distinguish friction belts from timing belts, read belt and pulley terminology, and recognize common wear patterns.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-drives.html#choose-a-belt-for-the-load-and-environment","text":"Choose a belt for the load and environment Belts can span separated shafts, soften vibration and avoid oil at the transmission contact. Flat and V-belts suit jobs where a small speed variation is acceptable. Choose a timing belt when the shafts must stay synchronized. Compare the alternatives in Belt, Chain and Gear Drives. Check belt section or tooth profile, width, minimum pulley size, available length, wrap, power rating, temperature and chemical compatibility. There is no single maximum speed or temperature range for every belt. Reinforcement cords limit stretch, but the belt still needs correct alignment and installation tension. Some specially designed flat-belt layouts connect nonparallel shafts. That does not make misalignment acceptable in an ordinary V-belt or timing-belt installation. Frictional slip may soften an overload, but it can also generate heat and destroy the belt; it is not a guaranteed overload-protection device."},
{"title":"Use the wear pattern to find the cause","page":"Belt Drives","summary":"Distinguish friction belts from timing belts, read belt and pulley terminology, and recognize common wear patterns.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-drives.html#use-the-wear-pattern-to-find-the-cause","text":"Use the wear pattern to find the cause Fault clues to investigate with the drive safely isolated. Symptom Possible cause to check Squeal, glazing or excess heat on a friction belt Low tension, overload, contamination or worn pulley grooves can reduce grip. Frayed edges or the belt tracking to one side Pulley alignment, damaged flanges and shaft movement. Cracks or hardening Age, heat, chemical attack or repeated bending around an unsuitable pulley. Missing timing-belt teeth or lost timing Overload, inadequate tension, poor engagement, debris or worn pulleys. Inspect the pulleys as well as the belt. Replacing the worn part without fixing its cause often repeats the failure. Follow the product's tension and inspection instructions; use Equipment Operating Procedure before touching the drive. Practice a timing-pulley speed Manufacturer references for belt geometry and faults Gates, Preventive Maintenance Manual, “Belt identification,” “Installation,” and “Belt drive problem diagnosis,” supplies the length/profile distinctions and troubleshooting checks. Gates' industrial synchronous-belt guide explains toothed engagement and dry belt contact. Use the exact belt's data for a real selection."},
{"title":"Explore how pulley size and spacing change belt length and wrap","page":"Belt Drives","summary":"Distinguish friction belts from timing belts, read belt and pulley terminology, and recognize common wear patterns.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/belt-drives.html#explore-how-pulley-size-and-spacing-change-belt-length-and-wrap","text":"Explore how pulley size and spacing change belt length and wrap Use pitch diameters D (large) and d (small), and center distance C in one length unit. For an open belt, the approximate pitch length is L ≈ 2C + π(D + d)/2 + (D − d)²/(4C). The last term is the correction for unequal pulleys. Increasing C increases length and brings small-pulley wrap closer to 180°; the no-slip speed ratio stays D/d. Real belts come in standard lengths from the manufacturer's catalog. Pick the nearest stock length in the correct length convention, then use “Enter L → find C” to estimate the required shaft spacing. These calculations do not set installation tension or replace the manufacturer's selection procedure. d = 100 mm D = 200 mm C = 400 mm Open belt: D = 200, d = 100 mm; n_small = 1200 r/min Belt pitch length L (approx., mm) 1277.49 Center distance C (mm) 400 Speed ratio n_small / n_large 2 Large-pulley speed (r/min) 600 Belt speed (m/min) 376.991 Small-pulley wrap (degrees) 165.638 Unequal-pulley correction (mm) 6.25 Less wrap means less grip for a friction belt and fewer teeth engaged for a timing belt. This geometry model does not rate load capacity. Worked example: D = 200 mm, d = 100 mm, C = 400 mm and n_small = 1200 r/min. L ≈ 800 + 150π + 6.25 = 1277.49 mm. The inverse returns C ≈ 400 mm. Ratio = 2; n_large = 600 r/min; v = π × 100 × 1200 / 1000 ≈ 376.99 m/min; wrap ≈ 165.64°. The static worked table above works without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to change pulley sizes, spacing and speed. Derive the inverse and check speed and wrap Multiply the length approximation by 4C: 4CL = 8C² + 2π(D + d)C + (D − d)². Set B = 4L − 2π(D + d), giving 8C² − BC + (D − d)² = 0. The quadratic formula gives C = [B + √(B² − 32(D − d)²)]/16. Use this larger root and require C &gt; (D + d)/2 so the pitch circles are separated. An impossible length produces no valid layout. Assuming no slip, n_small / n_large = D/d and v = πd n_small. With d in mm, divide v by 1000 to get m/min; with d in inches, multiply by 0.0254. Speed n is in r/min; N remains the symbol for teeth. The tangent-span angle is α = asin((D − d)/(2C)); small-pulley wrap is θ = 180° − 2α, with α expressed in degrees. Less wrap means less grip. The drawing uses circular arcs and tangent spans; the length equation approximates that geometry. Its error grows as (D − d)/(2C) grows, so very unequal, closely spaced pulleys need a more exact calculation for final selection. Practice the same method: Belt length Stock-length center distance Driven speed Belt speed Small-pulley wrap"},
{"title":"Sprocket teeth engage a chain of moving joints","page":"Chain Drives","summary":"Recognize chain components, decode ANSI pitch numbers, and distinguish ISO chain series before choosing sprockets.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/chain-drives.html#sprocket-teeth-engage-a-chain-of-moving-joints","text":"Sprocket teeth engage a chain of moving joints A chain drive is a positive drive: sprocket teeth engage the chain instead of relying on frictional grip. It can bridge separated shafts and carry substantial load, but its joints and sprockets need attention. Pitch, p The center-to-center distance between adjacent chain pins. A chain's pitch must match its sprocket. Roller A sleeve that turns around a bush as it contacts a sprocket tooth. Some small chains are bush chains and have no separate roller. Link A connecting unit of the chain. Ordinary roller chain alternates inner links, carrying bushes and rollers, with outer links, carrying pins and side plates. Sprocket The toothed wheel that engages the chain. Tooth count sets the speed relationship; tooth shape and width must suit the chain series. Master / connecting link The link used to join the ends into a loop. Use the specified type and installation method; retention and strength depend on the product. Three adjacent chain pin centers with pitch marked between the first two; side plates connect the joints and a roller surrounds each bush. Pitch measures joint spacing, not roller diameter or the length of two links together. Side view is simplified; the pin sits inside the bush and roller."},
{"title":"Read #25, #35 and #40 as pitch codes","page":"Chain Drives","summary":"Recognize chain components, decode ANSI pitch numbers, and distinguish ISO chain series before choosing sprockets.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/chain-drives.html#read-25-35-and-40-as-pitch-codes","text":"Read #25, #35 and #40 as pitch codes For the common ANSI short-pitch chain numbers here, remove the final digit of the base number and divide the remaining number by 8 to get pitch in inches. Thus #40 uses 4/8 in, not 40/8 in. #100 uses 10/8 in. Common ANSI designation examples, checked against Tsubaki's chain guide and sprocket catalog. Base number Pitch calculation Pitch Construction clue #25 2/8 in 0.250 in = 6.35 mm Final 5: bush chain, no separate rollers #35 3/8 in 0.375 in = 9.525 mm Final 5: bush chain, no separate rollers #40 4/8 in 0.500 in = 12.70 mm Final 0: roller chain #41 4/8 in 0.500 in = 12.70 mm Narrow variation; not identical to #40 #100 10/8 in 1.250 in = 31.75 mm Two digits remain before dividing by 8 A suffix such as -1 or -2 counts parallel strands: simplex or duplex. #40-2 still has a 0.500 in pitch. Heavy-series suffixes and manufacturer prefixes carry other information; do not apply the pitch shortcut to every chain product code. Checked public wording behind the ANSI numbers Tsubaki, The Complete Guide to Chain, printed page 86, states: The numbers to the left of the right-hand digit refer to the chain pitch in eighths of an inch. It also identifies the final 5 as bush construction and #41 as the narrow #40 variation. Tsubaki's Sprocket Catalog, “How to Order”, lists the pitch sizes independently. The checked ASME public listing names B29.1 as Precision Power Transmission Roller Chains, Attachments, and Sprockets, edition 2011 (R2022). See ASME B29.1. The examples above come from public manufacturer tables, not an unread clause of the paid standard."},
{"title":"“Metric chain” still needs a series and a pitch","page":"Chain Drives","summary":"Recognize chain components, decode ANSI pitch numbers, and distinguish ISO chain series before choosing sprockets.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/chain-drives.html#metric-chain-still-needs-a-series-and-a-pitch","text":"“Metric chain” still needs a series and a pitch ISO chain data are commonly tabulated in millimeters. That does not make every pitch a whole number of millimeters. ISO 606 covers both A-series chains derived from American practice and B-series chains derived from European practice. Selected B-series simplex chains from Renold's public tables. Designation Pitch 05B-1 8.000 mm 06B-1 9.525 mm 08B-1 12.700 mm 10B-1 15.875 mm The common 06B, 08B and 10B sizes correspond to 6/16, 8/16 and 10/16 in pitch. Use the table for the exact designation: small sizes such as 05B do not follow that shortcut. B identifies the series; -1 identifies one strand. Same pitch does not mean interchangeable. #40 and 08B both have 12.7 mm pitch, but roller diameter and inner width differ. Check series, pitch, width, roller diameter and strand count when matching a sprocket or replacement link. ISO scope and the source of the millimeter dimensions The ISO 606:2015 public abstract describes short-pitch precision roller and bush chains with associated sprockets. Its scope includes dimensional and strength characteristics; a chain number alone is not a load rating. The dimension examples are from Renold Roller Chain tables, “European (BS) Standard — Simplex.” Renold's Chain Standards guide explains series and strand suffixes."},
{"title":"Positive engagement still permits wear and speed ripple","page":"Chain Drives","summary":"Recognize chain components, decode ANSI pitch numbers, and distinguish ISO chain series before choosing sprockets.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/chain-drives.html#positive-engagement-still-permits-wear-and-speed-ripple","text":"Positive engagement still permits wear and speed ripple Chain is worth considering when separated shafts need a fixed average ratio and lubricant and noise are acceptable. Select it for the actual power, speed, starts, shock loads and environment. Tensile breaking strength is not an allowable continuous working load. One-stage reminder: n out = n in × N driver / N driven; reduction i = N driven / N driver, written i:1. N counts teeth and n is rotational speed. For the reasoning and further stages, use Gear Ratios. The chain wraps a sprocket as a series of straight links, forming a polygon rather than a perfectly smooth circle. This chordal action produces speed ripple, especially with small sprockets. Backlash and elastic stretch also limit precise positioning, even without ordinary slip. Wear elongation: pin/bush wear increases joint clearance and measured chain length. The worn chain rides poorly on sprocket teeth and can jump. Take-up removes slack; it does not restore worn joints. Stiff or seized joints: check lubrication reaching the joints, contamination and corrosion. Fractured plates or pins: investigate fatigue, overload, misalignment and connecting-link installation. Hooked or uneven sprocket teeth: inspect the chain and sprockets together and follow replacement limits for that drive. Use the specified lubricant, alignment and slack. A chain is not installed taut like a belt. Set inspection intervals and wear limits from the maker's guide; isolate the drive before measuring or servicing it. See Equipment Operating Procedure. Practice reading chain pitch Chain selection and maintenance reference Renold's transmission-chain installation and maintenance guide covers installation, lubrication, wear measurement and sprocket condition. Compare other drive families in Belt, Chain and Gear Drives."},
{"title":"Three quantities, three units","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#three-quantities-three-units","text":"Three quantities, three units Almost every electrical number you will read off a nameplate, a meter, or a schematic is one of three quantities. Each has a name, a quantity symbol used in formulas, and a unit symbol written after a value. Current, I The rate at which electric charge flows past a point. Its unit is the ampere, symbol A - one of the seven SI base units. One ampere is one coulomb of charge per second. Voltage, V Also called electric potential difference: the difference in electric potential between two points. Its unit is the volt, symbol V, defined as power per unit of current. Resistance, R How strongly something opposes the flow of current. Its unit is the ohm, symbol &Omega;, defined as voltage per unit of current. How each unit is built out of the others. Quantity Unit Symbol In other units Electric current ampere A SI base unit; 1 A = 1 C/s Electric charge coulomb C s-A Potential difference volt V W/A, and so (N-m)/(A-s) Resistance ohm &Omega; V/A, and so W/A 2 Say \"amp\" if you like; write A. The unit name is the ampere, and the symbol is a capital A because the unit is named after a person. So is the volt ( V ) and the watt ( W ). Lower case changes the meaning of a prefix, not just the look of it: mA is a milliampere and MA is a megaampere, a billion times larger. See Measurement Language. Why voltage and resistance are called derived units Of the four units above, only the ampere is a base unit - one of the seven that the rest of SI is built from. The others are derived: their definitions are ratios of units already defined. NIST expresses the volt as W/A and the ohm as V/A, and traces both back to base units as m 2 -kg-s &minus;3 -A &minus;1 and m 2 -kg-s &minus;3 -A &minus;2. That is why unit cancellation works on electrical problems exactly as it does on mechanical ones - the electrical units are made of metres, kilograms, seconds, and amperes underneath. The technique on Dimensional Analysis does not need a separate electrical version."},
{"title":"Two equations, and everything else follows","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#two-equations-and-everything-else-follows","text":"Two equations, and everything else follows For an ohmic resistor (constant resistance under the stated conditions), the set is generated by Ohm's law and the power relationship: V = IR - P = VI Rearranging and substituting gives three expressions for each of the four quantities. There is nothing to memorise beyond the two above; the table is a lookup, not a list of new facts. Resistive-load magnitudes: steady DC, or RMS voltage and current for AC. To find From V and I or R Using power Using power Voltage, V V = IR V = P/I V = &radic;(PR) Current, I I = V/R I = P/V I = &radic;(P/R) Resistance, R R = V/I R = V 2 /P R = P/I 2 Power, P P = VI P = I 2 R P = V 2 /R P = VI gives real power in watts on direct current or a purely resistive load. For single-phase AC, RMS volts times RMS amps is apparent power in volt-amperes, and the real power is that multiplied by the power factor - see Power and Efficiency."},
{"title":"Worked example: what a lamp draws","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#worked-example-what-a-lamp-draws","text":"Worked example: what a lamp draws How much current does a 100 W resistive lamp draw on a 120 V supply (RMS for AC)? Pick the form with the two quantities you have: I = P/V. I = 100 W &divide; 120 V = 0.833 A. Check the units rather than trusting the arithmetic. A volt is a watt per ampere, so watts divided by volts leaves amperes - which is what was asked for."},
{"title":"Worked example: voltage drop along a cord","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#worked-example-voltage-drop-along-a-cord","text":"Worked example: voltage drop along a cord A 100 ft extension cord has a total out-and-back conductor resistance of 0.1588 &Omega;. What is the resistive voltage drop across that path when 10 A flows through it? V = IR = 10 A &times; 0.1588 &Omega; = 1.588 V. About 1.6 V. That is 1.6 V that never reaches the tool: for DC or an AC resistive load, the supply voltage equals the cord drop plus the load voltage. The dropped voltage does not disappear The power lost in the cord is P = I 2 R = (10 A) 2 &times; 0.1588 &Omega; = 15.9 W. That power becomes heat in the conductor, spread along its whole length. Notice the squared current. Doubling the load to 20 A does not double the loss, it quadruples it, to about 64 W. This is why the drop that is harmless on a light load becomes a real problem on a heavy one, and why the length and gauge of a cord matter more the more current the tool draws. The condition and routing of the cord itself are covered on Electrical Safety."},
{"title":"The same method keeps working","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#the-same-method-keeps-working","text":"The same method keeps working These are not the last units you will meet. Each new quantity arrives the same way: it is defined as a combination of quantities you already have, and its unit is that same combination. A few more, and where each one comes from. Quantity Defined as SI unit Pressure or stress Force per unit area pascal, Pa = N/m 2 Volumetric flow rate Volume per unit time m 3 /s Specific weight Weight per unit volume N/m 3 Capacitance Charge per unit voltage farad, F = C/V Read the middle column and the right-hand column together and the pattern is the whole point: the unit is the definition, written in symbols. Once that clicks, a unit you have never seen tells you what the quantity is."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","summary":"The three quantities every schematic and nameplate uses, the units they are measured in, and the two equations that connect them.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/current-voltage-and-resistance.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked NIST SP 811, Chapter 4, Table 3, gives: volt V, expressed as W/A, base units m 2 -kg-s &minus;3 -A &minus;1; ohm &Omega;, expressed as V/A, base units m 2 -kg-s &minus;3 -A &minus;2; coulomb C, base units s-A; watt W, expressed as J/s; pascal Pa, expressed as N/m 2; and farad F, expressed as C/V. The ampere is listed as one of the seven SI base units, with symbol A, by both BIPM and NIST. The rule that a unit symbol takes a capital first letter when the unit is named after a person is NIST SP 811, Chapter 6."},
{"title":"More load torque means less speed on this curve","page":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","summary":"Use an ideal linear torque-speed curve to find a load point and see why peak output power occurs halfway to no-load speed.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dc-motor-curves.html#more-load-torque-means-less-speed-on-this-curve","text":"More load torque means less speed on this curve This model approximates a permanent-magnet DC motor at a fixed supply voltage with a straight line between two endpoints. Stall torque is the torque at zero speed. No-load speed is the speed at zero external shaft load. It is not a universal curve for induction motors, steppers, or regulated drives. Let T s be stall torque, n 0 no-load speed, and T the constant resisting load torque. The steady operating point is where motor torque equals load torque: n = n 0 (1 − T/T s ). This is an assumed linear approximation, not a measured motor rating. It omits acceleration, current limiting, temperature changes, and supply voltage drop. At stall it predicts zero shaft power, not zero electrical input. A stall rating is not a continuous operating rating. Manufacturer basis for the straight line maxon's Motor Data and Operating Ranges, p. 10, states: Enhancing the load torque leads to a linear reduction of the speed. It identifies no-load speed and stall torque as the endpoints and specifies fixed applied voltage."},
{"title":"Explore the load point and the peak-power point","page":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","summary":"Use an ideal linear torque-speed curve to find a load point and see why peak output power occurs halfway to no-load speed.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dc-motor-curves.html#explore-the-load-point-and-the-peak-power-point","text":"Explore the load point and the peak-power point Predict first: a model motor has 0.8 N·m stall torque and 3,000 rpm no-load speed. A constant load asks for 0.2 N·m. Does the motor run above or below half speed? Normalized torque falls from one at stall to zero at no-load speed. Normalized output power peaks at half no-load speed. A load of one-quarter stall torque operates at three-quarters no-load speed. Static example: n = 3,000(1 − 0.2/0.8) = 2,250 rpm. Angular speed is 2,250 × 2π/60 = 235.6 rad/s, so output power is 47.12 W. Peak power is 62.83 W at 1,500 rpm and 0.4 N·m. Ideal motor and constant load Stall torque (N·m) No-load speed (rpm) Load torque (N·m) The solid line is torque / stall torque; the dashed curve is power / peak power. The circle marks load torque; the diamond marks output power. The square marks peak power."},
{"title":"Peak power is halfway, not at either endpoint","page":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","summary":"Use an ideal linear torque-speed curve to find a load point and see why peak output power occurs halfway to no-load speed.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dc-motor-curves.html#peak-power-is-halfway-not-at-either-endpoint","text":"Peak power is halfway, not at either endpoint Use P = Tω, with torque in N·m and angular speed in rad/s. Convert rpm using ω = 2πn/60. The definitions and units are on Power and Efficiency and Angular Motion. At stall there is torque but no speed. At no load there is speed but no external load torque. Both endpoints therefore have zero shaft output power. Between them, torque times speed reaches a maximum. Derive the halfway result from the straight line Put x = n/n 0. Then T = T s (1 − x), so P = T s ω 0 x(1 − x). Since x(1 − x) = 1/4 − (x − 1/2) 2, the largest value is 1/4 at x = 1/2. Thus peak power = T s ω 0 /4, at half no-load speed and half stall torque. The two endpoint maxima cannot occur at the same time. Manufacturer check on peak power Portescap's Brush DC Motor Basics, p. 1, point 4, places maximum mechanical power at half the stall torque. On the linear curve, this also means half no-load speed, as derived above. Practice operating speed Practice output power"},
{"title":"Peak efficiency needs electrical input as well as shaft output","page":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","summary":"Use an ideal linear torque-speed curve to find a load point and see why peak output power occurs halfway to no-load speed.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dc-motor-curves.html#peak-efficiency-needs-electrical-input-as-well-as-shaft-output","text":"Peak efficiency needs electrical input as well as shaft output Efficiency is output power divided by input power. Peak output power and peak efficiency answer different questions. The torque-speed line alone does not give electrical input or losses, so this explorer deliberately does not calculate efficiency. For the brushed coreless DC motors described by Portescap, peak efficiency occurs at a lighter load and higher speed than peak output power. Its exact position needs loss or current data. No external load means no useful shaft output, even though a real motor draws current to overcome its internal losses. Consult the actual motor's efficiency and continuous-operation data before selecting a load point. Manufacturer basis for the efficiency comparison Portescap's Brush DC Motor Basics, p. 1, point 3, states: The maximum efficiency is obtained at high speed. Its accompanying torque plot places the efficiency peak below the torque of peak power; the descending speed line therefore puts it at higher speed."},
{"title":"Match the motor, reduction, and the same output load","page":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","summary":"Use an ideal linear torque-speed curve to find a load point and see why peak output power occurs halfway to no-load speed.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dc-motor-curves.html#match-the-motor-reduction-and-the-same-output-load","text":"Match the motor, reduction, and the same output load A gearbox changes the torque demanded at the motor shaft for the same output load. That can move the motor to a different point on its torque–speed curve. Predict both operating points before revealing them. Static example: a motor has 0.8 N·m stall torque and 120 rpm no-load speed. The output load resists with 0.6 N·m. Compare direct drive with a 20-tooth driver, 40-tooth driven gear, and a stated 90% running mesh efficiency. The same motor and output load, two connections Connection Torque required at motor Motor speed Output speed Motor shaft power Modeled output power Direct 0.600 N·m 30.0 rpm 30.0 rpm 1.885 W 1.885 W 2:1 reduction, 90% 0.3333 N·m 70.0 rpm 35.0 rpm 2.443 W 2.199 W Result: the reduction runs the output faster under this load, even though it divides motor speed. It reflects less load torque to the motor, so the motor reaches a higher speed. The gearbox did not create power: motor shaft power changed before the 10% modeled mesh loss. This is a fixed-voltage steady-running comparison with a constant resisting torque. It does not establish starting ability, acceleration, thermal or continuous rating, back-driving, electrical input, or gear strength. At stall, speed and shaft output power are zero; if required motor torque exceeds stall torque, the model reports no forward steady operating point instead of assigning a fictitious speed. Source and the standstill boundary The running conversion follows maxon's gear conversion note: motor speed equals reduction multiplied by load speed, while motor torque equals load torque divided by reduction and gear efficiency. The note also requires checking actual gear torque and speed limits. A separate maxon standstill note explains that power efficiency is undefined when both input and output power are zero. This lesson therefore treats stall as a boundary; it does not calculate blocked-state efficiency or a permissible output torque. Real loads may demand different torque at different speeds. Their operating point is an intersection of a load curve and a motor curve. Practice reflected load Practice output power"},
{"title":"Digital uses two states; analog uses a range","page":"Digital and Analog Signals","summary":"Decide whether a sensor communicates with two logic states, a changing voltage, or the timing of pulses.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/digital-and-analog-signals.html#digital-uses-two-states-analog-uses-a-range","text":"Digital uses two states; analog uses a range A digital input interprets two states: LOW/HIGH, or 0/1. The wire still carries a physical voltage; the receiving circuit decides which logic state it represents. A voltage in the undefined region between valid LOW and HIGH levels is not a useful third state. An analog signal represents a quantity by a continuous range of values. A potentiometer wired between ground and 5 V can provide a changing voltage at its middle terminal. On a classic Uno R3, the usual analog measurement range is nominally 0–5 V with the default reference. That is not a universal sensor range. Digital waveform switches between LOW and HIGH; analog waveform changes smoothly through intermediate voltages. Both are plotted against time."},
{"title":"Classify the sensor's output circuit, not just its name","page":"Digital and Analog Signals","summary":"Decide whether a sensor communicates with two logic states, a changing voltage, or the timing of pulses.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/digital-and-analog-signals.html#classify-the-sensor-s-output-circuit-not-just-its-name","text":"Classify the sensor's output circuit, not just its name Common examples Device and connection Signal at the input Button or switch with a pull-up Digital open/closed state. Quadrature encoder with compatible logic outputs Two digital pulse trains; their order gives direction. HC-SR04 ultrasonic module, Echo pin A timed digital pulse. Its duration represents distance; its voltage height does not. Potentiometer connected as a voltage divider Analog voltage from its wiper (middle terminal). Photoresistor in a voltage divider Analog voltage as resistance changes with light. Phototransistor with a load resistor Analog response to light unless a threshold circuit converts it to digital. Pressure or proximity sensor Depends on the model: analog voltage, switching output, or digital data. Read its documentation. A bare resistive sensor does not generate a usable voltage by itself: its surrounding circuit matters. A module may offer both analog and thresholded digital outputs. Phototransistor circuits can measure light or switch at a threshold Vishay's Ambient Light Sensors — Circuit and Window Design shows both light-meter circuits and light-switch circuits. The phototransistor current changes with illumination; the surrounding circuit determines whether the output preserves a range or switches between logic states. Why an ultrasonic measurement can be digital The HC-SR04 manufacturer datasheet, hosted by SparkFun describes an Echo pulse whose width represents the range. Digital describes the two voltage states; the information can be carried by their timing. A single digitalRead tells you the current state, not the pulse duration."},
{"title":"The ADC reads voltage; PWM controls time","page":"Digital and Analog Signals","summary":"Decide whether a sensor communicates with two logic states, a changing voltage, or the timing of pulses.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/digital-and-analog-signals.html#the-adc-reads-voltage-pwm-controls-time","text":"The ADC reads voltage; PWM controls time Analog-to-Digital Conversion turns an analog input into a number the program can use. Pulse-Width Modulation switches a digital output on and off for a controlled fraction of each cycle. Neither operation changes the fact that the processor stores and works with digital data. Practice identifying sensor signals"},
{"title":"Convert by multiplying by one","page":"Dimensional Analysis","summary":"Convert quantities by multiplying by named equalities, cancelling units, and treating squared and cubed units as powers.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dimensional-analysis.html#convert-by-multiplying-by-one","text":"Convert by multiplying by one A conversion factor is a ratio of two exactly equal physical quantities written as a fraction. For example, 1 ft and 0.3048 m name exactly the same length. Their ratio therefore has the numerical value 1, so multiplying by it changes the unit expression without changing the physical quantity. Either orientation is valid: 1 ft / 0.3048 m and 0.3048 m / 1 ft both equal 1. Choose the orientation that cancels the unwanted unit. To convert 12 m to feet, put meters on the bottom: 12 m &times; 1 ft 0.3048 m = 39.370078740&hellip; ft &asymp; 39.3701 ft. For the reverse conversion, turn the same exact equality over so feet cancel: 39.370078740&hellip; ft &times; 0.3048 m 1 ft = 12 m. Do not memorize an arrow. Write the desired unit, then arrange the factor so the unwanted unit cancels. The units tell you whether the fraction is facing the correct way before you calculate. Exact, defined 1 in = 0.0254 m, so 1 ft = 0.3048 m and 1 yd = 0.9144 m exactly. Keep guard digits Use exact factors or sufficient digits while calculating; round the displayed result only at the end and only as the task needs. Match dimensions Length converts to length, area to area, volume to volume, and force to force. A calculator cannot make incompatible dimensions meaningful."},
{"title":"Powers apply to the whole conversion","page":"Dimensional Analysis","summary":"Convert quantities by multiplying by named equalities, cancelling units, and treating squared and cubed units as powers.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dimensional-analysis.html#powers-apply-to-the-whole-conversion","text":"Powers apply to the whole conversion Area and volume are not lengths with decorative superscripts. The conversion factor itself must be squared or cubed. Why a single linear factor is not enough. Quantity Exact relationship Useful result Length 1 ft = 0.3048 m 1 ft = 0.3048 m Area (1 ft) 2 = (0.3048 m) 2 1 ft 2 = 0.09290304 m 2 Volume (1 ft) 3 = (0.3048 m) 3 1 ft 3 = 0.028316846592 m 3 Time squared (1 min) 2 = (60 s) 2 1 min 2 = 3,600 s 2 For example, 4 m 2 &times; (1 ft / 0.3048 m) 2 = 43.0556 ft 2, which is sensibly reported as 43.1 ft 2. For liquid capacity, 1 m 3 = 1,000 L and 1 U.S. gal = 3.785411784 L. The converter labels this as a U.S. liquid gallon so it cannot be mistaken for an Imperial gallon. Dimensional analysis is also a design check If a mass density is in kg/m 3 and a CAD model volume is in m 3, the cubic meters cancel and the result is kilograms. That is why CAD mass-property settings, material cards, and drawing units must be read together rather than as separate menus."},
{"title":"Conversion bench","page":"Dimensional Analysis","summary":"Convert quantities by multiplying by named equalities, cancelling units, and treating squared and cubed units as powers.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dimensional-analysis.html#conversion-bench","text":"Conversion bench This tool uses exact internal factors where standards define them. It only converts within one quantity type; a length cannot become a speed without additional information. Convert a quantity Value From unit second (s) minute (min) hour (h) day (d) millimeter (mm) centimeter (cm) meter (m) kilometer (km) inch (in) foot (ft) yard (yd) mile (mi) square meter (m²) square foot (ft²) square inch (in²) cubic meter (m³) liter (L) cubic foot (ft³) cubic inch (in³) U.S. liquid gallon (U.S. gal) kilogram (kg) pound mass (lbm) slug meter per second (m/s) foot per second (ft/s) mile per hour (mph) kilometer per hour (km/h) meter per second squared (m/s²) foot per second squared (ft/s²) newton (N) pound force (lbf) To unit second (s) minute (min) hour (h) day (d) millimeter (mm) centimeter (cm) meter (m) kilometer (km) inch (in) foot (ft) yard (yd) mile (mi) square meter (m²) square foot (ft²) square inch (in²) cubic meter (m³) liter (L) cubic foot (ft³) cubic inch (in³) U.S. liquid gallon (U.S. gal) kilogram (kg) pound mass (lbm) slug meter per second (m/s) foot per second (ft/s) mile per hour (mph) kilometer per hour (km/h) meter per second squared (m/s²) foot per second squared (ft/s²) newton (N) pound force (lbf) Speed is a nonnegative magnitude and has no direction. A negative signed value represents a velocity component opposite the chosen positive direction. Result: 39.3701 ft 12 m &times; (1 ft / 0.3048 m) = 39.370078740&hellip; ft &asymp; 39.3701 ft. JavaScript is off. The shown 12 m to 39.3701 ft example and the worked conversions on this page remain available."},
{"title":"CNC production calculation","page":"Dimensional Analysis","summary":"Convert quantities by multiplying by named equalities, cancelling units, and treating squared and cubed units as powers.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dimensional-analysis.html#cnc-production-calculation","text":"CNC production calculation A CNC program estimates 4 min 15 s per part. The operator uses 30 min of an 8 h shift for setup. How many complete parts fit in the shift? Convert the cycle: 4 min 15 s = 4.25 min. Find machine-running time: 8 h &times; (60 min / 1 h) &minus; 30 min = 450 min. Divide running time by cycle time: 450 min / 4.25 min per part = 105.882.... 105 complete parts. The fractional result is time into the 106th cycle, not a finished part. This assumes one machine, no unplanned stops, and that the stated cycle time already includes every repeatable operation. Two more conversion cases from the unit Machine run time: an automated machine has operated for 15 years and 150 days. With the stated classroom assumption of 365 days per year, (15 &times; 365 + 150) d &times; (24 h / 1 d) = 135,000 h. Real maintenance records should use actual calendar dates and include leap days. Supply-chain fuel: a truck rated 15 mpg has 15 mi/U.S. gal &times; (1.609344 km / 1 mi) &times; (1 U.S. gal / 3.785411784 L) = 6.37716 km/L. A 150 km one-way route is a 300 km round trip, requiring 300 km / 6.37716 km/L = 47.0429 L, or 47.0 L, assuming the quoted fuel economy holds for the trip."},
{"title":"Practice and sources","page":"Dimensional Analysis","summary":"Convert quantities by multiplying by named equalities, cancelling units, and treating squared and cubed units as powers.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/dimensional-analysis.html#practice-and-sources","text":"Practice and sources Use the practice bank to apply cubic conversion factors to a model volume. Add to practice Sources and exact factors The NIST SI Units reference documents SI base and derived units. The NIST conversion conventions define the inch as exactly 0.0254 m; this page derives square and cubic factors from that exact value. U.S. liquid gallon values are explicitly labeled whenever they are used."},
{"title":"The stator stays still; the rotor turns","page":"Electric Motor Principles","summary":"Find the stator and rotor, then follow the changing magnetic field that keeps a motor turning.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-principles.html#the-stator-stays-still-the-rotor-turns","text":"The stator stays still; the rotor turns An electric motor turns electrical input into mechanical motion. In a rotary motor, the stator is the stationary part and the rotor is the rotating part. An air gap separates them. Bearings support the rotating assembly; the shaft carries motion to the load. Two simplified motor cross-sections: the rotor field follows the stator field, then the stator field advances again. The outer stator is stationary while its magnetic field changes direction. The stator is often outside the rotor, but it does not have to be. The names describe motion, not inside versus outside. See Electric Motor Types for both arrangements."},
{"title":"Keep the magnetic target moving: carrot on a stick","page":"Electric Motor Principles","summary":"Find the stator and rotor, then follow the changing magnetic field that keeps a motor turning.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-principles.html#keep-the-magnetic-target-moving-carrot-on-a-stick","text":"Keep the magnetic target moving: carrot on a stick Picture the rotor turning to align its magnetic field with the stator's field. If the fields simply settled into alignment, that turning effect would disappear. To keep turning, the motor switches a field or keeps the stator field rotating. Think carrot on a stick: keep moving the target ahead. Commutation means switching current between windings at the right time. Brushes and a commutator can do it mechanically, or a drive can do it electronically. A winding is a coil of wire that creates a magnetic field when current flows through it. Where the alignment picture needs care This is a first picture, not a description of every motor's internal timing. A synchronous motor can rotate at the same speed as the stator field while keeping an angular offset that produces torque. An induction rotor runs slower than the field during normal motoring; that relative motion induces rotor current. A stepper can deliberately stop and hold a commanded position."},
{"title":"Separate turning effort, speed, and power","page":"Electric Motor Principles","summary":"Find the stator and rotor, then follow the changing magnetic field that keeps a motor turning.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-principles.html#separate-turning-effort-speed-and-power","text":"Separate turning effort, speed, and power Torque is turning effort. Angular speed tells how fast the shaft turns. Mechanical power combines the two. A stopped shaft can have torque and still deliver zero mechanical power. Start with Current, Voltage, and Resistance for the electrical quantities, then use DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power to see what happens when a load asks a motor for more torque."},
{"title":"Brushed DC: simple supply, wearing contacts","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#brushed-dc-simple-supply-wearing-contacts","text":"Brushed DC: simple supply, wearing contacts A small permanent-magnet brushed DC motor has stationary magnets and rotating electromagnets. Carbon brushes press against a segmented copper commutator. As the segments rotate past the brushes, the connections change and reverse current in rotor windings. The torque keeps acting in the intended direction. Simplified two-segment commutator: fixed positive and negative brushes touch rotating copper segments A and B. After half a turn the segments exchange brushes, reversing current in the attached coil. Strength: inexpensive and simple to run from DC, useful in small toys, pumps, and actuators. Weak points: brushes wear through contact and electrical arcing; the commutator and spring contact system can also deteriorate. Switching current at the contacts can cause sparks and electrical noise. Permanent-magnet construction is common in small motors; some brushed motors use field windings instead. A microcontroller can command a power driver with pulse-width modulation (PWM), switching the supply on and off rapidly to control the motor. The driver handles motor current; a logic output is not a motor power supply."},
{"title":"AC induction: the rotor follows a rotating field with slip","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#ac-induction-the-rotor-follows-a-rotating-field-with-slip","text":"AC induction: the rotor follows a rotating field with slip Stator windings arranged around the motor produce a rotating magnetic field. In a three-phase motor, the currents reach their peaks at different times, moving the combined field around the stator. That moving field induces current in conducting rotor bars. The rotor current and stator field interact to produce torque. The rotor runs slightly slower than the field during normal motoring. The difference is called slip. If the rotor caught the field exactly, there would be no relative motion to induce the rotor current needed for torque. For example, a field at 1,800 rpm and a rotor at 1,740 rpm differ by 60 rpm, or 3.33% of field speed. Strength: a squirrel-cage induction rotor needs neither brushes nor permanent magnets, making it useful for pumps, fans, and conveyors. Weak points: speed changes with load and slip; a variable-frequency drive is normally used when adjustable speed is needed. Why alternating current alone is not the full explanation A single winding supplied by single-phase AC makes a field that pulses back and forth; that alone does not establish a starting direction. Single-phase induction motors use an additional starting arrangement. The rotating field, rotor current, and relative speed explain rotation more clearly than saying the rotor field simply opposes the stator field."},
{"title":"Brushless permanent-magnet motors: switch the stator current","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#brushless-permanent-magnet-motors-switch-the-stator-current","text":"Brushless permanent-magnet motors: switch the stator current Here the rotor carries permanent magnets and the stator carries windings. A drive switches the stator currents in sequence so the magnetic field turns. There are no commutator brushes to wear. BLDC means brushless DC; PMSM means permanent-magnet synchronous motor. A DC supply can feed an inverter, an electronic circuit that creates changing phase currents. Most BLDC/PMSM drive systems use this electronic switching. The labels often distinguish waveform and control choices; they do not mean one rotor needs AC magnets and the other needs DC magnets. A suitably designed line-fed synchronous motor instead follows a rotating field set by the AC supply frequency and pole count. It needs an appropriate starting arrangement. Do not assume an arbitrary BLDC motor can connect directly to an AC line. Strengths: no brush contact wear and controllable speed; common uses include cooling fans, drones, and controlled machine drives. Weak points: drive electronics and correctly timed switching are needed; bearings still wear and winding temperature still limits output."},
{"title":"Inner-rotor and outer-rotor describe what rotates","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#inner-rotor-and-outer-rotor-describe-what-rotates","text":"Inner-rotor and outer-rotor describe what rotates Side-by-side cross-sections: an inner-rotor motor has rotating magnets inside fixed windings; an outer-rotor motor has a rotating magnet ring outside fixed windings. An inner-rotor motor, also called an inrunner, rotates inside the stator. An outer-rotor motor, or outrunner, rotates around the stator. These are the arrangements sometimes called “inboard” and “outboard.” Use inner-/outer-rotor when describing the construction. An inner rotor can have low rotational inertia, useful for rapid speed changes. An outer rotor puts magnetic force at a larger radius, useful for producing torque in fan and propeller drives, but its rotating shell contributes inertia. Dimensions and design still decide the actual performance."},
{"title":"Stepper: count commanded steps, but allow for missed motion","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#stepper-count-commanded-steps-but-allow-for-missed-motion","text":"Stepper: count commanded steps, but allow for missed motion A stepper drive energizes stator phases in a sequence of magnetic positions. As a first approximation, picture an inner-rotor brushless motor with many magnetic poles or teeth. That helps explain the small steps; it is a simplification, since stepper constructions differ. Open loop means the controller sends a move without measuring whether it happened. A stepper can position and hold without feedback while energized and within its torque capability. It is useful for printer axes and small indexing mechanisms. Holding position still uses current and produces heat. The weak point is missed steps. Excessive load or acceleration can prevent commanded motion. Without feedback, the controller keeps counting commands and does not know the shaft fell behind. Step count is not a guarantee of exact position, and holding torque is not a guarantee against any load. Practice motor selection"},
{"title":"Servo: measure motion and correct the error","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#servo-measure-motion-and-correct-the-error","text":"Servo: measure motion and correct the error A servo is a motor used with feedback and closed-loop control, not one particular magnetic construction. A sensor such as an encoder measures motion. The controller compares measured position or speed with the command and adjusts the drive to reduce the error. Strength: responds to a load disturbance and detects position error, useful for robot joints and machine axes. Weak points: adds sensor, controller, tuning, and cost. Feedback does not create extra torque; a servo can still reach its limits. A brushed, brushless, or other suitable motor can be part of a servo system."},
{"title":"Linear motors move directly along a track","page":"Electric Motor Types","summary":"Compare how motors switch their fields, what wears, and how they control motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electric-motor-types.html#linear-motors-move-directly-along-a-track","text":"Linear motors move directly along a track Think of the magnetic layout of a rotary motor opened out into a straight line. A traveling field pushes or pulls a moving member along a track, producing linear force instead of shaft torque. One permanent-magnet arrangement uses a magnet track and a moving coil assembly. Linear induction designs also exist; a magnetized rail is not universal. Strength: direct linear motion without a rotary-to-linear screw or belt, useful for positioning stages and transport systems. Weak points: the magnetic track, drive, cooling, and position measurement can add cost. Separate guides support and constrain the moving assembly; a motor does not replace the guide system. Compare the basic field picture in Electric Motor Principles and the load calculation in DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power."},
{"title":"Choose the drawing for the job","page":"Electrical Diagram Types","summary":"Choose a drawing by the question it answers: appearance, connections, function, control logic, or physical location.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-diagram-types.html#choose-the-drawing-for-the-job","text":"Choose the drawing for the job One electrical system can need several drawings. Start with the question: What does the part look like? Where does this wire go? How does the circuit work? Where does the equipment fit? Choose the drawing for the question you need to answer. Type Shows Usually leaves out Who uses it, and when Pictorial Recognizable appearances of parts. Exact electrical behavior and hidden connections may be omitted. A beginner or assembler identifying parts before tracing connections. Block Functions and the direction of signal or power flow. Individual terminals, component values, and return wires. A designer explaining a system before working out its circuits. Wiring Connections between terminals; wire identifiers and routing where specified. The reason the circuit behaves as it does; scale may be omitted. An assembler or service technician connecting or tracing wires. Schematic Electrical function and connections using component symbols. Actual component positions, wire lengths, and enclosure geometry. A circuit designer or technician analyzing how a circuit works. IC / PCB layout Physical geometry: a PCB has footprints, pads and copper traces; an IC layout describes structures and interconnect on a chip. A convenient explanation of circuit operation. A board or chip designer checking placement and routing before manufacture. Ladder Relay or PLC control logic arranged in rungs between rails. Exact equipment placement and wire routing. A controls technician tracing the conditions for an output. Logic Gates and the relationships between logical inputs and outputs. Power wiring, package pin locations, and physical placement unless added. A digital designer following Boolean decisions through a circuit. Electrical floor plan Locations of outlets, switches and fixtures on a building plan, interpreted with its legend. Internal circuit operation; complete routing may be on other drawings. An installer or building designer locating devices within rooms. Riser Distribution between building levels and the equipment serving them. Detailed room layouts and exact conduit paths unless specified. A building electrical designer or installer coordinating distribution across floors. Read the drawing title, legend, revision, and notes before treating a line or mark as an instruction. The same habit applies to mechanical drawings; see Choosing Drawing Views and The Alphabet of Lines."},
{"title":"Compare one circuit in pictorial, wiring, and schematic views","page":"Electrical Diagram Types","summary":"Choose a drawing by the question it answers: appearance, connections, function, control logic, or physical location.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-diagram-types.html#compare-one-circuit-in-pictorial-wiring-and-schematic-views","text":"Compare one circuit in pictorial, wiring, and schematic views This teaching example connects a battery, switch, resistor, and LED in one loop. The resistor is in series with the LED. The drawings explain identity and connections; they do not specify a buildable parts list, resistor value, battery rating, or package pinout. Choose a view. Choose a component to keep it marked when you change views. Pictorial view B1 battery S1 switch R1 resistor D1 LED Recognize the parts. These simplified silhouettes are not package dimensions or a lead-identification guide. Wiring view B1 battery − + S1 switch 1 2 R1 resistor 2 1 D1 LED K A Follow terminals: B1+ → S1.1; S1.2 → R1.1; R1.2 → D1.A; D1.K → B1−. A means anode; K means cathode. Terminal names here belong to this example. Schematic view + B1 battery S1 open R1 resistor D1 LED Common symbols preserve the wiring connections. S1 is shown open. The LED cathode is the bar on the battery-negative side; the outward arrows identify light emission. Block view B1 battery Supplies energy S1 switch Controls the path R1 resistor Limits current D1 LED Produces light Arrows show functional order, not individual conductors. The return conductor is omitted. The wiring view retains every terminal connection. The block view deliberately removes that detail to explain each part's job. The schematic retains those connections while replacing part pictures with common symbols. Compare the three drawings together, or select one view to trace a part. On narrow screens the comparison stacks vertically. For the electrical quantities and circuit behavior, continue to Current, Voltage, and Resistance, Series and Parallel Circuits, and AC and DC. For programmable control, see Microcontroller Basics. For work on real equipment, use Electrical Safety."},
{"title":"A layout, floor plan, and riser answer different location questions","page":"Electrical Diagram Types","summary":"Choose a drawing by the question it answers: appearance, connections, function, control logic, or physical location.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-diagram-types.html#a-layout-floor-plan-and-riser-answer-different-location-questions","text":"A layout, floor plan, and riser answer different location questions A PCB footprint is the land pattern where a component attaches to a board. Traces are the board's conductive paths. An IC is a chip; its internal layout is not the same drawing as the board that holds it. A floor plan places devices in rooms. Its legend tells you what each mark means. A riser makes the relationship between building levels easier to follow. Neither should be treated as a complete installation instruction by itself."},
{"title":"Read control logic without assuming physical placement","page":"Electrical Diagram Types","summary":"Choose a drawing by the question it answers: appearance, connections, function, control logic, or physical location.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-diagram-types.html#read-control-logic-without-assuming-physical-placement","text":"Read control logic without assuming physical placement A ladder diagram arranges control conditions and outputs in horizontal rungs between two vertical rails. A relay drawing and a PLC program may use a similar ladder shape, but a PLC instruction tests a stored state; it is not a picture of a physical contact. L1 and L2 rails: S1 controls relay coil K1 on rung one; a normally open K1 contact controls lamp H1 on rung two. Common relay forms, shown with S1 open and K1 de-energized. Closing S1 energizes K1; its normally open contact closes and H1 lights. Opening S1 releases K1 and turns H1 off. The shared K1 label links the coil and its contact; it is not a wire between rungs. This is a functional example, not an installation drawing. A logic diagram emphasizes operations such as AND, OR, and NOT. It helps answer which combinations of inputs produce an output. The diagram may omit supply connections and package pin numbers, so it does not replace a wiring drawing."},
{"title":"Practice selecting a useful drawing","page":"Electrical Diagram Types","summary":"Choose a drawing by the question it answers: appearance, connections, function, control logic, or physical location.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-diagram-types.html#practice-selecting-a-useful-drawing","text":"Practice selecting a useful drawing Practice diagram selection Continue with Reading Electrical Connections to recognize common component symbols, distinguish a joined wire from a crossing, and count electrical nodes."},
{"title":"A generator converts motion into electrical energy","page":"Electrical Generation","summary":"How turbines and generators turn other forms of energy into electricity, how photovoltaics differ, and how to compare a generation mix.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-generation.html#a-generator-converts-motion-into-electrical-energy","text":"A generator converts motion into electrical energy A generator does not create energy. It takes mechanical work at a shaft and delivers electrical energy to a circuit, with some energy becoming heat along the way. A turbine is a rotating machine driven by a moving fluid. Steam, flowing water, wind, or hot combustion gases can turn its blades. The turbine turns the generator; the generator supplies electricity. An engine can also drive a generator without a turbine. Heat makes steam; steam turns a turbine; the turbine turns a generator; the generator supplies electricity. Water, wind and combustion gases can drive turbines too. How rotation produces electricity Relative motion between a magnetic field and conductors induces a voltage. With a closed circuit, current flows and the shaft must supply work against the electrical load. Motor action and generator action reverse the direction of energy conversion; see Electric Motor Principles. Actual machines and controls determine how they can operate in either direction. See Power and Efficiency for losses and AC and DC for the electrical output forms."},
{"title":"Different heat sources can feed the same steam cycle","page":"Electrical Generation","summary":"How turbines and generators turn other forms of energy into electricity, how photovoltaics differ, and how to compare a generation mix.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-generation.html#different-heat-sources-can-feed-the-same-steam-cycle","text":"Different heat sources can feed the same steam cycle Burning fossil fuels or biomass can heat water to make steam. Nuclear fission supplies heat without combustion. Geothermal systems use heat from the Earth, and concentrated solar systems use mirrors to collect sunlight as heat. The equipment differs, but a steam-based plant follows the same basic chain: heat, moving steam, shaft rotation, electricity. Not every turbine uses steam. A hydroelectric turbine takes energy from water flowing downhill. A wind turbine takes energy from moving air. A combustion turbine uses hot gases directly; a combined-cycle plant also recovers exhaust heat for a steam cycle."},
{"title":"Photovoltaic cells skip the turbine","page":"Electrical Generation","summary":"How turbines and generators turn other forms of energy into electricity, how photovoltaics differ, and how to compare a generation mix.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-generation.html#photovoltaic-cells-skip-the-turbine","text":"Photovoltaic cells skip the turbine A photovoltaic (PV) cell converts light directly into electrical energy through the photovoltaic effect in a semiconductor. A connected load receives DC electricity. An inverter can convert that DC to AC. Solar PV and concentrated solar thermal are different routes. PV produces electricity in the cell; solar thermal first collects heat. That distinction matters when considering thermal storage."},
{"title":"A generation mix is more than a list of percentages","page":"Electrical Generation","summary":"How turbines and generators turn other forms of energy into electricity, how photovoltaics differ, and how to compare a generation mix.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/electrical-generation.html#a-generation-mix-is-more-than-a-list-of-percentages","text":"A generation mix is more than a list of percentages A generation mix describes how much electricity comes from each source over a stated place and time. Installed capacity describes how much power equipment is rated to supply. Those are different measures: a large rated capacity does not mean a source supplied the same share of annual energy. Compare cost, emissions, variability, and dispatchability - the ability to adjust output when requested. Wind and sunlight vary with weather and time. Fuelled generation and reservoir hydro can often be scheduled, but fuel, water, maintenance and operating limits still matter. Storage shifts energy in time; it is not an extra primary energy source. No current percentage mix is supplied here. For dated U.S. generation data, consult the U.S. EIA's Electricity explained: electricity in the United States, and record the reporting year and whether the figures describe generation or capacity."},
{"title":"Pumped hydro stores energy by lifting water","page":"Energy Storage Methods","summary":"Pumped water, stored heat and rechargeable batteries hold energy for later use in different ways.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/energy-storage-methods.html#pumped-hydro-stores-energy-by-lifting-water","text":"Pumped hydro stores energy by lifting water Use electricity to pump water uphill, then let it flow downhill through a turbine and generator when electricity is needed. The stored energy is gravitational potential energy. Charging pumps water from a lower reservoir to an upper reservoir using electricity. Discharging sends it downhill through a turbine and generator to return electricity. The cycle returns less electrical energy than it consumed because pumping, flow and generation all have losses. It needs suitable elevations, reservoirs and water management; it cannot be placed wherever a battery can. For the energy and efficiency relationships, see Work and Energy and Power and Efficiency."},
{"title":"Thermal storage saves heat for later","page":"Energy Storage Methods","summary":"Pumped water, stored heat and rechargeable batteries hold energy for later use in different ways.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/energy-storage-methods.html#thermal-storage-saves-heat-for-later","text":"Thermal storage saves heat for later Thermal storage holds energy in a hot material, such as a fluid or solid. A concentrated solar plant can collect heat while sunlight is available, store it, and later use it to produce steam and electricity. Stored heat can also serve heating loads directly. Insulation slows heat loss; it does not eliminate it. Returning heat to electricity adds conversion losses. Choose the storage route for the energy form the load needs."},
{"title":"Rechargeable batteries store energy chemically","page":"Energy Storage Methods","summary":"Pumped water, stored heat and rechargeable batteries hold energy for later use in different ways.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/energy-storage-methods.html#rechargeable-batteries-store-energy-chemically","text":"Rechargeable batteries store energy chemically A battery supplies DC through electrochemical reactions. A primary battery is intended for a single discharge life and replacement. A secondary battery is designed to be recharged with a suitable charger, restoring chemical energy for another discharge. Primary cells are useful portable energy supplies, but they are not reusable storage for electricity from a generator. Do not try to recharge a primary cell. Rechargeable does not mean unlimited life: cycling and aging reduce usable capacity. See Battery Chemistries for the families and Battery Capacity and Packs for the arithmetic."},
{"title":"Choose storage for the load and the site","page":"Energy Storage Methods","summary":"Pumped water, stored heat and rechargeable batteries hold energy for later use in different ways.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/energy-storage-methods.html#choose-storage-for-the-load-and-the-site","text":"Choose storage for the load and the site A small portable instrument needs a compact energy supply, so a battery is a practical fit. A site with suitable upper and lower reservoirs can consider pumped hydro for large stationary storage. A solar thermal plant needing heat after sunset can store heat without first converting it to electricity. Compare the required power, the energy needed over time, the storage duration, available space and terrain, losses, and service life. These examples identify a good fit; they do not establish that one technology is always cheapest. Practise choosing storage"},
{"title":"Spur teeth engage straight across; helical teeth engage progressively","page":"Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry","summary":"Compare spur and helical gears, identify tooth dimensions, and match pitch and pressure angle before choosing a gear pair.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-drives.html#spur-teeth-engage-straight-across-helical-teeth-engage-progressively","text":"Spur teeth engage straight across; helical teeth engage progressively Gears transmit rotation by direct tooth contact. They make a compact positive drive, but accurate mounting, tooth compatibility and lubrication matter. This page compares external spur and helical gears on parallel shafts. Spur gear Teeth run parallel to the shaft. It is comparatively simple to make and produces no axial thrust from the ideal tooth mesh. Engagement can be noisy at higher speed. Helical gear Teeth run at a helix angle around the gear. Contact enters progressively across the face and overlapping teeth share load. This generally gives smoother, quieter operation, with added manufacturing cost. The inclined helical tooth force also pushes along the shaft. That axial thrust needs a suitable bearing arrangement. A spur gear still loads its bearings radially; “no axial thrust” does not mean “no bearing force.” The driver supplies power. The pinion is the smaller gear in a pair; it can be the driver or the driven member. An idler passes motion between gears and still needs a shaft or other bearing support. Follow Gear Ratios for direction, tooth-count ratios and compound trains."},
{"title":"Measure teeth from the pitch circle","page":"Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry","summary":"Compare spur and helical gears, identify tooth dimensions, and match pitch and pressure angle before choosing a gear pair.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-drives.html#measure-teeth-from-the-pitch-circle","text":"Measure teeth from the pitch circle External gear tooth sector with tip, pitch and root circles; radial addendum above pitch, dedendum below pitch, and circular pitch between corresponding points of adjacent teeth. A second sketch shows pressure angle between the line of action and pitch-circle tangent. The pitch circle is a reference, not the outside edge of the gear. The enlarged sector flattens the arcs for clarity; it is not a cutting profile. Pitch diameter, d The diameter of the reference pitch circle. In a standard unshifted pair at its standard center distance, the pitch circles roll together without slipping. Module, m Metric tooth size: m = d/N, with d in millimeters and N teeth. Larger module means larger teeth. Diametral pitch, P d Inch-based tooth size: P d = N/d, with d in inches. More teeth per inch of pitch diameter means smaller teeth. Do not confuse it with circular pitch, the distance along the pitch circle between successive teeth. Pressure angle, α The angle between the line of action (the contact-force direction, neglecting friction) and the common tangent to the pitch circles at the pitch point. It describes tooth geometry, not the helix angle. Addendum / dedendum Radial tooth height from pitch circle to tip / radial depth from pitch circle to root. Neither is a diameter difference by itself. Backlash Clearance between mating tooth flanks. It allows clearance in the mesh but creates lost motion when the load reverses. Check the size definitions with one gear A spur gear with 30 teeth and 60 mm reference diameter has m = 60/30 = 2 mm. The diameter is 60/25.4 in, so its equivalent diametral pitch is 30/(60/25.4) = 12.7 teeth/in. In general, m = 25.4/P d. Two gears can have different diameters and tooth counts while sharing the same tooth size."},
{"title":"Matching pitch is necessary, but not sufficient","page":"Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry","summary":"Compare spur and helical gears, identify tooth dimensions, and match pitch and pressure angle before choosing a gear pair.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-drives.html#matching-pitch-is-necessary-but-not-sufficient","text":"Matching pitch is necessary, but not sufficient Ordinary spur gears intended to mesh must share module or diametral pitch and pressure angle. Matching outside diameter or tooth count does not establish compatibility. Check the tooth system, center distance, clearance and manufacturer's mating information too. For external helical gears on parallel shafts, match tooth size, pressure angle and helix angle. Use one right-hand and one left-hand gear (teeth slanting in opposite directions), and confirm the manufacturer's mating specification. Equal tooth counts are not required. Normal and transverse measurements use different planes The normal plane cuts across a tooth at right angles to its slant; the transverse plane cuts across the gear at right angles to its shaft. Module (tooth size) and pressure angle can be specified in either plane, so compare values measured in the same plane. For this helical pair, match normal module, normal pressure angle and helix-angle magnitude, with opposite helix hands. A larger tooth size can support more load when other conditions are comparable, but pitch alone cannot determine capacity, backlash or achievable reduction. Material, face width, heat treatment, alignment and speed remain part of the design. Primary references for the geometry and helical mesh KHK, Calculation of Gear Dimensions, sections 4.1 and 4.3, distinguishes reference geometry, profile shifts, and normal/transverse helical dimensions. KHK, ABCs of Gearing, B, develops tooth terminology and spur/helical calculations. KHK's helical-gear catalog gives mating-series and bearing-thrust cautions. Use the mating table for the exact product."},
{"title":"Gear damage tells you where the load went","page":"Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry","summary":"Compare spur and helical gears, identify tooth dimensions, and match pitch and pressure angle before choosing a gear pair.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-drives.html#gear-damage-tells-you-where-the-load-went","text":"Gear damage tells you where the load went Choose gears for compact packaging, a positive ratio and a supported shaft arrangement. Choose helical teeth when smoothness and noise justify their cost and thrust bearings. Check tooth bending strength, surface durability, speed, lubricant, seals and bearing loads before approving a pair. Common gear faults and conditions to investigate. Damage What to check Scoring or scuffing: streaked, torn tooth surfaces Lubricant supply and type, excess temperature, contamination and load. Broken tooth or root crack Overload, repeated bending fatigue, impact and material condition. Pitting / spalling: small pits or flakes leaving the surface Repeated contact stress, lubrication and local load concentration. Wear concentrated at one edge Shaft/bearing alignment, deflection and tooth contact pattern. A symptom is a clue, not a unique diagnosis. Correct the cause before replacing parts. Most loaded metal gear sets need a controlled lubricant supply; some specially selected materials can run dry. Follow the product data and Equipment Operating Procedure for access. Compare family trade-offs in Belt, Chain and Gear Drives, and follow Power and Efficiency for power losses. A positive drive is not automatically lossless."},
{"title":"Experiment: trade speed for turning effort","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#experiment-trade-speed-for-turning-effort","text":"Experiment: trade speed for turning effort Predict first: a 20-tooth gear turns at 120 rpm clockwise and drives a 40-tooth gear. Will the larger gear turn faster or slower? What changes if you add an idler between them? One mesh: 2:1 reduction A A · 20 teeth ↻ 120 rpm DRIVER / INPUT B B · 40 teeth ↺ 60 rpm DRIVEN / OUTPUT B has 2× as many teeth as A. Each passing tooth advances B by one tooth space; B turns 0.5× as fast, in the opposite direction. At 120 rpm clockwise and 2 N·m input, output B is 60 rpm CCW and 4 N·m ideally. The dashed circles are pitch circles, where the speeds match. Add an idler: still 2:1 A A · 20 teeth ↻ 120 rpm DRIVER / INPUT I I · 30 teeth ↺ 80 rpm IDLER B B · 40 teeth ↻ 60 rpm DRIVEN / OUTPUT The idler changes direction twice: A → I → B. Its 30 teeth cancel from (30 ÷ 20) × (40 ÷ 30). At 120 rpm clockwise and 2 N·m input, output B is 60 rpm CW and 4 N·m ideally. The dashed circles are pitch circles, where the speeds match. Two stages: 2 × 2 = 4:1 B + C: 60 rpm CCW One rigid shaft · same speed MESH 1 MESH 2 A A · 20 teeth B B · 40 teeth C C · 20 teeth D D · 40 teeth B and C turn together. Multiply the two reductions: (40 ÷ 20) × (40 ÷ 20) = 4. At 120 rpm clockwise and 2 N·m input, output D is 30 rpm CW and 8 N·m ideally. The two front-view meshes are separated on the page; the dashed connector represents their common shaft. All three examples use the same ideal input: 120 rpm and 2 N·m. Input power is 25.13 W, and ideal output power is also 25.13 W. More output torque comes with less speed. With each actual mesh at 96% efficiency, the pair delivers 3.84 N·m and 24.13 W; the idler train delivers 3.6864 N·m and 23.16 W; the compound train delivers 7.3728 N·m and 23.16 W. Their output speeds remain 60, 60 and 30 rpm. This comparison charges a loss at both idler meshes. The live controls need JavaScript. These drawings and worked comparisons use the same gear model."},
{"title":"Count the teeth to find the speed","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#count-the-teeth-to-find-the-speed","text":"Count the teeth to find the speed The driver supplies motion; the driven gear receives it. For one external gear mesh, each tooth passing on the driver advances the driven gear by one tooth space. A gear with twice as many teeth needs twice as many passing teeth to complete a turn. Output speed = input speed × driver teeth ÷ driven teeth. A 20-tooth driver at 120 rpm driving 40 teeth gives 120 × 20 ÷ 40 = 60 rpm. External meshing reverses the direction. This lesson uses reduction ratio i = input speed ÷ output speed, with speed magnitudes. A 2:1 reduction means two input turns for one output turn. Ratios above 1 reduce speed; ratios below 1 increase it. At zero speed, use tooth counts to find the ratio; do not divide 0 by 0. Notation map: this page's original equations use N for teeth and n for speed. The course uses n for teeth, ω for angular velocity and τ for torque. Thus page N = course n; page speed n is written ω in the additions below. GR and i name the same reduction ratio. In course notation, using magnitudes: GR = n out /n in = d out /d in = ω in /ω out = τ out /τ in for an ideal single pair. Here d is pitch diameter; the torque equality assumes no losses. For a compound train, multiply the stage ratios; the first and last tooth counts alone do not give the overall ratio. Why tooth counts work, and what the dashed circles mean The gears here share one tooth size, called module, and a 20° pressure angle. Their pitch diameters are proportional to tooth count. The dashed pitch circles roll together at equal tangential speed, so n A × N A = −n B × N B when clockwise is positive on both shafts. Here n is signed rotational speed and N is tooth count. Tooth flanks are involute curves. Actual tooth surfaces can slide during contact even though the pitch circles roll. The drawing samples those curves, simplifies the tooth roots and adds a small visual clearance. It is a motion model, not a manufacturing drawing."},
{"title":"An idler and a compound shaft do different jobs","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#an-idler-and-a-compound-shaft-do-different-jobs","text":"An idler and a compound shaft do different jobs One mesh reverses A → B turns the output opposite to the input, viewed from the same end of each shaft. An idler cancels A → I → B reverses twice. I affects its own speed and the spacing, but its tooth count cancels from the end-to-end ratio. Compound stages multiply A → B, then C → D, with B and C fixed together: i = (N B /N A ) × (N D /N C ). Same shaft means the same angular speed. A large B and a small C fixed to one shaft make the same number of turns in the same direction. Their rim speeds differ because their radii differ. They do not mesh with each other. In the compound example, 20 → 40 teeth halves the speed. A second 20 → 40 stage halves it again: 120 → 60 → 30 rpm. The two external meshes make the final output turn in the same direction as the input. An idler is not another reduction stage. Drop its tooth count when finding the end-to-end ratio, but count every external mesh for direction: odd means opposite; even means the same, viewed from the same end of the parallel shafts. Do not drop a gear that shares a shaft with a different gear in a compound train."},
{"title":"More torque costs speed; it does not create power","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#more-torque-costs-speed-it-does-not-create-power","text":"More torque costs speed; it does not create power Torque is turning effort. In this ideal, lossless train at steady load, output torque magnitude = input torque magnitude × reduction ratio. A 2:1 reduction changes 120 rpm and 2 N·m into 60 rpm and 4 N·m. The 4:1 compound example gives 30 rpm and 8 N·m. Rotational power is torque × angular speed: P = T × 2πn/60 when T is in N·m and n is in rpm. Both sides of either example carry 25.13 W ideally. Real friction and other losses leave less output power. Tooth strength, bearings, shafts, lubrication and the motor all limit usable torque. At zero speed, mechanical power is zero. A loaded, stationary gear train can still carry torque. The lab shows ideal static torque balance in that case; it does not model motor stall behavior or promise a safe load. Primary references and model boundaries KHK: Idler gear. The intermediate tooth count cancels from the overall speed ratio. KHK: Calculation of Gear Dimensions, section 4.1 and Table 4.1. Standard spur-gear pitch, base, tip and root dimensions and center spacing. MIT OpenCourseWare: Gear Trains. Gear bodies, shared shafts, and force and torque balance. The lab assumes rigid external spur gears, fixed shaft centers, matched tooth size and steady transmitted load. It starts lossless; its efficiency control applies the same specified loss to every actual mesh, including both meshes around an idler. Acceleration, elasticity, backlash dynamics, detailed friction, noise and tooth strength are omitted. Changing tooth counts moves the centers to maintain the correct mesh; it does not represent swapping a gear into a fixed gearbox housing. Connect the gears to Angular Motion, Rotation and Linear Speed and Torque."},
{"title":"Stage efficiencies multiply; losses accumulate","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#stage-efficiencies-multiply-losses-accumulate","text":"Stage efficiencies multiply; losses accumulate Each stage receives only the power that survived the stage before it. Use decimal efficiencies and multiply: η total = η 1 × η 2 × η 3. Never add or average them. See Power and Efficiency for the general meaning of efficiency. Power through a three-stage gear train Three successive driver and driven pairs, with the driven shaft of each stage connected to the next driver. Each stage passes 96 percent of its input power: 1000 watts becomes 960, then 921.6, then 884.736 watts. Total efficiency is 0.96 cubed. Stage 1: × 0.96 1000 → 960 W Stage 2: × 0.96 960 → 921.6 W Stage 3: × 0.96 921.6 → 884.736 W shared shaft ↓ shared shaft ↓ output η total = 0.96 × 0.96 × 0.96 = 0.884736 (88.47%) 11.53% of input power is lost Power-flow schematic, with gear pairs shown by pitch circles. The stages are separated for clarity; each connecting shaft turns its attached gears together. At 98% per stage, three stages give 0.98³ = 0.941192: about 5.88% total loss, not 6%. Fewer, higher-quality stages often beat many cheap stages for efficiency, provided they can meet the ratio and load requirements. Why adding losses is only an approximation For a 1000 W input and three 96% stages, the losses are 40 W, 38.4 W and 36.864 W. Each 4% applies to a smaller incoming power. Adding 4% + 4% + 4% incorrectly takes every loss from the original 1000 W."},
{"title":"Positive drives keep the speed ratio; losses reduce torque","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#positive-drives-keep-the-speed-ratio-losses-reduce-torque","text":"Positive drives keep the speed ratio; losses reduce torque A positive drive keeps input and output linked in a fixed ratio without slipping. Gears, chains and timing belts are positive drives. A slipping flat belt or V-belt is not. Follow Rotation and Linear Speed for the connection from rotation to belt travel. At a stated steady input speed and load, use magnitudes: ω out = ω in /i τ out = τ in × i × η total P out = η total × P in The teeth still set the speed exactly in this model. Efficiency reduces output torque from its ideal value. It does not reduce output rpm as well. Use matching torque units on both sides: lbf·ft or N·m. The course conversion is 1 rpm ≈ 0.1047 rad/s when angular speed in rad/s is needed. Follow both stages of one loaded train A 24-tooth driver at 1440 rpm and 4 lbf·ft drives 48 teeth. That shaft also carries a 15-tooth driver for a 45-tooth output. Both stages are 96% efficient, with no other losses. After stage 1: 720 rpm and 4 × 2 × 0.96 = 7.68 lbf·ft. The shared shaft carries that same 720 rpm into stage 2. Final output: 1440/(2 × 3) = 240 rpm and 7.68 × 3 × 0.96 = 22.1184 lbf·ft, versus 24 lbf·ft ideally. Overall efficiency is 0.9216."},
{"title":"Count stages for ratio, meshes for direction and stated losses","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#count-stages-for-ratio-meshes-for-direction-and-stated-losses","text":"Count stages for ratio, meshes for direction and stated losses For a simple train A → I → B, the idler cancels from (N I /N A ) × (N B /N I ) = N B /N A. The two external meshes still reverse direction twice. Follow the loss assumption in the problem. Real meshes lose some power, but a problem may explicitly ignore it. If every actual mesh is 96% efficient, A → I → B has η total = 0.96². If a whole stage including its idler is assigned 96%, use that stage value once. If η = 1.00 is stated, ignore losses. Do not infer the loss count from the reduction-stage count when an idler is present."},
{"title":"Choose teeth, then check both output limits","page":"Gear Ratios","summary":"Follow tooth counts through pairs, idlers and compound trains; multiply stage efficiencies and choose gears to meet speed and torque limits.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/gear-ratios.html#choose-teeth-then-check-both-output-limits","text":"Choose teeth, then check both output limits Record input speed and torque, the output speed ceiling and torque floor, available integer tooth counts, maximum ratio per stage, and the loss assumption. Use the speed ceiling to find i ≥ ω in /ω ceiling. With at most R:1 per stage, k reduction stages can provide at most R k:1. For each candidate stage count k, calculate η total and check the torque bound i ≥ τ floor /(τ in × η total ). Use the larger of the speed and torque bounds. Choose whole-tooth pairs within the range and per-stage limit. Connect successive pairs by shared shafts. Multiply their actual ratios; do not assume a rounded target is achievable. Calculate output speed and torque, keeping full precision for both limit checks. Check direction by counting external meshes. An added idler may require recalculating losses. To choose the faster of feasible designs at the same input speed, compare their actual overall ratios: the smaller one is faster. A feasible design is not automatically the fastest possible design. Worked design: a winch with a speed ceiling and torque floor A motor supplies 1680 rpm and 24 lbf·ft. The output must be at most 40 rpm and at least 950 lbf·ft. Available gears have 10–60 teeth, with unlimited copies; each reduction stage is at most 6:1 and 97% efficient. There are no idlers or other losses. Speed requires i ≥ 1680/40 = 42. Two stages give at most 6² = 36, so try three. Three stages give η total = 0.97³ = 0.912673. Torque requires i ≥ 950/(24 × 0.912673) ≈ 43.3708. Torque controls. Choose 12 → 48, 15 → 45, and 16 → 60 teeth: i = 4 × 3 × 3.75 = 45. Every tooth count and stage ratio meets its limit. ω out = 1680/45 ≈ 37.33 rpm ≤ 40 rpm. τ out = 24 × 45 × 0.912673 = 985.68684 lbf·ft ≥ 950 lbf·ft. Both pass. Three external meshes make the output opposite to the input. This is one feasible train, not a claim of an optimum or a complete winch specification. For load force at a drum, continue to Torque; for travel speed, use Rotation and Linear Speed. Practice multiplying efficiencies Practice designing to limits"},
{"title":"Scale changes area and volume by powers","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#scale-changes-area-and-volume-by-powers","text":"Scale changes area and volume by powers If every linear dimension of a model is multiplied by a scale factor s, area changes by s 2 and volume changes by s 3. A 20% larger model is not 20% larger in area or volume. Length L new = sL old Area A new = s 2 A old Volume V new = s 3 V old For s = 1.2, area factor = 1.2 2 = 1.44 and volume factor = 1.2 3 = 1.728. This is why a modest scale change can matter to material use, shipping mass, tank capacity, and actuator selection. Never scale an area with a linear factor or a volume with an area factor. The exponent belongs to the geometry, not to the number of decimal places."},
{"title":"Volume is capacity only after you name the unit","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#volume-is-capacity-only-after-you-name-the-unit","text":"Volume is capacity only after you name the unit CAD mass properties often report volume in a model's document units. Before treating that number as a tank capacity or a material quantity, state the volume unit and convert it to the form the drawing or specification requires. Volume relationships used in this unit. Relationship Meaning 1 m 3 = 1,000 L A cubic meter contains one thousand liters. 1 U.S. gal = 3.785411784 L This is a U.S. liquid gallon, not an Imperial gallon. 1 ft 3 = 7.48051948 U.S. gal Derived from the exact foot and U.S. gallon definitions. Static solved reservoir example A SolidWorks model gives a hydraulic reservoir internal volume of 2.5 ft 3. Its capacity is 2.5 ft 3 &times; 7.48051948 U.S. gal/ft 3 = 18.7013 U.S. gal, or 18.7 U.S. gal. This assumes the reported CAD volume is the usable internal volume and no design headspace has yet been subtracted."},
{"title":"Density connects volume to mass","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#density-connects-volume-to-mass","text":"Density connects volume to mass Density is mass per volume, and its symbol is &rho; (rho): &rho; = m/V. Turned around, when material density and CAD volume use compatible units, m = &rho;V produces mass. (kg/m 3 ) &times; (m 3 ) = kg Density units in the two systems. System Unit Conversion SI kg/m 3 1 kg/m 3 = 0.00194032 slug/ft 3 Foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second slug/ft 3 1 slug/ft 3 = 515.3788 kg/m 3 For example, a volume of 0.13824 m 3 made of material with a density of 2,700 kg/m 3 has mass m = 2,700 kg/m 3 &times; 0.13824 m 3 = 373.248 kg. Static solved steel example A steel I-beam has a volume of 0.2 m 3 and a density of 7,850 kg/m 3. Its mass is m = 7,850 kg/m 3 &times; 0.2 m 3 = 1,570 kg. That is the same mass the weight example below starts from - a CAD volume and a material card are usually where a weight calculation actually begins. Mass does not change merely because an object is moved from one planet to another. Weight can change because gravitational acceleration changes. CAD models, material databases, and shipping specifications commonly need mass; lifting and support calculations commonly need force or weight. Check the CAD assumptions before trusting mass properties Confirm the assigned material, density, document unit, bodies included, and whether the model represents solid material or an internal void. A precise calculation based on the wrong material card is still wrong."},
{"title":"Force and weight are separate quantities","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#force-and-weight-are-separate-quantities","text":"Force and weight are separate quantities Force is a push or pull. For constant mass, Newton's second law is F net = ma. Weight is one particular force: the force due to gravity, written W = mg. F net Net force, in newtons ( N ) or pound force ( lbf ). It is the combined force that produces acceleration. W Weight, a gravitational force. Near Earth's standard gravity, g 0 = 9.80665 m/s 2; local gravity can differ slightly. m Mass, in kilograms, pound mass, or slugs. It is not measured in newtons or pound force. In coherent SI, 1 N = 1 kg - m/s 2. In the coherent foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second system, 1 lbf = 1 slug - ft/s 2. Do not silently replace lbm with lbf. Static solved weight example For a 1,570 kg mass at standard gravity, W = mg = 1,570 kg &times; 9.80665 m/s 2 = 15,396.44 N, or 15.4 kN. This is a weight calculation at standard gravity, not a claim that the mass is 15.4 kN."},
{"title":"CAD scale, mass, and weight lab","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#cad-scale-mass-and-weight-lab","text":"CAD scale, mass, and weight lab This model begins with an area and volume in SI units, scales them, then uses density and gravitational acceleration to show the separate mass and weight results. Model inputs Linear scale factor Original area (m²) Original volume (m³) Density (kg/m³) Gravitational acceleration (m/s²) Area factor 1.44 Volume factor 1.728 Scaled area 1.8 m² Scaled volume 0.13824 m³ Mass 373.248 kg Weight force (N) 3660.3 N Weight force (lbf) 822.9 lbf JavaScript is off. The displayed case has area factor 1.44, volume factor 1.728, scaled volume 0.13824 m³, mass 373.248 kg, and weight 3660.3 N (822.9 lbf)."},
{"title":"Practice and sources","page":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","summary":"Scale CAD geometry correctly, connect volume and density to mass, and keep force and weight as separate quantities.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/geometry-mass-and-force.html#practice-and-sources","text":"Practice and sources Use the practice bank to apply squared-area conversion and complete-count reasoning. Add to practice Sources and engineering context NASA Glenn's weight-and-mass explanation distinguishes F = ma from W = mg. The NIST SI Units reference lists kilograms as an SI base unit and newtons as a derived unit. Those conventions are used here with g 0 = 9.80665 m/s 2 for the standard-gravity examples. The density conversion is from NIST SP 811, Appendix B, which lists slug per cubic foot to kilogram per cubic meter as 5.153 788 E+02; 0.00194032 is its reciprocal."},
{"title":"Pitch is thread spacing; lead is travel per revolution","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#pitch-is-thread-spacing-lead-is-travel-per-revolution","text":"Pitch is thread spacing; lead is travel per revolution Lead = pitch × number of starts. This is the distinction to get right before calculating either speed or force. A start is one continuous helical ridge. A single-start screw has one ridge, so its lead equals its pitch. A triple-start screw has three interleaved ridges: follow one ridge through a full turn and it advances three pitch distances. Unwrapped screw surfaces compare a single-start screw with pitch 2 mm and lead 2 mm to a triple-start screw with the same pitch 2 mm and lead 6 mm. A, B and C label the separate ridges. Imagine unwrapping the cylindrical surface. Top and bottom are the same angular position one full turn apart. Pitch is measured along the axis between adjacent ridges; lead follows the same ridge through a turn. At the same pitch, adding starts increases travel per turn. At the same torque it reduces theoretical force. The thread's cross-sectional form and its number of starts are separate choices; see Screw Threads. Check the definition, not just the label on a catalog Thomson: Screw Pitch and Lead defines lead as nut travel per screw revolution and states the pitch-times-starts relationship. A supplier may list lead directly. Do not multiply a value already labelled lead by the number of starts a second time."},
{"title":"Multiply rpm by lead to find linear speed","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#multiply-rpm-by-lead-to-find-linear-speed","text":"Multiply rpm by lead to find linear speed For an engaged screw and nut, with the nut restrained from rotating, v = nL. Here n is screw speed in revolutions per minute and L is lead in distance per revolution. The result is distance per minute. Divide by 60 for distance per second. A triple-start screw with 1.5 mm pitch has L = 1.5 × 3 = 4.5 mm/rev. At 480 rpm, v = 480 × 4.5 = 2,160 mm/min = 36 mm/s. Do not multiply that speed by efficiency. The teaching rule is: “RPM → velocity is 100% efficient unless the screw is malfunctioning; all efficiency losses are found in the torque → force conversion.” This describes a positive-drive motion relationship, not 100% energy efficiency. It is the same tooth-count principle used in Gear Ratios. Use the actual screw rpm. If the motor slows under load, the lower rpm gives a lower travel speed. Backlash at reversal and elastic deflection can affect positioning without being an efficiency factor on speed. These calculations describe steady engaged travel, after clearance is taken up. See Rotation and Linear Speed for the general rotation-to-travel connection."},
{"title":"Divide torque times 2π by lead, then apply efficiency","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#divide-torque-times-2-by-lead-then-apply-efficiency","text":"Divide torque times 2π by lead, then apply efficiency For steady motion driving a resisting axial load, F theoretical = 2πT / L and F actual = η × F theoretical. Here T is input torque and η (eta) is efficiency written as a decimal: 40% is 0.40. Make the length in torque match the length in lead. Torque Lead for force calculation Force N·m metres per revolution (mm ÷ 1,000) N lbf·in (often written lb-in) inches per revolution lbf To find the torque required for a target actual force, rearrange: T = FL / (2πη). For the same load and lead, lower efficiency requires more torque. Keep the efficiency boundary consistent: a whole screw assembly efficiency includes losses in the bearings or collar (a supporting thrust-contact surface) as well as the threads. Derive the 2π from equal power, or one revolution of work Ideally, power in equals power out: Tω = Fv. With n in rpm, ω = 2πn/60 rad/s and v = nL/60. Cancel n/60 at nonzero steady speed to get F = 2πT/L. Equivalently, one revolution puts in work 2πT and moves the load through L, doing work FL. With losses, Fv = ηTω. The motion ratio stays fixed, so the reduction appears in force. See Power and Efficiency. At zero speed both powers are zero; this derivation does not predict breakaway torque or static holding capacity. Lowering a load that drives the screw requires a different friction analysis. IIT Kharagpur: Power Screw Drives and Their Efficiency, section 6.1.2, relates output work, input work and friction. The calculator uses a given efficiency, not a friction coefficient or a thread-strength model."},
{"title":"Experiment: more starts change speed and force in opposite ways","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#experiment-more-starts-change-speed-and-force-in-opposite-ways","text":"Experiment: more starts change speed and force in opposite ways Predict first: keep pitch, rpm, torque and efficiency fixed, then change from one start to three. Lead and speed triple; theoretical force falls to one third. The same force ratio holds when efficiency is held fixed. Static comparison / print example: pitch 2 mm, 300 rpm, torque 1.2 N·m, given efficiency 40%. Same pitch and input, different starts. Starts Lead Speed Ideal force Actual force 1 2 mm/rev 10 mm/s 3,770 N 1,508 N 3 6 mm/rev 30 mm/s 1,257 N 503 N Changing efficiency to 20% halves each actual force; both speeds stay the same. Real efficiency can change with lead, lubrication and load. Here it is an independent input so the two effects can be studied separately. The controls require JavaScript. The comparison and formulas above provide the same calculation method without it."},
{"title":"Worked actuator: keep inch torque and inch lead together","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#worked-actuator-keep-inch-torque-and-inch-lead-together","text":"Worked actuator: keep inch torque and inch lead together A single-start ACME actuator has 0.20 in pitch, runs at 900 rpm and receives 12 lbf·in of torque. Its assembly efficiency is given as 60%. Find travel speed and useful axial force. L = 0.20 × 1 = 0.20 in/rev. v = 900 × 0.20 = 180 in/min = 3 in/s. F theoretical = 2π × 12 / 0.20 = 376.99 lbf. F actual = 376.99 × 0.60 ≈ 226 lbf. The 60% belongs in step 4, not step 2. It is an example input, not an efficiency assigned to every ACME screw. Practice actuator force"},
{"title":"Worked vise: turn hand force into torque before finding clamp force","page":"Lead Screws","summary":"Pitch times starts gives lead. Use lead to convert rpm into travel speed and torque into force, with efficiency applied only to the force conversion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/lead-screws.html#worked-vise-turn-hand-force-into-torque-before-finding-clamp-force","text":"Worked vise: turn hand force into torque before finding clamp force For an illustrative square-thread vise, a 120 N hand force acts perpendicular to the handle at a radius of 0.18 m from the screw axis. Pitch is 4 mm with one start. Assume a given overall tightening efficiency of 30%; this is not a measured or universal vise efficiency. Use torque = radius × perpendicular force: T = 0.18 × 120 = 21.6 N·m. Radius is the distance from the axis to the hand, not necessarily the full handle length. L = 4 mm/rev = 0.004 m/rev. F actual = 0.30 × 2π × 21.6 / 0.004 ≈ 10,179 N = 10.18 kN. This is a tightening estimate using the supplied efficiency. It is not a vise rating or a prediction of residual force after the hand stops turning. A separate free-travel calculation at 24 rpm gives 24 × 4 / 60 = 1.6 mm/s; the jaws do not continue at that speed through a rigid clamped part. Practice handle torque and clamp force Practice a triple-start CNC screw"},
{"title":"A linear guide leaves travel along one axis","page":"Linear Bearing Guides","summary":"Use the same degrees-of-freedom model to compare round shafts, profile rails and V-wheel carriages.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/linear-bearing-guides.html#a-linear-guide-leaves-travel-along-one-axis","text":"A linear guide leaves travel along one axis A linear bearing guides sliding motion just as a rotary bearing guides turning. Let x point along the travel. The guide supports transverse loads and, where its geometry allows, resists moments that would tilt or twist the carriage. A screw, belt or other actuator supplies travel force; the guide does not drive itself. In the ideal model, translation along x is free. The desired constraints depend on whether rotation about x is acceptable. Real guide selection also checks load, moment capacity, stiffness, clearance, lubrication and alignment. Round shaft allows axial travel and does not key rotation; profile rail and an opposed V-wheel carriage constrain rotation and leave axial travel. A table lists the six motions. T means translation; R means rotation. “Restrained” assumes adequate bearing length or block spacing, engagement and rated loads. It does not mean infinite stiffness."},
{"title":"A round shaft does not provide an anti-rotation key","page":"Linear Bearing Guides","summary":"Use the same degrees-of-freedom model to compare round shafts, profile rails and V-wheel carriages.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/linear-bearing-guides.html#a-round-shaft-does-not-provide-an-anti-rotation-key","text":"A round shaft does not provide an anti-rotation key A plain round sleeve or linear ball bushing guides motion along a cylindrical shaft. In the ideal geometry it leaves two freedoms: T x and R x. The four other motions are restrained by the support's length or spaced bearings. A second parallel guide can stop the carriage rotating. Geometric freedom is not a rotary service rating. A recirculating ball bushing may resist or wear under rotation even though its round shaft provides no positive anti-rotation feature. Use a product explicitly rated for combined linear and rotary motion when both are required. LMU is a common catalog family label associated with round-shaft linear ball bushings. Treat it as a catalog name, not a universal standard or proof of rotary capability. Check the supplier's construction, dimensions and limits."},
{"title":"A profile rail constrains twist as well as sideways motion","page":"Linear Bearing Guides","summary":"Use the same degrees-of-freedom model to compare round shafts, profile rails and V-wheel carriages.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/linear-bearing-guides.html#a-profile-rail-constrains-twist-as-well-as-sideways-motion","text":"A profile rail constrains twist as well as sideways motion A shaped rail and matching carriage have separated contact paths that resist twisting and tipping. Within their moment ratings, they leave one degree of freedom: T x. Mounting surfaces must keep the rail aligned; adding constraints can also make an assembly sensitive to misalignment. MGN commonly identifies a miniature, narrow-block profile-guide family. For example, HIWIN uses MGN within its miniature MG series, with recirculating balls. The label does not make every supplier's product interchangeable, and does not describe every plain or roller guide."},
{"title":"Opposed V-wheels guide a carriage; one wheel does not","page":"Linear Bearing Guides","summary":"Use the same degrees-of-freedom model to compare round shafts, profile rails and V-wheel carriages.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/linear-bearing-guides.html#opposed-v-wheels-guide-a-carriage-one-wheel-does-not","text":"Opposed V-wheels guide a carriage; one wheel does not A V-groove wheel runs on a matching edge or rail. A stable carriage commonly uses three or more wheels with contacts opposed across the rail and spaced along it. A triangular three-wheel arrangement can use an adjustable wheel to take up clearance. The assembled carriage should leave only travel along the rail. Three wheels placed arbitrarily do not guarantee that result: orientation, spacing, contact retention and adjustment determine which loads and moments it can resist. A carriage that can lift away from its track has not constrained that motion. Manufacturer reading and the rotary-motion qualification THK: Assembling the Linear Bushing includes the heading “Rotational Use Not Allowed.” This is why unkeyed geometry must not be taught as permission to run a ball bushing as a rotary bearing. HIWIN: Linear Guideways identifies MGN as its narrow miniature block and describes moment resistance. Bishop-Wisecarver: Guide Wheel Technical Data shows a three-wheel carriage and discusses wheel orientation. Compare rotary support on Bearing Types and Loads. Practice counting freedoms"},
{"title":"A measurement is a number and a unit","page":"Measurement Language","summary":"Use quantities, units, symbols, and SI prefixes precisely before a drawing, program, or calculation has a chance to drift.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measurement-language.html#a-measurement-is-a-number-and-a-unit","text":"A measurement is a number and a unit A dimension on a drawing, a speed in a robot program, and a torque setting are all quantities: a number paired with a unit. The number tells how many; the unit tells what kind of thing and how large each part is. 25 mm A length: twenty-five millimeters. Writing only 25 leaves the measurement incomplete. 4.25 min A CNC cycle time. It is not interchangeable with 4.25 seconds or 4.25 hours. 3.0 m/s A speed or velocity magnitude. The slash means meters per second. Carry units through every line of a calculation. They are an error check, not decoration. If the last line needs a length and the units say seconds, stop before the wrong number reaches a drawing or controller. Use the unit symbol exactly as specified: m for meter, s for second, kg for kilogram. Symbols are not pluralized and do not take a period: 12 mm, not 12 mms."},
{"title":"Two systems you will encounter","page":"Measurement Language","summary":"Use quantities, units, symbols, and SI prefixes precisely before a drawing, program, or calculation has a chance to drift.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measurement-language.html#two-systems-you-will-encounter","text":"Two systems you will encounter The International System of Units ( SI ) is the standard system for science and much technical work. U.S. drawings, hardware, materials, and equipment also use U.S. customary units. Neither system lets us skip the unit; the job is to state which one is being used and convert deliberately when they meet. SI drawing 25.0 mm millimeters The same machined plate measured in two unit systems A rectangular plate is dimensioned across the same width. One drawing calls it 25 millimeters and the other calls it about 0.984 inches. U.S. customary drawing &asymp; 0.984 in inches The part does not change. The measurement language does. A value copied without its unit can make the same feature look like two different sizes. SI base units used here meter ( m ) for length, second ( s ) for time, and kilogram ( kg ) for mass. U.S. customary units used here inch ( in ), foot ( ft ), mile ( mi ), second ( s ), pound mass ( lbm ), and U.S. liquid gallon ( U.S. gal ). Coherent BG engineering system The source slides call the coherent engineering form BG (British Gravitational): foot, slug, and second. In that system, slug - ft/s 2 = lbf. A pound force and a pound mass are not the same unit. A gallon must be named. This unit set uses the U.S. liquid gallon ( U.S. gal ), not an Imperial gallon. Why technical writing cares about the space Leave a space between a numerical value and its unit symbol: 8 m, 15.0 kg, and 0.50 s. SI also uses a space before &deg;C and %. Consistent notation is a low-cost way to keep a drawing, a spreadsheet, and a controller reading the same thing."},
{"title":"SI prefixes change scale, not quantity","page":"Measurement Language","summary":"Use quantities, units, symbols, and SI prefixes precisely before a drawing, program, or calculation has a chance to drift.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measurement-language.html#si-prefixes-change-scale-not-quantity","text":"SI prefixes change scale, not quantity A prefix is a power of ten attached to a unit. It makes a practical-sized number without changing the physical quantity: 0.025 mm and 25 &micro;m describe the same length. Common engineering prefix ladder Move right: each unit is 1,000 times smaller, so the number is 1,000 times larger. kilo km 10 3 m no prefix m 10 0 m milli mm 10 &minus;3 m micro &micro;m 10 &minus;6 m nano nm 10 &minus;9 m 1 m = 1,000 mm = 1,000,000 &micro;m = 1,000,000,000 nm The ladder is a teaching subset; the complete reference follows. It shows why 1 mm = 0.001 m and 1 &micro;m = 0.000001 m: changing a prefix changes the number used to describe a length, not the length itself. All current SI prefixes, plus the unprefixed unit used between them. Name Symbol Factor quetta Q 10 30 ronna R 10 27 yotta Y 10 24 zetta Z 10 21 exa E 10 18 peta P 10 15 tera T 10 12 giga G 10 9 mega M 10 6 kilo k 10 3 hecto h 10 2 deca da 10 1 none - 10 0 deci d 10 &minus;1 centi c 10 &minus;2 milli m 10 &minus;3 micro &micro; 10 &minus;6 nano n 10 &minus;9 pico p 10 &minus;12 femto f 10 &minus;15 atto a 10 &minus;18 zepto z 10 &minus;21 yocto y 10 &minus;24 ronto r 10 &minus;27 quecto q 10 &minus;30 Case matters. M is mega (10 6 ); m is milli (10 &minus;3 ). A wrong capital letter changes a result by a billion."},
{"title":"Prefix explorer","page":"Measurement Language","summary":"Use quantities, units, symbols, and SI prefixes precisely before a drawing, program, or calculation has a chance to drift.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measurement-language.html#prefix-explorer","text":"Prefix explorer Choose two prefixes for the same base unit. The calculation compares their powers of ten; it does not change what is being measured. Convert one prefixed meter value Value From prefix quetta (Q), 10 30 ronna (R), 10 27 yotta (Y), 10 24 zetta (Z), 10 21 exa (E), 10 18 peta (P), 10 15 tera (T), 10 12 giga (G), 10 9 mega (M), 10 6 kilo (k), 10 3 hecto (h), 10 2 deca (da), 10 1 no prefix, 10 0 deci (d), 10 &minus;1 centi (c), 10 &minus;2 milli (m), 10 &minus;3 micro (&micro;), 10 &minus;6 nano (n), 10 &minus;9 pico (p), 10 &minus;12 femto (f), 10 &minus;15 atto (a), 10 &minus;18 zepto (z), 10 &minus;21 yocto (y), 10 &minus;24 ronto (r), 10 &minus;27 quecto (q), 10 &minus;30 To prefix quetta (Q), 10 30 ronna (R), 10 27 yotta (Y), 10 24 zetta (Z), 10 21 exa (E), 10 18 peta (P), 10 15 tera (T), 10 12 giga (G), 10 9 mega (M), 10 6 kilo (k), 10 3 hecto (h), 10 2 deca (da), 10 1 no prefix, 10 0 deci (d), 10 &minus;1 centi (c), 10 &minus;2 milli (m), 10 &minus;3 micro (&micro;), 10 &minus;6 nano (n), 10 &minus;9 pico (p), 10 &minus;12 femto (f), 10 &minus;15 atto (a), 10 &minus;18 zepto (z), 10 &minus;21 yocto (y), 10 &minus;24 ronto (r), 10 &minus;27 quecto (q), 10 &minus;30 Move 3 powers toward larger units. The target unit is 10 3 times larger, so the numerical value is divided by 10 3. From: &micro;m - 10^&minus;6 To: mm - 10^&minus;3 smallest listed unit 10 &minus;30 no prefix 10 0 largest listed unit 10 30 Result: 0.025 mm 25 &micro;m = 25 &times; 10 &minus;6 m = 0.025 &times; 10 &minus;3 m = 0.025 mm. JavaScript is off. The displayed 25 &micro;m to 0.025 mm example and the prefix table are the static answer path."},
{"title":"Practice and sources","page":"Measurement Language","summary":"Use quantities, units, symbols, and SI prefixes precisely before a drawing, program, or calculation has a chance to drift.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measurement-language.html#practice-and-sources","text":"Practice and sources Use the practice bank to apply this lesson's prefix-conversion method. Add to practice Sources and definitions The BIPM SI prefix table is the primary list used here, including the 2022 ronna, quetta, ronto, and quecto additions. The NIST SI Units reference explains SI base and derived units and their technical-writing role."},
{"title":"Voltage is measured across; current is measured through","page":"Measuring Electric Circuits","summary":"Connect voltage, current, resistance and continuity measurements to the part of the circuit they describe.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measuring-electric-circuits.html#voltage-is-measured-across-current-is-measured-through","text":"Voltage is measured across; current is measured through A voltmeter measures the potential difference between two points. Connect it across the component, in parallel. A series ammeter becomes part of the current path: open the path and insert it in series with the element whose current you want. A DC source supplies an ammeter and resistor in series. A voltmeter connects across the resistor, on two separate parallel leads. A meter set for current has a low-resistance input; connecting that input across a source makes a short circuit. Read Electrical Safety before making physical measurements. The diagrams here explain connections, not a procedure for working on mains wiring. Real meters change the circuit a little An ideal voltmeter draws no current. A real one has high but finite input resistance. An ideal ammeter drops no voltage. A real one has some resistance and a voltage drop, sometimes called burden voltage. The practice problems use ideal meters unless stated otherwise."},
{"title":"Place both voltage probes, predict the sign, then reveal","page":"Measuring Electric Circuits","summary":"Connect voltage, current, resistance and continuity measurements to the part of the circuit they describe.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measuring-electric-circuits.html#place-both-voltage-probes-predict-the-sign-then-reveal","text":"Place both voltage probes, predict the sign, then reveal A DC voltmeter reports an ordered difference: V(red) − V(black). The two probes must touch the two named points you want to compare. Reversing them reverses the sign; putting both on the same conductor gives zero. Static example: an ideal 12 V source supplies 30 Ω and 60 Ω resistors in series. Each resistor has an ideal switch immediately before it. Signed readings from the same ideal circuit Circuit state Red point Black point Reading Why S1 open Supply + After S1 +12 V The single open switch takes the source voltage; no current flows. Both closed After S1 After R1 +4 V Current is 12/(30+60) = 0.1333 A; R1 drops 4 V. Both closed, probes reversed After R1 After S1 −4 V The two point potentials are unchanged; only the ordered subtraction reverses. S2 open After R1 After S2 +12 V The upstream side stays at +12 V and the downstream side returns to 0 V. Any supported state One named point The same point 0 V A point cannot have a potential difference from itself. With two open switches, the conductor between them floats in this ideal model. Its voltage is not assigned. The interactive intentionally offers no two-open state. The controls require JavaScript. The signed-reading table and practice links remain available. This is a low-voltage ideal-circuit model with an infinite-impedance voltmeter. It does not authorize live work, mains measurements, or current-mode probing. Fluke's DC-voltage guide explains the V and COM inputs and the negative sign from reversed DC probes; its 381 meter manual shows that voltage probes touch the two desired test points. Practice choosing probe points Practice open-switch readings"},
{"title":"A shunt or clamp lets you measure current indirectly","page":"Measuring Electric Circuits","summary":"Connect voltage, current, resistance and continuity measurements to the part of the circuit they describe.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measuring-electric-circuits.html#a-shunt-or-clamp-lets-you-measure-current-indirectly","text":"A shunt or clamp lets you measure current indirectly A shunt is a known small resistance in the current path. Measure the voltage across it and use the relationship in Current, Voltage, and Resistance to find current. The shunt is in series; the voltage measurement across it is in parallel. Many ammeters contain a shunt internally. An installed shunt avoids opening the circuit each time you read it; adding one still requires changing the path. A clamp meter senses the magnetic field around a conductor without breaking the circuit. Clamp around one conductor, not the outgoing and returning conductors together: their fields can cancel. The instrument must support the type of current being measured; an AC-only clamp does not measure steady DC."},
{"title":"Resistance and continuity are measured with the circuit unpowered","page":"Measuring Electric Circuits","summary":"Connect voltage, current, resistance and continuity measurements to the part of the circuit they describe.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/measuring-electric-circuits.html#resistance-and-continuity-are-measured-with-the-circuit-unpowered","text":"Resistance and continuity are measured with the circuit unpowered An ohmmeter applies its own small test signal. Use it on an unpowered circuit, with stored energy discharged as described in Electrical Safety. Other connected paths can change the result; isolating the component lets you measure its resistance instead of the whole network. Continuity is a resistance-threshold test. The beep means the measured resistance is below that meter's threshold. It does not prove exactly zero resistance, identify the intended path, or establish that a connection can carry its working current. The threshold comes from the meter's manual. A multimeter combines voltage, current and resistance functions, often with continuity too. Select the function, AC or DC mode, range and lead sockets for the measurement. In a schematic, follow the actual connection points rather than the physical positions of the symbols."},
{"title":"A microcontroller reads inputs and controls outputs","page":"Microcontroller Basics","summary":"Read inputs, make decisions in a program, and control outputs using a classic Arduino Uno R3.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/microcontroller-basics.html#a-microcontroller-reads-inputs-and-controls-outputs","text":"A microcontroller reads inputs and controls outputs A microcontroller is a small computer on a chip, with a processor, memory and connections to the outside world. Its program can read a switch, decide what that input means, and turn an output on or off. The Arduino Uno is a board that supplies the chip with power connections, a USB interface and accessible pins. This topic uses the classic Uno R3 with an ATmega328P, operating with 5 V logic. Other Arduino boards, including newer Uno models, can have different converters and electrical limits. Simplified Uno R3 outline: digital pins D0 through D13, PWM on D3 D5 D6 D9 D10 D11, and analog inputs A0 through A5. The drawing groups functions; it is not a wiring pinout. Check the labels on the actual board. Start with Digital and Analog Signals, then Analog-to-Digital Conversion and Pulse-Width Modulation."},
{"title":"A sketch runs setup once, then repeats loop","page":"Microcontroller Basics","summary":"Read inputs, make decisions in a program, and control outputs using a classic Arduino Uno R3.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/microcontroller-basics.html#a-sketch-runs-setup-once-then-repeats-loop","text":"A sketch runs setup once, then repeats loop An Arduino program is called a sketch. It uses C/C++ syntax: rules for names, parentheses, braces and statements. It is a programming language, not ordinary English. Capitalization matters: digitalRead and digitalread are different names. setup() runs once after startup or reset. loop() repeats while the program runs. This original example turns the built-in LED on for half a second and off for half a second. void setup() { pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT); } void loop() { digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH); delay(500); digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, LOW); delay(500); } delay uses milliseconds and pauses normal progress through the sketch. The braces group the statements belonging to each function."},
{"title":"Choose the pin mode before using the pin","page":"Microcontroller Basics","summary":"Read inputs, make decisions in a program, and control outputs using a classic Arduino Uno R3.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/microcontroller-basics.html#choose-the-pin-mode-before-using-the-pin","text":"Choose the pin mode before using the pin Three pin modes Mode What it does Typical use INPUT Reads a voltage level without enabling the internal pull-up. A sensor that actively drives a compatible signal; do not leave the input floating. OUTPUT Drives the pin HIGH or LOW. An LED through a current-limiting resistor, or a control signal to a driver. INPUT_PULLUP Reads an input with an internal resistor pulling it toward the logic supply. A button connecting the input to ground. pinMode(pin, mode) configures the pin. digitalRead(pin) returns HIGH or LOW. On an OUTPUT pin, digitalWrite(pin, HIGH) drives it toward the logic supply; LOW drives it toward ground. These are limited-current signals, not ideal power supplies. On an INPUT pin, digitalWrite instead controls the pull-up."},
{"title":"A button to ground reads LOW when pressed","page":"Microcontroller Basics","summary":"Read inputs, make decisions in a program, and control outputs using a classic Arduino Uno R3.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/microcontroller-basics.html#a-button-to-ground-reads-low-when-pressed","text":"A button to ground reads LOW when pressed With INPUT_PULLUP, an open switch reads HIGH. Closing the switch to ground reads LOW. This is called active-low: the action happens when the signal is low. Internal pull-up resistor from 5 V to the input node, with a normally open button from that node to ground. Open gives HIGH; closed gives LOW. void setup() { pinMode(2, INPUT_PULLUP); pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT); } void loop() { bool pressed = (digitalRead(2) == LOW); digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, pressed? HIGH: LOW); } The pull-up supplies a small current through the closed switch to ground. The switch sinks that current. A physical switch can bounce between states briefly; counting presses reliably needs debouncing beyond this simple state display. Pull-up resistance and current The Uno's internal pull-up is approximately 20–50 kΩ, not a fixed 10 kΩ. Arduino's Uno R3 documentation specifies “20-50k ohm.” At nominal 5 V, a closed switch carries about 0.10–0.25 mA through that resistor, using I = V/R. See Current, Voltage, and Resistance."},
{"title":"Use the official IDE and function reference","page":"Microcontroller Basics","summary":"Read inputs, make decisions in a program, and control outputs using a classic Arduino Uno R3.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/microcontroller-basics.html#use-the-official-ide-and-function-reference","text":"Use the official IDE and function reference Get the IDE from Arduino Software. Use the Arduino language reference to check spelling, arguments and return values. The Arduino getting-started guide introduces the programming environment. Confirm the board model and choose its matching board entry in the IDE. Select the port belonging to the connected board. Verify the sketch to catch syntax errors, then upload it. Check that the observed input and output match the program. Disconnect power before changing wiring. Keep signals within the board's ratings and use a suitable driver for a motor. See Electrical Safety for electrical precautions. Practice choosing a pin mode"},
{"title":"Path length and displacement answer different questions","page":"Motion Quantities","summary":"Distinguish path length from displacement, and speed from velocity, before using time to describe a machine or vehicle in motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/motion-quantities.html#path-length-and-displacement-answer-different-questions","text":"Path length and displacement answer different questions Distance is how much path was traveled. Displacement is the directed change from the starting position to the ending position. A return trip can add distance while leaving displacement at zero. Distance A nonnegative scalar path length. A conveyor that moves a tote 12 m out and 12 m back travels 24 m of distance. Displacement A change in position with direction. If the tote returns to its start, its displacement is 0 m even though it moved. Coordinate choice Choose a positive direction before calculating. Along a straight robot rail, right might be positive and left negative. Distance tells what the machine did along its path; displacement tells where it ended up relative to where it began. Do not substitute one for the other just because both use meters or feet."},
{"title":"Speed and velocity are not synonyms","page":"Motion Quantities","summary":"Distinguish path length from displacement, and speed from velocity, before using time to describe a machine or vehicle in motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/motion-quantities.html#speed-and-velocity-are-not-synonyms","text":"Speed and velocity are not synonyms Average speed is total distance divided by elapsed time. Average velocity is displacement divided by elapsed time. Velocity includes direction; speed does not. average speed = total distance / elapsed time average velocity = displacement / elapsed time Notation bridge: this site uses x for position and &Delta;x for displacement. Some course materials use s for position instead. For a stated interval, write average velocity as v avg = &Delta;x / &Delta;t; the symbols differ, but the physical idea is the same. For the 12 m out-and-back path completed in 16 s, average speed is 24 m / 16 s = 1.5 m/s. Average velocity is 0 m / 16 s = 0 m/s. Both statements can be true at the same time. A production-line example A mechanism must carry a part 12 m in 8 s. Its required average speed is 12 m / 8 s = 1.5 m/s. Converting with exact factors gives 1.5 m/s &times; (39.37007874 in / 1 m) = 59.055... in/s, or 59.1 in/s. If the programmed direction is stated, its average velocity is +59.1 in/s in that direction."},
{"title":"Average and instantaneous readings","page":"Motion Quantities","summary":"Distinguish path length from displacement, and speed from velocity, before using time to describe a machine or vehicle in motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/motion-quantities.html#average-and-instantaneous-readings","text":"Average and instantaneous readings An average uses a time interval. An instantaneous reading describes one moment: a car speedometer reports instantaneous speed; a robot controller's live velocity value is an instantaneous velocity in its coordinate frame. Use average speed for throughput, travel-time, and trip planning. Use average velocity for a net movement over a stated interval. Use an instantaneous reading when clearance, collision avoidance, or a machine limit depends on what is happening now. The calculus idea, after the practical one Over a smaller and smaller time interval, average velocity approaches instantaneous velocity. In calculus notation, velocity is the derivative of position with respect to time: v = dx/dt. For this course, begin by naming the interval, position change, and direction correctly; the derivative notation is a compact extension of the same idea."},
{"title":"Out-and-back path lab","page":"Motion Quantities","summary":"Distinguish path length from displacement, and speed from velocity, before using time to describe a machine or vehicle in motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/motion-quantities.html#out-and-back-path-lab","text":"Out-and-back path lab Set positions along a straight path and the outbound and return times. The text results make the distance and displacement distinction visible even when the track is not used. Position and time inputs Start position (m) Turn position (m) Outbound time (s) Return time (s) &minus;3 m 15 m Start: 0 m Turn: 12 m End: 0 m Distance 24 m Displacement 0 m Average speed 1.5 m/s Average velocity 0 m/s JavaScript is off. The displayed values are the solved 12 m out, 12 m back, 8 s each-way case: 24 m distance, 0 m displacement, 1.5 m/s average speed, and 0 m/s average velocity."},
{"title":"Practice and sources","page":"Motion Quantities","summary":"Distinguish path length from displacement, and speed from velocity, before using time to describe a machine or vehicle in motion.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/motion-quantities.html#practice-and-sources","text":"Practice and sources Use the practice bank to apply distance, speed, and round-trip rate reasoning. Add to practice Source note The NIST SI Units reference lists velocity as a derived unit example. Direction is an added physical requirement: two motions may have the same speed but different velocities because their directions differ."},
{"title":"Power is how fast work gets done","page":"Power and Efficiency","summary":"Power is work per unit time - three equivalent ways to calculate it, the horsepower conversions, and what an efficiency figure can and cannot be.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/power-and-efficiency.html#power-is-how-fast-work-gets-done","text":"Power is how fast work gets done Two hoists that lift the same load to the same height do the same work. The one that does it in half the time needs twice the power. P = W / t - average power equals work divided by elapsed time. The two power units and the factors between them. System Unit Symbol Definition and conversion SI watt W 1 W = 1 J/s; 1 W = 0.001341022 hp Foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second horsepower hp 1 hp = 550 ft-lbf/s; 1 hp = 745.6999 W The symbol W does double duty: upright W after a number is the watt, while W standing in a formula for a quantity is usually work. Context separates them: P = W/t uses W for work, while 1 W = 1 J/s uses it for the watt. Worked example: powering the lift On Work and Energy, lifting 2,000 lbf through 10 ft took 20,000 ft-lbf, or 27.1 kJ. How much power does doing it in 4 s require? In U.S. units: 20,000 ft-lbf &divide; 4 s = 5,000 ft-lbf/s, and 5,000 &divide; 550 = 9.09 hp. In SI: 27,116 J &divide; 4 s = 6,779 W, or 6.78 kW. Cross-check the two: 9.0909 hp &times; 745.6999 W/hp = 6,779 W. They agree, which is the point of doing both. That is the power delivered to the load. A motor sized for this job needs more than that, because no drive is perfectly efficient."},
{"title":"The same power, three ways to reach it","page":"Power and Efficiency","summary":"Power is work per unit time - three equivalent ways to calculate it, the horsepower conversions, and what an efficiency figure can and cannot be.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/power-and-efficiency.html#the-same-power-three-ways-to-reach-it","text":"The same power, three ways to reach it Which form you use depends on what you have been given, not on which is more correct. All three produce watts in SI. In P = Fv, force and velocity must be parallel; otherwise use the force component along the velocity. Three routes to a power, in each system. Form SI Foot&ndash;pound Work over time P = W/t J/s = W (ft-lbf/s) &divide; 550 = hp Force times speed P = Fv N &times; (m/s) = W (lbf &times; ft/s) &divide; 550 = hp Torque times angular velocity P = &tau;&omega; (N-m) &times; (rad/s) = W (lbf-ft &times; rad/s) &divide; 550 = hp In the third form the angular velocity must be in rad/s. An rpm figure substituted directly is wrong by a factor of about 9.55. Convert it first - Angular Motion. The divisor 550 is not a fudge factor. One horsepower is defined as 550 pound-force feet of work per second, so dividing by 550 is simply converting the unit."},
{"title":"Two shortcut formulas, and the conditions attached to them","page":"Power and Efficiency","summary":"Power is work per unit time - three equivalent ways to calculate it, the horsepower conversions, and what an efficiency figure can and cannot be.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/power-and-efficiency.html#two-shortcut-formulas-and-the-conditions-attached-to-them","text":"Two shortcut formulas, and the conditions attached to them These two save a lot of arithmetic in practice. Both are unit conversions with an assumption hidden inside. From volts and amps hp = (volts &times; amps) &divide; 746 The 746 is exact: NIST defines a separate unit, horsepower (electric), as 746 W. So the formula is really two steps - find the electrical power in watts, then convert watts to horsepower. The hidden condition is in the first step. Volts times amps gives real power in watts for a direct-current circuit or a purely resistive load. For single-phase AC, RMS volts times RMS amps gives apparent power, in volt-amperes; the real power in watts is that multiplied by the power factor, the ratio of real power to apparent power. Use the shortcut on an AC motor without its power factor and the answer comes out high. See Current, Voltage, and Resistance. From torque and rpm hp = (torque in lbf-in &times; speed in rpm) &divide; 63,025 This one is the P = &tau;&omega; row above, with the rpm-to-rad/s conversion and the inch-to-foot conversion already folded into the constant. It only works with the torque in pound-force inches and the speed in rpm; feed it lbf-ft and the answer is twelve times too small. Where 63,025 comes from One horsepower is 550 ft-lbf/s, which is 550 &times; 12 = 6,600 lbf-in per second. One revolution per minute is 2&pi;/60 rad/s. Putting a torque &tau; in lbf-in and a speed N in rpm into P = &tau;&omega; gives &tau;N(2&pi;/60) lbf-in per second, and dividing by 6,600 converts that to horsepower: hp = &tau;N &times; (2&pi;/60) &divide; 6,600 = &tau;N &divide; (6,600 &times; 60 &divide; 2&pi;) = &tau;N &divide; 63,025 Every constant in the shortcut is a unit conversion. Nothing about the physics is buried in it, which is why the same relationship in coherent SI units needs no constant at all."},
{"title":"Efficiency is the fraction that makes it through","page":"Power and Efficiency","summary":"Power is work per unit time - three equivalent ways to calculate it, the horsepower conversions, and what an efficiency figure can and cannot be.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/power-and-efficiency.html#efficiency-is-the-fraction-that-makes-it-through","text":"Efficiency is the fraction that makes it through The efficiency of a machine is the ratio of useful output to total input. Its symbol is &eta; (eta), and it can be written with either power or work as long as both sides of the ratio use the same one: &eta; = P out / P in - &eta; = W out / W in It is a ratio of two like quantities, so the units cancel: efficiency is a plain number, usually quoted as a percentage. For a real machine in steady operation, &eta; &lt; 1; the ideal lossless limit is 1. You cannot get more power out than you put in, and some of every input always leaves as heat, noise, or friction rather than as useful output. A calculated efficiency above 1 means an error in the numbers, not a remarkable machine. Worked example: an electric hoist motor A motor consumes 500 W and applies 1,000 N of force to lift an object at 0.45 m/s. What is the efficiency of the system? Useful output, using P = Fv: 1,000 N &times; 0.45 m/s = 450 W. &eta; = 450 W &divide; 500 W = 0.90, or 90%. The missing 50 W has not vanished. It is power lost rather than delivered to the load, typically as heat in the motor and drive. Efficiency figures are only comparable when the boundary is the same. A motor efficiency, a drive efficiency, and a system efficiency measured from the wall socket to the load are three different numbers for the same machine, and the largest of them is the one a datasheet prefers to print. Say which one you mean."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Power and Efficiency","summary":"Power is work per unit time - three equivalent ways to calculate it, the horsepower conversions, and what an efficiency figure can and cannot be.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/power-and-efficiency.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked NIST SP 811, Appendix B lists horsepower (550 ft-lbf/s) to watt as 7.456 999 E+02 and horsepower (electric) to watt as 7.46 E+02 - two separately defined units, which is why 745.6999 and 746 both appear above. The 0.001341022 hp in one watt is the reciprocal of the first. Foot pound-force to joule is 1.355 818 E+00 in the same table. NIST SP 811, Chapter 4, Table 3, gives the watt as W, expressed as J/s and in base units as m 2 -kg-s &minus;3. Power factor is defined by Fluke as \"the ratio of working power, measured in kilowatts (kW), to apparent power, measured in kilovolt amperes (kVA).\" The 63,025 constant is derived above from the horsepower definition and the rpm-to-rad/s conversion, not quoted."},
{"title":"Duty cycle is the fraction of each cycle spent HIGH","page":"Pulse-Width Modulation","summary":"Explore duty cycle, frequency, average voltage and RC smoothing, then compare PWM output with ADC input.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/pulse-width-modulation.html#duty-cycle-is-the-fraction-of-each-cycle-spent-high","text":"Duty cycle is the fraction of each cycle spent HIGH Pulse-width modulation (PWM) repeats a rectangular waveform. The period T is the time for one cycle; frequency f is cycles per second. Duty cycle D is the fraction of the period spent HIGH. T = 1/f, t on = D × T, and for ideal switching between 0 V and a supply V s, V average = D × V s. Convert a percentage to a fraction: 25% means D = 0.25. Three PWM waveforms with equal periods and duty cycles of 25, 50 and 75 percent. The HIGH time increases while the period stays fixed. At 500 Hz, T = 0.002 s = 2 ms = 2000 µs. At 25% duty, the on-time is 500 µs and the average of a 0–5 V output is 1.25 V. The pin itself still switches between 0 and 5 V."},
{"title":"The stock Uno R3 uses six PWM pins","page":"Pulse-Width Modulation","summary":"Explore duty cycle, frequency, average voltage and RC smoothing, then compare PWM output with ADC input.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/pulse-width-modulation.html#the-stock-uno-r3-uses-six-pwm-pins","text":"The stock Uno R3 uses six PWM pins Use digital pins 3, 5, 6, 9, 10 and 11. Default PWM is approximately 490 Hz on 3, 9, 10 and 11, and 980 Hz on 5 and 6. These correspond to periods of about 2.04 ms and 1.02 ms. analogWrite(pin, value) requests duty using values from 0 (always off) to 255 (always on). A useful nominal mapping is value = round(255 × D): 25% gives 64. Hardware timer modes introduce small differences in the exact duty, particularly near the ends of the range. Official frequency table and timer limitations The Uno R3 row of Arduino's analogWrite reference source states “490 Hz (pins 5 and 6: 980 Hz)”. See also the published analogWrite reference. A frequency near 31 kHz requires changing timer configuration; it is not the stock setting. analogWrite sets duty, not frequency. Arduino's Secrets of Arduino PWM explains prescalers and timer modes. Changing timers can also affect other functions and libraries."},
{"title":"Change duty and frequency, then compare an ADC reading","page":"Pulse-Width Modulation","summary":"Explore duty cycle, frequency, average voltage and RC smoothing, then compare PWM output with ADC input.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/pulse-width-modulation.html#change-duty-and-frequency-then-compare-an-adc-reading","text":"Change duty and frequency, then compare an ADC reading Print / static example: 25% duty at 500 Hz gives a 2 ms period, 0.5 ms on-time and 1.25 V average for a 0–5 V waveform. An unloaded RC filter with R = 10 kΩ and C = 1 µF has a 10 ms time constant; after settling, its output ripples around 1.25 V. With a 5.000 V ADC reference, 3.000 V gives code 614 in the ideal bin model; code 800 gives a lower-edge estimate of 3.90625 V. The controls require JavaScript. The equations, duty-cycle figure and worked example remain available. The explorer is a model, not a board connection. Its frequency control does not configure an Uno. ADC controls use the ideal bin convention on Analog-to-Digital Conversion; 5.000 V is assumed exact."},
{"title":"An RC filter smooths PWM but leaves ripple","page":"Pulse-Width Modulation","summary":"Explore duty cycle, frequency, average voltage and RC smoothing, then compare PWM output with ADC input.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/pulse-width-modulation.html#an-rc-filter-smooths-pwm-but-leaves-ripple","text":"An RC filter smooths PWM but leaves ripple Put a resistor in series with the PWM signal and a capacitor from the output node to ground; measure the voltage across the capacitor. This RC low-pass filter slows voltage changes. The capacitor charges while PWM is HIGH and discharges while it is LOW. The time constant is τ = R × C. A longer time constant relative to the PWM period reduces ripple but makes the output respond more slowly when duty changes. The explorer shows the settled waveform with τ = 10 ms, assuming an ideal source and no load. A load changes the result; the filter is not a motor power supply. What the smoothed trace calculates During each constant input interval, V(t) = V target + (V start − V target )e −t/τ. The model solves for a repeating steady state, then draws the charging and discharging curves. It omits startup, output resistance, component tolerances and loading. At 0% or 100%, the settled output is simply 0 V or 5 V."},
{"title":"PWM changes LED brightness and motor drive","page":"Pulse-Width Modulation","summary":"Explore duty cycle, frequency, average voltage and RC smoothing, then compare PWM output with ADC input.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/pulse-width-modulation.html#pwm-changes-led-brightness-and-motor-drive","text":"PWM changes LED brightness and motor drive Rapid switching changes an LED's average light output while a resistor or driver limits its on-state current. Perceived brightness is not perfectly proportional to duty. For a DC motor, PWM commands a suitable power driver; the board pin does not supply the motor current. The driver must handle the motor and its inductive current. Increasing duty usually increases the available drive, but motor speed also depends on load. Use DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power to connect speed, torque and current. See Electrical Safety before wiring. Practice PWM calculations"},
{"title":"Read the connection convention before tracing a wire","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#read-the-connection-convention-before-tracing-a-wire","text":"Read the connection convention before tracing a wire A schematic organizes electrical relationships, not the positions of parts on a bench. Stretching or bending a drawn wire does not change its connections. Moving a line onto a different terminal does. For the examples on this page, a filled dot at a crossing means the wires join. A crossing with no dot means they do not join. Other drawings may use a bridge or different conventions. Check the legend; an unclear or damaged junction mark is a reason to consult the drawing source. Two crossing-wire examples: the dotted intersection is one node; the undotted intersection is two separate nodes under this drawing's convention."},
{"title":"Count connected conductors, not corners or dots","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#count-connected-conductors-not-corners-or-dots","text":"Count connected conductors, not corners or dots A node is a set of points joined by ideal conductors. Every point along an uninterrupted wire belongs to the same node, including its bends and branches. A dot marks a connection; it is not a separate component or an extra node. In the figure, the dotted cross is one node. The undotted cross is two nodes: the horizontal conductor and the vertical conductor. This count concerns the wires drawn in the figure; no connection elsewhere is implied. Do not trace through a resistor, diode, or open switch as if it were wire. If an ideal switch closes, its two sides become connected; state the switch position before counting nodes. Matching net labels can also indicate a connection without a continuous line across the page. Check the label scope and drawing notes before joining similarly named nets."},
{"title":"Read component identity separately from its value","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#read-component-identity-separately-from-its-value","text":"Read component identity separately from its value A reference such as R1 identifies a particular part; a value such as 1 kΩ describes its resistance. D1 can identify a diode or LED, so its symbol and parts information still matter. A ground label identifies a reference or connection according to the drawing; it does not by itself establish a connection to protective earth. US practice (IEEE 315 / ANSI Y32.2) and international practice (IEC 60617) draw some symbols differently. Use the drawing legend; the forms below are teaching examples, not a claim of compliance with a standard."},
{"title":"Trace a connection one terminal at a time","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#trace-a-connection-one-terminal-at-a-time","text":"Trace a connection one terminal at a time Read the legend, notes, revision, and stated switch positions. Find the source and the named return or reference node. Follow each conductor to a component terminal, junction, or net label. Mark each uninterrupted group of conductors as one node. Check crossings and similarly named labels before assuming they join. Use the wiring drawing and parts documentation when physical terminal locations matter. Practice crossing wires See Electrical Diagram Types for choosing a drawing and Current, Voltage, and Resistance for the quantities measured between and through components."},
{"title":"Recognize common symbols before tracing their connections","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#recognize-common-symbols-before-tracing-their-connections","text":"Recognize common symbols before tracing their connections Use the symbol to identify the kind of component, then read its reference and value. The sheet includes both resistor forms and an unpolarized capacitor. A battery is one kind of DC source; other DC supplies may use a circle marked with polarity. Common symbols panel 1: battery, US and IEC resistors, open switch, lamp and LED. Common teaching forms, panel 1 of 2. Common symbols panel 2: buzzer, ground, fuse, motor, unpolarized capacitor and diode. Common teaching forms, panel 2 of 2. Buzzer and fuse shapes vary, so check the legend. The ground mark's meaning depends on the drawing; it does not establish protective earth by itself. The diode bar marks its cathode; the LED adds outward arrows for light. Practice symbol identification"},
{"title":"Count four nodes with the switch open, three with it closed","page":"Reading Electrical Connections","summary":"Follow conductors and node labels instead of judging connections by where components sit on the page.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/reading-electrical-connections.html#count-four-nodes-with-the-switch-open-three-with-it-closed","text":"Count four nodes with the switch open, three with it closed Trace this small component schematic with S1 open. Stop at each component terminal; do not count through the battery, resistor, or LED. Battery B1, open switch S1, resistor R1 and LED D1 form a loop. The same generic circuit as the drawing comparison. No off-page connections are implied. Check the node count terminal by terminal The four separate conductor groups are B1+ to S1; the other side of S1 to R1; the other side of R1 to D1 anode; and D1 cathode to B1−. Closing an ideal S1 merges the first two groups into one, leaving three nodes. None of the component bodies is an extra node. Practice counting schematic nodes"},
{"title":"The radius is what links turning to travelling","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#the-radius-is-what-links-turning-to-travelling","text":"The radius is what links turning to travelling A roller turns; the belt on it travels. A wheel turns; the vehicle moves. The two speeds are not the same number, and the thing that converts one into the other is the radius. v = &omega;r - a = &alpha;r Here a is the tangential acceleration, not the rim point's total acceleration: it also has an inward component &omega; 2 r. Belt and vehicle speeds match rim speed only when there is no slipping. v The straight-line speed of a point on the rim - the speed of the belt, the sheet, the vehicle. &omega; Angular velocity, in radians per second. See Angular Motion for the conversion from rpm. r The radius, in the same length unit you want the answer in. Rollers and wheels are almost always specified by diameter, so halve it first. Both of these formulas are only true when the angle is in radians. Feed them rpm and the answer is wrong by a factor of about 9.55; feed them degrees per second and it is wrong by a factor of about 57.3. Convert to rad/s first, every time. Two mistakes account for nearly every wrong answer here: using the diameter where the radius belongs, and leaving the speed in rpm. Both are worth a deliberate check before the arithmetic starts. Why the radian is the only angle unit that works A radian is defined as arc length divided by radius. Multiply an angle in radians by a radius and you get the arc length back - which is exactly the distance the rim travelled. Do the same thing per second and you have the rim speed. Degrees and revolutions carry an extra arbitrary count inside them, so they need a conversion factor first. The radian does not, because it was built out of the same geometry the formula describes."},
{"title":"Worked example: a conveyor drive roller","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#worked-example-a-conveyor-drive-roller","text":"Worked example: a conveyor drive roller A conveyor belt runs over a drive roller 200 mm in diameter, turning at 45 rpm. What is the belt speed, and how long does a package take to travel 15 m? Halve the diameter: r = 100 mm = 0.100 m. Convert the speed: 45 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 4.712389 rad/s. Multiply: v = 4.712389 rad/s &times; 0.100 m = 0.4712 m/s. Divide the distance by the speed: 15 m &divide; 0.4712389 m/s = 31.83 s. 0.47 m/s, and about 32 s for 15 m. That assumes the belt does not slip on the roller, which is the assumption a real drive is designed to make true."},
{"title":"Worked example: a roll-forming feed rate","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#worked-example-a-roll-forming-feed-rate","text":"Worked example: a roll-forming feed rate Forming rolls 150 mm in diameter rotate at 25 rpm. What is the feed rate of the sheet in m/min, and how long does 20 m of sheet take? r = 75 mm = 0.075 m. 25 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 2.617994 rad/s. v = 2.617994 rad/s &times; 0.075 m = 0.19635 m/s. The question asks for metres per minute, so convert the time unit: 0.19635 m/s &times; 60 s/min = 11.78 m/min. 20 m &divide; 11.78 m/min = 1.70 min, which is about 1 min 42 s. Note what step 4 did and did not do. Changing seconds to minutes in a speed takes one factor of 60. If the quantity had been an acceleration, the same change would take 60 2 - see Acceleration and Squared Time."},
{"title":"Worked example: running the conversion backwards","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#worked-example-running-the-conversion-backwards","text":"Worked example: running the conversion backwards Sometimes the linear quantity is what you know. The formulas simply rearrange: &omega; = v/r and &alpha; = a/r. A wheel, from angular to linear A tyre 28 in in diameter turns at 3.5 rad/s. How fast is the vehicle moving, in miles per hour? r = 14 in &times; 0.0254 m/in = 0.3556 m. v = 3.5 rad/s &times; 0.3556 m = 1.2446 m/s. 1.2446 m/s &divide; 0.44704 m/s per mph = 2.78 mph. A wheel, from linear to angular A vehicle accelerates at 5 m/s 2 on tyres 78 cm in diameter. What is the angular acceleration of a wheel? r = 39 cm = 0.39 m. &alpha; = 5 m/s 2 &divide; 0.39 m = 12.8 rad/s 2. Check the unit on the answer, not just the number. Dividing m/s 2 by m leaves 1/s 2, and because the radian is a ratio of two lengths, rad/s 2 is the same thing named for what it describes."},
{"title":"Rim speed is why wheel size changes the safe rpm","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#rim-speed-is-why-wheel-size-changes-the-safe-rpm","text":"Rim speed is why wheel size changes the safe rpm A grinding wheel is limited by how fast its rim moves, not by how fast its centre turns. Put a bigger wheel on the same spindle at the same rpm and the rim goes faster, because every point on it travels a longer circle in the same time. Take a wheel whose rim speed must stay at or below 35 m/s: Same 35 m/s rim-speed limit, two wheel sizes. Wheel diameter Radius &omega; = v/r Same speed in rpm 200 mm 0.100 m 350 rad/s 3,342 rpm 250 mm 0.125 m 280 rad/s 2,674 rpm The larger wheel has to run slower, by about 670 rpm, to keep the same rim speed. Nothing about the machine changed; only the radius did. Do not calculate your way to a grinding-wheel speed. The number that governs is the maximum operating speed marked on the wheel, and OSHA requires that the spindle speed of the machine be checked before a wheel is mounted, to be certain it does not exceed that marking ( 29 CFR 1910.215(d)(1) ). The arithmetic above explains why a bigger wheel is marked slower. It does not authorise a wheel, and it is not a substitute for reading the wheel. See Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Rotation and Linear Speed","summary":"Turn a shaft speed into the speed of what it drives, and back again, using the radius - belts, rollers, wheels, and grinding wheels.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/rotation-and-linear-speed.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked Conversion factors from NIST SP 811, Appendix B: revolution per minute to radian per second 1.047 198 E&minus;01, inch to meter 2.54 E&minus;02 (exact), and mile per hour to meter per second 4.4704 E&minus;01 (exact). The grinding-wheel requirement is quoted from 29 CFR 1910.215(d)(1): \"The spindle speed of the machine shall be checked before mounting of the wheel to be certain that it does not exceed the maximum operating speed marked on the wheel.\""},
{"title":"A fastener holds parts together; a power screw moves a load","page":"Screw Threads","summary":"Choose a thread form for the load, read an inch or metric callout, and identify which way a screw advances.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/screw-threads.html#a-fastener-holds-parts-together-a-power-screw-moves-a-load","text":"A fastener holds parts together; a power screw moves a load A thread is a ridge wrapped around a cylinder. A matching internal thread in a nut follows that ridge. Relative rotation can produce straight-line travel along the screw's axis. A machine screw usually holds parts together. A lead screw uses the thread to position or drive a load: a slide, a vise jaw, or a linear actuator. Belts, chains and gears commonly transfer rotation between shafts; a lead screw converts rotation into linear motion. See Lead Screws for the calculations. Keep four descriptions separate: form is the thread's cross-sectional shape; series selects diameter and pitch combinations; hand determines direction; starts count the separate helices. A helix is the spiral path around the cylinder."},
{"title":"Match the thread form to fastening, repeated travel, or one-way thrust","page":"Screw Threads","summary":"Choose a thread form for the load, read an inch or metric callout, and identify which way a screw advances.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/screw-threads.html#match-the-thread-form-to-fastening-repeated-travel-or-one-way-thrust","text":"Match the thread form to fastening, repeated travel, or one-way thrust Four schematic axial profiles: square has parallel sides; V has steeply inclined sides; ACME and metric trapezoidal have flat crests and sloping sides; buttress has an asymmetric load flank and supporting flank. Profiles cut through the screw axis. These drawings compare shapes, not manufacturing dimensions. The load-bearing side of a thread is its flank. Thread-form trade-offs for sliding screw-and-nut contact. Form and use What it offers What it costs Square: power screws and jacks High efficiency relative to angled flanks under otherwise comparable conditions. Difficult, expensive machining; wear adjustment is difficult. V / machine-screw thread: bolts and fastening screws Widely available fasteners; mass production by rolling makes standard parts economical. Flank wedging increases friction, so it is a poor choice for efficient repeated power transmission. ACME / metric trapezoidal: vises, clamps and linear actuators Easier machining than square; a broader thread root provides a sturdier profile than a comparable square thread. Split-nut (an adjustable, divided nut) arrangements can take up clearance. Flank wedging increases friction relative to square. A precision drive screw can cost more than an ordinary stock fastener. Buttress: heavy axial thrust (push along the screw) predominantly in one axial direction, such as a screw press A nearly perpendicular working flank limits wedging in the intended load direction; the sloping back supports the thread. Its strength and efficiency advantages are directional. A special asymmetric profile and matching nut affect manufacturing and cost. No thread form wins every comparison. Actual strength also depends on diameter, pitch, engaged length, material and the failure being checked. ACME is not automatically stronger than every V thread. Nor does its name guarantee a larger lead. ACME and metric trapezoidal are related forms, not interchangeable parts. Why the efficiency and strength comparisons need conditions The IIT Kharagpur lesson Power Screw Drives and Their Efficiency (sections 6.1.1–6.1.2, hosted by IDC) explains square-thread machining cost, trapezoidal sturdiness, and added friction from inclined flanks. Its force model supports a conditional comparison: hold lead angle, contact friction and other losses comparable. It does not establish a universal ranking against V-thread fasteners. Pratt & Whitney's ACME explanation describes the manufacturing and split-nut advantages. IIT Kharagpur's thread manufacturing lesson explains fastener rolling and other production routes. For thread cutting on a machine tool, see Lathe Operations. The buttress working flank makes efficient thrust transmission possible in its intended direction; that is a geometry-based explanation, not a promise of the same measured efficiency as square. Reverse travel is possible, but heavy loading on the opposite flank does not retain the same advantages. Compare actual screw-and-nut ratings, not shape alone."},
{"title":"No radial wedging (outward force on the nut) does not mean no wear","page":"Screw Threads","summary":"Choose a thread form for the load, read an inch or metric callout, and identify which way a screw advances.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/screw-threads.html#no-radial-wedging-outward-force-on-the-nut-does-not-mean-no-wear","text":"No radial wedging (outward force on the nut) does not mean no wear In the ideal square-thread geometry, axial load does not create the outward wedging associated with angled flanks. That reduces a source of radial loading on the nut. The loaded surfaces still slide against each other. Friction, lubrication, materials, load and speed still affect wear. Wear can increase backlash: clearance that must be taken up after reversing direction before the load moves. An adjustable nut can reduce that clearance; adjustment does not restore lost material or guarantee longer life. Do not infer nut life from the thread profile alone. Where wear and operating limits enter the selection Thomson: Lead Screws Q&A — Application Considerations describes sliding-contact heat and wear and the relationship between load and speed. Its discussion also distinguishes the generic lead screw from the ACME form. The absence of radial wedging does not remove contact pressure on the working flank."},
{"title":"Inch callouts count threads; metric callouts state pitch","page":"Screw Threads","summary":"Choose a thread form for the load, read an inch or metric callout, and identify which way a screw advances.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/screw-threads.html#inch-callouts-count-threads-metric-callouts-state-pitch","text":"Inch callouts count threads; metric callouts state pitch Pitch is the axial distance between adjacent thread crests. Inch fastener callouts commonly give threads per inch (TPI), so pitch = 1 / TPI in inches. Metric callouts give pitch directly in millimetres. Selected callouts transcribed from checked public standard sources; pitches in the last column are calculated. Callout Read it as Pitch 1/4-20 UNC Nominal diameter 1/4 in; 20 threads per inch; Unified coarse series 1/20 = 0.050 in 1/4-28 UNF Nominal diameter 1/4 in; 28 threads per inch; Unified fine series 1/28 ≈ 0.03571 in M6 × 1.0 Metric thread; nominal diameter 6 mm; pitch 1.0 mm 1.0 mm Fine means a smaller pitch at the same nominal diameter. It does not mean a better-quality thread. Metric threads also have coarse and fine pitch choices. The 6 in M6 is a diameter, not a pitch; the 20 in 1/4-20 is a count per inch, not a lead. These are basic size-and-pitch callouts, not complete manufacturing specifications. Fit and tolerance information may follow them. A fastener-series label does not tell you the efficiency of a lead screw; for a multiple-start drive, obtain both pitch and starts, or the lead directly. The exact standard entries used here NBS Circular 479, Unified Screw Thread Standards, tables 4 and 6, prints the designation entries 1/4, 20, UNC-1A and 1/4, 28, UNF-1A. The examples above omit the class suffix to teach size and pitch. This historical public standard verifies those combinations; it is not a source of current production acceptance limits. The current Unified inch thread standard is ASME B1.1-2024. NAAMS Metric Fastener Standards, archived sheet F–2.1, dated 05/29/15, lists M6 x1.0 under screw size and M6 x 1.0 under nominal thread diameter and pitch. Only that designation is used here. ISO 261 identifies the general-purpose metric M-thread family; its catalog entry is not being used as a dimension table. Practice reading an inch callout"},
{"title":"Right-hand and left-hand threads advance in opposite directions","page":"Screw Threads","summary":"Choose a thread form for the load, read an inch or metric callout, and identify which way a screw advances.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/screw-threads.html#right-hand-and-left-hand-threads-advance-in-opposite-directions","text":"Right-hand and left-hand threads advance in opposite directions Fix the nut and look along the screw from the end you turn. Clockwise turns a right-hand screw away from you into the nut. The same clockwise rotation moves a left-hand screw toward you. Reverse the rotation and the travel reverses. With the nut fixed, clockwise rotation viewed from the driving end moves the right-hand screw away from the viewer and the left-hand screw toward the viewer. Same observer, same rotating member, same clockwise rotation. State all three before assigning a travel direction. If the screw is held against axial motion and the nut travels instead, the nut moves opposite to the screw travel just described. Handedness changes the direction, not the magnitude of travel per revolution. Direction reference IIT Kharagpur: Friction in Screws, opening page, defines right-handed advance using clockwise rotation away from the person turning the screw. This observer convention avoids ambiguous arrows. Next, distinguish pitch from lead in Lead Screws."},
{"title":"Series has one path; parallel has separate branches","page":"Series and Parallel Circuits","summary":"Trace current paths, combine resistances, and explore how opening a switch changes voltage, current and power.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/series-and-parallel-circuits.html#series-has-one-path-parallel-has-separate-branches","text":"Series has one path; parallel has separate branches In a series circuit, the same current passes through every element. The voltage drops around the loop add up to the source voltage. One break stops current everywhere in that loop. In a parallel circuit, each branch connects to the same two supply nodes (a node is an electrically connected junction), so each complete branch has the same voltage. The branch currents add up to the supply current. A break in one branch leaves the other branches working, provided the source and shared wiring remain intact. Comparison of two resistors in one series loop and two resistors in separate parallel branches across a DC source. Equal resistance gives equal drops in series and equal currents in parallel. Different resistances make the drops or currents different. The formulas for each individual resistor are on Current, Voltage, and Resistance."},
{"title":"Add resistances in series; add their reciprocals in parallel","page":"Series and Parallel Circuits","summary":"Trace current paths, combine resistances, and explore how opening a switch changes voltage, current and power.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/series-and-parallel-circuits.html#add-resistances-in-series-add-their-reciprocals-in-parallel","text":"Add resistances in series; add their reciprocals in parallel Equivalent resistance is the single resistance that would draw the same total current at the supply terminals. Series: R eq = R 1 + R 2 + R 3. Parallel: 1/R eq = 1/R 1 + 1/R 2 + 1/R 3. A reciprocal is 1 ÷ R: one divided by the resistance. Include only connected branches. Two parallel resistors: R eq = R 1 R 2 / (R 1 + R 2 ). Check the result: series resistance is greater than any one positive resistor; parallel resistance is smaller than the smallest connected resistor when two or more branches are connected. An open circuit has no conducting path and is represented by infinite equivalent resistance. Why the parallel formula uses reciprocals Each branch draws the supply voltage divided by its resistance. Add those branch currents, then divide by the common voltage. The result is a sum of 1/R terms. This reciprocal of resistance is called conductance: parallel branches add ways for current to flow."},
{"title":"Change a resistor or open a switch and predict what changes","page":"Series and Parallel Circuits","summary":"Trace current paths, combine resistances, and explore how opening a switch changes voltage, current and power.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/series-and-parallel-circuits.html#change-a-resistor-or-open-a-switch-and-predict-what-changes","text":"Change a resistor or open a switch and predict what changes Use this ideal DC model to compare two or three fixed resistors. Before moving a control, predict which current, voltage and power will change. Switches are ideal, wires have zero resistance and the source holds its set voltage. Static example: a 15 V source and 30 &Omega; and 60 &Omega; resistors, with both switches closed. The same resistors in two connections Connection Equivalent resistance Total current R1: current / voltage / power R2: current / voltage / power Series 90 &Omega; 0.1667 A 0.1667 A / 5 V / 0.8333 W 0.1667 A / 10 V / 1.667 W Parallel 20 &Omega; 0.75 A 0.5 A / 15 V / 7.5 W 0.25 A / 15 V / 3.75 W Opening either series switch makes both resistor currents and both resistor voltage drops zero. Opening the R1 parallel switch makes R1 current and power zero while R2 still draws 0.25 A. The voltage across the whole open branch remains 15 V; it falls across the open switch, not the resistor. The live table reports voltage across each resistor, excluding its switch. In an open series loop the source voltage is across the break or breaks. With several open switches, this ideal model does not determine how that voltage divides between the breaks."},
{"title":"Power explains the bulb comparison, within the model","page":"Series and Parallel Circuits","summary":"Trace current paths, combine resistances, and explore how opening a switch changes voltage, current and power.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/series-and-parallel-circuits.html#power-explains-the-bulb-comparison-within-the-model","text":"Power explains the bulb comparison, within the model For fixed resistors in series, the larger resistance dissipates more power because both carry the same current. In parallel, the smaller resistance dissipates more power because both have the same voltage. Check the power column rather than assuming the same bulb wins in both arrangements. Greater electrical power is a useful brightness comparison for otherwise comparable incandescent bulbs. Real filaments change resistance as they heat, and LED lamps contain additional circuitry. The fixed-resistor model is not a universal lamp-brightness predictor. Build another circuit in PhET's Circuit Construction Kit: DC – Virtual Lab. Try an unequal pair of resistors, predict the effect of a break, and then test your prediction. Add a series-current problem to practice"},
{"title":"Locate the shaft at one end and allow growth at the other","page":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","summary":"Locate one end, allow thermal movement at the other, and distinguish end play from preload.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/shaft-support-and-adjustment.html#locate-the-shaft-at-one-end-and-allow-growth-at-the-other","text":"Locate the shaft at one end and allow growth at the other In a locating/non-locating arrangement, also called fixed/floating here, both supports carry radial load. The locating support also carries the applied axial load and establishes the shaft's axial position. The non-locating support allows axial movement as the shaft expands relative to the housing. A locating bearing on the left has axial stops; the radial support on the right has room to move along the housing as the shaft grows. “Fixed” means axially located, not prevented from rotating. “Floating” means axial travel, not loose radial support. Location needs a load path from shaft to bearing to housing: shoulders, suitable spacers, nuts, retaining rings or covers can retain the rings. Which end is fixed follows the required position of the driven parts; it is not automatically the left end or the motor end. The floating end can accommodate movement inside a suitable bearing, such as an NU cylindrical roller bearing, or by controlled sliding of a ring in its seat. Do not loosen an arbitrary fit: see Tolerances and Fits and the bearing maker's mounting guidance. Why two locating supports can create unwanted load A warming shaft wants to lengthen. If a housing prevents that length change, elastic deformation creates axial reactions. These can consume clearance, increase friction and cause heating. Actual force depends on shaft, bearing and housing stiffness, temperatures and initial clearance. A floating seat also has friction; “radial only” describes its intended support duty, not literally zero incidental axial force."},
{"title":"Heat the shaft and compare free travel with restraint","page":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","summary":"Locate one end, allow thermal movement at the other, and distinguish end play from preload.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/shaft-support-and-adjustment.html#heat-the-shaft-and-compare-free-travel-with-restraint","text":"Heat the shaft and compare free travel with restraint Support arrangement Left locating / right floating Both ends axially located Heating level: 50% Available floating travel Enough for the full heating range Limited to 40% of maximum free growth Static comparison: at half the heating range, the shaft wants half the model's maximum free growth. A floating support with sufficient travel accommodates all of it. Two locating supports prevent it. With travel limited to 40%, half-range heating leaves 10% of maximum growth unaccommodated. The drawing exaggerates growth. Heating level is a relative teaching scale, not a temperature or a material property. The left support and housing stay in place; only the shaft is heated. The dashed outline shows the length the shaft would reach if free. The solid outline shows the ideal constrained length. This model shows incompatible motion, not a binding force, stress or safe operating limit. The live controls require JavaScript; the comparison and shaft diagram remain available."},
{"title":"End play is free movement; preload is an initial internal load","page":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","summary":"Locate one end, allow thermal movement at the other, and distinguish end play from preload.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/shaft-support-and-adjustment.html#end-play-is-free-movement-preload-is-an-initial-internal-load","text":"End play is free movement; preload is an initial internal load End play is the axial free movement available before the bearing arrangement takes up its clearance. Preload is a deliberately applied internal load before the external working load arrives. Reducing end play to a specified value and applying preload are different adjustments. Do not tighten a bearing until it merely “feels solid.” Excess preload increases friction and temperature. Some arrangements require positive operating clearance; others use specified preload for stiffness or running accuracy. Temperature and fits change the operating condition. A shim is a thin, accurately sized spacer. Changing its thickness sets a position in a rigid stack. Whether adding a shim increases or decreases end play depends on where the shim sits and which faces it separates. Avoid burrs and rubbing contact that could release debris. Use the material and placement specified for the assembly."},
{"title":"A spring maintains force over travel; a shim sets distance","page":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","summary":"Locate one end, allow thermal movement at the other, and distinguish end play from preload.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/shaft-support-and-adjustment.html#a-spring-maintains-force-over-travel-a-shim-sets-distance","text":"A spring maintains force over travel; a shim sets distance A shim sets distance and a spring maintains force. A Belleville washer is a conical disc spring. A wave washer has an undulating shape that compresses axially. Both can apply preload when selected for the required force and working deflection. A spring can accommodate small dimensional changes while maintaining load. “Very light load” is not a universal specification. Use the bearing's required preload and the spring's load-deflection data; an ordinary lock washer is not a calibrated preload spring. Position preload versus spring preload Position preload sets the bearing rings at a fixed relative distance, for example with shims. Compared with that arrangement, a spring allows more axial movement under an added load. The spring can maintain preload as dimensions change, but the shaft position changes more under load. Identify which support locates the shaft and trace its axial load path. Find where the other support permits thermal movement and check available travel. Read the specified end play or preload and its measurement conditions. Choose the prescribed shim or spring arrangement and verify the final measured setting. Manufacturer reading and open material questions Further reading: SKF Railway Technical Handbook, bearing installation, describes both internal displacement and ring-seat sliding. NSK: Preload shows position adjustment using shims. Smalley: Bearing Preload covers wave springs. SPIROL: Precision Shims lists both stainless steel and brass among available materials. No manufacturer restriction confining brass shims to a nonrotating ring was verified. Material and mounting instructions must come from the actual assembly documentation. Continue with Linear Bearing Guides or practice locating a shaft."},
{"title":"Solder melts; the parts being joined do not","page":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","summary":"Choose solder, flux, and a tip that transfers heat, then prepare a stable bench and a tinned iron.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-equipment.html#solder-melts-the-parts-being-joined-do-not","text":"Solder melts; the parts being joined do not Soldering joins metal parts with a filler metal that melts below the parts' melting temperatures. It is closer to brazing than welding: the base metals do not melt. Electrical soldering connects wires, printed circuit boards (PCBs), and electronic components. A good connection must be mechanically secure and conduct electricity. The molten solder must wet both surfaces: spread onto and bond with them, rather than sit on top as a ball. Clean surfaces, flux, and heat at both parts make that possible. Start here, then follow Making and Inspecting Solder Joints and Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs."},
{"title":"Choose the tip for contact, not just access","page":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","summary":"Choose solder, flux, and a tip that transfers heat, then prepare a stable bench and a tinned iron.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-equipment.html#choose-the-tip-for-contact-not-just-access","text":"Choose the tip for contact, not just access A temperature-controlled station regulates the iron's tip temperature. A basic iron may have no temperature control; a cordless iron changes how it is powered, not how a joint is made. Check the tool's instructions and choose settings suitable for the solder and components. Tip shape and size matter. A fine point reaches small spaces, but a chisel's broad face often transfers heat more effectively. Use the largest tinned contact face that fits the joint without touching neighboring connections. Interchangeable tips let one iron handle different work; change them according to the tool's instructions. What the iron's “sweet spot” means Use the broad, clean, solder-coated working face of the tip against both parts. Do not interpret “side of the iron” as the barrel or an uncoated surface. The useful contact area, not the sharpest point or the highest temperature setting, delivers heat into the joint. A tiny point can be hot and still transfer heat poorly."},
{"title":"Match the solder and flux to electrical work","page":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","summary":"Choose solder, flux, and a tip that transfers heat, then prepare a stable bench and a tinned iron.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-equipment.html#match-the-solder-and-flux-to-electrical-work","text":"Match the solder and flux to electrical work Leaded solder: common tin-lead alloys melt at lower temperatures than common tin-copper or tin-silver-copper lead-free alloys. Leaded solder is still sold, but lead is restricted in many products. Follow the specified alloy for the work. Lead-free solder: common electrical alloys use tin with copper and/or silver. They may need a different heating process and can solidify with a duller finish. “Lead-free” does not mean “no fumes.” Diameter: fine solder wire makes it easier to meter a small amount into a small joint. Thick solder feeds more metal for the same length; it is easy to overfill a small pad. Flux: helps remove surface oxides during heating so solder can wet the metal. Rosin-core solder carries flux inside the wire; additional compatible electronics flux may help. Do not substitute plumbing acid flux. Read the alloy and flux label, including any residue-cleaning instructions. See Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets for handling information. Practice choosing solder"},
{"title":"Support the work and control the heat and fume","page":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","summary":"Choose solder, flux, and a tip that transfers heat, then prepare a stable bench and a tinned iron.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-equipment.html#support-the-work-and-control-the-heat-and-fume","text":"Support the work and control the heat and fume Helping hands or a PCB vise hold the parts still while your hands hold the iron and solder. Keep a proper stand or cradle within reach. Use a damp soldering sponge or brass wool for tip cleaning, and have solder wick (copper braid) or a desoldering pump available for removing excess solder. It gets hot. Really hot. Park the iron in its stand, and allow the work to cool before touching it. Use ventilation or fume extraction that keeps the plume away from your face and other people; most visible fume comes from flux. Wash your hands after handling leaded solder, and do not eat or drink at the bench. SHOP RULE: NEVER SOLDER BATTERIES. Why the battery rule stays absolute Heat applied to a cell can damage its seals and internal parts. Lithium cells in particular can fail dangerously if damaged. Use the intended battery holder or connector instead of applying a soldering iron to a battery. Work on disconnected circuits. Follow Electrical Safety and Personal Protective Equipment; flux and fume handling belongs with Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets. Further reading on flux fume The UK Health and Safety Executive's guidance for solderers describes rosin-based flux fume and recommends extraction and keeping your head out of the plume."},
{"title":"Clean, tin, and keep the working face coated","page":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","summary":"Choose solder, flux, and a tip that transfers heat, then prepare a stable bench and a tinned iron.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-equipment.html#clean-tin-and-keep-the-working-face-coated","text":"Clean, tin, and keep the working face coated With the iron at working temperature, briefly clean the tip on brass wool or a damp soldering sponge. Apply fresh solder to coat its working face, then remove the excess on the cleaner. Leave a thin coating; do not wipe it bare and let it sit hot. Re-tin as needed and before storage as directed by the tool manufacturer. Three stages: clean the working face, melt fresh solder onto it, and leave a thin solder coating after removing excess. Why tinning helps heat transfer A hot tip oxidizes. Oxide interferes with wetting, while a solder coating protects the working face and helps make thermal contact with the joint. Tinning the tip is the time to feed solder onto the tip. Making a joint is different: feed solder into the heated joint. See HAKKO's tip-life guidance for the construction of plated tips and the effects of oxidation."},
{"title":"Heat both parts, then feed solder into the joint","page":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","summary":"Heat both parts, feed solder into the joint, let it cool without movement, and inspect the bond rather than its shine alone.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-joints.html#heat-both-parts-then-feed-solder-into-the-joint","text":"Heat both parts, then feed solder into the joint Prepare the iron and bench using Soldering Equipment and Setup. The iron heats the parts; the heated parts melt the solder. Touch the tinned working face to both wires, or to both the pad and the component lead. Feed solder into the joint, away from the iron's contact point when access allows. First the iron contacts both pad and lead. Then solder wire approaches the heated joint from the other side while the iron stays in contact. Finally remove solder, remove iron, and hold still. A one-second count is a rhythm, not a temperature test “Apply iron, one-second count, apply solder” reminds you to heat before feeding. The actual time depends on the joint, tip, alloy, and component limits. Look for solder flowing onto both surfaces. If it will not flow, stop and check cleanliness, flux, contact, and tool settings rather than holding the iron there indefinitely."},
{"title":"Work through the joint in this order","page":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","summary":"Heat both parts, feed solder into the joint, let it cool without movement, and inspect the bond rather than its shine alone.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-joints.html#work-through-the-joint-in-this-order","text":"Work through the joint in this order 1. Strip and clean. Expose only the conductor needed for the connection without nicking it. Clean the surfaces to be joined. 2. Support the parts. Form the connection and secure it so it cannot move during soldering. 3. Flux if needed. Use flux compatible with the solder and electrical work. 4. Heat BOTH parts. Put the tinned working face of the iron against both parts at the joint. 5. Feed solder into the heated joint. Let it wet both surfaces. Do not melt a blob on the iron and wipe it onto cold parts. 6. Remove the solder, then the iron. Stop feeding once the connection has enough solder and both surfaces are wetted. 7. Hold still while it cools. Let the solder solidify without moving the connection. 8. Inspect. Check wetting, coverage, clearance from adjacent connections, and damage before powering the circuit. Practice the sequence"},
{"title":"Look for wetting and shape; shine alone is not a verdict","page":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","summary":"Heat both parts, feed solder into the joint, let it cool without movement, and inspect the bond rather than its shine alone.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-joints.html#look-for-wetting-and-shape-shine-alone-is-not-a-verdict","text":"Look for wetting and shape; shine alone is not a verdict A good joint flows onto both surfaces. On a typical through-hole pad, look for a smooth transition from pad to lead, often a concave fillet (an inward-curving slope). Tin-lead joints are often shiny. A sound lead-free joint may be dull; dullness alone does not diagnose a cold joint. Why lead-free solder may look different Kester's soldering FAQ, under lead-free joint appearance, explains that tin-silver-copper joints can have a dull surface without being poor joints. Judge the connection, not shine alone. Conceptual cross-sections: wetted solder slopes from pad to lead; a cold poorly wetted bead sits on the pad; a solder bridge connects two pads that should be separate. Cold or poorly wetted Solder balls up or fails to bond to one surface, often because the parts were not heated adequately. A rough or dull appearance can be a clue, but inspect the contact and shape too. Disturbed The connection moved while solidifying, leaving a cracked or disrupted joint. Secure it before reworking. Too much solder A large mound hides the connection and makes bridges easier to miss. Remove excess so the joint can be inspected. Too little solder The intended connection lacks coverage or a continuous bond. Reheat both surfaces and add only what is needed. Bridge Solder joins connections that should be electrically separate. Remove it before applying power. Practice joint inspection"},
{"title":"Correct the cause before reheating","page":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","summary":"Heat both parts, feed solder into the joint, let it cool without movement, and inspect the bond rather than its shine alone.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-joints.html#correct-the-cause-before-reheating","text":"Correct the cause before reheating Keep the circuit disconnected. Secure loose parts, clean and re-tin the tip, and use compatible flux if needed. Reheat both surfaces to remake a poorly wetted or disturbed joint. Use wick or a pump to remove excess solder or a bridge; do not pry at a pad while the solder is solid. Repeated heating can damage components or lift copper pads from the board. If a joint still will not wet, let it cool and get help finding the cause. Inspect again after repair. A continuity check can help find an open connection or an unintended short, but it does not prove mechanical strength. See Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs for splice support and through-hole work."},
{"title":"Make the splice mechanically sound before soldering","page":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","summary":"Form a supported wire splice, insulate it with heat-shrink, or heat a circuit-board pad and lead together for a through-hole connection.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-wires-and-pcbs.html#make-the-splice-mechanically-sound-before-soldering","text":"Make the splice mechanically sound before soldering Solder should not be the only thing holding loose wires in position. Form a stable splice, support it during soldering, and provide strain relief so pulling or bending the cable does not load the soldered joint. Follow the wire and assembly requirements; these sketches show splice shapes, not a qualification standard. Lap splice overlaps straight stripped ends; twist splice winds two ends together into a pigtail; Western Union splice wraps each free end around the opposite standing wire. Lay-over or lap: stripped ends overlap alongside one another. Support and strain relief are especially important because the overlap does not lock itself together. Twist or pigtail: stripped ends twist together and leave the connection on the same side. Cover the joined end completely when insulating it. Western Union: an inline connection with each free end wrapped around the opposite wire. It is a commonly used, strong splice when properly formed; it is not automatically the best choice for every wire or assembly. For conductor sizing, see Wire and Wire Gauge. Use the heating and inspection method on Making and Inspecting Solder Joints. Practice identifying splices"},
{"title":"Put heat-shrink on before closing the splice","page":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","summary":"Form a supported wire splice, insulate it with heat-shrink, or heat a circuit-board pad and lead together for a through-hole connection.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-wires-and-pcbs.html#put-heat-shrink-on-before-closing-the-splice","text":"Put heat-shrink on before closing the splice Slide suitable heat-shrink tubing onto a wire before joining it, and keep it away from the soldering heat. After soldering, allow the joint to cool and inspect it. Move the tubing over all exposed conductor, overlapping intact insulation at both ends of an inline splice, then shrink it with the appropriate heat source according to its instructions. Choose tubing whose unshrunk size passes over the splice and whose recovered size fits the wire. Avoid sharp wire ends that could puncture it. Ordinary heat-shrink provides insulation; do not assume it makes a waterproof connection or replaces strain relief. When pre-tinning helps Some wires and terminals are pre-tinned: a small amount of solder wets the surface before final assembly. This can help the final joint flow, but too much solder makes a wire end stiff and bulky. Follow the connection's assembly method and still heat both parts when making the final bond."},
{"title":"On a PCB, heat the copper pad and component lead together","page":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","summary":"Form a supported wire splice, insulate it with heat-shrink, or heat a circuit-board pad and lead together for a through-hole connection.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/soldering-wires-and-pcbs.html#on-a-pcb-heat-the-copper-pad-and-component-lead-together","text":"On a PCB, heat the copper pad and component lead together A pad is the exposed copper connection area on a printed circuit board. A component's lead is its metal leg. In through-hole assembly, the lead passes through a hole in the board. Insert the correct component in the correct orientation and support the board and component before heating. Place the tinned working face against both the pad and lead. Feed solder into the heated connection and let it flow onto both, then remove solder and iron in that order. Hold still while it cools. Inspect the joint and the gaps to neighboring pads. Where the component's assembly instructions call for trimming, cut the excess lead above the cooled joint without cutting into the solder fillet. Wear the required eye protection and control the clipped end so it does not fly away or remain on the board. Do not shorten connector pins that must still mate with another connector. Soldering header pins is one bridge to Microcontroller Basics. A stray bridge changes the intended circuit connections; review Series and Parallel Circuits when tracing those paths. Practice through-hole technique"},
{"title":"Torque is force applied at a radius","page":"Torque","summary":"Turning effort is force times radius - how to calculate it, which unit belongs to it, and why the order of the two words is not decoration.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/torque.html#torque-is-force-applied-at-a-radius","text":"Torque is force applied at a radius A force pushes. A torque turns. The same force produces more torque the further it acts from the axis of rotation, which is why a longer wrench loosens a tighter nut with the same pull. &tau; = rF - torque ( &tau;, tau) equals radius times force. Torque on a shaft A circle represents a pulley on a shaft, with a dot at its centre. A dashed line runs from the centre out to the rim, labelled r for radius. At the rim, an arrow points along the tangent, at right angles to that line, labelled F for force. The turning effort produced is the radius multiplied by the force. r F &#964; = rF The force acts at right angles to the radius. Everything below assumes that; a force pointing partly along the radius contributes only the part of it that is perpendicular. r is the radius, measured from the axis of rotation to where the force acts. Pulleys, rollers, and wheels are specified by diameter, so halve the number on the drawing before it goes into this formula. Note: when a turning effort is described about an axis rather than about a shaft, it is often called a moment and given the symbol M. It is the same calculation."},
{"title":"The unit, and the word order that identifies it","page":"Torque","summary":"Turning effort is force times radius - how to calculate it, which unit belongs to it, and why the order of the two words is not decoration.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/torque.html#the-unit-and-the-word-order-that-identifies-it","text":"The unit, and the word order that identifies it Torque units and rounded conversion factors between them. System Unit Symbol Conversion SI newton metre N-m 1 N-m = 0.7375621 lbf-ft Foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second pound-force foot lbf-ft 1 lbf-ft = 1.355818 N-m Case matters, and this one bites. The SI torque unit is N-m with a lower-case m. Written N-M it is not a valid SI torque unit: M is the prefix for 10 6, but a prefix cannot stand alone as a unit. The same trap catches Kg for kg and S for s. See Measurement Language. Here torque is written lbf-ft; energy is written ft-lbf. The same two units, the same numerical factor, written in the opposite order - and NIST prints them that way round on purpose: pound-force foot converts to the newton metre, foot pound-force converts to the joule. They are different physical quantities that happen to share a unit combination. Word order is a convention, not a mathematical distinction; use the quantity and context to tell them apart. SI avoids the ambiguity by giving energy its own name, the joule; a torque is never written in joules. See Work and Energy."},
{"title":"Worked example: can the motor hold the arm up?","page":"Torque","summary":"Turning effort is force times radius - how to calculate it, which unit belongs to it, and why the order of the two words is not decoration.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/torque.html#worked-example-can-the-motor-hold-the-arm-up","text":"Worked example: can the motor hold the arm up? A robot-arm joint motor produces 15 N-m. The arm segment is 0.35 m long and carries a 25 N load at its end. Will the motor hold the arm horizontal? The load acts straight down, at right angles to a horizontal arm, at the full 0.35 m: &tau; = 0.35 m &times; 25 N = 8.75 N-m. Compare with what the motor produces: 8.75 N-m &lt; 15 N-m. Yes, with 6.25 N-m to spare - for the load alone. The weight of the arm segment itself also produces a torque about the joint, and no mass was given for it, so this answer is a partial one. In a real selection you would add the arm weight acting at its own centre of mass, then keep margin on top of that. This is the shape of most torque questions: work out what the load demands, then check it against what the drive can supply, and be explicit about what you have left out."},
{"title":"Worked example: torque from a tangential force","page":"Torque","summary":"Turning effort is force times radius - how to calculate it, which unit belongs to it, and why the order of the two words is not decoration.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/torque.html#worked-example-torque-from-a-tangential-force","text":"Worked example: torque from a tangential force A wheel 250 mm in diameter needs 3.5 kN of tangential force to overcome resistance. What torque must the motor supply to the axle? r = 125 mm = 0.125 m. 3.5 kN = 3,500 N. &tau; = 0.125 m &times; 3,500 N = 437.5 N-m. The same formula, rearranged When the torque is what you know and the force is what you want, F = &tau;/r. That single rearrangement explains a family of everyday machines: a longer wrench, a bigger steering wheel, and a larger pulley all trade radius against force. Double the radius and the same torque delivers half the force - and, at the same rotational speed, the rim travels twice as far, which is why Power and Efficiency comes out unchanged."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Torque","summary":"Turning effort is force times radius - how to calculate it, which unit belongs to it, and why the order of the two words is not decoration.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/torque.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked NIST SP 811, Appendix B lists pound-force foot ( lbf-ft ) to newton meter as 1.355 818 E+00, and separately lists foot pound-force ( ft-lbf ) to joule as the same 1.355 818 E+00 - the two entries that establish the word-order convention used above. The reciprocal, 0.7375621, gives newton metres to pound-force feet. That M is the prefix symbol for 10 6 and m the symbol for the metre is from the prefix and base-unit tables in NIST SP 811, Chapter 4."},
{"title":"The conductor carries current; insulation separates conductors","page":"Wire and Wire Gauge","summary":"Separate the conductor from its insulation, and understand what a wire gauge does and does not tell you.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/wire-and-wire-gauge.html#the-conductor-carries-current-insulation-separates-conductors","text":"The conductor carries current; insulation separates conductors The metal inside a wire is the conductor. Its resistance depends on material, length, cross-sectional area and temperature. The covering is insulation: it keeps the conductor from contacting other conductors or surrounding objects. The outside diameter includes that covering and does not by itself tell you how much metal is inside. Solid wire has one solid conductor; stranded wire has several smaller strands forming a conductor. Insulation type and condition matter separately from the metal size. See Electrical Safety for use and inspection guidance."},
{"title":"A smaller AWG number means a thicker conductor","page":"Wire and Wire Gauge","summary":"Separate the conductor from its insulation, and understand what a wire gauge does and does not tell you.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/wire-and-wire-gauge.html#a-smaller-awg-number-means-a-thicker-conductor","text":"A smaller AWG number means a thicker conductor American Wire Gauge (AWG) uses smaller numbers for larger conductor cross sections. For the same material, length and temperature, a thicker conductor has less resistance. See Adafruit’s wire-gauge explanation. Under comparable installation and temperature conditions, it can carry more current without exceeding its temperature limit. Ampacity means the allowable current under specified conditions. Gauge alone is not an ampacity rating: material, insulation temperature rating, ambient temperature, bundling and installation all matter. Use the applicable manufacturer data and installation requirements for a real selection; this page is not a wire-sizing table. IEWC explains these ampacity factors. Choosing a wire involves voltage drop as well as allowable current. Why a long wire can matter even below its current limit A wire can stay within its temperature limit yet drop too much voltage for its load. Length increases resistance, and both voltage drop and heating follow from that resistance. The worked cord example in Current, Voltage, and Resistance shows the calculation."},
{"title":"Count the return wire: explore voltage drop and heating","page":"Wire and Wire Gauge","summary":"Separate the conductor from its insulation, and understand what a wire gauge does and does not tell you.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/wire-and-wire-gauge.html#count-the-return-wire-explore-voltage-drop-and-heating","text":"Count the return wire: explore voltage drop and heating A two-conductor circuit carries current out and back. Use twice the one-way run for its wire resistance. At the same current, longer or thinner wire drops more voltage and wastes more power as heat. Voltage drop is a separate check from ampacity: get the current rating from the applicable code table or manufacturer for the insulation and installation. Out: 50 ft Return: 50 ft Source Load Loop = 30.48 m AWG 18 · 5 A AWG 18, 50 ft one way, 5 A, 24 V source Single wire: Ω / 1000 ft 6.385 Single wire: Ω / km 20.95 Loop resistance (Ω) 0.6385 Voltage drop (V) 3.192 Source voltage lost (%) 13.3 Load voltage (V) 20.81 Wire heating loss (W) 15.96 Worked example: 50 ft out + 50 ft back = 30.48 m of copper. R = ρL/A = 0.6385 Ω. At 5 A, IR = 3.192 V and I²R = 15.96 W. Voltage drop: baseline 3.192 V; now 6.385 V Wire heating loss: baseline 15.96 W; now 63.85 W Outline = baseline; filled = now. Each pair shares its own scale; compare within a pair. Loss is heating power, not wire temperature. Doubling only current doubles the drop and quadruples heating loss. Doubling only length doubles both; a smaller AWG number lowers both at the same current. The worked table and comparison above work without JavaScript. Enable JavaScript to vary the inputs. This model assumes solid copper at 20 °C, equal outgoing and return wires, and DC/resistive behavior. Current is an input, not a prediction of how a particular load responds. Heating loss is not a temperature prediction; copper resistance rises with temperature (approx. 0.393%/°C near 20 °C). Connections and AC reactance are omitted. Definitions used to calculate every result For AWG number n, diameter d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm. Convert d to meters, then A = πd²/4 in m². Using annealed-copper resistivity ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m at 20 °C, R = ρL/A. One foot is exactly 0.3048 m. With one-way run ℓ, L = 2ℓ and R loop = ρ(2ℓ)/A. V drop = IR loop; percent drop = 100V drop /V source; V load = V source − V drop; wire loss P = I²R loop. A two-wire single-phase resistive approximation uses the same return path, but this model does not include AC effects. Practice: Loop resistance · Voltage drop · Percent drop · Load voltage · Choose a gauge."},
{"title":"Work is a force moving something through a distance","page":"Work and Energy","summary":"Force moving through a distance is work - the joule, the foot pound-force, and why an energy unit reads backwards from a torque unit.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/work-and-energy.html#work-is-a-force-moving-something-through-a-distance","text":"Work is a force moving something through a distance Holding a heavy box still is exhausting, and in this sense it is zero work. Work needs a component of force along the displacement; work is negative when that component opposes the movement. W = Fs - work equals force times displacement for a constant force parallel to the motion. Work is one of the ways energy moves from one place to another, so it is measured in energy units and is often written E when what matters is the energy delivered rather than the act of delivering it. The two systems, for work and every other form of energy. System Unit Symbol Built from SI joule J 1 J = 1 N-m Foot&ndash;slug&ndash;second foot pound-force ft-lbf 1 ft-lbf = 1.355818 J Worked example: lifting a load How much work is required to lift a 2,000 lb load to a height of 10 ft? The force needed to lift steadily is the load's weight, 2,000 lbf, and it acts straight up along the 10 ft of travel. W = 2,000 lbf &times; 10 ft = 20,000 ft-lbf. In SI: 20,000 ft-lbf &times; 1.355818 J per ft-lbf = 27,116 J, or 27.1 kJ. Two things this calculation assumes, both worth saying out loud: the load is raised at a steady speed, so no extra force goes into accelerating it, and the 2,000 lb figure is a weight in pound-force, not a mass in pound-mass. On Geometry, Mass, and Force those are separate quantities, and here it is the force that belongs in the formula."},
{"title":"Work and torque share dimensions - word order is a convention","page":"Work and Energy","summary":"Force moving through a distance is work - the joule, the foot pound-force, and why an energy unit reads backwards from a torque unit.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/work-and-energy.html#work-and-torque-share-dimensions-word-order-is-a-convention","text":"Work and torque share dimensions - word order is a convention Multiply a constant force by displacement along that force and you get work. Multiply a force by a radius and you get torque. Both are a force times a length, so in the foot&ndash;pound system both come out as some arrangement of feet and pounds-force. Here torque is written lbf-ft. Energy is written ft-lbf. The numerical factor is identical - 1.355818 of either - but word order alone does not establish the physical quantity. Torque describes turning effort; work describes energy transferred through displacement. SI removes the ambiguity by naming the energy unit separately. A torque stays N-m; energy becomes the joule. Numerically 1 J = 1 N-m, but a torque is never reported in joules. See Torque. Turning a torque into work, and where the radian goes A constant torque about a fixed axis that turns through an angle does work: W = &tau;&theta;, with the angle in radians. Because the radian is a ratio of two lengths, NIST gives its base-unit expression as m/m and its value as 1. Nothing is added to the units, so: 1 J = 1 N-m (torque) &times; 1 rad - 1 ft-lbf = 1 lbf-ft (torque) &times; 1 rad This is the practical payoff of the radian being dimensionless: a motor rated in N-m turning through an angle in rad gives you joules directly, with no factor to look up. Use degrees or revolutions instead and the relationship breaks, because both of those carry a chosen count inside them."},
{"title":"The energy units you will actually meet","page":"Work and Energy","summary":"Force moving through a distance is work - the joule, the foot pound-force, and why an energy unit reads backwards from a torque unit.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/work-and-energy.html#the-energy-units-you-will-actually-meet","text":"The energy units you will actually meet Energy is billed, rated, and specified in a lot of different units. They all convert; the only work is knowing which one a number is in. Common energy units, expressed in joules. Unit Symbol In joules Where it turns up joule J 1 J The SI unit. Everything else converts to it. foot pound-force ft-lbf 1.355818 J U.S. mechanical work and energy. watt hour W-h 3,600 J (exact) Battery capacity. kilowatt hour kW-h 3,600,000 J (exact) The line on an electricity bill. British thermal unit (IT) Btu IT 1,055.056 J Heating, cooling, and fuel ratings. horsepower hour hp-h &asymp; 2,684,520 J Engine and drive energy. calorie (IT) cal IT 4.1868 J (exact) Thermal work and material data. A kilowatt hour is a power multiplied by a time, and that is all it is. One kilowatt sustained for one hour. It is not an SI unit - NIST lists it among the units to be converted into SI - but it is exactly 3.6 MJ, and it is the unit an electricity meter counts in. \"Btu\" on its own is ambiguous. NIST lists six different British thermal units, from 1,054.35 J to 1,059.67 J depending on which definition is meant. The spread is about half a percent, which is small until it is multiplied by a fuel bill. Use Btu IT unless a specification names another one."},
{"title":"Sources","page":"Work and Energy","summary":"Force moving through a distance is work - the joule, the foot pound-force, and why an energy unit reads backwards from a torque unit.","collection":"Engineering Fundamentals","url":"engineering/work-and-energy.html#sources","text":"Sources Where these values were checked All conversions from NIST SP 811, Appendix B: foot pound-force to joule 1.355 818 E+00; watt hour to joule 3.6 E+03 (exact); kilowatt hour to joule 3.6 E+06 (exact); British thermal unit IT to joule 1.055 056 E+03, with Btu th 1.054 350 E+03, Btu (mean) 1.055 87 E+03, Btu (39 &deg;F) 1.059 67 E+03, Btu (59 &deg;F) 1.054 80 E+03 and Btu (60 &deg;F) 1.054 68 E+03 listed separately; calorie IT to joule 4.1868 E+00 (exact). The horsepower hour figure is derived rather than quoted: one horsepower is 7.456 999 E+02 watts in the same table, and an hour is 3,600 s, so 745.6999 W &times; 3,600 s = 2,684,520 J to six figures. NIST SP 811, Chapter 4, Table 3, gives the joule as J, expressed as N-m and in base units as m 2 -kg-s &minus;2, and the radian as rad, value 1, base-unit expression m/m."},
{"title":"Build the part by adding layers","page":"3D Printing from CAD","summary":"Export a solid model, choose how its layers carry load, and inspect the sliced toolpaths before printing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/3d-printing-from-cad.html#build-the-part-by-adding-layers","text":"Build the part by adding layers Additive manufacturing makes a shape by adding material. In fused deposition modeling (FDM), a printer feeds thermoplastic through a heated nozzle and deposits it along paths, much like a controlled hot-glue gun. A slicer divides the model into horizontal cross sections and plans the paths for each layer. The nozzle and build surface move relative to one another to build the next layer. A CAD model describes the desired shape; the sliced file describes how a particular printer will make it. This page focuses on filament printing. Resin printing uses a different process and requires its own preparation, handling, and finishing procedures."},
{"title":"Export the model, then check its size in the slicer","page":"3D Printing from CAD","summary":"Export a solid model, choose how its layers carry load, and inspect the sliced toolpaths before printing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/3d-printing-from-cad.html#export-the-model-then-check-its-size-in-the-slicer","text":"Export the model, then check its size in the slicer Finish and save the editable CAD model. Check that the intended part is a closed solid; see Feature-based Modeling. Export the intended body as STL or 3MF if supported by both programs. Inspect curved surfaces for coarse facets. Import into the slicer and compare a known dimension with the CAD model. Check units, scale, and the number of bodies. Select the actual printer, nozzle, and approved material profile. Do not assume settings imported with a file match the available machine. Orient the part, set walls, infill, layer height, and supports, then slice and inspect the layer preview. Export the printer's required job format using the posted procedure. Save the slicer project so you can revise the setup. STL and 3MF carry different information STL represents the surface with triangles and does not record a unit of length. A 3MF file can carry units and multiple objects; a slicer's 3MF project can also carry settings. Support for those extra settings varies between applications. Neither filename alone proves a job is ready for your printer. Prusa documents saving projects as 3MF and its supported file formats."},
{"title":"Use walls for the shell, infill for the interior, and supports where needed","page":"3D Printing from CAD","summary":"Export a solid model, choose how its layers carry load, and inspect the sliced toolpaths before printing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/3d-printing-from-cad.html#use-walls-for-the-shell-infill-for-the-interior-and-supports-where-needed","text":"Use walls for the shell, infill for the interior, and supports where needed Walls, also called perimeters, trace the outside and hole boundaries. Infill fills some of the interior. Solid top and bottom layers close the part. Increasing infill is not a substitute for choosing suitable walls and orientation. Layer height sets the thickness of each deposited layer. Smaller layers can reduce the steps on sloped surfaces but usually add print time. They do not by themselves make a thin wall or tiny hole printable: nozzle size and extrusion width matter too. Supports are temporary material beneath regions that cannot print reliably in the chosen orientation. They use material and time, may leave rough contact surfaces, and must be reachable for removal. A short bridge between two supported edges differs from an unsupported overhang; check the machine's demonstrated capability instead of assuming one universal angle limit. A side view shows stacked layers and temporary supports under an overhang. A top slice shows perimeter walls surrounding sparse infill. Why more infill does not solve every strength problem Material near the outside can be especially useful when resisting bending. Infill also supports top surfaces and can help resist compression. The useful balance depends on the load and shape. Prusa's infill guidance discusses these different roles."},
{"title":"Orient for load direction, surface quality, and support removal","page":"3D Printing from CAD","summary":"Export a solid model, choose how its layers carry load, and inspect the sliced toolpaths before printing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/3d-printing-from-cad.html#orient-for-load-direction-surface-quality-and-support-removal","text":"Orient for load direction, surface quality, and support removal Ask which way the part will be pulled or bent. Filament prints are often weaker when a load pulls adjacent layers apart. For a straight bar pulled along its length, laying its length in the build plane is usually a better starting point than printing it upright. Test a representative part before relying on it under load. Then consider the important faces. A face against the bed takes the bed's texture; a supported underside may need cleanup; a slope shows layer steps. Rotating the part can improve one surface while making another worse. A broad, stable bed contact usually makes the start easier than a narrow footprint. Practice choosing an orientation"},
{"title":"Use the preview and the failed part to find the next check","page":"3D Printing from CAD","summary":"Export a solid model, choose how its layers carry load, and inspect the sliced toolpaths before printing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/3d-printing-from-cad.html#use-the-preview-and-the-failed-part-to-find-the-next-check","text":"Use the preview and the failed part to find the next check Missing thin feature Inspect the sliced paths. A feature visible in CAD may be too thin for the selected extrusion width; revise the feature or an approved setup. Loose first layer or lifted corners Check bed preparation, first-layer setup, and the material profile against the machine procedure. Warping involves shrinkage as the part cools. Sagging underside Check the orientation, unsupported spans, and support placement before repeating the same job. Gaps, strings, or shifted layers Check material condition, extrusion, temperature/profile, and machine motion as appropriate. These symptoms have several possible causes; change one justified factor at a time and record the result. In the preview, inspect the first layer, small holes, supports, and the final top layers. Estimate time and material before starting. Follow Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for operation and handling; the nozzle and build surface may remain hot after motion stops. For parts that must fit together, continue to Choosing a Fabrication Process and Tolerances and Fits."},
{"title":"Read the pattern before deciding what the line means","page":"The Alphabet of Lines","summary":"Read line patterns, weights, and endpoints to distinguish part edges from centers, dimensions, cuts, and alternate positions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/alphabet-of-lines.html#read-the-pattern-before-deciding-what-the-line-means","text":"Read the pattern before deciding what the line means Every line on an engineering drawing has a job. Some describe the part; others tell you how to measure it, where to look, or how a view was made. Treating every line as a physical edge gives you the wrong part. Use three clues together: pattern (solid or dashed), weight (thick or thin), and context (arrows, letters, numbers, and nearby features). A thin solid line alone could be a dimension, an extension, a leader, or section hatching. Four labeled line samples: thick solid visible; short dashed hidden; thin long-short center; thin long-short-short phantom. Patterns are enlarged for recognition. These samples are not a specification for plotted line widths or dash lengths."},
{"title":"Visible and hidden lines describe edges; center lines locate axes","page":"The Alphabet of Lines","summary":"Read line patterns, weights, and endpoints to distinguish part edges from centers, dimensions, cuts, and alternate positions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/alphabet-of-lines.html#visible-and-hidden-lines-describe-edges-center-lines-locate-axes","text":"Visible and hidden lines describe edges; center lines locate axes Visible / object A relatively thick, continuous line shows an edge or outline you can see from this viewing direction. Hidden A thin series of short, evenly spaced dashes shows an edge concealed by material. A hole may appear as a visible circle in one view and two hidden lines in another. Center A thin long-short pattern locates a center, axis, or symmetry reference. The center line through a cylinder is not a seam in the cylinder. Phantom A thin long-short-short pattern can show an alternate position, repeated detail, or a nearby part used as a reference. An open position of a lever does not mean there are two levers. Read all views together. A line's visibility depends on where you are looking from. The same edge can be solid in one view and dashed in another."},
{"title":"Arrows, notes, and hatching tell you what to do with a view","page":"The Alphabet of Lines","summary":"Read line patterns, weights, and endpoints to distinguish part edges from centers, dimensions, cuts, and alternate positions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/alphabet-of-lines.html#arrows-notes-and-hatching-tell-you-what-to-do-with-a-view","text":"Arrows, notes, and hatching tell you what to do with a view Dimension arrows and extension lines, a leader to a note, a cutting plane with A arrows, diagonal section hatching, and short freehand and long zigzag break lines. Read the endpoints and surrounding information as well as the line pattern. Dimension A thin line with arrowheads identifies the extent of a measurement. Read the associated value; do not measure the picture on your screen. Extension A thin line carries a feature's location out to a dimension line. A small gap usually separates it from the part outline. Leader A thin line connects a note to the feature it describes, usually with an arrow at an edge or a dot on a surface. Follow it to its endpoint. Cutting plane A prominent line marks an imaginary cut used to expose the interior. Read its arrows for the viewing direction and its letters to find the matching section, such as A–A. The line pattern can vary; arrows and identification matter. Section / hatch Thin parallel strokes inside a section view identify material crossed by the imaginary cut. They are not grooves machined into the part. Break A short freehand break or a long thin line with zigzags marks an omitted portion of a view. The actual part is not necessarily broken; a long uniform member can be shortened on paper while its dimension gives the full length. For units and decimals, use Measurement Language. For allowable variation, use Tolerances and Fits. Creating the dimension callouts comes next in Drawing Dimensioning."},
{"title":"When lines coincide, do not invent a second feature","page":"The Alphabet of Lines","summary":"Read line patterns, weights, and endpoints to distinguish part edges from centers, dimensions, cuts, and alternate positions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/alphabet-of-lines.html#when-lines-coincide-do-not-invent-a-second-feature","text":"When lines coincide, do not invent a second feature Where a visible edge and a hidden edge project to exactly the same place, read the visible line. Where a hidden edge and a center line coincide, read the hidden line; the center line may still be identifiable where it extends beyond the edge. A crossing is different from an overlap. Two lines crossing on the sheet do not prove that two physical edges meet in space. Check another view before deciding. Why context matters more than one dash Short center marks may look different from long axes, and reduced drawings can make small gaps hard to see. Use a circle, an axis in another view, a section label, or an attached note to confirm the reading. If the supplied drawing is illegible, obtain a readable copy. The overlap examples agree with NPTEL's line-precedence explanation. For historical examples of the line families, see the NASA GSFC drawing manual (1994), §3.2.3. These are reading examples, not a replacement for the drafting standard specified on a job. Practice identifying lines"},
{"title":"An assembly records which parts belong together and how they fit","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#an-assembly-records-which-parts-belong-together-and-how-they-fit","text":"An assembly records which parts belong together and how they fit A part defines a component's shape. An assembly places instances of parts and subassemblies and records relationships between them. Two instances of one part can occupy different positions while using the same part definition. In a file-based SOLIDWORKS assembly, the.SLDASM normally references external.SLDPRT and other assembly files. An assembly drawing references the assembly. Sending only the assembly file can leave the recipient without its components. Virtual components are an exception: their definitions can be stored inside the assembly. Onshape organizes Part Studios, Assemblies, and Drawings as tabs in documents. Inserted instances reference their source definitions; a tab name is not a Windows filename. Share the document with the appropriate access and identify the intended version when a fixed design state matters. Definitions, instances, and design changes Changing a shared part definition can update several instances. Moving one instance in an assembly changes its placement, not the shape of every copy. A configuration can select a particular variant of a component; record the intended variant when sharing or drawing it. Onshape: Sharing and Collaboration explains document access and permissions."},
{"title":"Rename and package files with their references","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#rename-and-package-files-with-their-references","text":"Rename and package files with their references Renaming or moving a component in ordinary File Explorer can leave an assembly looking for its old name or location. Use the CAD system's reference-aware rename or move workflow. Changing a display name in the tree is not necessarily changing the file name. Save a recoverable copy before reorganizing an assembly's files. Use the supported rename workflow, include affected assemblies and drawings, and save the referencing documents. For file-based SOLIDWORKS sharing, use Pack and Go where available. Review the file list, drawing inclusion, library components, and destination. Open the packaged assembly from its destination and inspect the resolved reference paths. Confirm it is not quietly loading parts from the original folders. Vendor help for renaming and sharing SOLIDWORKS: Renaming Components distinguishes display names from file names and explains updating references in closed documents. Saving an open assembly alone does not automatically repair every closed drawing. Availability differs for platform-connected installations. SOLIDWORKS: Pack and Go Overview describes collecting related files into a folder or ZIP. Use your installed edition's help for the exact controls."},
{"title":"Ground a base, then count the motion you intend to keep","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#ground-a-base-then-count-the-motion-you-intend-to-keep","text":"Ground a base, then count the motion you intend to keep A free rigid component in three-dimensional space has six degrees of freedom (DOF): translation along three axes and rotation about three axes. A degree of freedom is an independent motion, not a count of arrows or mates. A free block can translate along x, y, and z and rotate about x, y, and z. After grounding the same block, none of those motions remains. Grounding removes motion relative to the assembly reference frame. Choose a base or housing as the reference. Fix it, or fully locate it to the assembly origin and planes. In SOLIDWORKS, insertion options include fixing only the first component, fixing inserted components, or leaving them floating. Check the actual state; do not assume that the first component is always fixed. Fixing a base away from the origin holds it there; it does not move it to the origin. A lever should still rotate about its pivot. A cover should stay put. Fully locating every moving part would turn a mechanism into a rigid model. Check the insertion setting SOLIDWORKS: Insert Components documents the Fix / Float choices. Grounding the base and choosing the remaining joint motion are separate decisions."},
{"title":"Use geometric mates to describe the intended contact","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#use-geometric-mates-to-describe-the-intended-contact","text":"Use geometric mates to describe the intended contact Select the actual locating faces, axes, or reference planes. The same mate name can accept different geometry, so its effect depends on what you select. The diagrams below are schematic before/after comparisons, not complete constraints for whole assemblies. Coincident For two planar faces, put their supporting planes together. The faces can still slide in that plane and rotate about its normal. Coincident planes do not guarantee that finite faces overlap. Concentric Put two cylindrical axes on the same line. Without a rotation lock or other mates, a shaft can still slide along and rotate about that line. Before: two separated planar faces. After: their planes coincide; in-plane sliding and spin remain. Before: a shaft axis is offset from a bore axis. After: the axes coincide; axial slide and spin remain. Why subtracting one DOF per mate gives the wrong answer For one free rigid part relative to a grounded part, a planar-face coincident mate leaves three DOF. A cylindrical-axis concentric mate leaves two. Adding a shoulder-face coincident mate perpendicular to that axis removes axial sliding and leaves one rotation. A redundant mate may remove no new freedom. Count the independent motions that survive each step. See SOLIDWORKS: Types of Mates for supported selections. These geometric names are not a one-to-one map to Onshape commands."},
{"title":"Test a shaft joint: keep the motion the design needs","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#test-a-shaft-joint-keep-the-motion-the-design-needs","text":"Test a shaft joint: keep the motion the design needs The housing is grounded. The shaft and its attached lever start with their axes aligned to the housing bore. Predict the motions left, test them, then seat the shoulder. Finally add angular alignment: does a hinge still work? Worked experiment: 2 → 1 → 0 independent motions This original ideal joint begins after axis alignment, not as a free rigid body. Its only coordinates are axial position z in millimeters and spin θ in degrees. The housing is grounded; the lever moves with the shaft. SIDE · +z → END · toward −Z z = 0; slide blocked θ = 65°; spin free Worked hinge state: shoulder at the housing datum, z = 0 mm; lever at θ = 65°. Looking toward −Z in the end view, positive spin is counterclockwise. Dashed lines show the joint axis and reference datums. Conditions remove independent motions, not one freedom per mate icon Condition after built-in axis alignment Example coordinates What can move? Neither additional condition z = 12 mm, θ = 35° → test: 22 mm, 65° Slide + spin: 2 DOF Seat shoulder at z = 0 z = 0 mm, θ = 65° Spin only: 1 DOF. Hinge goal met. Also align lever at θ = 0° z = 0 mm, θ = 0° Neither: 0 DOF. Fixed goal met; hinge goal defeated. Angular alignment only z may vary, θ = 0° Slide only: 1 DOF. Slider goal met. Diagnose and recover: to restore the hinge, remove angular alignment while keeping the shoulder seated. The shaft can turn again. For a slider, remove the shoulder condition and retain angular alignment. Choosing a goal alone does not change conditions. The shoulder condition enforces z = 0; angular alignment enforces θ = 0°. They do not freeze an arbitrary current placement. Removing a condition permits motion without itself moving the component. Angles wrap every 360°. Model limits: axial travel −30 to +30 mm is a declared lab range, not physical stops. Axis alignment and rigid components are built in. Shoulder seating means ideal plane coincidence, holding z = 0 in both directions; physical shoulder contact alone would not retain a shaft against separation. Contact, collision/interference, friction and load capacity are outside this model. Schematic overlap does not establish a fit or clearance. In a SOLIDWORKS geometric-mate sequence, concentric alignment followed by shoulder-face coincidence can leave shaft rotation free. This lab's conditions illustrate the resulting motion; they are not a vendor command recorder. Onshape expresses joint motion through mate connectors: Cylindrical permits axial translation and rotation (Tz, Rz), while Revolute permits rotation (Rz). These are not a one-to-one mapping of the SOLIDWORKS steps. For coupled shaft rotations, continue with Gear Ratios. Diagnose a locked hinge Preserve slider motion"},
{"title":"Parallel and perpendicular mates set orientation","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#parallel-and-perpendicular-mates-set-orientation","text":"Parallel and perpendicular mates set orientation Parallel keeps selected planes parallel without setting their separation. Perpendicular keeps selected planes at a right angle. Neither condition alone fully locates the part. These diagrams show the planes edge-on. Before: two planes have different orientations. After: the planes are parallel with a gap that is not fixed by this mate. Before: two planes meet at an oblique angle. After: the planes are at 90 degrees, without a complete location constraint."},
{"title":"Distance, angle, and tangent mates control gaps and contact","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#distance-angle-and-tangent-mates-control-gaps-and-contact","text":"Distance, angle, and tangent mates control gaps and contact Distance sets a separation between selected entities. For parallel planar faces, use the normal gap, not a slanted edge length. Angle sets the angle between selected entities; check alignment and direction so the model uses the intended side of the angle. Before: parallel faces have an unspecified gap. After: the normal gap is the specified distance d. Before: planes have an unspecified angle. After: their angle is set to 30 degrees. Tangent keeps compatible curved and planar or curved surfaces touching tangentially. Tangency alone does not enforce rolling without slipping. Before: a circular surface is separated from a plane. After: it touches the plane tangentially; rolling is not implied."},
{"title":"Choose a joint or mechanical relationship when motion is the point","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#choose-a-joint-or-mechanical-relationship-when-motion-is-the-point","text":"Choose a joint or mechanical relationship when motion is the point For a simple lever on a grounded pin, align the pivot axes and locate the lever axially. Leave rotation free. A hinge mate expresses this single rotational freedom directly. In Onshape, a Revolute mate serves that joint purpose; Cylindrical allows axial slide and spin, Slider allows one translation, and Fastened allows no relative motion. Mate connectors establish the joint frames. A slot mate locates a pin or similar feature relative to a slot. Select free travel or a prescribed position to match the design; other motions depend on the selections and remaining mates. Onshape's Pin Slot mate allows rotation about the connector's Z axis and translation along its X axis. Set appropriate travel limits; it is not an identical set of SOLIDWORKS slot options. A gear mate or gear relation couples rotations by a ratio. Locate the shafts first. The relation does not create teeth or prove they mesh without interference. Use Gear Ratios for the ratio calculation and Torque for the separate question of turning effort. Before and after comparisons: a loose lever becomes a hinge with only pivot rotation; an offset pin is guided into a slot; independently turning shafts become ratio-coupled. Compare vendor joint vocabulary Onshape: Mates describes joint motion through mate connectors. SOLIDWORKS: Machine Design Tools introduces mechanical mates. SOLIDWORKS slot settings distinguish free, centered, distance, and percentage positions."},
{"title":"Build reusable parts and subassemblies with deliberate dependencies","page":"Assembly Files and Mates","summary":"Keep component references intact and choose mates that locate parts while preserving their intended motion.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/assembly-files-and-mates.html#build-reusable-parts-and-subassemblies-with-deliberate-dependencies","text":"Build reusable parts and subassemblies with deliberate dependencies Bottom-up design starts with existing parts and brings them into an assembly. Top-down design starts with an overall layout or shared design references and develops parts around them. A mixed approach is common: use a shared layout for custom plates and insert purchased hardware. A subassembly groups parts into a reusable unit. Give it a useful function, such as a pivot cartridge, rather than hiding unrelated parts in it. Check whether its internal joints are allowed to move at the parent level; a rigid subassembly can make a working joint appear locked. Top-down references can keep mating holes aligned when the layout changes, but they also create dependencies. Keep the direction of those dependencies understandable and check them when copying or sharing the design. What makes a feature top-down? SOLIDWORKS: Top-Down Design explains how layout or other-component geometry controls a feature and creates external references. Merely editing a part while an assembly is visible does not by itself establish that dependency. Use SOLIDWORKS Toolbox or Onshape Standard Content, where available, for standard hardware. Choose the standard, size, thread pitch, length, and material or grade deliberately. A visually similar bolt or an automatic size suggestion is not a completed fastener selection. Library hardware still needs checking Onshape: Standard Content explains insertion and sizing. SOLIDWORKS Toolbox is an add-in described in its machine-design overview. Check the resulting model and BOM properties rather than modeling every thread from scratch. For sketching and part-modeling topics, browse Engineering Graphics and CAD. Continue with Checking CAD Assemblies and Exploded Views and Bills of Materials. Practice shaft freedom Choose locating mates"},
{"title":"Extra views make sloping surfaces and small features easier to read","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#extra-views-make-sloping-surfaces-and-small-features-easier-to-read","text":"Extra views make sloping surfaces and small features easier to read An auxiliary view is an extra drawing view that looks straight at a flat, sloping surface so its shape is clear. A tilted rectangular surface can look shortened from above; looking straight at it reveals its rectangular proportions. A detail view is an enlarged picture of a selected small region, such as a hole with crowded edges or dimensions. The actual part stays the same size. Use an auxiliary when the viewing direction makes the shape hard to read, and a detail when a small region needs more room. A view's scale tells you how large its picture is compared with the real part. Read the view labels and scale information to connect an added view to the original drawing. To expose an interior hidden behind solid material, use a section view."},
{"title":"Look perpendicular to a face to see its true shape","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#look-perpendicular-to-a-face-to-see-its-true-shape","text":"Look perpendicular to a face to see its true shape A face is a surface on the part. Look straight at a flat face, perpendicular to it, to see its true shape. In projection terms, that face is parallel to the imaginary flat surface on which the view is drawn: the projection plane. In the figure, the part is a wedge with a flat, sloping rectangular face. The front view sees that face edge-on, so the face is reduced to the line P–Q. The light-blue rectangle is the same face viewed straight on in the auxiliary view. An inclined face is a sloping line P–Q in the front view, but a rectangle when viewed straight on in the auxiliary view. The top view supplies the face's depth of 80. The same face looks different from different directions. P–Q is its edge in the front view; the auxiliary view shows its rectangular shape. The thin construction lines explain how the views connect. In this example, the front view sees the inclined face on edge. The top view shows it foreshortened: its sloping length appears shorter because the view is not looking straight at the face. The top view's 160-unit length is not the face's true 200-unit length. Scale is the ratio of a length on the drawing to its actual length. “True shape” still allows the stated drawing scale; it does not mean the image must be life size. The auxiliary view shows the face's correct proportions without the shortening caused by the viewing direction. A primary auxiliary view is constructed directly from a principal view, such as the front, top, or side. Choose its projection plane parallel to the inclined face. Review Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box and Choosing Drawing Views for the principal views. When one auxiliary view is not enough An oblique face is inclined to all three principal projection planes. It is not seen on edge in any of them. An initial auxiliary view can establish an edge view, followed by a secondary auxiliary view showing true shape. That two-stage construction is different from the primary construction below."},
{"title":"A detail enlarges a feature; a break shortens the view","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#a-detail-enlarges-a-feature-a-break-shortens-the-view","text":"A detail enlarges a feature; a break shortens the view The existing view from which a detail is taken is its parent view. A boundary and letter usually identify the selected region. In the figure, the circle marked B locates the region shown in Detail B. A selected hole region is enlarged as Detail B at scale 2:1. Below it, paired break lines omit the uniform middle of a long part while the part remains continuous. Detail B makes the hole easier to read. The broken view below saves space along a long part; it does not show a shorter physical part. Match the detail label and read its scale. 2:1 means a drawn length is twice the actual length; the hole itself has not grown. Enlargement alone does not change the viewing direction or reveal hidden features. A broken view omits a middle length of a long, substantially uniform part so its ends and features fit on the sheet. Break lines identify the omission. The actual part remains continuous, and dimensions across the break state its full size. Use those dimensions rather than measuring the shortened illustration. A broken view is different from a broken-out section, which shows interior geometry by representing a local portion of material as removed. See Section Views."},
{"title":"Project from the edge view and transfer the missing depth","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#project-from-the-edge-view-and-transfer-the-missing-depth","text":"Project from the edge view and transfer the missing depth The auxiliary figure above uses the front view as its parent. Its thin projectors are construction lines used to line up corresponding points in the two views. Find the principal view in which the face is a line. In the example, the front view shows the sloping face edge P–Q. Draw a fold or reference line parallel to P–Q. It represents the hinge between the parent and auxiliary projection planes. Draw thin projectors perpendicular to the fold line from the face’s corners. Transfer each corner’s depth from the corresponding principal view, using the same reference and drawing scale. Here the top view supplies a constant depth of 80. Connect corresponding points. The face is a 200-by-80 rectangle in the auxiliary view, although the top view shows its sloping length shortened to 160. These are invented drawing units for the construction. The projectors and fold line are not physical edges on the part. The auxiliary view shows this face's true shape, not necessarily the true shape of every other face."},
{"title":"Use a partial auxiliary view when only one face matters","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#use-a-partial-auxiliary-view-when-only-one-face-matters","text":"Use a partial auxiliary view when only one face matters A partial auxiliary view shows the useful inclined face and enough nearby geometry to identify it. Omit unrelated geometry that would make the view crowded, using an appropriate break boundary where the view is interrupted. Keep the viewing reference, label, and scale clear. Partial means limited coverage; it does not change the projection direction. The construction above isolates the inclined face for that reason."},
{"title":"Create auxiliary, detail, and break views from the parent in CAD","page":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","summary":"Show a flat sloping surface straight on, enlarge a small region, or shorten a long part on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/auxiliary-and-detail-views.html#create-auxiliary-detail-and-break-views-from-the-parent-in-cad","text":"Create auxiliary, detail, and break views from the parent in CAD Auxiliary view Select the parent and a reference edge representing the inclined face edge-on. Use Auxiliary View in SOLIDWORKS or Auxiliary view in Onshape, check the direction, and place the view. For a partial view, limit the displayed region with the application’s crop or partial-view controls while retaining identifying references. Detail view Use Detail View in SOLIDWORKS or Detail view in Onshape. Define the region, place the enlarged view, and set its scale and label. Verify that dimensions reference the intended model edges. Broken view Use Break View in SOLIDWORKS or Break view in Onshape. Choose break direction and style, then position the two break boundaries. Check that no feature needed to define the part was omitted. Review the result after every model revision. A generated view can be geometrically valid yet omit the feature the reader needs. Vendor help and dimensioning references See SOLIDWORKS derived drawing views, Creating an Auxiliary View (2025), and Break View. Onshape documents Auxiliary view, Detail view, and Break view. For formal conventions, consult the current ASME Y14.3 and related standards, using the edition invoked by the drawing or contract. These introductory explanations do not quote clauses. Continue through the Engineering Graphics and CAD collection for dimensioning topics. Practice choosing the true-shape view Practice reading a broken view"},
{"title":"Use certification as a goal for building dependable CAD skills","page":"CAD Certification Preparation","summary":"Build dependable modeling habits, check your work, and use official sources to plan an entry-level CAD certification attempt.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-certification-preparation.html#use-certification-as-a-goal-for-building-dependable-cad-skills","text":"Use certification as a goal for building dependable CAD skills A CAD certification gives you a way to demonstrate skills assessed by its provider. Preparing for one can help you become more productive, build models that survive changes, and describe your skills on a resume. Passing does not guarantee a job or prove that every design you make will work. The Certified SOLIDWORKS Associate (CSWA) is the worked case here, offered through Dassault Systèmes' SOLIDWORKS certification program. CourseWiki is an independent learning resource and is not affiliated with or endorsed by Dassault Systèmes."},
{"title":"Find the current exam rules before you register","page":"CAD Certification Preparation","summary":"Build dependable modeling habits, check your work, and use official sources to plan an entry-level CAD certification attempt.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-certification-preparation.html#find-the-current-exam-rules-before-you-register","text":"Find the current exam rules before you register Start at the official SOLIDWORKS certification program, then select the CSWA Design Associate page. Check the exact credential name: similarly named certifications can assess different tools. Read the current exam description, skill coverage, format, length, question count, passing rules, and any section weights on the official page. Follow the official registration or purchase route; confirm the current price and any voucher conditions before paying. Check software, computer, account, and testing-client requirements, plus the rules for permitted resources and retakes. Find the official sample or practice resources linked from the certification page and read their use instructions. For current numerical exam details, see the official page. This guide does not reproduce an exam specification or sample exam. The official certification FAQ is another starting point for registration and testing questions."},
{"title":"Practise reading, modeling, assembling, and checking","page":"CAD Certification Preparation","summary":"Build dependable modeling habits, check your work, and use official sources to plan an entry-level CAD certification attempt.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-certification-preparation.html#practise-reading-modeling-assembling-and-checking","text":"Practise reading, modeling, assembling, and checking Use these general skill areas to organize practice; use the official description to confirm what your chosen certification assesses. Read a drawing: identify views, dimensions, and feature notes before building. Start with Drawing Terminology Review. Sketch deliberately: use dimensions and geometric relations to control size and shape. Review Geometric Relations in Sketches. Build and revise parts: choose features that express the shape, then change a dimension and check the result. Review Feature-based Modeling. Assemble components: place parts and constrain their intended positions or motion with mates. Review Assembly Files and Mates. Inspect the result: measure dimensions, check mass properties, and confirm the correct model state. Review CAD Model Views and Checks."},
{"title":"Check geometry, units, and material before trusting mass","page":"CAD Certification Preparation","summary":"Build dependable modeling habits, check your work, and use official sources to plan an entry-level CAD certification attempt.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-certification-preparation.html#check-geometry-units-and-material-before-trusting-mass","text":"Check geometry, units, and material before trusting mass For a uniform solid, mass = density × volume. Use compatible units: density in g/cm³ multiplied by volume in cm³ gives mass in g. To convert mm³ to cm³, divide by 1,000 because 1 cm³ = 10 × 10 × 10 mm³. Changing only the displayed units changes the number and unit label, not the physical size or mass. Entering a dimension in the wrong units changes the geometry. Choosing a different density changes mass even when the shape stays the same. Confirm the drawing's units and the units used when entering dimensions. Check overall dimensions, holes, and removed material against all relevant views. Assign the specified material or density to the intended bodies or components. Rebuild after edits; confirm the intended configuration and which bodies or components are included. Read the requested quantity and its output units before recording an answer. Why matching mass does not prove the shape is right Different shapes can have the same volume and density, and therefore the same mass. A missing cut and extra material removed elsewhere can cancel numerically. Treat mass as one independent check alongside dimensions, feature locations, and drawing views. Practise mass from volume Practise cubic units Check a unit change"},
{"title":"Build accuracy first, then practise with a time limit","page":"CAD Certification Preparation","summary":"Build dependable modeling habits, check your work, and use official sources to plan an entry-level CAD certification attempt.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-certification-preparation.html#build-accuracy-first-then-practise-with-a-time-limit","text":"Build accuracy first, then practise with a time limit Choose an original, ungraded drawing you have permission to use. Build the part without following a click-by-click demonstration. Explain how each view supports the geometry, then check the dimensions and material. Repeat with a changed dimension to see whether the model updates as intended. Once that process is reliable, set a practice time limit. Include time to read, model, check, and save. Record where you lose time and practise that skill next. During an actual attempt, follow the provider's rules about navigation, returning to questions, and saving work. Keep a short error log: drawing interpretation, sketch control, feature choice, assembly position, units, or material. Rebuild after correcting the cause instead of memorizing the previous answer."},
{"title":"Choose a part, assembly, or drawing for the work you need to do","page":"CAD Files and Documents","summary":"Choose the right container for parts, assemblies, and drawings, then check templates, units, and references.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-files-and-documents.html#choose-a-part-assembly-or-drawing-for-the-work-you-need-to-do","text":"Choose a part, assembly, or drawing for the work you need to do A part describes geometry. An assembly positions components relative to one another. A drawing communicates that design through views, dimensions, and notes. Start by deciding which job you need the document to do. SOLIDWORKS part:.sldprt Contains part geometry and its modeling features. A part can contain one body or multiple bodies; the file extension does not mean there is exactly one solid. SOLIDWORKS assembly:.sldasm References components, including parts and subassemblies, and stores their arrangement and assembly relationships. SOLIDWORKS drawing:.slddrw Usually references a part or assembly to generate drawing views. It is a separate document from the model. Continue with Feature-based Modeling, Assembly Files and Mates, or Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box for the modeling and drawing methods."},
{"title":"An Onshape document holds tabs inside a workspace","page":"CAD Files and Documents","summary":"Choose the right container for parts, assemblies, and drawings, then check templates, units, and references.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-files-and-documents.html#an-onshape-document-holds-tabs-inside-a-workspace","text":"An Onshape document holds tabs inside a workspace An Onshape document is a container for related design information. Open a workspace to edit that information, then select the tab for the task. Part Studio: create geometry using sketches and features. One Part Studio can produce several related parts. Assembly: insert and position part or subassembly instances and define their relationships. Drawing: create drawing sheets and views of models. Tabs and tab folders: organize the document's contents. Use the Tab Manager to find a tab when the row becomes crowded. A tab folder organizes tabs; it does not mate parts together. Several parts created together in a Part Studio are not automatically an assembly with motion relationships. See Sketch Entities and Planes to begin geometry and CAD History, Versions, and Sharing to distinguish a workspace from a version. Vendor help for the document model Onshape Documents describes the container and its contents. Interface layout and available tools can vary by platform."},
{"title":"Check the template and units before entering dimensions","page":"CAD Files and Documents","summary":"Choose the right container for parts, assemblies, and drawings, then check templates, units, and references.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-files-and-documents.html#check-the-template-and-units-before-entering-dimensions","text":"Check the template and units before entering dimensions A template supplies starting settings. In SOLIDWORKS, part, assembly, and drawing templates use.prtdot,.asmdot, and.drwdot. Choose the appropriate template, then verify the new document's units. A familiar template name is not proof that its settings are right. In Onshape, check workspace units; account preferences provide defaults for new documents. Choose a suitable drawing template when creating a drawing, and check that drawing's units and sheet settings too. Confirm the active document or workspace and the tab you are editing. Check length and angle units before entering bare numbers. Enter a known dimension and verify its displayed value and units. Check drawing units and sheet scale separately from the model. Changing the display from inches to millimeters should express the same physical length in another unit. It does not repair geometry created at the wrong size. Display precision controls how many digits you see; it is not a manufacturing tolerance. Template and unit references See SOLIDWORKS file locations and template types and Onshape account preferences. Changing an Onshape account unit preference does not retroactively change existing documents."},
{"title":"Keep referenced files together when you transfer a design","page":"CAD Files and Documents","summary":"Choose the right container for parts, assemblies, and drawings, then check templates, units, and references.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-files-and-documents.html#keep-referenced-files-together-when-you-transfer-a-design","text":"Keep referenced files together when you transfer a design For a typical file-based SOLIDWORKS design, the assembly points to component files, and a drawing points to its model. Sending only the assembly file may leave the recipient without the parts. Renaming or moving files outside a reference-aware workflow can break those connections. Use Pack and Go to gather a design's related files. Review the package contents, including required drawings, then open the transferred design and check for missing references. Files can be reorganized when references are updated; they do not have to remain permanently in the original folder. Why a folder copy needs checking A component may live outside the folder you copied. SOLIDWORKS File Utilities describes Pack and Go and tools that update references when files move or change names. Check file references Choose a tab Check units"},
{"title":"The feature list describes how geometry is built","page":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","summary":"Separate feature order from edit history, preserve useful design states, and share the intended model with the right access.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-history-and-sharing.html#the-feature-list-describes-how-geometry-is-built","text":"The feature list describes how geometry is built The SOLIDWORKS FeatureManager design tree and the Onshape feature list help you find sketches and modeling features. Later features can depend on earlier geometry. Rename important features so another reader can tell what they do. A tree also contains items such as planes, bodies, or components; not every row is a modeling operation. To understand how features build a part, continue with Feature-based Modeling. A rollback bar lets you work at an earlier position in the feature sequence while later features are temporarily left out of regeneration. Move it forward again to evaluate the complete model and check dependent features. Rolling back does not retrieve an old saved revision, and it is not the same operation as separately suppressing one feature. Rollback follows the regeneration sequence SOLIDWORKS Rollback Bar help explains moving through the feature sequence. A file can be saved with its bar rolled back, so inspect the bar's position when reopening a model."},
{"title":"Onshape history records edits; versions preserve milestones","page":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","summary":"Separate feature order from edit history, preserve useful design states, and share the intended model with the right access.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-history-and-sharing.html#onshape-history-records-edits-versions-preserve-milestones","text":"Onshape history records edits; versions preserve milestones Onshape records completed edits in document history. The feature list shows the modeling sequence; history records changes to the document over time. Editing an early sketch today changes the history today even though the sketch remains early in the feature list. A version captures the design state of the workspace, including its tabs, at a named point. That captured design state is read-only. Use a meaningful milestone name and description so a reviewer knows which state you mean. A branch starts from a version and provides a separate workspace for further edits. Use it to explore an alternative while preserving the starting version. Compare changes before merging; merging needs review, not just a button click. Viewing history, restoring, and branching serve different purposes Viewing an earlier state is inspection. Restoring changes a workspace back to an earlier state. Branching gives you a separate place to continue editing. Check the active workspace before any restore or merge. See Onshape Versions and History and Document Management."},
{"title":"A SOLIDWORKS save or file copy needs its own revision plan","page":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","summary":"Separate feature order from edit history, preserve useful design states, and share the intended model with the right access.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-history-and-sharing.html#a-solidworks-save-or-file-copy-needs-its-own-revision-plan","text":"A SOLIDWORKS save or file copy needs its own revision plan In a local file workflow, Save writes the current document to disk. A deliberate copy can preserve an earlier state, but a filename alone does not establish a managed revision or capture all referenced files. Keep descriptive names and know which copy is the active design. Before copying an assembly or drawing for an alternative, check what its references will point to. Two assembly copies can still use the same part file; editing that shared part can affect both. Use the transfer checks in CAD Files and Documents. SOLIDWORKS installations may use PDM or connected data management for version and revision control. Follow that system's workflow when present; local Save and Save As are not a universal replacement for it."},
{"title":"Share the intended state and check the recipient's access","page":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","summary":"Separate feature order from edit history, preserve useful design states, and share the intended model with the right access.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-history-and-sharing.html#share-the-intended-state-and-check-the-recipient-s-access","text":"Share the intended state and check the recipient's access In Onshape, decide whether the recipient needs to inspect a fixed version or collaborate in an editable workspace. A link to a changing workspace can show later edits; a version identifies a fixed design state. Grant access using the document's sharing controls. Confirm the document, workspace or version, and tab you intend to share. Review document visibility and the intended recipient. Choose view or edit access and inspect additional permissions such as export, copy, and resharing. Check that the recipient can open the intended state with the intended access. Do not assume that sending a URL grants access, or that view access prevents copying or exporting. Available sharing options depend on the account and organization. Review Onshape Share Documents for those permissions. Choose version or branch Distinguish rollback from history Check sharing"},
{"title":"Zoom, pan, and rotate change your view of the model","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#zoom-pan-and-rotate-change-your-view-of-the-model","text":"Zoom, pan, and rotate change your view of the model Zoom changes how large the model appears. Pan shifts it across the window. Rotate orbits the view so you can inspect another side. These viewing operations do not resize the part or move an assembly component. Use zoom to fit to recover the whole model, zoom to window to enlarge a boxed region, and zoom to selection when you need a selected detail. Mouse buttons, modifier keys, and wheel direction depend on your device and control preferences. Check those settings instead of assuming two platforms use the same gestures. Use Onshape's view cube or SOLIDWORKS view-orientation controls to return to a known direction. The axis triad helps you keep track of model directions while orbiting."},
{"title":"Normal to looks straight at a selected plane","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#normal-to-looks-straight-at-a-selected-plane","text":"Normal to looks straight at a selected plane Front, rear, top, bottom, left, and right are standard model directions. Normal to a planar face or sketch plane means looking along a direction perpendicular to it. An inclined face can therefore have a normal view that is different from every standard view. A cube shown obliquely, then straight-on front and top views. A separate edge-on plane has a viewing arrow perpendicular to it and a right-angle marker. The face marked F establishes the front. Normal to depends on the selected plane, not on the name of a standard view. Isometric, dimetric, and trimetric are angled parallel-projection views. The three principal axes have equal foreshortening in isometric, two equal in dimetric, and three different in trimetric. Perspective adds apparent size change with distance; turn it off when comparing parallel projected shapes. Viewing direction is separate from drawing layout A standard view tells you where you look from. First-angle or third-angle projection tells you where that view belongs on a drawing sheet. Explore the geometry in Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box. An angled model view is useful for recognizing shape but is not a substitute for dimensions."},
{"title":"Choose a display style for the edges you need to inspect","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#choose-a-display-style-for-the-edges-you-need-to-inspect","text":"Choose a display style for the edges you need to inspect Keep the viewing direction fixed while comparing styles. Shading shows surfaces; edge settings decide which boundaries you can see. Labels differ between platforms, so read the option's effect. Shaded without edges Filled surfaces emphasize overall form without drawn edge outlines. Shaded with edges Filled surfaces plus visible edge outlines make face boundaries easier to follow. Hidden lines/edges removed An unshaded view shows visible edges and leaves concealed edges out. Hidden lines/edges visible Concealed edges are also shown, distinguished from visible edges. Useful for finding geometry behind the front surface. Wireframe Shows the edge framework through the model without opaque shaded faces. Overlapping edges can make depth difficult to read. Six views of the same cube: shaded without edges, shaded with edges, hidden edges removed, hidden edges visible with dashed concealed edges, wireframe with all edges solid, and shaded with dashed hidden edges. Original schematic: dashed lines mark concealed edges where they are distinguished. Colors and exact edge treatments vary with software settings. Onshape also provides translucent display and shaded display with hidden edges. Translucency lets you see through faces; hidden-edge display highlights concealed boundaries. Neither removes material. Find the view options in vendor help Onshape camera and render options links to navigation and display controls. Current browser controls can separate shading choices from hidden-edge choices; older outlines may list them as combined styles."},
{"title":"A model section view reveals the inside without cutting the part","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#a-model-section-view-reveals-the-inside-without-cutting-the-part","text":"A model section view reveals the inside without cutting the part Use a section view in the model window to clip away the near portion visually. Choose a plane or planar face, set the cut position and direction, and inspect the exposed interior. Turn the section off afterward to see the complete model again. A viewing section does not create an extruded cut feature. It also does not automatically create a documented section view on a drawing sheet. See Onshape Section View help for the available section controls."},
{"title":"Check the selection before trusting a measurement","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#check-the-selection-before-trusting-a-measurement","text":"Check the selection before trusting a measurement Select the actual entities you need, then use Measure. An edge can provide length; a circular edge can provide radius or diameter; two suitable entities can provide distance or angle. Read the result label: minimum distance, center distance, and distance along an axis answer different questions. Quick selection properties or status-bar values are useful checks, but they depend on what is selected. A face, an edge, a body, and an assembly component are different selections. Clear old selections before starting a new measurement. Measurement reports geometry; editing a driving dimension changes geometry. Clear the previous selection and select the intended entities. Confirm the highlighted face, edge, or component belongs to the intended part. Read the measurement type and unit with the value. Compare the result with a known dimension or a simple estimate. Measurement reference Onshape Measure Tool describes how results depend on selected entities."},
{"title":"Mass needs material density as well as geometry","page":"CAD Model Views and Checks","summary":"Navigate a model, choose what edges to show, and check dimensions and mass without changing the design.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/cad-model-views-and-checks.html#mass-needs-material-density-as-well-as-geometry","text":"Mass needs material density as well as geometry Assign a material to the intended part or body before relying on mass properties. A color or metallic appearance alone is not proof that the material density is correct. Check which bodies or components are included, the units, and any overrides before accepting mass or center of mass. For a uniform solid, mass = density × volume. A correct volume with the wrong density still gives the wrong mass. Center of mass describes how mass is distributed; it need not lie inside the solid material of a hollow or curved part. Material assignment and mass-property help See Onshape material assignment, Onshape Mass Properties, and SOLIDWORKS Mass Properties for selection and override controls. Choose a display style Check normal to Check a section Check mass Check a selection"},
{"title":"Judge remaining motion against the design intent","page":"Checking CAD Assemblies","summary":"Diagnose mate errors, inspect overlap and clearance, and test the intended motion before trusting an assembly model.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/checking-cad-assemblies.html#judge-remaining-motion-against-the-design-intent","text":"Judge remaining motion against the design intent A successful rebuild means the CAD system can solve the model. It does not mean the model describes the mechanism you intended. Try moving each component and name the motion that should remain. Under-mated means freedom remains. That is a defect for a loose cover, but rotation at a lever pivot can be intentional. Over-mated or conflicting constraints ask for relationships that the solver cannot satisfy together. A redundant constraint repeats an existing condition; it adds no useful location and can complicate diagnosis. For a shaft with concentric axes and seated shoulder faces, one rotation remains. Adding another mate that merely keeps those axes parallel is redundant. Adding an angle mate to lock that remaining rotation may be correct for a stationary shaft and wrong for a rotating lever. Review Assembly Files and Mates for the geometry."},
{"title":"Repair the cause of a mate error before adding more mates","page":"Checking CAD Assemblies","summary":"Diagnose mate errors, inspect overlap and clearance, and test the intended motion before trusting an assembly model.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/checking-cad-assemblies.html#repair-the-cause-of-a-mate-error-before-adding-more-mates","text":"Repair the cause of a mate error before adding more mates Read the reported mate error and identify the first failed relationship, rather than only the later errors it causes. Check which component is grounded and whether a moving part was fixed accidentally. Inspect selected faces and axes, alignment direction, offsets, and angle values. Check whether an edit removed or replaced the geometry used by a mate. Temporarily suppress a suspect mate to isolate the conflict, then repair or remove the unnecessary relationship. Rebuild and test motion again, including motion inside subassemblies at the parent level. Do not force incompatible hole locations together by adding duplicate constraints. Determine whether the parts, the chosen references, or the intended fit are wrong."},
{"title":"Check interference and clearance as different questions","page":"Checking CAD Assemblies","summary":"Diagnose mate errors, inspect overlap and clearance, and test the intended motion before trusting an assembly model.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/checking-cad-assemblies.html#check-interference-and-clearance-as-different-questions","text":"Check interference and clearance as different questions Interference is shared volume: two solid parts occupy the same space. Clearance is the gap between parts. A model with no overlap may still lack enough room for movement, manufacturing variation, or tool access. Three comparisons show overlapping parts, touching parts, and parts separated by a gap. No overlap alone does not prove adequate clearance. Run interference detection on the relevant components in the intended configuration and position. Inspect each result. An intentional press fit or simplified thread representation can produce overlap, but an unwanted bolt tip inside a moving path needs attention. Record the reason for any accepted interference. Selection scope can hide an interference SOLIDWORKS: Interference Detection describes overlap volumes and options affecting subassemblies and ignored results. Onshape: Interference Detection includes options for standard content and internal subassembly checks. Include the parts you mean to test."},
{"title":"Move the mechanism through its intended range","page":"Checking CAD Assemblies","summary":"Diagnose mate errors, inspect overlap and clearance, and test the intended motion before trusting an assembly model.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/checking-cad-assemblies.html#move-the-mechanism-through-its-intended-range","text":"Move the mechanism through its intended range Move the input joint slowly and watch the connected parts. Test intermediate positions as well as endpoints: a lever can clear a housing at both ends and strike it halfway through. Check which mate limits apply and whether the model passes through solid geometry because collision checking is not active. A motion preview checks the constraints represented in that preview. It does not automatically prove contact behavior, load capacity, friction, or real-world operation. In particular, a gear relation can drive two rotations even when the modeled teeth do not mesh correctly. Confirm that the input moves and connected parts follow in the expected direction. Inspect travel limits and intermediate positions for collisions and small gaps. Check that the components can be assembled and tools can reach the fasteners. Rebuild after changes and repeat the affected motion and interference checks. Review the final drawing and BOM against the checked assembly. A joint preview is not always a full-assembly test Onshape: Mates distinguishes the mate-dialog preview, which can ignore other mates, from assembly animation. Use a whole-assembly check to assess the connected mechanism. Spot a redundant mate Plan a motion check"},
{"title":"Choose from the required shape and material first","page":"Choosing a Fabrication Process","summary":"Match geometry, load, surfaces, and fit to filament printing or laser-cut sheet construction.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-a-fabrication-process.html#choose-from-the-required-shape-and-material-first","text":"Choose from the required shape and material first Start with what the part must do. A process is useful only if it can produce the geometry in a suitable material with the required performance. Filament printing Builds three-dimensional shapes in layers. It suits changing cross sections, bosses, and internal features when those features can be printed and supports can be removed. Layer direction and service temperature affect the choice. Laser-cut sheet Produces planar profiles from a sheet of existing thickness. It suits flat panels, plates, and repeated outlines. Three-dimensional structures need joints, stacked layers, or another forming operation. A flat nameplate with surface lettering is a natural laser candidate if the sheet is approved. A one-piece curved knob with a raised boss is a natural printing candidate if a suitable printable material meets the load and temperature requirements. Neither choice establishes production time without a job estimate. For setup details, use 3D Printing from CAD and Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD."},
{"title":"Design features the selected process can actually make","page":"Choosing a Fabrication Process","summary":"Match geometry, load, surfaces, and fit to filament printing or laser-cut sheet construction.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-a-fabrication-process.html#design-features-the-selected-process-can-actually-make","text":"Design features the selected process can actually make Printed walls and holes Check that the slicer generates the intended paths. Leave access for support removal. Do not assume a CAD hole prints at its nominal size; measure a representative sample. Laser-cut webs and corners Leave enough material between cuts for the part to survive fabrication and use. Account for kerf and heat effects. An infinitely sharp CAD corner does not guarantee an infinitely sharp cut corner. Sheet joints Design slots from measured sheet thickness and the intended clearance. Kerf compensation controls the cut edge; clearance controls how two finished parts fit. They solve different problems. Assembly Plan how pieces enter their final position and how they are held. A collision-free CAD assembly does not prove that real parts will fit or can be assembled; see Assembly Files and Mates. Why there is no universal minimum wall or perfect press-fit allowance Printer paths depend on nozzle and profile; laser edges depend on the sheet and setup. Material batches and finishing change dimensions too. A small test of the critical feature supplies evidence that a generic dimension cannot. Use Tolerances and Fits to distinguish the intended size from acceptable variation."},
{"title":"Test the critical feature before making the full part","page":"Choosing a Fabrication Process","summary":"Match geometry, load, surfaces, and fit to filament printing or laser-cut sheet construction.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-a-fabrication-process.html#test-the-critical-feature-before-making-the-full-part","text":"Test the critical feature before making the full part State the required shape, material, load direction, surface, and fit. Identify the feature most likely to fail: a thin wall, loaded layer joint, supported surface, or mating slot. Make an approved small sample that preserves that feature and the intended process setup. Measure or test the sample against the requirement. Record the setup and the result. Revise the design or setup, then compare time, material, cleanup, and assembly effort before making the full part. Practice selecting a process Practice clearance and kerf"},
{"title":"Ask what information is necessary to make this part","page":"Choosing Dimensions","summary":"Break a part into simple forms, define each feature’s size and location, and remove redundant requirements.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-dimensions.html#ask-what-information-is-necessary-to-make-this-part","text":"Ask what information is necessary to make this part Start with the geometry, not with the dimension tool. Break the part into familiar forms: blocks, cylinders, holes, steps, slots and arcs. A fresh rectangular plate with one off-center through hole is broken into a rectangular solid and a cylindrical opening. Three dimensions size the plate; one sizes the hole and two locate its center. For this simple plate, six independent numbers define the nominal geometry: length, height, thickness, hole diameter, and two center coordinates. THRU also defines the hole’s end condition. Size dimensions Describe the feature itself: a block’s length, width and thickness; a hole’s diameter; a slot’s width and length. Location dimensions Place that feature relative to other features or a chosen origin. A hole diameter alone does not say where the hole goes. Locate a round hole by its center, not by a tangent edge that moves when the diameter changes. Choose reference surfaces that make sense for how the part fits and is checked. A formal datum has a specific meaning in geometric tolerancing; an arbitrary sketch origin is not automatically a datum."},
{"title":"Dimension a feature in the view that shows its shape","page":"Choosing Dimensions","summary":"Break a part into simple forms, define each feature’s size and location, and remove redundant requirements.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-dimensions.html#dimension-a-feature-in-the-view-that-shows-its-shape","text":"Dimension a feature in the view that shows its shape Contour dimensioning keeps a feature’s dimensions with the view where its outline is clearest. Put a hole size on a view showing its circle. Put a step’s height where the step is visible. Use a section or another suitable view for an internal feature. Good: a hole diameter leader attaches to its visible circle in the front view. Bad: the same diameter is placed between hidden lines in the edge view. A hidden line tells you that an edge exists behind a surface; it is usually a poor place to attach a dimension. For the beginner drawings here, do not dimension to hidden lines: choose a view that exposes the feature. Keep the dimensions of one feature together when practical. See the Reading Drawings group in Engineering Graphics for projection, section views and view selection."},
{"title":"Build the dimension scheme in a deliberate order","page":"Choosing Dimensions","summary":"Break a part into simple forms, define each feature’s size and location, and remove redundant requirements.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-dimensions.html#build-the-dimension-scheme-in-a-deliberate-order","text":"Build the dimension scheme in a deliberate order Identify the simple forms and list the features that need size, location or orientation information. Choose the views that show those features clearly. Establish useful reference edges or surfaces. Add the main body sizes. Leave space for smaller feature dimensions nearer the outline. Add feature sizes and end conditions, such as a hole diameter and THRU. Locate the feature centers, steps and other boundaries from the chosen references. Check completeness, remove duplicate requirements, and arrange the lines for readability. Three stages on an original rectangular plate: body sizes 3.20 by 1.80 by.30; add a diameter.40 through hole; locate the center.90 from the left and.65 from the bottom. This sequence is a way to audit the information. CAD commands do not have to be clicked in this order. Do not add dimensions just because the CAD system offers them. Review each imported model dimension for purpose, view, placement and duplication."},
{"title":"Give each independent requirement once","page":"Choosing Dimensions","summary":"Break a part into simple forms, define each feature’s size and location, and remove redundant requirements.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-dimensions.html#give-each-independent-requirement-once","text":"Give each independent requirement once Completeness means every feature is defined. It does not mean every possible distance is dimensioned. A feature may need several dimensions, but the same size or location requirement should not be repeated in another view or restated by a closed chain. Good: two hole centers are located.80 and 2.30 from the left edge. Bad: the same scheme also controls their 1.50 center spacing, closing the chain. The nominal difference is 2.30 −.80 = 1.50. Controlling all three distances adds a redundant requirement. Choose the dimensions that express the design intent. A closed chain gives all the segments and their total as controlling dimensions. Remove one link, or make a useful derived value a reference dimension. Which link to remove depends on what the part must do. Why nominal arithmetic is not enough At the nominal values a closed chain may add up perfectly. Real parts vary within permitted limits, so the extra requirement can conflict with otherwise acceptable sizes. Changing one value also risks leaving its duplicate unchanged. The theory belongs in Tolerances and Fits."},
{"title":"Parentheses mark information, not an extra acceptance requirement","page":"Choosing Dimensions","summary":"Break a part into simple forms, define each feature’s size and location, and remove redundant requirements.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-dimensions.html#parentheses-mark-information-not-an-extra-acceptance-requirement","text":"Parentheses mark information, not an extra acceptance requirement A reference dimension is shown in parentheses, such as (1.50). It helps the reader without imposing another manufacturing or inspection requirement. Use one when the derived value is useful; leave it out when it only adds clutter. In the two-hole example,.80 and 2.30 can control the centers while (1.50) reports their nominal spacing. Parentheses do not mean that the physical distance cannot vary. A basic dimension, shown in a rectangular box, is different: it supplies a theoretically exact value for a geometric tolerance scheme. It does not demand a physically perfect part. See Tolerances and Fits before interpreting limits or fits. Find a redundant dimension Count the required dimensions"},
{"title":"Drawing views describe one part from different directions","page":"Choosing Drawing Views","summary":"Use flat views from different directions, with dimensions and notes, to describe a part clearly.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-drawing-views.html#drawing-views-describe-one-part-from-different-directions","text":"Drawing views describe one part from different directions A drawing view is a flat picture of a part seen from one direction. A set of views, read together with dimensions and notes, describes one three-dimensional part so someone can make or inspect it. A front view shows width and height. Looking from above or from the side can show the front-to-back depth that the front leaves out. In this stepped-block example, the front explains the step heights, while the top shows how far the block extends back. One stepped block viewed from the front, top, and right. The front has an L-shaped outline; the top shows its front-to-back depth, and the right shows that depth and the step heights. The picture on the left helps you recognize the part. The three flat views on the right show the same part from separate directions. Choosing views means selecting the directions that explain the shape clearly. Orthographic Projection explains how the views line up and how their positions tell you the viewing direction."},
{"title":"Choose the front view that explains the shape","page":"Choosing Drawing Views","summary":"Use flat views from different directions, with dimensions and notes, to describe a part clearly.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-drawing-views.html#choose-the-front-view-that-explains-the-shape","text":"Choose the front view that explains the shape The front view is a drawing of the part from the direction chosen as the starting point for the other views. The side shown need not be the one that happened to face you when you picked up the part. Start with a stable, understandable orientation. Prefer a view that shows characteristic shapes directly and keeps important features out of hidden lines, the dashed lines used for edges blocked from sight. For a stepped part, a profile that shows the steps is usually more useful than a plain rectangle. The front, top, and side are principal views: views along the part's main directions. Once the front is chosen, the other principal views follow from it. Keep their orientation and alignment consistent with Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box."},
{"title":"One or two views can be enough when the missing information is stated","page":"Choosing Drawing Views","summary":"Use flat views from different directions, with dimensions and notes, to describe a part clearly.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-drawing-views.html#one-or-two-views-can-be-enough-when-the-missing-information-is-stated","text":"One or two views can be enough when the missing information is stated One view A flat plate with a constant thickness can be defined by its face view and a thickness note, provided the outline, holes, and other requirements are completely specified. A circle alone does not say whether the part is a disk, a sphere, or a cylinder. Two views A constant-depth stepped shape can use a profile and a top view to establish the step widths, heights, and front-to-back depth. A third view may add no new information. A simple cylindrical part can often be described with a side view and an end view. Three views Front, top, and a side view are useful when features change across all three directions. Each view should resolve a question the others leave open. Three is a common arrangement, not a quota. Dimensions, notes, symmetry, and repeated geometry can supply information without drawing every side. The remaining views and written requirements must still define the whole part."},
{"title":"Add a view when it removes a real ambiguity","page":"Choosing Drawing Views","summary":"Use flat views from different directions, with dimensions and notes, to describe a part clearly.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-drawing-views.html#add-a-view-when-it-removes-a-real-ambiguity","text":"Add a view when it removes a real ambiguity A view is useful when it answers a question the existing views leave open. For example, the same circular front outline could belong to a thin disk or a long cylinder. A side view or a length note can resolve that difference. If two different parts still fit the drawing, identify the missing information and add the view or written callout that separates them. Hidden interior A section view can expose a bore or recess more clearly than a tangle of dashed lines. Its cutting-plane arrows tell you which way to look. Flat sloping surface An auxiliary view looks straight at the surface from an extra direction so its shape is clear. Another front, top, or side view may still make it appear shortened. See Auxiliary and Detail Views for that construction. Small feature An enlarged detail view makes a crowded feature readable. Read its stated scale; do not compare its apparent size directly with the main view. Why more pictures do not automatically mean more information Repeated information takes space and creates more places for a revision to disagree. A useful additional view makes something clearer: shape, depth, location, or access to a dimension. A pictorial can help orientation, but it does not automatically replace the views and callouts needed to define the part. The historical NASA GSFC manual (1994), §3.1 explicitly permits one or two views when they completely define an object. Its §3.5 describes sections, auxiliary views, and details. This is a useful design principle, not a claim that that manual governs every drawing."},
{"title":"Check the view set before moving on to dimensions","page":"Choosing Drawing Views","summary":"Use flat views from different directions, with dimensions and notes, to describe a part clearly.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/choosing-drawing-views.html#check-the-view-set-before-moving-on-to-dimensions","text":"Check the view set before moving on to dimensions Confirm that the front view exposes the most useful shape. Account for width, height, and depth without guessing. Locate every hole, step, slot, or recess in enough views to explain its direction and extent. Check hidden edges and the projection convention for consistency. Remove a redundant view only if the remaining views and callouts still define the part. Then continue to Drawing Dimensioning for placing the size and location information. Use Measurement Language for units and Tolerances and Fits for permitted variation. Practice choosing a view set"},
{"title":"Choose the model and sheet settings before placing views","page":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","summary":"Set up a sheet, place linked model views, add requirements, and check the drawing after every design change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/creating-drawings-from-cad-models.html#choose-the-model-and-sheet-settings-before-placing-views","text":"Choose the model and sheet settings before placing views Start with the intended part or assembly and confirm its configuration or version: the selected design variant or saved design state. A drawing of the wrong variant can look convincing and still describe the wrong product. See CAD Files and Documents and CAD History, Versions, and Sharing. A drawing template supplies starting settings. A sheet format supplies the border and title-block layout. CAD systems package these differently, so inspect the resulting sheet: size, units, projection method, scale, and dimension style all matter. Use the workflow below as a sequence of tasks. Interface names vary by product and release; these are generic descriptions rather than menu instructions for SOLIDWORKS or Onshape."},
{"title":"Place a base view, then derive aligned projected views","page":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","summary":"Set up a sheet, place linked model views, add requirements, and check the drawing after every design change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/creating-drawings-from-cad-models.html#place-a-base-view-then-derive-aligned-projected-views","text":"Place a base view, then derive aligned projected views Insert a base view from the model, choosing an orientation that communicates the shape clearly. Create projected views from that parent view, maintaining the required alignment and projection arrangement. Leave room for dimensions and notes. A rectangular block model supplies a drawing with a front base view, aligned top projected view above, and right projected view beside it in third-angle arrangement. The front and top share width, and front and right share height. The figure uses third-angle placement only as an example. Follow the drawing's declared projection method. Choose enough views to define the shape; three views are not a requirement for every part. Use Choosing Drawing Views, Section Views, and Auxiliary and Detail Views when ordinary views do not explain a feature clearly. Choose how to add a related view"},
{"title":"Distinguish dimensions that control geometry from dimensions that report it","page":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","summary":"Set up a sheet, place linked model views, add requirements, and check the drawing after every design change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/creating-drawings-from-cad-models.html#distinguish-dimensions-that-control-geometry-from-dimensions-that-report-it","text":"Distinguish dimensions that control geometry from dimensions that report it A driving model dimension controls geometry, such as the length of an extrusion. A dimension created by selecting drawing geometry usually reports that geometry. Changing a displayed label is not a sound way to change the part. Some CAD workflows import model dimensions into drawings and allow linked edits to affect the model. Check the dimension's type and behavior before editing. Do not assume that all drawing dimensions are editable model controls, or that all are independent annotations. Select dimensions to communicate manufacturing and inspection requirements, not merely every value used while modeling. Add needed tolerances, material information, and feature notes; CAD does not infer all design requirements from shape. Use Choosing Dimensions and Dimension Anatomy and Placement for the dimensioning method. Distinguish a dimension from a label"},
{"title":"Update linked views and inspect the sheet before release","page":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","summary":"Set up a sheet, place linked model views, add requirements, and check the drawing after every design change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/creating-drawings-from-cad-models.html#update-linked-views-and-inspect-the-sheet-before-release","text":"Update linked views and inspect the sheet before release Associativity is the connection between a model and the drawing information derived from it. When referenced geometry changes, linked views and dimensions can update to reflect it. Depending on the CAD system, update settings, and referenced version, an explicit update or reference change may be needed. An update is not a completed review. Deleted or replaced edges can leave dimensions detached from their intended features. Notes can become obsolete. A previously exported PDF stays a snapshot; generate and check a new export after approved changes. Confirm the referenced model, variant, and version, then update or rebuild the drawing as needed. Inspect every affected view for the intended geometry, alignment, and readable line display. Resolve detached dimensions and check that each dimension still refers to the intended feature. Review units, view scales, material, tolerances, and notes for completeness and conflicts. Complete the title block and required revision information; obtain the required review. Export the approved sheets and inspect the actual output for missing views, clipped text, and readability. See Working Drawings and Title Blocks for identifying the released information. Find the commands in your CAD version Start with the vendor's Onshape drawing help or SOLIDWORKS Help, selecting your installed release where applicable. Consult its drawing creation, projected view, dimension, and update topics. Command sequences are intentionally kept generic here. Review a model change"},
{"title":"A dimension connects a number to a specific feature","page":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","summary":"Read the lines, arrows and text of a dimension, then arrange them so the part stays easy to understand.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/dimension-anatomy-and-placement.html#a-dimension-connects-a-number-to-a-specific-feature","text":"A dimension connects a number to a specific feature A drawing shows the shape. Its dimensions tell the reader how big the features are and where they belong. Read the stated value; do not measure the picture. An original plate example labels the dimension line, extension lines, visible gap, arrowheads, dimension text and a leader pointing to a hole. Thin dimensioning lines keep the heavier part outline easy to follow. These teaching sketches illustrate dimensioning choices; they are not complete manufacturing drawings. Dimension line Runs in the direction being measured. Its arrowheads mark the extent of the dimension. Extension line Projects an edge, center or other point out to the dimension. Leave a visible gap at the part and continue a little beyond the last related dimension line. Arrowheads and text The arrows identify the endpoints; the text gives the value and any needed symbol. Use a consistent arrow style and readable text size. Leader A thin line connecting a note or callout to its feature. A hole leader ends with an arrow on the circle, directed toward its center. Keep the dimension line separate from the part outline. A visible edge or centerline is not a substitute for a dimension line. For line types and views, see the Engineering Graphics collection’s Reading Drawings group."},
{"title":"Put dimensions outside the view, with the shortest nearest the part","page":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","summary":"Read the lines, arrows and text of a dimension, then arrange them so the part stays easy to understand.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/dimension-anatomy-and-placement.html#put-dimensions-outside-the-view-with-the-shortest-nearest-the-part","text":"Put dimensions outside the view, with the shortest nearest the part Keep the outline clear. Put short dimensions close to the view and longer dimensions farther out. This also reduces the number of extension lines that cross dimension lines. Two good and bad comparisons: moving dimensions outside a tabbed plate clears its outline; putting the short dimension inside the overall dimension eliminates a crossing. Good placement makes both the shape and the dimension’s endpoints easier to find. A dimension inside the view can be appropriate when it is clearer there or avoids an excessively long leader. The aim is clarity, not an empty interior at any cost."},
{"title":"Leave room for the lines, arrows and text","page":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","summary":"Read the lines, arrows and text of a dimension, then arrange them so the part stays easy to understand.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/dimension-anatomy-and-placement.html#leave-room-for-the-lines-arrows-and-text","text":"Leave room for the lines, arrows and text Use at least 10 mm from the part outline to the first dimension line and 6 mm between successive parallel dimension lines as layout guides. Those are distances on the drawing sheet, regardless of the part’s scale. The familiar inch drafting guides are about 3/8 in and 1/4 in; they are rounded guides, not exact conversions. Good layout leaves 10 mm to the first dimension and 6 mm to the next, with visible extension-line gaps and overruns. Bad layout crowds both dimension lines against the outline. Check the final printed size. A readable CAD screen does not guarantee a readable reduced print. Keep the gap at the part visible and extend the extension line slightly beyond the dimension line. About 2 mm of overrun is a useful drafting target. Increase spacing when the annotation needs more room. Which parts are standards, and which are layout guides? ASME Y14.5 governs dimensioning conventions; its spacing guidance allows for legibility and reproduction. The current edition governs new ASME work unless the drawing or contract specifies another edition. ASME currently lists Y14.5-2018 (R2024). These pages explain conventions in plain words rather than reproducing the standard. For an existing drawing, read its stated standard and revision."},
{"title":"Keep text readable from the bottom of the sheet","page":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","summary":"Read the lines, arrows and text of a dimension, then arrange them so the part stays easy to understand.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/dimension-anatomy-and-placement.html#keep-text-readable-from-the-bottom-of-the-sheet","text":"Keep text readable from the bottom of the sheet Use unidirectional text for the ordinary linear dimensions here: the numbers read from the bottom even when the dimension line is vertical. Leave clear space around the number. On a horizontal line, the text may sit above it or in a break in it. Good and bad examples compare horizontal readable text with rotated text, outside arrows for a narrow dimension with crowded arrows, and staggered values with a tight column of numbers. Fit the value and arrows between the extension lines when there is room. Move them outside when there is not; do not shrink the lettering to squeeze it in. Stagger values on several parallel dimension lines so they are easier to separate. Keep lettering free of part outlines, extension lines and leaders. Why older drawings may look different Aligned text rotates with the dimension line. You may meet it on older drawings and in other drafting systems. “Old” and “new” alone are not enough to identify the applicable rule. These examples use the ordinary ASME orthographic convention; specialized coordinate dimensioning has additional presentation rules."},
{"title":"Rearrange crossing lines before accepting clutter","page":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","summary":"Read the lines, arrows and text of a dimension, then arrange them so the part stays easy to understand.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/dimension-anatomy-and-placement.html#rearrange-crossing-lines-before-accepting-clutter","text":"Rearrange crossing lines before accepting clutter Move dimensions or notes to avoid crossing dimension lines, extension lines and leaders wherever practical. Keep a leader short, with a clear arrow target and a short horizontal landing next to the note. Good leaders reach their own holes without crossing; bad leaders cross. A second comparison shows a radial leader hitting a circle clearly versus a tangent leader grazing it. A reader should be able to follow each line from value to feature without guessing. If extension lines must cross, normally leave them continuous. Do not introduce gaps that make their endpoints ambiguous. A break near an arrowhead can preserve its readability. Next: choose the information the drawing needs. Practice placement"},
{"title":"Identify the view before interpreting the shape","page":"Drawing Terminology Review","summary":"Recognize drawing terms used when turning a drawing into a CAD model, then follow links for the full methods.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/drawing-terminology-review.html#identify-the-view-before-interpreting-the-shape","text":"Identify the view before interpreting the shape This short vocabulary review supports CAD certification preparation. It is general drawing language, not a list of guaranteed exam terms. Orthographic view A view made with parallel lines of sight perpendicular to the viewing plane. Front shows width and height; top shows width and depth; a side view shows depth and height. See Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box. Isometric view A pictorial view in which the three principal axes are equally foreshortened, so their projections are drawn 120° apart. It helps you visualize the part; use stated dimensions to model it. Compare isometric with dimetric and trimetric views in CAD Model Views and Checks. Visible, hidden, and center lines Visible lines show edges you can see; hidden lines show concealed edges; center lines identify axes or centers. Review their patterns in The Alphabet of Lines."},
{"title":"Read dimensions and callouts as instructions about features","page":"Drawing Terminology Review","summary":"Recognize drawing terms used when turning a drawing into a CAD model, then follow links for the full methods.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/drawing-terminology-review.html#read-dimensions-and-callouts-as-instructions-about-features","text":"Read dimensions and callouts as instructions about features Dimension and extension lines A dimension gives a size or location. A dimension line is a thin line, usually ending in arrowheads, that shows the extent of the measurement; extension lines carry the edges of the measured feature out to it. Read the value and units instead of measuring a picture. See Dimension Anatomy and Placement. Diameter and radius Diameter spans a circle through its center; radius runs from its center to its edge. The diameter symbol is Ø; R denotes radius. Confusing them changes the modeled size. See how holes and arcs are called out in Hole and Feature Callouts. Hole and feature callout A note describing a feature, possibly including quantity, size, depth, or thread information. A through hole passes through the material; a blind hole stops within it. See Hole and Feature Callouts. Tolerance The permitted variation in a specified dimension. Read the stated limits or applicable drawing note; do not assume that every dimension has the same tolerance. See Dimension Anatomy and Placement. Practise radius and diameter"},
{"title":"Distinguish a section, a detail, and an auxiliary view","page":"Drawing Terminology Review","summary":"Recognize drawing terms used when turning a drawing into a CAD model, then follow links for the full methods.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/drawing-terminology-review.html#distinguish-a-section-a-detail-and-an-auxiliary-view","text":"Distinguish a section, a detail, and an auxiliary view Section view: shows the part as if cut by an imaginary plane so internal features can be read. A cutting-plane indication locates the cut; section lining identifies material crossed by it. See Section Views. Detail view: enlarges a selected area so small features are easier to read. Check its scale and labels. Auxiliary view: looks perpendicular to an inclined surface to show its true shape. Compare it with a detail view in Auxiliary and Detail Views. Before modeling, point to the view or callout that establishes each feature. If a shape is unclear, look for another view rather than guessing from a pictorial. Check the purpose of a detail view"},
{"title":"An exploded view separates parts to explain their relationship","page":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","summary":"Show how components fit together and connect drawing balloons to clear part identities and quantities.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/exploded-views-and-bills-of-materials.html#an-exploded-view-separates-parts-to-explain-their-relationship","text":"An exploded view separates parts to explain their relationship An assembled view shows the working arrangement. An exploded view spaces components apart so the reader can identify them and follow how they fit. The separation is for communication; it is not an operating clearance or a change to the part geometry. Separate components along understandable directions, keep related parts aligned, and use explode lines where the connection would otherwise be unclear. Preserve the working assembly and create a saved exploded view rather than deleting mates to pull the model apart. Side schematic of a pivot stack, assembled and exploded along one axis. Balloons identify support 1, lever 2, pin 3, washers 4 at two positions, and retainer 5. Original teaching schematic: the pin passes through the aligned holes; a retainer holds its end. Both washers use item 4. No manufacturing dimensions or fit are implied. Use the saved view in a drawing Onshape: Exploded Views describes saved explode steps and selecting the result in a drawing. An exploded presentation does not demonstrate that the parts can physically be installed in that order; check access separately."},
{"title":"A balloon finds a BOM row; a part number identifies the component","page":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","summary":"Show how components fit together and connect drawing balloons to clear part identities and quantities.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/exploded-views-and-bills-of-materials.html#a-balloon-finds-a-bom-row-a-part-number-identifies-the-component","text":"A balloon finds a BOM row; a part number identifies the component A bill of materials (BOM) lists the components required for the stated assembly. A drawing balloon points to a component and usually shows its BOM item number. The item number is a row identifier in that drawing's list. The part number identifies the part for reuse, manufacture, or purchase. One teaching pivot assembly — quantities per assembly Item Part number Description Quantity 1 PV-110 Support 1 2 PV-210 Lever 1 3 PV-310 Pivot pin 1 4 HW-410 Thrust washer 2 5 HW-510 Pin retainer 1 Balloon 4 identifies HW-410, not four washers. Its quantity is two per assembly. This list has five distinct rows and six component instances. Repeated instances of the same specified part share an item number; different sizes or variants need distinguishable identities. Linked tables reduce manual mismatches Onshape: BOM Table describes inserting the assembly's BOM and using balloons to pull its identifiers. Keep the drawing, table, and view tied to the same intended design state."},
{"title":"Decide whether the BOM lists units or their internal parts","page":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","summary":"Show how components fit together and connect drawing balloons to clear part identities and quantities.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/exploded-views-and-bills-of-materials.html#decide-whether-the-bom-lists-units-or-their-internal-parts","text":"Decide whether the BOM lists units or their internal parts A top-level list can show a purchased subassembly as one unit. A structured list can show the assembly hierarchy, while a flattened list collects the underlying components without that hierarchy. State the scope before interpreting a quantity. Do not order a complete unit and all its contents accidentally. Verify part numbers, descriptions, quantities, and relevant configuration or revision information. A library model's filename is not automatically a useful purchasing description. The teaching identifiers above are examples, not an orderable hardware specification. Subassembly display changes what the table means Onshape: Bill of Materials documents structured and flattened views and subassembly BOM behavior. A hidden or excluded row can change what the reader sees, so review the table's scope instead of trusting a row count alone."},
{"title":"Review the assembly drawing as an identification tool","page":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","summary":"Show how components fit together and connect drawing balloons to clear part identities and quantities.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/exploded-views-and-bills-of-materials.html#review-the-assembly-drawing-as-an-identification-tool","text":"Review the assembly drawing as an identification tool Show an assembled view that makes the working arrangement understandable. Add an exploded or section view where hidden relationships need explanation. Follow each balloon leader to the correct component and match its number to the BOM. Count repeated components and compare the counts with the stated BOM scope. Check the drawing's design state, units, scale, and necessary assembly notes. An assembly drawing explains how parts belong together. Individual part drawings supply the manufacturing detail for those parts; do not crowd every part dimension onto an assembly view. See Choosing Drawing Views and the Engineering Graphics and CAD collection for related drawing topics. Read a balloon Count repeated parts"},
{"title":"Extrude carries a cross-section in a straight direction","page":"Extrude and Revolve","summary":"Turn a sketch into a straight or rotational solid, and choose an end condition that preserves the intended shape when dimensions change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/extrude-and-revolve.html#extrude-carries-a-cross-section-in-a-straight-direction","text":"Extrude carries a cross-section in a straight direction For a plain solid extrusion, select a closed sketch region and give it depth. A rectangular region makes a block; a circular region makes a cylinder. Without draft, the cross-section remains constant along the extrusion. A boss/base extrusion creates or adds solid material. An extruded cut removes material where the extrusion intersects the target solid. Check the arrow, preview, and selected bodies. A cut pointing away from the part cannot remove material from it. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A closed rectangle is carried along a depth direction to create a rectangular solid. Sketch → straight depth → solid. This schematic shows the geometry, not a software dialog. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. In section, a rectangular sketch on a block is cut downward to leave a flat-bottomed pocket. Section through a rectangular cut: the floor and surrounding body remain. In Onshape, choose the result operation New, Add, or Remove. In SOLIDWORKS, check the boss/cut command and merge or feature scope settings. Adding touching material and creating a separate body are different intentions. Closed profiles, thin features, and surfaces Closed regions are the simplest starting point for solids. Open sketches can be used with thin-feature or surface options, but those are different constructions: a surface has no solid volume, while a thin feature adds a wall thickness. Check the result type before accepting the preview. See Onshape: Extrude."},
{"title":"Choose the end condition for what should happen after an edit","page":"Extrude and Revolve","summary":"Turn a sketch into a straight or rotational solid, and choose an end condition that preserves the intended shape when dimensions change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/extrude-and-revolve.html#choose-the-end-condition-for-what-should-happen-after-an-edit","text":"Choose the end condition for what should happen after an edit Condition What controls the end Use when Blind A specified distance in the selected direction. A pocket must keep a particular depth. Through all All target material along the selected direction. A cut must remain through as the part thickens. Check body scope and one- versus two-direction settings. Up to next The next terminating surface that intercepts the entire profile. The next suitable boundary is the intended stop. It is not simply the first edge encountered. Up to surface / face A selected terminating surface or face. The feature must follow that particular boundary when it moves. The selection becomes a dependency. Mid-plane A total depth split equally about the sketch plane. The part must stay centered. In SOLIDWORKS, a 14 mm Mid Plane depth extends 7 mm each way. Do not substitute an oversized blind cut for a through-all requirement. It may work today and leave a thin floor after a later edit. Conversely, through all is wrong for a pocket that must retain its floor. Onshape uses a Symmetric option for supported end types. Dialog names and available conditions depend on the selected operation; inspect the preview and measured result rather than transferring every setting by name. Practice end conditions Practice symmetric depth Vendor help for extrusion boundaries See SOLIDWORKS: Extrude PropertyManager and Onshape: Extrude. A failed up-to feature may indicate that its target no longer covers the profile, not that the sketch is wrong."},
{"title":"Revolve rotates a section around an axis","page":"Extrude and Revolve","summary":"Turn a sketch into a straight or rotational solid, and choose an end condition that preserves the intended shape when dimensions change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/extrude-and-revolve.html#revolve-rotates-a-section-around-an-axis","text":"Revolve rotates a section around an axis Select a section, an axis of rotation, and an angle. A full turn is 360°; a smaller angle produces a partial rotational shape. A boss revolve adds material, and a revolved cut removes it. Start with a closed section on one side of the axis for a straightforward solid revolve. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A rectangular section offset from a vertical axis rotates through 360 degrees to make a hollow cylindrical sleeve. The gap between the section and the axis becomes the bore. Rotating a section that reaches the axis can instead make a solid center. A straight sketch line, centerline, or reference axis can define rotation when supported by the command. The axis must be deliberately located. A diameter describes the distance across a round shape; the distance from the axis to its outer wall is the radius. A stepped sleeve can often be built with one revolved section including its bore. Repeated extrusions and cuts can also build it, but put the coaxial steps under separate controls. Choose the sequence that makes the important dimensions easiest to change. Check overlap and axis selection before adding complexity A section that crosses the axis, overlaps itself during rotation, or creates zero-thickness contact can fail. Simplify the section, verify its closure and axis, and inspect a small-angle preview. For the command entry points see SOLIDWORKS: Sketching with Extruded or Revolved Bosses/Bases. A turned part may suggest a revolve, but CAD history does not specify a lathe process; see Lathe Operations."},
{"title":"Pick the base feature from the shape's main organizing rule","page":"Extrude and Revolve","summary":"Turn a sketch into a straight or rotational solid, and choose an end condition that preserves the intended shape when dimensions change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/extrude-and-revolve.html#pick-the-base-feature-from-the-shape-s-main-organizing-rule","text":"Pick the base feature from the shape's main organizing rule Use extrusion when a useful section stays the same along a straight direction. Use revolution when a useful section repeats around an axis. A cylinder can be made either way. A rotationally stepped part often makes the reason for choosing revolve clearer than a plain cylinder does. Before accepting either feature, confirm the profile, direction or axis, end condition or angle, and result body. Then make one trial dimension change. Does the model still express the intent described in Feature-based Modeling? Practice choosing a base Practice a scoped feature count"},
{"title":"Build the main shape, then add and remove material","page":"Feature-based Modeling","summary":"Plan a solid part as a sequence of features, choose stable references, and test whether the model survives a change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/feature-based-modeling.html#build-the-main-shape-then-add-and-remove-material","text":"Build the main shape, then add and remove material A parametric model stores instructions for building a shape. Dimensions and relationships are inputs; the solid is the result. Change an input and rebuild to see the result update. The base feature creates the initial solid. Later features add material, remove material, or modify faces and edges. For a simple spacer, start with its main cross-section and extrude it. For a stepped round knob, a revolved section may describe the main shape more directly. There is often more than one valid feature sequence. A body is a connected piece of solid geometry. A SOLIDWORKS part file can contain multiple bodies. An Onshape Part Studio can create multiple parts from a shared feature list. Neither arrangement, by itself, defines assembly motion or assembly relationships. Start with sketch dimensions and relations in the Engineering Graphics collection. Then choose extrude or revolve for a straight or rotational shape, or sweep or loft for a path or a changing cross-section."},
{"title":"The feature tree records a build sequence and its dependencies","page":"Feature-based Modeling","summary":"Plan a solid part as a sequence of features, choose stable references, and test whether the model survives a change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/feature-based-modeling.html#the-feature-tree-records-a-build-sequence-and-its-dependencies","text":"The feature tree records a build sequence and its dependencies Read the tree from the base feature toward the end. A feature that supplies a sketch, face, edge, or other reference is a parent; a feature that uses that reference is a child. A child can depend on more than one parent. Being later in the list does not, by itself, make a feature a direct child of every earlier feature. For example: a plate extrusion supplies a top face for a hole-position sketch. A hole feature uses that sketch. A pattern uses that hole as its seed. Delete the hole and the pattern loses its seed; delete the plate and the sketch loses its support as well. Inspect the deletion dialog before accepting dependent deletions. Rename features for their purpose, such as Main plate, Locating hole, and Hole row. Renaming a feature in the tree is different from renaming a referenced part file. File references need their own reference-aware workflow. Why a small edit can break a later feature A face or edge can disappear when the shape changes. A sketch attached to that face, or a fillet attached to that edge, then has a missing reference. Repair the first failed feature before chasing errors farther down the tree. Reordering is constrained by dependencies: a feature cannot use geometry that has not yet been created."},
{"title":"Rollback lets you inspect an earlier stage without deleting later work","page":"Feature-based Modeling","summary":"Plan a solid part as a sequence of features, choose stable references, and test whether the model survives a change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/feature-based-modeling.html#rollback-lets-you-inspect-an-earlier-stage-without-deleting-later-work","text":"Rollback lets you inspect an earlier stage without deleting later work Move the rollback bar above later features to inspect or edit an earlier stage. Return it to the end when checking the completed part. Rollback is a temporary stopping point in regeneration. Suppression instead leaves a selected feature in the tree but excludes it from the active model; dependent features may also be affected. Hiding changes visibility, not the solid's definition. Find the earliest feature that fails or produces the wrong shape. Roll back to inspect the geometry and references available at that point. Edit the sketch for its dimensions or relations; edit the feature for its operation, depth, direction, or selected references. Roll forward to the end, rebuild, and inspect the downstream results. Vendor help for history and rollback See SOLIDWORKS: Rollback Bar, Suppress and Unsuppress Features, and Onshape: Feature and Part Lists. Onshape document history records editing activity; moving a Part Studio rollback bar is not restoring an older document version."},
{"title":"Choose references that express what must stay aligned","page":"Feature-based Modeling","summary":"Plan a solid part as a sequence of features, choose stable references, and test whether the model survives a change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/feature-based-modeling.html#choose-references-that-express-what-must-stay-aligned","text":"Choose references that express what must stay aligned A reference plane locates a sketch or defines symmetry. A reference axis supplies a direction or center of rotation. Neither adds material. Use the origin and default planes when they express the design; add offset, angled, or mid-planes when needed. Reference geometry can also have parents. Design intent is what should stay true when dimensions change. A centered plate should grow equally on both sides of its center plane. A hole required to pass through the plate should remain through when the plate gets thicker. A row of identical holes should share one seed definition and a spacing rule. Put major shape and functional references early. Add small edge treatments later when they are finishing details. This is a useful starting order, not a rule that every fillet must be last: a large blend that defines the main shape may belong before shelling or other features. Fewest features is not always the best model A single complicated sketch can hide many unrelated decisions. Several named features may be easier to edit. Count features only after stating which operations are allowed and what the model must do when changed. A feature tree is also not a manufacturing operation sheet: a CAD cut does not specify tooling or setups."},
{"title":"Check units, bodies, material, and mass after a change","page":"Feature-based Modeling","summary":"Plan a solid part as a sequence of features, choose stable references, and test whether the model survives a change.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/feature-based-modeling.html#check-units-bodies-material-and-mass-after-a-change","text":"Check units, bodies, material, and mass after a change Assign the intended material or density before relying on mass. A surface color is not evidence that density is correct. For a uniform solid, mass = density × volume. Compare volume with a simple estimate, and inspect unexpected mass or center-of-mass changes. A fresh example: a solid rectangular spacer measuring 30 × 20 × 8 mm has a volume of 4,800 mm³ = 4.8 cm³. With an assumed density of 2.50 g/cm³, its mass is 12.0 g. These are practice values, not a material specification. A hole should reduce that mass if the same body and density remain selected. Confirm the document units and overall dimensions. Confirm the expected body or part count and the scope of each add or cut. Change one important dimension, rebuild, and check the intended relationships. Check material assignment, selected bodies, and any mass overrides before trusting the result. Practice a mass check Practice dependencies Mass properties are a check, not proof of a correct part Different shapes can have the same volume and mass. Check the geometry as well. Onshape omits parts without assigned material from density-based calculations; inspect its notices and selection. See Onshape: Mass Properties Tool and SOLIDWORKS: Bodies and Features of Multibody Parts."},
{"title":"Check the projection convention before naming a side view","page":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","summary":"Identify the projection symbol before interpreting where the top and side views sit on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/first-and-third-angle-projection.html#check-the-projection-convention-before-naming-a-side-view","text":"Check the projection convention before naming a side view First-angle and third-angle projection can show the same part from the same directions. What changes is where those views are arranged on the sheet. Look for the projection symbol or an explicit note, often near the title block. Use it with the view labels. If the symbol and the views disagree, resolve the inconsistency before interpreting the part. View placement relative to the front view Named view Third-angle First-angle Top Above Below Bottom Below Above Right side To the right To the left Left side To the left To the right A right-side view is always the view from the part's right. In first-angle projection, it sits to the left of the front view. Do not rename it a left-side view because of its position."},
{"title":"The projection symbol is two views of a shortened cone","page":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","summary":"Identify the projection symbol before interpreting where the top and side views sit on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/first-and-third-angle-projection.html#the-projection-symbol-is-two-views-of-a-shortened-cone","text":"The projection symbol is two views of a shortened cone The trapezoid is the side view of a cone with its tip removed, called a frustum. The two concentric circles are its end view. Their arrangement tells you which projection method is being used. Projection symbols with the frustum's small end on the left: third-angle has the circular end view left of the trapezoid; first-angle has it on the right. Below, third-angle places top above front and right at right; first-angle places top below front and right at left. Read the circle's position together with the taper direction. These examples hold the frustum orientation fixed so the difference is easy to see. Use the glass box to explain the swap In third-angle projection, the viewing plane is between the observer and the object. In first-angle projection, the object is between the observer and the viewing plane. Unfolding those planes places the named views on different sides of the front view. The angle names refer to projection quadrants. They do not mean rotating the part by one degree or three degrees, and they do not specify the number of views. Compare the primary teaching diagrams in NPTEL's first- and third-angle lesson. Autodesk's drawing documentation also explains how the selected convention controls projected-view placement."},
{"title":"Expect third-angle on U.S. mechanical drawings, then verify it","page":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","summary":"Identify the projection symbol before interpreting where the top and side views sit on the sheet.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/first-and-third-angle-projection.html#expect-third-angle-on-u-s-mechanical-drawings-then-verify-it","text":"Expect third-angle on U.S. mechanical drawings, then verify it Third-angle is the usual U.S. mechanical drawing convention. A shared convention lets the designer and the person making or inspecting the part interpret the layout the same way. It is a communication choice; neither method changes the part's geometry. The drawing's declared convention is the deciding information. Country of origin, inch units, or a familiar company name is not a substitute for checking. Drawings cross borders, and CAD templates can use different defaults. A documented U.S. example, with its scope The NASA Goddard Engineering Drawing Standards Manual (1994), §3.1, printed p. 25 states: Third-angle orthographic projection shall be used for mechanical engineering drawings. That is a requirement in that manual, not proof that every U.S. drawing uses third-angle. Autodesk's Fusion documentation gives a current software example: its ASME drawing setting uses third-angle projection. Bricsys's projection-type reference likewise identifies third-angle with U.S. practice. Use the view explorer to switch layouts while holding the part fixed. Then work through the projection self-check."},
{"title":"Predict how an anchored rectangle grows, then test its remaining motion","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#predict-how-an-anchored-rectangle-grows-then-test-its-remaining-motion","text":"Predict how an anchored rectangle grows, then test its remaining motion Rectangle constraint lab. Start with a 64 × 22 mm rectangular label blank. Predict where its left and right edges will be when the driving width changes to 80 mm. Compare a lower-left corner at the origin with a center at the origin. Dashed: 64 mm width. Solid: 80 mm width. Left: corner anchored. Right: centered. Both retain 22 mm height. Width changes from 64 to 80 mm; both sketches are fully defined before and after Origin choice Left x (mm) Right x (mm) Free motion Lower-left corner 0 → 0 64 → 80 0 DOF Center −32 → −40 32 → 40 0 DOF Diagnose the freedom: keep width and height driving, but remove only the horizontal anchor. The rectangle can translate horizontally: 1 DOF remains. Remove the width instead while retaining both anchors and height; the corner-anchored rectangle's right edge can move, or the centered rectangle can grow symmetrically. Restore the conditions before testing the intended size edit. Measure versus constrain: a top-edge reference dimension reports the current width and adds no equation. A second driving width that agrees with the first repeats the same equation: five displayed conditions can still have only four independent conditions. A different second driving width contradicts the first; there is no feasible rectangle, so the lab retains its last valid drawing rather than displaying an invented solution. Bounded model: one closed axis-aligned rectangle, with four variables [left x, right x, bottom y, top y]. Closure, rectangular shape and orientation are built in. For a feasible set, DOF = 4 − rank (the independent equation count). Width and height stay between 1 and 120 mm; these bounds restrict admissible sizes and do not count as equality constraints. The lab projects toward the nearest solution of the active equalities and rejects changes outside its size range. It models no rotation or other sketch entities and does not reproduce a vendor's solver or error colors. With JavaScript enabled, use the prediction, size controls and two draggable corners. Keyboard: focus a corner and press arrow keys; Shift changes the requested step from 1 to 5 mm. Undo diagnostic motion restores the previous drawing. The interactive controls need JavaScript. The worked comparison and diagnosis above provide the same reasoning for reading and printing. Practice: predict centered growth Practice: reference dimensions and freedom"},
{"title":"Count independent ways the geometry can still change","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#count-independent-ways-the-geometry-can-still-change","text":"Count independent ways the geometry can still change A degree of freedom (DOF) is one independent variable still available to change. Constraints remove freedom. Count the geometry's possible motion, not the number of icons. Simple, isolated 2D entities before any dimensions or relations Entity Free variables DOF Point x and y position 2 Nonzero line segment x and y for each endpoint 4 Circle Center x, center y, radius 3 Circular arc Center x, center y, radius, two endpoint angles 5 A circle's diameter dimension removes one DOF; its center can still move in two directions. Making its center coincident with the origin removes those two. A line with one endpoint at the origin still has length and direction to determine. Why subtracting one per relation gives wrong answers Coinciding a free point with a fixed point supplies two coordinate conditions. Putting a free point on a fixed line supplies only one. A repeated rule may supply no new independent condition. These counts assume ordinary, nondegenerate geometry: nonzero line lengths and positive radii. At special configurations, independent equations and usable motion require more care; do not assign a universal DOF reduction to each icon."},
{"title":"A driving dimension controls size; a driven dimension reports it","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#a-driving-dimension-controls-size-a-driven-dimension-reports-it","text":"A driving dimension controls size; a driven dimension reports it A driving dimension is a constraint: changing its value asks the solver to change the geometry while keeping the other rules. Length, angle, radius, diameter, and location dimensions can all supply constraints. A driven or reference dimension measures a result already controlled elsewhere. It does not remove another DOF. For example, a circle whose size is set by a 24 mm driving diameter already has a 12 mm radius; a second driving radius dimension duplicates that control. Use a relation for an enduring geometric rule and a dimension for the independent value you intend to change. Two lengths that must remain equal need an equal relation and one driving length, rather than two unrelated numbers that happen to match today. Onshape automatically makes a newly added dimension driven if it would over-define the sketch. Check whether a displayed value is driving before expecting it to control geometry. Software treatment of redundant dimensions SOLIDWORKS can make a redundant dimension driven; its geometry-status help describes the driven dimension as gray. Onshape's Dimension tool distinguishes driving from driven dimensions and documents the automatic conversion. See Sketch Geometry Status and Onshape: Dimension. For drawing presentation and dimension placement, see Dimension Anatomy and Placement."},
{"title":"Read the sketch state in the software you are using","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#read-the-sketch-state-in-the-software-you-are-using","text":"Read the sketch state in the software you are using Under-defined / underconstrained: some position, orientation, or size remains free. Fully defined / fully constrained: no free motion remains with the current driving values. Editing those values can still change the sketch. Over-defined / overconstrained: dimensions or relations conflict or add control the solver treats as redundant. Repair the conflicting set. Documented colors for sketch geometry while editing; selection and hover highlighting are separate Software Under-defined Fully defined Problems SOLIDWORKS 2026 Blue Black Geometry-status help identifies yellow for conflicting or invalid items and red for unsolvable items; a redundant driving dimension can be red. Onshape Blue Black Red geometry indicates a constraint problem. A problem constraint icon is white on red. Read status messages and inspect relations as well as color. One black edge does not prove that the entire sketch is fully defined. An under-defined construction line or endpoint can be easy to overlook. Current vendor references for status colors SOLIDWORKS: Sketch Geometry Status distinguishes several error states. Onshape: Troubleshooting Sketch Geometry documents geometry and constraint-icon colors. These are editing-state conventions, not a universal color code for every CAD display."},
{"title":"Fully define a sketch in a repeatable order","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#fully-define-a-sketch-in-a-repeatable-order","text":"Fully define a sketch in a repeatable order State what must stay aligned, centered, equal, or tangent when the size changes. Choose the plane and an origin reference that suit that intent. Confirm the working units. Draw the simplest useful entities. Check the automatic relations and remove unintended ones. Connect the intended endpoints and apply the geometric relations that describe the shape. Anchor the sketch's position to the origin or other stable references; control its orientation. Add independent driving dimensions for the remaining sizes and locations. Inspect the remaining free points and curves. Drag gently to diagnose motion, then undo the diagnostic move. Resolve conflicts before adding more constraints. Inspect the whole sketch, including construction geometry. Change one intended driving value, check that the sketch behaves correctly, then restore the value. Fully defined is a useful check, not proof of good design intent. Fixing a misplaced point can remove freedom without giving it the correct location. A sketch can be completely constrained by dimensions that make future edits awkward."},
{"title":"Test design intent with a change, not just the starting shape","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#test-design-intent-with-a-change-not-just-the-starting-shape","text":"Test design intent with a change, not just the starting shape Fresh practice part: a circular inspection plate. Sketch two circular boundaries for an annular plate. Put their common center at the origin; use concentric and separate diameter dimensions of 46 mm and 18 mm. Increasing the outer diameter to 58 mm should leave the inner opening centered and unchanged in size. Fresh practice part: a rectangular label blank. Use an axis-aligned rectangle with its lower-left corner at the origin, width 64 mm, and height 22 mm. Increasing the width to 80 mm should move the right edge while the left edge stays at the origin. If the intended part must grow equally left and right, choose a centered construction instead. These two origin choices describe different editing behavior. There is no single best anchor for every part. Decide what should stay put before dimensioning. Practice: count remaining freedom Practice: spot duplicate control"},
{"title":"Repair the unwanted rule, then check the intended change again","page":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","summary":"Remove unwanted freedom with relations and dimensions, then test whether edits preserve the intended shape.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/fully-defined-sketches.html#repair-the-unwanted-rule-then-check-the-intended-change-again","text":"Repair the unwanted rule, then check the intended change again When an added dimension causes an error, inspect what already controls that measurement. Keep the relation or dimension that expresses the intended design. Remove the duplicate, or make a measurement driven if it is only there to report a result. A horizontal line cannot also have a nonzero vertical separation between its endpoints. Equal circles cannot have different driving diameters. These are contradictions; adding still more dimensions will not solve them. SOLIDWORKS offers Display/Delete Relations and SketchXpert for diagnosis. Onshape's Constraint manager filters constraints and dimensions to help locate problems. A suggested deletion is still a design decision: check what behavior you would lose. Redundancy is not the same as contradiction A repeated condition may agree mathematically yet be rejected as a second driving control. Some redundant geometric relations are tolerated: SOLIDWORKS documents a redundant perpendicular relation that leaves a sketch fully defined. Do not assume that every extra icon causes an error. See SOLIDWORKS: FAQ Sketch Relations and Onshape: Working with Constraints. Return to Geometric Relations in Sketches to compare rules, or Sketch Entities and Planes to review the geometry they act on."},
{"title":"Choose a graphic for the question you need to answer","page":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","summary":"Use sketches to explore ideas, models to develop geometry, and drawings to communicate a design for making and checking.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/graphics-in-the-design-process.html#choose-a-graphic-for-the-question-you-need-to-answer","text":"Choose a graphic for the question you need to answer Engineering graphics communicate technical ideas and problem solutions. Aim for accuracy, simplicity, and clarity. A graphic succeeds when another person can use it for the intended task. Ideation sketch A quick drawing for exploring and comparing ideas. Designers and teammates use it to discuss shape, motion, or arrangement before committing to detailed geometry. Label uncertain sizes and assumptions. CAD model A digital description of geometry. Designers use it to develop parts, examine how components fit, and support analysis or manufacturing preparation. A realistic appearance alone does not prove that the design works. Engineering drawing Selected views with dimensions, tolerances, and notes. People making, assembling, and inspecting the product use this information to understand what is required. See Working Drawings and Title Blocks. Choose a graphic for brainstorming"},
{"title":"Use testing to send the design back for refinement","page":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","summary":"Use sketches to explore ideas, models to develop geometry, and drawings to communicate a design for making and checking.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/graphics-in-the-design-process.html#use-testing-to-send-the-design-back-for-refinement","text":"Use testing to send the design back for refinement Begin with the task and its requirements: what must the design do, and what limits must it satisfy? Sketch alternatives, develop promising geometry, then test it against those requirements. Testing may involve calculations, simulation, a prototype, or a combination. Design sequence: define the need, sketch alternatives, develop a CAD model and draft drawings, test, refine, then check and release. A return arrow sends refinement back to the model and drawings. When a test reveals a problem, revise the design and test again. Update the affected graphics so that the model, drawings, and test record describe the same design. Why the process is a loop A model can reveal a space conflict that was hard to see in a sketch. A prototype can reveal a behavior that the model did not predict. These findings can send the team back to the geometry or even to the original concept. Sketches remain useful during refinement; drawings can be drafted before the design is final. Choose a graphic for a fit review"},
{"title":"Release identifies the information approved for use","page":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","summary":"Use sketches to explore ideas, models to develop geometry, and drawings to communicate a design for making and checking.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/graphics-in-the-design-process.html#release-identifies-the-information-approved-for-use","text":"Release identifies the information approved for use Release means approving a defined design state for its intended use through the organization's review process. Before release, check the model, drawing set, and supporting requirements together. A saved file is not automatically an approved file. A maker needs part requirements; an assembler needs component relationships; an inspector needs criteria for deciding whether the result is acceptable. Match the information to the reader's job. For drawing-based work, use Creating Drawings from CAD Models to prepare the sheets and CAD History, Versions, and Sharing to distinguish an editable design from a preserved milestone. Does every design require a separate drawing? Some workflows communicate requirements through an annotated model and associated data. Others use drawings as the controlling documents. Follow the agreed deliverables and identify which information governs. A plain shaded model does not automatically communicate material, tolerances, or approval status."},
{"title":"Follow the specified drawing standard and its applicable edition","page":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","summary":"Use sketches to explore ideas, models to develop geometry, and drawings to communicate a design for making and checking.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/graphics-in-the-design-process.html#follow-the-specified-drawing-standard-and-its-applicable-edition","text":"Follow the specified drawing standard and its applicable edition Standards give drawing readers shared rules for interpreting views, lines, dimensions, and symbols. The ASME Y14 series is widely used in US engineering drawing practice; ISO technical drawing standards are widely used internationally. These are not exclusive geographic boundaries. Read the drawing's stated requirements rather than choosing a convention from a country name. A convention is an accepted way of presenting information. A company may add conventions for sheet layout or numbering, but those choices must remain consistent with the governing requirements. Use the current applicable edition specified for the work; do not silently apply an old classroom example or change an existing drawing's governing edition. Check units, projection method, and the stated standards before interpreting a drawing. Continue with Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box and Choosing Drawing Views for view interpretation. This overview does not reproduce standard clauses or serve as a compliance checklist. Check the governing convention"},
{"title":"Read the units before reading the numbers","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#read-the-units-before-reading-the-numbers","text":"Read the units before reading the numbers Find the drawing’s unit note or title block first. On an inch drawing,.625 means 0.625 inch, not 625 inches and not millimeters. The unit need not be repeated beside every value when the drawing states it. Decimal-inch dimensions conventionally omit the leading zero below one inch. Keep trailing zeros:.50 and.500 have the same nominal size, but may select different rows in the drawing’s general tolerance block. There is no universal “three decimals means ±.005” rule; read the actual block and any local tolerance. An angle still needs its angle notation: 90°. Mixed units must be identified. For units and precision, use Measurement Language; for tolerance values, limits and fits, use Tolerances and Fits."},
{"title":"Use a diameter for a full circle and a radius for an arc","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#use-a-diameter-for-a-full-circle-and-a-radius-for-an-arc","text":"Use a diameter for a full circle and a radius for an arc ⌀ before a value means diameter. R before a value means radius. Thus ⌀.60 describes a full circular diameter of.60, while R.30 describes an arc radius of.30. The values are related, but the callouts describe different kinds of feature. A full hole has a diameter.60 callout and a rounded corner has radius.30. A separate rectangular pattern uses one 4X diameter.20 THRU callout for four identical holes, with center locations supplied independently. One 4X ⌀.20 THRU callout applies the same size and end condition to four identified holes. It does not locate them. n X means the requirement applies in n places. A repeated size still needs an unambiguous pattern: center coordinates, spacing, or a fully defined bolt circle. A sketch that merely looks evenly spaced is not enough."},
{"title":"A round-ended slot needs size, location and direction","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#a-round-ended-slot-needs-size-location-and-direction","text":"A round-ended slot needs size, location and direction For a straight slot with semicircular ends, give its width and either its overall length or the distance between the end centers. Make the chosen meaning clear. Then locate the slot and establish its direction relative to the part. Two alternative size schemes for the same straight slot:.50 width and 1.80 overall length, or two radius.25 ends and 1.30 between their centers. These are alternatives, not dimensions to combine. For this geometry, overall length = center spacing + width: 1.30 +.50 = 1.80. The drawing must also specify the slot’s location and whether it goes through or stops at a depth. Do not control the width, end radius, center spacing and overall length all at once. That repeats the relationships already built into the semicircular-ended shape. If a slot is not semicircular-ended, this shortcut does not apply."},
{"title":"Read a hole callout from the main hole to the added recess","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#read-a-hole-callout-from-the-main-hole-to-the-added-recess","text":"Read a hole callout from the main hole to the added recess The first size usually describes the main hole. Read the remaining symbols with the values immediately following them. All examples below are invented decimal-inch teaching values. Callout How it reads ⌀.30 THRU A.30 diameter opening goes through the part. THRU is an end condition, not a numeric depth. ⌀.30 ↧.55 A.30 diameter blind hole has.55 depth to the full-diameter portion. A drill-point cone, if present, extends farther unless otherwise specified. ⌀.30 THRU ⌴ ⌀.60 ↧.15 The.30 through hole has a cylindrical, flat-bottomed counterbore:.60 diameter and.15 deep from the entry surface. ⌀.30 THRU ⌵ ⌀.60 × 90° The.30 through hole has a conical countersink:.60 major diameter at the entry and a 90° included angle, measured across both sides of the cone. Original section sketches compare a through hole, a blind hole with full-diameter depth above its drill point, a flat-bottomed counterbore and a conical countersink. ⌀ = diameter; ↧ = depth; ⌴ = counterbore; ⌵ = countersink. Symbol shapes in a CAD font may look slightly different from browser text; the sketches show their meaning. Read the leader and section view to identify the entry side. A hole callout supplies size and end condition; it still needs location dimensions. A countersink angle is not automatically 90°."},
{"title":"A spotface provides a local flat seat","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#a-spotface-provides-a-local-flat-seat","text":"A spotface provides a local flat seat A spotface provides a flat circular seating surface around a hole. Its modern symbol puts SF inside the counterbore-shaped mark. Older drawings may use the counterbore mark with a note or context identifying the spotface. An original spotface sketch shows a shallow flat seat and the SF-in-counterbore symbol. A comparison translates legacy DIA, DP, CBORE and CSK abbreviations into diameter, depth, counterbore and countersink symbols. The spotface diameter measures the flat seat, excluding an edge fillet. A shallow-looking counterbore is not enough by itself to identify a spotface. A spotface may have a stated depth or remaining material thickness. If neither is specified, the ASME convention is the minimum depth needed to clean up the specified diameter. Do not invent a depth from the sketch. Reading old and new symbol styles Legacy notes may say.30 DIA,.55 DP, C'BORE, C'SINK or SP FACE. Modern symbol notation moves that meaning into the symbols shown above. A through hole may be clear from the drawing without THRU, but an isolated diameter value does not prove a through condition. Read the views and notes together. Use the standard and edition named on the drawing. The current ASME edition governs new ASME work unless another edition is specified; the ASME catalog lists Y14.5-2018 (R2024). A reference sheet headed “current 2009” is historical, not evidence of today’s edition. Surface finish requirements are a separate language: see Surface Texture and Finish."},
{"title":"Change a hole and watch its callout change","page":"Hole and Feature Callouts","summary":"Read radii, diameters, slots, repeated features and hole notes, including the units and decimal places on an inch drawing.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/hole-and-feature-callouts.html#change-a-hole-and-watch-its-callout-change","text":"Change a hole and watch its callout change Predict which line will change before changing a control. Compare counterbore depth with countersink angle, then change the main diameter while keeping the recess diameter fixed. Static example: 2X ⌀.300 THRU / ⌴ ⌀.600 ↧.150 means two holes, each.300 diameter through, each with a.600 diameter counterbore.150 deep. Their positions must be defined elsewhere. The section sketches above provide a printable comparison of the same hole types. The live builder uses invented dimensions and an ideal section; it does not select a fastener, tolerance or manufacturing process. Read a counterbore Check a slot length"},
{"title":"Use vector paths for outlines and raster scans for filled marks","page":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","summary":"Separate cutting paths from engraved areas, preserve scale, and account for the material removed by the laser.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/laser-cutting-and-engraving-from-cad.html#use-vector-paths-for-outlines-and-raster-scans-for-filled-marks","text":"Use vector paths for outlines and raster scans for filled marks A vector operation follows lines and curves. A raster operation scans across an area to make a surface image or filled mark. A vector path can cut through a sheet or make a shallow score, depending on the material and settings. Raster engraving typically changes the surface without cutting out the part. A bitmap picture of an outline is not automatically a usable vector cutting path. Vector motion follows a continuous rectangular boundary. Raster motion scans parallel rows across a filled rectangular area. Practice choosing cut or engrave"},
{"title":"Prepare a full-size profile and assign each operation deliberately","page":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","summary":"Separate cutting paths from engraved areas, preserve scale, and account for the material removed by the laser.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/laser-cutting-and-engraving-from-cad.html#prepare-a-full-size-profile-and-assign-each-operation-deliberately","text":"Prepare a full-size profile and assign each operation deliberately From CAD, export the intended planar face or sketch as a supported vector format such as DXF. View the face normal to its plane; a perspective picture is not a true-size profile. In the vector editor, set units and page/piece size. Import the file and measure a known feature to confirm 1:1 scale. Remove unwanted dimensions, notes, borders, and duplicate paths from the fabrication copy. Check for gaps in outlines that should be closed. Assign cut paths, score paths if used, and raster areas to the correct operations. Inspect fills as well as strokes. Match page/piece size and placement in the artwork and machine software. Preview the job and check its extent against the actual sheet. CorelDraw is one possible vector editor. Line weight and color can tell a particular driver how to treat an object, but those conventions are not universal. Follow the posted procedure for that driver and confirm the operation in its preview. Why a hairline is a workflow setting, not a cutting guarantee The teaching deck uses hairline strokes for vector cuts and strokes of at least 0.5 pt for raster content. Preserve those values only when the specified driver procedure calls for them. Stroke width alone does not establish cut depth, material suitability, or a safe setting."},
{"title":"Offset the path toward the waste by half the kerf","page":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","summary":"Separate cutting paths from engraved areas, preserve scale, and account for the material removed by the laser.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/laser-cutting-and-engraving-from-cad.html#offset-the-path-toward-the-waste-by-half-the-kerf","text":"Offset the path toward the waste by half the kerf Kerf is the width of material removed by a cut. In the simple model below, the laser follows the center of a constant-width kerf. Half the removed width lies on each side of the path. For an outside part boundary, move the path outward by kerf / 2. For an opening, move the path inward toward the material being removed. Without compensation, a cutout part becomes smaller and a hole becomes larger. An outside part has its cutting path outside the desired edge, while a hole has its cutting path inside the desired edge. Each path is half a kerf into the waste. For a desired outside width W, the path-to-path width is W + k, where k is kerf. For a desired opening width H, it is H − k. These are whole-width changes; the offset at each edge is only k / 2. Measure a test cut in the same material and setup. Kerf can vary with thickness, focus, and settings, and cut edges may taper. If software already compensates the path, do not apply the same allowance again. See Tolerances and Fits for fit requirements. Practice an outside dimension Practice an opening"},
{"title":"Treat power, speed, and pulse settings as a matched setup","page":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","summary":"Separate cutting paths from engraved areas, preserve scale, and account for the material removed by the laser.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/laser-cutting-and-engraving-from-cad.html#treat-power-speed-and-pulse-settings-as-a-matched-setup","text":"Treat power, speed, and pulse settings as a matched setup Power controls laser output; speed controls how quickly the spot travels. With other conditions held fixed, higher power or slower travel generally puts more energy into a length of cut. That can also increase scorching or melting rather than improve the edge. Frequency or pulse settings control how pulses are delivered. Controls may use pulses per second, pulses per distance, or a machine-specific scale; read the manual's definition. A percentage from one machine is not a transferable recipe for another. Use the posted procedure and the manufacturer's starting settings for the exact machine, material, and thickness. Confirm the result on an approved test piece. Check that the cut separates and that engraving is readable before running the complete job. Manufacturer guidance for selecting a starting setup Epilog's material settings guidance treats its recommendations as starting points. A settings table cannot identify an unknown sheet or replace approval for a material."},
{"title":"Confirm the material and stay with the operating laser","page":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","summary":"Separate cutting paths from engraved areas, preserve scale, and account for the material removed by the laser.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/laser-cutting-and-engraving-from-cad.html#confirm-the-material-and-stay-with-the-operating-laser","text":"Confirm the material and stay with the operating laser Only process materials approved for that machine. Suitable grades of wood and acrylic are common CO₂-laser examples, but coatings, adhesives, thickness, and machine capability matter. A material that can be marked is not necessarily one the machine can cut through. Do not cut or engrave PVC or vinyl containing PVC. Do not guess the identity of an unknown plastic. Never leave an operating laser unattended. Use the required extraction and follow the machine procedure for a flare-up or other abnormal condition. Use Equipment Operating Procedure, Fire Safety, and Personal Protective Equipment for the shared safety rules. Manufacturer safety reference Epilog's Fire Warning (Fusion Ascent manual, p. 1) states the unattended-operation prohibition and warns that PVC produces toxic and corrosive gases. These are process-specific limits, not a claim about this shop's inventory."},
{"title":"Each view looks straight at the part from one direction","page":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","summary":"Connect a part's width, height, and depth across aligned views, then unfold the six faces of an imaginary glass box.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/orthographic-projection.html#each-view-looks-straight-at-the-part-from-one-direction","text":"Each view looks straight at the part from one direction A pictorial helps you recognize a part. A multiview drawing helps you describe its shape without the apparent shrinkage of perspective. In orthographic projection, the lines of sight are parallel and perpendicular to the viewing plane. Choose a front direction and keep the part fixed. Look straight from the front, from above, and from the right. Do not turn the part independently to make each view look convenient. Front and rear Show width and height; depth points toward or away from you. Top and bottom Show width and depth; height points toward or away from you. Right and left Show depth and height; width points toward or away from you. A face parallel to its viewing plane shows its true shape at the drawing's scale. A face tilted to that plane is foreshortened; a face viewed exactly edge-on becomes a line."},
{"title":"The glass box unfolds into six related views","page":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","summary":"Connect a part's width, height, and depth across aligned views, then unfold the six faces of an imaginary glass box.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/orthographic-projection.html#the-glass-box-unfolds-into-six-related-views","text":"The glass box unfolds into six related views Imagine the part inside a transparent box. For third-angle projection, trace what you see onto the box face between you and the part. Each face receives one principal view: front, rear, top, bottom, left, and right. A fixed block inside a transparent box with front, top, and right viewing planes; beside it a third-angle net places top above front, bottom below, left to the left, right to the right, and rear beyond right. Keep the front face fixed. Hinge the top upward, bottom downward, and side faces outward. The rear can unfold beyond a side face; this net places it beyond the right. The names identify the direction you look from. A right-side view means you stood at the part's right, even if a different projection convention puts that view elsewhere on the sheet. Compare First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection."},
{"title":"Predict a view, then compare it with the same block part","page":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","summary":"Connect a part's width, height, and depth across aligned views, then unfold the six faces of an imaginary glass box.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/orthographic-projection.html#predict-a-view-then-compare-it-with-the-same-block-part","text":"Predict a view, then compare it with the same block part Choose a part and a viewing direction in the explorer. Before revealing the view, predict its outline and any step edges. Switch the layout convention and notice what moves on the sheet. The part and the named views stay the same. A block with a tall left strip and a lower right shelf, extruded uniformly in depth. Front is an L-shaped outline, top is a rectangle split at the step, and right is a rectangle with a visible horizontal shelf edge. Static example: the tall strip is on the left of the front view and extends through the full depth. The top view gives its width; the right view gives its height. These are simple solids with constant depth. Construction guides are teaching aids. The explorer includes the step edges visible or hidden from each selected direction; it is not a dimensioned manufacturing drawing."},
{"title":"Use alignment to follow one feature through the drawing","page":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","summary":"Connect a part's width, height, and depth across aligned views, then unfold the six faces of an imaginary glass box.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/orthographic-projection.html#use-alignment-to-follow-one-feature-through-the-drawing","text":"Use alignment to follow one feature through the drawing Identify the front view and confirm the projection convention. Track the same left-to-right position between front and top: they share width. Track the same elevation between front and side: they share height. Compare front-to-back positions between top and side: they share depth. Explain each solid or dashed edge using the shape seen in another view. At the same scale, a feature cannot be one width in the front view and a different width in the top view. A 45° miter construction can transfer depth between top and side while sketching; that construction line is not an edge of the part. Why one view can hide an entire change in shape Looking from the front collapses depth. A shallow block and a deep block can have identical front outlines. Looking down on them separates those possibilities. Even three matching outside outlines do not prove that internal holes or recesses are identical; hidden lines, sections, dimensions, and notes still matter. NPTEL's projection-method lesson and Autodesk's projection-angle explanation provide another way to compare the conventions. Next, decide which views the part actually needs."},
{"title":"Match the pictorial to its front, top, and right views","page":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","summary":"Connect a part's width, height, and depth across aligned views, then unfold the six faces of an imaginary glass box.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/orthographic-projection.html#match-the-pictorial-to-its-front-top-and-right-views","text":"Match the pictorial to its front, top, and right views Identify each numbered candidate using the viewing directions on the pictorial. These candidates are arranged as answer choices, not in a first-angle or third-angle layout. A tall-left stepped block with front, top, and right viewing directions. Candidate 1 is a rectangle with a horizontal line; candidate 2 has a tall left step in its outline; candidate 3 is a rectangle with a vertical line near its left edge. Use the self-checks to test your choices and read the explanations. Identify the front Identify the top Identify the right"},
{"title":"Pattern a seed when the copies should change together","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#pattern-a-seed-when-the-copies-should-change-together","text":"Pattern a seed when the copies should change together A seed is the original feature being repeated. A feature pattern makes the repetition explicit: edit the seed's size once and rebuild the copies. Model independent features when their sizes or locations must change independently. A linear pattern uses a direction, spacing, and count. For a single-direction row with N total instances including the seed, the distance from first center to last is (N − 1) × spacing. Some dialogs also offer a total-span mode; check which distance the field means. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. One hole is selected as the seed, a horizontal direction and spacing are set, and a row of four equally spaced holes results. Seed → direction and spacing → repeated features. A circular pattern repeats around an axis. N evenly spaced instances around a complete circle have an angular pitch of 360°/N. A partial arc with both endpoints occupied instead has N − 1 intervals across that arc. Inspect whether the dialog asks for total angle or angle between instances. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A hole offset from a center axis is repeated at four quarter-turn locations around the axis. Seed + axis → full-circle spacing → four total holes. Choose feature, face, or body/part pattern deliberately. A feature pattern repeats selected operations; a body/part pattern repeats a larger solid. Check target bodies, skipped instances, and whether every copy actually intersects the intended material. Copied geometry and recalculated end conditions can differ Onshape feature patterns have a Reapply features option for regenerating the seed's dependencies and end conditions at each instance. A seed that ends at a sloping face may need different geometry at each copy. Do not assume every pattern automatically recalculates that boundary. See Linear Pattern and Circular Pattern. Practice linear spacing Practice angular spacing"},
{"title":"Mirror across a plane when symmetry is the rule","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#mirror-across-a-plane-when-symmetry-is-the-rule","text":"Mirror across a plane when symmetry is the rule A mirror reflects selected features or bodies across a plane. Corresponding points lie at equal distances on opposite sides. It is not a rotation: an asymmetric shape changes handedness. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. An L-shaped tab on the left of a center plane reflects to an oppositely handed tab at the same distance on the right. Selected geometry + symmetry plane → reflected geometry. A stable center plane is usually a better symmetry reference than a face likely to move independently. Check whether the mirror should join material into one body or create another. Assemblies use separate tools to position components; part-level mirror features do not define assembly motion. See Onshape: Mirror for selection and result options. Practice symmetry"},
{"title":"Hole features store type and size as well as position","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#hole-features-store-type-and-size-as-well-as-position","text":"Hole features store type and size as well as position Use a hole feature when the hole needs structured information such as a fastener size, fit, counterbore, countersink, or tapped-hole specification. A counterbore is a larger cylindrical recess; a countersink is a conical recess. A plain circular cut can make geometry, but does not automatically carry the same hole definition. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A center point locates a hole; the counterbore type specifies a wide cylindrical recess over a smaller bore; the sectioned result shows both diameters. Position + hole definition → modeled hole. Section view shows the recess; no thread geometry is implied. In SOLIDWORKS Hole Wizard, use Type for the hole parameters and Positions for its centers. Select the appropriate standard, type, size, fit where offered, and end condition. Locate the centers with dimensions and relations. Different hole types require separate Hole Wizard features. For tapped holes, distinguish drill depth from thread depth. A cosmetic thread represents thread information without cutting a helical groove in the solid. Inspect the selected thread option before expecting detailed thread geometry in an export. Read hole callouts and drawing symbols in the Engineering Graphics dimensioning topics. This page covers the model feature, not callout syntax. Hole options depend on type and software version Use the preview and parameter descriptions instead of assuming every hole has the same options. See SOLIDWORKS: Hole Wizard Type PropertyManager and Hole Wizard Overview. Standard names in a menu do not replace checking the specified hole requirements."},
{"title":"Fillet rounds an edge; chamfer bevels it","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#fillet-rounds-an-edge-chamfer-bevels-it","text":"Fillet rounds an edge; chamfer bevels it A fillet creates a rounded transition. A chamfer creates a beveled transition. Select the intended edges, set the radius or chamfer dimensions, and inspect the preview all the way around the selection. A small radius can still fail where neighboring faces leave too little room. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A sharp outside corner is selected, a radius is applied, and a rounded corner replaces it in section. Fillet: selected edge → radius → curved transition. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A sharp outside corner is selected, two equal setback distances are specified, and a straight bevel replaces it in section. Chamfer: selected edge → setback dimensions → straight bevel in section. Keep edge treatments separate when they have different functions or are likely to change independently. Cosmetic edge breaks often belong late; a large blend that controls the main shape may belong early. See planning feature order."},
{"title":"Shell hollows a solid and leaves a controlled wall","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#shell-hollows-a-solid-and-leaves-a-controlled-wall","text":"Shell hollows a solid and leaves a controlled wall Select faces to remove and specify the remaining wall thickness. Removing the top face of a block makes an open container; keeping all faces can make an enclosed cavity. Check whether the thickness is offset inward or outward. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A solid block has its top face selected for removal; a wall thickness is set; the sectioned result is an open container with side walls and a floor. Solid + opening face → wall thickness → hollow part, shown in section. If shell fails, inspect small radii and narrow regions where offset faces collide. Try a simpler outer shape or revisit the intended thickness. Do not silently reduce a required wall just to make the command succeed. See Onshape: Shell. Practice hollowing a part"},
{"title":"Draft tapers faces; a rib adds a thin support","page":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","summary":"Repeat geometry with a rule, model useful hole information, and finish a solid with edge treatments, shell, draft, and ribs.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/patterns-mirror-and-applied-features.html#draft-tapers-faces-a-rib-adds-a-thin-support","text":"Draft tapers faces; a rib adds a thin support Draft inclines faces relative to a pull direction, often to help a molded part release. In a neutral-plane construction, that reference keeps its size while selected side faces tilt. Check the direction and which end grows or shrinks. There is no universal draft angle for every material and process. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. Vertical sides of a block are selected with its bottom as the neutral plane; the sides tilt inward toward the top while the bottom width stays fixed. Faces + neutral plane → angle → tapered sides, shown in section. A rib adds a thin web between supporting regions. It usually starts from sketch curves plus a thickness and direction. Although grouped here with finishing features, a rib needs sketch geometry. Check that it meets the intended walls and merges with the intended part. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A diagonal sketch line bridges the corner of an L-shaped support; a rib thickness is specified; a triangular web joins the two legs. Support + sketch → thickness → reinforcing web. Thickness runs across the sketch plane. See Onshape: Draft, Rib, and the feature tool glossary. After any applied feature, rebuild, inspect the section, and make a trial change to its parent geometry. Practice support features Practice draft"},
{"title":"Sketch the idea before committing to CAD","page":"Pictorial Sketching","summary":"Block out height, width, and depth, then use light construction lines to develop a clear freehand pictorial.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/pictorial-sketching.html#sketch-the-idea-before-committing-to-cad","text":"Sketch the idea before committing to CAD A quick hand sketch lets you compare shapes, explain an idea, and find missing information before building a CAD model. Aim for a readable shape and sensible proportions. A sketch does not need mechanically perfect lines to be useful. Use a pencil, eraser, and plain or grid paper. Isometric paper can help keep the three directions consistent. Start lightly so you can revise; darken the visible edges only after the overall form works. Add small features last. Label important features and write any known dimensions. Do not expect someone to recover an exact size by measuring your freehand sketch. For line meanings, see The Alphabet of Lines. CAD sketch tools are covered separately in Sketch Entities and Planes."},
{"title":"Isometric sketches use three equally spaced directions","page":"Pictorial Sketching","summary":"Block out height, width, and depth, then use light construction lines to develop a clear freehand pictorial.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/pictorial-sketching.html#isometric-sketches-use-three-equally-spaced-directions","text":"Isometric sketches use three equally spaced directions Draw one axis vertically and the other two down to the left and right, each 30° from horizontal. The three outward rays are 120° apart. Edges parallel to these axes keep those directions throughout the sketch; they can extend either way along each axis line. Use the same scale along all three axis directions. Lay out width, height, and depth along lines parallel to the axes; an isometric grid helps you keep equal intervals consistent. Three isometric axis rays separated by 120 degrees and a rectangular block with evenly spaced construction lines on its top face. Isometric drawing and isometric projection use different scales An ordinary isometric drawing lays out axis-parallel lengths at the chosen drawing scale. A geometrically projected isometric view foreshortens all three axes equally. Do not mix an isometric projection scale with ordinary drawing lengths in one sketch. The angle of a drawn edge on the paper is not generally its angle on the part. For CAD view types, see CAD Model Views and Checks."},
{"title":"Locate sloping edges by endpoints; sketch circles as ellipses","page":"Pictorial Sketching","summary":"Block out height, width, and depth, then use light construction lines to develop a clear freehand pictorial.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/pictorial-sketching.html#locate-sloping-edges-by-endpoints-sketch-circles-as-ellipses","text":"Locate sloping edges by endpoints; sketch circles as ellipses A non-isometric line is not parallel to any of the three isometric axes. Locate both endpoints using width, depth, and height offsets, then join them. Do not lay the true sloping-edge length directly along its apparent direction on the paper. For a circle on a face parallel to a principal plane, first sketch its enclosing square as an isometric rhombus. Mark the four side midpoints and sketch a smooth ellipse tangent there. Those are contact points, not sharp corners. Keep the curve inside the rhombus. Line PQ connects two offset points on a beveled front face. Below, an ellipse touches the four side midpoints of its isometric enclosing square. The circle-to-ellipse rule here applies to circles on the usual top, front, or side planes of the isometric sketch. Do not draw a round circle on one of these tilted faces."},
{"title":"Build a box, locate the cut, then remove material","page":"Pictorial Sketching","summary":"Block out height, width, and depth, then use light construction lines to develop a clear freehand pictorial.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/pictorial-sketching.html#build-a-box-locate-the-cut-then-remove-material","text":"Build a box, locate the cut, then remove material Start with the smallest simple box that encloses the part: height, width, depth. In this original example, a corner is removed uniformly through the depth. That makes the front profile a useful starting shape. An enclosing rectangular block, the triangular corner marked on its front face, and the finished block with that corner removed through its depth. Compare the overall height, width, and depth before adding detail. Mark where the cut begins and ends on the front profile. Carry those points back along parallel depth lines. Connect matching points to form the new face. Check the remaining edges, then darken visible edges and lighten or erase construction lines. “Extrude” means carry a profile through a distance. It is a useful mental model only when the cross-section stays constant over that distance. Check other views for features that stop partway through."},
{"title":"Choose isometric for three directions or oblique for a true front face","page":"Pictorial Sketching","summary":"Block out height, width, and depth, then use light construction lines to develop a clear freehand pictorial.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/pictorial-sketching.html#choose-isometric-for-three-directions-or-oblique-for-a-true-front-face","text":"Choose isometric for three directions or oblique for a true front face In an isometric sketch, the three principal directions share one scale, and none of the three principal faces appears in true shape. In an oblique sketch, draw the chosen front face in true shape at the drawing scale, then carry parallel edges back along one receding direction. A 45° receding direction is common, but it is not required. Full-scale depth is called cavalier; half-scale depth is called cabinet. Keep the chosen depth scale consistent. A circle on the oblique front face stays circular; one on a receding face generally does not. Panel 1 shows an isometric rectangular block; panel 2 shows an oblique block with a rectangular front face and parallel receding edges. Both methods use parallel edges rather than perspective vanishing points. Pick the front direction deliberately; see Choosing Drawing Views. Identify a pictorial Find a non-isometric line Check a circle sketch"},
{"title":"Use an imaginary cut to expose the interior","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#use-an-imaginary-cut-to-expose-the-interior","text":"Use an imaginary cut to expose the interior A section view shows what is inside a part without making you untangle a group of hidden lines. Imagine cutting the part, removing the material between you and the cutting plane, and looking at what remains. The cut changes the drawing representation, not the manufactured part. Hatch the material crossed by the plane and show the useful visible edges beyond it. Hidden lines are usually omitted in sections unless needed to make the shape clear. Start with The Alphabet of Lines and Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box if the line types or viewing directions are unfamiliar. For a sloping face or a small feature, see Auxiliary and Detail Views."},
{"title":"Follow the cutting-plane arrows and match the letters","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#follow-the-cutting-plane-arrows-and-match-the-letters","text":"Follow the cutting-plane arrows and match the letters The cutting-plane line locates the imaginary cut in a parent view. Its arrows point in the direction you look into the remaining part. Matching letters connect that line to a label such as SECTION A–A; the letters do not specify a dimension. Use a prominent cutting-plane line that extends beyond the part, with thin section lining in the resulting view. Line weight, dash pattern, and arrow size follow the drawing standard and template. These teaching diagrams use blue for cutting planes; color is not required to interpret a drawing. Find both cutting-plane ends and the matching section label. Follow the arrows to decide which side is removed from sight. Trace solid material and voids separately across the plane. Compare the resulting section with the parent view."},
{"title":"Full sections cut all the way through the part","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#full-sections-cut-all-the-way-through-the-part","text":"Full sections cut all the way through the part A full section uses one plane through the entire part. For this hollow cylinder, a plane through the axis exposes both walls and the bore. The bore is empty space and receives no hatching. Top of a hollow cylinder with a straight A–A cut and upward viewing arrows; below, two hatched walls flank a blank bore."},
{"title":"Half sections combine the exterior and interior","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#half-sections-combine-the-exterior-and-interior","text":"Half sections combine the exterior and interior A half section is useful for a symmetric part. Imagine removing one quarter with two perpendicular cutting planes. The resulting view shows one half in section and the other half as an exterior view, usually divided by a center line. It is not a full section with half its hatch erased. The unsectioned half shows the outside, and symmetry supplies the connection between the two halves. An L-shaped cut removes a quarter of a hollow cylinder; the resulting view shows exterior at left and a hatched wall at right."},
{"title":"Offset sections reach features that are not in one straight line","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#offset-sections-reach-features-that-are-not-in-one-straight-line","text":"Offset sections reach features that are not in one straight line An offset section jogs the cutting plane to pass through several features. Project the selected parallel portions into one section. The jogs help explain the cut location; they do not create physical edges on the part. Read the parent view for the holes’ actual locations. Their appearance together in the section does not mean they lie in one straight row. A jogged A–A cutting plane passes through three staggered holes in a plate; the section shows all three holes without false edges at the jogs."},
{"title":"Broken-out sections expose only a local area","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#broken-out-sections-expose-only-a-local-area","text":"Broken-out sections expose only a local area A broken-out section removes a limited patch to a chosen depth in an existing view. An irregular break line marks the boundary. Use it to expose a small passage or wall without adding a complete section view. A separate cutting-plane line is normally unnecessary: the local boundary identifies the area. Depth still matters; a cut that stops before a bore will not reveal it. This differs from a broken view, which merely shortens a long part on the sheet. A housing with a hidden horizontal passage, followed by a local irregular cutaway exposing the passage between hatched walls."},
{"title":"Revolved sections show a cross section in place","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#revolved-sections-show-a-cross-section-in-place","text":"Revolved sections show a cross section in place A revolved section turns a cross-sectional slice 90° into the plane of the drawing and places it on the parent view. It is useful for the shape of a bar, spoke, or arm. Show the slice itself, rather than the features behind the cut. A transverse cut through a rectangular bar is rotated into the drawing and overlaid as a hatched rectangle on the bar."},
{"title":"Removed sections place the slice clear of the parent view","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#removed-sections-place-the-slice-clear-of-the-parent-view","text":"Removed sections place the slice clear of the parent view A removed section places the cross section away from the parent view to keep it readable. Match its identifying letters to the cutting plane. Several removed sections can describe changes along a long part; keep each location unambiguous. The same transverse cut through a rectangular bar is shown separately as section A–A rather than overlaid on the bar."},
{"title":"Aligned sections rotate an angled portion into the section plane","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#aligned-sections-rotate-an-angled-portion-into-the-section-plane","text":"Aligned sections rotate an angled portion into the section plane An aligned section uses intersecting cutting planes through features arranged around an axis. An angled portion is rotated into alignment before it is shown in the section. This makes radial features easier to read. Do not infer angular spacing from the aligned result. In this disk, one small hole is rotated into the section plane; its original angular position is established by the top view. A disk has a central bore and two radial holes at different angles; the angled cutting-plane portion rotates into alignment to show the holes in section."},
{"title":"Hatch cut material, with conventional exceptions","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#hatch-cut-material-with-conventional-exceptions","text":"Hatch cut material, with conventional exceptions Use thin, evenly spaced diagonal lines, commonly near 45° to the main outline. Change the angle when the hatch would be confused with an edge. Keep a part’s hatch consistent; distinguish adjacent assembly parts by changing hatch angle or spacing. Voids: holes and open spaces are never hatched as solid material. Ribs, webs, and spokes: a cut along their length generally leaves them unhatched so they do not look like a thick solid wall. A transverse cut through their thickness is hatched. Shafts and fasteners in assembly sections: when cut lengthwise through their axes, solid shafts, bolts, nuts, washers, pins, and similar standard parts are commonly shown unsectioned. A transverse shaft section can be hatched; “never hatch a shaft” is too broad. An unhatched region may be empty space, an uncut surface, or a conventionally unsectioned component. Use its outline and the other views to decide which. Drawing standards and scope These are introductory conventions, not clause quotations. The current edition of ASME Y14.3 and related drawing standards governs formal work, subject to the edition invoked by the drawing or contract. Consult that edition for exceptions and exact line requirements. Do not identify a material solely from generic diagonal hatching."},
{"title":"Create the section from a CAD drawing view, then inspect it","page":"Section Views","summary":"Read imaginary cuts, choose a section type, and distinguish cut material from voids and conventional exceptions.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/section-views.html#create-the-section-from-a-cad-drawing-view-then-inspect-it","text":"Create the section from a CAD drawing view, then inspect it Start with a model-based parent drawing view. Choose a section command, define the cut, set its viewing direction, and place the result. These drawing operations do not remove material from the design model. Full, half, and offset Use a section-view command with a straight, half-section, or jogged cutting path. SOLIDWORKS calls the main tool Section View; Onshape uses Section view. Available half-section controls differ by application. Aligned Choose the aligned section option and define the intersection and angled portions. SOLIDWORKS uses its Section View PropertyManager; Onshape names the tool Aligned section view. Broken-out Define a closed local profile and a cut depth. The tools are Broken-out Section in SOLIDWORKS and Broken-out section view in Onshape. Removed and revolved Create a slice-only section, then place it apart or rotate and position it in the parent view. SOLIDWORKS documents Removed Section and a rotated-section workflow combining a break with section views. Onshape’s Show cut geometry only option can produce a slice without background geometry; check placement and rotation support in the installed version rather than assuming a dedicated revolved-section button. Check that the plane crosses the intended features and the arrows face the intended direction. Check the label, view scale, hidden-edge visibility, and hatch regions. Exclude applicable assembly components from the cut and inspect ribs separately. After a model change, update the drawing and recheck cut references and annotations. Vendor help for the drawing tools See SOLIDWORKS derived drawing views, Broken-out Section, and Break View. The Removed Section instructions linked here are from the 2022 help edition. For Onshape, see Section view, Aligned section view, and Broken-out section view. Practice identifying a section Practice hatch conventions"},
{"title":"Choose the sketch plane before drawing the shape","page":"Sketch Entities and Planes","summary":"Choose where a 2D sketch lives, then build its shape from simple entities and construction geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-entities-and-planes.html#choose-the-sketch-plane-before-drawing-the-shape","text":"Choose the sketch plane before drawing the shape A 2D sketch is a set of points and curves on one plane. The plane places the sketch in the model; the sketch's origin and axes give you references for locating its geometry. Standard plane: Front, Top, or Right is a useful starting reference for a new part. Choose the view that makes the intended profile easiest to describe. Model face: use an existing planar face when the new sketch belongs on that face. A curved face is not itself a 2D sketch plane. Reference plane: an added plane can locate a sketch where the standard planes and existing faces do not. The origin is a point, not a plane. Starting a line or circle at the origin can anchor its position, but you still need a sketch plane. You may start elsewhere when that suits the design; then constrain the location relative to a deliberate reference. Look normal to the sketch plane: straight at it, rather than at an angle. Horizontal and vertical refer to the sketch's axes, even when you rotate the model on screen. Sketch placement in Onshape Onshape creates sketches in a Part Studio and lets you choose a plane, a planar part face, or a mate connector that supplies a coordinate system. This page uses ordinary planes and faces. See Onshape: Sketch Basics."},
{"title":"Use the simplest entity that describes the shape","page":"Sketch Entities and Planes","summary":"Choose where a 2D sketch lives, then build its shape from simple entities and construction geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-entities-and-planes.html#use-the-simplest-entity-that-describes-the-shape","text":"Use the simplest entity that describes the shape Sketch entities and the information they need Entity What it describes What to control Line A straight segment between two endpoints. Position, direction, and length. Circle A complete round curve. Center position and radius or diameter. Arc Part of a circle. Center, radius, and where the arc begins and ends. Rectangle Four connected lines with right-angle corners. Position, orientation, width, and height. Tool-created relations often supply some of these. Slot A constant-width path with rounded ends. A simple straight slot has two parallel sides and two semicircular ends. Path position and direction, width, and length. State whether length means overall length or distance between end centers. Spline A curve shaped by fit points or control points and handles. Point locations and curve shape. Use it when a line or circular arc cannot express the intended contour. Rectangle and slot tools create useful groups of geometry. They do not guarantee that the resulting sketch is fully defined. Inspect their relations before adding more. Find the tools by their software names Onshape names include Line, Corner rectangle, Center point rectangle, Center point circle, 3 point circle, Tangent arc, 3 point arc, Spline, Point, and Construction. Its Slot tool builds around selected sketch curves. See Sketch Tools and Slot. These names describe ways to create geometry, not different rules for constraining it."},
{"title":"Construction geometry guides the sketch without outlining material","page":"Sketch Entities and Planes","summary":"Choose where a 2D sketch lives, then build its shape from simple entities and construction geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-entities-and-planes.html#construction-geometry-guides-the-sketch-without-outlining-material","text":"Construction geometry guides the sketch without outlining material A centerline is a common use of construction geometry: a reference for a center, symmetry, angle, or axis. Construction geometry participates in dimensions and relations, but does not serve as the ordinary boundary of a filled profile. A circular profile with a construction centerline A solid circle surrounds an origin cross. A dashed line through its center is labeled construction; the circle is labeled profile. Profile Construction Origin The reference line and the profile have different jobs. A dashed line can still need constraints. In SOLIDWORKS, Centerline creates construction geometry. In Onshape, use Construction to toggle selected sketch entities or to create construction geometry. Use a construction circle for a layout circle just as you would use a construction line for an axis. Vendor reference for construction geometry Onshape: Construction explains the reference-only role. SOLIDWORKS: Construction Geometry describes centerlines and converting sketch entities."},
{"title":"Check connections before moving on to a feature","page":"Sketch Entities and Planes","summary":"Choose where a 2D sketch lives, then build its shape from simple entities and construction geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-entities-and-planes.html#check-connections-before-moving-on-to-a-feature","text":"Check connections before moving on to a feature Two endpoints that look joined may still be separate. A tiny gap can prevent a profile from closing. Use a coincident relation to make the connection intentional, then inspect the sketch. Closed and fully defined are different checks. A closed circle can still move or change size. A fully defined chain of lines can still be open. Later features such as extrude or revolve use sketches in different ways; choose the profile that the intended feature needs. Continue with Geometric Relations in Sketches and Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent. For view interpretation, see Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box; for drawing dimensions, see Dimension Anatomy and Placement. Practice: choose a sketch reference"},
{"title":"Relations preserve geometry when dimensions change","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#relations-preserve-geometry-when-dimensions-change","text":"Relations preserve geometry when dimensions change A geometric relation is a rule the sketch must continue to satisfy: two lines stay parallel, two circles keep one center, or an endpoint stays on a curve. Onshape generally calls these rules constraints; SOLIDWORKS calls them relations. Choose the entities carefully. Selecting a circle's center is different from selecting its circumference. A relation can remove some motion while leaving other motion available. The diagrams show only the named rule, not a complete dimensioning scheme. The before/after drawings are original geometry diagrams, not software icons. Their line colors do not indicate sketch status."},
{"title":"Coincident connects a point; midpoint puts it halfway along","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#coincident-connects-a-point-midpoint-puts-it-halfway-along","text":"Coincident connects a point; midpoint puts it halfway along Coincident Two points share a location, or a point lies on a selected line or curve. Joining two endpoints closes their gap. A point on a line can still slide along it unless another rule locates it. Before: separate line endpoints. After: one shared endpoint. Coincident controls connection. It does not by itself set either line's length or angle. Midpoint A point stays halfway between a segment's endpoints. It is more specific than simply putting the point somewhere on the line. Onshape also supports midpoint on an arc. Before: a point above a line. After: the point lies at the line midpoint. The two halves stay equal as the line changes length. Point selection and vendor names See Onshape's Coincident and Midpoint. SOLIDWORKS also lists Merge Points for combining sketch points or endpoints in its relation reference."},
{"title":"Collinear shares a line; parallel shares only its direction","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#collinear-shares-a-line-parallel-shares-only-its-direction","text":"Collinear shares a line; parallel shares only its direction Collinear Two straight segments lie on the same infinite line. They may have a gap between them; collinear does not join their endpoints. Before: two differently directed segments. After: both lie on one dashed extended line, still separated. Same underlying line does not mean same length or touching endpoints. Parallel Two lines keep the same direction. Their spacing, lengths, and positions along that direction still need other rules. Before: two lines with different slopes. After: two separated lines with matching slopes. Parallel lines can rotate together; neither must be horizontal. Software vocabulary: SOLIDWORKS has a Collinear relation. Onshape's documented toolbar has no separate Collinear entry. For two nonzero straight segments, placing both endpoints of one coincident with the other's underlying line enforces the same geometric condition. This is a construction from point-on-line constraints, not an Onshape command name."},
{"title":"Horizontal and vertical use the axes; perpendicular uses another line","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#horizontal-and-vertical-use-the-axes-perpendicular-uses-another-line","text":"Horizontal and vertical use the axes; perpendicular uses another line Horizontal A line's endpoints have the same vertical coordinate. For two selected points, horizontal aligns their heights without joining them. Before: a sloping line. After: a line parallel to the sketch x axis. Length and position can still change. Vertical A line's endpoints have the same horizontal coordinate. Two selected points can likewise be aligned vertically. Before: a sloping line. After: a line parallel to the sketch y axis. Use the sketch axes, not the edges of the rotated screen view. Perpendicular Two line directions form a right angle. They may rotate together while preserving that angle. The relation does not require their finite segments to meet. Before: oblique crossing lines. After: two lines whose directions are 90 degrees apart. If a joint is required, also connect the intended endpoints. Why horizontal plus vertical can already supply a right angle A horizontal line and a vertical line already have perpendicular directions. Adding a 90-degree driving dimension can duplicate the existing control. Check the stored rules first. See Onshape: Working with Constraints."},
{"title":"Concentric shares a center; equal shares size; coradial shares both","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#concentric-shares-a-center-equal-shares-size-coradial-shares-both","text":"Concentric shares a center; equal shares size; coradial shares both Concentric Circles or circular arcs share a center. They can have different radii. For arcs, their endpoints and sweep angles are not determined by this relation. Before: two circles with different centers. After: nested circles with one center and different radii. Concentric suits two circular boundaries around the same axis. Equal Selected line segments keep equal lengths; selected circles or circular arcs keep equal radii. Equal does not move their centers together. Equal arc radii do not imply equal arc lengths when the sweep angles differ. Before: short and long segments. After: two equally long segments at different locations. Dimension one size and relate the other to it when they must change together. Coradial Two circular arcs share both center and radius: they belong to the same underlying circle. They need not touch or have equal sweep angles. SOLIDWORKS names this relation Coradial. In Onshape, concentric plus equal radius expresses this condition. Before: arcs on different circles. After: separate arcs on the same dashed supporting circle. Compare all three: same center, same size, or both. Check the circular relation definitions See Onshape's Concentric and Equal. The SOLIDWORKS 2025 relation table correctly places the coradial definition; the 2026 table misplaces its center-and-radius sentence under Perpendicular."},
{"title":"Tangent controls direction at contact; Fix holds a reference in place","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#tangent-controls-direction-at-contact-fix-holds-a-reference-in-place","text":"Tangent controls direction at contact; Fix holds a reference in place Tangent A line and a circle touch without crossing: the line is perpendicular to the radius at contact. Curves meeting tangentially have a common tangent direction. Tangency alone does not necessarily join the endpoints you intended; add endpoint coincidence when a continuous profile needs it. Before: a line cuts through a circle. After: the line touches the circle at the end of a perpendicular radius. A smooth-looking connection still needs the correct relations. Tangency does not require equal radii. Fix Fix grounds selected geometry at its current location. Fixing a point removes its freedom to move in the sketch plane. It is useful for a deliberate fixed reference, but does not explain why an arbitrary location or size is correct. Before: a point can move relative to the origin. After: a lock marks the point held at its current position. The example fixes a point. Do not generalize it to every entity's endpoints. SOLIDWORKS qualification: its relation reference says endpoints of a fixed line can still move along the underlying infinite line, and arc endpoints can move along the supporting circle. Check the entity and its endpoints. Onshape describes Fix as grounding the selected entity on the sketch plane. Vendor help for tangent and fixed geometry Read Onshape: Tangent, Onshape: Fix, and the Fix row in SOLIDWORKS: Description of Sketch Relations."},
{"title":"Watch automatic relations, then check what was stored","page":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","summary":"Match each relation to the motion it prevents, and distinguish shared position, direction, size, and curvature.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sketch-geometric-relations.html#watch-automatic-relations-then-check-what-was-stored","text":"Watch automatic relations, then check what was stored Sketching tools can add relations while you draw. A visual alignment is useful feedback; inspect the resulting relation icons before assuming the intended rule is present. SOLIDWORKS: with Automatic Relations enabled, its documented cursor cues include horizontal, vertical, coincident, midpoint, perpendicular, and tangent. Hold Ctrl while sketching to suppress automatic relations temporarily. Onshape: Automatic Inferencing documents horizontal, vertical, midpoint, parallel, and coincident as common inferences. Hover near existing geometry to activate the relevant inference; Shift suppresses inferencing while dragging. These are documented examples, not an exhaustive promise for every tool. For equal, concentric, coradial, collinear, or fix, deliberately apply or verify the required rule. Rectangle, slot, and other creation tools can supply their own relations. If an unwanted rule was stored, remove that specific rule before adding a conflicting dimension. Continue with Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent. Automatic behavior depends on the tool and settings Checked against SOLIDWORKS 2026: Using Automatic Relations and Onshape: Automatic Inferencing. The keys differ between programs. Practice: distinguish circular relations"},
{"title":"A sweep needs a profile and a path","page":"Sweeps and Lofts","summary":"Use a sweep to carry a section along a path and a loft to transition through ordered sections, with references that control the result.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sweeps-and-lofts.html#a-sweep-needs-a-profile-and-a-path","text":"A sweep needs a profile and a path A profile describes the cross-section; a path describes where it travels. A circular profile swept around a smooth bend makes a bent solid rod. An annular profile can make a tube. For a basic sweep, keep the section size constant and choose how it stays oriented along the path. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. A circular profile at the start of a curved path sweeps into a bent rod with a circular cross-section. Profile + path → sweep → bent rod. The path is a reference curve, not another solid. Place the profile on a plane normal (perpendicular) to the path at its start for an easy first construction. Constrain its position to the path. A shape that looks connected in one view can still be separated in three dimensions. Select the complete intended path, not a neighboring model edge. A sweep can create material or remove it from an existing part. Advanced sweep options can control twist or scaling; a constant circular section is a simpler starting case. See Onshape: Sweep."},
{"title":"A loft passes through profiles selected in order","page":"Sweeps and Lofts","summary":"Use a sweep to carry a section along a path and a loft to transition through ordered sections, with references that control the result.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sweeps-and-lofts.html#a-loft-passes-through-profiles-selected-in-order","text":"A loft passes through profiles selected in order A loft transitions between two or more sections. Put sketches on suitable planes, then select the sections in the order the shape should pass through them. A broad oval transitioning to a smaller oval is a simple example; the section size changes along the length. Three-step sequence: steps 1 and 2 across the top, then step 3 below. Three oval profiles on separated planes are selected from bottom to top and joined into a tapered transition. A guide touches the outside of every profile. Ordered sections → transition → solid. A guide can control the side between the sections. Use a guide curve when the profiles alone do not control the shape between them. For a straightforward boundary guide, constrain it to meet every profile at the intended boundary point. A Pierce relation can locate a sketch point where a curve crosses that sketch plane. Do not accept a connection solely because it looks right in an isometric view. Match corresponding locations around successive profiles. If one section's corner connects to an unrelated corner on the next, the loft can twist. Inspect connection handles and a section view before adding more profiles. More profiles do not automatically make a better surface Extra sections and guides can introduce unwanted waves or conflicting conditions. Start with the fewest that describe the intended shape, then add a control for a specific problem. Profile and guide rules vary with solid, surface, and thin options. See Onshape: Loft and SOLIDWORKS: Description of Sketch Relations."},
{"title":"Use the controlling geometry to choose sweep or loft","page":"Sweeps and Lofts","summary":"Use a sweep to carry a section along a path and a loft to transition through ordered sections, with references that control the result.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sweeps-and-lofts.html#use-the-controlling-geometry-to-choose-sweep-or-loft","text":"Use the controlling geometry to choose sweep or loft What you know First feature to consider What to inspect One section following a bent centerline Sweep Path continuity, section position, and orientation. Different sections at several locations Loft Profile order, connections, and shape between sections. One section along a straight direction Extrude Depth or end condition. A section repeated around an axis Revolve Axis position and rotation angle. These are starting choices, not exclusive capabilities. For example, a loft can follow a centerline, and a sweep can vary with advanced controls. Prefer the feature that states the intended change clearly. Practice profile and path Practice changing sections"},
{"title":"Troubleshoot the inputs before changing every option","page":"Sweeps and Lofts","summary":"Use a sweep to carry a section along a path and a loft to transition through ordered sections, with references that control the result.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/sweeps-and-lofts.html#troubleshoot-the-inputs-before-changing-every-option","text":"Troubleshoot the inputs before changing every option Check that the selected profile regions match the intended solid, surface, or thin result. For a sweep, check path continuity and the profile's location and orientation. For a loft, check profile order and each intended guide intersection. Look for self-intersection: a thick swept section may overlap itself around a tight bend. Inspect sections and confirm the expected part count; repair the first failed reference. Shelling a complicated loft is a separate geometry problem: offset walls can collide where the shape becomes tight. Build and inspect the outer shape first, then check the wall thickness. See shell and other applied features."},
{"title":"Rebuild a part by combining shared dimensions","page":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","summary":"Track the same surfaces through front, top, and side views, then reconstruct a pictorial without guessing hidden geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/visualizing-multiview-parts.html#rebuild-a-part-by-combining-shared-dimensions","text":"Rebuild a part by combining shared dimensions A multiview drawing gives several descriptions of one fixed part. Read them together: front and top share width, front and side share height, and top and side share depth. Review Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box and confirm the projection convention before interpreting positions on the sheet. Identify the front direction and the overall width, height, and depth. Sketch a light enclosing box using isometric construction. Choose a recognizable profile and locate its corners in the box. Use the other views to decide which features continue, stop, or change along the depth. Account for every visible and hidden edge before darkening the pictorial. Do not assume that a front profile extends all the way through. The top or side view must support that interpretation. If two shapes still fit the information, identify the missing view or dimension rather than inventing a feature."},
{"title":"Match a surface by its boundaries, not its apparent shape","page":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","summary":"Track the same surfaces through front, top, and side views, then reconstruct a pictorial without guessing hidden geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/visualizing-multiview-parts.html#match-a-surface-by-its-boundaries-not-its-apparent-shape","text":"Match a surface by its boundaries, not its apparent shape Face A in this original example is the front face. Face B is the sloping face made by removing a corner through the full depth. Follow their corner positions and shared boundaries across the views. A beveled prism with front face A and sloping face B. A is an area in front and an edge in top and right. B is an edge in front and an area in top and right. Views are individually labeled and stacked for a narrow screen; their positions do not specify a projection convention. A appears in true shape in the front view. From above, all its points have the same front-to-back position, so it collapses to the front boundary line. From the right, it also appears edge-on. B appears as a sloping edge in front, but as a foreshortened area in top and right. One drawn line can represent several boundaries The edge view of a surface can coincide with the outline of another surface. A line is therefore not a unique surface label. Match its endpoints and the surfaces meeting there using another view. The colored areas and heavy highlighted segments here are teaching aids, not extra physical edges."},
{"title":"Normal, inclined, and oblique describe a flat surface relative to the view planes","page":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","summary":"Track the same surfaces through front, top, and side views, then reconstruct a pictorial without guessing hidden geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/visualizing-multiview-parts.html#normal-inclined-and-oblique-describe-a-flat-surface-relative-to-the-view-planes","text":"Normal, inclined, and oblique describe a flat surface relative to the view planes Normal surface Parallel to one principal projection plane and perpendicular to the other two. It shows true shape in one principal view and an edge in the other two. Face A is an example. Inclined surface Perpendicular to one principal plane and tilted to the other two. It shows an edge in one principal view and a foreshortened area in the other two. Face B is an example. Oblique surface Tilted to all three principal planes, with none parallel or perpendicular to it. It shows a foreshortened area in all three principal views; none shows true shape or an edge view. Here “oblique surface” describes a face orientation. “Oblique sketch” describes a pictorial drawing method. A part with an inclined face can be drawn with either isometric or oblique sketching."},
{"title":"Turn a pictorial back into views one direction at a time","page":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","summary":"Track the same surfaces through front, top, and side views, then reconstruct a pictorial without guessing hidden geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/visualizing-multiview-parts.html#turn-a-pictorial-back-into-views-one-direction-at-a-time","text":"Turn a pictorial back into views one direction at a time Keep the front direction fixed. For the front view, discard depth and plot width against height. For the top, discard height and plot width against depth. For the side, discard width and plot depth against height. Include changes of surface inside the outside outline. A block with its upper-right front corner removed through its full depth. Three top-view candidates have a horizontal divider, a divider near the right, or a divider near the left. Before checking, explain which boundary remains visible from above and where it lies relative to the width. A matching outside rectangle alone is not enough. Match the top view Find an edge view Classify a surface Recognize an oblique surface"},
{"title":"Predict and reveal views in the projection explorer","page":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","summary":"Track the same surfaces through front, top, and side views, then reconstruct a pictorial without guessing hidden geometry.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/visualizing-multiview-parts.html#predict-and-reveal-views-in-the-projection-explorer","text":"Predict and reveal views in the projection explorer Choose a part, predict the missing view on paper, then reveal it. Change the part and repeat. Use the alignment guides to check shared dimensions. Changing the sheet layout changes view placement, not the part. Static fallback: front face A is an area only in front; sloping face B is an edge only in front. Top and right retain the boundary between the bevel and adjacent faces. On paper: cover the top view, predict its outer rectangle and the line where the sloping face meets the flat top, then uncover it. The explorer uses simple stepped solids of constant depth. Use the separate static example above to study the sloping surface. Check a reconstruction assumption"},
{"title":"Send the drawing type that answers the reader's job","page":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","summary":"Distinguish part and assembly drawings, then use title blocks and revision records to identify the information that applies.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/working-drawings-and-title-blocks.html#send-the-drawing-type-that-answers-the-reader-s-job","text":"Send the drawing type that answers the reader's job Detail or part drawing Defines an individual part for making and inspection, using the necessary views, dimensions, tolerances, material information, and notes. A part drawing is not the same thing as an enlarged detail view. Assembly drawing Shows how components relate and fit together. It may include item identification, assembly requirements, and overall or interface dimensions. See Exploded Views and Bills of Materials for exploded views, item balloons, and parts lists. Working-drawing set The coordinated drawings needed to make and assemble a product: typically assembly drawings and the required part drawings, with supporting lists and notes as needed. A set may span several sheets and drawing numbers. An assembly view alone usually does not provide every requirement needed to manufacture each component. Confirm that the recipient has the required sheets and revisions. Choose a drawing for making a part Choose a drawing for assembly"},
{"title":"Read identity, units, and scale before reading dimensions","page":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","summary":"Distinguish part and assembly drawings, then use title blocks and revision records to identify the information that applies.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/working-drawings-and-title-blocks.html#read-identity-units-and-scale-before-reading-dimensions","text":"Read identity, units, and scale before reading dimensions The title block identifies the drawing and records key information about it. Its layout varies. Read field labels; do not assume a familiar-looking border has familiar settings. Generic title-block example with separate fields for part name, drawing number, units, sheet scale, revision, and sheet count. Notes explain which question each field answers. The part name tells you what the item is; the part or drawing number identifies the controlled record. Those numbers may be different. Units tell you how to interpret dimension values. Scale describes the displayed view size relative to the object, not the part's actual size. Check individual view labels for scale overrides, and use stated dimensions rather than measuring a print. Find the units Interpret sheet scale"},
{"title":"Find material, tolerances, and responsibility in the block or notes","page":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","summary":"Distinguish part and assembly drawings, then use title blocks and revision records to identify the information that applies.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/working-drawings-and-title-blocks.html#find-material-tolerances-and-responsibility-in-the-block-or-notes","text":"Find material, tolerances, and responsibility in the block or notes General practice includes fields or notes for material, general tolerances, revision, and drawn/checked information. Some templates also carry dates, approval status, finish requirements, or a projection symbol. A material field may refer to a separate specification; read that reference rather than guessing from the appearance of the model. A tolerance states the permitted variation from a requirement. Read the scope of the general tolerance note and any specific tolerances at features. Displaying more decimal places is not, by itself, a complete tolerance instruction. See Dimension Anatomy and Placement, Choosing Dimensions, and Hole and Feature Callouts. Drawn and checked entries record responsibility. They do not replace the organization's approval or release process. Missing or conflicting requirements need clarification before the drawing is used. Locate a material requirement"},
{"title":"Match the revision record to the drawing being used","page":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","summary":"Distinguish part and assembly drawings, then use title blocks and revision records to identify the information that applies.","collection":"Engineering Graphics and CAD","url":"engineering-graphics/working-drawings-and-title-blocks.html#match-the-revision-record-to-the-drawing-being-used","text":"Match the revision record to the drawing being used The title block commonly shows the drawing's current revision identifier. A revision block or associated change record explains changes, often with a description, date, and authorization. Exact fields and identifier sequences depend on the organization's system. A new file date alone does not prove that a drawing is the authorized revision. Match its number and revision to the release record for the job. Related parts may legitimately have different revision identifiers; they must be the approved combination. Match the drawing and part identifiers to the item you need. Confirm the required revision and approval status. Confirm that every referenced sheet, note, and parts list is available. Read change descriptions and inspect affected views and requirements. Check a revision"},
{"title":"A slicer turns a solid model into deposited layers","page":"FDM 3D Printing","summary":"Read an FDM part as deposited roads and layers, then choose orientation, walls, infill and supports for its job.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/3d-printing-fdm.html#a-slicer-turns-a-solid-model-into-deposited-layers","text":"A slicer turns a solid model into deposited layers Fused deposition modeling (FDM), also called fused filament fabrication (FFF), feeds thermoplastic filament into a heated nozzle. The machine lays down narrow roads of softened material that bond as the part builds. Relative motion between the nozzle and build plate advances the layers; the nozzle itself does not have to move upward on every printer. A slicer converts the model into tool paths, including walls, internal fill and any temporary supports. Preview those paths before printing. An enclosed shape on a CAD screen does not tell you how its underside will be supported. Schematic FDM cross-section: filament enters the heated nozzle above deposited layers; outer walls enclose sparse infill; removable supports stand beneath an overhang on the build plate. A section through a partly printed part. Walls, infill and supports are different tool paths. The large lines are illustrative, not a scale drawing. Where additive manufacturing fits the five process categories This topic treats FDM under Fabricating: successive deposits bond into a part. Some books classify additive manufacturing under Forming because it creates shape. Neither convention adds a sixth process category. The useful description is the mechanism: material is added layer by layer. Subsequent support removal is Separating; annealing is Conditioning; sanding or painting is Finishing. The material-family and plastic-forming topics provide the wider context."},
{"title":"Layer height, walls and infill do different jobs","page":"FDM 3D Printing","summary":"Read an FDM part as deposited roads and layers, then choose orientation, walls, infill and supports for its job.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/3d-printing-fdm.html#layer-height-walls-and-infill-do-different-jobs","text":"Layer height, walls and infill do different jobs Layer height is the vertical increment between layers. A smaller height makes steps on sloping surfaces finer and usually adds print time. It does not make all small horizontal features printable: nozzle size, road width and motion accuracy also matter. Walls or perimeters trace the boundary. Top and bottom layers close the part. Infill is the pattern inside those boundaries. More infill generally adds material and time, but strength also depends on walls, orientation, bonding and load direction. A 20% infill setting does not mean the entire part uses 20% of its solid volume, because the walls and skins remain. Simple layer-count example: for a 24 mm height with every layer 0.20 mm thick, 24 ÷ 0.20 = 120 layers. Real slicers may use a different first-layer height or variable layers. Halving layer height roughly doubles the layer count, not necessarily the total print time."},
{"title":"Orient for the load as well as for fewer supports","page":"FDM 3D Printing","summary":"Read an FDM part as deposited roads and layers, then choose orientation, walls, infill and supports for its job.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/3d-printing-fdm.html#orient-for-the-load-as-well-as-for-fewer-supports","text":"Orient for the load as well as for fewer supports FDM parts are anisotropic: properties depend on direction. A load that pulls layers apart can expose a weaker bond than a load carried along continuous deposited roads. Avoid placing the main tensile load across layer interfaces when an orientation can carry it along the layers. This is a design starting point, not a strength rating. An overhang extends beyond the layer below. A short bridge spans between supported ends. A long unsupported underside may need temporary support material. Support contacts can leave marks, so keep them away from critical visible or mating surfaces where possible. There is no universal overhang angle that works for all materials and settings. For a rectangular test bar pulled along its length, laying its length in the build plane usually gives a better starting orientation than standing it upright and pulling across stacked layers. A bracket may have several important load directions; choose a compromise or change the design, then test it. Mark the main load direction and critical fit surfaces on the model. Compare orientations for layer direction, build-plate contact and unsupported undersides. Preview walls, infill and supports one layer at a time. Check that support can actually be removed from each enclosed space."},
{"title":"Choose filament for service conditions and printer capability","page":"FDM 3D Printing","summary":"Read an FDM part as deposited roads and layers, then choose orientation, walls, infill and supports for its job.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/3d-printing-fdm.html#choose-filament-for-service-conditions-and-printer-capability","text":"Choose filament for service conditions and printer capability Filament Useful starting point Check before choosing PLA Stiff models and easy-to-print prototypes Heat exposure and impact requirements; a neat print is not automatically suitable for a warm enclosure. PETG Tougher functional parts with useful layer bonding Stringing, moisture and support-removal behavior for the grade. ABS / ASA Applications needing greater heat resistance than ordinary PLA; ASA is often chosen for outdoor exposure Warping control, equipment capability and processing requirements. TPU Flexible parts Hardness grade and whether the feed system can handle flexible filament. Nylon (polyamide) Tough engineering parts Moisture control, dimensional change and the required printing conditions. PVA is used as a water-soluble support material in compatible multi-material systems; it is not a universal companion for every filament. Use the exact filament manufacturer's settings and the printer's limits. Consult Equipment Operating Procedure and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets for process controls. Material guidance is grade-specific Filament names describe families, not identical recipes. Pigments, fillers and blends change behavior. Prusa's material guide separates common filament families and their printing requirements. It does not establish what any particular shop printer can process."},
{"title":"Estimate mass from deposited volume, not infill alone","page":"FDM 3D Printing","summary":"Read an FDM part as deposited roads and layers, then choose orientation, walls, infill and supports for its job.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/3d-printing-fdm.html#estimate-mass-from-deposited-volume-not-infill-alone","text":"Estimate mass from deposited volume, not infill alone Mass = deposited volume × density. If a simplified model has 8 cm³ of solid walls and skins plus a 32 cm³ interior at 25% infill, its estimated deposited volume is 8 + (32 × 0.25) = 16 cm³. At an assumed density of 1.24 g/cm³, that is 19.84 g. Supports, brims and purge material add more. This estimate treats infill percentage as a volume fraction of the interior. A slicer estimates the actual paths more closely. Time also includes travel, acceleration, heating and cooling constraints; volume alone does not give an exact printing duration. Post-processing choices are in Conditioning and Finishing Plastics. Practice estimating filament mass"},
{"title":"Separate the bending step from the cutting and bonding steps","page":"Bending and Laminating Wood","summary":"Compare steam bending, kerf bending, bent lamination and veneering by what changes shape and what holds the result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bending-and-laminating-wood.html#separate-the-bending-step-from-the-cutting-and-bonding-steps","text":"Separate the bending step from the cutting and bonding steps Bending changes the shape of wood and belongs to Forming. A complete route can use several of the five process categories: cutting thin strips or kerfs is Separating; bonding layers is Fabricating. Veneering bonds a thin wood facing to a supporting base, so it is not automatically a bending operation. Wood remains in the Wood & Composites material family. See Manufacturing Process Categories for the process model. Wood structure, moisture and engineered-wood products belong to the wood-as-a-material topic; here the question is how to obtain a shape. What bends, and what retains the shape? Route What changes Main trade-off Steam bending One piece is softened, bent over a form and restrained while it sets and dries. Continuous grain can follow the curve, but stock quality and bend severity strongly affect success. Kerf bending Partial-depth slots let the remaining thin web bend; slots close on the inside. Material removal reduces the intact section and leaves visible or concealed slots. Bent lamination Thin strips bend together over a form; cured adhesive holds the assembly. Repeated curves are practical, but strip preparation, glue lines and springback matter. Veneering A thin facing bonds to a substrate, flat or curved. The base supplies the shape and support; a face veneer alone is not a thick structural member."},
{"title":"Steam makes suitable wood more bendable; the form holds the curve","page":"Bending and Laminating Wood","summary":"Compare steam bending, kerf bending, bent lamination and veneering by what changes shape and what holds the result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bending-and-laminating-wood.html#steam-makes-suitable-wood-more-bendable-the-form-holds-the-curve","text":"Steam makes suitable wood more bendable; the form holds the curve Heat and moisture soften the wood's lignin-rich matrix, allowing the piece to deform more readily. The wood does not become a liquid. It is bent around a form, a shaped support, and held while it cools and dries. Clear, straight-grained white oak and ash are common candidates; species alone does not guarantee a successful bend. The outside of a bend tends to stretch while the inside compresses. Too much stretching can split the outer fibers; too much compression can wrinkle the inner face. Grain deviations, drying history, moisture, thickness and bend radius all matter. A larger radius is a gentler bend. There is no single radius-to-thickness ratio that works for every piece. Why heat and moisture work together Moisture lowers the temperature at which the wood's matrix softens. This is why moist heat can make wood bendable without melting it. The USDA Forest Products Laboratory's Wood Handbook chapter on specialty treatments discusses plasticizing wood. Its Bending Solid Wood to Form compares species and bending conditions. These are process concepts, not steaming-equipment instructions. Equipment use belongs with Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Bent lamination uses thin strips and adhesive to retain a curve","page":"Bending and Laminating Wood","summary":"Compare steam bending, kerf bending, bent lamination and veneering by what changes shape and what holds the result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bending-and-laminating-wood.html#bent-lamination-uses-thin-strips-and-adhesive-to-retain-a-curve","text":"Bent lamination uses thin strips and adhesive to retain a curve Thin strips bend more easily than one solid piece of the same total thickness. Bent lamination brings prepared strips together with adhesive over a form, then restrains them until the bond has developed as specified for that adhesive. The strips commonly run with their grain along the curve. Alternating grain direction is not a requirement of bent lamination. Springback is the tendency for a bend to open when restraint is removed. It can occur in both steam-bent and laminated parts. Thinner laminations can reduce springback for a given total thickness and curve, but adhesive behavior, layer count and material also matter. A trial bend establishes any form compensation; there is no universal correction percentage. Steam bending curves one softened piece around a form; bent lamination curves several thin strips and bonds them together over a form. Both can spring back when released. Do not rank every “hot/wet” part above every “cold/dry” part in strength. Compare the actual grain path, remaining section, bond quality, loading and service conditions. Practice bending-route selection"},
{"title":"Kerf bending trades an intact section for easier curvature","page":"Bending and Laminating Wood","summary":"Compare steam bending, kerf bending, bent lamination and veneering by what changes shape and what holds the result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bending-and-laminating-wood.html#kerf-bending-trades-an-intact-section-for-easier-curvature","text":"Kerf bending trades an intact section for easier curvature A series of partial-depth slots on the inside of the intended curve leaves a thin continuous web on the outside. The slots narrow as the piece bends. Slot depth and spacing, the remaining web and the desired radius determine how it deforms. This method can suit curved coverings where the slotted side is concealed. It also removes load-carrying material. Filling or bonding the slots does not automatically restore the properties of the original uncut board. A backing or supporting structure may be part of the design; structural performance needs its own justification."},
{"title":"Veneer follows a prepared substrate; it does not replace it","page":"Bending and Laminating Wood","summary":"Compare steam bending, kerf bending, bent lamination and veneering by what changes shape and what holds the result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bending-and-laminating-wood.html#veneer-follows-a-prepared-substrate-it-does-not-replace-it","text":"Veneer follows a prepared substrate; it does not replace it Veneer is a thin wood layer. A substrate is the base it is bonded to. Veneering puts a chosen grain appearance over that base and can cover a flat surface or an appropriate curved form. The substrate must have the intended shape and a sound, even bonding surface. In book matching, adjacent veneer leaves are opened like book pages to make a mirrored pattern. In slip matching, leaves are placed beside one another in the same orientation for a repeated pattern. Neither arrangement is the same as alternating structural grain directions through a panel's thickness. Unequal face and back construction can encourage a panel to warp. Veneer thickness, grain, adhesive and the balancing back layer belong in the layup design. Glue selection and detailed bonding methods belong to wood fabrication; engineered panels belong to the material topic."},
{"title":"A bill of materials says what goes into one assembly","page":"Bills of Materials","summary":"List the parts in an assembly and estimate each part's material share without confusing stock purchases with per-part consumption.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bills-of-materials.html#a-bill-of-materials-says-what-goes-into-one-assembly","text":"A bill of materials says what goes into one assembly A bill of materials (BOM) lists the parts needed for a product. Give each part a stable number, a clear name, a quantity per assembly, a material and a make-or-buy designation. Identify the stock for made parts and the purchasing description for bought parts. Made means the part needs a drawing and a route such as an operation method sheet. Bought means it arrives as a component; it still needs a size and specification so the buyer can obtain the correct item. Match the BOM revision to the assembly drawing. State whether quantities are per assembly or for the whole batch."},
{"title":"Material cost per part is its allocated stock share times stock price","page":"Bills of Materials","summary":"List the parts in an assembly and estimate each part's material share without confusing stock purchases with per-part consumption.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bills-of-materials.html#material-cost-per-part-is-its-allocated-stock-share-times-stock-price","text":"Material cost per part is its allocated stock share times stock price Estimated material cost per part = stock share allocated to that part × price of that stock unit. Keep both amounts on the same basis: length for constant-section bar, area for sheet of the same thickness, or mass when purchased by mass. Allow for the saw cut's width ( kerf ), trimming and unusable stock. Finished-part volume alone can understate what making the part consumes. State how you allocate waste and reusable leftovers so two estimates can be compared. Consumption, purchase cost and batch yield answer different questions You may need to buy a whole sheet even when one part uses a small fraction. That purchase is a cash requirement; the allocated fraction is the material estimate for the part. For a batch that consumes the entire stock unit, dividing its cost by the number of acceptable parts includes the loss from rejected pieces. Avoid charging the same waste once in the allocated share and again as an added allowance."},
{"title":"Worked BOM: one display label assembly costs $1.20 in allocated materials","page":"Bills of Materials","summary":"List the parts in an assembly and estimate each part's material share without confusing stock purchases with per-part consumption.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bills-of-materials.html#worked-bom-one-display-label-assembly-costs-1-20-in-allocated-materials","text":"Worked BOM: one display label assembly costs $1.20 in allocated materials This original example matches DL-01 in Operation Method Sheets and Critical Dimensions for the Shop. All prices are invented teaching values, not quotations. Assembly DL-A, revision A, contains one made plywood plate and two purchased closed cord loops. The 110 × 50 × 6 mm starting blank is allocated a 120 × 60 mm area of a 1200 × 120 × 6 mm birch plywood strip costing $18. The larger allocation includes room for cutting and trimming. Allocation share = (120 × 60) / (1200 × 120) = 0.05. Plate material estimate = 0.05 × $18 = $0.90. DL-A revision A — quantities and material estimate per assembly Part number / name Make / buy; material and stock Quantity Cost each Extended cost DL-01 / display label plate Make; birch plywood, blank 110 × 50 × 6 mm; allocated stock area 120 × 60 mm as above. 1 $0.90 $0.90 DL-02 / closed cord loop Buy; waxed cotton cord, 1 mm diameter, 100 mm closed-loop circumference; example pack of 20 for $3. 2 $0.15 $0.30 Total allocated material and purchased-component cost per assembly $1.20 Extended cost is quantity × cost each. The $1.20 excludes labor, machine time, tooling, consumables, taxes and delivery. It is not a complete manufacturing cost or a selling price. Broader cost roll-up and design-for-manufacturing decisions belong with manufacturing economics. Buying one strip and one pack costs $21, even for one assembly. Unused material remains inventory; do not confuse that purchase total with $1.20 of allocated consumption."},
{"title":"Check quantities, units and waste before totaling the BOM","page":"Bills of Materials","summary":"List the parts in an assembly and estimate each part's material share without confusing stock purchases with per-part consumption.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/bills-of-materials.html#check-quantities-units-and-waste-before-totaling-the-bom","text":"Check quantities, units and waste before totaling the BOM Match every drawing part number to a BOM row. State the quantity per assembly and multiply once for the batch quantity. Specify stock dimensions and material for made parts, and purchasing details for bought parts. Use the same units in the stock-share numerator and denominator. Document the cutting allowance and treatment of leftovers. Multiply each quantity by its unit cost and sum the extended costs. Keep unrounded intermediate calculations; round money to cents at the stated reporting step. Practice stock-share cost Practice a BOM total"},
{"title":"Nominal size is a name; actual size is what fits","page":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","summary":"Separate sales size from measured size and calculate board footage using an explicit dimension convention.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/buying-lumber-and-board-feet.html#nominal-size-is-a-name-actual-size-is-what-fits","text":"Nominal size is a name; actual size is what fits A nominal lumber size identifies a product category. Dry, surfaced construction lumber sold as a 2 × 4 commonly measures 1-1/2 × 3-1/2 in in cross-section. Rough, green, specialty, and reclaimed stock can differ. Measure the piece when fit matters. Drying and surfacing help explain the difference, but nominal names are not a universal subtraction rule. Do not assume that removing the same amount from every nominal size will give its actual dimensions. Use Measurement Language for units and Dimensional Analysis for conversions. Keep length units explicit: an 8 ft board is 96 in long, not 8 in. Practice: actual width"},
{"title":"Quarter thickness and surfacing describe different things","page":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","summary":"Separate sales size from measured size and calculate board footage using an explicit dimension convention.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/buying-lumber-and-board-feet.html#quarter-thickness-and-surfacing-describe-different-things","text":"Quarter thickness and surfacing describe different things Hardwood thickness is often named in quarter-inch increments before final surfacing: 4/4 means nominal one-inch stock and 8/4 means nominal two-inch stock. Neither guarantees that much finished thickness. Drying, flattening, and planing reduce the usable material. Rough Sawn surfaces remain; allow material for flattening and sizing. S2S Surfaced on two sides, normally the two broad faces. Do not assume the edges are straight or ready for a joint. S4S Surfaced on all four long sides: both faces and both edges. Ends are not included in the four. Ask for the supplied thickness, width, length, moisture condition, grade, and surfacing. A thickness label alone is not a complete purchase specification."},
{"title":"A board foot is volume, not length","page":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","summary":"Separate sales size from measured size and calculate board footage using an explicit dimension convention.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/buying-lumber-and-board-feet.html#a-board-foot-is-volume-not-length","text":"A board foot is volume, not length One board foot (BF) is the volume of a piece 1 in × 12 in × 12 in: 144 cubic inches. With thickness and width in inches and length in feet: BF = thickness (in) × width (in) × length (ft) / 12. If all three dimensions are in inches, divide by 144 instead. Multiply by the number of identical boards only after finding the volume of one. Why the mixed-unit formula divides by 12 Convert the length in feet to inches by multiplying by 12. Dividing the resulting cubic inches by 144 gives 12/144 = 1/12. The formula counts 144-cubic-inch units; it is not a linear-foot conversion. State the measuring convention. A physical-volume calculation uses actual dimensions. A sales tally may use nominal thickness or other trade rules and may round individual board measurements. Ask the seller; do not replace a quoted tally basis with finished dimensions without saying so. The examples here explicitly specify their basis and use unrounded arithmetic until the final answer."},
{"title":"Keep purchase tally and finished volume separate","page":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","summary":"Separate sales size from measured size and calculate board footage using an explicit dimension convention.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/buying-lumber-and-board-feet.html#keep-purchase-tally-and-finished-volume-separate","text":"Keep purchase tally and finished volume separate Suppose a seller specifies a tally using nominal 1 in thickness, measured 7 in width, and 9 ft length. The tally is 1 × 7 × 9 / 12 = 5.25 BF. If that board finishes at 3/4 in thick with the same width and length, its physical volume is 0.75 × 7 × 9 / 12 = 3.9375 BF, or 3.94 BF to two decimals. These answers describe different quantities. The finished volume does not change the stated sales agreement. A board tally is not a finished-parts yield. Allow for saw cuts, defects, flattening, trimming, and the layout of the parts. Appearance grades and structural grades answer different questions; an attractive board is not automatically suitable for a load-bearing member. Build a list of boards below to compare the sales tally with actual volume, calculate cost, and estimate a material allowance. Say which allowance you mean. This calculator treats the allowance as the share of the material you buy that will not end up in parts: BF to buy = needed BF ÷ (1 − allowance), so a 20% allowance means buying 1.25 times what you need. Many shop guides instead say \"add 20%\" to what you need (× 1.20), which is a different definition and buys less. Both are used; check which one a source or an instructor means before you compare numbers. Practice: sales tally Practice: physical volume Practice: several boards Practice: all-inch dimensions Practice: material allowance"},
{"title":"Further reading on wood properties and products","page":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","summary":"Separate sales size from measured size and calculate board footage using an explicit dimension convention.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/buying-lumber-and-board-feet.html#further-reading-on-wood-properties-and-products","text":"Further reading on wood properties and products Reference resources NIST: American Softwood Lumber Standard PS 20-25 Penn State Extension: Valuing Standing Timber, board-foot section American Hardwood Export Council: hardwood measurement"},
{"title":"Check geometry and material before comparing prices","page":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","summary":"Use geometry, material, quantity and tooling cost to narrow the process choice, then estimate cost per part and production rate.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-plastic-forming-process.html#check-geometry-and-material-before-comparing-prices","text":"Check geometry and material before comparing prices A low quoted price is irrelevant if the process cannot make the required shape or use the required material. Start with Plastics as Materials and the capability cards in Plastic Forming Processes. Describe the geometry: hollow container, open sheet-like shell, constant cross-section or discrete solid part. Choose a material type and candidate grade that meet the load, temperature and environment. State the quantity, expected repeat orders and required delivery rate. Check how the part leaves the tool: draft, undercuts and access to internal spaces. Compare tooling, per-part cost, achievable detail and likely defects for the remaining processes."},
{"title":"Use part shape to build a short list","page":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","summary":"Use geometry, material, quantity and tooling cost to narrow the process choice, then estimate cost per part and production rate.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-plastic-forming-process.html#use-part-shape-to-build-a-short-list","text":"Use part shape to build a short list Starting candidates, not exclusive rules Need Start with Then ask Many thin-walled hollow containers Blow molding Can the hot tube or preform stretch into this shape with an adequate wall? A large hollow shell in a modest quantity Rotational molding Are the longer cycle and achievable detail acceptable? An open tray or sheet-like cover Thermoforming Will the sheet thin too much, and can it release from the tool? A long constant cross-section Extrusion Which end features or side holes would need later operations? Many detailed discrete parts Injection molding Can walls stay reasonably uniform, and does quantity justify the mold? Repeated thermoset parts or composite panels Compression molding Can the charge fill the matched tools before curing? A few detailed resin parts Casting Will mold life, trapped air and cure time meet the need? A custom fiber-reinforced shell Hand layup Is the labor acceptable, and can the reinforcement reach every region? “Solid” does not mean “make every region thick.” An injection-molded housing is a discrete part, yet hollowing out bulky sections often improves it. Several processes can make a tank; size, quantity, resin and detail determine the better candidate. Practice choosing a process"},
{"title":"Spread tooling cost over the number of acceptable parts","page":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","summary":"Use geometry, material, quantity and tooling cost to narrow the process choice, then estimate cost per part and production rate.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-plastic-forming-process.html#spread-tooling-cost-over-the-number-of-acceptable-parts","text":"Spread tooling cost over the number of acceptable parts A simple comparison is average cost per part = tooling cost / quantity + variable cost per part. Variable cost includes the costs that grow with production, such as material and processing time. Tooling cost is paid even if only a few parts are made. Illustrative estimates, not supplier prices: suppose two processes can both meet a cover's specification. Route A costs $900 for tooling and $14 per part. Route B costs $18,000 for tooling and $2 per part. Average cost per acceptable part, assuming no scrap or extra charges Quantity Route A Route B 100 $900 / 100 + $14 = $23.00 $18,000 / 100 + $2 = $182.00 5,000 $900 / 5,000 + $14 = $14.18 $18,000 / 5,000 + $2 = $5.60 Find the quantity where the two estimates are equal Set total costs equal: 900 + 14N = 18000 + 2N. Then 12N = 17100, so N = 1,425 parts. Below that quantity A costs less; above it B costs less under these assumptions. This model leaves out financing, mold replacement, setup changes, inspection, shipping and rejects. A flexible mold that must be replaced during a run adds another tooling cost. Recalculate when the assumptions change. Practice tooling cost per part Practice a cost crossover"},
{"title":"Cycle time and cavity count set the ideal production rate","page":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","summary":"Use geometry, material, quantity and tooling cost to narrow the process choice, then estimate cost per part and production rate.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-plastic-forming-process.html#cycle-time-and-cavity-count-set-the-ideal-production-rate","text":"Cycle time and cavity count set the ideal production rate A cavity makes one part per cycle. With cycle time in seconds, ideal parts per hour = 3600 / cycle time × cavities. For a 30-second cycle and two identical cavities: 3600 / 30 × 2 = 240 parts/h. That assumes uninterrupted operation and every part acceptable. Use the complete repeating cycle time, including opening and ejection; do not add overlapping cooling time twice. Actual useful output is lower when setup, stoppages or rejects occur. More cavities may increase tooling cost and require a larger machine, so a higher ideal rate does not by itself settle the process choice. Practice production rate"},
{"title":"Explain the choice with both a benefit and a limitation","page":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","summary":"Use geometry, material, quantity and tooling cost to narrow the process choice, then estimate cost per part and production rate.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-plastic-forming-process.html#explain-the-choice-with-both-a-benefit-and-a-limitation","text":"Explain the choice with both a benefit and a limitation A useful selection sounds like this: “For 300 large hollow PE garden reservoirs, investigate rotational molding because the shape fits and tooling can be economical at modest quantities. Confirm wall control and cycle time before committing.” The quantity alone does not prove that choice. A narrow neck, precision sealing face, delivery deadline or different resin could change it. State the assumptions, identify a credible alternative, and compare the features that matter. Further process-selection reading Formlabs' manufacturing-process overview compares form, volume and setup cost. Treat published volume ranges as examples, not universal cutoffs. The linked process references in Plastic Forming Processes explain individual capabilities."},
{"title":"Choose the required geometry before choosing the machine","page":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","summary":"Start with the required cut, surface or hole, then match the machine and cutter to that result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-woodworking-machine.html#choose-the-required-geometry-before-choosing-the-machine","text":"Choose the required geometry before choosing the machine A machine name does not describe a finished part. First state the result: a straight edge, a curved outline, a flat face, a hole or a round profile. Then consider the workpiece dimensions, accuracy, surface quality and available tooling. The machines below remove material and belong to Separating. This is a selection guide, not an operating procedure. Machine-specific use belongs with Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Equipment Operating Procedure. Grain and moisture are covered in the wood-as-a-material topic; the operation selected here depends on those properties. Describe the surface or cut that must exist when the operation is complete. Identify which existing face or edge can locate the workpiece. Check machine capacity and whether the cutter can reach the feature. Decide whether the cut is final size or leaves material for another operation."},
{"title":"Match the cut to a suitable starting machine","page":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","summary":"Start with the required cut, surface or hole, then match the machine and cutter to that result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-woodworking-machine.html#match-the-cut-to-a-suitable-starting-machine","text":"Match the cut to a suitable starting machine Common choices; capacity and tooling can change the selection Required result Starting choice What distinguishes it Straight rip to width Table saw The fence locates a straight reference edge for a parallel cut. Square crosscut or miter Miter saw; table saw with crosscut sled or miter gauge A miter is an angled cut across the width. Crosscut capacity limits the workpiece size. Dado across a face Router; table saw with compatible dado tooling A dado is a flat-bottomed channel across the grain. Not every table saw accepts a dado set. Curved outside outline Band saw or jig saw A band saw uses a continuous loop; a jig saw uses a short reciprocating blade. Resaw into thinner boards Band saw The cut passes through the board's width to divide its thickness. Resaw capacity and blade selection matter. Enclosed interior opening Jig saw or scroll saw A starter hole gives blade access without a cut from the outside edge. A scroll saw suits fine, intricate work; its blade can be threaded through the hole. Flat reference face; straight square edge Jointer Establishes geometry before sizing. See Milling Rough Lumber Square. Target thickness and parallel faces Thickness planer Uses an already flat reference face; it does not reliably flatten a warped board by itself. Edge profile, rabbet or repeated outline Router Bit shape makes the profile; a template guides repeated outlines. A rabbet is a recess open along an edge. Round spindle or bowl-like form Wood lathe The workpiece rotates around an axis while a cutting tool removes material. Located hole with controlled angle and depth Drill press A guided spindle and depth stop support repeatability; bit type determines the hole's form. Read How Saws Cut Wood before choosing a saw blade. Table saws can crosscut as well as rip; jig saws and scroll saws are not band saws. Practice resaw selection Practice interior cuts"},
{"title":"Router bits make profiles and channels; templates repeat shapes","page":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","summary":"Start with the required cut, surface or hole, then match the machine and cutter to that result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-woodworking-machine.html#router-bits-make-profiles-and-channels-templates-repeat-shapes","text":"Router bits make profiles and channels; templates repeat shapes A router spins a router bit. Straight or spiral bits make channels and pockets; roundover and chamfer bits change an edge's profile. A bearing-guided bit or guide bushing can follow a template, which is a pattern used to reproduce an outline. Routers may be handheld, mounted in a table or moved by a CNC machine. A shaper commonly carries profile cutters on a table-mounted spindle. Both shape wood, but their cutters and mounting systems are not interchangeable merely because the resulting profile looks similar. Why “router bit” is more useful than “end mill” here CNC routing can use end-mill-like spiral cutters, but routing also includes bearing-guided and shaped profile bits. “End mill” alone does not describe the tooling a beginning woodworker will encounter. Whiteside's template-bit range illustrates tooling for following patterns; its catalog also includes straight bits, flush-trim spiral bits and roundover profiles. Practice template selection"},
{"title":"Spindle and faceplate turning put grain differently relative to the axis","page":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","summary":"Start with the required cut, surface or hole, then match the machine and cutter to that result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-woodworking-machine.html#spindle-and-faceplate-turning-put-grain-differently-relative-to-the-axis","text":"Spindle and faceplate turning put grain differently relative to the axis In typical spindle turning, grain runs mainly parallel to the rotation axis, as in a handle or table leg. In typical faceplate or bowl turning, grain runs mainly across the axis, as in a bowl blank. Faceplates and chucks are workholding methods; the name alone does not guarantee the grain orientation. Turning removes material to make circular features. It does not bend the wood. Lathe Operations explains metal-lathe operations as a separate topic; wood turning has different tools and workholding practices."},
{"title":"Choose a drill bit for the hole's bottom, edge and diameter","page":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","summary":"Start with the required cut, surface or hole, then match the machine and cutter to that result.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/choosing-a-woodworking-machine.html#choose-a-drill-bit-for-the-hole-s-bottom-edge-and-diameter","text":"Choose a drill bit for the hole's bottom, edge and diameter Common wood-hole tools Bit Useful result Limit to remember Twist General holes in a range of diameters The usual point leaves a conical bottom in a blind hole. Brad-point Accurately located holes with clean entry edges in wood The center point projects beyond the cutting lips. Forstner Clean-sided blind recess with a largely flat bottom Most leave a small center-point mark; “flat” does not mean perfectly featureless. Spade Economical larger holes where finish is less critical Usually leaves a center-point recess and a rougher result. Hole saw Large through-hole by cutting around a core Leaves a plug; it does not clear a flat-bottomed pocket. A drill press improves control of axis and depth; it does not make every bit suitable for every hole. Cutter geometry, workpiece support and setup still affect accuracy. Practice bit selection"},
{"title":"Conditioning changes properties; finishing changes the surface","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#conditioning-changes-properties-finishing-changes-the-surface","text":"Conditioning changes properties; finishing changes the surface Conditioning changes the material's state or properties: relieving internal stress, developing a thermoset cure, or controlling absorbed moisture. Finishing changes the surface for appearance, protection or function. A part can need both. Removing a support is Separating; sanding the resulting surface is Finishing. Start with the resin, dimensions and service requirement. A glossy surface does not prove that a part is strong, properly cured or dimensionally correct."},
{"title":"Annealing relieves stress, but can change dimensions","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#annealing-relieves-stress-but-can-change-dimensions","text":"Annealing relieves stress, but can change dimensions Annealing uses a controlled heating, holding and cooling schedule. In acrylic it can reduce stress left by machining, bending or polishing. It is not a general instruction to heat near molding temperature. Use the resin supplier's schedule for the grade and thickness, including support of the part during treatment. Printed PLA can also be annealed. Depending on its formulation and thermal history, heating can allow more crystalline order and improve heat resistance. Shrinkage, expansion in another direction or warping can spoil a fit. Measure before and after; do not assume every strength property improves. Stress relief and crystallinity are different changes Residual stress is locked-in stress remaining after manufacturing. Heating can allow molecular motion that relaxes it. Some polymers, including PLA, can also develop more ordered regions during suitable heat treatment. Acrylic stress relief and PLA crystallization therefore do not share a universal temperature recipe. ACRYLITE's annealing guidance describes stress relief for its sheet. Prusa's annealing experiments show why dimensional change must be checked on printed specimens."},
{"title":"Curing creates a network; drying removes moisture","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#curing-creates-a-network-drying-removes-moisture","text":"Curing creates a network; drying removes moisture A thermoset cure is a chemical reaction that builds links between polymer chains. Resin and hardener, heat or light may initiate the reaction, depending on the system. Correct proportions, time and temperature matter. A prescribed post-cure can develop properties further; an already cured thermoset will not simply remelt like a thermoplastic. Drying moisture-sensitive filament addresses a different problem. Moisture can affect extrusion and part quality, but drying does not replace annealing or cure. Use a drying schedule intended for the material; excessive heat can deform filament or its spool. Other ways processors change plastic properties Industrial radiation treatment can crosslink selected polymers, but the result depends on the polymer and dose; some systems also undergo chain breakdown. Additives and reinforcements usually enter during material formulation rather than as a finishing step on a completed part. Fibers can increase stiffness, meaning a higher elastic modulus in the relevant direction, not a lower one. These changes require grade-specific data."},
{"title":"Remove scratches progressively before adding gloss or paint","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#remove-scratches-progressively-before-adding-gloss-or-paint","text":"Remove scratches progressively before adding gloss or paint Sanding replaces a coarse scratch pattern with progressively finer scratches. Start with the finest abrasive that will remove the defect efficiently. Within the same grit system, a larger number generally means a finer abrasive. A sequence such as P240 → P320 → P400 → P600 illustrates progression; it is not a required recipe for every plastic or coating. Remove the previous scratch pattern before moving on and clean away abrasive residue between stages. Wet sanding with suitable waterproof abrasive can reduce loading and heat where the material and equipment permit it. Keep corners and fit surfaces from rounding over. Paint may need a controlled scuffed surface rather than a polished one; follow its preparation specification. Tumbling can finish many parts together, but media and time affect edge rounding and small features. Buffing can improve gloss, but excessive heat or pressure can smear the surface. Check a representative sample rather than equating more polishing with a better part."},
{"title":"Flame and solvent smoothing are material-specific processes","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#flame-and-solvent-smoothing-are-material-specific-processes","text":"Flame and solvent smoothing are material-specific processes Flame polishing can smooth suitable acrylic edges by briefly softening the surface. It can also round edges and introduce stress. It is not a general treatment for clear plastics: polycarbonate is not interchangeable with acrylic, and a process must be validated for its exact grade. A clear edge is not necessarily an optically accurate or low-stress edge. Solvent or vapor smoothing softens a compatible polymer's surface chemically. Acetone smoothing is associated with ABS and ASA; do not transfer that recipe to PLA or PETG. Smoothing can erase fine lettering, change dimensions and alter mechanical performance. It does not guarantee that weak layer bonds inside a print have been repaired. These descriptions explain process selection, not operating procedures. Refer to Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets, Fire Safety and Equipment Operating Procedure for the relevant controls. Why a clear surface can crack later Surface treatment can leave stress or expose existing stress to a solvent. Fine cracks may appear during later cleaning or bonding. ACRYLITE's edge-finishing guidance discusses polishing and compatibility; Prusa's ABS guidance identifies acetone smoothing for ABS. Choose a verified material/process pair, then check both appearance and fit."},
{"title":"Coatings need adhesion as well as the right appearance","page":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","summary":"Separate property changes from surface treatments, and match annealing, smoothing and coatings to the exact plastic.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/conditioning-and-finishing-plastics.html#coatings-need-adhesion-as-well-as-the-right-appearance","text":"Coatings need adhesion as well as the right appearance For paint, remove contamination with a compatible cleaner, prepare the surface as specified and use a primer or adhesion promoter when the coating system calls for one. PE and PP often need special preparation or products because of low surface energy. A primer is not automatically compatible with every plastic. Trial the complete system, including cure, on a representative sample. Dip coating deposits a liquid film as the part is withdrawn, followed by drying or curing as the formulation requires. Withdrawal rate and liquid properties affect thickness. Powder coating plastics requires a suitable way to attract or deposit the powder and a cure that the part can withstand; an insulating plastic does not become conductive merely because a grounding wire touches it. Electroplating needs a conductive surface. Plastics therefore require preparation and a conductive layer before electroplating. Decorative options also include printing, decals, in-mold labels and dyes for receptive polymers. Choose them for the required abrasion, outdoor exposure and chemical resistance; decoration is not automatically protection. Powder coating needs a complete substrate and cure system Interpon's heat-sensitive-substrate overview distinguishes powder-coating systems for heat-sensitive substrates, including thermal and UV curing approaches. A conventional metal-part powder cure is not a default recipe for a plastic part. Practice choosing a finish"},
{"title":"A critical dimension controls fit, function or an interface","page":"Critical Dimensions for the Shop","summary":"Choose dimensions from fit, function and interfaces, then give the maker and inspector the same references and limits.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/critical-dimensions-for-the-shop.html#a-critical-dimension-controls-fit-function-or-an-interface","text":"A critical dimension controls fit, function or an interface A critical dimension is one whose variation can stop the part doing its job. Start with what touches another part, what must move or clear, and what locates the part. A hole's diameter may control whether a pin enters; its position may control whether two parts line up. Critical does not mean “make every tolerance tight.” A tolerance is the permitted variation in a dimension. Choose it from the function, then check that the process and inspection method can achieve it. See Tolerances and Fits for limits and fits, Measurement Language for units and measurement language, and Surface Texture and Finish for surface requirements."},
{"title":"Give hole locations from named reference edges","page":"Critical Dimensions for the Shop","summary":"Choose dimensions from fit, function and interfaces, then give the maker and inspector the same references and limits.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/critical-dimensions-for-the-shop.html#give-hole-locations-from-named-reference-edges","text":"Give hole locations from named reference edges The original DL-01 display plate below illustrates a shop drawing. Its two suspension holes must stay at the intended locations; length and width mainly establish the display area. These are invented teaching limits, not a general specification for plywood or hanging hardware. DL-01 revision A. Front view of a 100 by 40 mm plate with two through holes. From left edge L, centers are at 15 and 85 mm; both are 20 mm from bottom edge B. Hole diameter is 4.5 mm. Side view shows 6 mm thickness. Critical hole dimensions are marked C; all limits are in the table. All dimensions in mm. Not to scale for measurement. L and B are plain-language reference labels, not formal geometric-tolerancing datum symbols. C marks the dimensions selected as critical for this example. DL-01 revision A — drawing requirements Feature Requirement Reason / inspection Material and outline Birch plywood; length 100 ±1; width 40 ±1; thickness 6 ±0.5 mm. Display area and thickness. Measure with calipers; check adjacent edges with a try square for no visible gap. C — two through holes Each diameter 4.5 ±0.2 mm. Openings for the loops; use suitable plug gauges for 4.3–4.7 mm limits. C — center positions From L: x = 15 ±0.5 and 85 ±0.5 mm. From B: y = 20 ±0.5 mm for each. Consistent suspension locations. Check each coordinate from the stated edge. Surface and edges No coating; no loose fibers, splits or separated plywood layers. Visual inspection after sanding; retain the dimensional limits. All features have requirements, including those not marked C. See Operation Method Sheets for the manufacturing sequence and Bills of Materials for the matching parts list. How to check a center when a caliper touches the hole edge Measure from the reference edge to the nearest hole edge, then add half the measured hole diameter to obtain that center coordinate. Use the same reference and direction as the drawing. This example does not specify a precision pin fit; a more demanding interface needs a more capable measurement method and a reviewed tolerancing scheme. The nominal hole spacing is 70 mm, derived from 85 − 15. The separate ±0.5 mm x-coordinate limits can allow spacing from 69 to 71 mm. If an interface needs a tighter spacing, the designer must change the requirements; the inspector must not invent a tighter limit."},
{"title":"Dimension the part so it can be made without measuring the picture","page":"Critical Dimensions for the Shop","summary":"Choose dimensions from fit, function and interfaces, then give the maker and inspector the same references and limits.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/critical-dimensions-for-the-shop.html#dimension-the-part-so-it-can-be-made-without-measuring-the-picture","text":"Dimension the part so it can be made without measuring the picture Include the part identity, revision, material, units, overall dimensions, feature sizes and locations, thickness, and required finish. Supply enough views to remove ambiguity. Call a hole “through” when it passes through the material; otherwise specify depth. Locate related features from a common reference where that matches their function. Do not give multiple independent dimensions for the same feature unless their relationship is clear. A redundant dimension can contradict the dimensions used to make the part. Identify every mating surface, clearance and locating feature. Choose references the maker can establish and the inspector can reach. Check that each feature has a size, location and acceptance limit. Remove conflicting or missing dimensions; do not rely on drawing scale. Match the drawing revision to the method sheet and BOM. Choose a critical feature"},
{"title":"Choose the cut from the material and the feature","page":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","summary":"Choose a separating process for the plastic and the feature, then control heat, chips and edge damage.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/cutting-and-machining-plastics.html#choose-the-cut-from-the-material-and-the-feature","text":"Choose the cut from the material and the feature Separating removes material. A saw makes a blank, a drill makes a round hole, and a router or mill can make an outline, pocket or slot. Identify the resin and grade before choosing the process: two clear sheets can be acrylic and polycarbonate, with very different cutting behavior. Process Useful for What controls the result Sawing Straight cuts or curved blanks on a band saw Use a blade intended for the plastic and thickness. Teeth must cut without grabbing or packing the cut with chips; the finest tooth spacing is not always best. Drilling Holes through sheet or solid stock Use suitable drill geometry, support the exit surface and provide chip clearance. Brittle acrylic can crack when the drill grabs at breakthrough. Milling and routing Accurate edges, slots and pockets Sharp cutting edges, room for chips to escape and a supported workpiece reduce melting and chatter. Laser cutting Sheet profiles in an approved material The resin, additives, thickness and laser wavelength matter. A glossy edge alone does not prove dimensional accuracy. Hot-wire cutting Contours in suitable thermoplastic foam A heated wire softens a narrow path. This is not a general cutting method for solid plastic sheet or every foam formulation. See Milling Operations for tool paths and feature access. Machine use belongs with Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Make chips instead of rubbing the plastic hot","page":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","summary":"Choose a separating process for the plastic and the feature, then control heat, chips and edge damage.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/cutting-and-machining-plastics.html#make-chips-instead-of-rubbing-the-plastic-hot","text":"Make chips instead of rubbing the plastic hot Heat builds up near the cut because plastics generally conduct heat poorly. Softened chips can stick to the tool or weld back onto the edge. The symptoms are smeared surfaces, strings of softened material and chips packed into the cutter. Slowing the feed alone can make melting worse. Feed is how quickly the tool advances through the work; spindle speed is how quickly it turns. Too little advance per tooth lets the edge rub instead of taking a useful chip. Start with cutting data for the resin and tool, keep the tool sharp, and maintain chip clearance. Reducing excessive spindle speed may help, but feed, speed and cut depth must be considered together. Deep holes need a planned way to clear chips. Coolants and cleaners must be compatible with the plastic; an unsuitable liquid can cause fine cracks called crazing. Use Feeds and Speeds for the relationships, not its metal cutting values as plastic settings. Why a slower cut can produce more heat A tooth that advances too little spends more time sliding against the work. The next tooth may also recut trapped chips. Useful chips carry some heat away, so reducing chip thickness without reducing rubbing is not a reliable cooling strategy. ACRYLITE's drilling guidance identifies low feed or excessive RPM as causes of melted chips; its routing guidance treats feed, cutting edges and RPM together."},
{"title":"Acrylic, PVC and polycarbonate behave differently under a laser","page":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","summary":"Choose a separating process for the plastic and the feature, then control heat, chips and edge damage.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/cutting-and-machining-plastics.html#acrylic-pvc-and-polycarbonate-behave-differently-under-a-laser","text":"Acrylic, PVC and polycarbonate behave differently under a laser Acrylic (PMMA) absorbs the infrared energy of a CO₂ laser. Concentrated heating removes material along the cut and can leave a smooth, glossy edge. That result depends on the sheet grade, thickness and process settings; it is not a promise for every clear plastic or every laser. PVC is not a laser-cutting substitute for acrylic. Heating PVC can release hydrogen chloride; with moisture it forms hydrochloric acid, creating an exposure and corrosion concern. This is a chemical incompatibility, not simply a poor-looking edge. Polycarbonate is often a poor choice for a clean CO₂-laser-cut sheet edge. It can yellow, char and leave a degraded edge instead of acrylic's clear finish. Specialized processes can cut some polycarbonate, so “polycarbonate cannot be laser processed” is too broad. Routing or sawing is often the better starting choice for a clear polycarbonate profile. Do not assign PVC's chlorine chemistry to polycarbonate. Material approval and operating controls belong with Equipment Operating Procedure and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets. Appearance or a trade name is not enough to identify an unknown sheet. Manufacturer references and the limits of a material list Trotec's unsuitable-material guidance lists PVC and explains why composition matters. Boss Laser describes acrylic's cut behavior, PVC decomposition concerns and polycarbonate edge discoloration. Universal Laser Systems' polycarbonate processing guide discusses reducing char through a specialized cutting method. A material name on a capability list does not establish a suitable setup for a particular sheet."},
{"title":"Trim the molding excess without cutting into the part","page":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","summary":"Choose a separating process for the plastic and the feature, then control heat, chips and edge damage.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/cutting-and-machining-plastics.html#trim-the-molding-excess-without-cutting-into-the-part","text":"Trim the molding excess without cutting into the part A gate is the entry from a molding flow channel into the part cavity. Degating separates the part from that feed connection. Flash is a thin unwanted fin, often at a mold joint; trimming removes it. A burr is a raised edge left by cutting. These are different defects, even when the same trimming tool can remove them. Support the part near the cut and leave the required contour intact. Cutting into the wall creates a notch that may start a crack. For batches, tumbling with suitable media can remove small burrs, but it can also round wanted edges or damage thin features. Inspect the dimensions and surface after the trial. Practice diagnosing a melted cut"},
{"title":"Keep the function and simplify how it is made","page":"Design for Manufacturing","summary":"Make the required function easier to produce, assemble, inspect and maintain.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/design-for-manufacturing.html#keep-the-function-and-simplify-how-it-is-made","text":"Keep the function and simplify how it is made Design for manufacturing (DFM) considers production while a design can still change. Preserve required strength, fit and service performance; remove complexity that serves no requirement. Reduce separate parts when this simplifies production and service. Use standard components and available stock sizes. Match geometry to the process and provide tool and inspection access. Make assembly easy to orient, reach and verify. Fewer parts can reduce joining work, but one complex part may need costly tooling or be hard to repair. Compare the whole route."},
{"title":"Specify only the precision the function needs","page":"Design for Manufacturing","summary":"Make the required function easier to produce, assemble, inspect and maintain.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/design-for-manufacturing.html#specify-only-the-precision-the-function-needs","text":"Specify only the precision the function needs A tolerance is the allowed variation in a dimension or other feature. Tight tolerances may require careful setups, slower processing and more capable inspection. Keep tight control where fit or function needs it; do not apply it everywhere by habit. No machine name guarantees a tolerance or price. Geometry, material, tooling, setup and measurement all matter. Confirm capability for the actual feature. Why the loosest tolerance is not automatically cheapest A tolerance that permits poor fit can increase assembly trouble, rework and failure. The goal is a manufacturable design that still meets requirements."},
{"title":"Reduce material loss without shortening useful life","page":"Design for Manufacturing","summary":"Make the required function easier to produce, assemble, inspect and maintain.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/design-for-manufacturing.html#reduce-material-loss-without-shortening-useful-life","text":"Reduce material loss without shortening useful life Use Material Utilization and Cost to compare layouts. Also consider energy, durability, repair, disassembly and practical material recovery at end of life. A lighter part that fails early may consume more resources over its service life. Identify each part's function and critical features. Check standard components and stock sizes. Sketch manufacturing, assembly and inspection access. Check tolerances against function and process capability. Compare material use, service life and repair or recycling options."},
{"title":"Condition the wood for the environment where it will be used","page":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","summary":"Compare drying methods, prepare stock for its service environment and calculate moisture content on an oven-dry basis.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/drying-and-conditioning-wood.html#condition-the-wood-for-the-environment-where-it-will-be-used","text":"Condition the wood for the environment where it will be used Conditioning changes material properties. Drying removes moisture so wood can be prepared for a particular use. It does not permanently freeze the moisture content: dried wood still exchanges moisture with surrounding air. Review the wood-as-a-material topic for the underlying grain and movement behavior. Not every use requires dried wood, but closely fitted indoor furniture generally does. Choose a target moisture content appropriate to the expected indoor temperature and humidity, then check the actual stock before final machining and assembly. A mill's drying target and a suitable in-service range are not necessarily identical. “Kiln-dried” describes a process; it is not a measurement of what the board contains today. For indoor furniture in most of the United States, the USDA Wood Handbook recommends 8% average moisture content, with individual pieces at 6–10%, at installation. Regional guidance differs: the dry Southwest uses a 6% average (4–9% for individual pieces), while damp, warm coastal areas use an 11% average (8–13%). Use these as starting points and match the actual service environment. See Table 13–2 and Figure 13–1, page 13–5. Why the indoor moisture target depends on the building The handbook's interior recommendations were developed from well-ventilated buildings without unusual moisture sources or air conditioning. Air conditioning and other building conditions can change the appropriate target. These are installation guidelines, not a universal kiln-drying specification; see Recommended Moisture Content, page 13–3."},
{"title":"Air drying follows the weather; kiln drying controls the conditions","page":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","summary":"Compare drying methods, prepare stock for its service environment and calculate moisture content on an oven-dry basis.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/drying-and-conditioning-wood.html#air-drying-follows-the-weather-kiln-drying-controls-the-conditions","text":"Air drying follows the weather; kiln drying controls the conditions Two drying routes, often used in sequence Route How it works What it can and cannot do Air drying Boards are stacked with spacers called stickers so air can reach their faces, with support and protection from direct rain. Needs little purchased drying energy, but speed and final moisture depend on climate, species and thickness. Outdoor air drying may not reach the target for heated indoor furniture. Kiln drying A chamber controls temperature, humidity and airflow through a drying schedule. Can reach lower, more controlled moisture levels. An unsuitable schedule can cause cracks, internal damage, distortion or residual stress. Drying faster is not always drying better. The outside of a board may dry while the center remains wet. Suitable schedules manage this difference; elapsed time or a dry-feeling surface alone does not prove the board is ready. Radio-frequency and microwave systems both use electromagnetic energy for dielectric heating, but the terms are not synonyms. Industrial systems control frequency, power and moisture removal; this is a process overview, not a household-microwave procedure."},
{"title":"Acclimate stock with airflow and repeated measurements","page":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","summary":"Compare drying methods, prepare stock for its service environment and calculate moisture content on an oven-dry basis.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/drying-and-conditioning-wood.html#acclimate-stock-with-airflow-and-repeated-measurements","text":"Acclimate stock with airflow and repeated measurements Acclimating means giving lumber time to approach the conditions in the intended working or service environment. There is no reliable fixed number of days for every board. Thickness, initial moisture, species and humidity all affect the time required. Identify the intended service environment before choosing the moisture target. Support boards flat with aligned stickers and space for airflow. Check several boards and locations with a suitable moisture meter, using its species and temperature corrections where required. Record repeat measurements and look for stability, not just one favorable reading. Recheck after major material removal or a change of storage environment before final fitting. Meter depth and measurement method matter: a surface reading can miss a wetter core. Keep stock in suitable conditions after drying. Joint design still needs to allow movement; see Wood Joinery and Fasteners."},
{"title":"Calculate moisture content using oven-dry mass as the denominator","page":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","summary":"Compare drying methods, prepare stock for its service environment and calculate moisture content on an oven-dry basis.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/drying-and-conditioning-wood.html#calculate-moisture-content-using-oven-dry-mass-as-the-denominator","text":"Calculate moisture content using oven-dry mass as the denominator On the oven-dry basis used for wood, MC (%) = (initial mass − oven-dry mass) / oven-dry mass × 100. Both masses must refer to the same sample and use the same units. “Initial” means the sample's mass before the moisture determination; it need not be freshly cut green wood. For an illustrative sample with initial mass 230 g and oven-dry mass 200 g, the water removed is 230 − 200 = 30 g. Then MC = 30 / 200 × 100 = 15.0%. Why dividing by the initial mass answers a different question 30 / 230 × 100 = 13.0% gives the water fraction of the initial sample, not wood moisture content on the oven-dry basis. Wood MC can exceed 100% when water mass exceeds dry wood mass. The calculation does not set a suitable target for furniture. An actual oven-dry determination requires an appropriate laboratory method and equipment; these exercises provide the dry mass. Practice moisture content Practice rounding moisture content"},
{"title":"Distinguish temporary softening from permanent treatment","page":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","summary":"Compare drying methods, prepare stock for its service environment and calculate moisture content on an oven-dry basis.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/drying-and-conditioning-wood.html#distinguish-temporary-softening-from-permanent-treatment","text":"Distinguish temporary softening from permanent treatment Heat and moisture in steaming can soften wood enough for bending. The softening is conditioning; changing the shape is Forming. The part must be restrained as it dries and sets, and some springback can occur. Species, grain quality, thickness and bend radius govern success; there is no universal bending radius for all wood. Other treatments have specific purposes. Resin impregnation introduces resin into wood; impreg is resin-treated wood, while compreg also uses compression during manufacture. Increased dimensional stability or hardness can come with changes in other properties. Decorative color is not what defines compreg. Pressure treatment is a delivery method, not a promise of every kind of resistance. Preservative treatment and fire-retardant treatment use different systems for different purposes. Neither the word “treated” nor “pressure-treated” alone establishes water exclusion, chemical resistance or suitability for an application. Use the treatment identification and product specification. Wood–plastic composites made by combining wood particles or fibers with a polymer are a materials topic. Irradiation is not a universal requirement for making them. Finishing preparation is covered in Preparing and Finishing Wood; chemical handling belongs in Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Engineered wood changes the arrangement of the wood","page":"Engineered Wood Products","summary":"Compare veneers, strands, particles, fibers, and laminations to choose a wood product for its intended use.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/engineered-wood-products.html#engineered-wood-changes-the-arrangement-of-the-wood","text":"Engineered wood changes the arrangement of the wood Engineered products combine wood pieces with binders to make sheets or members that a single log could not supply as consistently. They belong to Wood & Composites: the wood supplies much of the structure, while the binder holds the selected arrangement together. Engineering redistributes natural variability; it does not make all products equally strong, waterproof, or free of manufacturing defects. Select the product grade, thickness, orientation, exposure suitability, and fastening details for the job."},
{"title":"Plywood cross-laminates veneers to restrain movement","page":"Engineered Wood Products","summary":"Compare veneers, strands, particles, fibers, and laminations to choose a wood product for its intended use.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/engineered-wood-products.html#plywood-cross-laminates-veneers-to-restrain-movement","text":"Plywood cross-laminates veneers to restrain movement Exploded five-ply plywood: veneer grain alternates lengthwise and crosswise; outer layers run the same way and the layup is symmetric about its middle ply. A veneer is a thin sheet of wood. In plywood, adjacent veneer layers commonly have grain at right angles. Each layer restrains the cross-grain movement of neighboring layers, making the panel more stable in its plane than a comparable wide solid-wood board. Conventional plywood commonly has an odd number of plies in a balanced layup, with matching face-grain directions. Odd ply count is not a universal rule for every panel construction. The grain direction of the faces still matters to panel stiffness and strength; cross-lamination does not mean equal performance in every direction. Plywood serves cabinet boxes, furniture panels, sheathing, and subfloors when the appropriate product is selected. Face appearance, core voids, bond durability, and panel rating vary. A decorative face veneer does not establish a structural rating."},
{"title":"Choose the sheet by its structure and service conditions","page":"Engineered Wood Products","summary":"Compare veneers, strands, particles, fibers, and laminations to choose a wood product for its intended use.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/engineered-wood-products.html#choose-the-sheet-by-its-structure-and-service-conditions","text":"Choose the sheet by its structure and service conditions Sheet goods: useful differences Product Structure and strengths Limits and typical uses OSB: oriented strand board Wood strands bonded in layers with deliberate orientation. Structural grades provide useful panel strength and stiffness. Common in wall, roof, and floor sheathing. Follow the marked strength direction and rating. Edges can swell when wetted; it is not a decorative substitute for every plywood face. MDF: medium-density fiberboard Fine wood fibers and resin produce a smooth surface without solid wood's visible grain pattern. Well suited to painted profiles. Common in interior cabinet doors and trim. Heavy; ordinary grades swell with moisture. Edge fastening needs suitable hardware and preparation. Moisture-resistant grades are not automatically waterproof. Particleboard Small wood particles bonded into an economical, comparatively uniform panel; useful as a substrate under a decorative surface. Common in interior furniture and laminate countertops. Ordinary grades are moisture-sensitive, edges are vulnerable, and fastener holding and shelf deflection need attention. Painting a moisture-sensitive board does not turn it into a product rated for prolonged wet exposure. For wood dust and product-handling hazards, use Personal Protective Equipment and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets rather than inferring precautions from a product's name. Practice: painted panel Practice: rated sheathing Practice: laminate substrate"},
{"title":"Glulam and LVL put wood along the member","page":"Engineered Wood Products","summary":"Compare veneers, strands, particles, fibers, and laminations to choose a wood product for its intended use.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/engineered-wood-products.html#glulam-and-lvl-put-wood-along-the-member","text":"Glulam and LVL put wood along the member Glulam bonds lumber laminations with their grain generally parallel to the member's length. It is used for structural beams and columns, including large members and curved forms. LVL (laminated veneer lumber) bonds thin veneers with grain predominantly along the member. It is part of the structural composite lumber group and is commonly used for beams and headers over openings. It is not simply thick plywood: its layup favors lengthwise performance. Use the manufacturer's design information for a particular structural product. Product names alone do not give allowable loads, spans, or permission to cut holes. Lamination and bending methods belong to the later wood forming and fabricating topics."},
{"title":"Further reading on wood properties and products","page":"Engineered Wood Products","summary":"Compare veneers, strands, particles, fibers, and laminations to choose a wood product for its intended use.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/engineered-wood-products.html#further-reading-on-wood-properties-and-products","text":"Further reading on wood properties and products Reference resources APA: oriented strand board APA: glulam USDA Forest Products Laboratory: Wood Handbook (2021), wood-based composites"},
{"title":"Match the fastener to the assembly and its service access","page":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","summary":"Choose mechanical joints for service access, thread compatibility and exposure to moisture and dissimilar metals.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fasteners-and-galvanic-corrosion.html#match-the-fastener-to-the-assembly-and-its-service-access","text":"Match the fastener to the assembly and its service access Common mechanical joining components Component Purpose Design check Bolt and nut Provide a removable threaded connection that can clamp parts together. Provide tool access, suitable thread engagement and a compatible strength and material specification. Washer Provides a bearing surface or distributes load where the design requires it. A plain washer does not by itself prevent loosening or electrically isolate an entire joint. Rivet Forms a retaining head through permanent deformation. Suitable for repeated sheet assembly; removal normally destroys the rivet. Blind rivets permit installation from one side. Threaded insert Provides an internal thread in a host part. Select an insert designed for the host material and thickness. Check installation access, pull-out and resistance to rotation. Standard fasteners often reduce dedicated tooling for short runs, but every fastener adds parts and assembly time. Riveting or automated fastening may suit long runs. Removability, inspection and repair can justify a threaded joint even when another method is faster."},
{"title":"A thread size alone does not specify a complete fastener","page":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","summary":"Choose mechanical joints for service access, thread compatibility and exposure to moisture and dissimilar metals.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fasteners-and-galvanic-corrosion.html#a-thread-size-alone-does-not-specify-a-complete-fastener","text":"A thread size alone does not specify a complete fastener The mating threads must agree in diameter, spacing and thread system. Similar-looking inch and metric parts are not interchangeable. Pitch describes axial spacing between adjacent thread forms; threads per inch describes a count over a unit length. A purchase or drawing specification also needs the relevant length, head style, material or strength grade, coating and thread-fit requirements. The wrench size is not the thread diameter. Use the applicable thread reference and supplier drawing to decode a designation before choosing mating parts. Read diameter and thread spacing separately Example Diameter Spacing and system 1/4-20 UNC Nominal thread diameter: 1/4 inch. 20 threads per inch; UNC is the Unified coarse series. The pitch is 1/20 inch, not 20 inches. M6 × 1 Nominal metric thread diameter: 6 mm. Pitch: 1 mm between corresponding points on adjacent threads. The 1 is not a count of threads per inch. These are thread-size examples, not complete fastener specifications. Nominal diameter identifies the size; tolerances govern the actual mating dimensions. A 1/4-20 thread and an M6 × 1 thread differ in both diameter and spacing. Sources for thread names and size examples McMaster-Carr explains inch diameter/TPI and metric diameter/pitch, including M6 × 1. Its UNC catalog identifies 1/4-20 as UNC. NASA's Fastener Design Manual (PDF), printed pages 12–13 / PDF pages 16–17, explains Unified coarse/fine series and thread classes. The manual is background on thread terminology, not a current procurement specification. Practice decoding a thread designation For dimensional fit concepts, see Tolerances and Fits. For a permanently joined alternative, see Joining Metal."},
{"title":"Galvanic corrosion needs a metal pair, electrical contact and an electrolyte","page":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","summary":"Choose mechanical joints for service access, thread compatibility and exposure to moisture and dissimilar metals.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fasteners-and-galvanic-corrosion.html#galvanic-corrosion-needs-a-metal-pair-electrical-contact-and-an-electrolyte","text":"Galvanic corrosion needs a metal pair, electrical contact and an electrolyte Dissimilar metals can form a corrosion cell when they are electrically connected and share a conductive liquid, called an electrolyte. Moisture containing salts can provide that liquid. The more active member of the pair acts as the anode and may corrode faster; the other acts as the cathode. A wet joint connects a more active metal to a more noble metal, completing a corrosion cell. A second sketch shows an insulating sleeve and washers separating a fastener from the surrounding plate. A galvanic series ranks metals by their behavior in a specified environment. It helps screen combinations; it does not predict a service life. Alloy condition, surface films, wetting and exposed areas matter. A dry pair is not automatically a working galvanic cell. Why the wetted areas matter A small active area coupled to a large noble area can suffer concentrated attack when both areas contact the electrolyte. That is why coating only the active member is not automatically a cure: a small defect in that coating can leave a small wetted anode coupled to a large cathode. The relevant areas are those participating in the wet corrosion cell, which can change as a joint dries. A compatibility decision must consider the complete joint and environment. Sources for galvanic contact, coatings and area effects ASSDA Technical FAQ 1 (PDF), pages 1–2, explains electrical contact, conductive liquid, coating defects and relative wetted areas. Hilti's Corrosion Handbook (PDF), printed pages 12 and 26–27, illustrates the cell, separation measures and environment-specific material pairing."},
{"title":"Review the complete joint before calling a fastener compatible","page":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","summary":"Choose mechanical joints for service access, thread compatibility and exposure to moisture and dissimilar metals.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fasteners-and-galvanic-corrosion.html#review-the-complete-joint-before-calling-a-fastener-compatible","text":"Review the complete joint before calling a fastener compatible “Stainless” does not mean compatible with every metal, and a coating is not a permanent guarantee of isolation. Consider material pairing, durable electrical separation, drainage and a suitable protective finish together. An insulating washer under a head does not isolate a shank that still touches the hole wall. Identify the base metal, fastener, washer and their coatings. Identify where electrical contact and moisture can occur. Check the material pairing for the actual service environment. Review sleeves, washers or gaskets for complete isolation and mechanical suitability. Provide drainage and an inspectable, maintainable finish. Compatibility review does not replace structural sizing or joint design. Practice spotting a corrosion risk"},
{"title":"Fiber direction changes elastic response","page":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","summary":"Predict how one unidirectional composite layer stretches and skews when its fibers turn relative to the same applied stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fiber-direction-and-stiffness.html#fiber-direction-changes-elastic-response","text":"Fiber direction changes elastic response A fiber-reinforced composite combines a reinforcement with a surrounding matrix. In a unidirectional layer, the reinforcement is continuous and parallel. The layer is much stiffer along the fibers than across them, so its elastic response depends on direction. Stress is force divided by loaded area. This lesson compares patches under the same uniform axial stress, so each square millimeter carries the same applied force. Strain is deformation divided by original dimension. Elastic stiffness connects stress to strain in the reversible small-strain model. The illustrative material uses E 1 = 40 GPa along the fibers, E 2 = 10 GPa across them, G 12 = 5 GPa, and ν 12 = 0.30. These round teaching values do not describe a named composite and are not design allowables. Review Plastics as Materials for reinforcement, matrix, thermosets, and thermoplastics. Review Plastic Forming Processes for hand layup and other forming routes."},
{"title":"Predict the least stretch, then turn the fibers","page":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","summary":"Predict how one unidirectional composite layer stretches and skews when its fibers turn relative to the same applied stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fiber-direction-and-stiffness.html#predict-the-least-stretch-then-turn-the-fibers","text":"Predict the least stretch, then turn the fibers Predict first: identical free material patches carry uniform axial stress σ x = 10 MPa, with no applied transverse or shear stress. Which orientation stretches least: 0°, 45°, or 90°? Same illustrative lamina and same 10 MPa stress Fiber angle Effective axial stiffness Axial strain Transverse strain Engineering shear strain 0° 40.00 GPa 0.0250% −0.0075% 0 µε +45° 12.90 GPa 0.0775% −0.0225% −375 µε −45° 12.90 GPa 0.0775% −0.0225% +375 µε 90° 10.00 GPa 0.1000% −0.0075% 0 µε Result: 0° stretches least because the stiff fiber direction is aligned with the load. Opposite 45° angles have the same axial stiffness and stretch, but their shear strain has opposite sign. This is a freely deforming material patch loaded by uniform edge traction. It is not a tensile-test coupon held in rigid grips: grips would restrain the off-axis skew and create another boundary-value problem."},
{"title":"Off-axis extension can also create shear strain","page":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","summary":"Predict how one unidirectional composite layer stretches and skews when its fibers turn relative to the same applied stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fiber-direction-and-stiffness.html#off-axis-extension-can-also-create-shear-strain","text":"Off-axis extension can also create shear strain At 0° and 90°, the loading direction matches a principal material direction. The patch stretches and narrows without extension–shear coupling. At an off-axis angle, an axial stress can also make the free patch skew. Turning +45° to −45° mirrors that skew while leaving the axial stiffness unchanged. The interactive drawing exaggerates every strain by the same factor of 120. A larger drawn change therefore means a larger calculated strain; the display does not secretly rescale each angle to fill the frame. How the axial stiffness is calculated Let m = cos θ and n = sin θ. For this plane-stress orthotropic layer, the compliance in the load direction is S̄ 11 = m⁴/E 1 + n⁴/E 2 + m²n²(1/G 12 − 2ν 12 /E 1 ). Then E x = 1/S̄ 11 and ε x = σ x /E x. The full stress and strain transformation supplies transverse and shear strain."},
{"title":"Stiffness does not predict strength","page":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","summary":"Predict how one unidirectional composite layer stretches and skews when its fibers turn relative to the same applied stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/fiber-direction-and-stiffness.html#stiffness-does-not-predict-strength","text":"Stiffness does not predict strength A smaller strain under the same stress means greater elastic stiffness. It does not say when the material fails, which direction is safest, or which laminate should be built. Strength needs failure data and criteria; a laminate also needs its complete stacking sequence, ply thicknesses, bonding, environment, loading, and boundary conditions. This lesson models one thin, continuous-fiber lamina in small-strain linear elasticity and plane stress. It omits failure, damage, buckling, bending, temperature, moisture, manufacturing defects, grips, and interaction between multiple plies. Primary mechanics source and model boundary NASA Reference Publication 1351, Basic Mechanics of Laminated Composite Plates, sections I and II, defines isotropic and orthotropic behavior, develops plane-stress compliance and off-axis transformation, and identifies extension–shear coupling. Its stated scope separates elastic response from strength. The constants and examples on this page are original illustrative teaching inputs, not values from that report. Practice axial strain Practice angle symmetry Practice model limits"},
{"title":"Choose the shape-making route before comparing production cost","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#choose-the-shape-making-route-before-comparing-production-cost","text":"Choose the shape-making route before comparing production cost Metal parts can begin as liquid poured into a mold, solid stock plastically deformed into a new shape, or powder pressed into a compact. Plastic deformation means a permanent shape change after the force is removed. Each route limits the shapes it can make and the tools it needs. A die is a tool that controls shape; a mold contains material while it takes shape. These shape-making routes sit under Forming in the classification used by Manufacturing Process Categories. In metalworking references, forming often means solid-metal deformation specifically, with casting listed separately. Cutting is Separating; joining is Fabricating; property-changing treatment is Conditioning; surface protection or appearance is Finishing. Metals are one of the four families in Material Families, alongside Plastics, Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass. Metal properties and heat treatment are separate topics. Identify the alloy, overall shape, holes and thin regions. State the quantity and whether the design will change. Check tool access and how the part will leave the mold or die. Compare tooling cost, repeatability and production rate. Allow for trimming, machining, joining and finishing afterward."},
{"title":"Casting makes a shape by solidifying metal inside a mold","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#casting-makes-a-shape-by-solidifying-metal-inside-a-mold","text":"Casting makes a shape by solidifying metal inside a mold Three casting routes and their trade-offs Route How it works Typical products and cost trade-off Sand casting A pattern makes a cavity in bonded sand. Remove the pattern, assemble the mold, pour metal, then break away the mold after solidification. Pump housings and large machine bases. Relatively flexible tooling can suit small batches; making a new mold for every part adds work. Investment casting A disposable pattern, often wax, receives a ceramic shell. Remove the pattern and fire the shell before pouring metal. Break away the shell afterward. Detailed brackets, valve parts and turbine components. Fine detail can reduce later machining, but pattern and shell preparation add time and cost. Pressure die casting Inject molten metal into a reusable metal die, let it solidify, then open the die and eject the part. Repeated aluminum or zinc housings. Expensive dies and equipment can pay off over long runs; alloy, size and geometry must suit the process. There is no universal quantity at which die casting becomes cheapest. Obtain estimates for the actual geometry, alloy, tool life and required secondary operations. Sources for the casting routes The Steel Founders' Society of America glossary defines sand-mold features. The Investment Casting Institute's process overview (PDF) follows wax pattern, ceramic shell, pattern removal and casting. NADCA's die-casting FAQ explains pressure injection into reusable steel dies and suitable alloys."},
{"title":"A casting needs room to release, shrink and feed","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#a-casting-needs-room-to-release-shrink-and-feed","text":"A casting needs room to release, shrink and feed Schematic sand mold with the cope above the drag, a sprue and runner feeding the cavity, a riser above it and a supported core forming an internal opening. The cope is the upper mold half; the drag is the lower half. The sprue leads downward from the pouring opening, and a runner and gate lead into the cavity. A riser supplies liquid metal as the casting solidifies and contracts. A core occupies space that must remain hollow. Draft is a slight taper that helps a pattern or casting leave its tooling. A shrinkage allowance changes tooling dimensions to account for contraction. Neither is a universal number: alloy, geometry and process matter. Critical faces or holes may need machining afterward. Why a larger pattern does not replace a riser Pattern allowance addresses final size. Feeding addresses a different problem: a solidifying region may need more liquid metal to avoid an internal shrinkage void. A riser must remain able to feed that region. The diagram identifies parts of a mold, not a production gating design. SFSA explains riser feeding and shrinkage voids."},
{"title":"Rolling squeezes, forging compresses and extrusion pushes through a die","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#rolling-squeezes-forging-compresses-and-extrusion-pushes-through-a-die","text":"Rolling squeezes, forging compresses and extrusion pushes through a die Side-by-side sketches: rolling reduces stock thickness between rolls; forging compresses stock between dies; extrusion pushes a billet through an opening into a long profile. Solid-metal forming routes Route Shape and examples Tooling and quantity Rolling Rotating rolls reduce thickness or produce a section. Sheet, plate and structural shapes are common products. Large equipment investment suits substantial throughput; a designer can often buy standard rolled stock. Open-die forging Dies compress a solid workpiece without enclosing its whole outline. Examples include large shafts and rings. Relatively simple dies accommodate varied sizes; repeated positioning and later machining may be needed. Closed-die forging Shaped die cavities guide metal flow into repeated parts such as connecting rods and wrenches. Dedicated tooling favors repeated production. Excess metal may form flash that is trimmed away. Extrusion A ram pushes a billet through a die opening. Long channels, rails and heat-sink profiles have a substantially constant cross-section. A dedicated profile die can serve many lengths. Cut ends, cross holes and local pockets require later operations. Wire drawing Pull wire through a reducing die to make it thinner and longer. This is different from pushing stock through an extrusion die. Die sets and handling equipment suit repeated lengths; several reductions may be needed. Hot working occurs at temperatures and deformation rates that allow recrystallization, the formation of new grains, during deformation. Cold working occurs where that process does not remove the work hardening as deformation proceeds. The boundary depends on the metal and processing conditions; it is not one temperature for every alloy. Hot rolling permits large shape changes, while cold rolling commonly improves dimensional control and surface quality and can increase strength through work hardening. Cold forming does not guarantee an unchanged dimension Cold forming generally requires greater force than comparable hot forming. It avoids a separate hot-working step, but that does not prove every production cycle is faster. Springback, residual stress and later processing can change dimensions. Forging can also be hot, warm or cold; heat is not part of its definition. Sources for solid-metal deformation The Forging Industry Association's Forging 101 (PDF) compares open- and closed-die processes and hot, warm and cold forging. Queen City Forging explains recrystallization and temperature classification. A Dartmouth-hosted metal-forming case study compares rolling, forging and drawing regimes. The Australian Aluminium Council's Extrusion Manual (PDF), page 10, explains flow through a die into a uniform section. Contenti's wire-drawing explanation (PDF) identifies pulling through progressively smaller openings; its operating steps are outside this selection overview."},
{"title":"Sheet forming changes shape; blanking and shearing separate material","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#sheet-forming-changes-shape-blanking-and-shearing-separate-material","text":"Sheet forming changes shape; blanking and shearing separate material Bending changes the angle of sheet, as in a bracket or enclosure. A press brake uses a punch and die to make bends; adaptable tooling suits prototypes and short runs. Roll forming progressively bends strip through roll stations into a long profile, such as a channel. Its dedicated roll tooling favors repeated lengths. It is different from rolling sheet thinner. Deep drawing pulls sheet from a flat blank into a die cavity to make a cup or shell. Tooling must control material flow to limit tearing and wrinkling. Dedicated tools suit repeat production. This is different from pulling wire through a reducing die. Stamping names a press-based production route that can combine several operations. Bending and drawing are Forming; shearing and blanking are Separating. In blanking, the cut-out piece is the wanted blank; in punching a hole, the removed slug is usually scrap. A stamping line can therefore perform more than one process category. Bend allowance accounts for the material length within a bend when developing a flat pattern. The sheet stretches on the outside and compresses on the inside. Use a validated bend table or supplier data for the alloy, thickness, radius and tooling; adding outside leg dimensions alone does not give the correct blank length. Practice selecting a sheet process Sources for sheet forming and flat patterns SSAB explains incremental roll forming and bending. 4Ming's forming handbook explains why low blank-holder pressure can allow wrinkling while excessive pressure can cause cracking. Mate defines blanking by the retained piece. SOLIDWORKS explains bend allowance, bend deduction and bend tables."},
{"title":"Powder metallurgy presses particles, then bonds them by sintering","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#powder-metallurgy-presses-particles-then-bonds-them-by-sintering","text":"Powder metallurgy presses particles, then bonds them by sintering In conventional press-and-sinter powder metallurgy, powder fills a die and is compressed into a green compact: a shaped part that is not yet fully bonded. Heating then bonds the particles during sintering, generally without melting the main metal throughout. Some systems include a liquid phase. Small gears and porous bearings are typical products. Dedicated dies and presses favor repeat quantities and can produce a near-net shape, close to the finished dimensions. Tool access, ejection and density variation limit geometry; critical features may still require sizing or machining. Porosity can be useful or unwanted Oil impregnation fills connected pores in a bearing so it can retain lubricant. Porite describes this use of porous sintered metal. That does not mean every powder-metal part is porous by design or that every part needs impregnation. MPIF explains conventional compaction, sintering and secondary operations. This section describes that route, rather than every process that begins with metal powder."},
{"title":"Plan the separating operations that finish the geometry","page":"Forming Metal","summary":"Compare casting, bulk deformation, sheet forming and powder metallurgy by shape, material, tooling and production quantity.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/forming-metal.html#plan-the-separating-operations-that-finish-the-geometry","text":"Plan the separating operations that finish the geometry Follow Milling Operations for machined faces and pockets, Lathe Operations for turned features, and Feeds and Speeds for cutting-data concepts. Use Tolerances and Fits to decide which dimensions require close control and Surface Texture and Finish to distinguish a surface requirement from a process name. Forming is often only the beginning of a route. A casting can be machined, joined and coated. These pages compare processes for design; operating procedures belong in Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Build the joint around sound, closely fitted glue surfaces","page":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","summary":"Match the adhesive to the fit and exposure, then prepare the surfaces and hold the assembly through the specified cure.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/gluing-and-clamping-wood.html#build-the-joint-around-sound-closely-fitted-glue-surfaces","text":"Build the joint around sound, closely fitted glue surfaces Glue needs clean, sound surfaces and a joint designed for the load. For ordinary PVA wood glue, prepare mating surfaces that contact closely without being forced into shape. Paint, finish, dust and old adhesive can prevent a reliable bond. Long grain is a surface running along the wood fibers; end grain exposes their cut ends. A long-grain edge joint can provide broad, continuous contact. End grain can absorb liquid adhesive rapidly and leave too little at the interface; a plain end-grain butt joint also has limited bonded area and unfavorable loading. End grain can bond, but adding more glue does not automatically make that joint equivalent to a fitted tenon or lap. Long-grain surfaces meet along parallel fibers with a continuous glue line. An end-grain butt connection exposes cut fiber ends that can absorb adhesive away from the interface. Fiber direction and adhesive coverage both matter. The diagram is a simplified comparison, not a strength ranking of every possible joint. What “stronger than the wood” actually demonstrates In a well-made joint tested under a particular load, failure may occur in the surrounding wood rather than at the bond line. That shows the bond outlasted that wood under those conditions. It does not show that every adhesive joint exceeds every strength of wood, or that an end-grain butt joint is as strong as an uncut member. Grain direction, stress type, moisture, temperature and time under load can change the result."},
{"title":"Choose the adhesive for fit, exposure and repair needs","page":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","summary":"Match the adhesive to the fit and exposure, then prepare the surfaces and hold the assembly through the specified cure.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/gluing-and-clamping-wood.html#choose-the-adhesive-for-fit-exposure-and-repair-needs","text":"Choose the adhesive for fit, exposure and repair needs Adhesive families: starting points, not product specifications Adhesive Useful for Fit and exposure limits PVA, including yellow wood glue Closely fitted wood-to-wood joints in furniture and cabinetry. Not a structural gap filler. Water resistance varies from interior formulations to products rated for specified wet exposure; color does not establish the rating. Polyurethane wood glue Moisture-curing bonds where the product lists the materials and exposure. Expanding foam is not sound replacement wood in a loose joint. Clamp to control expansion. Do not transfer one product's water rating to the whole family. Epoxy Some repairs, mixed-material joints and gaps using a suitable resin, hardener and structural filler. Mix the specified ratio. A fairing filler meant for easy sanding is not necessarily a structural adhesive filler. Water and temperature performance depend on the system and cure. Hide glue Traditional furniture and repairs where future disassembly matters. Hot hide glue and liquid hide glue have different working times. Close fit is important; heat and moisture can weaken the bond as well as aid repair. CA (cyanoacrylate) Small parts, small repairs and rapid positioning. Thin and gel grades suit different gaps. Short working time and often brittle bonds limit its use for large assemblies or impact loads; check the exact grade. Construction adhesive Building assemblies and dissimilar materials listed on the cartridge. A broad product category, not one chemistry. Bead size, gap limits, compatibility, support time and water resistance are product-specific. It is not automatically suitable for precision furniture joints. Hot-melt adhesive sets quickly as it cools and is useful for temporary positioning or packaging. Do not assume that quick set gives a durable furniture bond. Practice adhesive selection"},
{"title":"Separate working time, clamp time and full cure","page":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","summary":"Match the adhesive to the fit and exposure, then prepare the surfaces and hold the assembly through the specified cure.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/gluing-and-clamping-wood.html#separate-working-time-clamp-time-and-full-cure","text":"Separate working time, clamp time and full cure Open time is the period after spreading adhesive during which the surfaces may remain apart. Assembly time includes getting the joint together and into clamps; manufacturers may define open and total assembly time separately. Clamp time is how long the assembly must be restrained. Full cure is when the adhesive reaches its specified cured properties under the stated conditions. These are not interchangeable. A joint that can leave the clamps may still be unready for machining or service loads. Check the exact product's instructions for temperature, moisture, timing and exposure. A fixed “30 minutes for all yellow glue” rule is unreliable. PVA requires time for water loss and bond development; one-part polyurethane depends on moisture; epoxy working time depends on its resin/hardener system and temperature. Hot hide glue has a short working window as it cools. CA can fix parts very quickly, so trial assembly is especially important. Construction adhesive often needs continued support after initial grab. Where to find product-specific instructions Consult the current label and technical data sheet. Titebond's application FAQs and the WEST SYSTEM filler-selection guide are starting points for their own products, not interchangeable specifications for other brands. For chemical handling, use Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets and Personal Protective Equipment."},
{"title":"Use clamps to close the joint, not to repair a bad fit","page":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","summary":"Match the adhesive to the fit and exposure, then prepare the surfaces and hold the assembly through the specified cure.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/gluing-and-clamping-wood.html#use-clamps-to-close-the-joint-not-to-repair-a-bad-fit","text":"Use clamps to close the joint, not to repair a bad fit Apply enough pressure to seat the surfaces and maintain alignment, following the adhesive instructions. Excess force can dent the wood, bow a panel or force out too much adhesive. A gap that closes only under severe force is a fitting problem. Bar and pipe clamps span large assemblies; C-clamps apply local pressure; spring clamps suit light holding jobs. Cauls are straight blocks or beams used to distribute pressure and help keep faces aligned. Protect visible surfaces and prevent the cauls from becoming glued to the assembly. A small, reasonably even bead of squeeze-out can indicate coverage, but it does not prove the entire bond is sound. A dry spot may be hidden inside. Excess glue can seal the surrounding pores and leave a pale patch under stain. Remove it by a method appropriate to the adhesive and surface; do not smear it into a wider area. Inspect again before finishing. Dry-fit all parts and place the clamps and cauls within reach. Read the product's assembly, clamp and cure requirements. Spread adhesive as directed, assemble within its working time and close the joint. Check alignment, flatness and diagonals where a rectangular assembly must be square. Inspect squeeze-out and clean surrounding surfaces appropriately. Keep the joint restrained and unloaded for the specified intervals. Choose the geometry in Wood Joinery and Fasteners first; choose the surface treatment in Preparing and Finishing Wood after the assembly is ready."},
{"title":"Follow the grain through the face, edge, and end","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#follow-the-grain-through-the-face-edge-and-end","text":"Follow the grain through the face, edge, and end Grain describes the direction of wood cells. Figure is the visible pattern created by growth structure and the way the wood is cut. A board's broad surface is its face, its narrow long surface is its edge, and its cut end exposes end grain. Face and edge grain usually run generally along the board; they are not separate botanical structures. Wood is generally stronger in tension along the grain than across it. A short projection with grain running across its narrow base can split off even if the rest of the part is thick. Knots divert nearby grain, and sloping or interlocked grain makes a board less predictable. During machining, a cutting edge can lift fibers ahead of the cut and tear them out. Grain direction helps predict that result, but does not replace the machine's operating procedure. See Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Equipment Operating Procedure for safety; wood separating and finishing are later topics."},
{"title":"Sawing orientation changes figure and movement","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#sawing-orientation-changes-figure-and-movement","text":"Sawing orientation changes figure and movement Three selected boards in log cross-sections: plain-sawn tangent to rings, quarter-sawn radial, and rift-sawn oblique. Face sketches compare cathedral figure, straight grain with possible ray fleck, and straight grain. Read the growth rings on the end of a board. Plain-sawn boards have rings more nearly parallel to the broad face and often show cathedral-shaped face figure. Quarter-sawn boards have rings more nearly perpendicular to the face; their width follows the radial direction more closely. Rift-sawn boards have an intermediate, oblique ring angle and commonly show straight face grain. Quarter-sawn oak can expose conspicuous ray fleck. Rift-sawn oak is often selected for straight grain with less prominent fleck. Trade descriptions and angle boundaries vary, so inspect the end grain as well as the sales label. The diagram shows selected orientations, not a mill's complete cutting plan."},
{"title":"Moisture content uses oven-dry wood mass as its reference","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#moisture-content-uses-oven-dry-wood-mass-as-its-reference","text":"Moisture content uses oven-dry wood mass as its reference MC (%) = (wet mass − oven-dry mass) / oven-dry mass × 100. A sample weighing 150 g before drying and 100 g after oven drying has 50% MC. MC can exceed 100% because the reference is dry wood mass, not the original wet mass. Water occupies cell cavities and is also held in cell walls. At the fiber saturation point, the walls are saturated while the cavities contain no free liquid water. Below this region, loss of water from the cell walls causes appreciable shrinkage. It is not one exact threshold for every species and condition. Equilibrium moisture content (EMC) is the moisture content wood approaches in a particular surrounding humidity and temperature. A board can be dry today and absorb moisture later. Green Freshly cut or unseasoned; moisture varies greatly. Air-dried Dried by surrounding air; the achievable condition depends on the climate and storage. Kiln-dried Dried in a controlled chamber. This is a drying history, not a promise that the board will never move or that every kiln-dried product has the same MC. Choose and acclimate material for its intended service environment. Use measurements to judge readiness; a fixed number of days cannot guarantee equilibrium for every thickness and starting condition."},
{"title":"Most movement is across the grain","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#most-movement-is-across-the-grain","text":"Most movement is across the grain End-grain view labels tangential direction around a growth ring and radial direction toward the center; longitudinal direction is along the trunk, out of the view. Typical movement is greatest tangentially, then radially, and much smaller longitudinally. For normal straight-grained wood, shrinkage is usually greatest tangentially (along growth rings), smaller radially (across rings), and much smaller longitudinally (along the cells). Swelling reverses the moisture-loss trend. Actual amounts depend on species, grain orientation, and the moisture interval. Do not apply a green-to-oven-dry shrinkage percentage directly to a small seasonal humidity change. A movement estimate needs a species-specific coefficient, board orientation, starting MC, and expected service MC. Reaction wood and juvenile wood can also depart from ordinary longitudinal behavior. Estimate movement using starting dimension × coefficient × (final MC − starting MC), with both MC values within 6–14%. The attachment experiment below separates total width change from travel at a particular point. Wood movement and attachment clearance - both moisture contents must be 6&ndash;14%. Change = starting dimension &times; coefficient &times; (final MC &minus; starting MC), with MC entered as a percentage (8, not 0.08). Negative means shrinkage; positive means swelling. Use the tangential coefficient C T for the width of a flat-sawn board or when the ring orientation is unknown, and the radial coefficient C R for the width of a quarter-sawn board or the thickness of a flat-sawn one. Worked example: a flat-sawn white fir board 9.15 in wide at 8% MC, C T = 0.00245, reaching 11% MC: 9.15 &times; 0.00245 &times; (11 &minus; 8) = 0.0672525 in wider, about 9.2173 in final width. The exact conversion starts at 232.41 mm and ends at about 234.118 mm. The Handbook's separately rounded 232 mm example ends at about 233.705 mm. Predict: with one ideal fixed reference on a stationary support, does center fixing make the right edge move half as far, the same distance, or not at all, compared with fixing the left edge? Same total width change, different edge travel: 8% to 11% MC Fixed reference Left edge travel Right edge travel Center 0.0336 in outward 0.0336 in outward Left edge 0 (fixed) 0.0673 in outward For uniform free strain, signed point displacement = (signed distance from the fixed reference &divide; installed width) &times; total width change. Center fixing halves the edge travel, not the total width change. A point closer to the reference travels less. Compare available travel in each direction from the installed position. With 0.040 in outward travel, center-fixed edges remain inside the travel window, while the opposite edge of the left-edge-fixed board exceeds it. Drying reverses the movement: 8% to 6% MC gives 0.0224 in inward travel at each center-fixed edge, exceeding a separate 0.020 in inward allowance. Enough travel in one direction does not supply travel in the other. Each estimate starts from the installed dimension and MC. Edge points are geometric tracking points, not a fastening layout. The support is stationary, with one ideal fixed reference and otherwise free uniform width change; friction, warp, strength, restraint stress and cracking are not calculated. Exceeding a travel window does not predict what the restrained board will do. A result within the window does not approve a joint. Commercial lumber is rarely perfectly flat- or quarter-sawn, so this method can overestimate width shrinkage and underestimate thickness shrinkage. Board properties and ring angles vary. These are approximate estimates; no correction or safety factor is added. Outside 6&ndash;14% MC a different method applies. Moisture-movement source: USDA Wood Handbook (2021), chapter 13, Equation 13-3 and Table 13-5. The point-travel equation is derived geometry under the stated assumptions."},
{"title":"Name the distortion before deciding what to do","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#name-the-distortion-before-deciding-what-to-do","text":"Name the distortion before deciding what to do Defects described by shape Defect What you see Cup Curvature across the width: the face resembles a shallow trough. Bow Curvature along the length, out of the broad face's plane. Crook Curvature along the length within the face's plane; the edge wanders sideways. Twist The corners no longer lie in a common plane. Check / split A separation along the grain. A check may be confined to a surface or end; a split extends through the piece. Unequal shrinkage, uneven moisture, and irregular grain can contribute to warp. Drying stresses can open checks and splits. A stain is a color change, not automatically proof of lost strength; decay is a different concern. Practice: cup Practice: bow Practice: crook Practice: twist Practice: check"},
{"title":"Allow solid wood to change width","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#allow-solid-wood-to-change-width","text":"Allow solid wood to change width A solid-wood tabletop needs room to expand and contract across its grain. Suitable slotted attachments let it move relative to a supporting frame. A panel held in grooves needs clearance rather than rigid restraint across its full width. Details depend on the joint and expected movement. Choose the fixed reference deliberately, then check travel from each attachment's installed position in both moisture directions. Slot length alone does not give clear travel: the fastener occupies space, and its starting position divides the available movement. The experiment above uses clear travel directly rather than inferring a hardware size. Do not assume that a finish locks moisture out. It can slow moisture exchange, but does not eliminate seasonal movement. Plywood often solves a panel-stability problem better than simply making a solid board thicker. Identify the grain direction and width that can move. Check moisture against the intended service environment. Inspect for warp, splits, and grain interruptions before laying out parts. Check that attachments allow the expected movement. Wood conditioning and fabricating cover drying and joint-making in more detail; Engineered Wood Products compares alternatives to solid lumber. Practice: center-fixed travel Practice: directional clearance"},
{"title":"Further reading on wood properties and products","page":"Grain and Moisture Movement","summary":"Connect grain orientation and moisture changes to board movement, defects, and design choices.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/grain-and-moisture-movement.html#further-reading-on-wood-properties-and-products","text":"Further reading on wood properties and products Reference resources USDA Forest Products Laboratory: Wood Handbook (2021), moisture and physical properties USDA Forest Products Laboratory: Wood Handbook (2021), chapter 13, Drying and Control of Moisture Content and Dimensional Changes Oregon State Extension: Wood and Moisture Relationships"},
{"title":"Heat treatment is conditioning: change structure to change properties","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#heat-treatment-is-conditioning-change-structure-to-change-properties","text":"Heat treatment is conditioning: change structure to change properties Heat treatment uses controlled heating, holding and cooling to change a metal's internal structure. It belongs to Conditioning in Manufacturing Process Categories. It can change properties without intentionally changing the overall shape, although distortion can occur. The result depends on alloy, starting condition, section thickness and the complete cycle. Heating more or cooling faster is not automatically better. A treatment that strengthens one alloy can soften another. Steel microstructures are not the four steel families Ferrite and austenite are different iron-based crystal phases. Cementite is iron carbide, Fe₃C, a hard, brittle phase. Pearlite is a layered arrangement of ferrite and cementite. Martensite can form from austenite during sufficiently rapid cooling in suitable steel. These terms describe structure, not the buying categories carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steel. Carbon can diffuse during slower transformations. Quenching can suppress those transformations and produce martensite. The outcome is not determined by cooling speed alone: chemistry and section size affect what forms throughout the part."},
{"title":"Anneal to soften; normalize suitable steels to refine structure","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#anneal-to-soften-normalize-suitable-steels-to-refine-structure","text":"Anneal to soften; normalize suitable steels to refine structure Annealing is a family of treatments used to soften material, restore ductility or adjust its structure. Full annealing of suitable steels includes slow cooling; annealing cold-worked metal can form new grains and restore formability. Not every anneal uses the same temperature or cycle. Normalizing heats suitable steel into its austenitic range and then cools it in air. It can refine and make the structure more uniform after earlier processing. Air cooling and slow furnace cooling produce different results. Choose the process for the desired condition. Do not apply one universal temperature offset to all steels."},
{"title":"Quench hardening usually needs tempering afterward","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#quench-hardening-usually-needs-tempering-afterward","text":"Quench hardening usually needs tempering afterward Quench hardening heats suitable steel to form austenite, holds as required, then cools fast enough to obtain the intended hardened structure. It commonly raises hardness and strength but can reduce ductility and toughness. Cracking and distortion are possible. Tempering reheats hardened steel below the temperature at which austenite begins to form. It adjusts the balance of hardness and toughness and reduces some quenching stresses. It is not simply heating “just below” one critical temperature; the required cycle depends on grade and target properties. Qualitative temperature-time sketches: annealing has slow cooling; hardening has rapid quenching followed by a separate lower-temperature tempering cycle. No numerical times or temperatures are implied. Separate illustrative cycles, not a furnace program. Peak temperatures, holds and cooling rates are grade-specific. These concepts explain specifications; they are not operating instructions. Use the approved process for the identified material, equipment and quench medium. Practice the purpose of tempering"},
{"title":"Surface hardening puts wear resistance near the outside","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#surface-hardening-puts-wear-resistance-near-the-outside","text":"Surface hardening puts wear resistance near the outside A part may need a hard wear surface and a tougher interior. Carburizing adds carbon near the surface of suitable steel before hardening. Nitriding introduces nitrogen to develop a hard surface layer. These change surface chemistry. Induction hardening heats a surface region electrically and then quenches it. It uses the steel's existing composition rather than adding carbon. It therefore needs a suitable starting steel. Case depth, surface hardness and core properties must be specified together."},
{"title":"Cold work strengthens; stress relief addresses locked-in stresses","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#cold-work-strengthens-stress-relief-addresses-locked-in-stresses","text":"Cold work strengthens; stress relief addresses locked-in stresses Work hardening, also called strain hardening, occurs as metal is permanently deformed during cold working. It commonly raises strength and hardness while reducing remaining ductility. “Cold” here means below the relevant recrystallization range, not necessarily cold to the touch. Stress relief reduces residual stresses left by processing. These are stresses that remain without an external load. The goal is often dimensional stability during later machining or service. Stress relief is not automatically a full softening anneal and does not guarantee zero distortion. Practice restoring formability"},
{"title":"Aluminum strengthening does not use steel's martensite route","page":"Heat Treating Metals","summary":"Choose a conditioning process by its goal: soften, refine structure, harden, improve toughness or reduce residual stress.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/heat-treating-metals.html#aluminum-strengthening-does-not-use-steel-s-martensite-route","text":"Aluminum strengthening does not use steel's martensite route Some aluminum alloys can be strengthened by solution treatment, quenching and aging. Solution treatment puts selected elements into solid solution; subsequent aging develops small particles that obstruct deformation. Aging can occur naturally or through controlled heating, depending on the alloy and required condition. Solid-solution strengthening and precipitation strengthening are different mechanisms. Dissolved atoms provide the first; particles of another phase provide the second. Other aluminum alloys obtain much of their strength through cold work. Annealing can soften cold-worked material. An aluminum temper describes its processing condition. It is not synonymous with the steel process called tempering. Preserve suffixes such as -O and -T6 when reading or writing a material description; consult the relevant alloy-temper specification for their exact meanings. Further reading on conditioning processes Bodycote's heat-treatment overview provides entry points for annealing, hardening, tempering, stress relief and solution-and-age treatments. AEC's alloy and temper guide is a starting point for aluminum terminology. Neither an overview nor a qualitative sketch replaces a material-specific process specification."},
{"title":"Teeth remove chips; the kerf is the wood they remove","page":"How Saws Cut Wood","summary":"Match teeth to grain direction, leave room for chips, and include the saw's kerf in every cutting layout.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/how-saws-cut-wood.html#teeth-remove-chips-the-kerf-is-the-wood-they-remove","text":"Teeth remove chips; the kerf is the wood they remove Sawing is a Separating process: each tooth removes a small chip, and successive teeth make a slot. The slot's width is the kerf. The space ahead of a tooth, called the gullet, carries chips out of the cut. Many steel saw teeth have set: teeth bend slightly to alternating sides so the cut is wider than the blade body. Carbide-tipped circular blades commonly get that clearance from tips wider than the steel plate instead. Set and kerf are related, but they are not the same measurement. Front views of flat-top ripping teeth and alternating beveled crosscut teeth, plus a blade body narrower than the kerf it cuts. This page explains cutting concepts. For operation, use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Equipment Operating Procedure."},
{"title":"Rip along the grain; crosscut across the fibers","page":"How Saws Cut Wood","summary":"Match teeth to grain direction, leave room for chips, and include the saw's kerf in every cutting layout.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/how-saws-cut-wood.html#rip-along-the-grain-crosscut-across-the-fibers","text":"Rip along the grain; crosscut across the fibers A rip cut runs along the grain, usually to reduce a board's width. A crosscut runs across the grain, usually to reduce its length. Describe the cut relative to the grain, not just as a long or short cut. Ripping teeth commonly have a flat, chisel-like cutting edge. Crosscut teeth commonly have alternating bevels that score and sever fibers at the sides of the cut. These are useful patterns, not universal rules: combination and specialty blades use other arrangements. Why tooth geometry changes the surface Across the grain, fibers can break beyond the intended cut if they are pushed away before being severed. Beveled teeth help slice those fibers. Along the grain, chip removal is a major concern. A blade's tooth shape, spacing, sharpness and feed all affect the result; tooth count alone does not identify its job."},
{"title":"Balance tooth count, chip space and hook angle","page":"How Saws Cut Wood","summary":"Match teeth to grain direction, leave room for chips, and include the saw's kerf in every cutting layout.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/how-saws-cut-wood.html#balance-tooth-count-chip-space-and-hook-angle","text":"Balance tooth count, chip space and hook angle For circular blades of the same diameter, more teeth generally share the work into smaller bites at the same feed and rotational speed. That can improve a crosscut surface, but leaves less space for chips. Thick ripping cuts often benefit from fewer teeth and larger gullets. “Thicker wood needs more teeth” is not a general rule. Band and reciprocating blades commonly specify teeth per inch (TPI). Tooth spacing must suit the thickness engaged in the cut. A coarse blade has more chip space; a finer blade can suit thinner stock and finer work. Blade width also matters on a band saw: a narrower blade can follow a tighter curve. Hook angle describes the tooth face's lean relative to a radial line on a circular blade. More positive hook generally gives a more aggressive bite; low or negative hook is less aggressive. Machine type and the blade maker's intended application matter along with the desired finish. A ripping blade is not automatically appropriate on a miter saw. See Feeds and Speeds for the relationship between tooth engagement, feed and cutting speed; metal-cutting settings do not transfer directly to wood. Blade terminology reference Ridge Carbide Tool's saw-blade design glossary defines gullet, kerf and hook angle; its top-grind guide compares tooth shapes. Choose a blade specified for the machine and cutting application."},
{"title":"Count cuts before calculating strip yield","page":"How Saws Cut Wood","summary":"Match teeth to grain direction, leave room for chips, and include the saw's kerf in every cutting layout.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/how-saws-cut-wood.html#count-cuts-before-calculating-strip-yield","text":"Count cuts before calculating strip yield Start with the usable width after any edge preparation. Let W be usable board width, s the required strip width and k the kerf, all in the same units. These ideal layouts assume straight cuts, exact dimensions and no extra finishing allowance. One cut per strip, leaving an offcut: used width = n(s + k). The maximum whole number is floor(W / (s + k)); “floor” means round down. For a 12 in board, 3 in strips and a 1/8 in kerf, floor(12 / 3.125) = 3 strips. Three cuts use 9.375 in, leaving 2.625 in. Four strips alone use 12 in, leaving no room for the cuts. All final pieces use both original outside edges: W = ns + (n − 1)k for an exact partition. There are only n − 1 cuts between n pieces. If a layout leaves an extra offcut to separate, count that extra cut too. Draw the layout before choosing a formula. Real layouts also allow for defects, edge trimming and later machining. Kerf is material removed, not useful strip width. Practice strip yield Practice cuts between pieces Practice the remaining width"},
{"title":"Choose a joint around its load, access and need for disassembly","page":"Joining Metal","summary":"Compare welding, brazing, soldering and mechanical assembly by joint access, heat input, production needs and service requirements.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-metal.html#choose-a-joint-around-its-load-access-and-need-for-disassembly","text":"Choose a joint around its load, access and need for disassembly Joining parts is Fabricating. First identify the metals, thicknesses, loading, service environment and whether the assembly must come apart. Then check access for the joining equipment and for inspection. A welded frame can avoid a large casting tool, but fit-up, fixtures and inspection still cost money. A bolt-on cover allows service access, but needs clearance for the fasteners and tools. See Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion for mechanical assembly. This is a process-selection overview. For safety requirements, use Personal Protective Equipment, Fire Safety, Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets and Equipment Operating Procedure."},
{"title":"Welding joins the base metal; melting is not required in every process","page":"Joining Metal","summary":"Compare welding, brazing, soldering and mechanical assembly by joint access, heat input, production needs and service requirements.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-metal.html#welding-joins-the-base-metal-melting-is-not-required-in-every-process","text":"Welding joins the base metal; melting is not required in every process Fusion welding melts base metal at the joint, with or without added filler metal. The pool solidifies to form the joint. The common arc, oxy-fuel and resistance spot processes below are fusion processes. Solid-state welding forms a bond without bulk melting of the base metals. Friction welding is one example. A solid-state process can still heat and alter nearby metal; “not melted” does not mean “unchanged.” The heat-affected zone is nearby base metal whose structure or properties change because of the thermal cycle, without itself becoming weld metal. Selecting a process includes checking those changes and distortion, not merely whether a bead looks smooth. Sources for fusion welding, solid-state welding and the heat-affected zone TWI describes arc fusion welding and solid-state welding without melting the base metal. Its heat-affected-zone explanation identifies thermal changes in unmelted base metal."},
{"title":"Compare the heat source, filler and access of five welding processes","page":"Joining Metal","summary":"Compare welding, brazing, soldering and mechanical assembly by joint access, heat input, production needs and service requirements.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-metal.html#compare-the-heat-source-filler-and-access-of-five-welding-processes","text":"Compare the heat source, filler and access of five welding processes Comparison of oxy-fuel, stick, MIG, TIG and resistance spot welding by heat source, filler supply and typical application. Starting candidates for process selection Process How it works and where it fits Trade-off Oxy-fuel welding A fuel-and-oxygen flame heats the joint; a separate filler rod may be added. Oxyacetylene can suit selected thin-steel work and repair. Flexible equipment, but slower and with more broadly distributed heating than many production arc processes. SMAW / stick An arc melts a flux-coated consumable rod. The coating supplies protection for the weld pool. Common for steel repair and construction work. Portable equipment and flexibility; rods need replacement and slag needs removal between appropriate stages. GMAW (MIG/MAG) An arc melts continuously fed wire under external shielding gas. Common for repeated steel or aluminum fabrication with suitable wire, gas and settings. Continuous wire supports production and automation; shielding and equipment access must suit the job. GTAW / TIG An arc from a non-consumable tungsten electrode heats the joint under inert gas. Filler, when needed, is supplied separately. Useful for controlled joints in thin stainless steel and aluminum. Good control of heat and filler, but often slower than wire-fed production welding. Resistance spot welding Opposed electrodes press overlapping sheets together and pass current through them, forming a local fused nugget. Common in repeated sheet assemblies. Rapid repeat joints with suitable equipment and fixtures; conventional arrangements require access to both sides. Separate spots do not make a sealed seam. Why MIG is a familiar name but GMAW is the broader term MIG refers to metal inert gas welding. GMAW also covers processes using active shielding gases or mixtures, often called MAG. Gas and filler selection depend on the alloy and process; the familiar shop name is not a complete welding specification. Sources for the five process mechanisms TWI compares SMAW and GTAW arc processes and explains MIG/MAG wire feed and shielding, oxy-fuel fusion welding, and resistance spot welding. These are process explanations, not approved settings or operating procedures for a particular job. Practice choosing a joining method"},
{"title":"Brazing and soldering melt filler while the base parts stay solid","page":"Joining Metal","summary":"Compare welding, brazing, soldering and mechanical assembly by joint access, heat input, production needs and service requirements.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-metal.html#brazing-and-soldering-melt-filler-while-the-base-parts-stay-solid","text":"Brazing and soldering melt filler while the base parts stay solid In brazing and soldering, a molten filler wets the solid base parts. In a suitable close-fitting joint, capillary action draws filler into the gap. The usual dividing temperature is 450°C: the American Welding Society describes brazing fillers as having a liquidus above 450°C and soldering fillers below it. Liquidus is the temperature above which the filler is fully liquid. This distinction concerns the filler, not the flame temperature or the machine's setting. In both processes the base parts remain solid. Neither is simply a cooler version of fusion welding. Brazing can join suitable dissimilar metals or attach a carbide tip to a steel tool body. Soldering is common in electrical connections. Choose filler and joint design for compatibility, temperature and loads; a soldered electrical connection is not automatically suitable for a structural load. Joint clearance, surface condition and heating method matter. Brazing can be manual or performed in production equipment such as a furnace. Mechanization changes throughput and tooling costs; the process name alone does not identify the cheapest route. Sources for the filler-temperature distinction and capillary joint The AWS Brazing and Soldering Manufacturers Committee defines the liquidus distinction and explains wetting and applications. TWI's vacuum-brazing overview describes filler flow by capillary action while the parent materials stay below their solidus, the temperature at which melting begins."},
{"title":"Specify the required joint, not just the name of the machine","page":"Joining Metal","summary":"Compare welding, brazing, soldering and mechanical assembly by joint access, heat input, production needs and service requirements.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-metal.html#specify-the-required-joint-not-just-the-name-of-the-machine","text":"Specify the required joint, not just the name of the machine Identify both base materials, thicknesses and any coatings. Describe the joint location, overlap or preparation and equipment access. State load, leak-tightness and appearance requirements. Allow for distortion, inspection and any later machining. Coordinate the finish and any assembly sequence constraints. A drawing and its referenced specification communicate the required weld and acceptance criteria. A process abbreviation alone does not specify weld size, extent or quality. Likewise, a visually neat joint does not establish internal soundness. Heat-treatment selection is a separate topic."},
{"title":"Start with both plastics and what the joint must do","page":"Joining Plastics","summary":"Match solvent cement, adhesive, welding or mechanical joints to the plastic pair, load and need for disassembly.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-plastics.html#start-with-both-plastics-and-what-the-joint-must-do","text":"Start with both plastics and what the joint must do Fabricating joins pieces into a useful assembly. Name both materials, then state the load, service temperature, appearance and whether the assembly must come apart. A leak-tight seam, a removable cover and a clear display corner need different joints. Joining decision: identify both materials and requirements; choose mechanical joining for disassembly, evaluate welding for compatible thermoplastics, otherwise evaluate a validated cement or adhesive; test the selected joint. These routes identify candidates. The actual grade, joint shape and service conditions decide whether a candidate works. Identify the two resins and any coating or surface contamination. State the load direction, environment and need for disassembly or sealing. Check the joining product or process guidance for that material pair. Make a representative trial joint and inspect it after full cure or cooling."},
{"title":"Solvent cement softens the surfaces; adhesive adds a bond layer","page":"Joining Plastics","summary":"Match solvent cement, adhesive, welding or mechanical joints to the plastic pair, load and need for disassembly.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-plastics.html#solvent-cement-softens-the-surfaces-adhesive-adds-a-bond-layer","text":"Solvent cement softens the surfaces; adhesive adds a bond layer Solvent cementing uses a compatible liquid to soften or dissolve the surfaces so polymer chains can mix across the joint. As solvent leaves, the joint develops strength. It is chemical softening, not heat melting. Acrylic-to-acrylic is a common application; PVC and ABS also have specific cement systems. The cement must match the resin and fit of the joint. Adhesives bond through a separate layer. Epoxies, cyanoacrylates and other formulations have different gap, flexibility and temperature limits. “Plastic glue” does not mean it bonds every plastic. Allow the specified cure time before loading; a joint that holds itself together may not have reached service strength. Polyethylene (PE) and polypropylene (PP) resist many ordinary adhesives. Their low surface energy makes it difficult for an adhesive to spread into intimate contact, called wetting. Specialized adhesives or a controlled surface treatment can help; sanding alone is not a universal fix. See Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets for chemical handling. Why a clean-looking bond can peel off The bond is only as good as the material it touches. Mold-release residue, oil or an unsuitable coating can become the weak layer. Peel loads concentrate force at an edge; an overlapping joint can distribute load more effectively, although its dimensions still need validation. 3M explains low surface energy, and its bonding guidance shows why PE and PP are difficult, rather than impossible, to bond."},
{"title":"Welding needs compatible thermoplastics and a designed joint","page":"Joining Plastics","summary":"Match solvent cement, adhesive, welding or mechanical joints to the plastic pair, load and need for disassembly.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-plastics.html#welding-needs-compatible-thermoplastics-and-a-designed-joint","text":"Welding needs compatible thermoplastics and a designed joint Plastic welding heats the joint region so compatible thermoplastics can fuse. Cooling while the joint is held in position completes the connection. Cured thermosets do not remelt into a comparable fusion weld. Even two thermoplastics are not automatically weld-compatible. Method How the joint forms Typical reason to choose it Hot-gas welding A controlled stream of heated gas softens the joint faces and compatible filler rod. Accessible seams or repairs in weldable sheet. Extrusion welding A tool supplies molten compatible filler while the base surfaces are preheated. Larger seams needing more deposited material than a small rod weld. Hot-plate welding A heated tool softens mating faces; the tool withdraws and the faces are pressed together. Parts with accessible matching joint faces. Hot-wire or resistance-element welding Electrical resistance heats a wire or element at a designed joint region. Tool arrangements differ. A localized joint designed for that heating arrangement; not the foam-cutting process. Ultrasonic welding A horn applies rapid mechanical vibration under force, generating heat at the joint. Repeated assembly of compatible molded parts with a suitable joint geometry. Heat sealing Heat and pressure fuse compatible film surfaces or their sealant layers. Bag and packaging seams; not every multilayer film seals to itself. Why heat alone does not guarantee a strong or sealed weld Temperature, force, time and joint fit work together. Too little heating leaves an unfused interface; excessive heating can degrade the polymer. Ultrasonic joints often use a small molded ridge, an energy director, to concentrate heating. A continuous-looking bead does not prove a leak-tight seam. Leister's extrusion-welding guidance identifies matching filler and joint geometry; Dukane's Guide to Ultrasonic Plastics Assembly explains energy directors for ultrasonic welding."},
{"title":"Hot-melt adhesive is not a fusion weld","page":"Joining Plastics","summary":"Match solvent cement, adhesive, welding or mechanical joints to the plastic pair, load and need for disassembly.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-plastics.html#hot-melt-adhesive-is-not-a-fusion-weld","text":"Hot-melt adhesive is not a fusion weld A hot-melt adhesive is applied molten and usually gains handling strength as it cools. The adhesive melts; the two parts do not have to melt together. Choose the formulation for both surfaces, the available assembly time and the service temperature. An ordinary hot-melt joint may soften or creep under sustained load in warmth. Fast setting is not the same as suitability for structural service."},
{"title":"Use mechanical joints when access and disassembly matter","page":"Joining Plastics","summary":"Match solvent cement, adhesive, welding or mechanical joints to the plastic pair, load and need for disassembly.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/joining-plastics.html#use-mechanical-joints-when-access-and-disassembly-matter","text":"Use mechanical joints when access and disassembly matter A snap fit bends a feature during assembly so it catches behind another feature. It needs room to flex, rounded transitions and a resin that tolerates the required strain. Some snap fits release repeatedly; others are intentionally difficult to undo. Thread-forming screws for plastics create their mating thread in a designed hole. Repeated removal can wear plastic threads. A threaded insert supplies a metal thread; a heat-set insert is installed by locally softening a thermoplastic around it. Hole size, surrounding wall thickness and installation conditions come from the insert supplier. A metal insert cannot rescue an undersized plastic wall. Through-bolts and suitable washers can spread the clamping load. Excessive tightening can crack brittle plastic; sustained load can cause creep, a gradual change in shape, and reduce clamping force. Allow for movement and thermal expansion rather than trapping a sheet tightly at every hole. Compare two cases: a clear permanent acrylic corner is a candidate for compatible solvent cement; a PP cover that must be removed regularly is a candidate for screws and a suitable thread arrangement. An adhesive that works on the first pair tells you little about the second. Insert dimensions come from the chosen insert system SPIROL's hole-design guidance explains how oversized holes reduce performance and undersized holes can introduce stress or cracks. Its insert design guide distinguishes heat-installed thermoplastic inserts from other installation methods. Practice choosing a removable joint"},
{"title":"Compare equivalent acceptable parts over the same period","page":"Make or Buy","summary":"Compare relevant costs, capacity, quality, delivery and capabilities worth retaining.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/make-or-buy.html#compare-equivalent-acceptable-parts-over-the-same-period","text":"Compare equivalent acceptable parts over the same period Make means produce internally; buy means purchase from a supplier. Define quantity, specifications and delivery date before comparing options. Use Manufacturing Cost and Volume for an initial cost comparison. Include avoidable setup, tooling, material and processing for make; purchase price, freight, incoming checks and expected rework for buy. Compare costs that change with the decision. Shared rent that remains after outsourcing is not automatically a cash saving."},
{"title":"A cheaper quote can still be the wrong choice","page":"Make or Buy","summary":"Compare relevant costs, capacity, quality, delivery and capabilities worth retaining.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/make-or-buy.html#a-cheaper-quote-can-still-be-the-wrong-choice","text":"A cheaper quote can still be the wrong choice Factor Question Capacity Is machine and staff time available? What other useful work would be displaced? Quality Can each route meet the specification consistently, and how will it be verified? Lead time Can the route deliver when needed, including queues and transport? Core competence Is this capability central to what the organization does well and needs to retain? Risk What happens if demand changes, a supplier fails or the design changes? Standard parts often favor buying; distinctive capabilities may favor making. Neither is a rule based on volume alone."},
{"title":"Check assumptions before committing resources","page":"Make or Buy","summary":"Compare relevant costs, capacity, quality, delivery and capabilities worth retaining.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/make-or-buy.html#check-assumptions-before-committing-resources","text":"Check assumptions before committing resources Define matching requirements, quantity and timing. List relevant costs and identify charges already included in rates. Check capacity, quality evidence, lead time and supplier dependence. Repeat the comparison at lower and higher plausible demand. Record what would change the decision. Review Design for Manufacturing before treating a costly design as fixed."},
{"title":"A printed pattern defines the cavity in the silicone mold","page":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","summary":"Plan a one-part mold, document the product-specific mixing and curing conditions, and inspect a repeatable batch of castings.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/making-silicone-molds-from-a-printed-pattern.html#a-printed-pattern-defines-the-cavity-in-the-silicone-mold","text":"A printed pattern defines the cavity in the silicone mold A pattern is the original shape copied by a mold. For a simple one-part, open-face mold, use a shape with a flat back and features that can be withdrawn through the opening. An original example is a small decorative relief tile with a flat back, gently sloping sides and no hooked details. Draft is a slight taper that helps a part leave the mold. Let the shape widen toward the opening. A flat parting face, where the pattern meets the mold-box base, defines the open back of this mold. Avoid undercuts that hook behind the silicone; flexibility does not make every trapped shape removable. Cross-section sequence: printed pattern with flat back on the box base; silicone cured around it; mold inverted and pattern removed; casting material fills the open cavity. The cavity widens toward its opening. The mold copies the pattern's surface. Smooth and inspect the print before making the mold. Remove unwanted layer ridges and support marks without losing important dimensions. Choose pattern preparation, any sealer and any release agent for the specific print, silicone and casting material. Follow the manufacturers' compatibility instructions and test a small prepared sample before committing the full mold."},
{"title":"Read the technical data sheet before choosing a mix or cure schedule","page":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","summary":"Plan a one-part mold, document the product-specific mixing and curing conditions, and inspect a repeatable batch of castings.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/making-silicone-molds-from-a-printed-pattern.html#read-the-technical-data-sheet-before-choosing-a-mix-or-cure-schedule","text":"Read the technical data sheet before choosing a mix or cure schedule The technical data sheet (TDS) supplies processing instructions. The safety data sheet (SDS) supplies handling information; use Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets for that guidance. Neither a generic lesson nor a familiar-looking container replaces the instructions for the actual product. Record the silicone product, batch identification and technical-sheet revision. Record the A:B ratio and whether it is by mass (weight) or by volume. Record working time, mixing procedure, bubble-removal requirements and cure conditions. Check compatibility with the printed pattern, coatings, release agent and intended casting material. Complete a small compatibility trial and inspect its cure before the full pour. Working time is the available time to mix and place the material under stated conditions. It is not the demolding time. Cure instructions may distinguish demolding from full properties or further treatment. Record the actual temperature and times; do not substitute a universal overnight wait. Where to find product-specific instructions Start with the manufacturer's product documentation, such as Smooth-On's technical bulletin and SDS directory. Its printed-pattern compatibility FAQ is a further-reading lead when investigating an uncured surface against a resin print. Check the complete instructions for the particular materials before selecting a treatment."},
{"title":"A ratio by weight is not automatically a ratio by volume","page":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","summary":"Plan a one-part mold, document the product-specific mixing and curing conditions, and inspect a repeatable batch of castings.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/making-silicone-molds-from-a-printed-pattern.html#a-ratio-by-weight-is-not-automatically-a-ratio-by-volume","text":"A ratio by weight is not automatically a ratio by volume For a specified A:B = a:b by weight and total mixed mass M: A = M × a / (a + b) B = M × b / (a + b) Arithmetic example only: suppose the stated ratio is 10:1 by weight and the required total is 220 g. There are 11 ratio parts, so A = 220 × 10/11 = 200 g, and B = 220 × 1/11 = 20 g. The total is 220 g. If instead you already have 220 g of A, B would be 22 g and the total 242 g. Read what the given mass represents. Tare the container so its mass is excluded. Weigh with a scale suitable for the quantities. For a volume ratio, measure volumes as directed. Different component densities mean equal masses need not occupy equal volumes. Use only the ratio basis provided for that product. Practice a total-mass mix Practice a given-A mix"},
{"title":"Document pattern preparation, pouring and cure as separate operations","page":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","summary":"Plan a one-part mold, document the product-specific mixing and curing conditions, and inspect a repeatable batch of castings.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/making-silicone-molds-from-a-printed-pattern.html#document-pattern-preparation-pouring-and-cure-as-separate-operations","text":"Document pattern preparation, pouring and cure as separate operations Prepare the pattern and box. Inspect the print, complete the qualified surface treatment, secure its flat back to the box base, and check that the box contains the liquid. Set wall and backing thickness from the mold material and geometry requirements. Measure and mix. Record actual A and B quantities, the ratio basis and the start time. Follow the product's mixing instructions, including its directions for reaching material at the container walls and bottom. Reduce trapped air. Follow the product's bubble-removal and pouring instructions. A controlled pour into a low point can let the material rise around the pattern instead of repeatedly trapping air over its details. Vacuum degassing is product- and equipment-dependent; do not assume every silicone requires it or has enough working time for it. Cure and remove the pattern. Record temperature, elapsed time and the specified demolding condition. Inspect the mold surface and details after removal before using the cavity. Make a first casting. Follow the casting material's own mixing, release and cure instructions. Its ratio and timing need not match the silicone's. Inspect the first piece before repeating the batch. Use Operation Method Sheets to give each operation equipment, settings, timing and a pass/fail check. Record what actually happened, including a changed pour method or a delayed demold, rather than copying the planned values into the result column."},
{"title":"Inspect each casting for voids, dimensions and consistency","page":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","summary":"Plan a one-part mold, document the product-specific mixing and curing conditions, and inspect a repeatable batch of castings.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/making-silicone-molds-from-a-printed-pattern.html#inspect-each-casting-for-voids-dimensions-and-consistency","text":"Inspect each casting for voids, dimensions and consistency A void is an unintended empty region. Inspect visible faces, edges and small details for pits, missing material and incomplete filling. A good exterior does not prove that the interior has no voids; choose further inspection according to the part's function. Define batch checks before casting Check What to document Shape and dimensions Drawing limits, measurement method and actual readings for critical features. Visible surface Which faces matter and what counts as a reject. For a teaching relief tile, an example criterion is no visible pits or missing raised detail on the display face. Cure and release Product-specific acceptance condition; any tacky region, tearing or distortion observed on removal. Batch consistency Casting number, material batch, measured charge, mixing and cure times, appearance, dimensions, rejects and rework. If a defect repeats at the same position, inspect the corresponding mold feature. If it varies between pours, compare the recorded process conditions. These are starting points for investigation, not proof of a cause. Change one factor, make a trial, and retain the results before revising the approved method."},
{"title":"Count material, processing, quality and overhead costs once","page":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","summary":"Estimate unit cost and find where two production methods cost the same.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-cost-and-volume.html#count-material-processing-quality-and-overhead-costs-once","text":"Count material, processing, quality and overhead costs once Material, labor, machine time, tooling and scrap all affect the cost of an acceptable part. Setup prepares an operation; cycle time is the time for a repeating operation. Material: stock purchase, delivery and material lost during production. Processing: labor, machine time, tools, consumables and energy. Quality: prevention, inspection, rework, rejects and returns. Overhead: shared support such as facilities and indirect labor. Check what a machine rate already includes before adding labor, energy or depreciation. Scrap belongs in the estimate once, even if it appears under both material and quality headings."},
{"title":"Spread fixed cost over the quantity actually needed","page":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","summary":"Estimate unit cost and find where two production methods cost the same.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-cost-and-volume.html#spread-fixed-cost-over-the-quantity-actually-needed","text":"Spread fixed cost over the quantity actually needed For a simple batch model, C = F + vQ and u = C/Q = F/Q + v. C is total cost, F is fixed batch cost, v is variable cost per part, Q is the number of acceptable parts, and u is average unit cost. Use Q greater than zero. Fixed cost stays constant within the stated production range; variable cost grows with quantity. Setup and dedicated tooling may be fixed for a batch. Material and processing per part often contribute to v. State the period and cost boundary before classifying a cost. Illustrative process A costs $120 + $8Q. At 60 parts, total cost is $600 and unit cost is $10.00/part. Producing unwanted parts to spread fixed cost creates inventory; it does not create demand. Why a shorter cycle does not reduce every cost equally A 10% reduction in a time-based processing charge reduces that portion by 10%. Material, setup and rent do not automatically fall. Capacity limits, tool replacement, scrap and quantity discounts can change the model outside its assumed range. Practice unit cost"},
{"title":"Set total costs equal to find process break-even","page":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","summary":"Estimate unit cost and find where two production methods cost the same.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-cost-and-volume.html#set-total-costs-equal-to-find-process-break-even","text":"Set total costs equal to find process break-even Compare routes that meet the same requirements at the same quantity. From F_A + v_A Q = F_B + v_B Q, obtain Q* = (F_B − F_A)/(v_A − v_B). Let A cost $120 + $8Q and B cost $720 + $3Q. Then Q* = ($720 − $120)/($8 − $3) = 120 parts. Both cost $1,080 total or $9/part. A costs less below 120; B costs less above 120. Unit cost curves cross at 120 parts and 9 dollars per part. A costs less below the crossing and B above it. When there is no positive crossing Equal variable costs mean the lower fixed cost wins, or both routes tie everywhere if fixed costs also match. A negative crossing lies outside positive production quantities. For a fractional crossing, compare costs at the neighboring whole-part quantities. This is process-cost break-even, not sales-revenue profit break-even. Practice break-even"},
{"title":"Move the volume slider and predict which process wins","page":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","summary":"Estimate unit cost and find where two production methods cost the same.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-cost-and-volume.html#move-the-volume-slider-and-predict-which-process-wins","text":"Move the volume slider and predict which process wins Predict the cheaper process at 60 and 240 parts. Fixed costs are spread over different quantities while variable cost per part stays constant. Quantity (parts) At 120 parts, A and B both cost $1,080 total and $9.00/part. Static comparison: at 60 parts A is $10.00/part and B is $15.00/part; at 240 parts A is $8.50/part and B is $6.00/part. Use Dimensional Analysis to check units and Make or Buy to consider more than cost."},
{"title":"Five categories describe the change an operation makes","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#five-categories-describe-the-change-an-operation-makes","text":"Five categories describe the change an operation makes Name the operation's intended result. Are we removing material, creating shape, joining parts, changing internal properties or treating a surface? Five process categories: separating removes material; forming creates shape; fabricating joins parts; conditioning changes internal properties; finishing treats a surface. These are five kinds of change, not five steps that every product must follow in order. This collection uses the course's five-category model. In this model, forming includes casting, molding and additive manufacturing such as 3D printing. Other technical references may separate those into their own process groups."},
{"title":"Separating removes material to make a size, shape or opening","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#separating-removes-material-to-make-a-size-shape-or-opening","text":"Separating removes material to make a size, shape or opening Separating cuts material apart or removes it from a workpiece, the piece being worked on. Look for a cut edge, hole, slot or reduced dimension as the intended result. Sawing: cut a wooden board or plastic rod to length. Drilling: remove material to make a hole in a metal bracket or wooden panel. Milling: a rotating cutter removes material to make a flat face, pocket or slot. Laser cutting: remove material along a narrow path to cut a suitable sheet material. For metal-cutting examples, continue to Milling Operations and Lathe Operations. The tool motions differ, but both remove material. A laser cut does not have to vaporize all the removed material Material may melt and be expelled from the cut; vaporization can also contribute. The classification depends on removal, not on which thermal mechanism dominates. TWI explains melt removal in laser cutting."},
{"title":"Forming creates shape without using material removal as the shaping method","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#forming-creates-shape-without-using-material-removal-as-the-shaping-method","text":"Forming creates shape without using material removal as the shaping method Forming gives material a shape by filling a mold, deforming it or building it up. The central action creates the shape rather than cutting unwanted material away. Casting and molding: pour metal into a mold, mold a plastic cap, or cast a concrete planter. Bending: bend sheet metal into a bracket or bend prepared wood around a form. Forging: press or hammer metal into a new shape. 3D printing: build a plastic part in layers. Forming can still produce excess material. Trimming a molded edge or cutting away printed supports is a separate separating operation. “Forming” does not promise zero waste."},
{"title":"Fabricating joins parts into an assembly","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#fabricating-joins-parts-into-an-assembly","text":"Fabricating joins parts into an assembly Fabricating brings separate parts together so they work as one assembly. Ask whether the intended result is a joint. Welding: join metal frame members or suitable plastic parts. Adhesive bonding: glue wooden strips together or bond a panel to a frame. Fasteners: use screws, bolts or rivets to hold parts together. Joinery: assemble matching wooden joint features, such as a mortise and tenon. Cutting the joint features is separating; fitting and securing the parts is fabricating. In industry, “fabrication” can describe a whole production job. Here it has the narrower meaning joining."},
{"title":"Conditioning changes internal properties","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#conditioning-changes-internal-properties","text":"Conditioning changes internal properties Conditioning changes how the material behaves through its structure, moisture content or chemical state. A new outline or a decorative coating is not its main goal. Heat treating: use a controlled heating and cooling cycle to change properties of a suitable metal. Drying or seasoning wood: reduce moisture content before later processing or service. Curing: allow a resin's chemical reaction to develop its intended properties. Annealing: use a controlled thermal cycle to relieve stress or change structure, as appropriate to the metal, plastic or glass. Conditioning can also change dimensions. Drying wood may shrink; heat treatment may distort a part. “Changes properties” does not mean “leaves every dimension unchanged.”"},
{"title":"Finishing treats the surface for protection or appearance","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#finishing-treats-the-surface-for-protection-or-appearance","text":"Finishing treats the surface for protection or appearance Finishing changes surface condition, appearance or protection. It may add a layer, remove a little material or alter the surface itself. Painting: coat a wooden panel or metal housing. Plating: deposit a metal layer on a part. Staining: change wood's color; stain alone does not necessarily provide a protective film. Polishing: improve the surface of metal, suitable plastic or glass. A surface location alone does not decide the category. Surface hardening primarily changes material properties, so it belongs under conditioning in this model."},
{"title":"For overlapping processes, state the purpose and pick the primary category","page":"Manufacturing Process Categories","summary":"Classify an operation by its main purpose: separating, forming, fabricating, conditioning or finishing.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/manufacturing-process-categories.html#for-overlapping-processes-state-the-purpose-and-pick-the-primary-category","text":"For overlapping processes, state the purpose and pick the primary category Identify one operation. Break a long manufacturing description into separate actions. State its acceptance goal. What result tells you this operation is complete? Choose the category that best matches that goal. Name any secondary effect in the explanation. Sanding Sanding a block down to a specified thickness is primarily separating. Sanding out visible scratches before a clear coat is primarily finishing, although both remove material. Bent lamination Bonding thin wood layers around a curved form combines fabricating and forming. If the stated goal is the curve, call forming primary; if the question focuses on the bond between layers, call fabricating primary. Resin and adhesive curing Curing a resin part to develop properties is conditioning. Bonding two parts with that resin is primarily fabricating; curing is the property-changing step that enables the joint. Welding A weld can change nearby material properties, but a step intended to join two frame members is primarily fabricating. If the description only says “sanding” or “curing,” explain what purpose you are assuming. A defensible classification includes the reason. Practice an overlapping case Open classification practice Return to the six-stage material-unit map to see how these categories organize the collection."},
{"title":"Use four material families as the starting point","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#use-four-material-families-as-the-starting-point","text":"Use four material families as the starting point The course uses exactly four families: Metals; Plastics (polymers); Wood & Composites; Ceramics & Glass. A family helps you predict useful behavior. It does not specify a material well enough to manufacture a part. Classify the material in the named part, not the entire product. A kettle can have a metal body, a plastic handle and a glass window. Why another book may show a different family list Other books may give composites a separate family, split glass from ceramics, or organize materials by bonding and structure. This collection keeps the instructor's four-family model. Engineered composites belong within Wood & Composites, including composites with no wood in them. Some materials fit more than one description. In the course's examples, plain concrete sits with Ceramics & Glass; steel-reinforced concrete illustrates Wood & Composites when the combined material system is the subject. Concrete itself also has a composite structure. State what is being classified rather than treating these labels as mutually exclusive scientific definitions."},
{"title":"Metals combine useful strength, stiffness and conductivity","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#metals-combine-useful-strength-stiffness-and-conductivity","text":"Metals combine useful strength, stiffness and conductivity Metals include metallic elements and alloys, mixtures designed for useful properties. Steel, aluminum and copper are everyday examples. Many metals conduct heat and electricity well and can deform permanently without breaking. Many are strong and stiff compared with unreinforced plastics. Density varies: aluminum is much less dense than steel, so “metal” does not always mean the heaviest choice. Typical matches include copper wire for electrical conduction, aluminum cookware for heat transfer, and steel fasteners for carrying loads. Corrosion behavior depends on the alloy and environment. Rust specifically concerns iron-containing materials; other metals can corrode without rusting. Metals may be cast, formed, cut, joined, heat treated and coated. Those process choices depend on the specific alloy and condition. Detailed metal processing belongs in the later metals topics; Milling Operations and Lathe Operations show separating operations."},
{"title":"Plastics are usually light, moldable and poor conductors","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#plastics-are-usually-light-moldable-and-poor-conductors","text":"Plastics are usually light, moldable and poor conductors Plastics (polymers) are materials based on long molecular chains, usually with additives to adjust their behavior. Polyethylene containers, acrylic display panels and nylon gears belong here. Common unfilled plastics have relatively low density and low thermal and electrical conductivity. They can range from flexible to rigid, and from tough to brittle. Heat, sunlight and chemicals affect different grades differently. Thermoplastics can soften on heating and be reshaped under suitable conditions. Thermosets develop a chemically linked structure during curing and cannot simply be remelted into a new shape. “Light” does not mean “floats.” Solid acrylic is denser than water. A hollow product may float because its overall density includes the trapped air. A supplier value that checks the floating claim Plaskolite's OPTIX acrylic data lists a relative density of 1.19, with water as the comparison. This supports the conclusion that a solid piece is denser than water. Family descriptions are screening guides; use the actual grade's data for a design."},
{"title":"Wood & Composites depend on grain, layers and reinforcement","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#wood-composites-depend-on-grain-layers-and-reinforcement","text":"Wood & Composites depend on grain, layers and reinforcement Wood has a cellular structure and grain. It is often light for its useful stiffness, relatively easy to cut and join, and a poor heat conductor. Strength and movement depend on direction and moisture content. A composite combines materials so they work together. Examples include plywood, wood-fiber board, glass-fiber-reinforced plastic and carbon-fiber-reinforced plastic. The material surrounding and holding reinforcement is the matrix; the reinforcement helps carry load. Do not assume every composite behaves like wood or like unfilled plastic. Fiber direction, layer arrangement, binder and moisture exposure affect the result. A composite can offer useful strength or stiffness for its mass while still being difficult to cut, join or repair. Plywood shelves and glass-fiber-reinforced boat panels belong to the same course family, but need different processing choices. Later wood topics develop grain and moisture behavior."},
{"title":"Ceramics & Glass often resist heat and wear but can fracture abruptly","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#ceramics-glass-often-resist-heat-and-wear-but-can-fracture-abruptly","text":"Ceramics & Glass often resist heat and wear but can fracture abruptly Ceramics & Glass include fired clay products, porcelain, window glass and engineering ceramics such as alumina. Many are hard, resist chemical attack and carry compression well. Many also have little ability to deform permanently before fracture. A tile can resist scratching yet break when struck. Hardness is not toughness. Many familiar ceramics and glasses are electrical insulators, but the family contains exceptions; thermal conductivity also varies widely. Shaping may occur before firing, while hot, or through suitable cutting and grinding afterward. Glass can be formed while hot and annealed to reduce residual stress. Finished ceramic parts are often harder to machine than common metals or plastics. Hard to machine does not mean impossible to machine Corning's MACOR glass-ceramic is specifically designed for machining with conventional metalworking tools. That is a useful counterexample to a blanket claim that ceramics cannot be worked after forming."},
{"title":"Compare typical behavior, then check the specific material","page":"Material Families","summary":"Compare exactly four families: Metals, Plastics (polymers), Wood & Composites, and Ceramics & Glass.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-families.html#compare-typical-behavior-then-check-the-specific-material","text":"Compare typical behavior, then check the specific material Qualitative comparison of four families: metals tend to be stiff conductors; plastics light insulators; wood and composites depend on grain and reinforcement; ceramics and glass often hard and brittle. Each family has important exceptions. Common tendencies, not design values or a ranking from best to worst. Compare materials at the same temperature, condition and loading direction. Use Material Properties to put a precise name to the requirement. Then select a grade, species or composite construction and check its data. No family wins on every property. Practice a composite classification Open material-family practice"},
{"title":"State the requirement before choosing the material","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#state-the-requirement-before-choosing-the-material","text":"State the requirement before choosing the material “Strong,” “light” and “durable” are starting points. A useful comparison says what load, temperature, environment and lifetime the part must handle. Mechanical properties describe behavior under force. Physical properties describe features such as density and heat or electrical behavior. Chemical properties describe interaction with the environment. Cost and workability determine whether a suitable material can become a practical product. Start with the four material families, then compare actual materials within them. Processing and condition matter: a material name alone does not tell you its final properties."},
{"title":"Strength resists failure; stiffness limits elastic deformation","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#strength-resists-failure-stiffness-limits-elastic-deformation","text":"Strength resists failure; stiffness limits elastic deformation Strength describes the stress a material can withstand before a specified limit, such as permanent deformation or fracture. Stress relates force to the area carrying it. A thicker part may carry more load without being made of a stronger material. Tensile strength Resistance to a pulling load. Think of a tie rod being pulled along its length. Compressive strength Resistance to a pushing or crushing load. Think of a block squeezed between two surfaces. A material good in compression may be much less capable in tension. Stiffness Resistance to elastic deformation, the change that recovers when the load is removed. A shelf that sags too far may be strong enough to avoid failure but insufficiently stiff. A part's stiffness depends on both its material and its geometry. Compare similarly shaped specimens when comparing materials; do not infer material stiffness by comparing a thin wire with a thick bar. Stress, strain and modulus give the comparison units For a simple axial test, engineering stress is force divided by original cross-sectional area. Engineering strain is change in length divided by original length. Stress has units such as MPa; strain is a ratio with no unit. In a linear elastic region, the slope of stress against strain is Young's modulus, a measure of material stiffness. Yield strength concerns permanent deformation; ultimate tensile strength is the highest engineering tensile stress reached in the test. A nonlinear curve does not, by itself, prove that a material has no yield behavior. See Measurement Language for writing quantities and units consistently."},
{"title":"Hardness, toughness, brittleness and ductility answer different questions","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#hardness-toughness-brittleness-and-ductility-answer-different-questions","text":"Hardness, toughness, brittleness and ductility answer different questions Hardness Resistance to local indentation or scratching, depending on the test. It can help resist wear, but hardness alone does not determine wear performance. Toughness Ability to absorb energy before fracturing. A part that must survive a blow needs more than a hard surface. Brittleness A tendency to fracture with little permanent deformation. Glass may resist scratching but crack instead of bending permanently. Ductility Ability to deform permanently in tension before fracture. A ductile metal can be drawn into wire. Elastic stretch that disappears when unloaded is not the same as ductility. Do not treat these as synonyms. A hard ceramic tile can be brittle; a ductile metal can bend before it breaks. Toughness depends on both the stress sustained and the deformation before fracture, as well as test conditions. Why a single “toughness number” needs its test method The area under a tensile stress–strain curve through fracture measures energy absorbed per original volume in that test. Impact tests and fracture-toughness tests describe related but different behavior. Temperature, loading rate and flaws can change the result, so compare like tests."},
{"title":"Density and thermal behavior decide weight and temperature limits","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#density-and-thermal-behavior-decide-weight-and-temperature-limits","text":"Density and thermal behavior decide weight and temperature limits Density Mass per unit volume. At equal volume, lower density gives lower mass. A large hollow metal part can weigh less than a small solid plastic part, so compare the whole design too. Melting point or melting range The temperature or range where a material melts. It helps choose processing conditions, but it is not the allowable service temperature: a loaded part may lose useful stiffness or strength earlier. Thermal conductivity How readily heat travels through a material. A heat spreader benefits from high conductivity; an insulating handle usually benefits from low conductivity. Electrical conductivity How readily electric current passes through a material. A wire needs a conductor; its surrounding insulation needs low conductivity under the intended conditions. Many common metals conduct heat and electricity well. Many unfilled plastics, dry woods and familiar ceramics are poor electrical conductors. Additives, moisture and temperature can change those tendencies. Not every material has one useful melting-point number Pure crystalline substances can have a sharp melting point; many alloys melt over a range. Glass and amorphous thermoplastics soften over a range rather than showing a sharp crystalline melting transition. Thermosets and wood decompose rather than melting into a reusable processing liquid. Use material-specific processing and service data."},
{"title":"Corrosion resistance is resistance to a particular environment","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#corrosion-resistance-is-resistance-to-a-particular-environment","text":"Corrosion resistance is resistance to a particular environment Corrosion resistance is the ability to resist chemical or electrochemical deterioration in service. A metal that works well indoors may perform differently in salt water or against another metal. Rust is one form of corrosion involving iron. Aluminum and stainless steel can resist many exposures, but neither is immune to every environment. Coatings can protect a surface, and their condition matters. For plastics, wood and composites, also consider chemical attack, sunlight, moisture and decay as appropriate. “Does not rust” is not the same as “will last outdoors.” Name the exposure before selecting a family or grade."},
{"title":"Cost and workability belong in the same comparison","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#cost-and-workability-belong-in-the-same-comparison","text":"Cost and workability belong in the same comparison Workability means how readily a material can be processed into an acceptable part. It is specific to the operation: a material that molds well may be difficult to bond, and one that cuts readily may be unsuitable for bending. Cost includes more than price per mass. Consider how much material the design needs, available supply forms, tooling, processing time, waste, inspection and any protective finish. A family profile narrows the search: common metals often offer stiffness and conductivity; plastics often offer low mass and moldability; wood and composites offer useful directional performance; ceramics and glass often offer hardness and chemical resistance. The particular material decides whether those benefits meet the job. Use Manufacturing Process Categories to describe the required operations. Detailed manufacturing economics and design for manufacturing extend the cost comparison."},
{"title":"Choose a candidate, give a reason, and name what still needs checking","page":"Material Properties","summary":"Distinguish strength, stiffness, hardness and toughness, then compare physical and chemical behavior, cost and workability.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-properties.html#choose-a-candidate-give-a-reason-and-name-what-still-needs-checking","text":"Choose a candidate, give a reason, and name what still needs checking Write the required function and service conditions. Name the properties that control success. Screen the four families, then identify a specific candidate material. Check direction, temperature, moisture, processing condition and data source. Check workability and total cost, then verify the finished part against its requirements. For a heat-spreading plate, Metals is a sensible starting family because common choices such as aluminum and copper conduct heat well. The next checks include temperature, dimensions, corrosion exposure and cost. That is a justified candidate, not a complete design specification. Practice selecting a family Open requirements practice"},
{"title":"Prove the parts fit before dividing areas","page":"Material Utilization and Cost","summary":"Check a stock layout, account for cutting loss and roll up material costs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-utilization-and-cost.html#prove-the-parts-fit-before-dividing-areas","text":"Prove the parts fit before dividing areas Stock is material purchased for further work. Nesting arranges part outlines on stock to reduce waste. An area ratio cannot prove a layout fits. Check dimensions, orientation, grain direction where relevant, edge margins and spacing. Kerf is the width removed by a cut. An offcut is leftover stock; a usable offcut may be saved. Include trim cuts and holding allowances before promising a part count. Illustrative layout: a 1,000 mm bar supplies 98 mm blanks. Each blank requires one 2 mm separating cut, including the last blank, with no end trim. Ten blanks consume 10 × (98 + 2) = 1,000 mm. A different last-cut arrangement would need a different cut count. Practice kerf allowance"},
{"title":"Use a consistent physical basis for utilization","page":"Material Utilization and Cost","summary":"Check a stock layout, account for cutting loss and roll up material costs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-utilization-and-cost.html#use-a-consistent-physical-basis-for-utilization","text":"Use a consistent physical basis for utilization Utilization = material in acceptable parts / material purchased × 100%. Compare length for the same cross-section, area for the same thickness, or mass on a consistent basis. The bar example gives 980/1,000 × 100 = 98.0%. The remaining 2% is kerf. Distinguish reusable offcuts from material discarded. Why 20% loss means a 25% increase per useful amount If 80% of purchased material reaches the product, one useful unit needs 1/0.80 = 1.25 purchased units, not 1.20. State whether a waste percentage is based on purchased material or on the useful amount. Practice utilization"},
{"title":"Charge each part its share of stock and waste","page":"Material Utilization and Cost","summary":"Check a stock layout, account for cutting loss and roll up material costs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-utilization-and-cost.html#charge-each-part-its-share-of-stock-and-waste","text":"Charge each part its share of stock and waste Part material cost = share of stock charged to the part × stock price. A $48 sheet yielding eight identical acceptable blanks gives $48 × 1/8 = $6.00 per blank when the whole sheet is charged to those blanks. This includes their share of kerf and unusable offcuts. Finished-part area divided by stock area can understate cost by leaving waste unassigned. State any credit for reusable remnants. Add delivery only if it is not already in the stock price. Practice stock cost"},
{"title":"Multiply quantity by unit cost for each BOM line","page":"Material Utilization and Cost","summary":"Check a stock layout, account for cutting loss and roll up material costs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/material-utilization-and-cost.html#multiply-quantity-by-unit-cost-for-each-bom-line","text":"Multiply quantity by unit cost for each BOM line A bill of materials (BOM) lists the components and quantities needed for one product. Add quantity × unit cost for every line. Illustrative material-only BOM Item Quantity Unit cost Line cost Blank 2 $6.00 $12.00 Standard fastener 4 $0.25 $1.00 Material total $13.00 This is not finished product cost. Add processing, assembly and other applicable costs using Manufacturing Cost and Volume."},
{"title":"Stock is the material shape you buy before making the part","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#stock-is-the-material-shape-you-buy-before-making-the-part","text":"Stock is the material shape you buy before making the part Stock is material supplied in a usable starting form. Choose a shape that leaves enough material for the finished part and any cutting or machining allowance. Buying the smallest possible shape can fail if saw cuts, surface cleanup or distortion consume the allowance. Cross-sections of round, flat, square and hex bar, round and square tube, angle, channel and I-beam. Solid bar has no central opening; tube has a wall around an opening. Cross-sections show shape only. Dimensions and wall thickness still belong on the order."},
{"title":"Sheet, plate, bar and structural shapes use different size descriptions","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#sheet-plate-bar-and-structural-shapes-use-different-size-descriptions","text":"Sheet, plate, bar and structural shapes use different size descriptions What to specify for common forms Form Description Size information Sheet and plate Flat products; plate is the thicker category under the applicable product convention. Thickness, width and length. There is no single sheet/plate boundary for every metal and specification. Bar Solid round, flat, square or hexagonal cross-section. Diameter, width and thickness, square side, or hex size across flats, plus length. Structural shapes Angle, channel and I-shaped sections place material where the structure needs it. Section designation and dimensions, thicknesses and length under the relevant product system. A gauge number is a sizing convention, not a universal thickness. Specify actual thickness and units when ambiguity would affect the part. Check the applicable material's gauge table if a drawing uses gauge."},
{"title":"Tube dimensions and nominal pipe sizes are not interchangeable","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#tube-dimensions-and-nominal-pipe-sizes-are-not-interchangeable","text":"Tube dimensions and nominal pipe sizes are not interchangeable Tube is commonly ordered by outside dimensions and wall thickness: outside diameter for round tube, or outside width and height for rectangular tube. State the length too. Pipe commonly uses a nominal pipe size and a wall schedule. A nominal size is a label; it need not equal the measured outside or inside diameter. Use the applicable pipe dimensions table before calculating a fit or flow area. Structural tubing is not automatically suitable for pressure service. Measure the right boundary. For round hollow stock, inside diameter equals outside diameter minus twice the wall thickness. The wall occurs on both sides of the opening."},
{"title":"Hot-rolled and cold-finished describe processing, not alloy identity","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#hot-rolled-and-cold-finished-describe-processing-not-alloy-identity","text":"Hot-rolled and cold-finished describe processing, not alloy identity Hot-rolled steel is shaped at high temperature and commonly arrives with mill scale and comparatively broad dimensional tolerances. Cold rolling or other cold-finishing operations can improve surface finish and dimensional control. Cold-finished bar may be drawn rather than rolled. Cold work can increase strength and leave residual stresses. Removing material can release those stresses and move the part. Cold-finished does not automatically mean precision ground, and neither term guarantees a particular tolerance without a product specification. Why an attractive surface can still be the wrong purchase A shiny bar can have the wrong alloy, condition, straightness or size allowance. Conversely, inexpensive hot-rolled stock can be a sound choice when later machining removes the scale and establishes the final dimensions. Compare total processing cost, not appearance alone."},
{"title":"A purchase description must preserve alloy, condition, form and size","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#a-purchase-description-must-preserve-alloy-condition-form-and-size","text":"A purchase description must preserve alloy, condition, form and size An illustrative request is: 6061-T6 aluminum round bar, 25 mm nominal diameter × 500 mm cut length, quantity 2. Before purchasing, add the governing product specification, diameter and cut-length tolerances, finish and certification requirements that the design needs. This example is not a complete engineering specification. Identify the alloy and required delivery condition from the design. State the stock form and every necessary dimension with units. Allow for saw kerf (material lost in the cut), cleanup and final machining. State tolerances, quantity and any required material certificate. Compare availability, cut charges, shipping and minimum orders as well as material price. Confirm that the delivered label and documents match the order before using the stock. Drops or remnants are leftover pieces that may reduce cost. Their identification and usable dimensions still need checking."},
{"title":"Estimate mass from cross-sectional area, length and density","page":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","summary":"Recognize stock shapes, distinguish size conventions, and specify enough information to receive the metal a design requires.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metal-stock-forms.html#estimate-mass-from-cross-sectional-area-length-and-density","text":"Estimate mass from cross-sectional area, length and density For uniform stock, mass = cross-sectional area × length × density. For a solid round bar, area = πd²/4. For round tube, area = π(D² − d²)/4, where D is outside diameter and d is inside diameter. Subtract the opening before multiplying by length. Use the density for the actual alloy from a material data sheet and keep units consistent. A result in kilograms is mass; weight as a force also requires gravitational acceleration. See Geometry, Mass, and Force and Dimensional Analysis. Practice bar mass with a supplied density Practice tube volume"},
{"title":"Metal selection starts with the job the part must do","page":"Metals and Their Properties","summary":"How bonding, grains and alloy condition affect the properties used to choose a metal.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metals-and-their-properties.html#metal-selection-starts-with-the-job-the-part-must-do","text":"Metal selection starts with the job the part must do A metal can carry a load, conduct current, transfer heat or resist wear. Those are different jobs. Start with the required behavior, then compare a particular alloy in a particular condition. An alloy combines a base metal with other elements to change its behavior. Metals belong to the material-family foundations topic. Use Material Properties for the general property vocabulary; this page applies it to metals. Continue with Types of Steel, Nonferrous Metals, Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing and Heat Treating Metals."},
{"title":"Metallic bonding helps explain conduction and forming","page":"Metals and Their Properties","summary":"How bonding, grains and alloy condition affect the properties used to choose a metal.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metals-and-their-properties.html#metallic-bonding-helps-explain-conduction-and-forming","text":"Metallic bonding helps explain conduction and forming In a metal, some electrons are shared throughout the structure rather than tied to one bond between two atoms. Their mobility helps metals conduct electricity and heat. Metallic bonding can remain intact while atoms rearrange during permanent deformation, which helps explain why many metals can be rolled or drawn. Most engineering metals contain many tiny crystals called grains. Atoms have an ordered arrangement inside each grain; adjacent grains have different orientations. Grain size, alloy chemistry and processing history all influence properties. Why two pieces of the same alloy can behave differently Permanent deformation involves movement of crystal defects called dislocations. Grain boundaries, dissolved alloying atoms and small particles can obstruct that movement. Cold working and heat treatment change these obstacles. Composition alone therefore cannot tell you the strength of a finished piece."},
{"title":"Strength, hardness, ductility and toughness answer different questions","page":"Metals and Their Properties","summary":"How bonding, grains and alloy condition affect the properties used to choose a metal.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metals-and-their-properties.html#strength-hardness-ductility-and-toughness-answer-different-questions","text":"Strength, hardness, ductility and toughness answer different questions Mechanical properties used when comparing metals Property Question it answers Selection example Strength How much stress can the material withstand? Yield strength concerns the start of permanent deformation; tensile strength concerns the maximum stress in a tensile test. A loaded bracket must resist permanent bending. Hardness How strongly does the surface resist local indentation? A wear surface may need hardness, but hardness alone does not predict every kind of wear. Ductility How much permanent deformation can occur before fracture? A formed part needs enough ductility to survive bending. Toughness How much energy can the material absorb before fracture? A part struck repeatedly needs resistance to fracture, not simply a hard surface. Strong does not mean stiff. Stiffness describes elastic deflection. Hardening a steel can raise its strength substantially without a comparable change in its elastic stiffness. Geometry also affects how far a part bends."},
{"title":"Density, conductivity and corrosion can decide the choice","page":"Metals and Their Properties","summary":"How bonding, grains and alloy condition affect the properties used to choose a metal.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metals-and-their-properties.html#density-conductivity-and-corrosion-can-decide-the-choice","text":"Density, conductivity and corrosion can decide the choice Density is mass per volume. Compare equal volumes when comparing densities; a large aluminum part can still weigh more than a small steel part. Use Geometry, Mass, and Force and Dimensional Analysis for mass, weight and unit calculations. Conductivity describes how readily heat or current travels through a material. Metals vary widely: choosing a good conductor is different from choosing a high-strength alloy. Corrosion resistance depends on the metal and its surroundings. Stainless steel and aluminum can corrode. Water, salts, trapped moisture and contact with other metals can change performance. A protective surface film helps only while the environment allows it to remain effective. Why connecting two different metals can change corrosion When dissimilar metals are electrically connected in a conducting liquid, such as salty water, galvanic corrosion can accelerate attack on the more active metal. Material pairing, exposed areas and the environment matter. Isolation and drainage can be as important as the alloy choice."},
{"title":"Ferrous means iron-based; a magnet does not identify the grade","page":"Metals and Their Properties","summary":"How bonding, grains and alloy condition affect the properties used to choose a metal.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/metals-and-their-properties.html#ferrous-means-iron-based-a-magnet-does-not-identify-the-grade","text":"Ferrous means iron-based; a magnet does not identify the grade Ferrous metals have iron as their base: steels and cast irons belong here. Nonferrous metals have another base, such as aluminum, copper or titanium. Nonferrous does not mean that no trace of iron can be present. Magnetic response is a clue, not a material certificate. Some stainless steels are weakly magnetic or effectively nonmagnetic in certain conditions. Appearance and a magnet cannot establish an alloy, its temper or its strength. Practice choosing a property"},
{"title":"A jointer establishes flatness; a planer establishes thickness","page":"Milling Rough Lumber Square","summary":"Establish one flat face and one square edge before making the opposite surfaces parallel and cutting to length.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/milling-rough-lumber-square.html#a-jointer-establishes-flatness-a-planer-establishes-thickness","text":"A jointer establishes flatness; a planer establishes thickness Stock is the material a part starts from. Rough lumber may have uneven surfaces or warp. A jointer removes material to establish a flat face. With that face as a reference against a square fence, it can establish a straight edge at 90° to the face. A thickness planer cuts the upper face relative to the face supported by its bed. With a flat reference face down, it makes the opposite face parallel and brings the board to thickness. Jointing both faces separately does not ensure they are parallel. A jointer makes one face flat while the opposite face remains uneven; a planer references that flat face to make the opposite face parallel. Why a planer alone can preserve a bow Feed rollers can press a bowed board flatter during the cut. When the pressure is released, the board can spring back. Uniform thickness and flatness are different requirements. Specialized fixtures can provide a reference for other methods, but the sequence here assumes an ordinary jointer and thickness planer."},
{"title":"Build reference surfaces in order: face, edge, thickness, width, length","page":"Milling Rough Lumber Square","summary":"Establish one flat face and one square edge before making the opposite surfaces parallel and cutting to length.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/milling-rough-lumber-square.html#build-reference-surfaces-in-order-face-edge-thickness-width-length","text":"Build reference surfaces in order: face, edge, thickness, width, length This is the geometry sequence for preparing rough stock. It assumes enough material remains for the finished dimensions and that the stock fits the equipment. It is not a machine setup guide; use Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for operation. Five stages: face-joint a flat face, edge-joint square to that face, plane the opposite face parallel, rip the opposite edge parallel, then crosscut ends to length. Face-joint: establish a flat face and mark it as the reference. Edge-joint: use the reference face to establish one straight edge square to it. Plane: place the reference face against the planer bed and bring the opposite face parallel to it at the required thickness. Rip: use the straight reference edge to locate the cut; make the opposite edge parallel at the required width. Crosscut: establish a square end, then cut the other end to the required length. Any preliminary breakdown into shorter, oversized blanks comes before final sizing. Leave allowance for the later cuts; an early rough crosscut is not the final crosscut in this sequence. Practice the milling order Practice the reference face"},
{"title":"Check geometry instead of counting machine passes","page":"Milling Rough Lumber Square","summary":"Establish one flat face and one square edge before making the opposite surfaces parallel and cutting to length.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/milling-rough-lumber-square.html#check-geometry-instead-of-counting-machine-passes","text":"Check geometry instead of counting machine passes One pass is not proof that a surface is ready. The result of each stage is the reference for the next. Flatness means a surface lies in a plane; parallel faces maintain a constant separation; square surfaces meet at 90°. Check the reference face for flatness with a straightedge and for twist with winding sticks, a matched pair of sighting bars. Check the reference edge for straightness and squareness to the face. Measure thickness at several positions after planing. Check width, parallel edges, end squareness and final length. Recheck dimensions and geometry if material removal releases stresses and the board moves. Stop and reconsider the intended dimensions if flattening would make the board too thin or too narrow. No sequence can recover material already removed. Use How Saws Cut Wood to account for kerf in the width and length layout."},
{"title":"Choose the useful property, then check the tradeoff","page":"Nonferrous Metals","summary":"Aluminum, copper alloys, titanium, zinc and magnesium as choices for weight, conductivity, corrosion and manufacturing needs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/nonferrous-metals.html#choose-the-useful-property-then-check-the-tradeoff","text":"Choose the useful property, then check the tradeoff Nonferrous metals have a base other than iron. They are not all lightweight, corrosion-proof or easy to machine. A useful first comparison names both the benefit and the constraint. Starting points for material selection, not design allowables Metal Why choose it? Typical use and tradeoff Aluminum Low density, useful corrosion resistance and ready availability in extruded shapes. Frames and housings. Lower elastic stiffness than steel; alloy and temper strongly affect strength. Copper High electrical and thermal conductivity. Conductors and heat-transfer parts. Relatively high density and cost; alloying usually reduces conductivity. Titanium Useful strength relative to weight and corrosion resistance. Aerospace and chemical-service parts. Material and processing costs can outweigh the weight saving. Zinc Useful casting behavior and ability to protect steel as a coating. Die-cast hardware and galvanized products. Temperature and load requirements limit use. Magnesium Very low density among structural metals. Light housings. Corrosion protection and suitable manufacturing procedures matter. These are material-selection comparisons. For cutting operations, continue to Milling Operations, Lathe Operations and Feeds and Speeds."},
{"title":"Aluminum needs both an alloy label and a condition","page":"Nonferrous Metals","summary":"Aluminum, copper alloys, titanium, zinc and magnesium as choices for weight, conductivity, corrosion and manufacturing needs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/nonferrous-metals.html#aluminum-needs-both-an-alloy-label-and-a-condition","text":"Aluminum needs both an alloy label and a condition Aluminum is sold in many alloys and conditions. Wrought products are shaped by processes such as rolling or extrusion. Their familiar four-digit labels are organized into series, including 1xxx through 7xxx. A series is a family, not a hardness scale: a higher first digit does not guarantee a harder or better part. 6061 and 7075 are common alloy labels to look for in supplier literature. Compare data for the actual product form and temper rather than treating either number as a complete specification. Cast aluminum uses a different designation system; do not transfer wrought-number rules to cast products. A suffix such as -O or -T6 identifies a condition as part of an alloy-temper designation. It must stay attached to the alloy on an order. See Heat Treating Metals for why processing history matters. The Aluminum Extruders Council defines the two tempers: -O: “Annealed” — “Thermally treated to obtain the lowest strength temper.” -T6: “Solution heat-treated and artificially aged.” Use the governing product specification for an actual purchase."},
{"title":"Aluminum series 1xxx–7xxx identify purity or the main alloying additions","page":"Nonferrous Metals","summary":"Aluminum, copper alloys, titanium, zinc and magnesium as choices for weight, conductivity, corrosion and manufacturing needs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/nonferrous-metals.html#aluminum-series-1xxx-7xxx-identify-purity-or-the-main-alloying-additions","text":"Aluminum series 1xxx–7xxx identify purity or the main alloying additions Wrought aluminum series Series Principal alloying element or purity 1xxx Commercially pure aluminum: at least 99% aluminum. 2xxx Copper. 3xxx Manganese. 4xxx Silicon. 5xxx Magnesium. 6xxx Magnesium and silicon. 7xxx Zinc. Source for the series table The Aluminum Association's aluminum-alloy overview identifies these series and their principal additions. It describes 1xxx as “aluminum 99 percent or higher purity.” The series names do not list every element in an individual alloy; for example, the Association also identifies magnesium and possible copper and chromium additions in 7xxx alloys."},
{"title":"6061 favors fabrication; 7075 offers higher strength in the T6 condition","page":"Nonferrous Metals","summary":"Aluminum, copper alloys, titanium, zinc and magnesium as choices for weight, conductivity, corrosion and manufacturing needs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/nonferrous-metals.html#6061-favors-fabrication-7075-offers-higher-strength-in-the-t6-condition","text":"6061 favors fabrication; 7075 offers higher strength in the T6 condition Compare like conditions: 7075-T6 has higher tensile strength than 6061-T6. The alloy number alone does not establish the strength of the material in hand. 6061 and 7075 at selection level Alloy Typical uses Fabrication considerations 6061 Truck and marine frames; tanks. Weldable and readily machined. Welding can change the temper in the weld area. 7075 Aircraft components, molds and industrial tooling. Good machinability, but generally considered unsuitable for welding because of cracking susceptibility. Sources and comparison limits The Aluminum Association identifies 6061 use in truck and marine frames and 7075 use in aircraft. Metal Supermarkets' 6061/7075 comparison supports the T6 tensile-strength comparison, tank and tooling uses, machinability and welding differences. Its fabrication discussion notes that restoring the weld area's original temper in 6061 may require further heat treatment and aging. Compare the supplier's data for the actual temper and product form."},
{"title":"Brass and bronze trade some of copper's conductivity for other properties","page":"Nonferrous Metals","summary":"Aluminum, copper alloys, titanium, zinc and magnesium as choices for weight, conductivity, corrosion and manufacturing needs.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/nonferrous-metals.html#brass-and-bronze-trade-some-of-copper-s-conductivity-for-other-properties","text":"Brass and bronze trade some of copper's conductivity for other properties Brass is principally a copper-zinc alloy. Depending on grade, it can offer useful forming or machining behavior for fittings, hardware and decorative parts. Do not assume every brass has the same machinability or suitability for contact with drinking water. Bronze traditionally means copper-tin alloy, but commercial names also include aluminum bronze and silicon bronze. Bronzes serve in bearings, bushings, gears and corrosion-resistant hardware, depending on grade. The word “bronze” alone is not enough to buy a bearing material. For a conductor, begin with conductivity requirements. For a bearing, begin with load, speed, lubrication and wear. The same copper-based family will not automatically win both comparisons. Practice a material choice"},
{"title":"A method sheet tells another person how to make and check the part","page":"Operation Method Sheets","summary":"Turn a drawing into an ordered plan with equipment, setup, process settings and a check that proves each operation is complete.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/operation-method-sheets.html#a-method-sheet-tells-another-person-how-to-make-and-check-the-part","text":"A method sheet tells another person how to make and check the part A drawing describes the required part. An operation method sheet describes how to make it. A process plan or routing sheet serves the same planning purpose, although the amount of detail varies. Write one row for each operation, in the order it will be performed. Start with the part number and name, drawing revision, date, material and raw stock size: the starting piece before machining or cutting. Use actual dimensions and units. A material family alone is not a purchasing specification; identify the material needed for this part. What belongs in each operation row Field What the next person needs Operation An action and its intended result, such as drill two through holes. Equipment and setup/tooling The equipment, cutting tool, support, holding method and reference face or edge. Process parameters Settings with units, or an identified approved setup instruction. Do not write an unexplained number for speed or feed. Setup and cycle time Time to prepare the operation, separate from time to repeat it on one part. Pass/fail criteria and inspection tools The required result, permitted limits and how to measure or compare it. Keep quality checkpoints, failed-part decisions, actual times and improvement notes with the sheet. Equipment permission and operating procedures still come from Equipment Operating Procedure; a method sheet is not machine training."},
{"title":"Establish reference surfaces before locating features","page":"Operation Method Sheets","summary":"Turn a drawing into an ordered plan with equipment, setup, process settings and a check that proves each operation is complete.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/operation-method-sheets.html#establish-reference-surfaces-before-locating-features","text":"Establish reference surfaces before locating features Choose the order around what each later step needs. Make a straight, square reference edge before measuring hole locations from it. Make features while there is enough material to support and hold the work. Inspect a feature before the next step hides it. Planning sequence: establish references, locate and make features, separate if needed, finish, then inspect. Preserve enough stock for holding throughout. This is a planning pattern, not a mandatory route for every part. Holes may be easier to make before small parts are cut free from a larger piece. Final finishing usually follows cutting and drilling, so those operations do not damage the finished surface. Inspect afterward too: finishing can change dimensions. Why there is no universal operation order A feature may become inaccessible after assembly; a coating may need to be applied first. A larger blank may help holding, but its final cut must preserve the reference used for the holes. Record the reason for a departure from the usual sequence. The five process categories describe kinds of change, not a fixed order."},
{"title":"Worked method sheet: a hanging display label","page":"Operation Method Sheets","summary":"Turn a drawing into an ordered plan with equipment, setup, process settings and a check that proves each operation is complete.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/operation-method-sheets.html#worked-method-sheet-a-hanging-display-label","text":"Worked method sheet: a hanging display label Original teaching example, not a production release. Part DL-01, display label plate, revision A. Material: birch plywood, starting blank 110 × 50 × 6 mm. Finished dimensions and acceptance limits are on the example drawing. Two purchased cord loops complete the assembly in Bills of Materials. No coating is specified. This hand-tool route uses a supported blank, a fine-tooth handsaw, a drill press and sanding blocks. An actual shop must qualify its tooling and record machine settings before releasing the plan. The times below are invented planning estimates, not measured performance. DL-01 revision A — route and inspection Operation Equipment, setup and parameters Pass/fail check and tool 10 — Establish edges and trim Bench, clamps, handsaw and sanding block. Support the blank; establish straight bottom edge B and left edge L square to it. Mark the final outline from B and L, saw outside it, then sand to the lines. Calipers: length 99–101 mm; width 39–41 mm; thickness 5.5–6.5 mm. Try square: no visible gap along the checked edges against the blade. Reject split or separated plywood layers. 20 — Locate two hole centers Rule, square and marking tool. From L mark x = 15 and 85 mm; from B mark y = 20 mm for both. Keep the same references for both holes. Rule and square: confirm both marks against the drawing before drilling; this layout check does not replace finished-hole measurement. 30 — Drill through Drill press, 4.5 mm wood-cutting bit, backing board and clamped work. Record the qualified spindle speed in rpm and the approved drilling setup before use. Hole diameter 4.3–4.7 mm using suitable plug gauges; hole-center x and y coordinates within ±0.5 mm of the drawing using calipers and measured hole diameter. No splits at the holes. 40 — Finish and inspect Sanding block, example sequence 120 then 180 grit. Remove loose fibers at edges and hole mouths without enlarging holes beyond their limits. Visual inspection: no loose fibers, splits or separated layers. Recheck dimensions and hole limits using the tools above. 50 — Add two cord loops Hand assembly. Thread one purchased closed loop through each hole, then pass the rest of that loop through its emerging end to form a hitch. Seat both hitches alike. Visual comparison: two closed loops present, one through each hole, each hitched around the plate edge with no loose cut end. This is a display example, not a load-rated hanger. Planning time per operation, minutes Operation Setup per batch Cycle per assembly 10 3 5 20 1 2 30 4 2 40 1 3 50 1 1 Total 10 13 For three assemblies, the serial estimate is 10 + (3 × 13) = 49 minutes, assuming each setup is done once and all operations run one after another. Record waiting, interruptions and repeated setups separately when comparing with actual elapsed time."},
{"title":"Replace “make it right” with an action and an acceptance test","page":"Operation Method Sheets","summary":"Turn a drawing into an ordered plan with equipment, setup, process settings and a check that proves each operation is complete.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/operation-method-sheets.html#replace-make-it-right-with-an-action-and-an-acceptance-test","text":"Replace “make it right” with an action and an acceptance test “Drill the holes neatly” leaves the next person guessing. “Drill two through holes at the drawing coordinates; check diameter and location against revision A” gives an action and a result, but still needs the equipment, support, tooling and settings in the row. Pass means every stated criterion is met. Fail means identify and hold the part for a decision; do not quietly send it to the next operation. Rework is a specified additional operation that can bring the part into conformance. Removing more material cannot restore an undersize outside dimension or shrink an oversize hole. Match the part, drawing and method-sheet revisions. Check that every operation names equipment, tooling, holding and references. Fill required settings with units before releasing the plan. Give every quality checkpoint a measurable limit or an explicit comparison and an inspection method. Define who decides rework or rejection when a check fails. Practice operation order Find an incomplete step"},
{"title":"Record what changed so the next batch can repeat the improvement","page":"Operation Method Sheets","summary":"Turn a drawing into an ordered plan with equipment, setup, process settings and a check that proves each operation is complete.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/operation-method-sheets.html#record-what-changed-so-the-next-batch-can-repeat-the-improvement","text":"Record what changed so the next batch can repeat the improvement Record setup time once per setup and cycle time per piece. State when each timer starts and stops; include inspection consistently. Keep quantity attempted, quantity accepted, scrap count and rework count. A reworked part may later pass, so these counts are not all separate groups to add together. For each trial, record material identity, tool, setting and units, holding arrangement, actual measurements and the problem observed. A useful photo shows the reference edge, stop or tool position; caption it with the operation and revision. Use photos you have permission to share, with no personal information. Example improvement record — a proposed trial, not a claimed result Observation Change to test Evidence to keep Hole locations vary between pieces. Use the same reference edges and a verified locating stop instead of re-marking from different edges. Record each hole location before and after the change, batch size, setup time and rejects. Keep the acceptance limits unchanged. Change one factor at a time where practical. Verify the first piece after a setup change before repeating it through a batch. If the change helps, update the method-sheet revision and keep the evidence. A faster operation that produces more rejects is not automatically an improvement."},
{"title":"Start with the shape and how the material becomes solid","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#start-with-the-shape-and-how-the-material-becomes-solid","text":"Start with the shape and how the material becomes solid These processes make a shape by moving softened material, stretching sheet or curing resin in tooling. A mold shapes a part against its surfaces; a die gives flowing material a cross-section. Tooling is the equipment made specifically to produce the part. Use the process-category foundations topic for the five-category framework. Here, follow what happens to the material: thermoplastics usually set by cooling; thermosets set by curing. Review Plastics as Materials if that distinction is unclear. Four process comparisons: blow molding inflates a hot tube into a bottle; rotational molding coats a rotating mold to make a hollow tank; vacuum forming stretches sheet into an open tray; extrusion pushes melt through a die to make a continuous hollow profile. The starting form and the finished shape narrow the choice before cost enters the discussion. The diagrams show typical versions of each process. For equipment operation and hazards, use the Safety collection's Equipment Operating Procedure, Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets topics."},
{"title":"Injection molding fills a closed cavity, then repeats the cycle","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#injection-molding-fills-a-closed-cavity-then-repeats-the-cycle","text":"Injection molding fills a closed cavity, then repeats the cycle In conventional thermoplastic injection molding, a heated barrel and screw prepare the melt. The screw advances to push a measured shot (the material delivered in one injection) through a nozzle into a clamped mold. Five stages of a thermoplastic injection molding cycle: clamp the mold, inject melt, hold pressure to pack material as it shrinks, cool until the part is stiff enough, then open and eject before repeating. Cooling begins during filling and packing. A thermoplastic cycle. Cooling overlaps filling and packing; the five labels describe the main jobs, not five completely separate thermal events. Clamp: close the mold and hold it against the pressure of the incoming melt. Inject: fill the cavity. Hold/pack: maintain pressure to feed additional material as it shrinks, while the gate can still pass melt. Cool: allow enough heat to leave for the part to keep its shape during removal. Eject: open the mold and push the part free, commonly with ejector pins. Close for the next cycle. Materials and products: thermoplastics such as ABS, PP and nylon become housings, caps, clips and gears. Specialized injection systems also mold thermosets; those require curing, so the cooling-based cycle above is not universal. Tooling and quantity: detailed, pressure-resistant molds are a substantial initial investment. Repeated cycles and multiple cavities can make the investment worthwhile for many identical parts. See the machine and cycle in a public explainer The BPF injection-molding explainer describes the machine and process. No shop-specific settings or procedures are implied here."},
{"title":"Sprue, runner and gate carry melt into the part","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#sprue-runner-and-gate-carry-melt-into-the-part","text":"Sprue, runner and gate carry melt into the part In a conventional sprue-and-runner layout, the sprue receives melt from the nozzle, a runner distributes it, and a gate is the restricted entrance to a part cavity. One mold may contain several cavities, and one cavity may have several gates. Draft is a slight taper on a surface in the mold-opening direction. It helps the part release instead of scraping along the tool. Injection molding can produce ribs, bosses (raised features around holes) and fine detail, but every feature must have a way out of the mold. An undercut traps material behind a tool feature. It may require a moving side core, another special mechanism or a redesigned part. A simple two-part mold cannot produce every enclosed hollow shape."},
{"title":"Uniform walls reduce sink marks and uneven shrinkage","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#uniform-walls-reduce-sink-marks-and-uneven-shrinkage","text":"Uniform walls reduce sink marks and uneven shrinkage Aim for a reasonably uniform wall thickness, with gradual transitions where thickness must change. A thick mass cools differently from an adjacent thin wall. Its shrinkage can pull the surface inward, leaving a sink mark, or contribute to distortion. A solid block under a thin housing wall is therefore a warning sign. Hollow out the bulky region where possible and use appropriately sized ribs for support. Packing and cooling settings also matter; changing the drawing is not the only remedy. Three design clues to recognize A shallow depression opposite a heavy rib suggests sink. Scraping during release suggests inadequate draft or another ejection problem. A sudden thick-to-thin step suggests uneven cooling and difficult filling. Protolabs' defect guide illustrates these relationships. Wall and draft requirements depend on resin, texture, depth and tooling; no single angle or thickness fits every part. Practice mold design problems"},
{"title":"Blow molding inflates a hot tube or preform into a hollow part","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#blow-molding-inflates-a-hot-tube-or-preform-into-a-hollow-part","text":"Blow molding inflates a hot tube or preform into a hollow part A mold closes around a hot parison (tube), or a previously molded preform. Air pressure expands the material against the mold, then it cools. Extrusion blow molding starts with a tube; injection-based versions start with a molded preform. Materials and products: thermoplastics; HDPE detergent bottles and PET beverage bottles are familiar examples made by different blow-molding variants. Tooling and quantity: dedicated molds and machinery favor repeated production, especially large packaging runs. Mold complexity and the chosen variant affect cost; lower forming pressure does not make the whole production line inexpensive. Geometry: good for thin-walled hollow containers. Stretching affects wall thickness; external detail is controlled better than internal detail. It does not directly make a solid gear or complex internal ribs. Mold parting lines, and pinch-off seams in extrusion-blown parts, may remain visible. Public process reference BPF: extrusion blow molding shows how the tube becomes a container."},
{"title":"Rotational molding coats a mold to make a large hollow shell","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#rotational-molding-coats-a-mold-to-make-a-large-hollow-shell","text":"Rotational molding coats a mold to make a large hollow shell A measured charge, usually thermoplastic powder, goes inside a mold. Heating and slow rotation about two axes distribute material over the inside surface as it melts. The mold keeps moving during cooling before the shell is removed. Materials and products: predominantly polyethylene; tanks, playground shells and kayaks. Tooling and quantity: molds do not withstand injection pressure, so tooling is often less costly for a large part. Heating and cooling the mold takes time, making low-to-medium quantities attractive compared with faster processes. Geometry: large hollow parts with relatively even walls are a strength. Local thickness, corners and fine tolerances still need care. Tiny details, very thin walls and solid precision parts are poor fits. This is slow coating of the mold, not high-speed centrifugal casting. Public process reference BPF: rotational molding explains the sequence and the predominance of polyethylene."},
{"title":"Thermoforming stretches a sheet into a tray or shell","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#thermoforming-stretches-a-sheet-into-a-tray-or-shell","text":"Thermoforming stretches a sheet into a tray or shell Heat thermoplastic sheet until it is pliable, form it against a tool, cool it and trim the edge. In vacuum forming, removing air between sheet and tool lets outside air pressure press the sheet against the tool. Pressure forming adds pressure on the other side. Materials and products: thermoplastic sheets such as PETG, ABS and polystyrene; packaging trays, display covers and equipment liners. Tooling and quantity: a simple one-sided tool can suit prototypes or short runs. Durable tools and automated equipment also support very large packaging volumes. Geometry: best for open, sheet-like shapes with draft. Deep draws stretch and thin the sheet; the tool controls one surface most directly. It cannot create a solid boss or a closed bottle from one sheet in this basic process. Undercuts complicate release. Public process reference BPF: vacuum forming describes heating, forming, release and trimming."},
{"title":"Extrusion makes a continuous, constant cross-section","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#extrusion-makes-a-continuous-constant-cross-section","text":"Extrusion makes a continuous, constant cross-section A screw moves and melts plastic, then pushes it continuously through a shaped die. Cooling and sizing equipment establish the final dimensions. The product is cut to length or wound. Materials and products: commonly thermoplastics such as PVC and PE; pipe, tubing, sheet, film and window profiles. Tooling and quantity: a profile die is often simpler than a complex injection mold, but the production line and setup still cost money. Long runs spread those costs over many meters of product. Geometry: the die can make solid or hollow cross-sections. It cannot directly put a cap on the end of a tube or create isolated holes along one side; those require other operations. A long product with the same section everywhere is the main clue. Public process reference BPF: extrusion profiles and sheet explains the die and downstream sizing equipment."},
{"title":"Compression molding presses a charge between matched tools","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#compression-molding-presses-a-charge-between-matched-tools","text":"Compression molding presses a charge between matched tools Place a measured amount of material in an open mold. Closing the heated tool applies pressure and spreads the charge into shape. With a thermosetting compound, the part cures before removal; cooling alone is not what hardens it. Materials and products: often thermosets or fiber-filled thermosetting compounds; electrical components and composite body panels. Thermoplastic sheet composites can also be compression molded, with a cooling-based solidification step. Tooling and quantity: matched tools and a press require investment, often justified by repeated medium-to-high production. Tooling is not automatically cheap; size, surface quality and precision matter. Geometry: both faces can be shaped, including ribs and shaped panels. Material must flow before it cures; extremely thin features, deep narrow details and trapped undercuts are difficult. Public process reference The thermoset section of BPF's plastics-process reference explains the reaction under heat and pressure."},
{"title":"Casting fills a mold with liquid resin for small quantities","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#casting-fills-a-mold-with-liquid-resin-for-small-quantities","text":"Casting fills a mold with liquid resin for small quantities Liquid resin fills a mold and hardens. In common small-batch resin casting, mixed components react and cure. A flexible mold made from a master pattern can reproduce surface detail. Materials and products: often thermosets such as epoxy, polyurethane or unsaturated polyester; prototypes, decorative replicas and electrical encapsulation. Casting is broader than polyurethane casting; some cast plastics, such as cast acrylic, are thermoplastic after polymerization. Tooling and quantity: flexible molds can have a low initial cost but limited life. Labor and cure time make this useful for short runs rather than a default for millions of pieces. Geometry: good detail and some undercuts are possible with flexible tooling. Trapped air, cure shrinkage and heat from the reaction limit thin passages and thick pours. A closed hollow space still needs a removable or sacrificial core, or a different casting method. Public process reference The polymer-casting section of Formlabs' manufacturing guide explains flexible molds and short-run economics."},
{"title":"Hand layup builds a fiber-reinforced composite against a tool","page":"Plastic Forming Processes","summary":"How eight plastic and composite processes shape material, what they can make, and where tooling and geometry limit the choice.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastic-forming-processes.html#hand-layup-builds-a-fiber-reinforced-composite-against-a-tool","text":"Hand layup builds a fiber-reinforced composite against a tool In wet hand layup, reinforcing fabric or mat is placed against a mold, wetted with liquid resin and consolidated to reduce trapped air. Layers build thickness, then the resin cures. Materials and products: usually a thermosetting polyester or epoxy matrix with glass or carbon fibers; boat shells and custom covers. These are composites in the Wood & Composites material family. Prepreg layup uses reinforcement already containing resin and is a related but different route. Tooling and quantity: relatively simple one-sided molds support small runs and large custom parts. Manual work and cure time limit production rate. Geometry: good for broad shells. The mold gives one face its finish; the other depends on the layup. Tight corners, inaccessible cavities and precise thickness on both faces are difficult. Fiber direction affects strength, so layer arrangement is part of the design. Public process reference ACMA's composite guide, Appendix E.1.1, explains wet hand layup and its dependence on workmanship. Apply these comparisons in Choosing a Plastic Forming Process. Plastic separating, fabricating, 3D printing and finishing are separate topics; for the general milling process, see Milling Operations."},
{"title":"Plastics get their behavior from polymer chains","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#plastics-get-their-behavior-from-polymer-chains","text":"Plastics get their behavior from polymer chains A polymer is a substance made of very large molecules built from repeating units. Small starting molecules called monomers react to build those molecules. Many polymers have long chains; some also have branches or chemical links between chains. A plastic combines a polymer with ingredients that may change its color, flexibility, processing behavior or resistance to weather. The polymer name is a starting point, not a complete material specification. Two grades of the same plastic can behave differently. Plastics is one of the four material families in the material-family foundations topic. A polymer reinforced with glass or carbon fibers is a composite; the course places composites in Wood & Composites."},
{"title":"Thermoplastics soften again; cured thermosets do not remelt","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#thermoplastics-soften-again-cured-thermosets-do-not-remelt","text":"Thermoplastics soften again; cured thermosets do not remelt The useful distinction is behavior on reheating Type What happens Examples and recycling Thermoplastic Heating makes the material soft enough to reshape or flow. Cooling makes it firm again. Reheating can repeat this change, within the material's processing limits. PE, PP, PVC, PLA, ABS, PETG, acrylic, polycarbonate and nylon. Clean, compatible material can often be remelted for recycling; contamination, degradation and available collection systems limit actual recovery. Thermoset Curing forms a network of chemical links between molecules. The cured material cannot be melted back into a moldable liquid. Excessive heating degrades it. Cured epoxy and unsaturated polyester resin. They cannot be recycled by ordinary melt-and-remold processing; specialized recovery methods may exist. Curing is a chemical change; cooling is a temperature change. A two-part epoxy becoming solid is not simply a hot plastic cooling down. Softening, melting and heat resistance are different questions Some thermoplastics soften over a range rather than having one sharp melting temperature. A part can lose stiffness and fail under load well before it flows. Thermosets also have service-temperature limits: being unable to remelt does not make them heatproof. Use the grade's data sheet for the actual load and temperature. The British Plastics Federation's thermoplastics reference explains reheating behavior and shows how varied the materials are."},
{"title":"Light weight and corrosion resistance come with limits","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#light-weight-and-corrosion-resistance-come-with-limits","text":"Light weight and corrosion resistance come with limits Light weight: many unfilled plastics have much lower density than metals. Low weight does not automatically mean high stiffness. Chemical resistance: plastics do not rust like steel, but a chemical may soften, swell or crack a particular plastic. Check the material, chemical, temperature and exposure time together. Low softening or melting temperatures: many common thermoplastics are easy to form with heat but unsuitable for hot, loaded service. Creep: slow deformation under a sustained load. A plastic shelf can keep sagging even when its load stays constant. UV degradation: sunlight can discolor or embrittle susceptible grades. Outdoor service calls for a suitable resin and stabilization, not just a preferred color. Electrical insulation: many unfilled plastics resist current flow. Conductive fillers and special formulations can change that behavior. Why a short strength test does not settle a long service life Temperature, time, moisture and chemicals can change both dimensions and load-carrying ability. A part that survives a brief pull may still creep over months. Curbell's dimensional-stability guide connects these effects to material selection."},
{"title":"Match common thermoplastics to their useful properties","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#match-common-thermoplastics-to-their-useful-properties","text":"Match common thermoplastics to their useful properties These are typical uses and tradeoffs, not design ratings for every grade. All materials in this table are thermoplastics. Common plastics: a first selection, followed by a grade data sheet Material Typical uses and strengths Watch for PLA — polylactic acid Models, packaging and filament; stiff, often made from renewable feedstocks. Ordinary grades have limited heat resistance and can be brittle. Bio-based does not mean an item belongs in home compost or a recycling bin. ABS — acrylonitrile butadiene styrene Housings and toys; a useful balance of toughness and rigidity. Ordinary grades weather poorly outdoors; some solvents attack it. PETG — glycol-modified PET Clear displays, formed packaging and filament; tough and readily thermoformed. Scratches and limited service temperature. PETG is not interchangeable with bottle-grade PET. Acrylic / PMMA — polymethyl methacrylate Signs, displays and glazing; clarity, stiffness and good weathering. More prone to cracking under impact than polycarbonate; vulnerable to some solvents. PC — polycarbonate Impact-resistant glazing and housings; transparent and tough. Scratching and chemical attack; select an appropriate UV-resistant grade for outdoor exposure. HDPE — high-density polyethylene Bottles, tanks and pipe; tough with useful chemical resistance. Low stiffness compared with metals, creep and substantial thermal expansion. LDPE — low-density polyethylene Film and squeeze bottles; flexible and tough. Less stiff than HDPE and limited temperature capability. PP — polypropylene Containers, caps and living hinges (thin flexible connections); light and chemically resistant. Creep, UV sensitivity in unstabilized grades and reduced toughness in cold service. PVC — polyvinyl chloride Rigid pipe and window profiles; plasticized grades make flexible tubing and cable insulation. Properties depend strongly on additives; limited temperature range. Nylon / PA — polyamide Gears, bearings and cable ties; strength, toughness and wear resistance. Absorbed moisture changes dimensions and mechanical properties. Material references for checking a particular choice Compare BPF's thermoplastic families with supplier guidance for acrylic, polycarbonate and PETG sheet. Their grade differences matter more than a single generic strength number."},
{"title":"Epoxy and polyester resin cure into thermosets","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#epoxy-and-polyester-resin-cure-into-thermosets","text":"Epoxy and polyester resin cure into thermosets Two common thermosetting resin systems Resin Typical uses and strengths Limitations Epoxy Adhesives, cast parts and composite matrices; good adhesion and generally low cure shrinkage. Mixing and cure conditions affect properties. Often costs more than general-purpose polyester resin; unprotected epoxy can degrade in sunlight. Unsaturated polyester resin Fiberglass boat shells and panels; economical for many composite parts. Typically greater cure shrinkage and lower adhesive performance than epoxy. Cured resin cannot be remelted. “Polyester” alone is ambiguous. PET packaging is a thermoplastic polyester. The unsaturated polyester resin used in a conventional fiberglass layup cures into a thermoset. See the hand-layup card in Plastic Forming Processes. Where the resin comparison comes from The American Composites Manufacturers Association guide, Appendix D, compares composite resin systems. The resin holds and transfers load between the reinforcing fibers; the fibers and their orientation strongly affect the resulting composite."},
{"title":"Resin codes identify plastic; they do not promise recycling","page":"Plastics as Materials","summary":"Polymer chains, thermoplastics and thermosets, common plastics, and what resin identification codes actually tell you.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/plastics-as-materials.html#resin-codes-identify-plastic-they-do-not-promise-recycling","text":"Resin codes identify plastic; they do not promise recycling The number on a container is a resin identification code (RIC). It can help sorting, but it does not say whether the local recycling program accepts that product. The seven code-to-resin matches, checked against current public guidance Code Plastic 1 — PET / PETE Polyethylene terephthalate 2 — HDPE High-density polyethylene 3 — PVC Polyvinyl chloride (vinyl) 4 — LDPE Low-density polyethylene 5 — PP Polypropylene 6 — PS Polystyrene 7 — OTHER Resins outside the first six categories; also some combinations of resins. It is not one particular plastic. For example, code 5 identifies PP; it does not prove that a PP lid is accepted with PP containers. Use the collector's instructions for that item. Do not assign PETG to code 1 just because its name contains PET. Verified code sources and the standard's exact distinction ASTM D7611/D7611M-26, section 4.2, states: Resin Identification Codes are not “recycle codes.” Read the current ASTM significance-and-use statement. The number-to-material matches are published in the City of Sioux Falls RIC guide. The PET Resin Association consumer guide supplies the familiar label terminology. Checked October 27, 2026. This is an identification reference, not a specification for manufacturing compliant labels. Practice resin codes Practice reheating behavior Next: Plastic Forming Processes and Choosing a Plastic Forming Process."},
{"title":"Steel is iron-based, with carbon and other elements controlling behavior","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#steel-is-iron-based-with-carbon-and-other-elements-controlling-behavior","text":"Steel is iron-based, with carbon and other elements controlling behavior Steel contains iron and carbon, often with additional alloying elements. Both composition and processing matter. The same grade can arrive softened for machining or hardened for service. Use four practical groups: carbon steel, alloy steel, stainless steel and tool steel. These are useful buying categories, not four mutually exclusive chemical definitions: stainless and tool steels also contain alloying elements. Steel branches into carbon, alloy, stainless and tool groups; carbon steel divides into low, medium and high carbon. The four steel groups are a selection map. They do not replace grade and condition."},
{"title":"Carbon steel spans easy forming through hardenable cutting edges","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#carbon-steel-spans-easy-forming-through-hardenable-cutting-edges","text":"Carbon steel spans easy forming through hardenable cutting edges Relative carbon levels; actual grade limits come from the specification Group Typical behavior Typical uses Low carbon Generally readily formed and welded; limited response to ordinary through-hardening. Sheet parts, brackets and general fabrication. Medium carbon More potential for heat-treated strength; forming and welding require more consideration. Shafts, axles and machinery parts. High carbon Can develop high hardness after suitable treatment; less forgiving of forming and impact. Springs and some cutting tools. These are trends, not a strength ranking of every item on a shelf. Compare the delivery condition and required properties before substituting one grade for another."},
{"title":"Alloy, stainless and tool steels solve different service problems","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#alloy-stainless-and-tool-steels-solve-different-service-problems","text":"Alloy, stainless and tool steels solve different service problems Choose a family, then qualify a specific grade Family Composition idea and purpose Uses and limits Alloy steel Intentional additions such as chromium, nickel or molybdenum adjust properties and heat-treatment response. Loaded shafts and gears. Alloy steel is not automatically corrosion resistant. Stainless steel Chromium supports a protective surface film; other elements adjust performance. Food equipment, fittings and exposed hardware. Grade, cleaning and environment still matter. Tool steel Carbon and alloy additions support hardness, wear resistance or performance at elevated temperature. Dies, punches and cutting tools. Heat treatment must balance wear resistance against chipping. Use Heat Treating Metals to distinguish hardening from tempering. Hardenability means the ability to harden to depth under given cooling conditions; it is not the same as a measured surface hardness."},
{"title":"Grade labels identify a material; they are not a heat-treatment recipe","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#grade-labels-identify-a-material-they-are-not-a-heat-treatment-recipe","text":"Grade labels identify a material; they are not a heat-treatment recipe Common labels include 1018 and 1045 for carbon steels, 4140 for alloy steel, 304 and 316 for stainless steels, and O1, A2 and D2 for tool steels. Different designation systems have different rules. Do not apply a four-digit carbon-steel decoding rule to stainless or tool-steel labels. On an order, identify the governing material specification, grade, delivery condition, form and dimensions. A short grade name alone does not specify strength, hardness, surface finish or dimensional tolerance. Ask for the required certification when traceability matters. Use the current governing specification or the producer's grade sheet for composition limits and treatment requirements."},
{"title":"Decode carbon and alloy steel numbers; read stainless and tool grades separately","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#decode-carbon-and-alloy-steel-numbers-read-stainless-and-tool-grades-separately","text":"Decode carbon and alloy steel numbers; read stainless and tool grades separately In the common AISI/SAE four-digit carbon and alloy steel system, the first two digits identify the steel series: 10 is plain carbon steel and 41 is chromium-molybdenum steel. The last two digits give nominal carbon content in hundredths of a percent. Reading the series and nominal carbon content Grade Series Nominal carbon 1018 10: plain carbon 18 → 0.18% 1045 10: plain carbon 45 → 0.45% 4140 41: chromium-molybdenum 40 → 0.40% 304 and 316 stainless: their chemistries are similar, but 316 has an added alloying element, molybdenum. This improves resistance to chloride exposure and urban or industrial pollution compared with 304. Tool-steel groups: O1 is an oil-hardening grade, A2 is an air-hardening grade, and D2 is a high-carbon, high-chromium grade. These labels describe different tool-steel groups; they are not carbon-percentage codes. Sources and quoted designation meanings The Metals Service Center Institute identifies the first digit as “family of steel,” the second as “additional elements,” and the third and fourth as “nominal carbon content”; its example is 1018 at 0.18% carbon. Total Materia's SAE-AISI introduction gives the final two digits as “the carbon content in hundredths of a percent” and lists 41XX as “Chromium-molybdenum steels.” The International Molybdenum Association's 304/316 comparison states that “Type 316 contains a molybdenum addition” and explains the corrosion benefit. Hudson Tool Steel's A2 data sheet describes O1 as “oil-hardening,” A2 as “air-hardening,” and D2 as “high-carbon, high-chromium.” These are introductory meanings, not complete material specifications."},
{"title":"Cast iron is a separate iron-based material, and it can be machined","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#cast-iron-is-a-separate-iron-based-material-and-it-can-be-machined","text":"Cast iron is a separate iron-based material, and it can be machined Cast irons generally contain more carbon than steels. Their properties depend strongly on how carbon appears in the structure. Gray iron contains graphite flakes and is useful for machine bases and housings where vibration damping matters; it is relatively brittle in tension. Ductile iron contains graphite nodules and can provide much greater ductility. Casting creates the initial shape. Machining can then finish bores, faces and other accurate features. “Cast” and “machined” are not competing material identities. White cast iron contains substantial hard carbide and behaves differently from graphite-containing irons."},
{"title":"Watch the steel-family series at its original publisher","page":"Types of Steel","summary":"Carbon, alloy, stainless and tool steels, plus the difference between a steel family and a complete material specification.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/types-of-steel.html#watch-the-steel-family-series-at-its-original-publisher","text":"Watch the steel-family series at its original publisher Metal Supermarkets hosts the five-part series: overview, carbon steel, alloy steel, stainless steel and tool steel. These are external learning resources, not specifications for purchasing or heat treatment. Practice reading a material description"},
{"title":"Manufacturing turns materials into useful products","page":"What Is Manufacturing?","summary":"Turn materials into useful products by choosing processes that fit the material, quantity, cost and required quality.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/what-is-manufacturing.html#manufacturing-turns-materials-into-useful-products","text":"Manufacturing turns materials into useful products Manufacturing changes materials into parts and products people can use. A board becomes a shelf, plastic pellets become a bottle, and metal sheet becomes a bracket. Shape, properties, joints and surfaces all contribute to the result. A process is an operation that makes one of those changes. Most products need several processes. A useful plan states what each operation must achieve and how the result will be checked. Two organizing questions keep the subject manageable: What is the material? Use the four material families. What change are we making? Use the five process categories."},
{"title":"Primary processing makes usable material; secondary processing makes parts","page":"What Is Manufacturing?","summary":"Turn materials into useful products by choosing processes that fit the material, quantity, cost and required quality.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/what-is-manufacturing.html#primary-processing-makes-usable-material-secondary-processing-makes-parts","text":"Primary processing makes usable material; secondary processing makes parts Primary processing converts raw resources into usable materials and common supply forms. Secondary processing turns those materials into parts or products. Stock means material supplied for further work, such as sheet, rod, boards or pellets. Metal Ore is refined into metal and made into stock such as sheet; sheet is then cut and bent into a bracket. Plastic Chemical feedstocks are made into polymer resin, often supplied as pellets; pellets are then molded into a bottle cap. Wood Logs are sawn into lumber; boards are then cut, joined and finished as furniture. Glass Mineral ingredients are melted and made into sheet glass; sheets are then cut and edge-finished for a window. These terms describe a material's place in the supply chain. They do not replace the five process categories: sawing a log and sawing a furniture part are both separating. Why the boundary needs context Different industries draw the primary/secondary boundary differently. Here, use the raw-resource-to-stock and stock-to-part distinction. Extraction supplies the raw resource; mining alone does not turn ore into an ingot. A supplier's finished product can also be the next manufacturer's starting material."},
{"title":"Choose a process that fits material, quantity, cost and quality","page":"What Is Manufacturing?","summary":"Turn materials into useful products by choosing processes that fit the material, quantity, cost and required quality.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/what-is-manufacturing.html#choose-a-process-that-fits-material-quantity-cost-and-quality","text":"Choose a process that fits material, quantity, cost and quality Material Can it tolerate the heat, force or chemical exposure? A process that bends metal sheet may crack a ceramic plate. Quantity A few parts favor flexible methods with little dedicated tooling. Repeated production can justify a mold or other tool made specifically for that part. Cost Count material, setup, tooling, labor, processing time, scrap and inspection. Cheap starting material can be expensive to turn into an acceptable part. Required quality Specify dimensions, allowable variation, strength and surface condition. Choose a route that can meet them consistently, then verify it. For a small plastic cover, 3D printing may be useful for a few trial pieces; molding may suit repeated production after the design settles. Neither route wins from quantity alone: material behavior, geometry and acceptance requirements still matter. Prototype, batch and mass production describe production situations, not universal numerical cutoffs. Manufacturing economics and design for manufacturing develop this comparison further. Use Measurement Language to state dimensions clearly. 5S Workplace Organization explains how organized tools and materials support repeatable work."},
{"title":"Read every material unit through the same six-stage map","page":"What Is Manufacturing?","summary":"Turn materials into useful products by choosing processes that fit the material, quantity, cost and required quality.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/what-is-manufacturing.html#read-every-material-unit-through-the-same-six-stage-map","text":"Read every material unit through the same six-stage map Characteristics → Forming → Separating → Fabricating → Conditioning → Finishing Characteristics: What is the material like? Compare properties, useful forms and limitations. Forming: How can it take a shape? Separating: How can material be removed or cut apart? Fabricating: How can parts be joined? Conditioning: How can its internal properties be changed? Finishing: How can its surface be protected or improved? This is the collection's learning map for the later plastics, wood and metals topics. Characteristics is the starting description, not a sixth process category. The map is not a mandatory factory sequence. Wood may be dried before cutting; a metal part may be annealed between forming steps; some surfaces are finished before assembly. A product may repeat a category or omit one."},
{"title":"Remove each grit's scratches before moving to a finer abrasive","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#remove-each-grit-s-scratches-before-moving-to-a-finer-abrasive","text":"Remove each grit's scratches before moving to a finer abrasive Start with the least coarse abrasive that removes the actual defects efficiently. Higher grit numbers generally mean finer particles within the same grading system. A possible bare-wood sequence is 80 → 120 → 150 → 180, but sound, smooth stock may start later. Stop at the grit recommended for the chosen stain or finish; 220 is not a universal destination. Each step removes the previous step's scratches. Jumping straight from a coarse grit to a very fine grit leaves deep scratches that the fine abrasive removes slowly. Inspect under light across the surface, remove dust between steps and replace clogged abrasive. Finish hand sanding along the grain where practical; machine sanding also requires control of pressure and motion. Do not use prolonged sanding to hide a poorly fitted joint. It can round edges, dish a surface or sand through a thin veneer. Remove glue residue before coloring; a smooth glue patch may still reject stain. Practice grit order"},
{"title":"Treat raised fibers, defects and open pores as different problems","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#treat-raised-fibers-defects-and-open-pores-as-different-problems","text":"Treat raised fibers, defects and open pores as different problems Water can raise loose fibers and make a previously smooth surface feel rough. When the finishing system calls for it, lightly dampen bare wood, let it dry, then sand the raised fibers lightly with the final preparation grit. This grain raising step reduces roughness under later water-based coats. Heavy sanding can expose fresh fibers and restart the problem. Preparation products do different jobs Product or step Purpose Check before using it Defect filler or putty Fill a small hole or surface defect. Some products harden and sand; others remain softer and belong after finishing. Color and stain uptake may differ from wood. Sawdust mixed with glue does not reliably stain like the surrounding wood. Grain/pore filler Fill open pores for a level surface, especially under a glossy film. Choose compatible filler and test whether it goes before or after coloring. Filling pores is optional when visible texture is wanted. Pre-stain conditioner Reduce uneven stain absorption on blotch-prone wood. It can lighten or alter the result; it does not guarantee uniform color. Follow the compatible stain system and its timing. Sanding sealer Provide a readily sanded initial coating in a compatible system. It is not required under every finish. The wrong sealer can reduce adhesion or durability. Bleaching changes wood color chemically; it is not the same as staining. Product chemistry determines the sequence, drying and any required follow-up preparation. Do not use a universal “bleach immediately after final sanding” rule. Consult Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets and Personal Protective Equipment for chemical handling and protection."},
{"title":"Use stain or dye for color, then decide what protection is needed","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#use-stain-or-dye-for-color-then-decide-what-protection-is-needed","text":"Use stain or dye for color, then decide what protection is needed Pigment stain contains fine solid particles that lodge in pores and surface texture. It can emphasize grain and partly obscure it. Dye dissolves in its carrier and can give transparent color; it can still absorb unevenly. Some commercial stains contain both pigment and dye. A gel stain has a thicker consistency and often gives more surface color control on vertical or blotch-prone work. Coloring alone is not normally the protective finish. Follow the product's application and wipe-off interval: leaving ordinary wiping stain on indefinitely is not a dependable way to make it darker. Test the complete stain-and-topcoat sequence because the clear coat changes the appearance too."},
{"title":"Compare a low-build oil finish with a protective surface film","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#compare-a-low-build-oil-finish-with-a-protective-surface-film","text":"Compare a low-build oil finish with a protective surface film Wood cross-sections compare a low-build oil treatment near the surface with a continuous film coating above the surface. Neither stops all moisture movement. Idealized finish families. Real products overlap: an oil–varnish blend can build some film, and film finishes also penetrate surface pores. Choose by the service conditions and exact formulation Finish Appearance and use Tradeoff Drying oils, such as linseed or tung oil Usually applied thinly for a low-build, grain-enhancing surface. Easy to renew in many applications, but thin oil treatments usually give less wear and spill protection than a suitable film. Drying oil cures; mineral oil does not cure the same way. Shellac A resin coating available in different colors; useful for furniture and compatible sealing applications. Repairable, but vulnerable to alcohol and heat. Check wax content and compatibility before applying another finish over it. Lacquer A family of film coatings often used for furniture and sprayed production work. Many dry quickly; conventional solvent lacquers can be repaired by re-dissolving the surface. Catalyzed products cure differently and are not equally repairable. Varnish/polyurethane Film-forming resin systems often selected for wear and spill resistance. Repair and recoating need preparation. Modern varnishes commonly use synthetic resins. Exterior protection requires an exterior formulation; “varnish” alone does not promise UV resistance. Water-based clear coatings Often chosen to retain a lighter color; may use acrylic, polyurethane or blends. Water-based identifies the carrier, not a separate guarantee of durability. Grain raising and intercoat adhesion need attention. Color change depends on both coating and wood. Wax or paint Wax offers a low-build, easily refreshed surface. Paint supplies opaque color in a film. Wax alone offers limited protection. Paint hides grain and needs compatible preparation, primer where specified, and a suitable topcoat system. Names such as “Danish oil” or “tung oil finish” may describe blends rather than pure oil. Read the product description. No ordinary finish makes wood completely moisture-proof or eliminates the movement allowance in Wood Joinery and Fasteners."},
{"title":"Match application method and recoat timing to the product","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#match-application-method-and-recoat-timing-to-the-product","text":"Match application method and recoat timing to the product Brushing suits many small assemblies; use a compatible brush and maintain a wet edge without repeatedly working a coating that has begun to set. Wiping gives thin, controllable applications when the product is designed for it. Spraying can coat complex shapes evenly but needs suitable equipment, setup and the safety controls in Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets. Roller coating suits suitable products on broad surfaces. Industrial curtain coating passes panels through a flowing sheet of finish; dip coating immerses parts. Electrostatic spraying requires a suitable coating and process setup to attract charged droplets to the work; ordinary dry wood does not automatically provide that setup. Build the recommended coats and observe the recoat window. Sand between coats only as directed, with the specified abrasive and after sufficient drying. A fixed 320-grit rule does not fit every coating. Dry-to-touch is not full cure; allow the specified cure before service or final rubbing to adjust sheen."},
{"title":"Choose with a sample board and the expected wear in mind","page":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","summary":"Prepare a uniform surface, distinguish color from protection and choose a finish for appearance, wear and repair.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-finishes.html#choose-with-a-sample-board-and-the-expected-wear-in-mind","text":"Choose with a sample board and the expected wear in mind A frequently wiped surface needs a finish rated for that use; a lightly handled decorative object may favor appearance and easy renewal. Compare resistance to water, cleaning products, abrasion and heat, then consider color, sheen, repairability and available application equipment. A durable film is useful only if it adheres and cures properly. Prepare a sample of the same wood with the intended sanding sequence. Test any conditioner, filler and colorant together with the topcoat. Record products, grits, application method and drying intervals. Inspect color, texture and adhesion after the specified cure. Choose the complete system before coating the finished assembly. Further product-specific preparation guidance General Finishes' wood preparation guide is a starting point for that manufacturer's systems. Use the exact product's current instructions to choose the final grit and recoat schedule. Practice finish selection"},
{"title":"Choose the connection for the load and the grain direction","page":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","summary":"Choose joints and fasteners by the load, glue surfaces, assembly access and movement the connection must allow.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-joinery.html#choose-the-connection-for-the-load-and-the-grain-direction","text":"Choose the connection for the load and the grain direction Joining separate parts is Fabricating. Start with the direction a load will push or pull, then choose the surfaces that carry it. A shelf needs support underneath; a frame must resist being pushed out of square. A joint that works well for one can be a poor choice for the other. There is no single strongest joint. Wood species, dimensions, grain direction, fit, adhesive and loading all matter. A snug fit locates parts; it should not require enough force to split them. Most glued joinery is not an engineering interference fit. Use the wood-as-a-material topic for grain and moisture movement. Use Gluing and Clamping Wood for bond preparation and Drying and Conditioning Wood before final fitting."},
{"title":"Recognize the shoulders, sockets and mating surfaces","page":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","summary":"Choose joints and fasteners by the load, glue surfaces, assembly access and movement the connection must allow.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-joinery.html#recognize-the-shoulders-sockets-and-mating-surfaces","text":"Recognize the shoulders, sockets and mating surfaces Six separated joint diagrams: butt, rabbet, dado, half-lap, miter, and mortise and tenon. Arrows show how the parts meet. Schematic exploded profiles, not cutting dimensions. The colored parts remain separate so the contact surfaces are visible. Simple joints: geometry, use and limits Joint What it is and where it fits Strength consideration Butt One unshaped end or edge meets another piece. Common in simple assemblies. An end-to-face butt joint offers little resistance to opening without reinforcement. A long-grain edge-to-edge panel joint is a different, often effective glued connection. Rabbet A step cut along an edge or end receives another part; useful for cabinet backs and box corners. The shoulder locates the part and carries load in one direction. Glue or fasteners resist separation. Dado A channel across the grain receives a shelf or divider. The bottom and shoulders provide bearing and location. Too deep a cut weakens the remaining side panel. Groove A channel along the grain; useful for a panel edge or drawer bottom. A groove can retain a panel while allowing it to move. Do not automatically glue a solid panel all around. Lap Parts overlap. In a half-lap, material is removed from each so the assembled faces are flush. A fitted half-lap can give broad glue contact in frames; the cut also reduces each member's section. Miter Angled ends meet, often two 45° cuts for a 90° frame corner. Hides end grain, but an unreinforced corner is vulnerable to opening. A spline, dowel or other reinforcement can help."},
{"title":"Add glue area, alignment or mechanical interlock where needed","page":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","summary":"Choose joints and fasteners by the load, glue surfaces, assembly access and movement the connection must allow.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-joinery.html#add-glue-area-alignment-or-mechanical-interlock-where-needed","text":"Add glue area, alignment or mechanical interlock where needed Reinforced and interlocking connections Joint Geometry and typical use What to check Mortise and tenon A projecting tongue, the tenon, enters a matching socket, the mortise. Used in rails and legs. Long-grain cheeks provide glue area; shoulders help resist racking (being pushed out of square). Keep enough wood around the socket. Dowel A separate round wooden pin enters matching holes in both parts; used in furniture frames and alignment. Hole position, depth, dowel size and bonded embedment determine performance. A misaligned hole can prevent the shoulders from closing. Biscuit A thin oval insert enters matching slots; useful for aligning panel edges during assembly. Do not assume an alignment biscuit gives the same reinforcement as a deep dowel or tenon. Pocket screw A screw driven through an angled recess joins one part to another; common in face frames. Use the matching screw and jig setting. Clamp the parts against shifting; leave enough wood for the head and threads. Box/finger Alternating rectangular fingers mesh at a corner; common in boxes. Many glue surfaces resist separation when properly bonded. Straight fingers do not lock against withdrawal as dovetails do. Here “finger” means a box corner, not an industrial end-splice. Dovetail Flared tails fit between matching pins; common at drawer corners. The taper mechanically resists withdrawal in one direction. Thin pins, short grain or poor fit can still fail; orientation matters. Practice choosing a shelf joint"},
{"title":"Give screw threads something to grip and the head something to pull","page":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","summary":"Choose joints and fasteners by the load, glue surfaces, assembly access and movement the connection must allow.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-joinery.html#give-screw-threads-something-to-grip-and-the-head-something-to-pull","text":"Give screw threads something to grip and the head something to pull A pilot hole guides the screw and reduces splitting in the receiving piece. A clearance hole lets the screw pass freely through the near piece so its head can pull that piece against the far one. A countersink is a tapered seat for a matching flat head. These holes do different jobs. Choose pilot diameter from the screw maker's guidance for the wood and screw. A hole that is too small risks splitting; one that is too large removes thread engagement. Threads gripping both pieces before the gap closes can hold the pieces apart. Use wood screws intended for the application. Drywall screws are intended to attach gypsum board; their head geometry and often brittle behavior make them a poor default for furniture or load-bearing wood joints. Purpose-designed structural screws also differ from ordinary wood screws. Match corrosion protection to the environment and any wood treatment. Nails are quick to install and many tolerate bending better than hardened screws, but withdrawal resistance depends on shank form and grain direction. Brads are slender finish nails with small heads: useful for light trim and positioning, not a substitute for a designed load-carrying joint. For repeated disassembly, consider bolts with nuts, cross-dowels, threaded inserts or cam connectors. These knock-down fittings use reusable mating hardware. Capacity still depends on the wood bearing around the fitting, edge distance and the specific hardware, not merely on having metal threads."},
{"title":"Let a wide solid-wood part move across its grain","page":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","summary":"Choose joints and fasteners by the load, glue surfaces, assembly access and movement the connection must allow.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-joinery.html#let-a-wide-solid-wood-part-move-across-its-grain","text":"Let a wide solid-wood part move across its grain A tabletop fastened rigidly across its width can split or distort as moisture changes. Use a connection designed to permit movement, such as tabletop clips or correctly oriented slotted screw holes. Locate the part while leaving travel in the direction it needs; a screw tightened so hard that nothing can slide defeats the slot. A frame-and-panel assembly can hold a solid panel in grooves without gluing the panel's perimeter. A manufactured panel may need a different detail: do not assume every wood product moves like solid wood. Identify the grain direction and the expected load in each part. Dry-fit the joint and confirm that shoulders close without splitting or distortion. Check fastener access, edge distance and the intended disassembly method. Confirm that the attachment leaves room for cross-grain movement. Practice allowing movement"},
{"title":"Wood belongs to the Wood & Composites family","page":"Wood Structure and Species","summary":"Read tree structure, distinguish botanical groups, and choose species for their properties rather than their names.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-structure-and-species.html#wood-belongs-to-the-wood-composites-family","text":"Wood belongs to the Wood & Composites family Wood is a cellular material whose properties depend on direction. Long cells run mainly along the trunk; their arrangement helps explain why a board carries loads and splits differently along and across its grain. In the material-family model, wood belongs to Wood & Composites. Engineered wood products combine veneers, strands, particles, or fibers with binders. These products are why composites share this family. See Engineered Wood Products for the product choices; the material families and manufacturing process categories are foundation topics. What makes the cell walls work together? Cellulose reinforces the cell wall, while lignin and hemicelluloses help form its surrounding structure. This is a useful natural-composite analogy, but a tree is more complex than fibers suspended in glue. Cell arrangement, moisture, knots, and grain direction all affect a real board."},
{"title":"Read a trunk from bark to heartwood","page":"Wood Structure and Species","summary":"Read tree structure, distinguish botanical groups, and choose species for their properties rather than their names.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-structure-and-species.html#read-a-trunk-from-bark-to-heartwood","text":"Read a trunk from bark to heartwood Trunk cross-section: outer bark, inner bark, thin cambium, sapwood, heartwood, central pith, growth rings and radial rays. Bark Outer bark protects the tree. Inner bark carries sugars made by the leaves. Cambium A thin growing layer produces new wood toward the inside and new inner bark toward the outside. Sapwood The outer wood conducts water and contains living storage cells. Many of its conducting cells are already dead at maturity. Heartwood Older inner wood no longer conducts water. Deposits can change its color and decay resistance; heartwood is not automatically stronger or decay-resistant in every species. Pith and rays The pith marks the central stem tissue. Rays run radially across the wood and contribute to storage and transport across the trunk. A growth ring records a growth cycle. In many temperate trees, earlywood develops earlier in the season and latewood later. Their cells differ in size and wall thickness, creating visible bands. Ring contrast varies by species; ring width alone is not a strength grade."},
{"title":"Hardwood and softwood describe the tree, not its hardness","page":"Wood Structure and Species","summary":"Read tree structure, distinguish botanical groups, and choose species for their properties rather than their names.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-structure-and-species.html#hardwood-and-softwood-describe-the-tree-not-its-hardness","text":"Hardwood and softwood describe the tree, not its hardness Hardwoods come from angiosperm trees; softwoods come from gymnosperm trees, commonly conifers. Leaf shedding is not the defining test: some hardwood trees are evergreen, and some conifers shed their needles. Balsa is a very soft hardwood. Southern yellow pine is a softwood but can be much harder than balsa. Ask about the species and the property you need, rather than treating the category name as a test result. How the cell structure differs Most commercial hardwoods have vessels, visible as pores in end grain, along with supporting fibers. Softwoods rely mainly on long cells called tracheids for support and water transport. These differences influence appearance and processing, but do not make every member of either group behave alike. Practice: balsa Practice: yellow pine"},
{"title":"Match common species to the job","page":"Wood Structure and Species","summary":"Read tree structure, distinguish botanical groups, and choose species for their properties rather than their names.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-structure-and-species.html#match-common-species-to-the-job","text":"Match common species to the job These are typical starting points, not grades or guaranteed design values. Inspect the actual board for grain, knots, moisture, and defects. Common North American woods and familiar uses Species or group Typical character Common uses Pine Varies widely: eastern white pine is relatively light and soft; southern yellow pines are generally denser. Trim, construction lumber, furniture parts. Yellow-poplar (tulip-poplar) Relatively soft hardwood; easy to machine and often painted. Not the same tree as aspen. Painted trim, cabinet parts, concealed furniture parts. Red oak Hard, prominent open pores and grain. Flooring, furniture, cabinetry. White oak Hard, prominent rays; heartwood is generally more decay-resistant than red oak heartwood. Furniture, flooring, cooperage such as barrels. Hard maple Hard, fine texture, good wear resistance. Flooring, work surfaces, furniture. Black walnut Dark heartwood and valued figure; sapwood is lighter. Furniture, cabinetry, decorative veneer. Black cherry Fine texture; heartwood darkens with exposure. Furniture, trim, veneer. Yellow birch Hard, fine texture; often used as veneer. Cabinetry, furniture, plywood faces. Continue with Grain and Moisture Movement before selecting a board by appearance alone."},
{"title":"Further reading on wood properties and products","page":"Wood Structure and Species","summary":"Read tree structure, distinguish botanical groups, and choose species for their properties rather than their names.","collection":"Materials and Manufacturing Processes","url":"materials-and-processes/wood-structure-and-species.html#further-reading-on-wood-properties-and-products","text":"Further reading on wood properties and products Reference resources USDA Forest Products Laboratory: Wood Handbook (2021), wood structure and species American Hardwood Information Center: species guide"},
{"title":"G90 gives a destination; G91 gives a signed change","page":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","summary":"Convert between positions and signed moves, and trace the endpoint of a program as its distance mode changes.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/absolute-and-incremental-positioning.html#g90-gives-a-destination-g91-gives-a-signed-change","text":"G90 gives a destination; G91 gives a signed change In common milling usage, G90 makes axis values absolute coordinates in the active coordinate system. G91 makes them signed changes from the current position. Neither command moves an axis by itself. Units and work offsets still matter. Increment = next position − current position. Reverse the calculation with next position = current position + increment. Keep a separate running total for every axis. For an ordinary G00/G01 move, an omitted axis stays in place. In G90, X0 requests X zero; in G91, X0 requests zero X movement. Do not reuse an omitted incremental value from the preceding block. These G90/G91 meanings must not be assumed on every lathe. Some turning controls use X/Z for absolute and U/W for incremental coordinates, and use G90 for a turning cycle. Check the control before translating a mill program to a lathe."},
{"title":"One path can be written as points or as moves","page":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","summary":"Convert between positions and signed moves, and trace the endpoint of a program as its distance mode changes.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/absolute-and-incremental-positioning.html#one-path-can-be-written-as-points-or-as-moves","text":"One path can be written as points or as moves Start at A = (6,4) mm. Visit B = (26,4), C = (26,19), then D = (11,19). Z is unchanged; these are coordinate exercises with no cutting setup implied. Move Absolute destination, G90 Increment, G91 A → B X26 Y4 X20 Y0 B → C X26 Y19 X0 Y15 C → D X11 Y19 X−15 Y0 Path from A 6,4 to B 26,4 to C 26,19 to D 11,19. Signed moves are plus 20 in X, plus 15 in Y, then minus 15 in X. Check the conversion in both directions Subtract endpoints: (26−6,4−4) = (20,0); (26−26,19−4) = (0,15); (11−26,19−19) = (−15,0). Add those changes to A: (6+20+0−15,4+0+15+0) = (11,19). The net move is (5,15), not the final absolute position. Practice point-list conversion"},
{"title":"Trace the active mode before evaluating each block","page":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","summary":"Convert between positions and signed moves, and trace the endpoint of a program as its distance mode changes.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/absolute-and-incremental-positioning.html#trace-the-active-mode-before-evaluating-each-block","text":"Trace the active mode before evaluating each block Assume millimetres, one unchanged work coordinate system, no compensation and an initial position (6,4,12). This is a paper trace, not a machine-ready program. F120 is an arithmetic example. G21 G90 G94 G01 X26 Y4 F120 G91 Y15 X-15 G90 X8 M30 Motion block Mode Position afterward (mm) G01 X26 Y4 F120 Absolute (26,4,12) G91 Y15 Incremental (26,19,12) X−15 Incremental; G01 remains active (11,19,12) G90 X8 Absolute; G01 remains active (8,19,12) M30 ends this trace; it is not a command to move to zero. For arcs, the endpoint mode and centre mode are separate questions: see G-code and Toolpaths. For a broader view of modes use CNC Program Structure. Practice a program endpoint"},
{"title":"NC and CNC put machining motions under program control","page":"Automated Manufacturing Cells","summary":"See how programmed machining, tool changes, pallet handling and robots work together—and what must be reliable before unattended production makes sense.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/automated-manufacturing.html#nc-and-cnc-put-machining-motions-under-program-control","text":"NC and CNC put machining motions under program control Numerical control (NC) directs machine actions using coded instructions. Computer numerical control (CNC) uses a computer-based controller to execute those instructions. This is a base for automation, not a promise that loading, inspection and fault recovery are automatic too. Programs still depend on correct tools, work location, offsets and a verified setup. See G-code and Toolpaths and CNC Control Fundamentals for the controls and program details."},
{"title":"Tool changers and pallet changers automate different waits","page":"Automated Manufacturing Cells","summary":"See how programmed machining, tool changes, pallet handling and robots work together—and what must be reliable before unattended production makes sense.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/automated-manufacturing.html#tool-changers-and-pallet-changers-automate-different-waits","text":"Tool changers and pallet changers automate different waits An automatic tool changer exchanges cutting tools so a program can perform several operations. Correct tool identification, dimensions and available tool life remain essential. A pallet changer exchanges work carriers. In a suitable system, one pallet can be loaded at a designated station while another is being machined. The pallet locates the fixture; the fixture locates the part. Dirt at either interface can undo the expected repeatability. Repeatable fixturing and planned inspection are part of the automation system, not tasks that disappear after a changer is installed."},
{"title":"A manufacturing cell coordinates machines and handling","page":"Automated Manufacturing Cells","summary":"See how programmed machining, tool changes, pallet handling and robots work together—and what must be reliable before unattended production makes sense.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/automated-manufacturing.html#a-manufacturing-cell-coordinates-machines-and-handling","text":"A manufacturing cell coordinates machines and handling A flexible manufacturing cell combines machining and handling resources arranged to produce a planned family of parts. Flexibility comes from suitable programs, tools, fixtures and scheduling; it does not mean any part can be run without preparation. A loading robot uses a programmed manipulator and a gripping device to transfer parts between defined positions. The cell must confirm conditions such as part presence, clamp state and machine readiness before the next action. A robot's repeatable motion does not compensate for a wrongly seated part. Cell, system and integration describe different scopes A flexible manufacturing system may coordinate multiple machines or cells with material handling and scheduling. Computer-integrated manufacturing is the broader idea of connecting manufacturing information and activities. These terms overlap in use, but are not interchangeable names for one machine configuration."},
{"title":"Lights-out production requires a plan for faults and variation","page":"Automated Manufacturing Cells","summary":"See how programmed machining, tool changes, pallet handling and robots work together—and what must be reliable before unattended production makes sense.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/automated-manufacturing.html#lights-out-production-requires-a-plan-for-faults-and-variation","text":"Lights-out production requires a plan for faults and variation Lights-out machining means planned operation without an operator continuously attending the machines. It relies on a validated process, sufficient material and tool life, dependable chip and coolant management, and a defined response to faults. Monitoring may detect tool breakage, missing parts or measurement trends, but only for conditions the system is designed to detect. Automatic compensation and inspection of every part are options, not universal features of automation. Decide what is measured, what stops production and how affected parts are contained. Unattended operation is a system-level decision under the applicable equipment and facility procedures. These concepts are not authorization to leave a machine running unattended or enter an automated cell. A stopped cycle alone does not establish safe access."},
{"title":"Distinguish a thermal cut from an erosive cut","page":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","summary":"Compare focused light, moving water and a plasma arc by material, thickness, edge quality and thermal effects.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/beam-and-jet-cutting.html#distinguish-a-thermal-cut-from-an-erosive-cut","text":"Distinguish a thermal cut from an erosive cut Laser and plasma use concentrated heat. Waterjet uses mechanical energy. A heat-affected zone (HAZ) is material beside a cut whose properties or structure have changed from heating without being melted away. Three cut sections: laser light and a plasma arc make thermal cuts; an abrasive waterjet erodes a cut without a thermal heat-affected zone. Conceptual cross-sections, not a scale comparison of kerf or HAZ widths. Kerf means the width removed by a cut."},
{"title":"Laser cutting concentrates light into a small region","page":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","summary":"Compare focused light, moving water and a plasma arc by material, thickness, edge quality and thermal effects.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/beam-and-jet-cutting.html#laser-cutting-concentrates-light-into-a-small-region","text":"Laser cutting concentrates light into a small region A focused laser beam supplies thermal energy; cutting gas commonly helps remove material. Laser cutting is a candidate for fine profiles in sheet material. Metals, wood and selected plastics require suitable laser systems; a machine that cuts acrylic is not automatically a metal cutter. A narrow kerf and fine detail are possible, particularly in thinner material. Edge finish, taper and dimensional accuracy depend on material, thickness, focus, gas and cutting speed. Melting, discoloration or charring may affect the edge. Do not assume the HAZ is zero. Why the material and laser must be matched Different materials absorb different wavelengths differently. Power and beam delivery also limit the usable thickness and quality. See TRUMPF's laser-cutting overview."},
{"title":"Waterjet cuts soft materials; added abrasive cuts hard materials","page":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","summary":"Compare focused light, moving water and a plasma arc by material, thickness, edge quality and thermal effects.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/beam-and-jet-cutting.html#waterjet-cuts-soft-materials-added-abrasive-cuts-hard-materials","text":"Waterjet cuts soft materials; added abrasive cuts hard materials Pure waterjet uses a high-speed water stream for materials such as foam, rubber and other soft sheet. Abrasive waterjet adds hard particles to erode metals, stone, non-tempered glass and many ceramics. Electrical conductivity is not required. Waterjet normally creates no thermal HAZ. It is a candidate for thick stock or a heat-sensitive edge, but thickness still affects speed and cut quality. Taper, where the cut width changes through the thickness, and striations, visible lines down the cut face, can limit accuracy and finish. Slower quality cuts may reduce these effects. Brittle edges may chip and need finishing. Identify the glass type before choosing a process: tempered glass is not a candidate for ordinary waterjet cutting. A no-HAZ process is not a promise of no mechanical damage. Further reading on glass and waterjet limits OMAX explains waterjet materials and cold cutting; its glass-cutting notes distinguish glass types and edge damage."},
{"title":"Plasma is a thermal cutting candidate for conductive metal","page":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","summary":"Compare focused light, moving water and a plasma arc by material, thickness, edge quality and thermal effects.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/beam-and-jet-cutting.html#plasma-is-a-thermal-cutting-candidate-for-conductive-metal","text":"Plasma is a thermal cutting candidate for conductive metal A plasma cutter uses an electric arc through ionized gas to melt metal; the gas stream expels it. The work must conduct electricity. Steel, stainless steel and aluminum are candidates; ordinary glass and wood are not. Plasma often suits productive plate cutting where its edge quality meets the drawing or later machining is planned. Compared with a fine laser cut in thin sheet, small details can be harder to hold. Edge bevel, attached solidified metal (dross) and a HAZ may need attention. Equipment and thickness determine actual capability; plasma is not automatically a rough-only process. Compare capability on the actual thickness Hypertherm describes the energy sources and selection by material, thickness and cut quality. Qualitative comparisons narrow candidates; they do not replace a supplier's capability check. Continue with Selecting a Material-Removal Process. Safety references: Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Electrical Safety, Fire Safety and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Use the vise to make the part steady","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#use-the-vise-to-make-the-part-steady","text":"Use the vise to make the part steady A bench vise holds the part while both hands control the tool. Clamp near the working area with enough material between the jaws to resist movement. Excessive projection makes the part flex and chatter. Use suitable soft jaw covers when the ordinary jaws would mark a finished surface, and support long pieces. Keep the cut or filing area accessible. A thin-walled part may need shaped support so clamping does not distort it. Check dimensions with Choosing Shop Measuring Tools and place lines using Shop Layout and Angle Measurement. For hazards and precautions, see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment, and Metal Chips and Swarf."},
{"title":"A conventional hand hacksaw cuts on the push stroke","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#a-conventional-hand-hacksaw-cuts-on-the-push-stroke","text":"A conventional hand hacksaw cuts on the push stroke Install the blade with its teeth pointing away from the handle for the conventional push-cut setup, and tension it according to the frame's instructions. Choose pitch for the material actually crossed by the teeth: thin sections need finer teeth. Apply the three-teeth rule of thumb from Saw Blade Selection. Start a shallow groove with controlled strokes. Once established, use long, even strokes that spread the work over the blade. Apply cutting pressure on the forward stroke and ease it on the return. Keep the blade aligned with the cut instead of twisting it to steer back to the line. Ease pressure as the cut nears completion and plan support for the separated piece. Practice blade direction"},
{"title":"File cut and coarseness are separate choices","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#file-cut-and-coarseness-are-separate-choices","text":"File cut and coarseness are separate choices A file removes small chips with many teeth. Choose its shape to reach the surface: flat for a flat face, round for a round opening, or half-round for both flat and curved areas. A safe edge has no cutting teeth, allowing work close to an adjoining surface that should remain uncut. Single-cut file face has one family of diagonal teeth; double-cut face has two intersecting families. Cut pattern is not a coarseness grade. The drawing enlarges the tooth spacing for comparison. Single-cut files have one family of parallel tooth cuts; they are useful for controlled finishing and draw filing. Double-cut files have crossing cuts that divide the teeth into smaller cutting points; they commonly suit faster stock removal. Coarseness describes tooth spacing. Common grades run from rough, coarse, bastard, second-cut, smooth, to dead smooth, from coarser to finer. Availability varies. Compare grades within the same file family and length; the name alone is not a universal tooth-spacing measurement. “Second-cut” is a coarseness grade, not another name for double-cut. Practice file choice"},
{"title":"Keep the file flat and its teeth clear","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#keep-the-file-flat-and-its-teeth-clear","text":"Keep the file flat and its teeth clear Fit a suitable handle to a tanged file. For ordinary forward filing, guide the file with both hands, keep it level, apply pressure on the cutting stroke, and ease pressure on the return. Rocking rounds the edges of a surface intended to stay flat. Check progress frequently rather than filing past size. For draw filing, hold a suitable single-cut file crosswise to the work and move it along the surface, with its length across the direction of travel. Use light, controlled pressure and orient it so the teeth cut on the working stroke. This helps blend marks and refine a flat surface. Chips lodged in the teeth are called pinning; they can scratch the work. Clean with a file card, brushing with the tooth grooves to clear trapped chips. Store files so their teeth do not rub against other tools. File selection and care resources The Crescent Nicholson file selection guide covers filing methods and selection. Its file card documentation describes cleaning the teeth."},
{"title":"Remove the burr without changing the required edge","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#remove-the-burr-without-changing-the-required-edge","text":"Remove the burr without changing the required edge Deburring removes the thin unwanted lip left by cutting. Use a file for accessible straight edges, a suitable swivel-blade deburring tool for edges it can follow, or a suitable countersinking tool for a hole entrance. Match the cutter to the material and edge shape. Take light passes and inspect the result. Removing a burr does not authorize a large chamfer or a rounded edge. Preserve the drawing's dimensions and edge requirements; if an edge break is specified, make that feature deliberately."},
{"title":"Scribers mark; hammers and punches move or indent material","page":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","summary":"Hold a part in a bench vise, saw by hand, choose and clean files, and remove burrs without losing the intended shape.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/bench-work.html#scribers-mark-hammers-and-punches-move-or-indent-material","text":"Scribers mark; hammers and punches move or indent material A scriber makes a fine line with a hard point. Use it for the marking method described in Shop Layout and Angle Measurement, rather than as a substitute for a punch. A ball-peen hammer supplies controlled blows for appropriate metalworking tasks. A soft-face hammer can reduce surface marking when positioning a part; it does not guarantee that a delicate surface will be unmarked. A center punch makes an indentation for locating a drill start. A pin punch drives a pin with a flat-ended tip sized for that pin. Choose the tool for the task: the pointed center punch is not a pin driver. Detailed measuring and layout procedures belong in Shop Layout and Angle Measurement."},
{"title":"A broach divides a shaped cut among a row of teeth","page":"Broaching","summary":"See how progressive teeth cut a profile, how a guided keyway broach works, and where rotary broaching differs.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/broaching.html#a-broach-divides-a-shaped-cut-among-a-row-of-teeth","text":"A broach divides a shaped cut among a row of teeth In conventional broaching, a tool moves in a straight line relative to the work. Successive cutting teeth reach farther into the material. Rise per tooth is that increase in cutting height between successive teeth; it is not the tooth spacing along the tool. The spaces between teeth hold chips. Roughing teeth remove most of the allowance. Semi-finishing teeth approach the final profile with smaller cuts. Finishing teeth establish size and finish; several may have the same height. The exact arrangement depends on the tool. Broach teeth increase in height through roughing and semi-finishing, then repeat the final height. An enlarged pair distinguishes rise per tooth from tooth spacing. Schematic side profile. Tooth heights are exaggerated; the small teeth enter first. Why the finishing teeth need not keep getting taller Once the intended profile is reached, further rise would make the feature larger. Repeated finishing teeth can maintain the final size as earlier teeth wear. See Nidec's precision cutting tools reference for broach terminology."},
{"title":"Push or pull describes the drive; internal or external describes the surface","page":"Broaching","summary":"See how progressive teeth cut a profile, how a guided keyway broach works, and where rotary broaching differs.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/broaching.html#push-or-pull-describes-the-drive-internal-or-external-describes-the-surface","text":"Push or pull describes the drive; internal or external describes the surface A push broach is driven from behind; a pull broach is drawn through by its leading end. An internal broach works inside a prepared opening. An external broach works on an outside surface, such as a flat or an external spline. These are separate distinctions: internal does not mean pull-only. A suitable hole broach can produce a square, hexagon or internal spline; a keyway broach cuts a slot in an existing bore. Conventional through broaching needs an exit path. Choose the tool from the required finished profile and starting-hole specification, not just a nominal shape name. Repeated parts can justify dedicated tooling because many cutting edges complete the profile in a short cycle. A small keyway set can also suit repair or short-run work. Compare Material-Removal Process Families and Selecting a Material-Removal Process; Vertical Band Machining covers cutting contours with a continuous band instead. Production advantages depend on the job Tool cost, material, cut length, machine capacity and required finish determine the economics. Broaching is not automatically the cheapest process. Pilot Precision's broach overview includes both short-run sets and one-pass production tools. Cutting Fluids and Application Methods explains lubrication choices."},
{"title":"A bushing guides the keyway broach; shims move its cutting edge outward","page":"Broaching","summary":"See how progressive teeth cut a profile, how a guided keyway broach works, and where rotary broaching differs.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/broaching.html#a-bushing-guides-the-keyway-broach-shims-move-its-cutting-edge-outward","text":"A bushing guides the keyway broach; shims move its cutting edge outward The hub is the part surrounding the shaft. A slotted bushing fits its bore and guides the broach. A shim is a thin spacer behind the broach that increases the depth reached on a later pass. Broach, bushing and shims must be a compatible set. Section through a hub supported on an arbor press, with the ram above the broach, a bushing in the bore, and a shim behind the broach. Open space below permits the broach to exit. Conceptual later-pass setup, not a construction drawing or a statement of shop inventory. The shim is behind the broach, opposite its teeth. Before operation, use Equipment Operating Procedure and press and mill hazard guidance in the safety collection. Follow the specific press and broach instructions for capacity, alignment, support, stroke, cut length and lubrication. Check the drawing, prepared bore and required keyway. Select the matching broach, bushing and specified shim schedule. Seat the bushing in the hub. Insert the broach in its slot for the first pass, normally without a shim on a multi-pass set. Support the hub squarely on the press bed with clearance below for the entire broach to pass through. Align the ram, tool and guide. Apply the specified lubricant and press the broach through according to its instructions. After the completed pass, remove the broach and clean its teeth. If another pass is specified, insert the prescribed shim behind it, reinsert, lubricate and check alignment again. Repeat with the specified shim stack for each remaining pass. Stop at the prescribed final setup; deburr and inspect as described in Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats. Do not add a shim after the final pass. One-pass tools and some small broaches require none. Adding arbitrary shims is not a way to correct an uncertain dimension. Manufacturer reference for the pass sequence Pilot Precision, Engineering Section: How to Use Broaches, printed page 35 describes guided keyway broaching and the successive shim stack. The tool's own instructions govern the actual setup."},
{"title":"Count the first pass separately from the added shim passes","page":"Broaching","summary":"See how progressive teeth cut a profile, how a guided keyway broach works, and where rotary broaching differs.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/broaching.html#count-the-first-pass-separately-from-the-added-shim-passes","text":"Count the first pass separately from the added shim passes Rise per tooth and depth per pass are different quantities. For a simple planning exercise, let d 1 be the depth reached by the unshimmed pass and s the extra depth from each added shim increment. After N passes, depth is d 1 + ( N − 1) s. For a target depth D greater than d 1, the minimum count to reach at least the target is 1 + ceiling((D − d 1 )/s). Ceiling means round upward to a whole number. If the first pass already reaches the target, the count is one. All depths here are measured radially from the original bore surface. Example: an invented first-pass depth of 1.0 mm and two 0.5 mm increments give 1.0, 1.5 and 2.0 mm: three passes. This is counting practice, not a real broach specification. If rounding upward exceeds the allowed finished depth, that shim schedule cannot make the part to size. Practice counting passes"},
{"title":"Rotary broaching uses an angled end-cutting tool","page":"Broaching","summary":"See how progressive teeth cut a profile, how a guided keyway broach works, and where rotary broaching differs.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/broaching.html#rotary-broaching-uses-an-angled-end-cutting-tool","text":"Rotary broaching uses an angled end-cutting tool Rotary broaching can make polygon forms such as a hex socket using a special holder on a suitable lathe or mill. A small angular offset makes successive portions of the tool's end engage as the tool and work move relative to one another. It does not use the long row of progressively taller teeth shown above. It needs a prepared opening for internal work. Tool form, available thrust, material, depth and chip clearance limit the application; a blind socket needs a planned place for the chips. This is a process overview, not a setup procedure. How the rotary holder produces the cutting motion Slater Tools' rotary broaching explanation describes the holder's angled spindle and the different tool/work motions on a lathe and mill. Its application guide covers preparation and operating requirements."},
{"title":"A mask defines where chemical removal occurs","page":"Chemical Milling and Etching","summary":"A mask protects selected areas while an etchant dissolves exposed metal; sideways etching limits the detail.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/chemical-machining.html#a-mask-defines-where-chemical-removal-occurs","text":"A mask defines where chemical removal occurs Chemical machining uses a chemical reaction to dissolve exposed material. A protective mask covers areas that should remain. Chemical milling can make shallow recesses or reduce a metal part's weight. Photochemical etching uses a patterned resist and can produce intricate thin-sheet parts, including through openings. Unlike ECM, ordinary chemical etching does not use an external machining current between a tool and the work. Select an etchant compatible with the particular material; “chemical machining” does not name one chemistry that works on everything."},
{"title":"Allow for sideways etching beneath the mask","page":"Chemical Milling and Etching","summary":"A mask protects selected areas while an etchant dissolves exposed metal; sideways etching limits the detail.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/chemical-machining.html#allow-for-sideways-etching-beneath-the-mask","text":"Allow for sideways etching beneath the mask Etching can spread sideways beneath the mask edge as well as into the surface. This undercut limits narrow lands, corner sharpness and dimensional control, especially as etch depth increases. The mask design must compensate for the etching behavior. Surface finish depends on the material and chemistry. Chemical removal avoids a cutting-induced thermal HAZ, but that is not the same as saying the chemical bath has no temperature requirements. Why thin-sheet patterns are a useful application A mask can define many features at once without a separate shaped cutting tool for each opening. Fotofab describes partial and through etching. Compare Electrochemical Machining and Selecting a Material-Removal Process. Safety reference: Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Feed direction decides whether each chip starts thick or thin","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#feed-direction-decides-whether-each-chip-starts-thick-or-thin","text":"Feed direction decides whether each chip starts thick or thin A milling cutter takes metal off in small slices, one slice per tooth. Whether each slice starts thick and ends thin, or starts thin and ends thick, comes down to one thing: which way the work is fed past the turning cutter. Climb milling Also called down milling. Where a tooth is cutting, it moves the same way the work is fed. Each chip starts thick and ends thin. Conventional milling Also called up milling. Where a tooth is cutting, it moves against the feed. Each chip starts thin and ends thick. Feed per tooth How far the work moves while the cutter turns from one tooth to the next. It sets how thick a chip can get. Width of cut How far the cutter reaches into the side of the work, measured across the cutter. Machinists also call it the radial depth of cut. How to read the drawings on this page. Each drawing is a slice straight across the fluted part of an end mill, seen looking down its axis from the spindle; the small side view in the corner marks where the slice is taken, the way a section view does on a drawing. This sketch places the cutter's axis horizontally, with the table below it; screen-down is toward that table. It is not a top view of a vertical spindle. From the spindle end a standard right-hand cutter turns clockwise. The cutter stays where it is and the work is fed past it, the way a mill table carries the work. Every Work feed arrow is the work's motion. On a machine that moves the cutter instead, nothing changes: the cutter simply travels the opposite way to that arrow. The cutter is the same in both modes and spins the same way. What switches climb to conventional is the direction of feed relative to the side of the cutter that is doing the cutting. The down/up force arrows belong to this horizontal arrangement. A top view of a vertical end mill would show forces across the table, not axial force into or away from it."},
{"title":"Watch one tooth take its chip","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#watch-one-tooth-take-its-chip","text":"Watch one tooth take its chip The dashed gold outline is the slice of metal the cutting tooth has taken so far, drawn where it was. The solid gold shape is that metal as a chip, curling off the cutting edge into the flute. The curled chip is scaled to fit the flute at this exaggerated feed; its drawn size is not a thickness measurement. Play it, or drag the tooth through the cut, and use the dashed slice to compare thickness. Work feed turns clockwise slice seen from spindle Enters thick Leaves thin Climb milling Chip runs thick to thin Work feed turns clockwise slice seen from spindle Leaves thick Enters thin Conventional milling Chip runs thin to thick peak 87% of feed per tooth tooth enters tooth leaves position through the cut slice 0 Climb Conventional Both cutters turn clockwise, and each cutting tooth is about a third of the way through its cut. In climb milling the work feeds left and the tooth has already taken the thick end of its chip. In conventional milling the work feeds right and the tooth is still in the thin end. The graph says the same thing as a line: climb runs down from its peak, conventional rises to it. The feed per tooth is drawn many times larger than it would be on a machine so the chip can be seen at all. The dashed outline is the slice as the tooth cut it; the solid chip is the same metal curled into the flute. JavaScript is off, so the drawing is fixed at one moment. Everything it shows is also written out on this page."},
{"title":"Each chip forms on the edge, rides out in the flute and is thrown clear","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#each-chip-forms-on-the-edge-rides-out-in-the-flute-and-is-thrown-clear","text":"Each chip forms on the edge, rides out in the flute and is thrown clear A chip does not stay where the slice was. The cutting edge shears it off the work, it slides along the face in front of the edge and curls into the flute, and the flute carries it round until it is thrown clear. Where that happens depends on the direction of feed. Swipe the four moments sideways on a narrow screen, or focus the drawing and use the arrow keys to scroll. Climb milling 1 Forms on the edge 2 Finished as it leaves 3 Carried out 4 Thrown clear Conventional milling 1 Forms on the edge 2 Finished as it leaves 3 Carried out 4 Thrown clear One chip from each direction, at four moments. In climb milling the chip is finished at the finished surface, where the tooth leaves the work, and the end that curled furthest is its thick end. It is thrown out behind the cutter, over the surface that has already been cut. In conventional milling the chip is finished at the top of the cut with its thick end still on the edge, and it is thrown up and away from the uncut work. Reading the cutter Cutting edge: the tip of each tooth, the only part that touches the finished surface. The tooth that is cutting is outlined in blue. Rake face: the face just in front of the edge. The chip slides along it as it forms. Flute: the curved valley in front of each tooth. The chip curls in it and is carried out of the cut in it, so a flute packed with chips cannot take the next one. Core: the solid centre, hatched because the drawing cuts through it. In this illustrated family, more flutes leave a thicker core and less room for chips. Actual proportions depend on the tool design. The chip is drawn shorter and thinner than the slice so it fits the flute at a feed drawn many times too large. A continuous metal chip usually becomes shorter and thicker through deformation; IIT Kharagpur's chip-formation lesson explains the change. The path it takes is illustrative; coolant, tool geometry and the material affect actual chip motion. The cutter's flutes and chip space follow Harvey Performance's end mill anatomy."},
{"title":"Six things to try in the demo","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#six-things-to-try-in-the-demo","text":"Six things to try in the demo Choose Compare both and step until each tooth has only just entered the work. Which chip is already thick, and which is barely there? Drag Tooth position in the cut from 0% to 100%, once in each mode. Watch the uncut-thickness readout: in climb it runs down from the peak, in conventional it runs up to it. Set the width of cut to 50%. The tooth now cuts through a quarter of a turn and the chip reaches the full feed per tooth. Drop it to 10%: the peak falls to 60% of the feed. At the same feed, a narrower cut makes a thinner chip. Raise the feed per tooth and watch the dashed uncut slice grow thicker. Its thick-to-thin trend stays the same. The curled chip is scaled to fit the flute and may stop growing on screen; compare thickness using the dashed slice. Follow one chip from start to finish. Pause just before a tooth leaves the work, then step forward. In which mode is the thick end of the chip at the curled tip, and in which is it still on the edge? Where does each chip go once it is free? Watch the red-orange arrow, the pull of the cut on the work. In climb it pulls the work the way it is already going and presses it down. In conventional it pushes back against the feed and lifts. Keep that in mind for the card on backlash."},
{"title":"Starting thin means rubbing; starting thick means a bite","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#starting-thin-means-rubbing-starting-thick-means-a-bite","text":"Starting thin means rubbing; starting thick means a bite A real cutting edge has a rounded tip. When the uncut layer is extremely thin, the edge can push and rub the metal before a chip forms. The peripheral cuts shown here make that difference visible. Conventional milling starts every chip at zero, so every tooth rubs before it bites. Rubbing makes heat and wears the edge. Look at where the tooth enters, too: on the finished surface, the one you are keeping. Climb milling starts the chips shown here at their thickest, so the tooth bites at once, with a knock each time a tooth enters. That is the main reason climb milling is often the preferred direction when the machine can hold the work steady against it. Whether it can is the next card. Which way the cut pulls on the work Climb: the teeth drag the work along the way it is already being fed, and tend to push it down onto the table in the peripheral arrangement shown. Conventional: the teeth push back against the feed and tend to lift the work in this arrangement, so whatever holds the work has to resist that lift."},
{"title":"Backlash: why climb milling depends on the machine","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#backlash-why-climb-milling-depends-on-the-machine","text":"Backlash: why climb milling depends on the machine A feed screw moves the table by turning inside a nut. Unless the drive is built to take it out, there is some clearance between the screw's thread and the nut. That clearance is called backlash, and it lets the table shift a little while the screw stands still. Conventional The cut pulls against the feed: the nut stays on the driving flank. Table feed Table feed screw gap pull of the cut Climb The cut pulls with the feed: the nut can jump the gap. Table feed Table feed screw gap pull of the cut The clearance is drawn far larger than it is, and a real drive engages many turns of thread, not one. What matters is which side of the gap the nut ends up on. Conventional: the cut pushes the table back, against the feed. That holds the nut against the flank of the thread the screw is driving with, so the gap stays on the far side and the table moves only as fast as the screw turns. Climb: the cut pulls the table forward, the way it is already moving. If there is clearance, the table can slide across it ahead of the screw, and the cutter takes a bigger bite than the feed was set for, all at once. That sudden, oversized bite is hard on the cutter and on whatever is holding the work. This is a question to ask about a machine, not a rule. How much clearance a feed drive has, and whether it is built to take it out, differs from machine to machine. This page does not tell you which direction to use on any machine you will meet. Ask the person responsible for that machine. Why some machines climb mill without trouble A feed drive can be designed to reduce backlash, for example with a preloaded ball nut. The important question is whether the machine controls that movement, not simply whether it is called CNC. The chip is the same shape either way; what changes is whether the machine can hold the table against the pull of the cut. Sources: Sandvik Coromant: down milling versus up milling explains chip formation, rubbing and the backlash condition. Harvey Performance: climb versus conventional milling describes the lifting and downward force tendencies in peripheral horizontal milling. The arrows illustrate directions; they do not calculate force magnitudes."},
{"title":"A slot is both at once","page":"Climb and Conventional Milling","summary":"Which way the work is fed past a turning cutter decides whether each chip starts thick or thin, which way the cut pulls on the work, and why backlash in a feed screw matters.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/climb-and-conventional-milling.html#a-slot-is-both-at-once","text":"A slot is both at once When the cutter is buried across its whole width, cutting a slot, each tooth enters at one wall and leaves at the other. Its chip starts thin, is thickest halfway across, and ends thin. The first half of the sweep moves against the feed, like conventional milling, and the second half moves with it, like climb milling, whichever way the work is fed. That is why the demo stops at half the cutter's diameter: past that point the two can no longer be pulled apart."},
{"title":"Describe tool motion relative to the work with a right-handed frame","page":"CNC Axes and Offsets","summary":"Identify mill and lathe axes, distinguish machine and work zero, and separate work offsets from tool compensation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-axes-and-offsets.html#describe-tool-motion-relative-to-the-work-with-a-right-handed-frame","text":"Describe tool motion relative to the work with a right-handed frame Use your right hand: thumb along +X, index finger along +Y and middle finger along +Z, with all three perpendicular. Equivalently, curling from +X toward +Y makes the thumb point along +Z. Z runs parallel to the principal spindle axis. On a conventional vertical mill viewed from the front, +X is to the right, +Y is toward the back and +Z raises the tool away from the work. These are tool-relative-to-work directions. If the table moves instead of the tool, its physical motion is opposite the tool-relative motion. Vertical mill: tool-relative X right, Y back, Z up. Basic lathe: Z along the spindle away from the headstock, X radially outward on the illustrated tool side. For a basic lathe, Z is along the spindle and +Z is away from the headstock. X is radial; positive X values commonly describe the tool side of the centreline. A slant bed or rear turret changes what this looks like from the operator's position. Do not infer axis signs from screen orientation. Two-axis turning normally uses the XZ plane."},
{"title":"A lathe X value may describe diameter or radius","page":"CNC Axes and Offsets","summary":"Identify mill and lathe axes, distinguish machine and work zero, and separate work offsets from tool compensation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-axes-and-offsets.html#a-lathe-x-value-may-describe-diameter-or-radius","text":"A lathe X value may describe diameter or radius In diameter programming, X24 describes a surface 24 mm in diameter, 12 mm from the spindle centreline. In radius programming, the same surface is X12. Z distances are not halved. For a change from diameter 30 mm to diameter 26 mm, the X coordinate changes by −4 mm in diameter mode, but the tool moves only 2 mm radially inward. Confirm the control's convention for absolute X, incremental X and arc-centre words separately; do not halve every number in a turning block. Practice diameter and radius"},
{"title":"Machine zero belongs to the machine; work zero belongs to the setup","page":"CNC Axes and Offsets","summary":"Identify mill and lathe axes, distinguish machine and work zero, and separate work offsets from tool compensation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-axes-and-offsets.html#machine-zero-belongs-to-the-machine-work-zero-belongs-to-the-setup","text":"Machine zero belongs to the machine; work zero belongs to the setup Machine zero is the machine coordinate origin. A reference-return or homing procedure establishes the machine's position reference; the home position need not be the coordinate origin. Work zero is a chosen datum on the part or fixture, such as a corner, hole centre or faced end. Common milling codes G54 through G59 select stored work coordinate systems. Selecting G54 does not measure the part, write the offset table or redefine machine zero. The setup procedure establishes the stored values. Simplified XY translation: work zero is at machine coordinates 100,60. A work point 20,15 is at machine coordinates 120,75. With only a translation, machine position = work-zero location in machine coordinates + work position. If the work origin is (100,60) and the program point is (20,15), the machine point is (120,75). This example omits rotation, local shifts and tool offsets. In common Fanuc-style milling, G53 selects machine coordinates for the block containing it. It does not erase G54 or force G54 to become the next active work system. The previously selected work system remains selected afterward. Tool-offset interaction and permitted motion modes are control-specific; check your control's manual. Machine-coordinate zero is not necessarily a clearance position."},
{"title":"Tool length and cutter radius compensate for different dimensions","page":"CNC Axes and Offsets","summary":"Identify mill and lathe axes, distinguish machine and work zero, and separate work offsets from tool compensation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-axes-and-offsets.html#tool-length-and-cutter-radius-compensate-for-different-dimensions","text":"Tool length and cutter radius compensate for different dimensions In common Fanuc-style milling, G43 H... applies positive tool-length compensation: it adds the signed value stored in the selected H register to commanded Z positions so Z can refer to the intended tool-tip position. The offset's sign, established by the touch-off convention, determines the direction of that shift. G43 does not mean an unconditional upward move. Check your control's manual for its offset and touch-off conventions. For example, H03 selects register 3; it does not mean a length of 3 mm. T selects a tool; H selects offset data. Their numbers may match by shop convention, but the programmer must verify that relationship. G49 commonly cancels length compensation. Why positive compensation can use a negative offset Positive compensation describes addition, not the sign of the stored number: adding a negative value shifts the compensated Z position in the negative direction. The Haas mill programming workbook's tool-length compensation section explains G43's addition of the selected offset to commanded positions. G41 offsets a cutter to the left of the programmed path looking in the direction of travel; G42 offsets it to the right. G40 cancels it. These are not clockwise/counterclockwise commands. Moving along +X in XY, left is +Y. On many mills a D word selects cutter compensation data; check whether that register stores radius, diameter or a wear adjustment. Compensation needs suitable entry and exit moves. Do not add a full radius correction to a CAM path that already includes that correction. Turning tool nose compensation also depends on nose radius and tool orientation; it is not simply a milling H offset. See G-code and Toolpaths for tool-centre paths without compensation. Tormach's PathPilot guidelines describe H-based tool offset selection; CIMCO's Fanuc milling lesson illustrates left/right compensation."},
{"title":"CNC repeats programmed motion; the operator still defines the process","page":"CNC Control Fundamentals","summary":"How a control turns a program into motion, how feedback works, and what changes between milling and turning.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-control-fundamentals.html#cnc-repeats-programmed-motion-the-operator-still-defines-the-process","text":"CNC repeats programmed motion; the operator still defines the process Computer numerical control (CNC) uses a program to command machine motion and functions. On a manual machine, the operator directs each move with handwheels or power-feed controls. On a CNC machine, the operator prepares the tools, workholding and coordinate references, then checks the programmed sequence. CNC can repeat a complex path consistently. It does not remove variation from tool wear, deflection, temperature or setup errors. Programming and setup take time, so CNC is not automatically faster for every one-off job. The machine control unit (MCU) reads program blocks, keeps track of active settings, calculates intermediate positions along a path, and sends commands to the axis drives and machine functions. The drives supply power to the motors. The control also presents positions, settings and alarms to the operator."},
{"title":"Feedback distinguishes open-loop and closed-loop motion","page":"CNC Control Fundamentals","summary":"How a control turns a program into motion, how feedback works, and what changes between milling and turning.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-control-fundamentals.html#feedback-distinguishes-open-loop-and-closed-loop-motion","text":"Feedback distinguishes open-loop and closed-loop motion System How position is controlled What that means Open loop The drive sends motion commands without measuring actual position back into that positioning loop. The control may not detect a missed step. A displayed commanded position is not proof of actual position. Closed loop A sensor reports position or motion; the drive/control compares feedback with the command and corrects error. Excessive following error can produce an alarm. Feedback does not make the mechanics error-free. A stepper motor advances through commanded angular steps and is often used in open-loop systems. A servo system uses feedback to regulate motion. Closed-loop stepper systems also exist: motor type alone does not tell you whether feedback is present. Where the sensor sits matters A motor encoder measures the motor, while a linear scale can measure the slide directly. Screw error, backlash and elastic deflection between the sensor and the cutting point may remain. Homing establishes a reference; repeating homing is not a general cure for lost motion. See Oriental Motor's closed-loop stepper overview for an example of feedback used with steppers."},
{"title":"Milling rotates the cutter; turning rotates the work","page":"CNC Control Fundamentals","summary":"How a control turns a program into motion, how feedback works, and what changes between milling and turning.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-control-fundamentals.html#milling-rotates-the-cutter-turning-rotates-the-work","text":"Milling rotates the cutter; turning rotates the work Basic machine Cutting motion Common programming concerns Three-axis vertical mill A rotating cutter moves relative to the work along X, Y and Z. Tool lengths, cutter radius, XY contours and hole locations. Two-axis lathe The work rotates while a cutting tool moves along X and Z. Diameter/radius convention, tool geometry and wear offsets, tool nose radius and changing work diameter. Machines with live tooling, extra linear axes or rotary axes extend these basic cases. A mill's tool changer and a lathe's turret do not necessarily use the same tool-selection commands. Learn the geometry in CNC Axes and Offsets, then compare Milling Operations and Lathe Operations. For cutting data use Feeds and Speeds; for milling direction use Climb and Conventional Milling."},
{"title":"G-code is a family of control dialects","page":"CNC Control Fundamentals","summary":"How a control turns a program into motion, how feedback works, and what changes between milling and turning.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-control-fundamentals.html#g-code-is-a-family-of-control-dialects","text":"G-code is a family of control dialects These pages describe common Fanuc-style milling usage, with turning differences called out. G- and M-code meanings vary by control, machine type, options and configuration. Check the machine's own manual before applying a code or example. These examples teach interpretation; they are not production programs. PathPilot is Tormach's machine control. Tormach publishes PathPilot programming documentation and post-processor guidelines. Select documentation for the actual machine and software version. A general description does not establish its installed tools, options or setup. Continue with CNC Program Structure and CNC Programming and Prove-Out. Use G-code and Toolpaths to explore a deliberately limited teaching interpreter."},
{"title":"CAD describes the shape; CAM plans how to cut it","page":"CNC Programming and Prove-Out","summary":"Follow the CAD-to-CAM-to-post chain and use graphics, dry run, single block and overrides to check a program against its setup.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-proving.html#cad-describes-the-shape-cam-plans-how-to-cut-it","text":"CAD describes the shape; CAM plans how to cut it CAD supplies design geometry and drawings. CAM uses geometry plus the programmer's stock, tool, workholding, operation and cutting-data choices to calculate toolpaths. Depending on the operation, CAM may work from 2D profiles or a 3D model; a solid model is not always required. CAD geometry and dimensions flow to CAM operations and tools, then to a machine-specific post-processor, then to G-code reviewed against the actual setup. A post-processor translates CAM output into the syntax and conventions for a particular machine/control combination. Selecting the wrong post can change tool calls, arc format, offsets or startup behavior. Review the posted code as well as the CAM preview. Other ways to create a program A short program can be written directly in a text editor. Conversational programming asks for feature dimensions at the control. Manual data input (MDI) lets the operator enter commands at the control, often for immediate execution. These routes still require correct modes, geometry and setup data; they do not bypass prove-out. Tormach's post-processor guidelines identify control-specific requirements."},
{"title":"Each prove-out aid answers a different question","page":"CNC Programming and Prove-Out","summary":"Follow the CAD-to-CAM-to-post chain and use graphics, dry run, single block and overrides to check a program against its setup.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-proving.html#each-prove-out-aid-answers-a-different-question","text":"Each prove-out aid answers a different question Prove-out compares the intended operation, posted code and actual setup. Follow Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for safety requirements; this page explains program-checking concepts, not a machine operating procedure. Aid Useful for Limit Graphics or simulation Inspecting path shape, endpoints, sequence and modeled interference. A missing clamp, wrong tool model or incorrect offset can make the preview disagree with the machine. Dry run Observing a trial cycle under the control's defined test conditions. It may substitute feed rates; the name does not guarantee no motion or no contact. Use the manual's procedure. Single block Advancing through blocks while comparing position and active modes with expectations. One canned-cycle block may contain several motions. Not every internal action stops separately. Feed override Adjusting eligible feed motions during observation. It need not affect rapids, tool changes or synchronized operations. Rapid override may be a separate control."},
{"title":"Review the program against the setup before a supervised trial","page":"CNC Programming and Prove-Out","summary":"Follow the CAD-to-CAM-to-post chain and use graphics, dry run, single block and overrides to check a program against its setup.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-proving.html#review-the-program-against-the-setup-before-a-supervised-trial","text":"Review the program against the setup before a supervised trial Match the posted file and revision to the intended part, machine and post-processor. Identify the active units, plane, distance mode and feed mode at each operation. Compare work offsets, tool numbers and offset registers with the documented setup. Trace the first approach, tool changes, cycle returns and final retract against the modeled stock and fixtures. Check that the actual setup matches what the preview includes; resolve any discrepancy with the instructor before proceeding. Identify the manual's dry-run, single-block and override behavior before using those functions in a supervised trial. After an edit, repeat the affected checks; compare the first part's measured features with the drawing. Practise interpretation with G-code and Toolpaths and explore the existing 3D/WebGL milling lab in Milling Operations. A successful simulation only establishes behavior within that model. It does not approve a program for a real machine."},
{"title":"A block can combine motion, settings and machine functions","page":"CNC Program Structure","summary":"Read program identifiers, startup modes, machine functions, feed modes and drilling cycles in common milling G-code.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-structure.html#a-block-can-combine-motion-settings-and-machine-functions","text":"A block can combine motion, settings and machine functions A word is a letter and value, such as X12 or F100. A block is a line of words. The control applies its execution rules; words are not necessarily executed in left-to-right typing order. N numbers label blocks and are often optional. Many Fanuc-style programs use an O number such as O2468 as the program identifier. File naming, percent delimiters and identifiers vary by control. A modal command stays active until replaced or cancelled within its group. G01 can remain active while G90 changes to G91, because motion and distance mode are different groups. A startup line sets specific modes; it does not clear every stored setting or offset."},
{"title":"A safe-start line declares assumptions; it does not prove the setup","page":"CNC Program Structure","summary":"Read program identifiers, startup modes, machine functions, feed modes and drilling cycles in common milling G-code.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-structure.html#a-safe-start-line-declares-assumptions-it-does-not-prove-the-setup","text":"A safe-start line declares assumptions; it does not prove the setup A common milling startup example is G17 G21 G90 G94 G40 G49 G80: XY plane, millimetres, absolute endpoints, feed per minute, cutter compensation cancelled, tool-length compensation cancelled and canned cycles cancelled. This is a reading example, not a universal restart line. Cancelling an offset changes the coordinate relationship; startup and restart sequences must match the actual control and tool position. Identify the program and establish required units, plane and modes. Select the intended work coordinate system and tool; apply the correct offset data. Establish spindle, feed and coolant settings for the operation. Perform positioning and machining blocks, including any cycle cancellation. Use the machine-approved retract and shutdown sequence, then end the program. The order and motion details around tool changes and offset activation require the machine manual. See CNC Axes and Offsets and CNC Programming and Prove-Out."},
{"title":"Separate spindle, tool and coolant commands from motion","page":"CNC Program Structure","summary":"Read program identifiers, startup modes, machine functions, feed modes and drilling cycles in common milling G-code.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-structure.html#separate-spindle-tool-and-coolant-commands-from-motion","text":"Separate spindle, tool and coolant commands from motion These are common Fanuc-style meanings, not a promise for every machine. Code Common meaning Read with it M03 / M04 / M05 Spindle clockwise / counterclockwise / stop S sets the speed value. Direction is conventionally viewed from the spindle toward the work; confirm machine orientation. M06 Tool change on many mills T selects a tool. The changer may be manual or automatic; offset activation is a separate issue. M08 / M09 Flood coolant on / coolant off Available coolant systems and auxiliary M codes depend on the machine. M30 End program and commonly reset/rewind It does not command a return to machine or work zero, and does not mean automatically run another part. For example, S1800 M03 requests 1800 rpm in a fixed-rpm mode; it is not a cutting recommendation. A lathe using constant surface speed interprets S differently. See Feeds and Speeds. Practice code meanings"},
{"title":"Units, feed mode and plane answer different questions","page":"CNC Program Structure","summary":"Read program identifiers, startup modes, machine functions, feed modes and drilling cycles in common milling G-code.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-structure.html#units-feed-mode-and-plane-answer-different-questions","text":"Units, feed mode and plane answer different questions Setting Common milling interpretation G20 / G21 Inch / millimetre length units. G94 / G95 Feed in units per minute / units per spindle revolution, where supported. G17 / G18 / G19 XY / XZ / YZ plane for plane-dependent operations such as arcs. G00 / G01 Rapid positioning / straight-line motion at programmed feed. G02 / G03 Clockwise / counterclockwise arc in the selected plane. In G21 G94, F90 means 90 mm/min. In G21 G95, F0.15 means 0.15 mm/rev; at 600 rpm that corresponds to 90 mm/min. G20/G21 alone does not choose the time basis. Some lathes use G98/G99 for feed mode, while mills commonly use those codes for drilling return levels. Rapid path shape depends on the control and can differ from G01. G01 may feed through air as well as cut. Learn the motion and arc geometry in G-code and Toolpaths, including the existing simulator; learn distance modes in Absolute and Incremental Positioning."},
{"title":"G81 and G83 repeat a drilling sequence at hole locations","page":"CNC Program Structure","summary":"Read program identifiers, startup modes, machine functions, feed modes and drilling cycles in common milling G-code.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-program-structure.html#g81-and-g83-repeat-a-drilling-sequence-at-hole-locations","text":"G81 and G83 repeat a drilling sequence at hole locations A canned cycle packages repeated motions. In common milling use, G81 feeds a drill to depth then retracts; G83 divides the depth into pecks with retracts for chip clearance. X/Y locate the hole, Z specifies the drill-tip depth, R specifies a return/approach plane, F sets feed, and Q commonly specifies peck depth for G83. Q is not the final hole depth. With G90 on a conventional mill, Z and R are coordinates in the active work system. Incremental canned-cycle rules need separate study in the manual. G98 commonly returns to the initial level and G99 to R; choose a return level for the actual setup. G80 cancels the cycle. Modal hole cycles can drill again at subsequent position blocks. The simulator in G-code and Toolpaths demonstrates G81, not G83. See Tormach's programming reference for control-specific modes and cycles, and the Haas lathe manual for examples of meanings that differ from milling."},
{"title":"A rounded tool nose does not cut at the imaginary sharp tip","page":"CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles","summary":"Account for a rounded tool nose on tapers and arcs, then distinguish roughing, finishing, grooving and threading cycles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-profiles.html#a-rounded-tool-nose-does-not-cut-at-the-imaginary-sharp-tip","text":"A rounded tool nose does not cut at the imaginary sharp tip A turning insert has a rounded corner, called its nose radius. Touching off along X and Z establishes an imaginary tip: the intersection of the two tangent reference lines. There is no cutting material at that intersection. The actual contact point moves around the nose as the direction of the surface changes. Axis-parallel cuts can reach the measured diameter or face with the corresponding touch-offs. On a taper, chamfer or arc, simply driving the imaginary tip along the desired surface leaves a profile error. Account for the nose radius with a calculated toolpath or the control's tool nose compensation. See Turning Tapers for taper geometry. A rounded nose touches the X and Z reference lines, whose intersection is the imaginary tip. A taper through that tip misses the rounded edge; a parallel tangent line shows the resulting surface without compensation. Why a good diameter measurement does not prove a taper is correct The two axis touch-offs locate tangent lines, not every possible contact point on the nose. The normal to an angled surface points in another direction, so its tangency requires another offset. Haas's tool nose compensation discussion describes the resulting undercut or overcut on tapered and curved profiles."},
{"title":"Compensation needs nose radius, tip orientation and a clear entry and exit","page":"CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles","summary":"Account for a rounded tool nose on tapers and arcs, then distinguish roughing, finishing, grooving and threading cycles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-profiles.html#compensation-needs-nose-radius-tip-orientation-and-a-clear-entry-and-exit","text":"Compensation needs nose radius, tip orientation and a clear entry and exit In common Fanuc-style usage, G41 selects compensation to the left of the programmed direction of travel and G42 to the right; G40 cancels it. Judge the side in the control's XZ-plane convention. Do not choose a code from the screen's apparent left or right, or assume one code means every outside cut. Enter the actual nose radius and the control's tip orientation, which tells it where the imaginary tip sits relative to the nose centre. Select the correct tool-offset register, use the manual's lead-in and lead-out requirements, and check the resulting path with CNC Programming and Prove-Out. Tip numbers differ by control; there is no universal number table here. Do not apply full nose-radius compensation again to a path already corrected for that radius. Check whether a CAM post expects full geometry compensation, wear-only compensation or no control compensation. Where to check the control behavior The Haas G41/G42 entry connects the compensation side to the selected tool offsets. The Haas SL-10 Programming Workbook (hosted by the University of Florida), printed pages 59–61, explains the imaginary tool tip, tip direction and approach/depart moves. Use the current manual for the installed control when programming."},
{"title":"A roughing cycle removes layers while leaving stock for finishing","page":"CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles","summary":"Account for a rounded tool nose on tapers and arcs, then distinguish roughing, finishing, grooving and threading cycles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-profiles.html#a-roughing-cycle-removes-layers-while-leaving-stock-for-finishing","text":"A roughing cycle removes layers while leaving stock for finishing A G71-type roughing cycle uses a defined final profile to generate repeated stock-removal passes, commonly along Z for outside or inside turning. The programmer supplies the applicable cut depth, retract and finish allowance. A G70-type finishing cycle follows the defined profile to remove the remaining allowance. Upper half of turning stock: successive roughing passes run along Z toward the headstock and leave an allowance above a stepped finished profile. Finishing follows the final outline. Read the control's cycle definition before entering parameters. One-block and two-block formats differ. A depth word may be radial while an X allowance is diametral; the same letter can mean different things in different blocks. P and Q commonly identify the first and last numbered profile blocks. Check the referenced blocks, permitted profile direction changes, return path and compensation rules. Why no universal cycle template is shown The Haas G71 reference distinguishes path types and radial cut depth from diametral finish allowance. The Hurco ISNC G71 reference supplies its own two-block definition. A familiar cycle number does not establish compatible parameter meanings."},
{"title":"Grooving repeats plunges; threading synchronizes feed with rotation","page":"CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles","summary":"Account for a rounded tool nose on tapers and arcs, then distinguish roughing, finishing, grooving and threading cycles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-profiles.html#grooving-repeats-plunges-threading-synchronizes-feed-with-rotation","text":"Grooving repeats plunges; threading synchronizes feed with rotation A grooving or peck cycle divides a plunge into smaller advances and retracts. Some formats repeat the plunge at different Z positions to make a wider groove. G75 is one common outside/inside grooving family; face-grooving and peck-drilling families may use other codes. Check peck size, final depth, groove width and tool-width convention separately. A G76-type threading cycle makes repeated synchronized passes with increasing depth. The thread lead determines the axial advance per spindle revolution. The cycle also needs control-specific information about final size, infeed, pass depth and runout. Use Single-Point Threading for pitch, lead and thread form; a threading cycle does not choose those dimensions for you. Formats differ by control, so no complete parameter syntax is universal. See the Haas G75 reference and the G70/G76 entries in its lathe workbook as examples. CNC Turning Program Walkthrough spells out ordinary moves so the sequence can be read without decoding a cycle."},
{"title":"Read the setup assumptions before the first block","page":"CNC Turning Program Walkthrough","summary":"Trace an original facing, outside roughing, chamfering, finishing and cutoff sequence with every block explained.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-program.html#read-the-setup-assumptions-before-the-first-block","text":"Read the setup assumptions before the first block Illustrative; check your control's manual. This is a reading example in a common Fanuc-style lathe dialect, not a machine-ready file. Read CNC Program Structure for blocks and modes, CNC Turning Setup and Offsets for offsets, and CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes for S and F. The mill simulator in G-code and Toolpaths cannot validate this lathe program. The invented part is 20 mm in diameter and 25 mm long, with a 1 mm × 45° front chamfer. The chamfer runs from X18 Z0 to X20 Z−1: the 2 mm diameter change is a 1 mm radial change. Stock is 24 mm diameter with its raw face at Z+0.5. G54 has Z0 at the finished face and X0 on the spindle centreline. Stock and clearance extend beyond Z−28; workholding is farther toward negative Z. Assume diameter programming and absolute X/Z endpoints; this dialect does not need G90 for absolute moves. T0101 selects station 1 and offset 1: a right-hand OD/facing tool with a 0.4 mm nose radius, suitable for the illustrated cuts. Its imaginary tip and radius are set, and G42 is the correct side for this setup's finish contour. T0202 selects a 2 mm wide cutoff tool and offset 2. Its Z reference is the edge toward positive Z, so Z−25 puts the blade in the interval Z−27 to Z−25. The cutoff tool is capable of the illustrated small travel past centre. The example assumes X60 Z10 is an already-verified tool-change clearance location for both tools and the turret sweep, and starts there with spindle and coolant off. These coordinates are not a general clearance rule. All speeds, feeds and the 1800 RPM ceiling are teaching values, not material recommendations. Verify the real setup, tool calls, compensation entry/exit, centre crossing and file delimiters using CNC Programming and Prove-Out and the Safety collection."},
{"title":"Face the end, then leave a small allowance on the outside diameter","page":"CNC Turning Program Walkthrough","summary":"Trace an original facing, outside roughing, chamfering, finishing and cutoff sequence with every block explained.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-program.html#face-the-end-then-leave-a-small-allowance-on-the-outside-diameter","text":"Face the end, then leave a small allowance on the outside diameter Every parenthesized comment explains its block. G01 remains active until G00 replaces it; X/Z values here are destinations. Facing travels slightly past the centre to account for the rounded nose. Roughing uses two explicit lengthwise passes rather than a control-specific canned-cycle format. O2460 (IDENTIFY THIS EXAMPLE PROGRAM) G18 G21 G40 G80 G99 (XZ PLANE, MM, COMP OFF, CYCLES OFF, MM/REV) G54 (SELECT THE PRESET PART WORK OFFSET) T0101 (SELECT OD TOOL 1 WITH OFFSET 1 AT VERIFIED CLEARANCE) G50 S1800 (SET THE EXAMPLE MAXIMUM SPINDLE RPM) G97 S600 M03 (FIXED 600 RPM, START SPINDLE IN SETUP DIRECTION) G00 X26.0 (APPROACH OUTSIDE THE 24 MM STOCK AT Z10) G00 Z0.0 (MOVE TO FINISHED FACE PLANE WHILE OUTSIDE STOCK) M08 (TURN ON THE ASSUMED FLOOD COOLANT SYSTEM) G96 S90 (SELECT 90 M/MIN CSS WITH THE RPM CEILING ACTIVE) G01 X-0.8 F0.10 (FACE THROUGH CENTRE: 0.4 MM RADIAL OVERTRAVEL) G00 Z2.0 (RETRACT AXIALLY BEYOND THE FACED END) G00 X22.0 (POSITION FOR THE FIRST OD ROUGHING PASS IN AIR) G01 Z-28.0 F0.20 (ROUGH LENGTHWISE TO 22 MM DIAMETER) G00 X26.0 (RETRACT RADIALLY OUTSIDE THE ORIGINAL STOCK) G00 Z2.0 (RETURN TO THE FRONT AT CLEAR X) G00 X20.6 (POSITION FOR THE SECOND OD ROUGHING PASS) G01 Z-28.0 F0.20 (LEAVE 0.6 MM ON DIAMETER FOR FINISHING) G00 X26.0 (RETRACT OUTSIDE STOCK AGAIN) G00 Z2.0 (RETURN AHEAD OF THE FACE FOR FINISHING) Check roughing depth and remaining material The first pass changes diameter from 24 to 22 mm, a 1 mm radial cut. The second changes 22 to 20.6 mm, a 0.7 mm radial cut. Finishing from 20.6 to 20 removes 0.3 mm radially. Extending the OD cuts beyond Z−25 leaves room for the cutoff blade and places the finishing departure on material that will not belong to the finished part."},
{"title":"Compensate the chamfer and finish, then cut off at fixed RPM","page":"CNC Turning Program Walkthrough","summary":"Trace an original facing, outside roughing, chamfering, finishing and cutoff sequence with every block explained.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-program.html#compensate-the-chamfer-and-finish-then-cut-off-at-fixed-rpm","text":"Compensate the chamfer and finish, then cut off at fixed RPM The finish contour uses the same tool at a smaller feed. Nose compensation is active for the chamfer and OD; it is entered ahead of the face and cancelled after departing the finished contour. See CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles for why a sharp-point assumption would give the wrong chamfer. G00 X16.0 (POSITION AHEAD OF THE FACE FOR THE COMPENSATED ENTRY) G01 G42 X18.0 Z0.0 F0.10 (ENGAGE RIGHT COMP ON THE FACE APPROACH) G01 X20.0 Z-1.0 (CUT THE 1 MM RADIAL BY 1 MM AXIAL CHAMFER) G01 Z-26.0 (FINISH THE 20 MM OD PAST THE CUTOFF FACE) G01 X22.0 Z-27.0 (FEED OUT FROM THE OD BEHIND THE FINISHED PART) G01 G40 X26.0 (CANCEL COMPENSATION ON THE OUTWARD DEPARTURE) G97 S600 (CANCEL CSS AND REQUEST FIXED 600 RPM) G00 X60.0 (RETRACT RADIALLY TO THE ASSUMED CLEARANCE X) G00 Z10.0 (RETURN AXIALLY TO THE VERIFIED TOOL-CHANGE LOCATION) M09 (COOLANT OFF) M05 (STOP THE SPINDLE BEFORE THE TOOL CHANGE) T0202 (SELECT CUTOFF TOOL 2 AND ITS EDGE-REFERENCED OFFSET 2) G97 S500 M03 (START AT FIXED 500 RPM FOR THIS CUTOFF EXAMPLE) G00 X26.0 (POSITION OUTSIDE THE STOCK WITH THE NEW TOOL) G00 Z-25.0 (LOCATE THE POSITIVE-Z BLADE EDGE AT THE REAR FACE) M08 (COOLANT ON) G01 X-0.2 F0.05 (PART OFF WITH THE ASSUMED 0.1 MM RADIAL OVERTRAVEL) G00 X60.0 (WITHDRAW RADIALLY FROM THE CUTOFF SLOT) G00 Z10.0 (RETURN TO THE ASSUMED CLEARANCE Z) M09 (COOLANT OFF) M05 (SPINDLE STOP) M30 (END AND RESET THE PROGRAM; NO RETURN MOVE IS IMPLIED) File-start/end percent delimiters, tool-call behavior and supported startup codes must be supplied or adapted as required by the target control. No bar-feeder or parts-catcher sequence is implied."},
{"title":"Trace the modal state and finished dimensions on paper","page":"CNC Turning Program Walkthrough","summary":"Trace an original facing, outside roughing, chamfering, finishing and cutoff sequence with every block explained.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-program.html#trace-the-modal-state-and-finished-dimensions-on-paper","text":"Trace the modal state and finished dimensions on paper Mark where S means RPM and where it means metres per minute; locate the preceding RPM clamp. Confirm that every F value is interpreted in mm/rev under G99. Calculate the radial depth of each OD pass and the two chamfer endpoints. Locate the nose-compensation entry and departure, and identify the selected offset register. Check that the cutoff blade's positive-Z edge leaves the intended 25 mm length. The syntax conventions can be compared with the Haas lathe programming reference. This original example is not copied from a manufacturer program. A paper trace establishes intended geometry and modal state; actual prove-out still follows CNC Programming and Prove-Out. Spot a feed-mode error"},
{"title":"Read X as a size and Z as a position along the spindle","page":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","summary":"Read lathe X and Z coordinates, identify tooling and workholding, and separate part location from tool geometry and wear.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-setup.html#read-x-as-a-size-and-z-as-a-position-along-the-spindle","text":"Read X as a size and Z as a position along the spindle Start with CNC Control Fundamentals for how CNC works and Lathe Operations for the cuts a lathe makes. Here the work rotates and the ordinary turning tool moves in the XZ plane. Z runs along the spindle; positive Z is away from the headstock in the basic arrangement shown. X moves radially, even when its displayed value is a diameter. A slant bed or rear turret changes the view, so check the machine's axis diagram. Confirm diameter or radius programming before reading an X value. For a 28 mm diameter, diameter mode uses X28 and radius mode uses X14. Moving from X28 to X24 in diameter mode removes 2 mm radially. Z is not halved. Confirm incremental and arc-centre conventions separately. Lathe side view: work zero is at the face on the spindle centreline. Positive Z points away from the headstock; X28 in diameter mode represents a surface 14 mm from the centreline. Use Absolute and Incremental Positioning for coordinate arithmetic. On many Fanuc-style lathes X/Z are absolute endpoints and U/W are increments; G90 can be a turning cycle. Do not paste a mill's G90/G91 startup into a lathe program. Practice lathe X commands"},
{"title":"The tool arrangement determines how the next cutter reaches the work","page":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","summary":"Read lathe X and Z coordinates, identify tooling and workholding, and separate part location from tool geometry and wear.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-setup.html#the-tool-arrangement-determines-how-the-next-cutter-reaches-the-work","text":"The tool arrangement determines how the next cutter reaches the work A turret indexes a selected tool station into working position. Gang tooling mounts several tools on a slide; moving the slide brings the selected tool into position without turret indexing. Each tool still needs the correct offsets and a checked approach path. Live tooling powers a rotating cutter on the lathe for operations such as drilling or milling. A C axis controls the spindle's angular position, allowing the work to be indexed or rotated under position control. These are options, not features of every CNC lathe. Some machines also add a Y axis. Live-tool and spindle-mode commands belong to the machine's manual. Why an extra axis changes the work a turning center can do Ordinary turning produces surfaces around the spindle axis. A powered tool plus controlled work angle can reach features at selected angular positions. See the Haas options programming reference for one manufacturer's C-axis implementation."},
{"title":"Match the workholding and automation to the part","page":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","summary":"Read lathe X and Z coordinates, identify tooling and workholding, and separate part location from tool geometry and wear.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-setup.html#match-the-workholding-and-automation-to-the-part","text":"Match the workholding and automation to the part A chuck grips with jaws. A self-centering chuck moves its jaws together; independent jaws permit individual adjustment. Jaw style and setup determine which shapes can be held. A collet closes around a matching stock size and shape. Its useful size range depends on the collet design. A tailstock center supports the free end of suitable work. It does not replace the chuck or collet that drives the work. A bar feeder advances bar stock for successive parts. A parts catcher receives a separated part. Their timing and interlocks are machine-specific; neither is assumed in an ordinary two-axis program. For operating requirements use the Safety collection and CNC Programming and Prove-Out. No particular shop machine or installed option is assumed here."},
{"title":"Locate the part face once, then locate each tool relative to it","page":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","summary":"Read lathe X and Z coordinates, identify tooling and workholding, and separate part location from tool geometry and wear.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-setup.html#locate-the-part-face-once-then-locate-each-tool-relative-to-it","text":"Locate the part face once, then locate each tool relative to it Use CNC Axes and Offsets for machine zero, work zero and the general offset relationship. On a lathe, a common part datum is Z0 at the finished face and X0 on the spindle centreline. Establish the Z work shift using the control's documented reference-tool, probe or touch-off method. Allow for any material still to be faced away. For X touch-off, the reference is a measured diameter. In a trial-cut method, a tool cuts a short cylindrical surface and that diameter is measured; the control's measurement function uses the known size to establish that tool's X geometry. Use the manual's procedure for the actual machine, including which table receives the value. Do not enter the measured diameter as though it were automatically the raw geometry-offset value. Stored information What it describes Work shift or work offset The part datum, especially its Z location. X and Z tool geometry The position of each tool's reference tip relative to the established reference system. X and Z wear Small dimensional corrections after measurement, separate from the basic tool setup. Nose radius and tip orientation The rounded cutting corner and how its imaginary tip relates to it; see CNC Turning Profiles and Cycles. Check whether an X wear entry is interpreted radially or diametrally, its sign, and whether the control adds to or replaces the existing value. A work-offset change affects the shared datum; a tool correction addresses one tool. Selecting G54 does not measure or store a new zero. G53 does not erase the offset table. Control example: separate geometry and wear fields The Haas lathe manual, Operation, describes separate X/Z geometry wear, radius and tip-direction fields. Its button sequences and offset conventions must not be assumed on another control."},
{"title":"G96 changes RPM with diameter; G97 holds a requested RPM","page":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","summary":"Calculate constant-surface-speed RPM, apply an RPM ceiling, and distinguish feed per revolution from feed per minute.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-speeds-and-feeds.html#g96-changes-rpm-with-diameter-g97-holds-a-requested-rpm","text":"G96 changes RPM with diameter; G97 holds a requested RPM In the Fanuc-style lathe convention used here, G96 selects constant surface speed (CSS). S gives cutting speed: surface feet per minute with inch units, or metres per minute with metric units. G97 cancels CSS and makes S an RPM value. Confirm the actual control's units and code meanings. Use the work diameter at the cutting point, not the insert size. From Feeds and Speeds: Inch: RPM = SFM × 12 / (π × diameter in inches). Metric: RPM = cutting speed in m/min × 1000 / (π × diameter in mm). At 180 SFM on a 1.5 in diameter, RPM = 180 × 12 / (π × 1.5) = 458 RPM, rounded to a whole RPM. At 90 m/min on a 30 mm diameter, the result is 955 RPM. These are arithmetic examples, not recommended cutting data. Practice CSS calculations"},
{"title":"Set the maximum RPM before enabling constant surface speed","page":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","summary":"Calculate constant-surface-speed RPM, apply an RPM ceiling, and distinguish feed per revolution from feed per minute.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-speeds-and-feeds.html#set-the-maximum-rpm-before-enabling-constant-surface-speed","text":"Set the maximum RPM before enabling constant surface speed Establish an appropriate spindle-speed ceiling for the actual setup before G96. On the Fanuc-style lathe convention here, G50 S1800 limits requested speed to 1800 RPM. The number is only an illustration; determine the permissible limit from the machine, workholding, tooling and setup documentation. Other controls use different commands or parameters. G50 S1800 (ILLUSTRATIVE RPM CEILING) G96 S90 (METRIC CSS: 90 METRES PER MINUTE) G97 S600 (LEAVE CSS: REQUEST 600 RPM) As diameter approaches zero during facing, CSS requests increasing RPM. Once the clamp is reached, RPM stays limited and surface speed falls. At the centre, a nonzero surface speed cannot be maintained at any finite RPM. Use CNC Programming and Prove-Out and the Safety collection for the required operating checks. Calculate where a speed ceiling starts to matter With 90 m/min and a ceiling of 1800 RPM, the crossover diameter is 90 × 1000 / (π × 1800) = 15.92 mm. At 10 mm, unrestricted CSS would request 2865 RPM, so the simplified steady-state result is 1800 RPM. Acceleration and other machine limits can reduce actual speed further. See Haas's spindle-speed commands workbook for its G50/G96/G97 implementation. Worked example: 240 SFM, 2 in stock outside diameter (OD), and a 1000 RPM clamp. These are teaching values, not cutting recommendations. D* = 240 × 12 / (π × 1000) = 0.917 in. Spindle RPM 0 0 2 Diameter (in) 1000 clamp D* The dashed horizontal line is the RPM ceiling. The dot shows D = 0.5 in. Surface speed (SFM) 0 0 2 Diameter (in) 240 setpoint D* Below D*, RPM stays at 1000 and surface speed falls linearly toward zero. One setpoint, three cutting diameters D (in) RPM SFM Clamped? 2 458.37 240 No 0.5 1000 130.9 Yes 0 1000 0 Yes At 2 in: 240 × 12 / (π × 2) = 458.37 RPM. At 0.5 in, CSS would request 1833.46 RPM; the clamp gives 1000 RPM and π × 0.5 × 1000 / 12 = 130.90 SFM. At D = 0, the model commands the clamp RPM and surface speed is zero. The live sliders need JavaScript. The plots and worked example above show the same model without it. This ideal steady-state model uses π without rounding in the calculations. Displays round RPM and surface speed to two decimal places, diameters to three; trailing zeros are omitted. It assumes the commanded RPM is reached immediately and omits acceleration and other machine limits. Unit changes load a fresh teaching preset. For operating checks, see CNC Programming and Prove-Out and the Safety collection. Explain the RPM clamp"},
{"title":"Read the feed mode before interpreting F","page":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","summary":"Calculate constant-surface-speed RPM, apply an RPM ceiling, and distinguish feed per revolution from feed per minute.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cnc-turning-speeds-and-feeds.html#read-the-feed-mode-before-interpreting-f","text":"Read the feed mode before interpreting F In this common Fanuc-style lathe convention, G99 means feed per spindle revolution and G98 means feed per minute. With inch units, G99 F0.006 means 0.006 in/rev; G98 F0.006 means 0.006 in/min. With metric units, the corresponding lengths are millimetres. Codes vary: the milling return-level meanings of G98/G99 do not apply here. Feed per minute = feed per revolution × RPM. At 800 RPM, 0.006 in/rev gives 4.8 in/min. If CSS increases RPM while G99 holds F constant, the feed per minute increases too. If G98 holds feed per minute constant, feed per revolution changes with RPM. Set the intended feed mode explicitly. See CNC Program Structure for modal behavior and CNC Turning Program Walkthrough for a commented turning example. Reference for the code convention used here The G96–G99 entries in the Haas lathe manual describe S in CSS versus RPM and F per minute versus per revolution. Consult the manual for your own control before transferring these meanings. Practice feed conversion"},
{"title":"Zero the refractometer, then multiply Brix by the product factor","page":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","summary":"Read Brix, apply the product factor, and balance concentrate and water when mixing a fresh charge or correcting a partly full tank.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-concentration.html#zero-the-refractometer-then-multiply-brix-by-the-product-factor","text":"Zero the refractometer, then multiply Brix by the product factor A refractometer measures how a sample bends light. On an analog Brix instrument, read where the light and dark areas meet. Brix is a scale reading; it is not automatically coolant percent. Find the product's refractometer factor and working concentration range in its technical data sheet. Use the specified method for that product. Clean the prism. Calibrate to zero with water, using the water and temperature procedure specified for the instrument and fluid. Fluid suppliers commonly specify the water used for mixing. Remove the calibration water. Apply a representative, well-mixed coolant sample, close the cover and read the light/dark boundary. Avoid sampling a floating oil layer. Calculate concentration (%) = Brix reading × product factor. Record the result and compare it with the product's range for the operation. Clean the prism after use. Recheck a doubtful reading; contamination can blur the boundary or bias the result. Use the supplier's alternative test if the sample cannot be read reliably. Illustrative Brix scale from 0 to 6 in 0.2 divisions. The dark upper field meets the light lower field at 3.2 Brix. Read the boundary: 3.2 Brix. With an invented teaching factor of 1.5, concentration is 3.2 × 1.5 = 4.8%. That factor is not a shop recommendation. There is no universal factor or target range. The SDS explains hazards and handling; the product's technical data sheet supplies the mixing and measurement information. See Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets. Measurement references Cimcool's refractometer FAQ covers zeroing, the boundary and factor conversion. Master Fluids' concentration guide discusses product factors and contamination. A refractometer is a concentration estimate, not a bacteria test or proof that the fluid is fit for reuse."},
{"title":"Add concentrate to water, and state what the ratio means","page":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","summary":"Read Brix, apply the product factor, and balance concentrate and water when mixing a fresh charge or correcting a partly full tank.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-concentration.html#add-concentrate-to-water-and-state-what-the-ratio-means","text":"Add concentrate to water, and state what the ratio means Water first; add concentrate while mixing. Follow the product's mixing instructions in a clean container or approved mixing system. For emulsions, this lets the emulsifier disperse oil into the water. Adding water to a body of concentrate can form an unstable or inverted mixture that does not disperse properly. Write the order of a ratio. 24 parts water to 1 part concentrate contains 25 total parts, so its concentrate percentage is 100 × 1 ÷ (24 + 1) = 4.00%. It is not 100 ÷ 24. For water:concentrate = W:1, use C = 100 ÷ (W + 1). To make 12 L of that 4.00% premix, measure 0.48 L concentrate and 11.52 L water. Put the water in first. This is a volume-based teaching example with additive volumes, not a product recommendation. Why mixing order matters The aim is small oil droplets dispersed throughout the water, rather than water trapped inside an oil-rich mixture. Cimcool's mixing explanation describes the emulsifier's role. Mixing order is separate from the arithmetic: the right measured amounts still need the right preparation method."},
{"title":"A falling level does not tell you how strong the coolant is","page":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","summary":"Read Brix, apply the product factor, and balance concentrate and water when mixing a fresh charge or correcting a partly full tank.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-concentration.html#a-falling-level-does-not-tell-you-how-strong-the-coolant-is","text":"A falling level does not tell you how strong the coolant is Measure both level and concentration before topping up. Evaporation mainly removes water, raising concentration. Coolant carried out on parts and chips removes both water and concentrate. Adding plain water lowers concentration. A tank below the target is lean; one above it is rich. Compare with the actual permitted range before deciding it needs adjustment. A tank can be low in volume and rich in concentrate at the same time. Top up fluid that is still serviceable. Persistent odor, separation, sludge or contamination calls for the evaluation and cleaning decisions in Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance. New premix does not make spoiled fluid sound."},
{"title":"Calculate the premix needed to reach both target level and concentration","page":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","summary":"Read Brix, apply the product factor, and balance concentrate and water when mixing a fresh charge or correcting a partly full tank.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-concentration.html#calculate-the-premix-needed-to-reach-both-target-level-and-concentration","text":"Calculate the premix needed to reach both target level and concentration Use the same volume unit throughout. Let V be the current tank volume, C its measured concentration, F the intended final volume, and T the target concentration. Concentrations below are entered as percent numbers, such as 6 for 6%. Premix volume A = F − V Premix concentration P = (F × T − V × C) ÷ A Concentrate to measure = A × P ÷ 100. Water to measure = A − concentrate volume. Example: a hypothetical tank has 36 L at 4.0%. Bring it to 48 L at an assumed target of 5.5%. Add A = 48 − 36 = 12 L. The premix must be (48 × 5.5 − 36 × 4.0) ÷ 12 = 10.0%: 1.2 L concentrate added to 10.8 L water. Check the balance: the tank contains 1.44 L concentrate, the addition supplies 1.20 L, and 2.64 ÷ 48 × 100 = 5.5%. These assumed values teach the calculation; the product must permit the proposed makeup mix. Stop when the result cannot be made. A must be positive. A negative P means even plain water cannot achieve the target at that final level; P above 100% asks for more concentrate than the addition can contain. A full tank has no top-up space. Seek an approved partial replacement or other correction instead of overfilling. Even a mathematical result between 0 and 100% may be outside the product's permitted mixing method. Mix the approved addition separately, add it, circulate as specified, then measure again. The calculation assumes accurate volumes, uniform mixing and no losses during the adjustment. 20:1 is not 5% 20:1 water: concentrate 1 ÷ (20 + 1) × 100 = 4.76% 5.00% concentrate 5 parts concentrate in 100 total parts. 95:5 = 19:1 water: concentrate. Brix needs the product factor 6 Brix × an invented teaching factor of 1.0 = 6.00%. Use the actual product data sheet factor. Correct a lean sump: account for the added volume Teaching example: 100 L at 3%, target 5%, capacity 120 L. With x litres of neat concentrate, the balance is 3 × 100 + 100x = 5(100 + x). x = 100(5 − 3) ÷ (100 − 5) = 2.1053 L. Final volume = 102.1053 L, within capacity. Check: (3 + 2.105263…) ÷ 102.105263… × 100 = 5.00%. 102.11 L · 5.00% Capacity 120.00 L This is a concentrate balance, not an instruction to pour neat concentrate into a sump. Use the product-approved makeup method described on this page. Predict what water replaces Start with 100 L at 5%. Losing 20 L of well-mixed coolant leaves 80 L at 5%. Replace it with water: 4 L concentrate ÷ 100 L = 4%. Replace it with 5% premix: (4 + 1) ÷ 100 × 100 = 5%. If instead only 20 L of water evaporates, all 5 L of concentrate remains: 5 ÷ 80 × 100 = 6.25%. Replacing that water restores 5%; adding 5% premix raises it to 6%. The loss mechanism matters. The controls need JavaScript. These worked examples teach the same balances without it. For coolant condition and contact guidance, see Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance and Safety collection. Derive the balance, or solve for an unknown addition volume Concentrate already in the tank plus concentrate added equals concentrate in the final tank: VC/100 + AP/100 = FT/100. Multiply through by 100 and solve for P. This tracks concentrate, not the Brix number. If a premix concentration P is already chosen, solve for its addition volume: A = V(T − C) ÷ (P − T). Check that A is positive and that V + A fits within the allowed fill level. If P = T and C differs from T, no finite addition reaches T exactly. If C = T already, adding premix at T preserves it."},
{"title":"Practice the reading, the ratio and the top-up balance","page":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","summary":"Read Brix, apply the product factor, and balance concentrate and water when mixing a fresh charge or correcting a partly full tank.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-concentration.html#practice-the-reading-the-ratio-and-the-top-up-balance","text":"Practice the reading, the ratio and the top-up balance Use fresh hypothetical values to check the method. Every question states its rounding and assumptions; none supplies a shop concentration recommendation. Practice Brix conversion Practice lean or rich Practice top-up concentration Practice mixing ratios The practice catalog contains the full Coolant Concentration and Mixing bank."},
{"title":"Look for oil, odor, solids and foam—not just a low level","page":"Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance","summary":"Recognize concentration drift and contamination, decide whether to top up or replace, and work through a general tank-cleaning sequence.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-maintenance.html#look-for-oil-odor-solids-and-foam-not-just-a-low-level","text":"Look for oil, odor, solids and foam—not just a low level The sump is the reservoir that collects coolant for reuse. It also collects material that does not belong in the cutting fluid. Tramp oil: unwanted lubricant leaking into the coolant, such as hydraulic oil or oil from sliding machine surfaces. Skim the floating layer and investigate its source; it can interfere with fluid condition and concentration readings. Bacteria and odor: water-mixed fluids can support microbial growth. New or persistent bad odor is a reason to investigate, not a test that identifies the organism. Lack of odor does not prove the fluid is healthy. Concentration drift: evaporation tends to make the tank richer; repeated water additions can make it lean. Measure using Brix and the product factor. Chips and fines: chips settle and fine particles collect as sludge. They occupy tank space and can obstruct screens, filters and flow paths. Foam: check concentration, water quality, contamination and air entering the delivery system. Foam is not a reliable concentration measurement. Skin irritation: an overly strong mix can irritate skin; an overly weak mix can lose protection against microbial growth. Irritation is a reason to report exposure and investigate, not to guess a dilution. Why inspection and measurement belong together Concentration alone does not describe contamination or biological condition. HSE's metalworking-fluid guidance calls for maintaining fluid quality and checking bacterial contamination. A supplier-directed fluid-management program may include pH and microbial checks as well as concentration; use its methods and action limits. Master Fluid Solutions' FAQ explains odor, residue and concentration-related skin irritation."},
{"title":"Top up a serviceable tank; replace fluid that cannot be restored","page":"Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance","summary":"Recognize concentration drift and contamination, decide whether to top up or replace, and work through a general tank-cleaning sequence.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-maintenance.html#top-up-a-serviceable-tank-replace-fluid-that-cannot-be-restored","text":"Top up a serviceable tank; replace fluid that cannot be restored Use a top-up when the existing coolant is fit for continued use and the problem is level or a correctable concentration. Choose the addition from the measured condition, not from the fresh-fill recipe. A rich tank may need a weaker makeup mix; a lean tank may need a stronger one. Arrange a cleanout and replacement when contamination, unstable mixing or persistent odor cannot be brought under control by the approved maintenance process, or when the machine/fluid maintenance instructions require it. Investigate leaks and other causes so the fresh charge does not immediately inherit the problem. Straining removes larger solids; it does not remove dissolved contamination or establish that drained fluid is reusable. Do not return rejected fluid just because it looks cleaner. Do not add household disinfectant or an unapproved chemical to mask odor. Use the machine and fluid suppliers' maintenance intervals and condition checks. There is no universal calendar changeout interval or guaranteed sump life."},
{"title":"Clean the tank and its return path before refilling","page":"Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance","summary":"Recognize concentration drift and contamination, decide whether to top up or replace, and work through a general tank-cleaning sequence.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-maintenance.html#clean-the-tank-and-its-return-path-before-refilling","text":"Clean the tank and its return path before refilling Use this as a general sequence with the machine's approved maintenance procedure. Stop the machine and follow its isolation requirements before accessing the tank, pumps or chip-handling equipment. Only run pumps for a cleaning or circulation step when that procedure calls for it and the system is safely reassembled. Tank-cleaning flow: prepare and isolate; skim and drain through a strainer; remove chips and sludge; clean residue and return paths; remove cleaner and rinse as specified; refill, circulate and measure. Removing old fluid without removing deposits leaves contamination ready to enter the fresh charge. Identify the fluid, approved cleaner and disposal route. Review Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf before starting. Stop and isolate the equipment as required by its maintenance procedure. Prepare suitable labeled receiving containers before opening or draining the system. Skim accessible floating tramp oil. Drain or transfer coolant through a suitable strainer to catch larger chips, keeping the destination consistent with the reuse or waste decision. Remove accumulated sludge and chips with the approved tools. Clean accessible screens, filters and return passages; inspect for blocked flow and leaks. Clean dried coolant residue with a compatible cleaner diluted to its labeled instructions. Use the specified contact time and cleaning method; do not invent a stronger mixture. Remove the spent cleaning solution. Rinse and drain as the machine, cleaner and fluid suppliers specify; capture those liquids for the approved waste route. Account for retained liquid before refilling. Reinstall components and guards. Prepare fresh coolant by adding concentrate to water at the product's specified concentration, then fill to the machine's operating level. Restore and circulate the system according to its procedure. Check flow and leaks, measure concentration after mixing, and record the fluid condition, result and service performed. For a public demonstration, watch Haas's Machine Tool Coolant: Cleaning Your Tank. Its examples distinguish a routine tank cleanout from cleaning an extensively contaminated coolant system. Haas's Machine Tool Coolant: Tools of Coolant is a companion resource. Supplier instructions determine the cleaning treatment HSE's sump-cleaning guidance emphasizes supplier-selected cleaner and circulation times. An extensively contaminated system may need treatment before draining and additional cleaning cycles; the general checklist is not a microbial decontamination recipe."},
{"title":"Use the safety pages for contact, PPE and waste handling","page":"Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance","summary":"Recognize concentration drift and contamination, decide whether to top up or replace, and work through a general tank-cleaning sequence.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/coolant-maintenance.html#use-the-safety-pages-for-contact-ppe-and-waste-handling","text":"Use the safety pages for contact, PPE and waste handling Minimize skin contact with coolant and cleaning liquid. Use the SDS for the actual products and the PPE guidance for exposure control. Use Metal Chips and Swarf for removing and handling solids; this maintenance page does not replace those rules. Manage spent coolant, removed oil, sludge and cleaning liquid through the approved regulated-waste process. Do not pour them down a drain or onto the ground. The responsible waste handler must determine the applicable classification and disposal route from the fluid, contaminants and local requirements. “Water-based” or “biodegradable” is not disposal permission. Why the waste route depends on what is in the tank Washington State Department of Ecology's metalworking-fluid guide, pages 12–13, illustrates how formulation and contamination change waste management. Its state-specific rules are not a disposal authorization elsewhere. EPA's used-oil guidance also distinguishes qualifying used oil from other wastes and notes that state requirements can be stricter. The product SDS is a starting point; used coolant also contains material picked up during machining."},
{"title":"Saw a blank with room for finishing","page":"Cutoff Sawing","summary":"Choose a saw for the blank, hold the material securely, and leave enough material for the next operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutoff-sawing.html#saw-a-blank-with-room-for-finishing","text":"Saw a blank with room for finishing Sawing often turns a long bar or a large plate into a manageable blank: the starting piece for machining. Plan both the material left for finishing and the kerf, the slot removed by the saw. Kerf is material lost between pieces; it is not finishing allowance. Choose allowance from the required finished dimensions, cut quality, and later operations. A saw cut is not automatically a finished datum surface. Check dimensions and squareness before using it to locate the next cut. See Choosing Shop Measuring Tools and Shop Layout and Angle Measurement."},
{"title":"Match the saw's motion to the cut","page":"Cutoff Sawing","summary":"Choose a saw for the blank, hold the material securely, and leave enough material for the next operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutoff-sawing.html#match-the-saw-s-motion-to-the-cut","text":"Match the saw's motion to the cut Saw How it removes material Typical use Power hacksaw A straight blade moves back and forth, cutting on one stroke and relieving on the other. Cutting-stroke direction depends on the machine. Occasional straight cutoff work; the return stroke does no cutting. Horizontal bandsaw A continuous toothed loop advances through material clamped in a vise. Bar, tube, and structural sections cut to length. Miter capability depends on the head or vise design. Vertical bandsaw The band passes through a table while the work is fed along a path. Straight cuts and curved outlines in plate; see Vertical Band Machining. Cold circular saw A toothed circular blade shears chips. Repeatable cutoffs where squareness and surface finish matter. Inspect for burrs even when the cut looks clean. Abrasive cutoff saw A thin abrasive wheel grinds through the section. Cutoff work suited to the wheel's material rating, including some hardened materials. Heat and cut quality can require further finishing. “Cold” distinguishes chip cutting from abrasive or friction cutting; it does not mean zero heat. Use Saw Blade Selection for toothed blades. An abrasive wheel has its own selection rules."},
{"title":"Hold every piece, including pieces in a bundle","page":"Cutoff Sawing","summary":"Choose a saw for the blank, hold the material securely, and leave enough material for the next operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutoff-sawing.html#hold-every-piece-including-pieces-in-a-bundle","text":"Hold every piece, including pieces in a bundle NEVER hand-hold material for a horizontal saw cut. Use the machine's vise or a suitable fixture. Support long material at the saw's working height so it does not tilt the clamped piece. Arrange support for the cutoff without trapping the blade as the cut closes. Nesting means arranging several pieces for one cut. Clamping the outside of a bundle does not prove that its inner pieces are held. Use a nesting arrangement and clamping system approved for the machine, and select the blade for the resulting section. Identify the material, section shape, finished size, and finishing allowance. Confirm that the vise or fixture grips each piece and that long stock is supported. Account for the kerf when locating the cut or setting a length stop. Plan how the cutoff will be supported as the blade breaks through. After cutting, inspect the blank and remove the burr before the next setup. At break-through, the last connection between blank and stock disappears. Cutting resistance changes, and a thin lip of displaced metal may remain at the edge: a burr. See Bench Work with Hand Tools for deburring, and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment, and Metal Chips and Swarf for hazards and precautions."},
{"title":"Choose speed, feed, and coolant together","page":"Cutoff Sawing","summary":"Choose a saw for the blank, hold the material securely, and leave enough material for the next operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutoff-sawing.html#choose-speed-feed-and-coolant-together","text":"Choose speed, feed, and coolant together Band speed is the distance the blade travels per minute, usually stated in feet/min or m/min. Feed is the rate or force that advances the blade into the work. They are different adjustments. Material grade and hardness, blade material, tooth geometry, and section shape all affect the starting settings. Use the blade maker's cutting chart for the actual material, then follow the machine manual for setting it. Follow the blade maker's break-in procedure for a new blade. There is no single speed table or fixed percentage adjustment that fits every saw. Connect these terms to Feeds and Speeds. On a saw designed to use cutting fluid, the fluid helps lubricate the cut, carry away heat, and clear chips. Use the approved fluid, concentration, and delivery method. Some abrasive cutoff systems are dry and others are wet; do not add coolant to a dry-only setup. Why finer teeth do not fix every bad cut More teeth share the load, but smaller gullets have less room for chips. Too much feed can overload the teeth; too little can make them rub. A wandering cut also calls for checking the blade condition, tension, guides, and workholding against the manuals. Changing speed alone cannot correct loose work or a worn guide."},
{"title":"Cool the cut, reduce friction and carry chips away","page":"Cutting Fluids and Application Methods","summary":"Cutting fluids cool, lubricate, carry chips away and protect surfaces; match the fluid and its delivery to the tool, material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutting-fluids.html#cool-the-cut-reduce-friction-and-carry-chips-away","text":"Cool the cut, reduce friction and carry chips away Coolant does more than cool. A cutting fluid removes heat from the cutting zone, lubricates contact between the tool, chip and workpiece, flushes loose chips away, and helps protect the machine and work against corrosion. Lubrication can reduce material sticking to the cutting edge, called built-up edge. Together with cooling and chip removal, that can improve surface finish and dimensional control. Fluid cannot make up for a dull tool, poor workholding or unsuitable feeds and speeds. The fluid must reach the cutting zone to do its job. A full tank with a blocked filter or a nozzle aimed away from the cut is not effective delivery."},
{"title":"Choose a fluid family for the operation","page":"Cutting Fluids and Application Methods","summary":"Cutting fluids cool, lubricate, carry chips away and protect surfaces; match the fluid and its delivery to the tool, material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutting-fluids.html#choose-a-fluid-family-for-the-operation","text":"Choose a fluid family for the operation Use a product approved for the machine, work material and tooling. These are general tendencies, not a product selection chart for a particular shop. Check compatibility with seals, coatings and the metals being cut. Family Strength and typical use Tradeoff Straight oil (neat oil) Used without water dilution. Strong lubrication suits operations where sliding contact dominates, such as some tapping, broaching and slow cutting. Less cooling than water-based fluids; oil mist, smoke and fire risk depend on the product and process. Not suitable for every machine. Soluble oil (emulsion) Concentrate disperses oil droplets in water. Combines lubrication with water's cooling for many general machining operations. Needs concentration and contamination control. Water quality affects stability; residue and foam can develop. Semi-synthetic A water-diluted formulation with less mineral oil than a conventional soluble oil. Often balances cooling, lubrication and cleanliness in general machining. Still needs water-quality and sump maintenance. Foam, residue and compatibility depend on the formulation. Synthetic A water-diluted formulation without mineral oil. Cooling and visibility can suit grinding and suitable machining operations. Lubrication depends on its additives; do not assume all synthetics suit heavy cutting. Concentration, corrosion protection and contamination still need attention. Concentrate and working fluid are different. A supplier's oil-content figure for the concentrate is not the percentage to put in the tank. The working concentration comes from that product's technical data sheet. Why the family name does not settle performance Base fluid, additives, dilution and water quality all affect the result. A neat oil can use mineral, vegetable or other base oils; “straight” describes using it undiluted. A synthetic is not automatically harmless or maintenance-free. See the fluid-family comparison on page 7 of Washington State Department of Ecology's metalworking-fluid guide and HSE's exposure overview."},
{"title":"Run dry, mist or flood according to the cutting setup","page":"Cutting Fluids and Application Methods","summary":"Cutting fluids cool, lubricate, carry chips away and protect surfaces; match the fluid and its delivery to the tool, material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/cutting-fluids.html#run-dry-mist-or-flood-according-to-the-cutting-setup","text":"Run dry, mist or flood according to the cutting setup Dry: use when the tool supplier's guidance for that material and operation calls for it. Some carbide milling benefits from avoiding repeated heating and sudden cooling of the edge. Dry does not mean chips or dust need no control. Mist or minimum-quantity lubrication: a designed system meters a small amount of fluid toward the cut when lubrication and access matter more than bulk cooling. It does not provide flood-level heat removal or washing. Use the specified enclosure and mist control. Flood: a continuous flow can remove heat and wash chips from the cut when the setup calls for wet machining. Supply enough flow to reach the edge consistently; a weak, intermittent splash is not the same thing. Oil mist is liquid droplets carried by air, not a residue-free gas. Neither “all carbide runs dry” nor “more coolant is always better” is a useful rule. Follow the tool and machine guidance for the actual cut. Why some milling runs dry while other milling needs fluid A milling tooth repeatedly enters and leaves the material. Coolant can increase its temperature swing and promote thermal cracking in susceptible edges. Other operations need lubrication to limit adhesion, cooling to control distortion, or flow to clear chips. Sandvik Coromant's dry and wet milling guidance explains these competing demands. Use Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf for exposure and chip-handling rules. Continue with Coolant Concentration and Mixing and Coolant Condition and Tank Maintenance."},
{"title":"Locate and secure the work before starting a hole","page":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","summary":"Plan hole location, workholding, starting, chip evacuation and depth before drilling, then inspect and deburr the result.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/drilling-operations.html#locate-and-secure-the-work-before-starting-a-hole","text":"Locate and secure the work before starting a hole A drill press feeds a rotating drill along its spindle axis. A mill adds controlled table positioning, which helps place a pattern of holes from drawing datums. On either machine, the hole follows the relationship between spindle, tool and workholding. Clamp the work or hold it in a suitable vise or fixture rather than by hand; see Machine Hazards for why. Round stock needs support that prevents rolling, such as a properly clamped V-block arrangement. Provide clearance for a through drill without drilling into the table, vise or supports. For the operating rules, use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf. The sequence here assumes those requirements and the machine's operating instructions have been met."},
{"title":"A spot starts the hole; a pilot removes the center material","page":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","summary":"Plan hole location, workholding, starting, chip evacuation and depth before drilling, then inspect and deburr the result.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/drilling-operations.html#a-spot-starts-the-hole-a-pilot-removes-the-center-material","text":"A spot starts the hole; a pilot removes the center material Spot drilling makes a short starting feature to help locate the next drill. A center drill is primarily designed to produce a center hole for supporting work between centers; its small pilot and conical portion make it different from a spot drill. Select a starting tool compatible with the following drill, rather than treating the two tools as interchangeable. A pilot hole is a smaller hole drilled before a larger one. It can reduce the thrust required at a conventional drill's chisel edge. It is not automatically required: some drill geometries are intended to enter solid material. Follow the drill maker's starting-hole and pilot recommendations. Why “always center drill first” is too broad The starting recess determines which part of the next drill touches first. An unsuitable angle or an oversized pilot can load the drill's outer corners before its center is supported. Accurate starting also depends on tool rigidity, alignment and the surface being entered. A center-drilling step alone does not guarantee location."},
{"title":"Choose speed and feed for this drill and material","page":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","summary":"Plan hole location, workholding, starting, chip evacuation and depth before drilling, then inspect and deburr the result.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/drilling-operations.html#choose-speed-and-feed-for-this-drill-and-material","text":"Choose speed and feed for this drill and material Use Feeds, Speeds and Removal Rate for the surface-speed-to-RPM calculation. For drilling, its diameter input is the drill's cutting diameter. Surface speed is a cutting condition; RPM is the spindle setting. They are not the same quantity. Feed per revolution describes how far the drill advances each turn. Select cutting data for the tool material, work material, diameter, depth and coolant arrangement. A generic material table is a starting reference only when its tool assumptions match. Use an available machine setting consistent with the tool and machine instructions. Practice drill RPM"},
{"title":"Plan chip evacuation and full-diameter depth","page":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","summary":"Plan hole location, workholding, starting, chip evacuation and depth before drilling, then inspect and deburr the result.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/drilling-operations.html#plan-chip-evacuation-and-full-diameter-depth","text":"Plan chip evacuation and full-diameter depth Peck drilling advances in stages with retractions to break or evacuate chips. The needed stroke and retraction depend on the drill, material, hole depth and fluid delivery; there is no universal peck distance. Do not add a peck cycle to a tool that calls for continuous feed without checking its instructions. For a blind hole, distinguish the depth reached by the tip from the depth that has the full drill diameter. The conical point extends beyond the full-diameter portion. A tapped blind hole also needs space for the tap's lead-in and chip clearance. For a through hole, account for breakthrough and the drill point below the work. If chip shape, sound, thrust or finish changes unexpectedly, stop and inspect the setup before resuming; use Metal Chips and Swarf for chip handling. Why changes in the cut matter Changes in chip shape, sound, thrust or finish can indicate rubbing, chip packing, a damaged point or unsuitable cutting data. Continuing to force the tool does not diagnose the cause."},
{"title":"Check the hole before calling it finished","page":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","summary":"Plan hole location, workholding, starting, chip evacuation and depth before drilling, then inspect and deburr the result.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/drilling-operations.html#check-the-hole-before-calling-it-finished","text":"Check the hole before calling it finished Confirm the hole location, required diameter and whether it is through or blind. Check the tool, starting method, workholding, exit clearance and depth plan. Set suitable speed, feed and chip-evacuation conditions. After drilling and stopping the machine, inspect the hole and remove burrs without changing the specified feature. Measure the required dimensions and complete any specified finishing operation. Deburring removes the raised edge left by cutting. It does not automatically mean making a large countersink. Preserve the drawing's edge requirements and inspect the exit side too."},
{"title":"EDM needs an electrically conductive workpiece","page":"Electrical Discharge Machining","summary":"Sinker and wire EDM use controlled sparks to shape conductive work, with a gap between electrode and finished surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrical-discharge-machining.html#edm-needs-an-electrically-conductive-workpiece","text":"EDM needs an electrically conductive workpiece Electrical discharge machining (EDM) removes material with repeated electrical discharges across a small gap. Local heating melts and vaporizes tiny amounts of material. The electrode does not cut by rubbing against the work. Check conductivity before considering EDM. Hardened tool steel is a candidate; ordinary nonconductive glass or ceramic is not. Hardness is less restrictive than in cutting with a conventional tool, but composition and electrical and thermal properties still affect machining behavior. Why the fluid and the gap matter The dielectric fluid normally insulates the gap. A voltage pulse breaks down that insulation locally and permits a discharge. Between pulses, the fluid helps cool the region and flush debris. Debris removal and gap control help keep discharges stable. Sinker EDM commonly uses dielectric oil; wire EDM commonly uses controlled deionized water, though oil-based wire machines also exist."},
{"title":"Use sinker EDM for cavities and wire EDM for through profiles","page":"Electrical Discharge Machining","summary":"Sinker and wire EDM use controlled sparks to shape conductive work, with a gap between electrode and finished surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrical-discharge-machining.html#use-sinker-edm-for-cavities-and-wire-edm-for-through-profiles","text":"Use sinker EDM for cavities and wire EDM for through profiles Sinker, ram or die-sinking EDM feeds a shaped electrode toward the work to make a cavity. The cavity is larger than the electrode because removal extends across the spark gap. Mold cavities and hardened dies are typical uses. Electrode wear must be allowed for. Wire EDM feeds a thin wire through the cutting zone while controlled motion traces a profile. The wire is continuously renewed. An enclosed opening normally needs a start hole for threading; the wire must pass through the work. A blind pocket calls for another approach, such as sinker EDM. Wire EDM is useful for intricate die openings and thin features with low cutting force. Internal corners still have a radius: the wire radius plus the working gap limits how tight a corner can be. Specify a small radius rather than a mathematically sharp corner."},
{"title":"Offset the wire center by its radius plus the spark gap","page":"Electrical Discharge Machining","summary":"Sinker and wire EDM use controlled sparks to shape conductive work, with a gap between electrode and finished surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrical-discharge-machining.html#offset-the-wire-center-by-its-radius-plus-the-spark-gap","text":"Offset the wire center by its radius plus the spark gap Top view of a wire beside a retained workpiece wall. The wire center path lies in the removed region, one wire radius plus one spark gap from the wall, in dielectric fluid. The wire runs perpendicular to this top view. Gap and wire are enlarged. The dashed line is the wire center path; the solid boundary is the finished wall. Center-path offset = wire radius + spark gap. Measure this distance from the finished contour toward the material being removed. In a simple straight cut, the full cut width (kerf) is wire diameter plus twice the gap. For an illustrative radius of 0.120 mm and a one-sided gap of 0.018 mm, the offset is 0.138 mm. These are teaching inputs, not machine settings. A control may apply compensation to a programmed part contour; do not also offset the geometry a second time. Practice a wire offset Why one offset does not describe every pass Discharge energy, flushing and finishing passes change the working gap and surface. Actual compensation comes from the machine's process data and inspection. CHMER's EDM FAQ defines the offset as the wire radius plus the discharge gap, and notes that the gap changes with discharge energy."},
{"title":"Low cutting force does not mean no thermal change","page":"Electrical Discharge Machining","summary":"Sinker and wire EDM use controlled sparks to shape conductive work, with a gap between electrode and finished surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrical-discharge-machining.html#low-cutting-force-does-not-mean-no-thermal-change","text":"Low cutting force does not mean no thermal change EDM can hold fine features and produce a fine finish with suitable finishing passes, but its surface contains small discharge craters. Melted material can solidify again as a recast layer. Heat can alter nearby material, so surface integrity and any required finishing belong in process selection. Further reading on EDM surfaces and wire systems Makino discusses thermal surface effects; Sodick explains wire EDM. Low mechanical force is not a guarantee of zero distortion or an unchanged surface. Compare Electrochemical Machining. Safety references: Electrical Safety, Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"ECM removes metal by electrochemical dissolution","page":"Electrochemical Machining","summary":"ECM dissolves conductive metal through an electrolyte rather than removing it with sparks or a cutting edge.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrochemical-machining.html#ecm-removes-metal-by-electrochemical-dissolution","text":"ECM removes metal by electrochemical dissolution In electrochemical machining (ECM), the metal workpiece is the positive electrode (anode), and the tool is the negative electrode (cathode). Direct current passes through a flowing, electrically conductive liquid called an electrolyte. Metal dissolves from the work surface while the tool advances without cutting contact. Consider ECM for conductive metals, including hard alloys and complex contours. The tool shape, gap and electrolyte flow govern the resulting feature. A suitable electrolyte must be matched to the metal; a generic salt solution is not a universal process recipe."},
{"title":"ECM uses a conducting liquid; EDM uses controlled breakdown","page":"Electrochemical Machining","summary":"ECM dissolves conductive metal through an electrolyte rather than removing it with sparks or a cutting edge.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/electrochemical-machining.html#ecm-uses-a-conducting-liquid-edm-uses-controlled-breakdown","text":"ECM uses a conducting liquid; EDM uses controlled breakdown Two electrically powered processes with different removal mechanisms Question ECM EDM What removes material? Electrochemical dissolution Heat from discharges What does the liquid do? Conducts current and carries reaction products away Insulates between discharges; cools and flushes Does the tool wear? No removal-related tool wear in principle; real tooling still needs maintenance Electrode wear occurs; wire is renewed What surface results? Can be smooth and burr-free, without a thermal recast layer Discharge craters and possible recast material ECM is not a melting process and normally avoids a heat-affected zone. That does not mean the electrolyte stays cold: current flow heats it. Flow, temperature, chemistry and gap control all matter. Why hardness alone does not set ECM performance The reaction removes metal without requiring a harder cutting edge. Metal chemistry and current conditions still affect dissolution, so “all metals machine at the same rate” is not a valid selection rule. Tooling and electrolyte control can make ECM attractive for repeated parts, while adding development effort for a one-off feature. Further reading: EMAG's ECM overview and the Electrochemical Society's machining explanation. Compare Electrical Discharge Machining. Safety references: Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets and Electrical Safety."},
{"title":"Two numbers come from cutting data; two go into the machine","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#two-numbers-come-from-cutting-data-two-go-into-the-machine","text":"Two numbers come from cutting data; two go into the machine A milling setup is described by four numbers. Two describe what each cutting edge goes through, and they come from cutting data for the tool and the material. The other two are what you actually set on the machine, and you work them out from the first two. Cutting speed How fast the cutting edge moves through the material: the speed at the outside of the cutter. Written in surface feet per minute (SFM) or metres per minute (m/min). Also called surface speed. Comes from cutting data. Feed per tooth How far the work moves while the cutter turns from one tooth to the next, in inches or millimetres per tooth. Also called chip load, because it sets the size of the bite each tooth takes. Comes from cutting data. Spindle speed How many turns the cutter makes each minute, in revolutions per minute ( rev/min, usually said RPM ). Set on the machine. Table feed How fast the work moves past the cutter, in inches or millimetres per minute. Also called the feed rate. On a machine that moves the cutter instead, it is how fast the cutter travels. Set on the machine. \"Speeds\" are the cutter turning; \"feeds\" are the work moving. That is all the phrase feeds and speeds means. The two are set separately on the machine, and the rest of this page is about how they depend on each other, how much metal they remove, and the power that takes."},
{"title":"Spindle speed: the turns it takes to reach the cutting speed","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#spindle-speed-the-turns-it-takes-to-reach-the-cutting-speed","text":"Spindle speed: the turns it takes to reach the cutting speed Cutting speed is a distance per minute, measured at the edge of the cutter. In one turn, a point on that edge travels once around the cutter: its circumference, &pi; &times; D. So the spindle speed is the cutting speed divided by the distance one turn covers, once the two are in the same units. D = 0.5 in one turn = π × D = 1.571 in In one rotation about its center, a point on its edge travels its circumference. One turn of a 0.5 in cutter carries its edge 1.571 in. A cutting speed of 300 SFM asks the edge to cover 300 ft, which is 3,600 in, every minute. Spindle speed (rev/min) = 12 &times; cutting speed (SFM) &divide; (&pi; &times; cutter diameter in inches) The 12 is there because cutting speed is in feet and the diameter is in inches: 12 inches to the foot. In metric units the same idea needs a 1000, millimetres to the metre: Spindle speed (rev/min) = 1000 &times; cutting speed (m/min) &divide; (&pi; &times; cutter diameter in mm) Worked example, with illustrative numbers. A 0.5 in cutter, with cutting data that calls for 300 SFM: 12 &times; 300 &divide; (&pi; &times; 0.5) = 2,292 rev/min. The 300 SFM is there to make the arithmetic concrete. It is not a recommendation for any tool or any material. The feed value below is illustrative too; use the later material guide for the carbide starting bands. Look at where the diameter sits: underneath. Halve the diameter and the spindle speed doubles. A small cutter has to turn faster to give its edge the same cutting speed, which is why small cutters so often ask for more speed than a machine has. That case has its own card below."},
{"title":"Table feed: each tooth takes one chip load as it passes","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#table-feed-each-tooth-takes-one-chip-load-as-it-passes","text":"Table feed: each tooth takes one chip load as it passes Each time a tooth passes, the work should have moved one feed per tooth. The number of teeth passing each minute is the spindle speed times the number of flutes, so: Table feed = spindle speed &times; flutes &times; feed per tooth Worked example, continued. A 4-flute cutter at 2,292 rev/min, with a feed per tooth of 0.002 in: 2,292 &times; 4 &times; 0.002 = 18.3 in/min. The units work out on their own: turns per minute, times teeth per turn, times inches per tooth, leaves inches per minute. Turned around, the same relation tells you what chip load a feed gives. That is the way to check a feed someone else has set: Feed per tooth = table feed &divide; (spindle speed &times; flutes) Flutes here means the teeth that actually take a chip in each turn. For an ordinary end mill that is simply the number of flutes."},
{"title":"Removal rate: width &times; depth &times; table feed","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#removal-rate-width-times-depth-times-table-feed","text":"Removal rate: width &times; depth &times; table feed Two more numbers describe the cut itself. The width of cut is how far the cutter reaches sideways into the work, across the direction of feed. The depth of cut is how far down its side the cutter is buried, along its axis. A side cut can be no wider than the cutter; a wider path takes more than one pass. While the table feeds, the cutter sweeps through the work and leaves a channel behind it. In a stretch of time the cutter travels table feed &times; time, and the solid metal it takes out in that stretch is a slab as wide as the cut, as deep as the cut, and as long as that travel. Per minute, that is the material removal rate: Removal rate (in&sup3;/min) = width of cut (in) &times; depth of cut (in) &times; table feed (in/min) The front of the cut is curved, because it is the cutter's own shape. The slab's two ends are the same curve, one travel apart, so whatever the curve, the slab holds exactly width &times; depth &times; travel. Worked example, continued. The same cutter, 0.1 in wide (20% of its diameter) and 0.5 in deep, at 18.3 in/min: 0.1 &times; 0.5 &times; 18.3 &asymp; 0.917 in&sup3;/min, or 15.0 cm&sup3;/min. In 5 seconds the cutter travels 1.53 in and removes 0.0764 in&sup3;. That is the volume of solid metal. Once it is chips, the same metal lies loose and takes up far more space, so the removal rate is not the size of the chip pile."},
{"title":"Cutting power: removal rate times the energy each cubic inch takes","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#cutting-power-removal-rate-times-the-energy-each-cubic-inch-takes","text":"Cutting power: removal rate times the energy each cubic inch takes Cutting a cubic inch of a given material takes roughly a given amount of energy. That is its specific cutting energy, and it is why the same cut needs more power in steel than in aluminum. Written as power per unit of removal rate, it turns a removal rate straight into power: Cutting power (hp) = removal rate (in&sup3;/min) &times; unit power (hp per in&sup3;/min) Cutting power (W) = removal rate (mm&sup3;/min) &times; specific energy (J/mm&sup3;) &divide; 60 The 60 turns minutes into seconds, because a watt is a joule per second. One mechanical horsepower is 745.7 W. Worked example, continued. For 6061-T6 aluminum the guide in the calculator gives a band of 0.16 to 0.30 hp per in&sup3;/min. At 0.917 in&sup3;/min that is about 0.15 to 0.28 hp, or 110 to 210 W. The same cut in 304 stainless steel, whose band is 0.65 to 1.3 hp per in&sup3;/min, needs about 0.60 to 1.2 hp. The answer is usually a band. Some table rows give one factor; that still produces an estimate, not a measured certainty. Published bands cover a family of materials over a range of hardness, and the energy a real cut takes also depends on chip thickness and cutting-edge geometry. Treat the band as the size of the job, not a prediction to the watt. Three different powers get called \"the power\", and they are not the same: Cutting power is what the cutting edge spends. That is the estimate above. Motor shaft power is more, because the belts, gears and bearings between the motor and the cutter lose some on the way. If the drivetrain passes on 80% of what the motor delivers, the motor must deliver the cutting power &divide; 0.8. Electrical power drawn from the supply is more again, because the motor and its drive lose some too. None of these tells you whether a machine can take the cut. A spindle motor's rating is not necessarily available at every speed, and power is only one limit: the tool, the holding and the machine's stiffness are others. Use the estimate to compare cuts and to see why a cut is heavy, not to approve one."},
{"title":"Calculator: from material guidance to removal and power","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#calculator-from-material-guidance-to-removal-and-power","text":"Calculator: from material guidance to removal and power Choose a material to see generic carbide starting ranges for it, then enter the cutter, the cutting data and the cut. Nothing is filled in for you. Each result appears as soon as the fields it needs are filled, and says what it is still waiting for until then. Use the middle of these ranges fills in only the cutting speed and feed per tooth. How to read the drawings. You are looking at a block of work from the front, a little above and to the right. The cutter feeds to the right along the front top edge. Gold is the solid metal removed in the time shown, drawn where it was before the cutter took it. The dashed blue curve is where the front of the cut stood when that time began; the outlined cylinder is the cutter now. The three labeled axes use the same length scale, and the drawings in one comparison share that scale, so a slab twice as long is twice the volume. The block around the cut is there only to hold it; its size means nothing. The cut is already underway when the interval starts; only the gold volume counts toward that interval. The comparison below the drawings prices the setup's removal rate in every material. The cut is the same in every row; only the energy per cubic inch changes. Setup 2,292 rev/min · 18.3 in/min feed depth width travel in 5.00 s 0.500 in In 5.00 s: travel 1.53 in, removed 0.0764 in³ Removal rate 0.917 in³/min · power ≈ 0.15–0.28 hp Spindle ×2, table feed held 4,584 rev/min · 18.3 in/min feed depth width travel in 5.00 s 0.500 in In 5.00 s: travel 1.53 in, removed 0.0764 in³ Removal rate 0.917 in³/min · power ≈ 0.15–0.28 hp Spindle ×2, chip load held 4,584 rev/min · 36.7 in/min feed depth width travel in 5.00 s 0.500 in In 5.00 s: travel 3.06 in, removed 0.153 in³ Removal rate 1.83 in³/min · power ≈ 0.29–0.55 hp Material Estimated cutting power at 0.917 in³/min 6061-T6 aluminum 0.15–0.28 hp 7075-T6 aluminum 0.15–0.28 hp 1018 mild steel 0.56 hp one factor 1045 steel 0.56–0.59 hp 4140 prehard steel 0.72–0.83 hp 304 stainless steel 0.60–1.2 hp 316 stainless steel 0.60–1.2 hp Class 30 gray cast iron 0.40–0.60 hp C360 brass 0.28 hp one factor Ti-6Al-4V titanium 0.69–1.1 hp 0 1.0 2.0 hp The worked example, 0.5 in cutter, 0.1 in wide and 0.5 in deep, three ways over the same 5 seconds and at one scale. Doubling the spindle speed with the table feed held leaves the slab unchanged: the same metal is removed, in chips half as thick. Doubling it with the chip load held doubles the table feed, so the slab is twice as long and the removal rate and power estimate double. Below, the setup's 0.917 in&sup3;/min priced in each material, from the guide's cutting-energy bands. JavaScript is off, so the calculator is not running. The formulas and the worked example above are everything it calculates."},
{"title":"Things to try in the calculator","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#things-to-try-in-the-calculator","text":"Things to try in the calculator Before typing anything, read the results. Each one names the fields it is waiting for. With 6061-T6 aluminum chosen, enter a 0.5 in diameter and 4 flutes, then press Use the middle of these ranges. Cutting speed and feed per tooth fill in at 400 SFM and 0.00415 in/tooth. Nothing else changes. Enter a width of 0.1 in and a depth of 0.5 in. Check the removal rate against width &times; depth &times; table feed in the arithmetic. Change the material to 304 stainless steel. The drawings, the table feed and the removal rate stay exactly as they were; only the power estimate moves. The guide now marks your cutting speed as above its range for stainless, but the calculator leaves your entries alone. Hold the table feed and drag the spindle speed to 200%. The slab does not change and nor does the removal rate: the same metal comes off, in chips half as thick. Now hold the chip load and do the same. The table feed doubles, the slab grows twice as long in the same time, and the removal rate and power estimate double. Switch to metric and back. The cut and every result stay the same, written in other units, and any field you left empty stays empty. Try a width larger than the diameter. The calculator refuses it: that is more than one pass. Enter a 0.125 in cutter with a 4,000 rev/min machine top speed. Compare the speed the cutting speed asks for with the speed the machine can reach, and where the cutting speed actually reached sits against the guide."},
{"title":"When the machine cannot turn as fast as the formula asks","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#when-the-machine-cannot-turn-as-fast-as-the-formula-asks","text":"When the machine cannot turn as fast as the formula asks Because small cutters ask for high spindle speeds, the formula can easily give more than a machine's top speed. A 0.125 in cutter at 300 SFM asks for 9,167 rev/min. On a machine that stops at 4,000, it runs at 4,000. Work the feed from the speed the spindle really turns. At 4,000 rev/min, with 2 flutes and 0.0008 in per tooth, that is 4,000 &times; 2 &times; 0.0008 = 6.4 in/min. Each tooth then takes the chip load you meant it to. The cutting speed is what you give up. At 4,000 rev/min the edge of a 0.125 in cutter moves at &pi; &times; 0.125 &times; 4,000 &divide; 12 = 131 SFM, not the 300 the data asked for. Working the feed from the speed that was asked for, rather than the one the spindle is turning, gets the chip load wrong by the same ratio. A feed worked from 9,167 rev/min would give each tooth 9,167 &divide; 4,000 = 2.3 times the chip load intended. The same applies to a machine that offers only certain speeds, through belts or gears: set the nearest speed it has, and work the feed from that one. The calculator's machine top speed box does this for a top speed."},
{"title":"Generic ranges are a starting point, not your tool's data","page":"Feeds and Speeds","summary":"How a cutting speed and a cutter diameter set the spindle speed, how the spindle speed, flute count and chip load set the table feed, how width, depth and feed set the volume of metal removed, and how that volume and the material set the cutting power.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/feeds-and-speeds.html#generic-ranges-are-a-starting-point-not-your-tool-s-data","text":"Generic ranges are a starting point, not your tool's data What determines the surface speed? Start with the material and its hardness, then the cutting tool. Carbide is a tool material, not one universal cutting edge: its grade, coating and geometry matter, as do coolant and how steadily the setup can cut. A harder material generally calls for a lower starting speed. Once that surface speed is chosen, the diameter determines the RPM; a smaller cutter must turn faster to reach the same surface speed. What determines the feed per tooth? Choose a bite the cutting edge can carry and the flute can clear. That is why the guide changes with cutter diameter: small cutters generally take smaller bites. Width and depth of cut, a full-width slot, a long tool and a flexible setup change the loading and chip clearance. The number of flutes then tells you how many of those bites happen per turn; it does not by itself choose the size of each bite. Use the diameter-based range as a starting point, with its stated engagement, and refine it from the actual tool's cutting data. The arithmetic on this page is exact. Its inputs are the part that takes judgement. The ranges in the calculator are generic starting bands for solid-carbide end mills, by material family, from published toolmaker tables. The cutting data for your tool comes first: normally the toolmaker's for that tool, that material and that kind of cut, or the person responsible for the machine. Cutting data accounts for things these formulas cannot see: the exact grade and hardness of the material, the tool's coating and geometry, how far it reaches out of the holder, coolant, and how the setup is held. The guide's feed-per-tooth ranges assume a side cut about 20% of the cutter's diameter wide; a full-width slot or a long reach needs the tool's own data. A calculator turns data into machine settings. It cannot tell you whether the data suit your cut. Feed per tooth is not always the thickness of the chip Feed per tooth is how far the work moves between teeth. The chip a tooth actually cuts is only that thick when the cutter is at least half buried across its diameter. In a narrower cut, each tooth meets the work at a slant and the chip comes out thinner. Climb and Conventional Milling shows this: at a width of cut of 10% of the diameter, the thickest part of the chip is 60% of the feed per tooth. Some cutting data raises the feed per tooth for narrow cuts to make up for this. Read what your data assumes before using its numbers, and ask if it does not say. Thinner chips also take more energy per cubic inch, which is one reason a power estimate is a band. The spindle-speed, table-feed and feed-per-tooth relations follow Sandvik Coromant's milling formulas and definitions. The calculator's cutting-speed and feed-per-tooth ranges are from GARR TOOL's general purpose milling guide, and its cutting-energy bands are converted from Kennametal's milling power factors. Sandvik's specific cutting-force explanation shows why chip thickness and cutting-edge geometry affect the energy estimate. Haas's spindle power curves illustrate why available power depends on RPM; read the machine's own curve and duty rating rather than treating its headline horsepower as available everywhere."},
{"title":"A CNC program combines motion and settings in blocks","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#a-cnc-program-combines-motion-and-settings-in-blocks","text":"A CNC program combines motion and settings in blocks A CNC mill moves its tool by reading a program from the top down, one line at a time. The language most mills read is called G-code. Each line holds a few words, and together they say where the tool goes next, how fast, and what the spindle and tool are doing. Start with CNC Control Fundamentals for the control and machine types, CNC Axes and Offsets for coordinate references, and CNC Program Structure for common Fanuc-style milling codes. G- and M-code meanings vary by control. The simulator below uses its own documented subset; check the machine's manual for real programs. Word A letter and a number, such as X20, G1 or F300. The letter says what the number means: X, Y and Z are positions, F is the feed rate, S the spindle speed, T a tool number. G and M words are codes that choose what kind of move or machine action the line is. Block One line of the program. Machinists often call a line a block; in the simulator below, each block you snap into place is one line of G-code, with the same number. Work zero The point the program's numbers count from. Here, as in Milling Operations, X 0, Y 0 is the front left corner of the block and Z 0 is its finished top face. The uncut stock starts at Z +1. Positions refer to the tip of the tool, on its centre line: Z &minus;3 is 3 mm below work zero and 4 mm below that raw top. A pointed drill reaches its programmed Z at its tip; the full-diameter hole is shallower. Modal Most codes stay in force until another code from their group replaces them. After a G1, a line with only X80 is still a G1 move; a feed rate set with F remains available until changed. Not every word is modal. For ordinary G0/G1 moves, an omitted axis stays in place; it does not repeat the preceding incremental displacement. A first cut: one slot (A first cut: a closed-ended slot) G21 G90 G17 T1 M6 S2500 M3 G0 X10 Y30 G0 Z2 G1 Z-3 F100 G1 X90 F300 G0 Z20 M5 M30 0 50 100 0 30 60 X Y X0 Y0 start TOP VIEW back vise jaw front vise jaw Z +20 Z +10 Z 0 Z −10 Z −20 jaw top FRONT VIEW, CUT THROUGH THE BLOCK AT Y 30.0 Cut depth Z 0 −5 −10 −15 −20 through feed (G1, G2, G3): cuts rapid (G0): through the air A first cut: one slot. Mill a closed-ended slot 10 mm wide to Z−3 with a Ø10 end mill. The raw top is Z+1, so the cut is 4 mm deep. Lift clear and stop. The program on the left, numbered line by line, and the block after it has run. Solid lines are feed moves, dashed lines are rapids; the darker the cut, the deeper it goes. Read the program above from the top. Line 2 sets millimetres, absolute positions and the XY plane. Line 3 puts tool 1, a &Oslash;10 end mill, in the spindle, and line 4 starts the spindle at 2500 rpm. Lines 5 and 6 move the tool through the air to just above the start of the slot. Line 7 feeds it to Z &minus;3, cutting 4 mm below the raw top at Z +1. Line 8 feeds it along the closed-ended slot, and line 9 lifts it clear. Line 10 stops the spindle and line 11 ends the program."},
{"title":"Rapid moves put the tool in place through the air; feed moves cut","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#rapid-moves-put-the-tool-in-place-through-the-air-feed-moves-cut","text":"Rapid moves put the tool in place through the air; feed moves cut G0 is a rapid move. The control uses its rapid positioning rate, subject to machine limits and applicable overrides. It is for getting the tool to where a cut starts, through the air, never for cutting. G1 is a feed move. The tool moves in a straight line at the feed rate set by F, here in millimetres per minute. It can cut or feed through air. The spindle has to be turning first. S sets its speed in revolutions per minute, M3 starts it turning clockwise seen from above, and M5 stops it. T1 M6 changes to tool 1. A move goes in one straight line. G1 X80 Y40 Z-2 moves all three axes together and they all arrive at once. An axis left out of the line stays where it is: G1 X80 moves along X only. A rapid into metal is a crash: the tool arrives at full traverse speed with nothing to say it should be cutting. The simulator stops a rapid at the point where it would touch the block and says so. It does the same if a feed move reaches the metal with the spindle stopped. This simulator moves a rapid in one straight line, the way the LinuxCNC G-code reference describes G0. Controls differ in how they move during a rapid, so check how the one you use moves before you count on the path a rapid takes."},
{"title":"G90 and G91, G20 and G21: two modes that change what every number means","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#g90-and-g91-g20-and-g21-two-modes-that-change-what-every-number-means","text":"G90 and G91, G20 and G21: two modes that change what every number means G90, absolute A number is a position, measured from work zero. With the tool at X 10, G1 X30 moves it to X 30. G91, incremental A number is a distance from where the tool is now. With the tool at X 10, the same G1 X30 moves it 30 mm, to X 40. G21, millimetres X30 means 30 mm and F300 means 300 mm per minute with the simulator's G94 feed mode. G20, inches The same X30 means 30 inches, which is 762 mm, and, with G94, the feed rate is in inches per minute. A millimetre program read in inches asks for moves 25.4 times as long. Because these modes change the meaning of every line after them, a program sets both near its start, before the first move. The simulator insists on it: a move before G20 or G21 and G90 or G91 have been set stops the program, and so does a change of units after the machine has started moving. Work through a point list in Absolute and Incremental Positioning. Units and feed mode are separate settings; G95, where supported, uses feed per spindle revolution."},
{"title":"G2 turns clockwise and G3 counterclockwise, seen from above","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#g2-turns-clockwise-and-g3-counterclockwise-seen-from-above","text":"G2 turns clockwise and G3 counterclockwise, seen from above An arc move cuts along part of a circle in the XY plane ( G17 ) at the feed rate. Its direction is the way it turns as seen looking down on the work from above: G2 clockwise, G3 counterclockwise. The line gives the arc's end point with X and Y, and its centre in one of two ways. I and J say where the centre is, measured in X and Y from the point where the arc starts. That is the usual mode, G91.1, and it holds even while G90 makes every X and Y a position. G90.1 makes I and J a position too in this simulator. Those decimal codes are not universal; check the control's centre-mode rules. R gives the radius instead, and the control works out the centre. A positive R selects an arc of up to half a turn, a negative R the longer way round in the convention used here. An R arc cannot be a full circle, because a circle through one point has no single centre; a full circle uses I and J with its end point the same as its start. Worked example. The tool is at X 72, Y 15, moving right along the bottom of an outline. G3 X80 Y23 I0 J8 puts the centre 0 in X and 8 in Y from the start, at X 72, Y 23. Turning counterclockwise about it, the tool rounds a corner of radius 8 and ends heading up the right-hand side at X 80, Y 23. The end point has to lie on the circle: change J8 to J6 and the start radius is 6 mm while the end radius is &radic;(8&sup2; + 2&sup2;) = &radic;68, about 8.246 mm, so the simulator stops at that line instead of guessing. A radius-10 quarter arc from A 12,8 to B 22,18 around C 12,18 uses G03 I0 J10. Reversing from B to A uses G02 I-10 J0. In the diagram, absolute endpoints and start-relative centres give G03 X22 Y18 I0 J10 from A to B, then G02 X12 Y8 I-10 J0 back to A. Each start point needs its own centre offsets. Radius format can instead use G03 X22 Y18 R10 for the first arc under the stated convention. G17 uses X/Y and I/J; G18 uses X/Z and I/K; G19 uses Y/Z and J/K. I, J and K correspond to centre components along X, Y and Z. Judge arc direction looking from the positive normal axis toward the plane: +Z for XY, +Y for XZ, +X for YZ. The simulator supports XY arcs only. Practice quarter arcs Arcs: a rounded outline (Groove around a rounded rectangle) G21 G90 G17 T2 M6 S3000 M3 G0 X28 Y15 G0 Z2 G1 Z-1.5 F80 G1 X72 F250 G3 X80 Y23 I0 J8 G1 Y37 G3 X72 Y45 I-8 J0 G1 X28 G3 X20 Y37 I0 J-8 G1 Y23 G3 X28 Y15 I8 J0 G0 Z20 M5 M30 0 50 100 0 30 60 X Y X0 Y0 start TOP VIEW back vise jaw front vise jaw Z +20 Z +10 Z 0 Z −10 Z −20 jaw top FRONT VIEW, CUT THROUGH THE BLOCK AT Y 15.0 Cut depth Z 0 −5 −10 −15 −20 through feed (G1, G2, G3): cuts rapid (G0): through the air Arcs: a rounded outline. With a Ø6 end mill, follow a 60 × 30 rounded rectangle with R8 corners at Z−1.5. The groove is 2.5 mm below the raw top, Z+1. Four G3 arcs turn the corners. The program on the left, numbered line by line, and the block after it has run. Solid lines are feed moves, dashed lines are rapids; the darker the cut, the deeper it goes."},
{"title":"A drilling cycle packs a whole hole into one line","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#a-drilling-cycle-packs-a-whole-hole-into-one-line","text":"A drilling cycle packs a whole hole into one line G81 X15 Y15 Z-8 R2 F120 drills a hole at X 15, Y 15. The tool moves there at rapid, comes down at rapid to R, the height just above the work where feeding begins, feeds down to Z, the drill-tip depth, and rises back up at rapid. How high it rises. In this simulator G99 rises to R between holes, and G98 rises to the height the tool was at when the cycles began, or to R if that is higher. The simulator needs one of the two before the first G81. The cycle is modal. After a G81, a line with only X or Y drills another hole with the same depth, R and feed. G80 ends the cycle; after it, a line with only X or Y is an error until a G0 or G1 says how to move."},
{"title":"Without cutter compensation, program the tool centre path","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#without-cutter-compensation-program-the-tool-centre-path","text":"Without cutter compensation, program the tool centre path With cutter compensation off, as in this simulator, X and Y describe where the centre of the tool goes. The metal is cut by its edge, one radius further out. A &Oslash;10 end mill fed along Y 30 cuts a slot from Y 25 to Y 35. To leave a wall at X 80 with the metal beyond it cut away, the centre runs along X 85. Milling Operations works through the same rule for a shoulder and a pocket. Controls can do that offset for you with cutter radius compensation, G41 and G42. The simulator does not model it, so every program here is written as the path of the centre, and a line with G41 or G42 stops with a message. CNC Axes and Offsets explains left/right compensation and tool-length offsets."},
{"title":"Simulator: build a program from blocks and watch it run","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#simulator-build-a-program-from-blocks-and-watch-it-run","text":"Simulator: build a program from blocks and watch it run Pick a program, change it, and run it on a simulated block of metal in a vise. Every block is one line of G-code; the same interpreter reads it whether you build it from blocks or type it, and the machine runs exactly what it reads. How to drive it. Press a block in the program to open it and change its numbers; leave a number empty to leave that word out of the line. Add a block from the row above the program, either by pressing it, which puts it after the open block, or by dragging it into place. Drag a block by its handle to move it; with the keyboard, focus the handle and press Alt with &uarr; or &darr;. Play runs the program, Step runs one block and stops, Back goes back one block and Restart starts again with fresh stock. The block that is running is outlined in gold, and its move is drawn in gold on the views; press a move in the top view to open the block that makes it. Any change to the program puts the simulation back to its start, so it always shows the program as it now is. Holes: a drilling cycle (Four holes with a drilling cycle) G21 G90 G17 T3 M6 S1500 M3 G0 X15 Y15 G0 Z10 G98 G81 X15 Y15 Z-8 R2 F120 X85 Y45 X15 G80 G0 Z20 M5 M30 0 50 100 0 30 60 X Y X0 Y0 start TOP VIEW back vise jaw front vise jaw Z +20 Z +10 Z 0 Z −10 Z −20 jaw top FRONT VIEW, CUT THROUGH THE BLOCK AT Y 45.0 Cut depth Z 0 −5 −10 −15 −20 through feed (G1, G2, G3): cuts rapid (G0): through the air Holes: a drilling cycle. Drill four Ø6 holes with the tip reaching Z−8: 9 mm below the raw top, Z+1. Full-diameter depth is shorter because of the drill point. G81 drills the first hole; X or Y lines repeat it until G80. The program on the left, numbered line by line, and the block after it has run. Solid lines are feed moves, dashed lines are rapids; the darker the cut, the deeper it goes. JavaScript is off, so the simulator is not running. The figures on this page show two of its example programs run to their end."},
{"title":"Things to try in the G-code simulator","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#things-to-try-in-the-g-code-simulator","text":"Things to try in the G-code simulator Step through the slot program one block at a time. Watch the machine state change at blocks 2, 3 and 4 before anything moves. Make the slot deeper and shorter, then run it again. Which numbers did you change? Drag the Spindle on block below the first feed move, and read why the machine stops. Open block 7 of the slot program and switch it from G1 to G0. What does a rapid do when it meets the metal? Change the slot program to G91. Before you press Play, work out where its first move will go. In the arcs program, swap one G3 for a G2, and see which way that corner turns now. In the holes program, try G99 instead of G98, then add a fifth hole. In the G-code text, type a line the simulator does not read, such as G41 or M7, and read what it says."},
{"title":"What the simulator reads, and what it leaves out","page":"G-code and Toolpaths","summary":"How a CNC mill reads a program line by line: rapid and feed moves, absolute and incremental coordinates, units, arcs and a drilling cycle. Build a program from blocks and watch it cut a simulated block.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/gcode-and-toolpaths.html#what-the-simulator-reads-and-what-it-leaves-out","text":"What the simulator reads, and what it leaves out The simulator reads a small part of G-code, modelled on the way the LinuxCNC control reads it. Any other code, or a line that is not G-code at all, stops the program at that line with a message saying what is wrong. Nothing is skipped. Motion G0 rapid, G1 feed, G2 and G3 arcs in the XY plane, G81 drilling cycle and G80 to end it. Modes G17 XY plane, G20 inches, G21 millimetres, G90 absolute, G91 incremental, G90.1 and G91.1 for I and J, G94 feed per minute, G98 and G99 for the drilling cycle. G40, G49 and G54 are read as the settings the simulator always has: no cutter compensation, no tool length offset, and its one work offset. Machine M3 spindle on, M5 off, M6 tool change, M8 and M9 coolant on and off (shown, with no effect on the cut), M2 or M30 program end: stops spindle and coolant, restores G90 and G1, and leaves the tool at its last position. Words X Y Z positions, I J arc centre, R arc radius or drilling R plane, F feed rate, S spindle speed, T tool, N line number, and comments in brackets or after a semicolon. Tools T1 &Oslash;10 end mill, T2 &Oslash;6 end mill, T3 &Oslash;6 drill, T4 &Oslash;12 spot and chamfer tool, T5 &Oslash;50 face mill: the tools of Milling Operations, with the same rules about what each can cut. Some rules are the simulator's own. It needs units and distance mode set before the first move, a speed before M3, G98 or G99 before a drilling cycle, and, for a tool change, the spindle stopped and the tool at Z +20 or higher. A real control may be less strict, or strict about different things. Only straight down and straight across, through metal. The block is modelled as a height above each point, so the tool can cut moving along Z alone, or in X and Y at one height. A move in X or Y and Z at once is followed exactly through the air, but stops if it would reach the metal: ramping and helical moves are not modelled. Arcs are cut as short straight chords, each within 0.01 mm of the true arc. They are drawn as true arcs. Simple timing. Feed moves run at their F and rapids at 5000 mm per minute, with no acceleration, no blending of one move into the next, and no time for tool changes. Not a check of a real program. The speeds and feeds in the examples are round teaching numbers, not cutting recommendations; Feeds and Speeds is about choosing them. A program that runs here has met only this simulator's rules. Controls differ in the codes they accept, in their starting modes and in how they change tools, so a program for a real machine is checked against the manual for its control. See CNC Programming and Prove-Out for graphics, dry run, single block and overrides; follow Equipment Operating Procedure for machine use. How the simulator reads and runs a program Each line is read in the order a control carries out the parts of a line: feed rate, spindle speed and tool first, then the tool change, the spindle, coolant, the modes, the move, and last the program end. So S2500 M3 G1 X20 F100 starts the spindle before it moves. Two codes from one group on a line, such as G0 G1, are an error. The interpreter first reads the whole program, and a second machine runs it at once to find its path, its time and the first place it would stop; that is the path drawn before you press Play. Pressing Play runs the program again on the block, move by move. The metal, the vise and the tools are those of the simulator in Milling Operations: every cut is kept as the space the tool swept, and a move that would break a tool rule or hit the vise stops where it would happen. An arc's end point has to be on its circle to within 0.005 mm or 0.1 % of the radius, whichever is larger. A rapid, or a feed with the spindle stopped, stops a hair short of the first metal it would touch. The volume removed is an estimate from a 0.25 mm grid."},
{"title":"A grinding wheel cuts with many small grains","page":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","summary":"Match the abrasive to the material, distinguish grit from grade, and read the five main fields of a conventional wheel specification.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/grinding-wheels.html#a-grinding-wheel-cuts-with-many-small-grains","text":"A grinding wheel cuts with many small grains A bonded wheel holds abrasive grains in a supporting material called the bond. The grains act as small cutting edges. Spaces between them provide room for removed material and, in wet grinding, fluid. Grains can fracture or pull free as the wheel works; dressing restores a useful cutting surface when that renewal is insufficient. Choose the complete wheel for the machine, material and operation. Abrasive type alone does not establish suitability: shape, dimensions, bond, speed rating and permitted grinding surfaces also matter. See Offhand Grinding and Precision Grinding."},
{"title":"Match the abrasive to the work material","page":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","summary":"Match the abrasive to the material, distinguish grit from grade, and read the five main fields of a conventional wheel specification.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/grinding-wheels.html#match-the-abrasive-to-the-work-material","text":"Match the abrasive to the work material Abrasive General starting applications Aluminum oxide Steels, including high-speed steel (HSS) tool bits. Different formulations serve different grinding conditions. Silicon carbide Cast iron, some nonferrous materials, and brittle nonmetals; also conventional carbide-tool grinding with a suitable wheel. Cubic boron nitride (CBN) A superabrasive used for hardened steels and HSS. Diamond A superabrasive used for cemented carbide, ceramics and other hard nonferrous materials. It is not the general choice for steel grinding. These are selection starting points, not permission to use any wheel containing that abrasive. In particular, a standard bench wheel for steel is not a wheel for aluminum. Confirm the manufacturer's application listing. Manufacturer resources for abrasive selection Norton: choosing a grinding wheel discusses conventional abrasives. 3M: CBN and diamond superabrasives covers the superabrasive families. Use product-specific data for a real selection."},
{"title":"Grit is grain size; grade is how firmly grains are held","page":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","summary":"Match the abrasive to the material, distinguish grit from grade, and read the five main fields of a conventional wheel specification.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/grinding-wheels.html#grit-is-grain-size-grade-is-how-firmly-grains-are-held","text":"Grit is grain size; grade is how firmly grains are held Grit size: within the same conventional grit-number system, a lower number means coarser grains and a higher number means finer grains. Coarse grit generally provides more space for chips and favors material removal; fine grit generally favors smaller scratches. Finish also depends on dressing, feeds, wheel condition and the work material. Grade: the wheel's resistance to releasing abrasive grains. A hard grade holds grains more strongly; it does not mean that the abrasive mineral itself is harder. A hard work material may need a softer grade so dull grains can release. Structure: the spacing of the grains. An open structure provides more space between grains than a dense structure. Do not confuse spacing with grain size. Bond: vitrified bonds are glasslike; resinoid bonds use resin. Rubber and metal bonds serve other applications. Bond choice affects wheel behavior, dressing and permissible operating conditions; no bond name establishes a universal RPM rating."},
{"title":"Read the conventional marking from abrasive to bond","page":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","summary":"Match the abrasive to the material, distinguish grit from grade, and read the five main fields of a conventional wheel specification.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/grinding-wheels.html#read-the-conventional-marking-from-abrasive-to-bond","text":"Read the conventional marking from abrasive to bond The usual core sequence is abrasive → grit → grade → structure → bond. Prefixes and suffixes may identify a manufacturer's particular abrasive or bond. Some markings omit a structure field. Superabrasive specifications may include concentration and use a different format. Five fields in order: abrasive identifies the cutting material, grit its size, grade grain retention, structure grain spacing, and bond the binder. Read the meaning of each field before interpreting a manufacturer's code. For a teaching example, suppose a supplier defines A 60 J 7 V as aluminum oxide, grit 60, grade J, structure 7 and vitrified bond. The 60 is not an RPM limit; the J is not the hardness of aluminum oxide. Use that supplier's key to interpret J and 7 rather than assuming all manufacturers' grades behave alike. A manufacturer's explanation of the marking Norton: Grinding Wheel Basics explains the five fields and manufacturer-specific variations. A wheel specification and its separately marked maximum operating speed answer different questions. Practice decoding a wheel Practice grit selection"},
{"title":"Choose the operation from the finished shape","page":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","summary":"Choose a finishing operation by the feature needed: a sized bore, a cylindrical recess, a conical seat or a flat bearing surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/hole-finishing.html#choose-the-operation-from-the-finished-shape","text":"Choose the operation from the finished shape Four schematic sections compare a deep flat-bottomed counterbore, a conical countersink, a shallow flat spot face and a straight reamed hole. Section views through the hole axis. Gray is material; white is open space. A spot face provides a bearing surface; a counterbore provides a recess. Needed feature Operation Improve the diameter and finish of a nearly correct hole Ream Enlarge an existing hole with control of its axis and diameter Bore Cylindrical recess with a flat shoulder for a fastener head Counterbore Conical seat for a matching flat-head fastener Countersink Local flat bearing surface around a hole Spot face"},
{"title":"Ream for size and finish; bore when the axis needs control","page":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","summary":"Choose a finishing operation by the feature needed: a sized bore, a cylindrical recess, a conical seat or a flat bearing surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/hole-finishing.html#ream-for-size-and-finish-bore-when-the-axis-needs-control","text":"Ream for size and finish; bore when the axis needs control A reamer removes a small amount of material with multiple cutting edges. Prepare an undersize hole with enough material for the reamer to cut, rather than rub. Too much allowance overloads the tool; too little may leave the previous surface. Use the reamer maker's allowance for that diameter and material, not one stock allowance for every hole. A reamer tends to follow the existing hole. Do not rely on it to relocate a badly misplaced or crooked hole. Boring enlarges an existing hole with a cutting edge whose path can be set relative to the machine axis. With a suitable setup and enough material left to clean up, it can correct location and straightness as well as diameter. Use Tolerances and Fits to interpret the size limits and select inspection methods. See Lathe Operations for boring and threading on the lathe. A nominal tool size is not a finished-hole guarantee Tool condition, alignment, runout, temperature and workholding affect the result. A reamer stamped with the desired diameter still needs an appropriate prepared hole and cutting conditions. Inspect the actual bore rather than accepting it from the tool marking."},
{"title":"Match the head seat to the fastener drawing","page":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","summary":"Choose a finishing operation by the feature needed: a sized bore, a cylindrical recess, a conical seat or a flat bearing surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/hole-finishing.html#match-the-head-seat-to-the-fastener-drawing","text":"Match the head seat to the fastener drawing A counterbore has a cylindrical wall and a flat shoulder. A countersink has a conical seat. The screw head must match that seat's included angle; the thread diameter alone does not determine the head angle or the countersink diameter. A spot face removes enough surface material to create a local flat seat for a head, nut or washer. It is useful on a rough or uneven surface. A spot face and a counterbore may use similar tools, but their required depth and purpose differ. Read the specified diameters, depths and angle before cutting. A tool used only to break a sharp edge is not necessarily producing a fastener seat to size. Do not substitute a preferred cutter angle for the drawing requirement."},
{"title":"Test the choice before choosing the cutter","page":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","summary":"Choose a finishing operation by the feature needed: a sized bore, a cylindrical recess, a conical seat or a flat bearing surface.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/hole-finishing.html#test-the-choice-before-choosing-the-cutter","text":"Test the choice before choosing the cutter A smooth pin hole already on location suggests reaming. A hole whose axis must be corrected suggests boring, if there is enough material. A socket-head screw recessed into a cylindrical pocket suggests counterboring. A conical flat-head seat suggests countersinking. Practice choosing hole operations"},
{"title":"A jig guides the tool; a fixture locates and holds the work","page":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","summary":"Locate a part repeatably, clamp it against supports, and distinguish tool-guiding jigs from workholding fixtures.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/jigs-and-fixtures.html#a-jig-guides-the-tool-a-fixture-locates-and-holds-the-work","text":"A jig guides the tool; a fixture locates and holds the work A drill jig locates and holds the part and guides the drill with a bushing. A fixture locates and holds the part while the machine controls the tool path. A plate with clamps is not automatically a jig: ask whether it guides the cutting tool. Both reduce the need to recreate the part position for every cycle. Neither removes the need for trained setup, secure restraint and inspection. Follow the approved mounting method; do not infer that a jig may be held by hand. For machine workholding and indicating procedures, use Milling Vises and Strap Clamps and Mill Indicators and Part Zero. For reading the part's views, see Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box. The drawing specifies the required relationships; the fixture must support making them."},
{"title":"Three, two and one contacts establish six independent constraints","page":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","summary":"Locate a part repeatably, clamp it against supports, and distinguish tool-guiding jigs from workholding fixtures.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/jigs-and-fixtures.html#three-two-and-one-contacts-establish-six-independent-constraints","text":"Three, two and one contacts establish six independent constraints A free rigid body can translate along X, Y and Z and rotate about X, Y and Z: six degrees of freedom. For an ideal rectangular block seated against perpendicular locating planes, the 3-2-1 arrangement establishes its position without redundant locating contacts. Contacts Plane in this example Additional motions constrained while seated Three non-collinear supports Primary, XY underside Z translation, rotation about X and Y: three Two separated side locators Secondary, XZ side Y translation and rotation about Z: two One end locator Tertiary, YZ end X translation: one Transparent block with three underside primary supports, two secondary side contacts and one tertiary end contact. Clamp arrows press down, toward the side contacts and toward the end contact. Schematic contact locations, with the hidden contacts shown through the block. A, B and C name the locating planes in this example, not a replacement for the part drawing. These contacts locate only while the work stays seated. Clamps maintain contact and resist forces that would lift or separate the part. Do not count positive and negative directions as twelve independent degrees of freedom."},
{"title":"Direct clamp force into supports and leave room for chips","page":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","summary":"Locate a part repeatably, clamp it against supports, and distinguish tool-guiding jigs from workholding fixtures.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/jigs-and-fixtures.html#direct-clamp-force-into-supports-and-leave-room-for-chips","text":"Direct clamp force into supports and leave room for chips Place clamp forces toward locators and supported regions, not toward open space where the part can bend. Use enough restraint for the operation without distorting the part. Plan the load path from the cutting force through the work and supports into the machine. A fourth fixed support under an imperfect surface may make the part rock instead of improving location. Extra supports can be useful for stiffness if they are adjusted to the already located part without shifting it. Avoid redundant locators that fight one another. Foolproofing (poka-yoke) makes an incorrect loading orientation difficult or impossible—for example, a locating feature that accepts an asymmetric part in only one orientation. Keep chips away from locating contacts with accessible cleaning paths and relief spaces; a trapped chip can relocate every subsequent cut. Identify the intended locating contacts and check that the part seats without rocking. Check that clamps press into supports and that loading, cutting and unloading paths are clear. Check that incorrect orientation is prevented or clearly detectable. Verify the first part after setup before repeating the cycle."},
{"title":"Modular fixtures trade dedicated tooling for reusable components","page":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","summary":"Locate a part repeatably, clamp it against supports, and distinguish tool-guiding jigs from workholding fixtures.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/jigs-and-fixtures.html#modular-fixtures-trade-dedicated-tooling-for-reusable-components","text":"Modular fixtures trade dedicated tooling for reusable components A modular fixture combines a reusable base with locating pins, supports and clamps. It suits changing work when the arrangement can be documented and rebuilt repeatably. A dedicated fixture may reduce loading time for repeated production, but takes its own design and manufacturing effort. A tombstone is an upright fixture carrier with mounting faces that can present several setups to a machining centre. It still needs suitable locating, clamping and tool clearance on each face. Select fixture materials for stiffness, wear, temperature change and the required accuracy. Aluminium can be useful; thermal expansion is a design consideration rather than an automatic ban on close-tolerance work. Wear surfaces may need hardened inserts or replaceable components. Count locating constraints Identify a jig or fixture"},
{"title":"A key joins the shaft slot to the hub slot to transmit torque","page":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","summary":"Identify common keys, choose a shaft or hub cutting method, and check slot location, width, depth and fit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/keys-and-keyseats.html#a-key-joins-the-shaft-slot-to-the-hub-slot-to-transmit-torque","text":"A key joins the shaft slot to the hub slot to transmit torque A key is a separate piece fitted between a shaft and the bore of a hub, such as a pulley or gear. Contact between the key and slot faces transfers turning force. Here, keyseat means the recess in the shaft and keyway means the matching slot in the hub. Other references sometimes use keyway for both; always identify which part is being cut. Separate end views of a shaft keyseat and hub keyway, followed by an assembled section with a key spanning both recesses. Schematic parallel-key joint. Clearances are exaggerated; obtain dimensions and fits from the drawing. The key does not by itself define how the hub is held against movement along the shaft. Check the assembly drawing for axial retention as well as torque transmission."},
{"title":"Identify the key by its cross-section and seating shape","page":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","summary":"Identify common keys, choose a shaft or hub cutting method, and check slot location, width, depth and fit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/keys-and-keyseats.html#identify-the-key-by-its-cross-section-and-seating-shape","text":"Identify the key by its cross-section and seating shape Key Recognizing feature Square parallel key Equal width and height in cross-section; uniform section along its working length. Rectangular parallel key Unequal width and height; uniform section along its working length. Woodruff key A segment of a disk, with a curved bottom seated in a matching circular recess in the shaft. Gib-head key A tapered key with a projecting head that provides a place to engage it for removal. For a parallel key, the side fit controls how the turning load is carried. Excessive side clearance permits movement; an overly tight fit can prevent assembly. Verify the specified top clearance too. A tapered key has a different seating action, so do not apply a parallel-key fit rule to it. Use the drawing's key designation, dimensions and tolerances. Do not choose a key size from shaft diameter alone or assume that half the key height belongs in each part. Public reference for key forms Engineering Institute of Technology, Fundamentals of Mechanical Engineering introduces keys, keyways and the common key types in its keys section. No standard size table or shaft-to-key size rule is reproduced here. Practice identifying keys"},
{"title":"Mill a shaft keyseat with the cutter that matches its end and bottom shape","page":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","summary":"Identify common keys, choose a shaft or hub cutting method, and check slot location, width, depth and fit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/keys-and-keyseats.html#mill-a-shaft-keyseat-with-the-cutter-that-matches-its-end-and-bottom-shape","text":"Mill a shaft keyseat with the cutter that matches its end and bottom shape An end mill can make a straight, flat-bottom shaft keyseat that ends within the shaft's length. Its diameter leaves rounded ends in plan view; confirm that the key and drawing allow that shape. Use a permitted entry method for the selected cutter. A Woodruff keyseat cutter is a disk-shaped cutter on a shank. Its diameter forms the curved recess for a Woodruff key and its cutting width establishes the slot width. Match both dimensions to the required recess. Not every tool sold as a keyseat cutter is the correct tool for every key. Read the keyseat's width, depth, length or cutter diameter, and axial location from the drawing. Support and secure the shaft against rotation and displacement. Use Milling Vises and Strap Clamps for the setup. Align the shaft axis with the intended machine travel and locate its centerline using Mill Indicators and Part Zero. Set the cutter's cutting plane or path on that centerline, with the specified axial location and depth reference. Choose the tool and entry method with End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection and plan the cut with Milling Operations. Cut to the planned allowance, measure, then finish to the required dimensions. Review Equipment Operating Procedure and mill hazards in the safety collection before operation. Why cutter selection includes more than width Reach, neck clearance and tooth arrangement affect access and cutting behavior. See Harvey Performance's keyseat cutter considerations. A nominal tool width does not certify the width of the finished slot."},
{"title":"Broach a through hub keyway; consider slotting when the geometry needs it","page":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","summary":"Identify common keys, choose a shaft or hub cutting method, and check slot location, width, depth and fit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/keys-and-keyseats.html#broach-a-through-hub-keyway-consider-slotting-when-the-geometry-needs-it","text":"Broach a through hub keyway; consider slotting when the geometry needs it A conventional keyway broach and guide bushing are useful for a straight keyway through a hub bore. See Broaching for the pass sequence. The same arrangement does not make a closed-end keyseat on the outside of a shaft. Shaping or slotting uses a reciprocating single-point tool to remove material in successive strokes. A suitable internal slotting setup can make a hub keyway when a through broach is unsuitable. Blind work still needs a planned tool runout and chip space; the method must fit the actual geometry. Practice choosing a method"},
{"title":"Check width, depth and location before using assembly as a fit check","page":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","summary":"Identify common keys, choose a shaft or hub cutting method, and check slot location, width, depth and fit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/keys-and-keyseats.html#check-width-depth-and-location-before-using-assembly-as-a-fit-check","text":"Check width, depth and location before using assembly as a fit check Clean the feature and remove burrs without rounding the functional edges. Check width at several accessible positions using a suitable slot gauge, gauge blocks or measuring contacts. Select a tool with enough access and resolution for the tolerance; see Choosing Shop Measuring Tools. Measure depth from the drawing's stated reference. A depth instrument needs a stable reference bridge or fixture on a round shaft; resting it arbitrarily on the curved surface changes the result. For a hub, establish the specified bore-to-keyway measurement with an appropriate internal/keyway gauge or fixture. A reading across the bore and slot includes the bore dimension; it is not automatically the radial slot depth. Check length, axial position and centerline location. Compare actual measurements with the drawing limits. Check the specified key fit and assembly clearance. Investigate interference or unwanted movement instead of forcing the assembly together. A key that enters the slot is a useful functional check, but it does not prove depth, location or dimensional compliance."},
{"title":"Identify the parts that rotate, guide and feed","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#identify-the-parts-that-rotate-guide-and-feed","text":"Identify the parts that rotate, guide and feed Schematic engine lathe with headstock and spindle at left, tailstock at right, and carriage, cross slide, compound, apron, feed rod and leadscrew along the bed. Typical engine-lathe arrangement. Controls and drive arrangements vary by machine. Headstock and spindle The headstock supports the rotating spindle and its drive. The spindle carries the chuck, faceplate or other workholding. Its axis is the reference for turning. Bed and ways The bed supports the assemblies. Its guide surfaces, called ways, guide carriage travel and locate the tailstock. Tailstock Supports the far end of work with a center, or holds axial tools such as drills and machine reamers. Its quill extends along the spindle axis when aligned. Carriage, cross slide and compound rest The carriage moves along the bed. The cross slide moves across the axis. The compound sits above it and swivels to guide short angled movements. The tool post holds the cutting tool on the compound. Apron The assembly on the front of the carriage contains feed mechanisms and controls. Feed rod and leadscrew On machines with separate drives, the feed rod powers ordinary turning or facing feeds; the leadscrew synchronizes threading. Some lathes combine functions, so identify the actual drive arrangement before selecting a feed. Half-nuts and quick-change gearbox The half-nuts close around the leadscrew to drive the carriage during threading. The gearbox selects ratios for feeds or thread pitches; its chart tells you which control combinations produce each ratio."},
{"title":"Choose workholding for the shape and the surface that must run true","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#choose-workholding-for-the-shape-and-the-surface-that-must-run-true","text":"Choose workholding for the shape and the surface that must run true Running true means the reference surface is centered on the spindle axis, with little measured runout. Gripping a part does not automatically establish that alignment. Three-jaw scroll chuck Moves its jaws together for quick gripping of round or suitable hexagonal stock. Self-centering is convenient, but does not promise zero runout, especially after removing and replacing a part. Four-jaw independent chuck Moves each jaw separately. Useful for square or irregular work, intentional offsets, or indicating an existing surface true. Adjustment takes more time. Collet Grips around a matching stock shape over a limited size range. Useful for repeated work and small diameters; choose the correct collet and its permitted clamping range. Centers and drive dog Centers locate work through prepared center holes. A dog clamped to the work transmits torque from a drive plate; the centers provide support. Useful when several surfaces must share the center-hole axis or the work must be removed and returned. Faceplate Provides slots or mounting features for clamping work that does not suit chuck jaws. The fixture must locate the intended axis and account for the off-center mass. Steady rest Mounts on the bed and supports a long workpiece at a fixed position. It can support work when the end must remain accessible for drilling or boring. Follow rest Mounts to the carriage and travels with the tool. It supports slender work near the cut to reduce bending under cutting force. Its support contacts must suit the diameter being supported. For why a chuck key must never remain in the chuck, and for rotating-work precautions, see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. See Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf for protection and chip handling."},
{"title":"Match tool travel to the feature, and set the edge on center","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#match-tool-travel-to-the-feature-and-set-the-edge-on-center","text":"Match tool travel to the feature, and set the edge on center Facing feeds across an end to establish a plane perpendicular to the axis. Straight turning feeds parallel to the axis to make a cylinder. Shoulder turning joins a cylindrical diameter to a step face; the tool needs clearance to reach both surfaces without its holder or unused edge contacting the work. Center drilling prepares the seat for a supporting center. It is not automatically the best starting method for every drill. Tailstock drilling makes an axial hole; reaming removes a controlled small allowance to improve hole size and finish. Select that allowance from the reamer maker. A reamer generally follows the existing hole, so it is not a reliable way to correct a misplaced hole. Boring uses a single-point tool to enlarge an existing hole and establish its surface relative to the spindle axis. Grooving makes a recess; parting continues a similar radial cut until the part separates. Chamfering makes a bevel at an edge. Form knurling presses a pattern into the surface and increases the diameter over the raised points; cut knurling is a different process. Use spindle center height as the starting reference for ordinary turning and threading tools, then follow any tool-specific instruction. An off-center edge changes the effective rake and clearance. In facing, a tool that cannot reach the axis leaves a small central nub. Boring also needs clearance between the tool and the curved hole wall. Rake and chipbreakers depend on the whole cutting setup Rake describes the slope of the surface the chip flows over. Clearance keeps the tool below the edge from rubbing. Material matters, but hardness alone does not dictate positive, neutral or negative rake: tool material, edge strength, holder geometry and rigidity also matter. Use the tool maker's cutting geometry and range for the application. A chipbreaker guides a chip into a tighter curl so it can break. It may be molded into an insert or ground into a tool. It works within a range of feeds and depths; a very thin chip can curl without breaking. A chipbreaker is not a guarantee of short chips at every setting. Continue with Turning Tapers and Single-Point Threading. Cutting speed and feed belong on Feeds and Speeds; dimensional acceptance and finish belong on Tolerances and Fits and Surface Texture and Finish."},
{"title":"A lathe spins the work and moves the tool on two slides","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#a-lathe-spins-the-work-and-moves-the-tool-on-two-slides","text":"A lathe spins the work and moves the tool on two slides In ordinary turning the work rotates while the tool feeds along it or toward its center. This simulator models parts that are round about the spindle's axis. The shape comes from where the tool is put against the turning work, and in what order. Chuck Grips the bar and turns with the spindle. The jaws hold one end; the tool works on the part of the bar that sticks out. Carriage (Z) Carries the tool along the bed, parallel to the axis. On this page Z minus is toward the chuck and Z plus toward the tailstock. Cross slide (X) Moves the tool straight toward or away from the axis. The simulator's X readout is the diameter the tool would cut. On a real lathe, find out whether a dial or readout shows diameter or radius before you trust it. Tailstock At the far end of the bed, on the centerline. It holds the center drill, the drills and the countersink, which feed straight along the axis. Z0 Where the drawing's finished front face belongs. Every length on the drawings is measured from the front face. How to read the simulator's picture. It is the lathe seen from the front, with the whole work in one of two views; the switch under the picture changes view at any time without changing the work. Section cuts the work in half along its centerline, the way a section drawing shows a part: metal is hatched, a hole shows open, and a thread or knurl shows as its profile. Normal shows the outside, as you would see the part on the machine, with its thread and knurl; a hole is drawn as a dashed hidden line, and a tool working inside the part is hidden by it. Tools on the cross slide come in from above, in the plane the section is cut on, so a tool and the metal it cuts are in the same plane. The dashed blue outline is the drawing, placed at Z0. Tinted metal is still to come off. A heavy red-orange dashed line marks work that is already smaller than the drawing allows. On each tool, the gold edge is the part that cuts and the small ring is the point the readout follows. Before using a real lathe, read Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. The chuck key stays in your hand until it is out of the chuck."},
{"title":"Lathe simulator: make the part on the drawing","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#lathe-simulator-make-the-part-on-the-drawing","text":"Lathe simulator: make the part on the drawing The simulator needs JavaScript. Below are a picture of it part-way through a job and both views of the four parts it asks you to make. The next cards explain what each tool does. Chuck end from tailstock Z0 The stepped pin part-way through. The facing blade made the end flat; the body was rough-turned short of its shoulder. Its angled allowance still needs radial facing. The rougher is halfway along the first pin pass. The tinted metal is still to come off. Each target is shown in a complete Normal outside view and a complete Section through its centerline. Dashed lines in Normal identify hidden internal features. Section thread bands are schematic crest/root envelopes; the knurl remains a surface callout. Dimensions are nominal inches; the simulator's feature table gives the limits. Target drawing: Stepped pin. Normal: outside view Dashed lines show hidden internal features. Stepped pin, 1.500 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Pin: Ø0.500, from 0.000 to 0.625 in from the front face. Body: Ø0.750, from 0.625 to 1.250 in from the front face. Head: Ø1.000 (bar size), from 1.250 to 1.500 in from the front face. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. Normal: outside view 0.625 1.250 1.500 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer Ø1.000 (bar size) Ø0.750 Ø0.500 Target drawing: Stepped pin. Section: through the centerline Hatching marks the material cut by the section plane. Stepped pin, 1.500 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Pin: Ø0.500, from 0.000 to 0.625 in from the front face. Body: Ø0.750, from 0.625 to 1.250 in from the front face. Head: Ø1.000 (bar size), from 1.250 to 1.500 in from the front face. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. Section: through the centerline 0.625 1.250 1.500 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer Ø1.000 (bar size) Ø0.750 Ø0.500 Stepped pin. Two turned diameters, a chamfer on the end and a head left at bar size. From 1.000 in bar. Facing, turning in passes, square shoulders, chamfering and parting off. Target drawing: Bushing. Normal: outside view Dashed lines show hidden internal features. Bushing, 1.000 in long, from 1.250 in bar. Outside: Ø1.125, from 0.000 to 1.000 in from the front face. Bore inside: Ø0.625, through. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the back edge. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the mouth of the hole. Normal: outside view 1.000 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer 0.030 × 45° at the hole Hole Ø0.625, through 0.030 × 45° chamfer Ø1.125 Target drawing: Bushing. Section: through the centerline Hatching marks the material cut by the section plane. Bushing, 1.000 in long, from 1.250 in bar. Outside: Ø1.125, from 0.000 to 1.000 in from the front face. Bore inside: Ø0.625, through. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the back edge. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the mouth of the hole. Section: through the centerline 1.000 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer 0.030 × 45° at the hole Hole Ø0.625, through 0.030 × 45° chamfer Ø1.125 Bushing. A turned sleeve with a through bore, two outside chamfers and a chamfer at the bore mouth. From 1.250 in bar. Center drilling, drilling, boring, countersinking and chamfering a back edge before parting. Target drawing: Thumb screw. Normal: outside view Dashed lines show hidden internal features. Thumb screw, 1.250 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Thread: Ø0.375, 16 threads per inch, from 0.000 to 0.625 in from the front face. Relief groove: Groove Ø0.290, from 0.625 to 0.750 in from the front face. Knurled head: Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points, from 0.750 to 1.250 in from the front face. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. Normal: outside view 0.625 0.750 1.250 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points Groove Ø0.290 Ø0.375, 16 threads per inch Target drawing: Thumb screw. Section: through the centerline Thread bands are schematic crest/root envelopes, not a literal helical cut face. Thumb screw, 1.250 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Thread: Ø0.375, 16 threads per inch, from 0.000 to 0.625 in from the front face. Relief groove: Groove Ø0.290, from 0.625 to 0.750 in from the front face. Knurled head: Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points, from 0.750 to 1.250 in from the front face. 0.030 in by 45 degree chamfer at the front edge. Section: through the centerline Thread envelopes (schematic) 0.625 0.750 1.250 front face 0.030 × 45° chamfer Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points Groove Ø0.290 Ø0.375, 16 threads per inch Thumb screw. A knurled head, a thread relief groove and a 16-per-inch thread. From 1.000 in bar. Knurling, grooving, external threading in passes, and why a thread needs somewhere to stop. Target drawing: Knurled nut. Normal: outside view Dashed lines show hidden internal features. Knurled nut, 0.500 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Knurled outside: Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points, from 0.000 to 0.500 in from the front face. Thread inside: 13 threads per inch, through. Normal: outside view 0.500 front face 13 threads per inch inside, through Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points Target drawing: Knurled nut. Section: through the centerline Thread bands are schematic crest/root envelopes, not a literal helical cut face. Knurled nut, 0.500 in long, from 1.000 in bar. Knurled outside: Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points, from 0.000 to 0.500 in from the front face. Thread inside: 13 threads per inch, through. Section: through the centerline Thread envelopes (schematic) 0.500 front face 13 threads per inch inside, through Diamond knurl, Ø0.880 over points Knurled nut. A knurled ring with a 13-per-inch thread inside it. From 1.000 in bar. Drilling to the thread's inside size, internal threading and parting off a ring. Pick a part and read its drawing ( Drawing opens it). Then make it from bar: choose a tool, start the spindle, and move the tool against the work. Measure the part against the drawing whenever you stop. Move the tool by dragging it in the picture, one slide at a time as if you were turning one handwheel; with the handwheel buttons, which repeat while held; with the arrow keys once the picture is selected; or by typing a position and pressing Feed to Z or Feed to X. Stop feed, or Escape, stops a feed part-way. The spindle has to be running for anything to cut, and stopped before you change a tool, change the drill size or thread pitch, or measure the part. A caliper or micrometer never goes on turning work. Each tool does only its own kind of work. When a move would ask a tool for something it cannot do, the simulator stops the tool there and says why. What the tool in use can and cannot do is listed under the tool rack. Turn / face offers two actual tools in its Cutting tool selector; stop the spindle before changing between them. Undo takes back one cut at a time. New bar starts the part again, and undo brings the old bar back. Measure compares every feature with the drawing. The list under the simulator says what each feature still needs, or whether it has already gone too far to save."},
{"title":"Each simulator tool has a defined job; its form knurl displaces metal","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#each-simulator-tool-has-a-defined-job-its-form-knurl-displaces-metal","text":"Each simulator tool has a defined job; its form knurl displaces metal Facing, turning, drilling, threading and knurling are not the same action with a different shape of tool. Each tool reaches different places and moves in different ways. Turning tool The roughing tool supplied here has a 10&deg; lead and a 0.008 in nose radius. Its angled edge reaches ahead of its readout point. Stopping an axial pass leaves an angled shoulder; leave allowance instead of running the nose to the finished shoulder plane. Facing tool A square, 1/16 in wide finishing blade set for radial feed. Put its left corner at the finished Z and feed X across the end or shoulder. Its actual sweep removes the allowance and leaves the face flat. Retract clear before moving Z. These are the simulator's selected tools; other turning tools and settings can both turn and face. Chamfer tool A 90&deg; point. Plunged at an edge, each side cuts a 45&deg; chamfer. Grooving and parting blade Cuts only on its front edge. Plunged part of the way it makes a groove; plunged to the center, or into the hole, it parts the work off. Center drill, drill, countersink Fed from the tailstock along the axis. A center drill makes a small pilot and a 60&deg; seat for a lathe center; a spotting drill has different starting geometry. The twist drill makes the hole and the countersink chamfers its mouth. Choose a real drill's starting method and spot angle from its maker's guidance. Boring bar Turns the inside of a hole that is already there, the way the turning tool works on the outside. Its bar has to fit in the hole. Threading tools Form tools with a 60&deg; point, one for outside threads and one on a bar for inside threads. For a threading pass the carriage is driven by the leadscrew, so the tool moves one thread lead for every turn of the work; for the single-start threads here, lead equals pitch. Knurling tool A straddle head with two opposed forming wheels, with left-hand and right-hand diagonal teeth. Their axles lie parallel to Z; together they roll a diamond pattern. Gap Ø is a simulated gap/contact-diameter control. A real tool's arm adjustment and locking are separate from cross-slide infeed. The picture shows the barrels and axles in side projection; the highlighted rims are the forming contact. What makes the simulator stop a move. Each of these is something the real tool cannot do, or should not be asked to: The spindle is stopped, so the tool would only rub. More of the cutting edge would be in the work than the tool takes in one pass. Take it in more passes. A part of the tool that does not cut would touch the work: the holder, the top of an insert, the bar of a boring tool, the side of a blade. The tool would hit the chuck jaws. A threading tool is still in the thread when the carriage moves. Back it out first. A knurl is pressed past full depth, or into a shoulder. A drill is three of its own diameters into a cut without being backed out to clear its chips. The simulator's limits are teaching values chosen for these four parts. A real tool's limits come from its maker and depend on the machine, the material and the setup. Tool shape and setting matter: Sherline's tool-grinding guide describes cutting edges, clearance and form tools. The particular blade widths, nose radius and operating limits used here are declared teaching choices, not specifications for every lathe tool."},
{"title":"Most parts start with a facing cut and end with parting","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#most-parts-start-with-a-facing-cut-and-end-with-parting","text":"Most parts start with a facing cut and end with parting More than one order can make the same part, and the simulator accepts any order that gets there. Some orders are still better than others, for reasons you can see in the work. Face first. The faced end is the datum: every length on the drawing is measured from it. A sawn end is neither flat nor square to the axis. Rough, then finish. A large step comes off in several passes, and only the last one goes to the size on the drawing. On the stepped pin, stop the roughing passes 0.040 in short of each shoulder, retract clear and change to the facing tool. With its left corner at the final shoulder Z, feed X to the smaller diameter. A deeper shoulder needs more clearance for the angled edge; the facing blade can remove its allowance in slices up to 0.0625 in wide. Drill, then bore to size. The boring bar needs a hole its bar fits in. A center drill is available here to explore its pilot and center-seat shape, but it is not a universal prerequisite for drilling. On a real machine, use the starting method recommended for the following drill. Heavy work before thin sections. Knurling pushes hard on the work. Knurling before the thin sections next to it are turned keeps the work stiff while it happens. Make room for the threading tool. A thread needs somewhere for the tool to stop: a relief groove, or a stop short of the shoulder. Chamfer the end first so the thread starts on a clean edge. Chamfer the back edge before parting. Once the part is cut off, its back edge is out of reach without a second setup. Part off last. The part falls free, and anything not done to it by then needs another setup. The small Chuck end circle is a separate axial view, looking from the tailstock toward the chuck. Its forward-rotation arrow and animation run counterclockwise in that view. The main picture is longitudinal, not a view along the spindle. See the viewpoint diagram in Haas's spindle-direction training, page 16."},
{"title":"A thread is cut in several passes along one helix","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#a-thread-is-cut-in-several-passes-along-one-helix","text":"A thread is cut in several passes along one helix For a threading pass the carriage is coupled to the leadscrew, so the tool advances exactly one lead for every turn of the work. For the single-start threads here, that is one pitch: 1/16 in for 16 threads per inch. Every pass follows the same helix, so every pass deepens the same groove. The section uses a schematic thread envelope. Its unhatched bands lie between the surviving crests and dashed roots; they are not solid annular thread teeth. A real single-start helix intersects opposite sides half a pitch apart. The model samples a thread profile for depth checks and preserves its material envelope for later cuts and parting. The carriage still stops at the requested Z, even between full turns. Sandvik explains the lead/pitch relation for single- and multi-start threads. A little deeper each pass. The simulator allows 0.015 in of infeed per pass outside and 0.012 in inside. The thread is done when its roots reach the root diameter on the drawing. Back out before you come back. At the end of a pass the tool is still in the groove. Bringing the carriage back without backing the tool out would cut the crests off. Keep the pitch. A pass at another pitch cuts a second helix across the first: a crossed thread. Leave somewhere to stop. The tool runs into a shoulder unless a relief groove gives it room, which is why the thumb screw has one. How a manual lathe keeps every pass on one helix The carriage is coupled to the leadscrew by closing the half-nut. On a lathe with an inch leadscrew cutting an inch thread, the thread dial shows when to close it so that each pass drops into the same groove. Many machinists also feed the tool in with the compound travel set near 30&deg; from the radial cross-feed direction, so that most of the cut is taken by one flank of the tool. The simulator simplifies all of this. It keeps every pass on one helix for you, feeds the tool straight in, and checks a simplified 60&deg; thread by its crest and root diameters. A real thread is checked against a thread table with thread gauges or measuring wires."},
{"title":"A form knurl moves metal instead of cutting it","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#a-form-knurl-moves-metal-instead-of-cutting-it","text":"A form knurl moves metal instead of cutting it The knurling tool makes no chips. Its wheels press a pattern into the turning work, and the metal they push aside has to go somewhere: it rises between their teeth. The diameter over the points ends up larger than the blank was. Blank The diameter turned before knurling. On these drawings it is not given: the knurled size is, and you work back to the blank. Full points The wheels pressed deep enough that the diamond points are fully formed. Too shallow leaves flat tops; pressing past full depth crushes and flakes the points. Over the points The knurled diameter, measured across the crests. In the simulator the roots sink exactly as far as the crests rise, 0.010 in each at full depth, so a full knurl adds 0.020 in to the diameter. A real knurl's growth depends on its pitch, the material and how hard it is pressed. This paired-wheel forming arrangement supports feeding along Z after engagement. Dorian's straddle setup instructions (H-19) and paired diagonal-wheel example (H-48) describe that combination. The drawing is a generic teaching head, not a dimensioned catalog tool or a setup instruction for a particular machine."},
{"title":"Things to try in the simulator","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#things-to-try-in-the-simulator","text":"Things to try in the simulator On the stepped pin, try to turn the body from 1.000 to 0.750 in in one pass. Read why the tool stops, then do it in two. Stop a roughing pass short of a shoulder and Measure. Choose Face without cutting and measure again. Then make the radial facing cut: which geometry actually changed? Face the end but stop the cut at X 0.200. Measure: what is left at the center? Face 0.010 in past Z0 and make the rest of the pin to the readout. Measure the lengths. Which ones moved, and why? On the bushing, bring the boring bar to the end before there is a hole. Then drill a 1/4 in hole and try again. Which drill gives the bar room? Drill the bushing without the center drill and read the note under the inspection. On the thumb screw, try a threading pass up to the head before cutting the relief groove. Take one threading pass at 16 per inch, change to 13 and take another. Measure the thread. Knurl the head straight on the 1.000 in bar, without turning a blank first. Measure it against the drawing. On the knurled nut, part the ring off and watch where the blade breaks through."},
{"title":"What this simulator leaves out","page":"Lathe Operations","summary":"Identify lathe parts, choose workholding and tools, and explore turning operations in a simulator before studying tapers and threads.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-operations.html#what-this-simulator-leaves-out","text":"What this simulator leaves out The simulator is about which tool does what, in what order, to what size. It leaves out: Speed and feed. The spindle is on or off. Spindle speed, feed rate and what they do to the cut are on Feeds and Speeds. The cut itself. No tool wear, heat, chatter, deflection or surface finish. A drill cannot wander, so a hole started without a center drill is only noted, not drawn off center. Setups. One setup only: no turning the part around, no second operation, no tailstock center supporting long work. Threads and knurls, simplified. Thread pitch, crests and roots are checked along the required length; pitch diameter and functional thread fit are not verified. The form knurl illustrates displaced material and diameter growth. Its patterned section is schematic, not an exact volume-conservation calculation. Form and cut knurling use different mechanisms. Its numbers. Inch only. The tolerances, the tool limits and the parts are teaching values, not tool-maker data and not a standard. Tolerances on the drawings mean the same as on Tolerances and Fits: a size on a limit passes. The starting method for a real hole depends on the drill. For example, Haas distinguishes center drilling from spotting for carbide twist drills; this model does not predict drill wandering."},
{"title":"A taper changes diameter at a constant rate","page":"Turning Tapers","summary":"Calculate taper rate, compound angle and tailstock offset, then choose a method and check both slope and size.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-tapers.html#a-taper-changes-diameter-at-a-constant-rate","text":"A taper changes diameter at a constant rate A straight taper is a cone, or part of one. Name the large diameter D, small diameter d and axial length of the tapered portion l. Use the same length unit for all three. Taper rate = (D &minus; d) / l. With inch dimensions, this is taper per inch, often abbreviated TPI. Here TPI means diameter change per inch of length; in Single-Point Threading, the same abbreviation means threads per inch. Write the units to distinguish them. TPF = 12 &times; TPI, and TPI = TPF / 12. Taper per foot describes the diameter change over twelve inches; the actual tapered portion need not be a foot long. Check the rate with a fresh example A taper goes from 1.100 to 0.950 in over 3.000 in. Its diameter changes 0.150 in, so the rate is 0.050 in/in, or 0.600 in/ft. At half the length, the diameter has changed half as much: 0.075 in. These are teaching dimensions, not a standard machine taper."},
{"title":"Set the compound to the half-angle measured from the spindle axis","page":"Turning Tapers","summary":"Calculate taper rate, compound angle and tailstock offset, then choose a method and check both slope and size.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-tapers.html#set-the-compound-to-the-half-angle-measured-from-the-spindle-axis","text":"Set the compound to the half-angle measured from the spindle axis The tool travels along one side of the cone. Its angle to the spindle axis is the half-angle &alpha;; the angle between the two sides is the included angle 2&alpha;. &alpha; = atan((D &minus; d) / (2l)) = atan(TPI / 2). Use degree mode for a setting in degrees. A rate of 0.050 in/in gives &alpha; = 1.432&deg;, while the included angle is 2.864&deg;. Swivel the compound so its travel makes that half-angle with the spindle axis, then feed along the compound. The taper length is limited by compound travel. Check how the lathe labels its scale: a scale measured from cross-slide travel uses the complementary angle, 90&deg; &minus; &alpha;. The taper half-angle has radial rise (D minus d) divided by two and axial run l; the included angle is twice the half-angle. The radius changes by half the diameter change. The drawing exaggerates the slope. Why the factor of two belongs in the angle formula Cut the cone through its axis. One side forms a right triangle whose rise is (D &minus; d)/2 and whose run is l. Tangent is rise divided by run. Using the whole diameter change as the rise gives the wrong tool path."},
{"title":"Tailstock offset uses the full supported work length","page":"Turning Tapers","summary":"Calculate taper rate, compound angle and tailstock offset, then choose a method and check both slope and size.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-tapers.html#tailstock-offset-uses-the-full-supported-work-length","text":"Tailstock offset uses the full supported work length For a shallow external taper turned between centers, offsetting the tailstock tilts the work while the carriage still travels parallel to the bed. The usual small-angle setup estimate is offset &asymp; TPI &times; L / 2 = (D &minus; d)L / (2l). L is the total work length between the effective center support points, approximated by the total part length in elementary problems. It is not just the length l of the tapered portion. Center-hole geometry matters in an actual setup. Use inches for L when the taper rate is in in/in; if given TPF, first divide it by 12. The full length L spans the two center supports; offset s shifts the tailstock sideways. A shorter taper length l does not replace L in the offset calculation. Plan view: offset is transverse to the spindle axis, not a vertical change in center height. For a rate of 0.050 in/in and L = 8.000 in, the estimate is 0.200 in even if only 3.000 in is tapered. Machine offset capacity and the center arrangement limit this method. Check a trial cut and restore center alignment before straight turning. Why this is an estimate rather than an exact setting The taper gives tan(&alpha;) = TPI/2. If L is the actual distance along the tilted work between support points, the ideal geometric offset is L sin(&alpha;). At shallow angles sin(&alpha;) and tan(&alpha;) are nearly equal. The usual formula substitutes L tan(&alpha;); real center contact and setup error add further differences. It is not a method for steep cones."},
{"title":"Choose the taper method for length, access and support","page":"Turning Tapers","summary":"Calculate taper rate, compound angle and tailstock offset, then choose a method and check both slope and size.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-tapers.html#choose-the-taper-method-for-length-access-and-support","text":"Choose the taper method for length, access and support Compound rest Useful for short external or internal tapers. The compound guides the tool at the half-angle, but its limited travel restricts length. Tailstock offset Useful for shallow external tapers between centers. It changes the work axis and does not provide a method for boring an internal taper. Taper attachment A guide coordinates cross-slide motion with carriage travel. It can produce internal or external tapers within its travel and angle limits while the work stays aligned with the spindle. Connection and cross-feed arrangements depend on the attachment. Form tool A shaped cutting edge produces a short taper by feeding across the axis. A wide edge engages much of the cut at once, increasing cutting load and the tendency to chatter. Start with Lathe Operations for the machine and workholding. Use Feeds and Speeds for cutting conditions and Surface Texture and Finish for the finish left by the cut."},
{"title":"A matching taper needs both the right slope and the right size","page":"Turning Tapers","summary":"Calculate taper rate, compound angle and tailstock offset, then choose a method and check both slope and size.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/lathe-tapers.html#a-matching-taper-needs-both-the-right-slope-and-the-right-size","text":"A matching taper needs both the right slope and the right size Measure diameters at two known axial positions to check the rate. A correct rate can still leave the entire taper too large or too small. A suitable taper plug or ring gauge checks the mating geometry; contact pattern and seating position answer different questions. Morse tapers are commonly used for centers and tool shanks. Jacobs tapers commonly join a drill chuck to its arbor. They are separate families: identify the exact mating designation before selecting a tool or adapter. No dimensions or angles should be inferred from the family name alone. No standard taper table is reproduced here. Use the specified component drawing and verified manufacturer or standard dimensions for a real fit; see Tolerances and Fits. Practice taper rate Practice half-angle Practice offset"},
{"title":"Start layout from a reference you can return to","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#start-layout-from-a-reference-you-can-return-to","text":"Start layout from a reference you can return to Layout places lines, arcs, and points on a part to guide cutting or locate features. A neat line is useful only when it is in the right place. Identify the reference surfaces from which dimensions are taken. Keep using those references rather than measuring each new line from the last one. A layout mark guides the work; it does not prove the finished part is within tolerance. A surface plate supplies a reference plane for layout and inspection. Clean the plate, the part's supporting face, and the tool base. Support the part so it cannot rock. Use an appropriate angle plate or V-block when the shape needs support. Protect the plate from impact and abrasive work. Its flatness depends on its size, grade, condition, and calibration; “a surface plate” does not imply one universal accuracy. Follow its specified support arrangement. Level and flat are different: leveling a plate does not repair a worn surface."},
{"title":"Dye makes a fine scribed line easier to see","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#dye-makes-a-fine-scribed-line-easier-to-see","text":"Dye makes a fine scribed line easier to see Layout dye adds contrast. Prepare the surface, apply a thin coating as directed, and let it dry before marking. Use Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets for chemical handling guidance. A scriber scratches a narrow line through the coating. Hold the guide firmly and draw the point along it with light, consistent pressure. Repeated passes can make a wide, ambiguous mark. Scribe only where the drawing and process allow a permanent scratch. A surface gage carries an adjustable scriber at a fixed height above its base. Set it from a separate height reference, then move the base along the plate to scribe a line parallel to that plane. A surface gage ordinarily has no direct height scale. A height gage adds a scale or readout. Set its reference correctly, seat the base flat, set and lock the height as intended, and make the line. With a suitable probe it can compare or measure heights. It is not simply another name for a depth gage. Finding the middle of two heights If the lower edge of a slot is 0.600 in above the reference and the upper edge is 0.900 in above it, the center is (0.600 + 0.900) / 2 = 0.750 in above that reference. Half the difference, 0.150 in, is half the slot height; it still must be added to the lower height."},
{"title":"Dividers and trammels carry a distance into an arc","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#dividers-and-trammels-carry-a-distance-into-an-arc","text":"Dividers and trammels carry a distance into an arc Dividers have two pointed legs. Set their spacing from a suitable scale, hold one point at the center, and swing the other to mark a circle or arc. They can also transfer or step off equal distances. Trammels put adjustable points on a beam for radii too large for ordinary dividers. Lock the points after setting, support the beam, and check the spacing again before marking. The point spacing is the radius, not the diameter. For a 3.00 in diameter circle, set 1.50 in between points. Recheck transferred dimensions against the original reference; repeated stepping can accumulate error."},
{"title":"Prick-punch the location; center-punch after checking it","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#prick-punch-the-location-center-punch-after-checking-it","text":"Prick-punch the location; center-punch after checking it A prick punch has a sharper point for a small, precise location mark at intersecting layout lines. A center punch makes a wider depression to help start a drilling operation. Point angles vary by tool; the important distinction here is the fine locating mark versus the wider starting mark. Locate the point carefully, bring the punch upright, and make a light mark. Check the location before enlarging it. A heavy first mark is difficult to correct. Support the work on a suitable punching support, away from the precision surface plate. A punch mark is not a substitute for the machine setup or spotting method needed to locate a hole accurately. Check the finished feature with a suitable measuring method. Use Personal Protective Equipment and Equipment Operating Procedure for the applicable shop rules. Punch types and their uses Starrett's punch guide explains the sharper prick punch, the wider center punch, and why the locating mark is checked before it is enlarged."},
{"title":"A combination set uses one blade with three different heads","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#a-combination-set-uses-one-blade-with-three-different-heads","text":"A combination set uses one blade with three different heads Square head Provides 90&deg; and 45&deg; reference faces. With the blade it can lay out right angles, check squareness, or establish an offset or depth. Seat the reference face fully before reading or scribing. Center head Its two faces seat on round stock, positioning the blade along a diameter. Scribe one line, rotate the work or head, and scribe another; their intersection locates the center on the end face. Protractor head Sets the blade to an angle on a graduated head. Read the scale in the correct direction, lock the setting, and recheck contact with the reference. Clean the mating faces and blade groove so the head seats properly. Pick the tool's accuracy for the task: a combination set is useful for layout, but it does not automatically replace a precision square or a finer angle instrument."},
{"title":"A universal bevel transfers an angle; a vernier protractor reads it","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#a-universal-bevel-transfers-an-angle-a-vernier-protractor-reads-it","text":"A universal bevel transfers an angle; a vernier protractor reads it A universal bevel has an adjustable blade and stock that can fit against two surfaces. Lock it to transfer or compare an angle. A plain bevel has no numerical scale; obtain the angle from a separate reference. A vernier bevel protractor combines the adjustable blade with degree and vernier scales. On a 5-minute instrument, each matching vernier division adds 5 arcminutes. 60 minutes = 1 degree, so 5 minutes is 1/12 degree. This is the reading increment, not a universal accuracy claim. Seat the stock and blade against the intended surfaces. Identify the angle being measured. Read the whole degrees between the main zero and the vernier zero. Use the vernier on the same side and in the same reading direction as those degrees. Find the matching division. Add the matching division number &times; 5 minutes to the whole degrees. Example: 32 whole degrees and the seventh matching vernier division give 32&deg; + 7 &times; 5&prime; = 32&deg;35&prime;. That is 32 + 35/60 = 32.5833&hellip;&deg;, not 32.35&deg;. Check the contact geometry before reporting an obtuse angle. If the instrument has measured the acute supplement, a reading of 32&deg;35&prime; corresponds to 180&deg; &minus; 32&deg;35&prime; = 147&deg;25&prime; for the required obtuse angle. Do not automatically subtract every reading from 180&deg;. Practice bevel-protractor readings Why the 5-minute vernier works On the arrangement described in the Starrett catalog, page 306, 12 vernier spaces span 23 main-scale degrees. Each vernier space is 23/12 degrees, or 1&deg;55&prime;: 5 minutes short of two degrees. The line alignment reveals that difference. Page 308 distinguishes a plain universal bevel from the graduated protractor used with it."},
{"title":"Check the layout before making a permanent cut","page":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","summary":"Carry dimensions from a reference onto the work with clear lines, checked centers, and correctly read angles.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/layout-and-angle-measurement.html#check-the-layout-before-making-a-permanent-cut","text":"Check the layout before making a permanent cut Confirm the reference surfaces and dimension directions. Check each line or center from the intended reference, including radius versus diameter. Verify the height or angle after locking the tool. Check the mark before punching more deeply or beginning the cut. Plan how to measure the finished feature; use Choosing Shop Measuring Tools and Tolerances and Fits. Drawing views and dimensioning conventions belong in the Engineering Graphics collection. Layout follows those requirements; it does not redefine them."},
{"title":"Start with what removes the material","page":"Material-Removal Process Families","summary":"Sort machine tools by how they remove material, then follow the process that fits the feature.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/material-removal-processes.html#start-with-what-removes-the-material","text":"Start with what removes the material A machine tool controls motion between a tool and the workpiece, the part being made. In material removal, the final shape is what remains after unwanted material is taken away. Material removal branches into chip-forming tools, abrasive grains, and nontraditional energy sources. This is a teaching map of removal mechanisms. Abrasives also make small chips; the branches distinguish the kind of tool or energy doing the work. CNC is a control method, not a removal family. Computer numerical control can guide a mill, lathe, grinder, EDM machine or cutting jet. See G-code and Toolpaths. Forming moves material without removing it; see Manufacturing Process Categories for the wider manufacturing map."},
{"title":"Conventional cutting uses defined cutting edges","page":"Material-Removal Process Families","summary":"Sort machine tools by how they remove material, then follow the process that fits the feature.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/material-removal-processes.html#conventional-cutting-uses-defined-cutting-edges","text":"Conventional cutting uses defined cutting edges Turning rotates the work past a cutting tool; milling rotates the cutter and feeds it relative to the work. Drilling makes holes; sawing separates stock, the starting material, with successive teeth. Broaching uses a sequence of teeth to progressively cut a profile, such as a keyway. Dedicated tooling often suits repeated production, but broaching is not limited to large runs. Shaping and planing use a back-and-forth straight cutting stroke. A shaper normally moves the tool; a planer normally moves the work. A process name describes the action, not every possible shape. Milling can make curved contours as well as flat faces."},
{"title":"Abrasive processes use many small cutting grains","page":"Material-Removal Process Families","summary":"Sort machine tools by how they remove material, then follow the process that fits the feature.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/material-removal-processes.html#abrasive-processes-use-many-small-cutting-grains","text":"Abrasive processes use many small cutting grains Grinding wheels hold abrasive grains that remove material. Precision grinding controls size and surface texture, often on hardened parts. Grinding can also remove substantial stock; it is not restricted to light finishing. Honing commonly uses abrasive stones with combined motions to refine a bore's size, form and surface. Lapping uses fine abrasive between a lap and the work to refine a surface. Neither is simply another name for grinding."},
{"title":"Nontraditional processes change the removal mechanism","page":"Material-Removal Process Families","summary":"Sort machine tools by how they remove material, then follow the process that fits the feature.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/material-removal-processes.html#nontraditional-processes-change-the-removal-mechanism","text":"Nontraditional processes change the removal mechanism Thermal: EDM, laser and plasma remove material with concentrated heat. EDM's energy supply is electrical, but its removal mechanism is thermal. Electrochemical: ECM uses electric current to dissolve metal. Chemical: Chemical Milling and Etching uses an etchant to dissolve exposed material. Mechanical energy: waterjets and Ultrasonic Machining erode material with moving water or abrasive particles. Use Selecting a Material-Removal Process to narrow the candidates. Safety references: Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Equipment Operating Procedure and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Choose from the tolerance and the shape of the feature","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#choose-from-the-tolerance-and-the-shape-of-the-feature","text":"Choose from the tolerance and the shape of the feature Start with what must be measured: an outside size, an opening, a depth, a height, or a small change from a reference. As a rule of thumb, choose a tool that reads about ten times finer than the total tolerance. This is a starting point, not a standard or a guarantee that the measurement is good enough. Check its specified accuracy, condition, measuring range, and the way its contacts reach the part. For a size of 0.625 &plusmn; 0.001 in, the total tolerance is 0.002 in. A starting resolution target is 0.002 / 10 = 0.0002 in. An outside micrometer reading 0.0001 in is a candidate for an accessible outside diameter. A 0.001 in caliper is too coarse for that rule of thumb. See Tolerances and Fits for the allowed size range, Reading Measuring Scales for the numbers, and Reliable Shop Measurement for the technique."},
{"title":"Resolution is the increment; accuracy is agreement with a reference","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#resolution-is-the-increment-accuracy-is-agreement-with-a-reference","text":"Resolution is the increment; accuracy is agreement with a reference Resolution The smallest indicated change or scale increment. A display that changes by 0.0005 in has that resolution; its last digit is not an accuracy certificate. Accuracy How closely a measurement agrees with the reference value. Use the instrument's error specification and checks against suitable standards to judge its capability. Precision How closely repeated measurements agree under stated conditions. A dirty measuring face can give repeatable readings that are all wrong. Extra digits cannot remove jaw wear, a tilted contact, dirt, or heat. Record the instrument and units along with the result when the measurement matters."},
{"title":"Use a steel rule for layout and quick size checks","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#use-a-steel-rule-for-layout-and-quick-size-checks","text":"Use a steel rule for layout and quick size checks A steel rule is useful for stock lengths, approximate sizes, and setting a divider. Read the graduation pattern on the edge you are using: fractional edges may divide an inch into 8, 16, 32, or 64 parts; decimal edges may use 0.1, 0.05, 0.02, or 0.01 in increments. A decimal rule does not ordinarily have 0.001 in graduations. Place the rule along the distance being measured and look straight at the marks. A damaged end is a poor zero. If you start at the 1 in line instead, subtract 1 in from the far-end reading. Do not use a rule to accept a feature with a tight size tolerance."},
{"title":"Calipers cover outside, inside, depth, and step measurements","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#calipers-cover-outside-inside-depth-and-step-measurements","text":"Calipers cover outside, inside, depth, and step measurements Dial, digital, and vernier calipers use a sliding jaw. The larger jaws measure outside sizes; the smaller jaws measure openings. A depth rod reaches down from the end of the beam. On tools with step faces, the back shoulders measure a step. The readout changes how you read the tool, not how carefully you must position it. Readout Use and reading Watch for Dial Quick mechanical reading: beam scale plus dial remainder. Check the marked value per revolution. Dirt in the rack can disturb the reading. Digital Direct numerical display; often offers inch/mm and relative zero. Confirm units and zero mode. Resolution varies by model; a common display increment is 0.0005 in / 0.01 mm. Vernier Main scale plus the aligned vernier division; no battery or dial mechanism. Read the correct scale and its least count. Inch 0.001 in tools can use different main-scale spacings. Use light contact and keep the jaws square. Measure near the beam where access allows. Long jaws can flex; small inside jaws may not reach the true diameter of a small hole. A caliper's versatility does not make it the best tool for every tolerance."},
{"title":"Micrometers specialize in outside sizes, inside sizes, or depths","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#micrometers-specialize-in-outside-sizes-inside-sizes-or-depths","text":"Micrometers specialize in outside sizes, inside sizes, or depths An outside micrometer measures between a fixed anvil and a moving spindle. Choose a range that contains the dimension: a 1&ndash;2 in instrument does not start from zero like a 0&ndash;1 in instrument. A conventional inch scale reads 0.001 in; a sleeve vernier can add 0.0001 in readings. A conventional metric scale reads 0.01 mm. Digital models differ. Outside micrometer with the frame, fixed anvil, moving spindle, sleeve, thimble, lock, and force-limiting ratchet labeled. The gap between the faces is the measured size. The sleeve supplies the coarse reading; turning the thimble moves the spindle. The ratchet or friction device helps control contact force. An inside micrometer spans an opening using internal contacts. Rod and two-point types require careful alignment; three-point types have a different contact arrangement. Check the reference and any extension length for the actual tool. A depth micrometer rests its flat base on the reference face and extends a rod to the lower surface. Keep the base seated. Check the installed rod's range and the scale direction: many depth scales run opposite to an outside micrometer. Do not add a rod length without checking how that instrument is marked."},
{"title":"Indicators show change from a reference","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#indicators-show-change-from-a-reference","text":"Indicators show change from a reference A dial indicator commonly has a plunger moving along its axis. A test indicator has a pivoting lever stylus and usually a shorter measuring range. Both are useful for alignment, comparing heights, and observing runout as a part turns. Read the marked graduation and count revolutions where needed. Zeroing the bezel sets a reference; it does not turn the indicator into an absolute diameter tool. Total indicator reading, or TIR, is the maximum indication minus the minimum during a sweep. From &minus;0.001 to +0.002 in, TIR is 0.003 in. Use a rigid stand. A magnetic base needs a suitable magnetic support; it will not attach magnetically to granite. For plunger indicators, align motion with the plunger. For lever test indicators, follow the maker's stylus-angle instructions; the lever is normally close to parallel with the surface. These are different geometries. What a sweep can and cannot establish A sweep combines the part's shape with its orientation and support. Runout is not a direct measurement of formal concentricity. A height sweep relative to a plate can reveal variation, but calling it a flatness inspection requires the proper setup and evaluation. Follow the drawing's specified inspection method."},
{"title":"Gauge blocks supply a reference; transfer gauges carry a size","page":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","summary":"Match the tool to the feature, the allowed size range, and the measurement you need to trust.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/measurement-tools.html#gauge-blocks-supply-a-reference-transfer-gauges-carry-a-size","text":"Gauge blocks supply a reference; transfer gauges carry a size Gauge blocks have accurately finished opposing faces and a known length. Use suitable blocks to set a height, compare a measurement, or check an instrument. Clean and inspect the faces before wringing blocks together; follow their care instructions. A zero check alone is not a full calibration. Build stacks from sizes actually present in the set. For example, 1.0000 + 0.1340 + 0.1005 = 1.2345 in uses three blocks if those sizes are available. Do not assume a set contains separate 0.0040 or 0.0005 in blocks. A telescoping gauge transfers a bore size to an outside micrometer. With light locking friction, set the contacts slightly across the bore at an angle and sweep gently through the diameter; withdraw without changing the setting, then measure across the contacts. Follow the tool's instructions for locking and repeat until the technique is consistent. A small-hole gauge expands rounded contacts inside a smaller opening. Adjust for light drag, withdraw as its design permits, and measure across the contacts with a micrometer. Neither transfer tool has its own numerical readout. Take readings at several depths and directions; keep them separate to reveal taper or out-of-roundness rather than averaging the shape away. Practice choosing a tool Instrument references Consult the actual tool's instructions and specifications. The Mitutoyo Quick Guide covers micrometers, calipers, height gages, gauge blocks, and indicators. The Starrett catalog separates graduations from accuracy and illustrates several instrument arrangements. The Mitutoyo ABSOLUTE caliper specifications provide examples of 0.0005 in / 0.01 mm display increments."},
{"title":"A test indicator sweeps with a lever; a dial indicator measures plunger travel","page":"Mill Indicators and Part Zero","summary":"Choose an indicator for alignment, account for edge-finder radius, and keep machine home, work zero and tool length separate.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-indicating-and-zero.html#a-test-indicator-sweeps-with-a-lever-a-dial-indicator-measures-plunger-travel","text":"A test indicator sweeps with a lever; a dial indicator measures plunger travel Instrument Contact motion Setup use Test indicator A short stylus pivots through a small angle. Convenient for sweeping a vise jaw or a bore where access is tight. Plunger dial indicator A stem moves straight into the body. Useful for displacement checks and alignment when the stem can face the measured motion. Both can compare alignment. “Dial” describes a display as well as a common instrument name, so identify the contact mechanism. Use the maker's stylus angle for a test indicator; on a common lever design the stylus lies nearly parallel to the measured surface. Align a plunger with the displacement being measured. Incorrect contact angles change the scale factor. Keep the mounting rigid, preload within the instrument's range and confirm repeatability. During a fixed-jaw sweep, keep the spindle stopped and the contact geometry unchanged. For a bore-centering sweep, turn the spindle by hand using the approved setup method. Follow Equipment Operating Procedure for the machine state during setup. Contact angle affects the reading Mitutoyo: Dial Test Indicators and the Cosine Effect shows the stylus geometry and correction. Do not apply a plunger-indicator correction rule to a lever indicator without checking its instructions."},
{"title":"An edge finder locates the spindle center one tip radius from the edge","page":"Mill Indicators and Part Zero","summary":"Choose an indicator for alignment, account for edge-finder radius, and keep machine home, work zero and tool length separate.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-indicating-and-zero.html#an-edge-finder-locates-the-spindle-center-one-tip-radius-from-the-edge","text":"An edge finder locates the spindle center one tip radius from the edge A mechanical edge finder has a spring-loaded contact tip that becomes concentric and then kicks sideways at contact. Use its specified spindle speed and approach method. Electronic edge finders and probes use different signals and procedures; do not assume they rotate like a mechanical edge finder. Use a clean, defined edge, repeat the approach and account for the contact-tip radius. If the tip is 10 mm in diameter, the spindle center is 5 mm outside the edge at the trigger point. Setting that center position to zero without the radius correction moves every feature by 5 mm. Example: the left face is X = 0 and positive X goes into the part to the right. At contact from the left with a 10 mm tip, the spindle center is X = −5 mm. Enter that position in the work-coordinate setting method for the control. On approach from a right-hand face, the sign changes. Sketch the spindle center and edge before choosing a sign. Check both repeatability and the offset sign Repeat the touch from the same direction. On manual feed screws, account for backlash when reversing; a handwheel reading can change while the table has not yet moved. Do not use a rough saw edge as though it were a finished datum. The radius correction is geometry, independent of the control's button sequence. MIT's mill introduction covers mechanical edge finding and feed-screw backlash."},
{"title":"Home the machine, locate the part, then account for each tool's length","page":"Mill Indicators and Part Zero","summary":"Choose an indicator for alignment, account for edge-finder radius, and keep machine home, work zero and tool length separate.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-indicating-and-zero.html#home-the-machine-locate-the-part-then-account-for-each-tool-s-length","text":"Home the machine, locate the part, then account for each tool's length Machine home establishes the machine's reference positions. It does not locate the part. Work zero is the origin chosen on the part or fixture. Its work offset relates that origin to the machine coordinates. Tool length offset accounts for where a particular tool tip lies relative to the control's tool reference. Choose the origin used by the drawing and program, locate X and Y, and establish Z using the approved touch-off or probing method. Account for any gauge or touch plate thickness. Confirm the active work offset, tool number and tool length before a trial path. Recheck after moving the part or changing how a tool is seated. Use Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown to check the sequence and G-code and Toolpaths to connect coordinates to a program. Tormach's basic-operation reference provides links to work/tool offset instructions; select documentation matching the installed control."},
{"title":"Use the checklist with the installed machine's procedure","page":"Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown","summary":"A Tormach-based sequence for preparing the machine, locating the part and tools, checking a path, and closing down.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-setup-checklist.html#use-the-checklist-with-the-installed-machine-s-procedure","text":"Use the checklist with the installed machine's procedure This checklist follows a Tormach vertical-mill workflow. Match each control, inspection method and tool-change step to the installed machine and controller. It is a setup reminder for trained users, not a substitute for instruction or the machine manual. The operating and hazard requirements live in Equipment Operating Procedure, Personal Protective Equipment, Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Metal Chips and Swarf. Complete those requirements before using this sequence."},
{"title":"Startup: inspect, power up, enable and reference the axes","page":"Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown","summary":"A Tormach-based sequence for preparing the machine, locating the part and tools, checking a path, and closing down.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-setup-checklist.html#startup-inspect-power-up-enable-and-reference-the-axes","text":"Startup: inspect, power up, enable and reference the axes Complete the pre-use requirements in Equipment Operating Procedure and Personal Protective Equipment. Check coolant level and lubrication of the ways—the sliding guide surfaces—using the machine's maintenance procedure. Complete the prescribed guard, emergency-stop and work-area checks in Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. Confirm the machine and controller have power available. Turn on the controller computer, then the mill as directed by the installed-machine procedure. Reset depressed emergency-stop controls after completing the prescribed checks, then use the machine's Start/enable control to activate its motor controllers. When the controller is ready, reference (home) all installed axes by the machine's procedure. Confirm that referencing completed; homing has not set the part zero."},
{"title":"Part and tool setup: seat, grip, locate and record","page":"Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown","summary":"A Tormach-based sequence for preparing the machine, locating the part and tools, checking a path, and closing down.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-setup-checklist.html#part-and-tool-setup-seat-grip-locate-and-record","text":"Part and tool setup: seat, grip, locate and record Plan the datum faces, work zero, tool access and fixture clearance for the intended operations. For a vise setup, clean and inspect the vise and part, remove raised burrs, and check the fixed-jaw alignment. See Milling Vises and Strap Clamps. Fit parallels if needed. Snug the part, seat it with light dead-blow taps, tighten the vise and recheck seating. For table clamping, have the support, locating and clamp arrangement checked against the job and installed procedure; confirm every intended contact is seated and every clamp is firm. Use strap-clamp geometry to review placement. Choose the cutter and matching holder. For ER tooling, capture the collet in its nut, thread the assembly loosely, insert the shank with the planned reach, and tighten as specified. For a set-screw holder, align the designated shank flat and follow its seating method. See Mill Toolholders and Collets. Install the holder using the procedure for the actual spindle and drawbar. For a manual drawbar arrangement, verify completion of its spindle-lock release and spindle-cover closure steps before operation; do not apply that sequence to a different retention system. Set and verify tool lengths and the work offset using Mill Indicators and Part Zero. Confirm the active tool number, units and work coordinate system."},
{"title":"Before cutting: verify the program or manual movement plan","page":"Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown","summary":"A Tormach-based sequence for preparing the machine, locating the part and tools, checking a path, and closing down.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-setup-checklist.html#before-cutting-verify-the-program-or-manual-movement-plan","text":"Before cutting: verify the program or manual movement plan Confirm cutter, spindle direction, speed, feed, width and depth of cut against the tool data and Feeds and Speeds. For programmed work, load the intended program and check the displayed path, tools, offsets and limits. See G-code and Toolpaths. Perform the approved air-cut or dry-run procedure with verified clearance. If that method changes offsets or modes, restore and verify them before cutting the part. For manual movement, confirm the selected axis, direction, jog rate and increment. Use a low approach rate near a surface and incremental motion for fine positioning, as directed in the machine procedure. Select cutting feed from the tool data rather than treating a low approach rate as a cutting-feed prescription. Complete the cutting-readiness checks in Equipment Operating Procedure, then perform the operation and its planned measurements."},
{"title":"Shutdown: end operation, shut down the controller and restore the work area","page":"Vertical Mill Startup, Setup and Shutdown","summary":"A Tormach-based sequence for preparing the machine, locating the part and tools, checking a path, and closing down.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-setup-checklist.html#shutdown-end-operation-shut-down-the-controller-and-restore-the-work-area","text":"Shutdown: end operation, shut down the controller and restore the work area Finish the operation and complete the stopping and measurement requirements in Equipment Operating Procedure. Follow the installed Tormach shutdown sequence. In this workflow: depress emergency stop, shut down the controller computer normally, then remove its power and turn off the mill. Complete cleanup using Metal Chips and Swarf and Equipment Operating Procedure. Return tools and leave the work area ready for the next user. Use the machine-specific instructions for any required parking position, tool removal, lubrication or power sequence. These details vary with the machine and its options."},
{"title":"The spindle connection and the tool grip are separate choices","page":"Mill Toolholders and Collets","summary":"Separate the spindle interface from the tool grip, assemble ER collets in the right order, and control runout and unnecessary stickout.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-toolholding.html#the-spindle-connection-and-the-tool-grip-are-separate-choices","text":"The spindle connection and the tool grip are separate choices A holder has a machine end and a cutting-tool end. “ER” describes a collet system at the tool end; it does not tell you which spindle the holder fits. Interface How to recognize the idea R8 A taper seats in the spindle and a drawbar threads into the collet or holder to pull it in. A direct R8 collet grips a matching tool shank. CAT / BT Steep-taper holder families with flanges and retention knobs, also called pull studs, for compatible spindle retention and tool changing. Similar-looking tapers do not establish interchangeability: family, size, flange and retention knob must match the machine. TTS The Tormach Tooling System uses a straight holder shank gripped in a compatible collet and a shoulder that seats against the spindle face. TTS is a toolholding system, not a CAT/BT-style taper. Its seated shoulder provides a repeatable axial reference. Identify the installed interface before selecting a holder. A Tormach name alone does not identify the spindle system or drawbar arrangement. Public toolholder references Haas: Tool Holder Essentials compares holder choices. Tormach's R8/TTS explanation is for the machine configuration named in that manual, not a statement of this shop's inventory."},
{"title":"Choose a collet, end mill holder or drill chuck for the grip needed","page":"Mill Toolholders and Collets","summary":"Separate the spindle interface from the tool grip, assemble ER collets in the right order, and control runout and unnecessary stickout.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-toolholding.html#choose-a-collet-end-mill-holder-or-drill-chuck-for-the-grip-needed","text":"Choose a collet, end mill holder or drill chuck for the grip needed Collet: a split sleeve closes around the tool shank. Match the series and the specified gripping diameter or range. A direct R8 collet and an ER collet are different systems. Set-screw end mill holder: a fitted bore locates the shank and a screw bears on the tool's designated flat. Match the bore to the shank and the flat to the screw; follow the holder's seating and tightening method. Drill chuck: adjustable jaws grip round-shank drilling tools. An ordinary drill chuck is not an end-milling holder; its grip and mounting are not intended for milling side loads. Grip the intended shank surface, not the cutting flutes. Keep the tool's specified engagement in the holder while leaving only the reach needed to cut and clear the part."},
{"title":"Snap the ER collet into its nut before threading the nut onto the holder","page":"Mill Toolholders and Collets","summary":"Separate the spindle interface from the tool grip, assemble ER collets in the right order, and control runout and unnecessary stickout.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-toolholding.html#snap-the-er-collet-into-its-nut-before-threading-the-nut-onto-the-holder","text":"Snap the ER collet into its nut before threading the nut onto the holder Match the ER series, nut, collet and tool shank. Read the collet's actual range; the ER series number is not the tool diameter. Do not force an oversized shank into it or collapse it below its rated range. Clean and inspect the holder socket, collet, nut and tool shank. Follow the manufacturer's lubrication instructions for the named surfaces; do not treat every taper and gripping surface alike. Engage the collet's extraction groove with the nut's eccentric retaining ring. Tilt and snap it into place as instructed by the maker. Thread the nut-and-collet assembly loosely onto the holder. Insert the tool with the required shank engagement and planned stickout. Tighten to the specified torque using the proper wrench and holding fixture. ER assembly sequence: clean and match parts; snap the collet groove into the nut's eccentric ring; thread the captured nut and collet loosely onto the holder; insert the shank and torque as specified. The nut captures the collet before either goes on the holder. Do not place the collet in the holder first and try to force the nut over it. Range example: a collet explicitly rated 9–10 mm can grip a 9.5 mm shank; it cannot grip a 10.2 mm shank. This is a reading exercise, not a universal collapse range for every ER collet. Why the snap matters The eccentric ring retains and extracts the collet as the nut is loosened. Missing that engagement can prevent correct seating. Use REGO-FIX's ER assembly instructions for the mechanism and Haas: ER Collet Essentials for a public demonstration."},
{"title":"Runout changes the chip taken by each tooth; stickout changes stiffness","page":"Mill Toolholders and Collets","summary":"Separate the spindle interface from the tool grip, assemble ER collets in the right order, and control runout and unnecessary stickout.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/mill-toolholding.html#runout-changes-the-chip-taken-by-each-tooth-stickout-changes-stiffness","text":"Runout changes the chip taken by each tooth; stickout changes stiffness Runout is the variation seen as a tool or reference shank rotates about the spindle axis. If one cutting edge projects farther than the others, it can carry more of the load. Dirt, damaged seating surfaces, a poor collet fit, the tool, holder or spindle can contribute. Clean and reseat before blaming cutting data; measure against the job and tool requirements. Stickout is the unsupported reach from the grip. Keep it as short as the feature and holder clearance allow. Do not solve a reach problem by leaving too little shank in the collet. Explore the effect in Tool Stickout and Deflection; use Diagnosing Milling Chatter when vibration appears during a cut."},
{"title":"A repeating mark or loud cut is evidence, not a diagnosis","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#a-repeating-mark-or-loud-cut-is-evidence-not-a-diagnosis","text":"A repeating mark or loud cut is evidence, not a diagnosis A common mechanism, regenerative chatter, is self-excited: one cutting edge leaves a waviness that changes the chip thickness seen by the next edge, and the changing force can reinforce the motion. That mechanism does not describe every vibration or finish defect. A repeating surface pattern and a strong sound can be clues; they do not prove the cause by themselves. Several faults can look or sound like chatter Runout or one damaged edge can make one flute carry more load and leave uneven tooth marks or non-uniform edge wear. Rubbing or built-up edge can smear material, generate heat and produce a poor finish without a regenerative vibration. Chip re-cutting can scratch the finish and damage edges when chips remain in the cutting zone. A forced vibration follows a repeating input from the cutter, interrupted cut, drive or surrounding system. Chatter is the self-reinforcing case; both still require finding the flexible path and the excitation. After the approved safe stop, inspect the pattern, cutter and setup together. Sound is useful for noticing a change, not for identifying the failed component."},
{"title":"Stop the cut before inspecting or changing the setup","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#stop-the-cut-before-inspecting-or-changing-the-setup","text":"Stop the cut before inspecting or changing the setup End the cut with the approved machine procedure before approaching or changing anything. Use Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping for the operating rules. Do not tune around a part, tool or holder that moved. A shifted workpiece, incomplete seating, damaged edge or visibly slipping tool is a mechanical problem to correct or report before another cutting trial."},
{"title":"Record the evidence before trying another cut","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#record-the-evidence-before-trying-another-cut","text":"Record the evidence before trying another cut Record where the symptom began, where it appears on the part, which operation and tool were active, and the actual speed, feed, engagement and holder projection. This creates a comparison for the next check instead of relying on sound or memory."},
{"title":"Trace force through the complete cutting system","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#trace-force-through-the-complete-cutting-system","text":"Trace force through the complete cutting system Two connected force paths meet at the cut. The tool path runs from machine and spindle through the spindle interface, holder and collet, and cutter. The work path runs from the machine table through fixture and workholding to the workpiece. The cut, programmed engagement and chip evacuation connect both paths. Each link names evidence to inspect. The cut closes the loop. A stiff cutter cannot compensate for a moving part, and rigid workholding cannot correct a damaged holder interface."},
{"title":"Route suspected machine or spindle faults to authorized staff","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#route-suspected-machine-or-spindle-faults-to-authorized-staff","text":"Route suspected machine or spindle faults to authorized staff After the cutter, holder, workholding, path and chips have been checked, a persistent symptom is evidence worth giving to authorized staff. Abnormal motion or a visibly damaged or contaminated interface also calls for that route. It does not prove one component failed. Do not remove covers, adjust bearings or gibs, or service a drawbar; manufacturer guidance treats those as separate checks for trained personnel."},
{"title":"Check the holder, collet and cutter as one assembly","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#check-the-holder-collet-and-cutter-as-one-assembly","text":"Check the holder, collet and cutter as one assembly Confirm the correct holder and collet, complete seating, clean contact surfaces under the approved procedure, adequate shank engagement, and no visible damage. Compare tool and holder projection with the last stable setup. Inspect for wear, chipping, built-up material and chip damage. Check runout only with the approved instrument and method; compare it with the tool, holder and job requirements. See Mill Toolholders and Collets, End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection and Tool Stickout and Deflection."},
{"title":"Check the fixture, workpiece and support","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#check-the-fixture-workpiece-and-support","text":"Check the fixture, workpiece and support Check that the fixture is seated and secured, the work is against its locators, clamps direct force into supports, and the part is not perched or springing. A symptom confined to an unsupported end, thin wall or one clamping orientation points first toward support and force direction. Do not keep tightening until the part distorts. See Milling Vises and Strap Clamps."},
{"title":"Check engagement, path and chip evacuation","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#check-engagement-path-and-chip-evacuation","text":"Check engagement, path and chip evacuation Compare where the pattern begins with changes in radial or axial engagement, entry and exit, cornering, cutter engagement, and chip evacuation. A clean straight wall followed by chatter in an inside corner is evidence to inspect the corner path and the sudden engagement change. Packed or re-cut chips call for the approved chip-control method and a review of the operation, not an automatic speed change. See Milling Operations and Cutting Fluids and Application Methods."},
{"title":"It changed with a longer tool or holder","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#it-changed-with-a-longer-tool-or-holder","text":"It changed with a longer tool or holder Check: tool projection, holder projection, shank engagement and both interfaces. Next: restore the shortest permitted assembly that reaches the feature, then repeat the same cut conditions."},
{"title":"Straight walls are clean; an inside corner chatters","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#straight-walls-are-clean-an-inside-corner-chatters","text":"Straight walls are clean; an inside corner chatters Check: the engaged arc, chip load through the corner and programmed path. Next: use an approved path or engagement change, then compare the same corner again."},
{"title":"Only an unsupported or thin area vibrates","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#only-an-unsupported-or-thin-area-vibrates","text":"Only an unsupported or thin area vibrates Check: workpiece support, clamp-force path and local flexibility. Next: improve support or revise force direction without distorting the work; verify the setup before cutting."},
{"title":"One edge is chipped or marks repeat once per revolution","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#one-edge-is-chipped-or-marks-repeat-once-per-revolution","text":"One edge is chipped or marks repeat once per revolution Check: edge condition, chip re-cutting, holder seating and runout. Next: correct the physical cause or replace the damaged tool before tuning speed."},
{"title":"It persists after the student-controlled setup checks","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#it-persists-after-the-student-controlled-setup-checks","text":"It persists after the student-controlled setup checks Check: after the cutter, holder and workholding are verified, ask whether the symptom still follows the same path, engagement or chip condition. Next: if it persists across different permitted cutting conditions, stop and give the complete evidence to authorized staff. It is evidence for inspection, not proof of a spindle fault. Practice chatter diagnosis"},
{"title":"Clear mechanical causes and unnecessary leverage first","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#clear-mechanical-causes-and-unnecessary-leverage-first","text":"Clear mechanical causes and unnecessary leverage first Clear mechanical causes first. Verify seating, condition, projection, support and chip clearance. Do not use a parameter change to hide motion or damage. Reduce unnecessary leverage. Shorten the permitted tool and holder projection, improve support, or choose a more suitable assembly. Keep the required shank engagement and feature clearance."},
{"title":"Review engagement and cutter geometry after the setup is sound","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#review-engagement-and-cutter-geometry-after-the-setup-is-sound","text":"Review engagement and cutter geometry after the setup is sound Review engagement and force direction. A smaller radial or axial engagement, different entry, or revised corner path may reduce the force variation. Stay inside the approved process and tool limits. Reconsider cutter geometry. Diameter, flute count and pitch, helix, edge geometry and material suitability can change force and excitation. Record the selected tool and why it was changed."},
{"title":"Run one permitted speed trial","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#run-one-permitted-speed-trial","text":"Run one permitted speed trial A stable speed can be higher or lower. Change to one permitted speed, then compare the same feature. Do not assume that slowing down is always the answer."},
{"title":"Review feed when speed changes","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#review-feed-when-speed-changes","text":"Review feed when speed changes If the trial is intended to preserve feed per tooth, calculate the matching table feed rather than changing chip load accidentally. Too little feed can rub; excessive feed can overload the edge. Use Feeds and Speeds and the tool maker's data."},
{"title":"Record the controlled result","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#record-the-controlled-result","text":"Record the controlled result Record the observation, the one intentional change and the result. If two settings move together to preserve a stated quantity such as feed per tooth, record both and the quantity held constant. That is one controlled trial, not two unexplained changes."},
{"title":"Manufacturer references behind this diagnostic order","page":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","summary":"Use the finish, setup and cutting conditions as evidence, then check the whole force path before changing one permitted variable at a time.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-chatter.html#manufacturer-references-behind-this-diagnostic-order","text":"Manufacturer references behind this diagnostic order Haas: Avoid Chatter on Your Mill is the existing public demonstration. Haas TG0100: Mill Chatter Troubleshooting separates tool wear, projection, chip load, engaged flutes, toolpath, corners, coolant, drawbar force and machine support. Its drawbar and foundation actions belong to authorized machine work. Tormach: Chatter separates tool wear, toolholding, workholding and toolpath causes. Its drawbar, pressure and gib instructions are machine-specific maintenance—not generic student steps. Sandvik Coromant: milling vibration guidance connects workpiece and machine stability with overhang, engagement, force direction, pitch and toolpath. Seco: vibration in machining explains resonance, overhang and why a stable speed may be above or below the current speed. Seco: end-mill wear patterns distinguishes chipping, built-up edge and chip-evacuation damage."},
{"title":"The shank is held; the cutting edges remove material; the flutes carry chips","page":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","summary":"Read end mill geometry, compare flute counts and tool materials, and choose a cutter for the material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-cutter-selection.html#the-shank-is-held-the-cutting-edges-remove-material-the-flutes-carry-chips","text":"The shank is held; the cutting edges remove material; the flutes carry chips Schematic end mill labeled with shank, cutting edge, flute groove, helix angle, cutting diameter and length of cut, plus square, corner-radius and ball end profiles. Schematic geometry, not a scale drawing of a particular cutter. Shank diameter and cutting diameter need not be equal. Flutes are grooves beside the teeth that provide room for chips. The sharp edges bordering them do the cutting. Helix angle describes the spiral relative to the tool axis. It affects how the edge enters the cut and the direction of cutting forces; it is not the spindle's rotation direction. Cutting diameter is the diameter swept by the cutting edges. Length of cut is the axial length of the usable cutting edges, not permission to cut that entire depth in one pass. Square end makes a flat-bottom form with a sharp nominal corner. A corner-radius or bull-nose end rounds that corner. A ball end has a rounded tip for curved surfaces. Center-cutting geometry can cut at the tool center, allowing axial entry when the manufacturer permits it. Flute count alone does not prove a tool can plunge; check its entry method and limits. Reach also depends on neck clearance and holder clearance. Use Tool Stickout and Deflection for the cost of extra reach and Milling Operations for the shapes these cutters produce. Public anatomy reference Harvey Performance: The Anatomy of an End Mill provides an illustrated reference for the dimensions found in a tool catalog."},
{"title":"Choose flute count for chip space and engagement, not material alone","page":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","summary":"Read end mill geometry, compare flute counts and tool materials, and choose a cutter for the material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-cutter-selection.html#choose-flute-count-for-chip-space-and-engagement-not-material-alone","text":"Choose flute count for chip space and engagement, not material alone Flutes Useful starting point Check before choosing 2 Generous chip space, often useful for aluminum slots. Geometry, edge strength and the maker's slotting recommendation. 3 Common aluminum-specific choice balancing chip space with another cutting edge. That the tool is designed for the material and engagement; three flutes alone are not a material specification. 4 Common for steel and for cuts where chip space is less restrictive. A full-width slot can pack the smaller flute spaces. Do not assume more teeth always improve removal rate. A side cut and a full-width slot load the same cutter differently. Select the actual tool's data for the operation, then use Feeds and Speeds for the calculations and Climb and Conventional Milling for feed direction. Why the two-for-aluminum, four-for-steel rule is only a starting point Tool designers trade chip space against the core and cutting-edge arrangement. Purpose-built tools and controlled engagement can make other flute counts appropriate. See Harvey Performance: Why Flute Count Matters."},
{"title":"Match HSS, carbide and coating to the job and machine","page":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","summary":"Read end mill geometry, compare flute counts and tool materials, and choose a cutter for the material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-cutter-selection.html#match-hss-carbide-and-coating-to-the-job-and-machine","text":"Match HSS, carbide and coating to the job and machine High-speed steel (HSS) is relatively tough and often economical. It can be a useful choice at lower cutting speeds, especially where the setup cannot take full advantage of carbide. Cobalt HSS is an HSS family with improved hot hardness. Cemented carbide retains hardness at higher cutting temperatures and is stiffer than HSS, but is less tolerant of impact and poor support. A carbide tool does not make a loose setup rigid. Select its grade and geometry as well as its material. Coatings change wear, friction and heat behavior. TiN and TiCN are familiar options; AlTiN/TiAlN are common on tools for hot-running ferrous applications. For aluminum, choose an uncoated polished tool or a coating explicitly recommended for that alloy, such as TiB2 where specified. Color is not a reliable selection method, and no coating guarantees a fixed multiple of tool life. Job examples: for an aluminum slot, start by comparing aluminum-specific two- and three-flute tools with enough chip space. For steel side milling on a rigid setup, a steel-rated four-flute carbide tool may be a good starting point. For a modest-speed, interrupted job, a suitable HSS tool may be more forgiving. Check the manufacturer's recommendations before selecting the cutting data. Practice cutter selection Check the coating's material recommendation Harvey Tool's coating guide distinguishes ferrous applications from aluminum applications. Its AlTiN and TiB2 entries illustrate why a coating name must be matched to the work material."},
{"title":"Face mills and fly cutters cover broad flat surfaces","page":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","summary":"Read end mill geometry, compare flute counts and tool materials, and choose a cutter for the material and operation.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-cutter-selection.html#face-mills-and-fly-cutters-cover-broad-flat-surfaces","text":"Face mills and fly cutters cover broad flat surfaces A face mill uses several cutting edges, often replaceable inserts, to machine a broad face. Match its diameter, insert geometry, holder and power demand to the machine and part. A fly cutter carries a single cutting point that sweeps a circle. It can make a broad finishing pass, but its single edge and interrupted loading need suitable rigidity, balance and cutting data. It is not simply a face mill that can use the same feed setting. Check tram when a broad facing pass leaves steps. Cutter selection belongs with machine alignment, toolholding and the operation being planned."},
{"title":"A vertical mill shapes a block with a turning tool moved in X, Y and Z","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#a-vertical-mill-shapes-a-block-with-a-turning-tool-moved-in-x-y-and-z","text":"A vertical mill shapes a block with a turning tool moved in X, Y and Z On a vertical mill the cutting tool turns in a spindle above the work. The work is clamped on the table, the table moves it front to back and side to side, and the tool comes down into it. Every shape the mill makes is the space the turning tool swept through. X and Y The two directions across the table. In this lesson X runs left to right as you face the machine and Y runs away from you, toward the back. Z Up and down, along the tool. Z+ is up, away from the work, so negative Z is below the finished top face. The raw top is at Z +1, so facing can remove metal at zero or positive Z while taking off that allowance. Readout The display that shows where the tool is, in X, Y and Z. A readout on a manual mill is often called a DRO, for digital readout. Here it shows the position of the tool's tip. Work zero The point the readout counts from. In this lesson X 0, Y 0 is the front left corner of the block and Z 0 is the finished top face. Every dimension on the drawings is measured from those same edges and that same face. X Y X0 Y0 back jaw front jaw (operator side) block 100 × 60, finished 20 thick parallels under the part (hidden) Z +1.00 raw top Z 0.00 finished top Z −8.00 top of the jaws Z −20.00 bottom, on the parallels The setup used in every job: a block 100 &times; 60, finished 20 thick, held by its front and back faces in a vise and resting on two parallels, bars of equal height that lift it off the floor of the vise. The raw block stands 1 mm proud of the finished top. Below Z &minus;8 the vise jaws are in the way at the front and back, and a hole drilled through along those edges would run into a parallel. On a real knee mill the table carries the work under a spindle that stays put in X and Y. In the simulator you move the tool instead. The readout tells the same story either way: where the tool's tip is, measured from work zero."},
{"title":"Each kind of tool cuts a shape of its own, and some cannot cut sideways","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#each-kind-of-tool-cuts-a-shape-of-its-own-and-some-cannot-cut-sideways","text":"Each kind of tool cuts a shape of its own, and some cannot cut sideways Face mill Ø50 End mill Ø12 shown Spot and chamfer, 90° Drill, 118° Ø6.8 shown Tap M8×1.25 shown Boring head set to Ø20 The tools in the simulator, side on and at one scale, each with its tip on the same line. The darker part of each is the part that cuts. Face mill A wide body with cutting inserts round its rim. It is fed across the work to leave a broad flat face. The one in the simulator takes at most 4 mm in one pass. The inserts sit at the rim, not across the middle, so it is brought down beside the work and then fed in, never pushed straight down into the metal. End mill Cuts on its sides and its end. The end mills here are centre-cutting: their end teeth reach the middle, so they can be fed straight down into the metal and then moved across to cut pockets, slots, steps and walls. An inside corner they leave is rounded to the cutter's own radius. Spot and chamfer tool A pointed tool with a 90&deg; point. Fed down, it cuts a cone: the start of a hole, or a countersink round the mouth of one. Run along an edge, it bevels the edge, which is a chamfer. Drill Cuts only at its point, as it feeds down along its own axis. It leaves a round hole its own size, with a cone at the bottom from its point. It must never be pushed sideways in the metal. Tap Cuts a screw thread into the wall of a hole that is already there. It only goes straight in and straight back out. Boring head An adjustable single cutting point on an offset bar. Set to a diameter and fed down a hole, it opens the hole to that size. Its bar has no cutting edge at the centre, so it needs a hole to start in. This is a teaching rack, not a list of what to buy or use. The sizes are illustrative. A real cutter's length, number of teeth and depth of cut come from its own data and from the person responsible for the machine."},
{"title":"The tool's centre runs one radius away from the wall it cuts","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#the-tool-s-centre-runs-one-radius-away-from-the-wall-it-cuts","text":"The tool's centre runs one radius away from the wall it cuts The readout shows where the centre of the tool is. The metal is cut by its edge. To leave a wall at a given place, put the centre one radius away from it, on the side being cut away. Worked example. A shoulder's wall belongs at X 75, with the metal to the right of it cut away. A &Oslash;12 end mill has a 6 mm radius, so its centre runs along X 75 + 6 = X 81. Run it along X 75 instead and the wall ends up at X 69, 6 mm too far into the part. That metal cannot be put back. Inside corners take the cutter's radius. A round tool cannot reach into a sharp inside corner, so a pocket's corners come out rounded to the radius of the cutter that made them. A drawing that allows R4 at most calls for a cutter no bigger than &Oslash;8. Outside corners stay sharp. Going round the outside of a corner, the tool swings about it and never cuts into it. A slot is the width of the cutter that made it in one pass. A cutter moved in a straight line leaves a groove exactly its own width, with round ends of its own radius. When a cutter cuts with its side, the metal is on one side of it or the other. With the cutter turning clockwise seen from above, metal on the cutter's right as it moves is climb milling, and metal on its left is conventional milling. The simulator says which each side cut was. Climb and Conventional Milling explains the difference."},
{"title":"Holes: drilled to size, threaded by a tap, opened up by a boring head","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#holes-drilled-to-size-threaded-by-a-tap-opened-up-by-a-boring-head","text":"Holes: drilled to size, threaded by a tap, opened up by a boring head A drill leaves a hole of its own diameter. A hole's depth on a drawing is measured to where the hole stops being full size, not to the tip of the cone the drill point leaves below that. A tapped hole starts as a smaller drilled hole A tap cuts its thread into the wall of a hole, so the hole has to be drilled smaller than the thread first, to the tap drill size. For the common metric coarse threads that size is close to the thread's outside diameter minus its pitch, the distance from one thread to the next: Tap drill &asymp; thread diameter &minus; pitch. M6&times;1: 6 &minus; 1 = &Oslash;5.0. M8&times;1.25: 8 &minus; 1.25 = &Oslash;6.75, drilled &Oslash;6.8. Drilled too small, the tap has to cut too much metal and can break. Drilled too big, the thread is shallow. The simulator checks the hole against a simplified minor-diameter band and refuses a severely undersize pilot. This does not establish functional thread fit: pitch diameter, thread form and a real thread gauge are separate checks. Drill deeper than the thread you need The first few threads of a tap are ground away to a taper so that it can start in the hole. Those threads cut only part of a thread, so full thread stops short of the tap's tip. In a hole with a bottom, the tap has to go deeper than the full thread you need, and the drilled hole deeper again, past the drill's point. This simulated tap has a 4-pitch cutting chamfer; real taps vary. The job calls for full thread reaching Z &minus;10 from the finished face. Its countersink removes the first part of the thread, so that is not 10 mm of surviving full-thread length. Sizes no drill gives Drills come in fixed sizes. A boring head is set to the size you need and fed down a hole already big enough for its bar, which makes it the tool for a hole of an exact, larger size."},
{"title":"Simulator: make the part on the drawing, then inspect it","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#simulator-make-the-part-on-the-drawing-then-inspect-it","text":"Simulator: make the part on the drawing, then inspect it A A SECTION A–A 75 25 60 6 20 Job 1: Face the top, then cut a shoulder. Face the top flat at Z 0.00, then mill a shoulder 25 mm wide and 6 mm deep across the right-hand end. A A SECTION A–A 15 30 40 15 18 24 30 POCKET 6 DEEP 4× R4 MAX 2× Ø6.6 THRU Job 2: A pocket with tight corners, and two clearance holes. Face the top, mill a 40 × 24 pocket 6 deep whose inside corners are no rounder than R4, and drill two Ø6.6 holes right through. A A SECTION A–A 15 30 40 15 30 SLOT 10 WIDE, 5 DEEP FULL ROUND ENDS 2× M6×1-6H FULL THREAD 10 DEEP CSK Ø7 × 90° Job 3: A slot and two tapped holes. Face the top, mill a slot 10 wide with full-round ends, and make two countersunk M6×1 holes whose full thread reaches Z −10 or deeper, measured from the finished face. A A SECTION A–A 25 50 15 30 30 5 Ø20.00 ±0.05 THRU 1 × 45° 4 EDGES Job 4: A boss with a bored hole and chamfered edges. Face the top, mill away everything round a 50 × 30 boss so it stands 5 high, bore a Ø20.00 hole through it, and chamfer its top edges 1 × 45°. JavaScript is off, so the simulator is not running. The drawings above are the four jobs it sets. Start by examining the finished Target, then choose a tool and cut Your work. In Compare, gold is metal still to remove and striped red-orange is metal cut past the drawing. The inspection measures the part that is left after every move. Nothing checks which tools you used or in what order: any way of making the right part passes. How to drive it. Target shows the finished nominal part; Your work shows the block you are making. Compare marks what still needs work, using the drawing's allowed limits. In Inspect / orbit, dragging and the arrow keys move only the camera. Home restores the view; the View list also offers top, front, below and 2D sections. Choose a tool and its size, then choose Feed X, Feed Y or Feed Z to drag its tip along that machine axis. The path previews while you drag; releasing starts the feed. Place XY places the centre at the current Z. In these feed modes the arrow keys move the selected machine axis. To go to an exact place, type X, Y and Z under Move to and press Move; the tool goes up before it goes across, and across before it goes down. The handwheel buttons and the selected-axis keys move one step at a time. A move that would break a tool or hit the vise stops where it would happen, and says why. Undo takes back the last move: the one thing a real mill cannot do."},
{"title":"Things to try in the simulator","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#things-to-try-in-the-simulator","text":"Things to try in the simulator In job 1, load the face mill, move it over the block and try to lower it straight onto the metal. Read why it stops. Lower the face mill to Z 0.00 beside the block and drag it across. Watch the top view and the sections: the raw top is 1 mm above Z 0. Overlap a second pass until the top face check matches. Cut the shoulder with the &Oslash;12 end mill with its centre on X 75 instead of X 81. Read the inspection, then use Undo and cut it again in the right place. In job 2, clear the pocket with the &Oslash;12 end mill and read what the inspection says about its corners. Undo, and clear it with a smaller cutter. Watch the gold as you cut: it is metal the drawing does not keep. Cut one pass a little too deep and see where the striped red-orange appears in the top view and the section. In job 3, try to tap a hole you drilled with the &Oslash;6.0 drill, then one you drilled with the &Oslash;4.2. Then drill &Oslash;5.0 only 10 mm deep and tap it as deep as it will go. How much full thread do you get? In job 4, try the boring head before there is any hole. Then drill one first. Make one part two different ways, for example job 4 with the face mill doing the area round the boss, and again with an end mill. Both pass."},
{"title":"What the simulator leaves out","page":"Milling Operations","summary":"Make a part on a simulated vertical mill: choose each tool, move it through the metal, and check what is left against the drawing. Facing, shoulders, pockets, slots, drilled, tapped and bored holes, countersinks and chamfers.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-operations.html#what-the-simulator-leaves-out","text":"What the simulator leaves out The 3D target is a nominal guide sampled at 0.25 mm in X and Y. Its flat floors have the drawing's depth; its small curved outlines are approximations. The cut surface is derived separately from the analytic tool paths. Neither picture is a dimensional gauge: inspection uses the drawing's allowed limits, including smaller corners where the drawing says R4 max. Complex conical histories or very long cut histories switch to 2D sections to keep the controls responsive. The work is kept, and Undo or a fresh block can restore the 3D view. The simulator is a model of where metal is and where tools can reach. It does not check speeds, feeds, forces or finish, and passing a job here says nothing about whether a real setup could take the same cuts. Feeds and Speeds is about those numbers. Only straight down and straight across. The tool axis is always vertical and nothing is undercut, so every shape is a height above each point of the table. Tools are exact and rigid. A hole is exactly the drill's size, a cutter never bends or wears, and edges come out without burrs. Threads are recorded, not drawn. The simulator keeps the size of the hole a thread was cut in and how deep its full thread runs, and checks that later cuts have not taken the metal it is in. It does not model the thread's form or fit. No chips, coolant or heat, and no limit on depth of cut except where a tool's cutting edges run out. How the inspection decides, and what its numbers are The part is kept as a list of every cut: the space each tool swept through. The top of the metal at any point is the raw top lowered by every cut that reaches that point. The inspection measures walls, floors, holes and chamfers at sampled locations on that surface, with numbers rounded to a hundredth of a millimetre. A display grid plus probes derived from each cut and drawing boundary look for excess metal and gouges. Passing means the modeled checks pass; it does not certify every point of a real surface. Removed volume is an estimate from the 0.25 mm grid; very small cuts can change the analytic surface without a resolved grid volume. Walls and holes pass within &plusmn;0.10 mm, the bore within &plusmn;0.05 mm, and floors and faces within &plusmn;0.05 mm. Tapped holes are accepted when their drilled diameter falls inside the minor-diameter limits for a 6H internal thread: 4.917 to 5.153 mm for M6&times;1, and 6.647 to 6.912 mm for M8&times;1.25. These are finished minor-diameter limits, used here as a teaching proxy for pilot quality; they are not universal tap-drill recommendations or a complete 6H inspection. Sources: G&uuml;hring's metric table, page 46, distinguishes nominal tap-drill sizes from finished minor limits. Haas's M6&times;1 example specifies a 5 mm tap drill and a 2P&ndash;3P chamfer, illustrating why the model's 4P is not universal. Vermont Gage explains the separate thread and cylindrical gauge checks."},
{"title":"Clean contact surfaces before tightening anything","page":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","summary":"Seat and align a milling vise, support work on parallels or soft jaws, and understand how strap-clamp position divides the clamping force.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-workholding.html#clean-contact-surfaces-before-tightening-anything","text":"Clean contact surfaces before tightening anything A vise locates the part against its fixed jaw; the moving jaw supplies the grip. Clean the table and underside of the vise before mounting it. Clean the jaw faces, vise bed, supports and part before loading. A chip trapped underneath changes the position you are trying to establish. Remove raised burrs without rounding away a locating surface. Choose a grip that leaves access for the cutter while supporting the cut. A part perched high above shallow jaw contact is a different setup from a part seated low in the vise. For the operating rules that govern this work, use Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping."},
{"title":"Sweep the fixed jaw, adjust, tighten, then sweep again","page":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","summary":"Seat and align a milling vise, support work on parallels or soft jaws, and understand how strap-clamp position divides the clamping force.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-workholding.html#sweep-the-fixed-jaw-adjust-tighten-then-sweep-again","text":"Sweep the fixed jaw, adjust, tighten, then sweep again Mount the vise with its fasteners engaged. Snug one mounting point enough to act as a pivot and leave the other adjustable. Mount an indicator rigidly with the spindle stopped. Contact a clean, undamaged part of the fixed jaw's locating face with some preload. See indicator setup. Choose a convenient reference near one end; zero there if it makes comparison easier. Traverse so the contact point follows the jaw toward the other end, keeping contact height and indicator orientation unchanged. Alignment comes from the differences across the sweep, whether or not you re-zero. Read the difference, then tap the adjustable end of the vise body lightly with a suitable soft mallet. Sweep again. Use the demonstrated indicator response to choose the direction. Tighten the mounting fasteners to the vise/mounting instructions and repeat the full sweep. Accept the setup against the job's alignment requirement, not an invented universal tolerance. First use: apply the same small corrective rotation about different illustrative pivots. The tilt can disappear while both ends settle at different common offsets; re-zeroing afterward is optional convenience. Rotate about a chosen illustrative pivot Physical contact — deviation exaggerated, not to scale P25.000 +0.060 mm toward body contact 100.000 mm Illustrative pose and pivot — the digital value uses the exact model Before: +0.060 mm tilt, old zero at the left Measuring span 0–100 mm; 1 mm face margins Stored zero at 0.000 mm Next pivot P=(25.000, 0) mm Accumulated |d| 0.0000 / 0.0040 mm/mm Left reference 0.000 mm Center reference +0.030 mm Right reference +0.060 mm Right − left +0.060 mm Model summary — full sweep range 0.060 mm After −0.0006 about 25 mm: both ends +0.015 mm Physical contact — deviation exaggerated, not to scale P25.000 +0.015 mm toward body contact 100.000 mm Illustrative pose and pivot — the digital value uses the exact model Measuring span 0–100 mm; 1 mm face margins Stored zero at 0.000 mm Next pivot P=(25.000, 0) mm Accumulated |d| 0.0006 / 0.0040 mm/mm Left reference +0.015 mm Center reference +0.015 mm Right reference +0.015 mm Right − left 0.000 mm Model summary — full sweep range 0.000 mm Optional convenience: re-zero at 50 mm Measuring span 0–100 mm; 1 mm face margins Stored zero at 50.000 mm Next pivot P=(25.000, 0) mm Accumulated |d| 0.0006 / 0.0040 mm/mm Left reference 0.000 mm Center reference 0.000 mm Right reference 0.000 mm Right − left 0.000 mm Model summary — full sweep range 0.000 mm The rotation removes the linear tilt while the chosen pivot determines the common offset. Re-zeroing changes the displayed references, not the alignment. Equal endpoints can hide a local high spot Physical contact — deviation exaggerated, not to scale P25.000 +0.080 mm toward body contact 50.000 mm Illustrative pose and pivot — the digital value uses the exact model Measuring span 0–100 mm; 1 mm face margins Stored zero at 0.000 mm Next pivot P=(25.000, 0) mm Accumulated |d| 0.0000 / 0.0040 mm/mm Left reference 0.000 mm Center reference +0.080 mm Right reference 0.000 mm Right − left 0.000 mm Model summary — full sweep range 0.080 mm The endpoints both read 0.000 mm, while the center reads +0.080 mm. Traverse the whole jaw instead of checking only its ends. Model: physical jaw position is a + m·x + b·4(x/L)(1 − x/L). Displayed reading = response × physical position − the raw reading stored as zero. A small illustrative rotation d about world pivot P=(p, 0) updates m to m + d and a to a − p·d; the stored zero is unchanged. The fixed 100 mm measuring span is inset 1 mm from each end of the finite modeled face. This is a first-order small-angle model with |d| ≤ 0.001 mm/mm, 0.0001 mm/mm control steps, and total rotation travel ≤ 0.004. The step is model resolution, not real-vise quantization. It does not model adjustment force. The drawing exaggerates deviation and is not a literal scale. Worked example: over 100 mm, start with left 0.000 and right +0.060 mm. Apply d = −0.0006 about 25 mm: both ends become +0.015 mm, so the tilt is gone but the old zero reveals a common offset. About 50 mm both become +0.030 mm; about 0 mm both become 0.000 mm. Re-zeroing is optional convenience, not an alignment requirement. A real setup is accepted against the job requirement, not a universal tolerance from this model. Alignment procedure: MIT, Introduction to the Mill, pp. 2–3. The comparison geometry and examples here are original. The controls require JavaScript. The model-derived examples above retain the comparison lesson for print and no-JavaScript readers. Prove the sign first. Check which way the reading changes when the contact is displaced a known small amount toward the indicator body. With the response shown above, a higher right-end reading means that end is nearer the indicator: move it away. A lower right-end reading needs the opposite correction. Reverse the contact arrangement and the tap direction can reverse. Read the whole sweep, not just two endpoints Top-view fixed jaw sweep: indicator body on the operator side; increasing readings mean the jaw is closer to the indicator. Left reads zero and right plus 0.06 mm, so move the right end away from the indicator. The simplified direction diagram is another view of the linear preset. Jaw tilt is exaggerated; the traverse arrow follows the contact point and does not name physical table travel. A smooth change suggests angular misalignment. A sudden jump at one spot suggests a chip, burr, damaged jaw or unstable contact. Tightening can move the vise, which is why the final sweep matters. Equal endpoint readings alone cannot reveal a bowed or damaged face between them. Public demonstration: Haas: How to Square and Indicate a Vise. The alignment principle transfers; machine controls do not. Practice tap direction Practice re-zeroing Practice full-sweep interpretation"},
{"title":"Parallels establish height; soft jaws establish a shaped seat","page":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","summary":"Seat and align a milling vise, support work on parallels or soft jaws, and understand how strap-clamp position divides the clamping force.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-workholding.html#parallels-establish-height-soft-jaws-establish-a-shaped-seat","text":"Parallels establish height; soft jaws establish a shaped seat Parallels are matched support bars placed beneath the part. Choose a height that gives cutter clearance and enough jaw engagement. Keep the planned drilling and milling paths clear of them. Snug the part, seat it with light downward taps from a dead-blow mallet, then tighten and check the seating again. Check that both supports are contacting; a loose parallel or visible gap means the part may have lifted. A mallet cannot make a chip underneath disappear. Soft jaws are machinable replacement jaws. A step or pocket cut into them can support a thin part or fit a contour. Machine the pocket with the jaws preloaded in the intended clamping direction, following the vise maker's method. Clean the pocket and check full seating when loading each part; the jaw shape does not excuse insufficient grip."},
{"title":"Put the clamp stud nearer the part than the heel support","page":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","summary":"Seat and align a milling vise, support work on parallels or soft jaws, and understand how strap-clamp position divides the clamping force.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/milling-workholding.html#put-the-clamp-stud-nearer-the-part-than-the-heel-support","text":"Put the clamp stud nearer the part than the heel support A strap clamp bridges the part and a heel support. A step clamp has a toothed heel that mates with a step block. Set the heel to the clamp maker's specified height, normally level with or slightly above the part contact. Locate the part against suitable stops and support it beneath the clamping points; a strap pressing on an unsupported span can bend the work. Place the stud toward the part end of the strap. Keep adequate contact on the part and full seating at the heel. The stud must lie between these contacts. Plan the straps, studs and supports so the tool and holder can pass them. Strap-clamp side view and free-body diagram: stud load P acts downward between upward reactions at the part and heel. Part-to-stud distance is a, stud-to-heel distance is b. Part clamping force is P times b divided by a plus b. A short distance from the part to the stud gives the part a larger share of the same stud load. In the ideal two-contact model, part force = P × b / (a + b). Here P is the downward force from the nut, a is part-contact-to-stud distance, and b is stud-to-heel-contact distance. With P = 6,000 N, a = 20 mm and b = 60 mm, the part receives 4,500 N and the heel receives 1,500 N. Why the force divides this way—and what the model cannot tell you Take moments about the heel: F part (a + b) = Pb. Vertical balance gives F part + F heel = P. These are upward reactions on the strap; the strap pushes downward on the part and heel with equal magnitudes. This neglects strap weight, friction, deformation and additional contacts. It does not calculate stud force from wrench torque, allowable loads, sliding resistance or the number of clamps needed. Those depend on the hardware, workpiece and cutting forces. See Carr Lane's strap-clamp guide for the underlying geometry and hardware guidance. Practice clamp-force sharing"},
{"title":"Offhand grinding guides the work by hand","page":"Offhand Grinding","summary":"Understand bench and pedestal grinder setup, wheel condition, tool-bit shaping and the difference between rim speed and a wheel's maximum RPM.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/offhand-grinding.html#offhand-grinding-guides-the-work-by-hand","text":"Offhand grinding guides the work by hand A bench grinder mounts on a bench; a pedestal grinder stands on a base. In offhand grinding, the operator guides the work against the wheel, usually supported by a tool rest. This suits shaping and sharpening; it does not provide the controlled workholding and feeds of Precision Grinding. For a conventional straight bench wheel, the intended grinding surface is usually its outer circumference. Move the work across that surface to distribute wear. Use only the surfaces approved for that wheel and machine; a wheel designed for peripheral grinding is not automatically suitable for side grinding. Before operating, use Equipment Operating Procedure. Injury hazards and protective measures belong in Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf. This page explains the process, not a machine-specific operating authorization."},
{"title":"Wheel wear changes both rest and tongue-guard clearances","page":"Offhand Grinding","summary":"Understand bench and pedestal grinder setup, wheel condition, tool-bit shaping and the difference between rim speed and a wheel's maximum RPM.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/offhand-grinding.html#wheel-wear-changes-both-rest-and-tongue-guard-clearances","text":"Wheel wear changes both rest and tongue-guard clearances The tool rest supports the work near the wheel. The tongue guard is the adjustable part of the surrounding guard above the working opening; it is not the transparent eye shield. As the wheel becomes smaller, both clearances need checking and adjustment to the applicable machine instructions and requirements. Side-view schematic with the wheel inside a surrounding guard, a tongue guard above the opening, and a tool rest below it; the two clearances are separately labeled. Positions only, not a scale drawing or a gap-setting gauge. The eye shield is omitted for clarity. Make these adjustments with the wheel stopped and the machine secured according to its operating procedure. Recheck after dressing or wheel replacement. Use the specified clearance limits, not the proportions in the drawing. Where to check the clearance requirements OSHA's abrasive-wheel machinery requirements address work rests and adjustable guards. Consult the applicable text and the machine manual for the dimensions and scope."},
{"title":"Inspection, ring testing and dressing do different jobs","page":"Offhand Grinding","summary":"Understand bench and pedestal grinder setup, wheel condition, tool-bit shaping and the difference between rim speed and a wheel's maximum RPM.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/offhand-grinding.html#inspection-ring-testing-and-dressing-do-different-jobs","text":"Inspection, ring testing and dressing do different jobs Inspection checks condition and suitability before mounting. A ring test is an additional pre-mounting check for applicable vitrified wheels: a light nonmetallic tap produces a sound that a trained person evaluates for indications of cracking. Wheel size, shape and composition limit where the test applies. A clear ring does not replace the other checks. Truing restores the required wheel shape and concentric running. Dressing renews the cutting surface by removing unwanted material and exposing useful abrasive. An appropriate dressing operation may do both. Use the dresser and method specified for the wheel. Loading means work material fills the wheel's spaces. Glazing means dull abrasive remains at the surface and rubs instead of cutting effectively. Either condition calls for correction, not simply more pressure. Why a wheel can glaze, and where ring testing applies Glazing depends on the wheel grade and operating conditions as well as the work material. Hard material alone does not diagnose it. Norton's ring-test guidance explains the method's application and limitations; follow the full manufacturer procedure before mounting."},
{"title":"Shape an HSS tool bit without overheating its edge","page":"Offhand Grinding","summary":"Understand bench and pedestal grinder setup, wheel condition, tool-bit shaping and the difference between rim speed and a wheel's maximum RPM.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/offhand-grinding.html#shape-an-hss-tool-bit-without-overheating-its-edge","text":"Shape an HSS tool bit without overheating its edge Grinding a high-speed steel tool bit creates the faces that meet at the cutting edge. Clearance behind the edge lets it cut without the adjacent tool face rubbing; rake influences chip flow. The required angles come from the intended tool and work material, not one universal sharpening angle. Use light, controlled contact and check the shape frequently. Periodic dipping of a warm HSS bit in water can control temperature when the tool's instructions permit it. This is cooling during sharpening, not heat-treatment quenching; do not heat the bit severely and then plunge it into water. Do not transfer that dipping method to a hot cemented-carbide tip. Sudden cooling can crack carbide. Use the specified dry method or a compatible continuous wet-grinding process. Intermittently quenching hot carbide is not equivalent to supplying coolant throughout the cut. Carbide needs consistent temperature control General Carbide: The Designer's Guide to Tungsten Carbide, page 50, explains thermal shock and the effects of interrupted coolant during grinding. Follow the tool manufacturer's cooling guidance for HSS as well; permission to cool a warm bit is not permission to quench an overheated one. Aluminum and other soft, ductile metals can smear into and load a standard wheel intended for steel. Use equipment and abrasives specifically approved for the material. Changing from aluminum oxide to silicon carbide by itself does not establish that the whole setup is suitable."},
{"title":"Compare RPM ratings directly; calculate rim speed separately","page":"Offhand Grinding","summary":"Understand bench and pedestal grinder setup, wheel condition, tool-bit shaping and the difference between rim speed and a wheel's maximum RPM.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/offhand-grinding.html#compare-rpm-ratings-directly-calculate-rim-speed-separately","text":"Compare RPM ratings directly; calculate rim speed separately The machine's spindle speed must not exceed the wheel's printed maximum operating RPM. A wheel's grade or grit marking is not that rating. Passing the RPM comparison is only one compatibility check; it does not approve the wheel's mounting, dimensions or application. Rim speed is distance traveled at the wheel's outside diameter. With diameter D in inches and spindle speed N in revolutions per minute: SFPM = π × D × N / 12, in surface feet per minute. The 12 converts inches to feet. Use diameter, not radius. For an illustrative 7 in wheel at 1,750 RPM, π × 7 × 1,750 / 12 = 3,207 SFPM, rounded to the nearest whole foot per minute. This calculation is not a recommended operating speed. A smaller worn wheel has a lower rim speed at the same RPM; that does not authorize exceeding its marked maximum RPM. See Feeds and Speeds for the same surface-speed relationship. Practice wheel speed Practice the RPM comparison"},
{"title":"Controlled motion lets many small cuts produce fine size and finish","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#controlled-motion-lets-many-small-cuts-produce-fine-size-and-finish","text":"Controlled motion lets many small cuts produce fine size and finish Precision grinding combines an abrasive wheel with controlled workholding and machine feeds. Each active grain removes a small amount. A dressed wheel, stable support, small finishing increments and temperature control can produce accurate dimensions and fine surface texture, including on hardened materials. A smooth-looking surface is not proof of correct size or flatness. Wheel condition, vibration, heat and workholding can all affect the result. Inspect the drawing's requirements using Surface Texture and Finish and Tolerances and Fits. Choose the abrasive with Grinding Wheels and Abrasives. Use Equipment Operating Procedure for operating preparation; see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf for injury hazards and protective measures."},
{"title":"A surface grinder separates table travel, crossfeed and downfeed","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#a-surface-grinder-separates-table-travel-crossfeed-and-downfeed","text":"A surface grinder separates table travel, crossfeed and downfeed On a horizontal-spindle reciprocating-table surface grinder, the table carries the work back and forth under the wheel. Crossfeed shifts the contact across the width of the work. Downfeed moves the wheel toward the work in small increments to remove thickness. Other surface grinders use different arrangements, including rotary tables. Three separate views show table travel along the work, crossfeed across its width, and wheel downfeed toward the top face. Separate views keep the three directions distinct. Arrows show motion, not feed amounts. After the finishing downfeed, sparking out continues passes without adding downfeed. The wheel can still remove a small amount as elastic deflection relaxes. Spark-out is not a substitute for measuring the part, and the number of passes is process-dependent."},
{"title":"A magnetic chuck holds suitable ferromagnetic work","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#a-magnetic-chuck-holds-suitable-ferromagnetic-work","text":"A magnetic chuck holds suitable ferromagnetic work A magnetic chuck uses magnetic attraction to hold suitable material such as many steels. It does not directly hold aluminum, brass or every stainless steel. Holding depends on material, contact area, thickness and the chuck's pole arrangement, not simply whether a magnet attracts one corner. Clean mating surfaces and remove burrs that would lift the part. Use an approved fixture for parts that the chuck cannot hold adequately. A thin part can be pulled flat during grinding and spring back after release, so check the finished condition after removing the holding force. Follow the chuck's release procedure after grinding. Residual magnetism may require demagnetizing so particles do not cling to the work. Demagnetizing is not the act that secures the work for grinding. Magnetic holding and release are equipment-specific Industrial Magnetics: grinding chucks shows different chuck arrangements. Industrial Magnetics: electromagnet chuck controllers describes an automatic release cycle that replaces manual demagnetizing. Use the manual for the installed chuck and control, not a generic switch sequence."},
{"title":"Count downfeed increments separately from table strokes","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#count-downfeed-increments-separately-from-table-strokes","text":"Count downfeed increments separately from table strokes For an ideal plan with constant downfeed, number of increments = total thickness to remove / downfeed per increment. One increment may require many table strokes and crossfeed steps to cover the entire face. State what a “pass” means before using the equation. Example: removing 0.024 in from one face at an assumed 0.002 in per full-face layer takes 12 downfeed increments. These are arithmetic teaching values, not machine settings. If the division is not exact, use a smaller final increment rather than removing extra stock. Why a real grinding plan needs more than the quotient The calculation assumes a flat starting face and no wheel wear or elastic error. A real plan may reserve material for lighter finish cuts, include dressing and spark-out, and stop to measure. Stock removal from two faces must be assigned to each face; do not silently count it twice. Practice downfeed counts"},
{"title":"Coolant helps control heat and carry away grinding debris","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#coolant-helps-control-heat-and-carry-away-grinding-debris","text":"Coolant helps control heat and carry away grinding debris Grinding fluid can cool the work, lubricate contact and flush away removed particles. Heat changes dimensions during grinding and can alter the work surface. Delivery into the grinding zone, cleanliness and compatibility with the wheel and work material matter as much as having fluid in the tank. Select the fluid and delivery method for the machine and operation. Do not assume every wheel or grinder permits wet operation. See Cutting Fluids and Application Methods for fluid selection and care, and Coolant Concentration and Mixing for mixing and measurement."},
{"title":"Cylindrical and centerless grinding control round surfaces differently","page":"Precision Grinding","summary":"Follow surface-grinder motions and workholding, plan small downfeed increments, and distinguish surface, cylindrical and centerless grinding.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/precision-grinding.html#cylindrical-and-centerless-grinding-control-round-surfaces-differently","text":"Cylindrical and centerless grinding control round surfaces differently Cylindrical grinding commonly rotates work held between centers or in a chuck while a grinding wheel removes material from an outside diameter. Traverse grinding moves along the length; plunge grinding feeds into a selected width. Internal grinding finishes bores and can use rotating work or other machine arrangements. Centerless grinding supports the work on a blade between the grinding wheel and a regulating wheel. The regulating wheel controls work rotation and, in through-feed operation, helps advance the work axially. The work does not need center holes. Infeed arrangements serve shapes that cannot pass straight through. Each process needs its own setup and wheel selection. A fine finish alone does not establish roundness, straightness or size."},
{"title":"The drawing sets the limits; the process produces the spread","page":"Process Capability and Variation","summary":"Compare the allowed tolerance band with the process's centre and spread, without mistaking five good parts for proof of capability.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/process-capability.html#the-drawing-sets-the-limits-the-process-produces-the-spread","text":"The drawing sets the limits; the process produces the spread A tolerance band is the permitted interval from the lower size limit to the upper size limit. Process spread describes how much the manufactured values vary. Changing a machine setting may shift the centre; it does not necessarily reduce the spread. A capable process consistently fits its variation within the required limits with useful margin. A narrow spread near a limit can still produce rejects if the process drifts. A wide spread cannot be fixed simply by moving its average to the middle. The same tolerance band contains a narrow centred illustrative process spread, while a wider spread shifted to the right crosses the upper limit. Illustrative spreads, not probability curves or measured capability results. Both rows use the same size axis. Read Tolerances and Fits for the size-limit method and Shop Inspection and Quality Control for how these observations guide checks."},
{"title":"Mean locates a sample; range describes its width","page":"Process Capability and Variation","summary":"Compare the allowed tolerance band with the process's centre and spread, without mistaking five good parts for proof of capability.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/process-capability.html#mean-locates-a-sample-range-describes-its-width","text":"Mean locates a sample; range describes its width Consider five invented readings: 24.98, 24.99, 25.00, 25.01 and 25.02 mm, with limits of 24.95 to 25.05 mm. Mean: add all five readings and divide by five: 125.00 / 5 = 25.00 mm. Range: subtract the smallest from the largest: 25.02 − 24.98 = 0.04 mm. Midpoint of the limits: (24.95 + 25.05) / 2 = 25.00 mm. This sample mean is centred on that midpoint, and all five values are inside the limits. Now add 0.04 mm to every reading. The mean becomes 25.04 mm and the range remains 0.04 mm, but the largest reading becomes 25.06 mm and fails. Centre and spread answer different questions. Why a small sample range is not the full process spread The range uses only the two extremes observed in this sample. More readings, different tools, temperature changes and later production may reveal variation not present in these five parts. Standard deviation is another measure of spread that uses every reading's distance from the mean; no standard-deviation calculation is needed for this example."},
{"title":"Passing parts are evidence, not proof of a capable process","page":"Process Capability and Variation","summary":"Compare the allowed tolerance band with the process's centre and spread, without mistaking five good parts for proof of capability.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/process-capability.html#passing-parts-are-evidence-not-proof-of-a-capable-process","text":"Passing parts are evidence, not proof of a capable process Capability concerns a process over time, not a single successful part. First establish that the measurement method is suitable and the process is stable enough to describe meaningfully. Collect representative readings across the conditions the conclusion is meant to cover. A stable process can still be too wide for the tolerance. An unstable process can happen to produce five passing parts. Neither case is resolved by averaging away the problem. Investigate a trend or sudden change using the inspection plan. Where capability indices fit Indices such as Cp and Cpk compare estimated process variation and its position with specification limits. Their interpretation depends on the data, stability, distribution and application requirements. A single threshold is not a universal acceptance rule, and a five-reading classroom example is not a capability study. Capability explorer: assume a stable process with roughly normal output. μ is the process mean; σ (standard deviation) measures spread. These are chosen teaching inputs, not estimates from the five readings above. The five-readings example above explains why passing parts are evidence, not proof of a capable process. Cp = (USL − LSL) / (6σ) compares tolerance width with spread, ignoring centring. Cpk = min(USL − μ, μ − LSL) / (3σ) uses the nearer limit. Cpk is negative when the mean lies outside the limits. LSL and USL are the lower and upper size limits. Predict first: which case has the higher Cp? Does it also have fewer parts outside the limits? Precise but off-centre LSL USL 19.95 20.05 Size (mm) · dashed line = mean Mean 20.045 mm; σ 0.005 mm. Cp 3.33 Cpk 0.33 Outside 158,655 ppm Below LSL: 0.000% · Above USL: 15.866% · Total: 15.866% The narrow spread gives a high Cp, but the mean is only one σ below the upper limit. Cpk reveals that small margin. Centred but wide LSL USL 19.95 20.05 Size (mm) · dashed line = mean Mean 20.000 mm; σ 0.025 mm. Cp 0.67 Cpk 0.67 Outside 45,500 ppm Below LSL: 2.275% · Above USL: 2.275% · Total: 4.550% Centring makes Cp and Cpk equal. The wide spread still puts parts beyond both limits. The sliders need JavaScript. These two calculated cases show the same lesson without it. Expected ppm means parts outside the limits per million under this normal model, not a measured reject count. Values are rounded; less than 1 ppm is not a guarantee of zero rejects. Requirements depend on the application; no universal pass threshold is used here. Practice mean Practice range Check sample centring"},
{"title":"Read the unit and the graduation before the number","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#read-the-unit-and-the-graduation-before-the-number","text":"Read the unit and the graduation before the number The same-looking line can mean 0.025 in, 0.5 mm, or something else. Identify the instrument and its scale before adding anything. On a fractional rule, count spaces from the last whole inch using that edge's denominator. Seven 1/16 in spaces beyond 2 in gives 2 + 7/16 = 2.4375 in. That exact fraction-to-decimal conversion does not make the rule accurate to four decimal places. On a decimal rule with 0.01 in spaces, eight spaces beyond 1.20 in gives 1.28 in. On a digital caliper, read the displayed unit and confirm whether the tool shows an absolute size or a deviation from a stored zero. In inch shop speech, a thou is 0.001 in and a tenth often means 0.0001 in, one tenth of a thousandth. Write units and decimals to avoid confusing a shop tenth with 0.1 in."},
{"title":"An inch micrometer adds sleeve plus thimble","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#an-inch-micrometer-adds-sleeve-plus-thimble","text":"An inch micrometer adds sleeve plus thimble For the conventional 0.001 in micrometer, one turn advances the spindle 0.025 in. The sleeve has a line every 0.025 in; each fourth line marks another 0.100 in. The thimble divides one turn into 25 spaces of 0.001 in. Read the last sleeve line reached by the thimble edge. Count the 0.100 in intervals and the extra 0.025 in lines. Read the thimble line at the sleeve's horizontal datum line. Add the two. Include the range offset for a larger micrometer according to its markings. Example: sleeve 0.300 in plus two 0.025 in lines, with thimble 18 at the datum: 0.300 + 0.050 + 0.018 = 0.368 in Watch the rollover. Sleeve 0.375 and thimble 24 gives 0.399 in. The next 0.001 in step gives sleeve 0.400 and thimble 0: 0.400 in. Do not add the old 0.025 in interval again."},
{"title":"A vernier micrometer adds the fourth decimal place","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#a-vernier-micrometer-adds-the-fourth-decimal-place","text":"A vernier micrometer adds the fourth decimal place The sleeve vernier divides a thimble space into ten parts. Read the sleeve and the whole 0.001 in thimble amount first, using the thimble graduation just passed when the datum falls between lines. Then find the sleeve vernier line that aligns with a thimble line. Example: the last sleeve line is 0.450 in, the datum is between thimble 12 and 13, and sleeve vernier line 7 aligns: 0.4500 + 0.0120 + 7 &times; 0.0001 = 0.4627 in The matching vernier line adds 0.0007 in, not 0.007 in. Do not round the thimble to 13 and then add the vernier. If the zero line aligns, add zero; the end line represents the same alignment at the next interval."},
{"title":"A metric micrometer includes the exposed half millimeter","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#a-metric-micrometer-includes-the-exposed-half-millimeter","text":"A metric micrometer includes the exposed half millimeter On a conventional 0.01 mm micrometer, one turn moves the spindle 0.5 mm. The sleeve shows whole millimeters and half-millimeter marks; 50 thimble spaces divide the turn into 0.01 mm increments. If 8 mm and the following 0.5 mm mark are exposed, and thimble 23 aligns, the reading is 8 + 0.5 + 0.23 = 8.73 mm. If that half-millimeter mark is still covered, the same thimble position gives 8.23 mm. At the rollover, 8.49 mm is sleeve 8.0 plus thimble 49; 8.50 mm is sleeve 8.5 plus thimble 0. Use the marked unit; these increments are not the inch micrometer's increments converted by eye."},
{"title":"A 0.001 in vernier caliper adds the aligned division","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#a-0-001-in-vernier-caliper-adds-the-aligned-division","text":"A 0.001 in vernier caliper adds the aligned division For the 25-division inch vernier used here, each main-scale space is 0.025 in. Twenty-five vernier spaces occupy the length of 24 main-scale spaces, so each vernier space is 0.024 in. Their 0.001 in difference makes the fine reading possible. Two original scale diagrams compare aligned zeros with a vernier shifted 0.003 inch. Main spaces are 0.025 inch and vernier spaces are 0.024 inch; after the shift the third vernier line aligns. The shortened diagram shows the first few lines of a 25-division scale. The mismatch accumulates by 0.001 in per division. Read the last main-scale line at or before the vernier zero. Find the vernier division that lines up with a main-scale line. Add its division number &times; 0.001 in. Vernier zero just beyond 1.325 in, with division 17 aligned, gives 1.325 + 0.017 = 1.342 in. It is the vernier zero, not the right end of the slider, that locates the coarse reading. Other 0.001 in verniers can have different spacing A 50-division vernier can span 49 main-scale spaces of 0.050 in. Each vernier space is 0.049 in, again leaving a 0.001 in difference. Read the actual main scale; do not apply the trainer's 0.025 in spacing to that tool. The Starrett catalog, page 102, illustrates the 50-division arrangement."},
{"title":"A dial caliper adds the beam reading and the dial remainder","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#a-dial-caliper-adds-the-beam-reading-and-the-dial-remainder","text":"A dial caliper adds the beam reading and the dial remainder On an inch dial caliper marked 0.100 in per revolution, the dial's 100 divisions each represent 0.001 in. Read whole inches and completed 0.100 in intervals on the beam, then add the dial reading. Beam 1.200 in and dial 47 gives 1.200 + 0.047 = 1.247 in. Beam 1.200 plus dial 99 is 1.299 in. At the next thousandth the beam reaches 1.300 and the dial returns to zero. Some dial calipers use a different travel per revolution. Check the dial markings rather than assuming this example applies. Clean the jaws, close gently, check zero, and confirm the reading again after measuring."},
{"title":"Practice reading a new micrometer or vernier scale","page":"Reading Measuring Scales","summary":"Read rules, micrometers, calipers, and verniers by separating the coarse scale from the remainder.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reading-measuring-scales.html#practice-reading-a-new-micrometer-or-vernier-scale","text":"Practice reading a new micrometer or vernier scale The trainer draws ideal scales with zero error. It teaches reading, not instrument accuracy. Micrometer examples stay within 0&ndash;1 in; caliper examples stay below 2 in. Enter the exact indicated reading in inches. Read these scale positions before uncovering the answers. These examples also serve as the print version of the trainer. Inch micrometer: sleeve 0.275 in, thimble 24 at the datum. Vernier micrometer: sleeve 0.275 in, datum between thimble 24 and 0, vernier line 9 aligned. 25-division vernier caliper: zero past main line 1.150 in, division 8 aligned. Readings as sums 1. 0.275 + 0.024 = 0.299 in. 2. 0.2750 + 0.0240 + 0.0009 = 0.2999 in. 3. 1.150 + 0.008 = 1.158 in. Enable JavaScript for random drawn scales. The described examples and practice bank work without it. Add a micrometer reading to practice Add a vernier reading to practice Reading references The Starrett catalog, page 22, explains inch and vernier micrometer scales. The Mitutoyo Quick Guide, page 9, explains the metric micrometer. The examples and diagrams here are original teaching examples."},
{"title":"Clean, check, measure, and check again","page":"Reliable Shop Measurement","summary":"Control zero, contact force, alignment, cleanliness, and temperature before trusting the last digit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reliable-shop-measurement.html#clean-check-measure-and-check-again","text":"Clean, check, measure, and check again A good reading starts before the scale is read. Use this sequence with the instrument's own instructions. Identify the feature, its limits, and its reference surfaces. Choose a suitable measuring tool. Clean the contact faces and the part. Look for chips, dirt, raised burrs, and damage that would hold the contacts apart. Let the part and instrument settle to the measuring environment. Avoid a freshly cut hot part or a tool warmed by long handling. Check zero or the correct setting reference. Close a 0&ndash;1 in outside micrometer gently; use the appropriate setting standard for larger ranges or inside tools. For a critical size, check a suitable known reference near the working size as well. Confirm the instrument is within its required calibration status. Measure with the intended contact force and alignment. Release and repeat to check that you can reproduce the reading. Record the result with units, location, and enough method information to repeat it. Check zero again afterward; investigate any change. Zero adjustment removes an offset at one position. It cannot repair worn jaws, a damaged screw, or an error that changes along the range. If a tool fails its checks, have it evaluated rather than hiding the error with a new zero."},
{"title":"Use consistent light contact instead of squeezing out a number","page":"Reliable Shop Measurement","summary":"Control zero, contact force, alignment, cleanliness, and temperature before trusting the last digit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reliable-shop-measurement.html#use-consistent-light-contact-instead-of-squeezing-out-a-number","text":"Use consistent light contact instead of squeezing out a number With a micrometer, bring the faces close, then use the ratchet or friction thimble as its maker directs. These devices help repeat the measuring force. They do not make dirt, tilt, or a flexible part disappear. Use a smooth, slow approach. The required clicks or turns depend on the mechanism; a friction thimble may slip without clicking. Do not force the main thimble after the force-limiting device operates. The spindle lock holds a setting; it is not a clamp for tightening the measurement. With a caliper or transfer gauge, feel means consistent light contact or drag, not a hard squeeze. Too much force bends jaws and compresses thin parts. Repeated readings should agree because the setup is repeatable, not because the tool has been forced to the expected number."},
{"title":"Keep contacts square and look straight at the scale","page":"Reliable Shop Measurement","summary":"Control zero, contact force, alignment, cleanliness, and temperature before trusting the last digit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reliable-shop-measurement.html#keep-contacts-square-and-look-straight-at-the-scale","text":"Keep contacts square and look straight at the scale A tilted instrument can span a diagonal rather than the intended distance. For an outside diameter, keep the measuring direction perpendicular to the cylinder's axis. For a two-point inside measurement, the contacts must pass through the bore center in the cross-section while staying square along its length. A single instruction to “always find the largest” or “always find the smallest” does not cover both alignment directions. Parallax is a reading error from looking at separated scales at an angle: the marks appear to shift against each other. Put your eye square to the reading line. Good lighting helps distinguish a line from a reflection. For an indicator, keep its intended motion and contact geometry aligned. Preload enough to maintain contact throughout the expected movement, while staying within its usable travel. Do not apply a universal number of needle turns to both plunger and lever instruments."},
{"title":"Abbe error grows when tilt acts through an offset","page":"Reliable Shop Measurement","summary":"Control zero, contact force, alignment, cleanliness, and temperature before trusting the last digit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reliable-shop-measurement.html#abbe-error-grows-when-tilt-acts-through-an-offset","text":"Abbe error grows when tilt acts through an offset The scale on a caliper runs along the beam, while the part often touches farther down the jaws. If the moving jaw tilts a little, its tip moves more than the scale location does. The scale misses that extra motion. This is Abbe error. Picture a door: a small rotation moves the handle much farther than a point near the hinge. Greater distance between the measurement line and the scale line gives tilt more room to create error. Keep the part near the beam when access permits, use light contact, and avoid loose or worn sliders. An ordinary outside micrometer puts its measurement and screw axes in line, reducing this particular source of error. Alignment still matters. The offset-and-angle relationship For a small angle, the error is approximately the offset multiplied by the tilt in radians. An offset of 2 in and a tilt of 0.0005 rad give about 0.001 in of error. This is distinct from simply looking at the scale from an angle. See the Abbe discussion in the Mitutoyo Quick Guide, page 11."},
{"title":"Temperature and surface condition can move the result","page":"Reliable Shop Measurement","summary":"Control zero, contact force, alignment, cleanliness, and temperature before trusting the last digit.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/reliable-shop-measurement.html#temperature-and-surface-condition-can-move-the-result","text":"Temperature and surface condition can move the result Parts and instruments change size with temperature. Let them approach the same stable measuring conditions; follow the reference temperature and corrections required by the inspection procedure. Avoid direct sun, drafts, and prolonged hand contact with precision tools. Clean contact faces matter because the instrument measures what touches them. A chip can enlarge an apparent outside size; a raised edge can hold a depth-micrometer base above its reference. Surface peaks can affect contact too. See Surface Texture and Finish for surface condition rather than treating every last-digit change as a size change. Measure at several positions when taper or out-of-roundness matters. Keep the individual values. An average can hide an oversize region and does not prove the whole feature meets its limits. For the operating rules around machines, use Equipment Operating Procedure and Machine Hazards and Housekeeping. For the numerical limits themselves, use Tolerances and Fits."},
{"title":"Pitch spaces the teeth; gullets carry the chips","page":"Saw Blade Selection","summary":"Use section shape, teeth in the cut, tooth form, set, and blade material to narrow the choice before consulting the maker's chart.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/saw-blade-selection.html#pitch-spaces-the-teeth-gullets-carry-the-chips","text":"Pitch spaces the teeth; gullets carry the chips TPI means teeth per inch along the blade. A higher TPI is a finer pitch. For evenly spaced teeth, pitch in inches is 1 ÷ TPI. The gullet is the hollow between teeth that provides chip space. Set is the sideways displacement of teeth, making clearance for the blade body in the kerf. Side view shows pitch between adjacent tooth tips and the gullet between teeth; an edge view shows teeth set to either side of the blade body. Pitch is measured along the blade. Set is seen from a different direction. Shapes are schematic."},
{"title":"Estimate teeth in the cut before choosing TPI","page":"Saw Blade Selection","summary":"Use section shape, teeth in the cut, tooth form, set, and blade material to narrow the choice before consulting the maker's chart.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/saw-blade-selection.html#estimate-teeth-in-the-cut-before-choosing-tpi","text":"Estimate teeth in the cut before choosing TPI As a rule of thumb, aim to keep at least about three teeth engaged. For a uniform pitch crossing one continuous thickness of material: Estimated teeth engaged = contact length in inches × TPI Minimum TPI for three teeth = 3 ÷ contact length in inches Use thickness measured along the tooth line through solid material, not automatically the height of the part. For 0.25 in of continuous contact at 12 TPI, the estimate is 0.25 × 12 = 3 teeth. At the same thickness, the three-tooth calculation gives 3 ÷ 0.25 = 12 TPI. At the same 8 TPI, a 0.25 inch contact length spans about two teeth and a 0.75 inch contact length spans about six teeth. Same tooth spacing, different contact length. The thinner section needs a finer pitch to meet the three-tooth estimate. A calculated minimum is a screening value, not a blade specification. If it falls between available pitches, select a suitable finer option from the maker's chart rather than rounding down. Excessively fine teeth can run out of chip space in thick stock. Why the answer can be a fraction of a tooth The product counts tooth spacings across the contact length. Actual engaged tooth tips are discrete, and the number changes as the blade moves. Entry, exit, variable pitch, and curved sections also change contact. This estimate explains the selection principle; it does not guarantee a fixed count throughout a real cut. Predict first: at mid-depth in the thin-wall tube below, would doubling TPI halve, double, or leave the estimated engagement unchanged? It doubles: the same contact length contains twice as many tooth spacings. Dashed line = about three teeth; shading = below three. Each plot uses its own vertical scale. Thin-wall tube, coarse blade Teeth · top of scale 4.00 0 0 50.80 Cut depth (mm) 50.80 mm outside diameter; 1.00 mm wall; 6 TPI. At depth 25.40 mm: 0.47 teeth across 2.00 mm of material. Across the cut: minimum 0.00; maximum 3.33 teeth. Below the common rule of thumb of about three teeth. Contact tends to zero at entry and exit. At mid-depth only the two walls count. The peaks occur where the blade touches the inside circle. Thick solid bar, fine blade Teeth · top of scale 55.20 0 0 25.40 Cut depth (mm) 50.80 mm contact thickness; 25.40 mm feed height; 24 TPI. At depth 12.70 mm: 48.00 teeth across 50.80 mm of material. Across the cut: minimum 48.00; maximum 48.00 teeth. At or above about three teeth here; this is not a blade approval. Gullets may fill with chips; check the blade maker's guidance. The controls need JavaScript. These calculated presets show the engagement curves without it. Estimated teeth = solid contact length ÷ tooth spacing; mm per tooth = 25.4 ÷ TPI. Readouts round to two decimal places; calculations keep the unrounded, real-valued estimate. Highlighted tooth tips illustrate one blade position, not a guaranteed integer count. Round-section minima include the exact entry and exit tangencies, where contact is zero. A flat bar has constant contact through its feed height, including the limiting faces. Rotate the flat bar 90° to swap contact thickness and feed height; rotating a round bar or round tube about its axis changes neither. This model is a straight tooth line crossing an ideal section, with uniform pitch and no blade tilt. The thick-bar, fine-blade preset highlights the full contact region as a reminder: gullets may fill with chips; check the blade maker's guidance. It sets no numeric upper limit. Tooth count alone cannot predict chip filling. For shop practice and hazards, see Cutoff Sawing and Safety collection. Practice teeth in the cut Practice minimum TPI"},
{"title":"A tube's outside diameter is not its solid thickness","page":"Saw Blade Selection","summary":"Use section shape, teeth in the cut, tooth form, set, and blade material to narrow the choice before consulting the maker's chart.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/saw-blade-selection.html#a-tube-s-outside-diameter-is-not-its-solid-thickness","text":"A tube's outside diameter is not its solid thickness A solid bar offers continuous material across the blade's path. A tube, angle, or channel presents walls and gaps, and the contact changes as the cut progresses. Select from a chart for tubes or structural sections, using wall thickness, overall dimensions, and orientation as that chart requires. Thin walls generally need finer teeth than a solid bar of the same outside size. Do not multiply tube outside diameter by TPI and count the empty center as cutting engagement. Nor does simply adding two wall thicknesses describe every stage of a round tube cut. Variable pitch alternates tooth spacing within a repeating group. It can reduce repeated tooth-impact vibration and suit changing engagement, but it still needs to match the material and section. Practice tube versus solid selection"},
{"title":"Tooth form and set describe different features","page":"Saw Blade Selection","summary":"Use section shape, teeth in the cut, tooth form, set, and blade material to narrow the choice before consulting the maker's chart.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/saw-blade-selection.html#tooth-form-and-set-describe-different-features","text":"Tooth form and set describe different features Tooth form Selection idea Regular or standard Common general-purpose form, typically with a zero rake face. Fine versions suit thinner sections. Skip Widely spaced teeth with generous gullets, typically zero rake; useful when softer materials and larger chips need clearance. Hook A positive rake face takes a more aggressive bite. Use where the maker recommends it for the material and the machine can support the cut. Rake describes the angle of the tooth face that meets the material. Exact angles vary by blade; “hook” does not specify one universal angle. Set pattern Arrangement and use Raker A common sequence is left, right, straight. A general-purpose arrangement; the straight tooth is unset. Alternate Successive teeth are set left and right. Wavy Groups of teeth vary their sideways set in a wave. Common on fine-pitch blades for thin sections. Set pattern does not replace pitch selection. Choose the complete blade specification, not a tooth-form name alone."},
{"title":"Choose blade material and dimensions for the machine","page":"Saw Blade Selection","summary":"Use section shape, teeth in the cut, tooth form, set, and blade material to narrow the choice before consulting the maker's chart.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/saw-blade-selection.html#choose-blade-material-and-dimensions-for-the-machine","text":"Choose blade material and dimensions for the machine Carbon steel: an economical choice for suitable easy-to-machine materials and lighter service. Bimetal: high-speed-steel cutting teeth joined to a flexible alloy-steel back; widely used for metal cutoff work. Carbide-tipped: carbide cutting tips for applications needing greater wear resistance, with a compatible machine and cutting conditions. Match blade length and thickness to the machine. Blade width, measured from tooth tip to back, also affects contouring; see Vertical Band Machining. Material name alone does not establish a blade's hardness limit or speed. Use the maker's product chart for the actual alloy and condition. Manufacturer resources for the next selection step Consult the chart supplied with the blade. Public starting points include Starrett's carbon blade range and DoALL's blade FAQ. Product-specific recommendations govern; the estimates here are teaching calculations."},
{"title":"Screen the material and feature before choosing a machine","page":"Selecting a Material-Removal Process","summary":"Screen candidates by material, feature access, thickness and surface requirements before comparing production cost.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/selecting-a-removal-process.html#screen-the-material-and-feature-before-choosing-a-machine","text":"Screen the material and feature before choosing a machine Identify the material, its condition and conductivity. Check Material Families and Material Properties. Mark the feature: through profile, blind cavity, shallow recess or finished surface. Identify tool access and any start-hole requirement. Record thickness, minimum internal radius and the thinnest remaining feature. Read the drawing's dimensional and surface requirements. Decide whether a thermal HAZ or recast layer is acceptable. Shortlist processes, then check actual equipment capability, finishing needs and production quantity. Hardness alone does not select a process. A hard nonconductive ceramic rules out ordinary EDM even though EDM can cut hardened steel. See Tolerances and Fits and Surface Texture and Finish for what the finished part must meet."},
{"title":"Use the feature to narrow the candidates","page":"Selecting a Material-Removal Process","summary":"Screen candidates by material, feature access, thickness and surface requirements before comparing production cost.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/selecting-a-removal-process.html#use-the-feature-to-narrow-the-candidates","text":"Use the feature to narrow the candidates Starting candidates, not guaranteed process approvals Part requirement Candidate and reason Check next Hardened steel die with a through opening and small inside radii Wire EDM: conductive work, low cutting force, fine profile Wire radius plus gap, threading access and surface integrity Blind cavity in hardened conductive metal Sinker EDM: shaped electrode reaches a cavity Electrode manufacture, wear and flushing access Thick non-tempered glass plate with a through profile Abrasive waterjet: no conductivity requirement or thermal HAZ Glass type, thickness capability, piercing and edge quality Shaped hole in a hard nonconductive ceramic Ultrasonic Machining: abrasive impact removes brittle material Tool wear, depth, chipping and required finish Fine profile in thin metal sheet Laser: concentrated beam and narrow kerf Material match, HAZ and edge quality Conductive metal plate blank for later machining Plasma: productive thermal cutting candidate Thickness capability and machining allowance Soft foam gasket Pure waterjet: water alone can separate soft material Support, water compatibility and detail size Repeated complex metal contours without an EDM recast layer ECM: dissolution instead of melting Material chemistry, electrolyte flow and tooling cost Many shallow areas in metal sheet Chemical milling: masked areas can be etched together Undercut, depth control and surface finish"},
{"title":"Keep conventional machining on the candidate list","page":"Selecting a Material-Removal Process","summary":"Screen candidates by material, feature access, thickness and surface requirements before comparing production cost.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/selecting-a-removal-process.html#keep-conventional-machining-on-the-candidate-list","text":"Keep conventional machining on the candidate list For an accessible flat face, hole or round shaft in a machinable material, conventional cutting may be the simplest route. Use the family map to reach those methods. A part may combine sawing, milling and grinding, or use waterjet for a blank followed by machining of critical faces. Increasing thickness can increase cutting time, taper or flushing difficulty. A thin remaining wall may deflect or be damaged even when cutting forces are small. Ask for evidence of capability on the actual material and geometry rather than relying on a universal tolerance ranking. Practice selecting a process Practice HAZ reasoning Safety references: Equipment Operating Procedure, Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"Inspect before a setup error becomes a batch of parts","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#inspect-before-a-setup-error-becomes-a-batch-of-parts","text":"Inspect before a setup error becomes a batch of parts Quality control uses evidence to keep production meeting requirements. Inspection checks what was made; it does not repair a process or make a bad part good. First-article inspection Check the first part from a new or changed setup against the required features before releasing continued production. A good first part supports the setup decision; it does not prove that later parts will stay good. In-process checks Check selected features while production continues. Record results in production order so tool wear or a shifting setup is visible before many parts are affected. Final inspection Check the completed part or lot against the acceptance plan, including requirements that earlier operations could not verify. Keep its identity connected to its results. If a check fails, identify and hold the affected material, report the result, and investigate before continuing under the applicable procedure. Consider parts made since the last known good check; checking only the next part leaves that interval unresolved."},
{"title":"A gauge gives a decision; a measurement gives a value","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#a-gauge-gives-a-decision-a-measurement-gives-a-value","text":"A gauge gives a decision; a measurement gives a value Attribute gauging reports a category such as pass/fail. Variable measurement reports a number, such as 18.012 mm. A number can show how close the process is getting to a limit; a passing gauge alone cannot. For a plain hole, the GO plug checks the small-hole limit and should enter over the specified inspection length. The NO-GO plug checks the large-hole limit and should not enter as defined by the gauging procedure. Do not force either end. For a plain shaft, a GO ring should pass over the specified length and a NO-GO ring should not pass: the GO ring checks the large-shaft limit. Two ends of a plain plug gauge: the smaller GO end should enter the hole; the larger NO-GO end should not enter under the specified gauging procedure. Conceptual plain plug gauge. The diameter difference is exaggerated; these are not gauge manufacturing dimensions. A GO plug that will not enter is a failed check, not proof of one particular cause: an undersize hole, burr, dirt, poor alignment or form error can obstruct it. Clean and investigate using the approved method. A NO-GO plug that enters beyond the permitted engagement is also a failed check. Use the correctly identified gauge with current calibration status. Do not derive gauge sizes directly from workpiece limits. This plain-gauge explanation does not supply the engagement rules for threaded gauges. Why the gauge itself needs limits and wear checks Gauges cannot be manufactured to an infinitely exact size. Their own tolerances and wear allowances are selected under the applicable gauging specification. Repeated use can shrink a plug or enlarge a ring and change its decision."},
{"title":"Build a clean, repeatable surface-plate setup","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#build-a-clean-repeatable-surface-plate-setup","text":"Build a clean, repeatable surface-plate setup A surface plate provides a reference plane. A height gauge measures from that plane; an indicator can compare the part with a reference or show variation as a surface is swept. Neither tool corrects a rocking part or dirt under a support. Read the required feature and reference surfaces on the drawing; select a suitable instrument and range. Clean the plate, supports and contact surfaces. Address burrs under the approved preparation method without altering the feature being inspected. Support the part stably on the intended reference surfaces and allow temperature differences to settle as the measurement procedure requires. Check the instrument against the appropriate reference, set zero or reference height, and use consistent contact force and alignment. Repeat the reading, record the value and units, and investigate disagreement before accepting the result. Instrument handling belongs in Choosing Shop Measuring Tools and Reading Measuring Scales. Machine alignment is covered in Mill Indicators and Part Zero. Size acceptance and surface finish are separate requirements: see Tolerances and Fits and Surface Texture and Finish."},
{"title":"A CMM relates measured points to the part's references","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#a-cmm-relates-measured-points-to-the-part-s-references","text":"A CMM relates measured points to the part's references A coordinate measuring machine (CMM) records positions in three dimensions using a probe or sensor. Software uses sampled points to evaluate features such as a plane, bore or hole location relative to the chosen part alignment. The operator still needs a suitable setup, probe qualification, measurement strategy and drawing interpretation. A few points cannot reveal every possible surface defect. A CMM is a measurement system, not an automatic guarantee that every requirement has been checked."},
{"title":"Increase checks when the process is new or changing","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#increase-checks-when-the-process-is-new-or-changing","text":"Increase checks when the process is new or changing Sampling means inspecting some parts to learn about a larger group or process. It reduces inspection effort but leaves a chance that unmeasured defects will escape. Select samples by a defined plan, not by choosing the nicest-looking parts. A new setup, tool replacement, unexpected variation or a trend toward a limit calls for closer attention and potentially more frequent checks under the inspection plan. A demonstrated stable process may justify fewer checks; feature consequences and customer requirements still matter. Some features require every part to be inspected. Keep the readings in order and compare their trend and spread, not just their pass/fail status. Process Capability and Variation explains that distinction. Do not repeatedly adjust a stable process just because its readings differ slightly."},
{"title":"Trust a result only as far as the measurement supports it","page":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","summary":"Check the first part, watch the process during production, and use suitable measurements to decide whether parts meet requirements.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/shop-inspection.html#trust-a-result-only-as-far-as-the-measurement-supports-it","text":"Trust a result only as far as the measurement supports it Accuracy concerns agreement with a reference or true value; precision concerns agreement among repeated measurements under stated conditions. Readings can cluster tightly and still be consistently wrong. Calibration compares instrument indications with reference values under specified conditions and establishes their relationship and uncertainty. It is not merely setting zero, and adjustment is a separate action. Traceability belongs to a measurement result supported by a documented chain of calibrations, each contributing uncertainty. For classroom limit comparisons, treat the stated numbers as exact and include both endpoints. In real inspection, uncertainty matters near a limit: apply the specified acceptance decision rule rather than assuming the last displayed digit is exact. Practice limit decisions Practice gauge interpretation"},
{"title":"Pitch spaces the teeth; lead sets carriage travel per revolution","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#pitch-spaces-the-teeth-lead-sets-carriage-travel-per-revolution","text":"Pitch spaces the teeth; lead sets carriage travel per revolution A thread follows a helix around a cylinder. Pitch P is the axial distance between corresponding points on adjacent teeth. For N threads per inch, P = 1/N inch. A 20-per-inch thread has a pitch of 0.050 in. Lead is the axial advance along one helix in one complete revolution. A single-start thread has one helix, so lead equals pitch. A two-start thread has two interleaved helices and lead equals 2P. In general, lead = number of starts &times; pitch. Threading feed follows lead; the examples here use single-start threads. The crest is the top of a tooth and the root is the bottom of the groove. The sides are flanks. The thread angle is the included angle between flanks in an axial section. This page discusses 60&deg; profiles; other thread forms need other tools and geometry. Axial section of an external thread showing crests, roots, pitch, flank angle and major, pitch and minor diameter levels. Schematic external thread, with only the upper half shown. Diameters span across the axis to the opposite side."},
{"title":"Major diameter alone cannot tell you whether a thread fits","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#major-diameter-alone-cannot-tell-you-whether-a-thread-fits","text":"Major diameter alone cannot tell you whether a thread fits Major diameter The largest thread diameter: across external crests or internal roots. Minor diameter The smallest thread diameter: across external roots or internal crests. Pitch diameter The diameter of an imaginary cylinder at which thread tooth width and groove width are equal for the ideal profile. It strongly affects flank clearance and fit. The outside of a screw can measure correctly while its flanks are still too thick. Use the thread designation and its specified limits, not just nominal outside diameter. Tolerances and Fits explains limits; Lathe Operations explains what the simulator's simplified thread check measures."},
{"title":"The tool stays square while the compound feeds near one flank","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#the-tool-stays-square-while-the-compound-feeds-near-one-flank","text":"The tool stays square while the compound feeds near one flank For a 60&deg; thread, use a tool with the required 60&deg; profile and the specified tip shape. A center gauge, also called a fishtail gauge, checks a ground tool's angle and helps align its bisector perpendicular to the work axis. Set the cutting edge at spindle center height. Swiveling the compound does not mean turning the tool away from square. A common manual arrangement sets compound travel about 29&ndash;30&deg; from the radial cross-feed direction for this thread form. That is about 60&ndash;61&deg; from the spindle axis. Check the scale's zero reference before setting it. A square 60-degree tool advances diagonally about 29 degrees from radial travel, nearly parallel to one 30-degree flank, while its orientation remains unchanged. The angle describes the infeed path between passes, not the tool's included angle or the thread helix angle. Near-flank infeed puts most of the cutting on one edge. Exactly 30&deg; is parallel to a flank in the ideal axial profile; slightly less also gives the other edge a small cut. Tool-maker guidance may call for a different infeed strategy. Radial depth and compound travel are different quantities If compound travel is c at angle &beta; from radial, its radial component is h = c cos(&beta;); therefore c = h / cos(&beta;). The corresponding diameter reduction is 2h. Check whether a dial reads actual slide travel or diameter change before using it."},
{"title":"Keep every pass synchronized with the same helix","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#keep-every-pass-synchronized-with-the-same-helix","text":"Keep every pass synchronized with the same helix The gearbox establishes the relationship between spindle rotation and leadscrew rotation. Closing the half-nuts connects the carriage to the leadscrew so that each spindle revolution advances the tool by the selected lead. An ordinary power-feed setting is not a substitute for a threading setting. Check the machine's threading-dial chart before re-engaging the half-nuts. On an inch-leadscrew lathe, the engagement pattern depends on how the dial is graduated. With four numbered lines and unnumbered half-lines between them, common on older inch lathes with an 8-TPI leadscrew, an odd number of threads per inch (TPI) can usually be caught on any numbered line, but not on the half-lines (see LeBlond's four-numbered-line chart ). On dials numbered 1&ndash;8, odd TPI are often restricted to lines of one parity (all odd or all even). Even TPI can usually be caught on any line, numbered or half. Fractional TPI may require returning to the same specified line or keeping the half-nuts engaged. These are examples, not universal dial settings: leadscrew pitch and dial gearing determine which marks work, and the machine's chart controls. Why only certain threading-dial positions return to the groove The threading dial is geared to the leadscrew and carried with the carriage. Its rotation tracks their relative position. For a selected thread, certain dial positions repeat the relationship that lets the tool enter the same helical groove when the half-nuts close. Other positions can put the tool between previous passes, cutting a second groove. For some combinations, notably many metric threads on inch-leadscrew lathes, ordinary dial re-engagement will lose synchronization. Such setups commonly keep the half-nuts engaged and use the maker's prescribed return method. Confirm the machine and workholding permit that method; this page supplies no universal reversing procedure. Identify the thread form, hand, pitch, number of starts and required limits on the drawing. Check the machine chart for the gearing and permitted half-nut/dial procedure. Check a light scratch pass with a pitch gauge before deepening the thread. Plan the end of each pass and the return path before cutting to depth. Measure as the thread approaches size; use the specified inspection method to decide when it is finished. Machine operation and hazards belong on Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf."},
{"title":"Calculate a profile depth only after naming the profile","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#calculate-a-profile-depth-only-after-naming-the-profile","text":"Calculate a profile depth only after naming the profile A 60&deg; angle and a pitch do not, by themselves, specify a finished thread depth. Crest flats, root shape, tip geometry and dimensional limits all matter. Use the actual thread specification and tooling data for a production cut, then measure the result. Total depth is not a per-pass infeed instruction. For the ideal sharp-V teaching profile used in the practice bank, radial depth is H = P / (2 tan 30&deg;) = (&radic;3 / 2)P &asymp; 0.8660254P. This is the full sharp triangle height, not a finished Unified or metric thread depth. Actual truncated or rounded profiles remove less radial material than this sharp triangle. Verify the formula without a thread-depth table Split the symmetric 60&deg; tooth down the middle. The right triangle has axial width P/2 and radial height H. Its 30&deg; angle gives tan(30&deg;) = (P/2)/H. Rearranging gives the formula above. If a defined profile removes radial heights a at the crest and b at the root from this triangle, its height is H &minus; a &minus; b. Neither a nor b can be guessed from pitch alone. For a hypothetical sharp-V, 20-per-inch thread, P = 0.050 in and H = 0.04330 in. At 29&deg; from radial, ideal compound travel would be H/cos(29&deg;) = 0.04951 in. These calculations isolate geometry; they are not settings for cutting a real 20-per-inch fastener. Practice thread pitch Practice ideal profile depth Practice compound travel"},
{"title":"A pitch gauge checks spacing; wires help measure pitch diameter","page":"Single-Point Threading","summary":"Read thread geometry, distinguish pitch from lead, understand compound infeed and check thread size beyond the outside diameter.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/single-point-threading.html#a-pitch-gauge-checks-spacing-wires-help-measure-pitch-diameter","text":"A pitch gauge checks spacing; wires help measure pitch diameter A pitch gauge has toothed leaves that compare with the thread spacing. It identifies pitch and helps detect a wrong gearbox selection, but it does not certify pitch diameter or fit. In the three-wire method for an external thread, two calibrated wires sit in grooves on one side and a third on the opposite side. A micrometer measures over them. Because the wires contact the flanks, the measurement can be converted to pitch diameter using the correct relation for the thread form and wire size. Use calibrated wire sizes and their applicable instructions or a verified table. Wire diameter, thread angle and lead-angle effects matter; this page does not supply an unchecked wire formula. A thread micrometer uses dedicated contacts for a similar purpose. Appropriate thread plug or ring gauges check functional acceptance against the specified gauge limits. Trying a mating nut may reveal gross interference, but it does not establish conformance to a thread class."},
{"title":"A surface has lay, waviness and roughness","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#a-surface-has-lay-waviness-and-roughness","text":"A surface has lay, waviness and roughness Every manufactured surface carries marks from the process that made it. Surface texture describes those marks: which way they run, how far apart they are, and how tall they stand. Roughness The finest, closely spaced marks, such as the feed marks a cutting tool leaves at each pass or the scratches of abrasive grit. Waviness A longer, gentler undulation that the roughness sits on, for example from vibration or deflection while the part was made. Lay The direction of the main surface pattern, set by how the tool or the part moved. Form The overall shape, such as a face that is slightly curved. Size and geometric tolerances control form; surface texture is what is left on top of it. A patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back, 0.20 mm apart, on a gentle waviness. The stylus trace runs left to right, across the marks. The solid part of the trace is the evaluation length, five sections of 0.80 mm. Heights are drawn 50 times larger than widths. STYLUS FRONT RIGHT SIDE 5.6 mm Heights ×50 Heights drawn 50 times larger than widths. A made-up 5.6 mm patch of surface with parallel marks 0.2 mm apart running front to back, on a gentle waviness. At true scale the marks would be far too small to see. The lines are the crests of the marks; the blue line is a stylus trace across them."},
{"title":"A stylus traces one line, and a filter splits it","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#a-stylus-traces-one-line-and-a-filter-splits-it","text":"A stylus traces one line, and a filter splits it A contact instrument drags a fine stylus along a straight line and records its height. That record is the measured profile. Everything else is worked out from it. Trace direction Usually across the lay, so the stylus crosses the marks. If the drawing specifies a measuring direction, that direction governs. Mean line A smooth line the filter draws through the measured profile, a weighted average of the heights around each point. This mean line is the waviness. Cutoff &lambda;c The wavelength where the filter splits the profile. Undulations much longer than &lambda;c stay in the mean line; marks much shorter than &lambda;c are left over for the roughness. A wave exactly &lambda;c long is shared half and half. Roughness profile The measured profile minus the mean line. Evaluation length The part of the trace the numbers come from. In this lab it is five sections, each &lambda;c long. The trace runs one more &lambda;c at each end so the filter has heights on both sides of every evaluated point. The measured profile, its mean line, and the roughness profile Top: the measured profile along the trace, with the filter's mean line through it; that mean line is the waviness. Bottom: the roughness profile, which is the measured profile minus the mean line, over the five 0.80 mm sections of the evaluation length. Ra 1.55 µm is shown as dashed lines at plus and minus Ra. Section heights from peak to valley: 6.55, 6.56, 6.70, 6.57, 6.87 µm; their mean is Rz 6.65 µm. The largest peak-to-valley height over the whole evaluation length is Rt 7.10 µm. Heights are drawn far larger than lengths. MEASURED PROFILE mean line, which is the waviness -5 0 5 10 extra trace for the filter µm heights ×92 ROUGHNESS PROFILE = MEASURED − MEAN LINE -10 -5 0 5 10 µm heights ×85 1 2 3 4 5 +Ra −Ra mean Rt Z1 6.55 Z2 6.56 Z3 6.70 Z4 6.57 Z5 6.87 0 1 2 3 4 5 mm along the trace The same trace, before and after the filter, with &lambda;c 0.8 mm. Top: the measured profile and its gold mean line; the shaded ends are the extra trace. Bottom: the roughness profile over the five sections, with each section's peak (&#9660;) and valley (&#9651;), its height as a red bar, and &plusmn;Ra dashed. Heights are drawn far larger than lengths; each plot gives its factor at the right. The filter decides what counts as roughness, not the size of the undulation. Change &lambda;c and the same undulation can move from the waviness into the roughness. That is why a measurement result needs its cutoff, and why a drawing or its standard says which one applies."},
{"title":"Ra, Rz and Rt read the roughness differently","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#ra-rz-and-rt-read-the-roughness-differently","text":"Ra, Rz and Rt read the roughness differently All three come from the same roughness profile. They answer different questions about it. This lab uses the older ISO 4287 convention with five sections for Rz; the drawing-symbol comparison separately shows older and current graphics. Ra The average distance of the roughness profile from its mean line over the evaluation length. Peaks and valleys count alike. Rz The average of the sections' peak-to-valley heights. Each section counts once, whatever is in it. Rt The largest peak-to-valley height over the whole evaluation length: the single highest peak to the single deepest valley. Ra = average of |roughness| over the evaluation length Rz = (Z1 + Z2 + Z3 + Z4 + Z5) &divide; 5 For the trace above: Ra 1.55 &micro;m; Rz = (6.55 + 6.56 + 6.70 + 6.57 + 6.87) &divide; 5 = 6.65 &micro;m; Rt 7.10 &micro;m. The values are in micrometres (&micro;m). They can also be given in microinches (&micro;in): one inch is exactly 25.4 mm, so 1 &micro;in is exactly 0.0254 &micro;m, and Ra 1.6 &micro;m is 63.0 &micro;in. Name the standard. Current documents use the ISO 21920 series. Many existing drawings and instruments use the older ISO 4287 parameters and ISO 1302 symbols. The names Ra, Rz and Rt carried over, but the standards are not identical, so this page says which one each symbol comes from and does not mix them. For a real part, check which standard the drawing invokes."},
{"title":"Surface texture lab: trace a surface and hold it to a drawing","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#surface-texture-lab-trace-a-surface-and-hold-it-to-a-drawing","text":"Surface texture lab: trace a surface and hold it to a drawing Change the surface, the trace and the drawing, and watch the numbers. Start with experiment 1. The block is a few millimetres of surface. Lines are the crests of the marks; the blue line is the stylus trace, solid over the evaluation length. The plots are that trace: the measured profile with its mean line, then the roughness profile with its five sections. The drawing holds one requirement against this one trace and writes it as a callout in the view you choose. The interactive lab needs JavaScript. The table below gives the four experiments, measured with the same calculation. Parallel marks 0.2 mm apart, mark height 6 &micro;m, &lambda;c 0.8 mm unless stated. Teaching surfaces, not predictions for any process. Experiment Setting Ra, &micro;m Rz, &micro;m Rt, &micro;m Across or along the marks Trace left to right, across 1.55 6.65 7.10 Trace front to back, along 0.328 1.59 1.70 Where waviness ends 12 &micro;m waviness, &lambda;c 0.8 mm 1.57 6.75 7.43 Same surface, &lambda;c 2.5 mm 2.11 11.3 11.8 One deep scratch No scratch 1.55 6.65 7.10 Scratch three times the mark height 1.88 10.5 23.4 The same-Ra experiment is the figure in the next section."},
{"title":"Same Ra, different surfaces","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#same-ra-different-surfaces","text":"Same Ra, different surfaces These three roughness profiles all have Ra 1.6 &micro;m. They are not the same surface. All three plots use the same height scale. Roughness profile: rounded valleys, sharp crests Top: the measured profile along the trace, with the filter's mean line through it; that mean line is the waviness. Bottom: the roughness profile, which is the measured profile minus the mean line, over the five 0.80 mm sections of the evaluation length. Ra 1.6 µm is shown as dashed lines at plus and minus Ra. Section heights from peak to valley: 6.76, 6.77, 6.90, 6.78, 7.08 µm; their mean is Rz 6.86 µm. The largest peak-to-valley height over the whole evaluation length is Rt 7.32 µm. Heights are drawn far larger than lengths. ROUGHNESS PROFILE = MEASURED − MEAN LINE -20 -10 0 10 20 µm heights ×43 1 2 3 4 5 +Ra −Ra mean Rt Z1 6.76 Z2 6.77 Z3 6.90 Z4 6.78 Z5 7.08 0 1 2 3 4 5 mm along the trace Rounded valleys, sharp crests. Ra 1.6, Rz 6.86, Rt 7.32 &micro;m. Roughness profile: flat tops, narrow grooves Top: the measured profile along the trace, with the filter's mean line through it; that mean line is the waviness. Bottom: the roughness profile, which is the measured profile minus the mean line, over the five 0.80 mm sections of the evaluation length. Ra 1.6 µm is shown as dashed lines at plus and minus Ra. Section heights from peak to valley: 11.1, 10.4, 10.6, 10.9, 11.1 µm; their mean is Rz 10.8 µm. The largest peak-to-valley height over the whole evaluation length is Rt 11.2 µm. Heights are drawn far larger than lengths. ROUGHNESS PROFILE = MEASURED − MEAN LINE -20 -10 0 10 20 µm heights ×43 1 2 3 4 5 +Ra −Ra mean Rt Z1 11.1 Z2 10.4 Z3 10.6 Z4 10.9 Z5 11.1 0 1 2 3 4 5 mm along the trace Flat tops, narrow grooves. Ra 1.6, Rz 10.8, Rt 11.2 &micro;m. Most of the profile sits above the mean line. Roughness profile: narrow peaks, flat valleys Top: the measured profile along the trace, with the filter's mean line through it; that mean line is the waviness. Bottom: the roughness profile, which is the measured profile minus the mean line, over the five 0.80 mm sections of the evaluation length. Ra 1.6 µm is shown as dashed lines at plus and minus Ra. Section heights from peak to valley: 10.4, 11.0, 11.1, 10.4, 11.0 µm; their mean is Rz 10.8 µm. The largest peak-to-valley height over the whole evaluation length is Rt 11.2 µm. Heights are drawn far larger than lengths. ROUGHNESS PROFILE = MEASURED − MEAN LINE -20 -10 0 10 20 µm heights ×43 1 2 3 4 5 +Ra −Ra mean Rt Z1 10.4 Z2 11.0 Z3 11.1 Z4 10.4 Z5 11.0 0 1 2 3 4 5 mm along the trace Narrow peaks, flat valleys. Ra 1.6, Rz 10.8, Rt 11.2 &micro;m. Most of the profile sits below the mean line. Ra alone cannot tell these apart. Rz separates the rounded marks from the other two, but not flat tops from narrow peaks. Where the extremes sit, above or below the mean line, separates those two. One number does not describe a surface. That is why a drawing names the parameter, not just a value."},
{"title":"Reading a surface texture symbol","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#reading-a-surface-texture-symbol","text":"Reading a surface texture symbol The symbol's point touches the line that represents the surface in a drawing view. Its shape says how the surface may be made; the text beside it says what texture is required. The three process requirements, in the older and the current graphics. Schematic drawings: the standards dimension the symbols exactly. Symbol Older, ISO 1302 Current, ISO 21920-1 Meaning Basic Older symbol (ISO 1302): any process permitted, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 1302:2002 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. Ra 3.2 Current symbol (ISO 21920-1): any process permitted, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 21920-1:2021 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. A separate short dash floats above the short leg, touching neither leg. Ra 3.2 Any manufacturing process is permitted. This does not forbid machining; it simply does not require it. Bar Older symbol (ISO 1302): material removal required, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 1302:2002 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface, with a bar across the top of the short leg closing the lower triangle. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. Ra 3.2 Current symbol (ISO 21920-1): material removal required, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 21920-1:2021 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface, with a bar across the top of the short leg closing the lower triangle. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. A separate short dash floats above the short leg, touching neither leg. Ra 3.2 Material removal is required: the surface must be produced by removing material, for example by machining. Circle Older symbol (ISO 1302): material removal prohibited, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 1302:2002 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface, with a circle in the V touching both legs. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. Ra 3.2 Current symbol (ISO 21920-1): material removal prohibited, Ra 3.2 µm Schematic ISO 21920-1:2021 symbol. A check-mark shape whose short left leg and long right leg meet at the surface, with a circle in the V touching both legs. A line runs right from the top of the long leg, with Ra 3.2 written below it. A separate short dash floats above the short leg, touching neither leg. Ra 3.2 Material removal is prohibited: the surface must not be machined or otherwise have material removed to meet this requirement. Current or older An ISO 21920-1 symbol carries a short separate dash above the short leg, touching neither leg. The older ISO 1302 graphic has none, so the familiar older marks are not the current symbols unchanged. The requirement The parameter and its limit, such as Ra 3.2, sit below the long line, to the right of the long leg, in both. The lay In an ISO 21920-1 callout the process and the lay sit above the long line. In the older symbols the lay symbol sits beside the long leg, below the line. A complete specification can state more than this page draws, such as the measuring direction. The process mark is a requirement about how the surface is made; a profile measurement checks the texture, not whether material was removed."},
{"title":"Lay symbols belong to the drawing view","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#lay-symbols-belong-to-the-drawing-view","text":"Lay symbols belong to the drawing view A lay symbol says which way the marks run. = and &perp; are judged against the plane of projection of the view the symbol is drawn in, not against the page or the screen. A patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. An arrow shows the direction the right side view looks. = Parallel. Parallel to the plane of projection of the view in which the symbol is used. A patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with parallel marks running front to back, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. An arrow shows the direction the front view looks. ⊥ Perpendicular. Perpendicular to the plane of projection of the view in which the symbol is used. A patch of surface with two sets of marks crossing, each 30 degrees off front-to-back A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with two sets of marks crossing, each 30 degrees off front-to-back, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. X Crossed. Crossed in two oblique directions relative to the plane of projection of the view in which the symbol is used. A patch of surface with marks running in many directions A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with marks running in many directions, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. M Multidirectional. Multidirectional. A patch of surface with circular marks around the centre A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with circular marks around the centre, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. C Circular. Approximately circular relative to the centre of the surface to which the symbol applies. A patch of surface with marks radiating from the centre A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with marks radiating from the centre, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. R Radial. Approximately radial relative to the centre of the surface to which the symbol applies. A patch of surface with scattered pits with no direction A 5.6 mm square patch of surface with scattered pits with no direction, 0.60 mm apart. Heights are drawn 100 times larger than widths. P Particulate. Particulate, non-directional or protuberant. The first two figures are the same surface. Its marks run front to back: parallel to the right side view's plane of projection, so = there, and perpendicular to the front view's, so &perp; there. The arrow shows which way each view looks. Lay and trace direction are different things. The lay says how the marks run. The trace direction says how they were measured; it is usually across the lay unless the drawing specifies one. The lay symbols and meanings here are those of ISO 1302:2002."},
{"title":"Older notation: N grades and triangle marks","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#older-notation-n-grades-and-triangle-marks","text":"Older notation: N grades and triangle marks Older drawings may give a roughness grade number or a row of triangles instead of a parameter and value. Roughness grade numbers Each grade number stands for one Ra value. A grade is an Ra value: it does not convert to Rz. Grade numbers and the Ra values they stand for. Microinches are exact conversions, rounded for display. Grade Ra, &micro;m Ra, &micro;in N1 0.025 1.0 N2 0.05 2.0 N3 0.1 3.9 N4 0.2 7.9 N5 0.4 15.7 N6 0.8 31.5 N7 1.6 63.0 N8 3.2 126 N9 6.3 248 N10 12.5 492 N11 25 984 N12 50 1969 Triangle marks One catalog prints a comparison of downward triangle marks with Ra values, under the heading \"Extract from JIS B 0601-2001\". It calls the comparison approximate, not rigorous. It gives no acceptance limits, no dates for the marks, and no Rz equivalents, so read it as a rough historical guide. The catalog's comparison, as printed. Marks Ra values listed, &micro;m 4 downward triangles 4 triangles 0.012, 0.025, 0.05, 0.1, 0.2 3 downward triangles 3 triangles 0.4, 0.8, 1.6 2 downward triangles 2 triangles 3.2, 6.3 1 downward triangle 1 triangle 12.5, 25 One requirement, four ways Slide the Ra limit and compare the current symbol, the older symbol, the grade number and the triangle marks for the same value. The interactive comparison needs JavaScript. The two tables above hold the same values."},
{"title":"Read the callout","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#read-the-callout","text":"Read the callout Ten short questions on the symbols, the lay, the older notations and what the numbers respond to. The older ISO 1302 symbol with a circle in its V, and Ra 3.2. May this surface be machined to reach Ra 3.2 µm? No: material removal is prohibited. The circle in the V prohibits material removal. Material removal is prohibited: the surface must not be machined or otherwise have material removed to meet this requirement. A current symbol with an open V, no connecting material-removal crossbar or circle, a separate identifying dash above the short leg, and Ra 6.3. Does this callout forbid machining the surface? No, any process is permitted. The V has no connecting material-removal crossbar or circle: it is the basic symbol. The separate floating dash identifies ISO 21920; it does not require machining. Any manufacturing process is permitted. This does not forbid machining; it simply does not require it. A symbol with a bar closing its V, a short separate dash above the short leg, and Ra 0.8. Which standard's graphic is this? ISO 21920-1 (current). The short separate dash above the short leg, touching neither leg, marks an ISO 21920-1 symbol. The older ISO 1302 graphic has no dash. A block of surface whose marks run front to back. An arrow shows the right side view looking at its right side. The marks run front to back. Which lay symbol belongs on this surface in the right side view? =. The right side view looks along the arrow. Marks running front to back are parallel to that view's plane of projection, so the symbol is =. The same surface takes ⊥ in the front view: the symbol depends on the view, not on the screen or page. A block of surface whose marks run front to back. An arrow shows the front view looking at its front. The marks run front to back. Which lay symbol belongs on this surface in the front view? ⊥. The front view looks along the arrow. Marks running front to back are perpendicular to that view's plane of projection, so the symbol is ⊥. The same surface takes = in the right side view: the symbol depends on the view, not on the screen or page. The grade number N8. An older drawing gives grade N8. What does that stand for? Ra 3.2 µm. N8 stands for Ra 3.2 µm (126 µin). Each step of the grade number doubles or halves the Ra value, near enough. Three downward triangles. In the catalog comparison, which Ra values sit beside three triangles? 0.4, 0.8, 1.6 µm. Three triangles sit beside Ra 0.4, 0.8, 1.6 µm. The catalog calls this comparison approximate, not rigorous. Read it as a rough historical guide, not as acceptance limits. A current symbol with Rz 6.3. Which N grade means the same as this Rz 6.3 µm requirement? None: grades are Ra values. Grade numbers each stand for an Ra value; N9 is Ra 6.3 µm, not Rz 6.3 µm. Ra and Rz measure different things, and how they compare depends on the shape of the profile, as the same-Ra experiment in the lab shows. One deep scratch crosses this otherwise even trace. Which number has the largest proportional change? Rt. In the lab, a scratch three times the mark height takes Rt from 7.10 to 23.4 µm, Rz from 6.65 to 10.5 µm and Ra from 1.55 to 1.88 µm. Rt spans the highest peak to the deepest valley over the whole length. Rz averages five section heights, diluting localized extremes; filtering can spread a scratch across section boundaries. Ra averages every point. A block of surface with parallel marks running front to back, and a stylus trace running left to right across them. The trace on this surface crosses the marks. Traced along the marks instead, Ra would be: Much smaller. In the lab, Ra is 1.55 µm across the marks and 0.328 µm along them: the stylus misses the marks when it runs beside them. Tracing across the lay is the usual choice, but a direction the drawing specifies governs."},
{"title":"What this lab leaves out","page":"Surface Texture and Finish","summary":"A machined surface has a lay, a waviness and a roughness; a stylus trace and a filter turn them into Ra, Rz and Rt, and a drawing symbol says what is required and how the surface may be made.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/surface-texture.html#what-this-lab-leaves-out","text":"What this lab leaves out The lab is a teaching model of one straight trace. A real measurement and a real acceptance decision involve more: The instrument. A real stylus has a tip radius, and instruments apply a short-wavelength filter and remove the part's form before filtering. The lab's stylus is sharp and its part is flat. The filter and parameters. This lab follows the older ISO 4287 teaching convention of five sections for Rz, with a Gaussian mean line having 50% transmission at &lambda;c. Dense samples resolve the narrow marks; numerical integration on a coarser grid computes the smooth mean line. This is not a compliant instrument or a complete ISO 21920 inspection setup, and a drawing or standard can call for other settings. The decision. The lab compares one trace with one limit. The drawing and its standard say how many traces are taken, where, and how the results are judged. The process. No trace shows whether material was removed. The process requirement is checked against how the part was made. Scratches and pits. Whether an isolated flaw counts against a texture requirement is for the drawing and its standard to say. Symbols. The drawings are schematic. The lay symbols are those of ISO 1302:2002; the lab writes the same symbols in the ISO 21920-1 lay position. Check the current standard before relying on one there. Inch-drawing (ASME) practice is not covered here. Sources ISO 21920-1:2021 preview (section 6.2, Figure 4) ZEISS surface parameter poster Mitutoyo, Surface Roughness Measurement (DIN EN ISO 4287:1998 parameters) KEYENCE, surface roughness symbols and lay (ISO 1302:2002) DIATEST Metrologist Reference Book, roughness grade numbers (printed p. 62) MISUMI catalog, printed p. 518: comparison headed &quot;Extract from JIS B 0601-2001&quot; Related pages: Tolerances and Fits, Lathe Operations, Milling Operations and Feeds and Speeds."},
{"title":"Subtract the pitch to estimate a cutting-tap drill","page":"Tap Drills and Tapping","summary":"Estimate a cutting-tap drill, distinguish thread percentage from engagement length, and plan tap geometry, alignment and depth.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tapping.html#subtract-the-pitch-to-estimate-a-cutting-tap-drill","text":"Subtract the pitch to estimate a cutting-tap drill A tap makes an internal thread in a prepared hole. Start by reading the full thread callout. For common straight Unified and metric cutting taps, a useful roughly 75% thread estimate is: Inch: drill diameter ≈ nominal major diameter − 1/TPI. Both diameters are in inches; TPI means threads per inch. Metric: drill diameter ≈ nominal major diameter − pitch. Both dimensions are in millimeters. This is an estimate, not a final hole specification. Use the major diameter, not the minor or pitch diameter. Select the actual drill from the tap maker's chart for the material and required thread. Do not apply this cutting-tap estimate to forming taps, pipe threads or insert-installation taps. Thread Estimate Cutting-tap chart comparison 1/4-20 0.250 − 1/20 = 0.200 in No. 7, 0.2010 in M6 × 1 6 − 1 = 5.00 mm 5.00 mm 3/8-16 0.375 − 1/16 = 0.3125 in 5/16 in, 0.3125 in Chart basis and the approximation's limits The three comparisons were checked against page 1, cutting-tap column, of North American Tool's Tap Drill Sizes — Inch & Metric with Drill Size Formulas, using the supplied four-page copy. Only these selected rows are used here; the chart is not reproduced. The estimate and a selected stock drill need not be identical. The chart's metric percentage formula gives about 77% when the entire pitch is subtracted, which is why “about 75%” must remain approximate. Practice the inch estimate Practice the metric estimate"},
{"title":"Thread percentage is not percent of bolt strength","page":"Tap Drills and Tapping","summary":"Estimate a cutting-tap drill, distinguish thread percentage from engagement length, and plan tap geometry, alignment and depth.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tapping.html#thread-percentage-is-not-percent-of-bolt-strength","text":"Thread percentage is not percent of bolt strength Percent thread describes how much thread height the hole permits relative to a reference profile. With the same cutting tap, a larger prepared hole leaves less thread height; a smaller hole leaves more material for the tap to remove and generally increases tapping torque. It does not describe how far the screw enters the hole. That is engagement length. Nor does 75% thread mean 75% joint strength. Material, engagement length, thread geometry and loading all affect the joint. More thread height is not automatically a better choice. Keep percentage and fraction separate For a metric cutting-tap estimate, the supplied chart gives hole diameter = major diameter − (percentage × pitch / 76.98), entering 75 for 75%. Equivalently, multiplying a reference thread depth by 75% requires the fraction 0.75. Mixing those conventions introduces a factor-of-100 error. This formula estimates geometry, not allowable joint load. Practice thread-percentage reasoning"},
{"title":"Taper, plug and bottoming describe the tap's lead-in","page":"Tap Drills and Tapping","summary":"Estimate a cutting-tap drill, distinguish thread percentage from engagement length, and plan tap geometry, alignment and depth.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tapping.html#taper-plug-and-bottoming-describe-the-tap-s-lead-in","text":"Taper, plug and bottoming describe the tap's lead-in The chamfer is the gradual lead-in where the first teeth progressively cut the thread. A taper tap has a long lead-in that helps starting; a plug tap has a shorter lead-in; a bottoming tap has the shortest of the three and reaches full thread closer to the bottom of a blind hole. A bottoming tap still has a lead-in. It cannot produce full thread all the way to the physical bottom. For hand tapping, start with a tap that can establish alignment, then use a bottoming tap when the required depth calls for it. Leave room for incomplete threads, chips and the drill point. Chamfer style is separate from chip direction. A spiral-point cutting tap generally pushes chips ahead and needs exit space; a spiral-flute cutting tap can draw chips back toward the entrance. Select a tap intended for the material and through or blind hole."},
{"title":"Use a guide to start a hand tap square","page":"Tap Drills and Tapping","summary":"Estimate a cutting-tap drill, distinguish thread percentage from engagement length, and plan tap geometry, alignment and depth.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tapping.html#use-a-guide-to-start-a-hand-tap-square","text":"Use a guide to start a hand tap square Confirm thread size, tap type, drill size, actual hole size and required full-thread depth. Prepare the hole entrance as specified. Clamp the work or hold it in a vise rather than by hand; see Machine Hazards for why. Choose the tap maker's recommended lubricant for the material. Align the tap with a suitable tapping guide. With a spindle used as a guide, keep it unpowered and follow the approved hand-tapping setup. Turn with a suitable tap wrench while maintaining alignment. Follow the tap's chip-breaking instructions; do not force a tap whose resistance rises unexpectedly. Keep within the planned travel, withdraw appropriately, clean and inspect the thread with the specified gauge or inspection method. To help prevent broken taps, use the correct tap drill, maintain alignment, clear chips as the tap's instructions specify, use the recommended lubricant and do not force the tap. Reverse-to-break-chip routines depend on tap design; do not apply one hand-tapping rhythm to every tap. For injury hazards, operating rules and chip handling, see Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment and Metal Chips and Swarf."},
{"title":"Power tapping needs a machine and holder designed for it","page":"Tap Drills and Tapping","summary":"Estimate a cutting-tap drill, distinguish thread percentage from engagement length, and plan tap geometry, alignment and depth.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tapping.html#power-tapping-needs-a-machine-and-holder-designed-for-it","text":"Power tapping needs a machine and holder designed for it Machine tapping coordinates spindle rotation with axial travel at the thread's lead. Depending on the machine, that may use synchronized rigid tapping or a suitable tapping attachment. The machine, holder, tap, reversal and depth control must work together. An ordinary drill-press setup is not automatically a power-tapping setup. A cutting tap removes chips. A forming tap displaces suitable ductile material to make the thread without cutting chips. Forming taps require their own, generally larger, prepared-hole sizes and suitable lubrication. A cutting-tap drill can be much too small for a forming tap of the same thread size. Use the machine's instructions and the approved shop procedure for tapping equipment. Why the local setup decides what is permitted Recognizing a production process does not authorize it on a particular machine. Spindle speed, tapping attachment and power-tapping procedure depend on the particular machine and setup."},
{"title":"Read diameter, spacing, series and class separately","page":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","summary":"Decode inch and metric thread names, distinguish thread fit from strength, and choose fastener features by their function.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/threaded-fasteners.html#read-diameter-spacing-series-and-class-separately","text":"Read diameter, spacing, series and class separately In 1/4-20 UNC-2B, 1/4 is the nominal major diameter in inches, 20 is the number of threads per inch, UNC is the Unified coarse series, and 2B identifies the internal thread's fit class. The major diameter is the largest thread diameter; it is not the diameter of the hole drilled before tapping. The callout 1/4-20 UNC-2B is separated into nominal major diameter, threads per inch, coarse series and class 2 internal thread. M6 x 1 is separated into metric thread, 6 mm nominal major diameter and 1 mm pitch. Read the whole callout. Matching only the nominal diameter is not enough. UNF identifies the Unified fine series. At a given nominal diameter, fine threads have more threads per inch and therefore a smaller pitch than coarse threads. In M6 × 1, M identifies a metric thread, 6 is the nominal major diameter in millimeters, and 1 is the pitch in millimeters. It does not mean one thread per inch. Practice reading a callout"},
{"title":"Thread class controls fit, not fastener strength","page":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","summary":"Decode inch and metric thread names, distinguish thread fit from strength, and choose fastener features by their function.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/threaded-fasteners.html#thread-class-controls-fit-not-fastener-strength","text":"Thread class controls fit, not fastener strength Unified external thread classes are 1A, 2A and 3A; internal classes are 1B, 2B and 3B. A means external, as on a screw; B means internal, as in a tapped hole. Class 1 permits a looser fit, class 2 serves general applications, and class 3 uses closer dimensional control. The class number is not a material grade or a load rating. A class 3 thread does not by itself make a stronger bolt. Read the drawing for the required class and use the appropriate inspection limits. M6 × 1 alone does not specify a metric tolerance class; do not append an inch A/B class to it. For the general distinction between a nominal dimension, its tolerance and a mating fit, see Tolerances and Fits. Exact thread acceptance limits require the applicable specification and gauges."},
{"title":"Pitch is spacing; lead is travel per revolution","page":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","summary":"Decode inch and metric thread names, distinguish thread fit from strength, and choose fastener features by their function.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/threaded-fasteners.html#pitch-is-spacing-lead-is-travel-per-revolution","text":"Pitch is spacing; lead is travel per revolution Pitch is the axial spacing between adjacent thread crests. For an inch thread, pitch in inches = 1/TPI. A 20-TPI thread has a pitch of 0.050 in. Lead is the axial travel in one complete relative revolution. On a single-start thread, lead equals pitch. On a multiple-start thread, lead equals pitch times the number of starts. Right- and left-hand threads move in opposite axial directions for the same rotation. Look for a left-hand designation rather than forcing a fastener that does not start normally. The lathe topic owns the process of single-point thread cutting. The tapping topic explains preparing and making internal threads with taps."},
{"title":"Choose the fastener by how it locates and clamps the parts","page":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","summary":"Decode inch and metric thread names, distinguish thread fit from strength, and choose fastener features by their function.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/threaded-fasteners.html#choose-the-fastener-by-how-it-locates-and-clamps-the-parts","text":"Choose the fastener by how it locates and clamps the parts Part Function and selection question Bolt or screw A bolt commonly passes through clearance holes and uses a nut; a screw commonly engages a tapped part. Names overlap in catalogs, so check the actual product specification. Cap screw A headed screw, often with a hex or socket drive. Choose the head clearance, bearing seat, thread and specified material properties, not just appearance. Set screw Its point bears on a mating part, such as a shaft, to resist relative motion. Point shape and the mating surface matter; it is not simply a small clamping bolt. Stud A headless threaded member, often installed in one part with a nut on the exposed end. Check which end engages the part and how much usable thread remains. Nut Provides the mating internal thread. Match thread size, pitch and required properties; head shape alone does not establish compatibility. Washer Provides a bearing surface beneath a head or nut. Choose its size, hardness and type for the joint rather than assuming every washer also locks it. Threaded insert Provides an internal thread in a parent material or repairs a damaged thread. Follow that insert system's hole, tap and installation requirements."},
{"title":"Engagement, grade and locking are separate decisions","page":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","summary":"Decode inch and metric thread names, distinguish thread fit from strength, and choose fastener features by their function.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/threaded-fasteners.html#engagement-grade-and-locking-are-separate-decisions","text":"Engagement, grade and locking are separate decisions Engagement length is the axial length of mating threads carrying load. There is no single diameter multiplier that establishes adequate engagement for every combination of screw and parent material. Use the joint design or fastener-system requirements, and ensure the screw does not bottom before it clamps. Strength identification must come from the specified fastener grade or property class and its traceable markings or documentation. Thread fit class and head style are not substitutes. Do not assign a strength grade to an unidentified fastener from its appearance. A flat washer is not a locking device. A lock washer or thread-locking compound is not a guarantee against loosening in every joint. Use the specified locking method; for a compound, follow its compatibility, preparation, cure and removal instructions. Check the entire joint before substituting a fastener Diameter, pitch, length and head clearance are only part of the selection. The nut or tapped material, coating, bearing surfaces, installation conditions and intended load also matter. A bolt that can be screwed into the hole has passed an assembly check, not a strength calculation."},
{"title":"A size tolerance gives two limits","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#a-size-tolerance-gives-two-limits","text":"A size tolerance gives two limits Manufacturing produces variation. For each diameter in this lab, the drawing gives a size between two limits. A measured diameter inside that interval passes this size check. Nominal size The size a feature is named by, such as &Oslash;0.7500 in. &Oslash; means diameter. Every limit is measured from this number. Deviation How far a limit sits above ( + ) or below ( &minus; ) nominal. +0.0010 puts a limit 0.0010 in above it. Limits Nominal plus each deviation: the largest and smallest sizes that pass inspection. &Oslash;0.7500 +0.0010 / 0.0000 means anything from 0.7500 in to 0.7510 in. Tolerance The width of that range: largest limit minus smallest limit. Here, 0.0010 in. A size exactly on a limit passes. On this page the limits are part of the allowed range: a 0.7500 in hole and a 0.7510 in hole are both good. A 0.7511 in hole is not."},
{"title":"Clearance is the hole size minus the shaft size","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#clearance-is-the-hole-size-minus-the-shaft-size","text":"Clearance is the hole size minus the shaft size Put a shaft in a hole and the number that decides the fit is the difference between their two diameters. clearance = hole size &minus; shaft size Positive: the hole is bigger. There is a gap, and the shaft slides in. Negative: the shaft is bigger. That is interference: on paper the parts overlap, so the shaft has to be pressed in, or it does not go at all. Zero: exactly the same size, called line-to-line. No gap and no overlap. Clearance is measured across the whole diameter. With the shaft centred in the hole, the gap you would see on each side is half of it: gap on each side = clearance &divide; 2 A shaft in a bored hole, cut in half along its axis A 0.7506 inch hole with a 0.7501 inch shaft through it. The 0.0005 inch clearance is split between the two sides, leaving a 0.00025 inch gap above the shaft and the same below it. The gap is drawn far wider than it would really look. SHAFT &#8960; 0.7501 in HOLE &#8960; 0.7506 in gap each side = (0.7506 &#8722; 0.7501) &#247; 2 = 0.00025 in The gap is drawn hugely enlarged. At the size of this drawing, a 0.00025 in gap would be far too thin to see. Hatching marks the cut face of the block."},
{"title":"Three kinds of fit, decided by the two worst cases","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#three-kinds-of-fit-decided-by-the-two-worst-cases","text":"Three kinds of fit, decided by the two worst cases You do not have to check every pair of parts a drawing allows. Two pairs settle it: the tightest and the loosest. Tightest legal pair The smallest hole with the largest shaft. Its clearance is the least any good pair can have: hole minimum &minus; shaft maximum Loosest legal pair The largest hole with the smallest shaft. Its clearance is the most any good pair can have: hole maximum &minus; shaft minimum The fit is named by where those two numbers land. Fit Tightest pair Loosest pair What it means Clearance gap, or exactly zero gap No permitted pair has dimensional overlap. Equality has no gap. Transition overlap gap Some good pairs slide together; some have to be pressed. Interference overlap overlap, or exactly zero No permitted pair has a positive gap. Equality has no overlap. The zero cases are deliberate. When the tightest pair comes out at exactly zero, every good pair either has a gap or just meets, so this page counts it as a clearance fit and the lab marks it zero at the limit. The same goes the other way for interference. Hole and shaft tolerance ranges for the three kinds of fit Three panels. In each, a box shows the range of sizes allowed for the hole and a second box the range allowed for the shaft, drawn against the same dashed nominal line. For a clearance fit the whole hole box sits above the shaft box. For a transition fit the two boxes share part of their height. For an interference fit the whole shaft box sits above the hole box. HOLE SHAFT CLEARANCE hole always bigger HOLE SHAFT TRANSITION could be either HOLE SHAFT INTERFERENCE shaft always bigger Higher means bigger. Each box is the range of sizes one part may have; the dashed line is the nominal size. The hatched band in the middle panel is the range of sizes both parts are allowed to have, which is what makes an overlap possible."},
{"title":"Fit lab: make two parts, then try them together","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#fit-lab-make-two-parts-then-try-them-together","text":"Fit lab: make two parts, then try them together Pick a drawing, then set the size each part actually came out at. Watch whether each part passes inspection, and whether the two go together. The section view is the pair you made, cut in half. The gold and blue bands are where each wall is allowed to be. The pair map is every pair the drawing allows. Anything above the diagonal has a shaft bigger than its hole. The interactive lab needs JavaScript. The table below holds the same example drawings, worked out. The lab's example drawings. All are made-up teaching limits on a &Oslash;0.7500 in nominal, not values from any standard. Drawing Hole limits Shaft limits Tightest pair Loosest pair Fit Slides freely 0.7500 to 0.7510 0.7480 to 0.7490 +0.0010 +0.0030 Clearance Could go either way 0.7500 to 0.7510 0.7498 to 0.7506 &minus;0.0006 +0.0012 Transition Pressed together 0.7500 to 0.7510 0.7515 to 0.7525 &minus;0.0025 &minus;0.0005 Interference"},
{"title":"Two good parts can still make a bad pair","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#two-good-parts-can-still-make-a-bad-pair","text":"Two good parts can still make a bad pair Checking each diameter against its own limits does not by itself check the clearance of the selected pair. Take the transition drawing from the lab: holes from 0.7500 to 0.7510 in, shafts from 0.7498 to 0.7506 in. Hole made at 0.7502 Inside 0.7500 to 0.7510. Passes. Shaft made at 0.7505 Inside 0.7498 to 0.7506. Passes. Together 0.7502 &minus; 0.7505 = &minus;0.0003 in. The shaft is bigger than the hole. They overlap. Neither part is wrong. The drawing allows that pair, and that is what transition fit allows. To exclude dimensional overlap for every permitted pair, the tightest pair must have zero clearance or more. To guarantee a positive dimensional gap, it must be greater than zero. Neither check by itself guarantees assembly: shape, surface finish, temperature and the assembly method also matter."},
{"title":"Call the fit from the drawing alone","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#call-the-fit-from-the-drawing-alone","text":"Call the fit from the drawing alone Read the two callouts, work out the tightest and loosest pairs, and call it before you look. Five drawings, each on a &Oslash;0.7500 in nominal. Work out the limits, then the tightest and loosest pairs. The answer follows each one. Hole +0.0012&thinsp;/&thinsp;0.0000, shaft &minus;0.0004&thinsp;/&thinsp;&minus;0.0011. Clearance: +0.0004 to +0.0023. Hole +0.0008&thinsp;/&thinsp;0.0000, shaft +0.0010&thinsp;/&thinsp;+0.0003. Transition: &minus;0.0010 to +0.0005. Hole +0.0006&thinsp;/&thinsp;0.0000, shaft +0.0018&thinsp;/&thinsp;+0.0012. Interference: &minus;0.0018 to &minus;0.0006. Hole +0.0010&thinsp;/&thinsp;0.0000, shaft 0.0000&thinsp;/&thinsp;&minus;0.0007. Clearance, zero at the limit: 0.0000 to +0.0017. Hole +0.0015&thinsp;/&thinsp;+0.0005, shaft +0.0007&thinsp;/&thinsp;&minus;0.0003. Transition: &minus;0.0002 to +0.0018."},
{"title":"What this lab leaves out","page":"Tolerances and Fits","summary":"The permitted size ranges on a hole and a shaft decide whether the two have clearance, interference, or could have either.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tolerances-and-fits.html#what-this-lab-leaves-out","text":"What this lab leaves out The lab compares two diameters and nothing else. A real assembly also depends on: Shape. No hole is perfectly round or straight, and no shaft is either. A shaft can measure in tolerance everywhere a micrometer touches it and still be bent too far to go in. Temperature. Parts grow when they warm up, so the same part can measure differently straight off the machine and an hour later. Surface finish and measurement. A rough surface and an imperfect measurement both blur the last decimal place the drawing asks for. Force. Whether an interference pair can be pressed together, how hard, and what that does to the parts are questions about the material, not about the two sizes. The limits in the lab are made-up teaching values. Real drawings often take limits from published tables of standard fits; those tables are not reproduced here. For measuring a real part, start with choosing a measuring tool, reading its scale, and checking the measurement method. See Shop Layout and Angle Measurement for transferring dimensions onto the work."},
{"title":"Experiment: how much does a shorter tool help?","page":"Tool Stickout and Deflection","summary":"A longer tool reaches farther, but bends much more under the same sideways load. Predict a change, test it, and compare the tool with a fixed baseline.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tool-deflection.html#experiment-how-much-does-a-shorter-tool-help","text":"Experiment: how much does a shorter tool help? Predict first: keep the same solid tool, force and material. If its unsupported length drops from 80 to 40 mm, will it bend half as much, one eighth as much, or the same amount? IDEAL SOLID ROUND TOOL Bending ×100 in both BASELINE 57.947 µm 100 N L = 80 mm Ø 10 mm E 600 GPa YOUR SETUP 7.243 µm 100 N L = 40 mm Ø 10 mm E 600 GPa Halving the clamp-to-load length, from 80 to 40 mm, cuts ideal tip bending from 57.947 to 7.243 µm: one eighth as much. Both drawings use the same geometry scale and ×100 bend magnification. Dashed outlines are the unloaded tools. WORKPIECE Dashed: intended wall Solid: shifted cutting edge 7.243 µm extra material One-sided offset at the tip · gap ×100 · schematic In this ideal one-sided wall pass, the cutting edge bends away from the work. The short setup leaves 7.243 µm of extra material at the tip. This is a geometric consequence of the assumed load, not a prediction of a real finished part. The live sliders need JavaScript. The drawings and worked experiment above show the same model without it."},
{"title":"The length that matters starts at the clamp","page":"Tool Stickout and Deflection","summary":"A longer tool reaches farther, but bends much more under the same sideways load. Predict a change, test it, and compare the tool with a fixed baseline.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tool-deflection.html#the-length-that-matters-starts-at-the-clamp","text":"The length that matters starts at the clamp Stickout is unsupported length. Here it is the distance from the rigid clamp face to the point where the sideways force acts. It is not the total tool length, and it is not automatically the flute length. A force bends the tool and moves its cutting edge away from its unloaded position. The clamp end stays in place and keeps its direction; the bend grows along the unsupported length. In a real cut, force acts over the engaged cutting edges rather than at one perfect point. Reach is a tradeoff. The tool must reach the feature and clear the work and holder. Within those constraints, reducing unnecessary stickout improves bending stiffness. A larger diameter can also help if the feature has room for it."},
{"title":"Length is cubed; diameter is raised to the fourth power","page":"Tool Stickout and Deflection","summary":"A longer tool reaches farther, but bends much more under the same sideways load. Predict a change, test it, and compare the tool with a fixed baseline.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tool-deflection.html#length-is-cubed-diameter-is-raised-to-the-fourth-power","text":"Length is cubed; diameter is raised to the fourth power For the ideal solid round tool in this lab, tip bending is proportional to F × L³ ÷ (E × d⁴). Change one input at a time to see which factor is responsible. ½ length → ⅛ bend Half × half × half. Shortening the unsupported reach is a powerful change. 2× diameter → ¹⁄₁₆ bend 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 goes into the denominator. This comparison is for solid round sections. 2× force → 2× bend Force acts linearly. Double E instead, with the other inputs fixed, and bending halves. Try a combined change: start at L = 40 mm and d = 10 mm, save that baseline, then set L = 80 mm and d = 20 mm. The length contributes 8× and the diameter contributes 1/16×, leaving half the bending at the same load and modulus. The two changes compete. The calculation and its units The solid round section has area moment of inertia I = πd⁴/64. With x measured from the clamp, displacement magnitude is y(x) = Fx²(3L − x)/(6EI). At the tip, x = L, so δ = FL³/(3EI). Both displacement and slope are zero at x = 0. Use N for force, mm for length and diameter, and N/mm² for E. The controls show E in GPa: multiply by 1,000 to get N/mm². The answer is in mm; multiply by 1,000 for µm. For F = 100 N, L = 40 mm, d = 10 mm and E = 600 GPa, I = 490.874 mm⁴ and δ = 0.007243 mm = 7.243 µm. Halfway along that tool, y(L/2) is 5/16 of the tip value, or 2.264 µm. These are calculated examples, not measured cutter data."},
{"title":"Use the trend; know what this model leaves out","page":"Tool Stickout and Deflection","summary":"A longer tool reaches farther, but bends much more under the same sideways load. Predict a change, test it, and compare the tool with a fixed baseline.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/tool-deflection.html#use-the-trend-know-what-this-model-leaves-out","text":"Use the trend; know what this model leaves out This is a static, linear-elastic, slender-beam model: a uniform solid round tool, a perfectly rigid clamp, a steady sideways point load at the free end and deflections small compared with the length. Elastic modulus E measures stiffness, not hardness or breaking strength. The lab starts at E = 600 GPa, a chosen teaching value within Hyperion's published 430–690 GPa range for cemented-carbide wear grades. It is not a universal value for carbide cutters. The force is an assumed input, not calculated from a milling operation. Flutes and necks change stiffness. The holder, spindle and workpiece can move too, and a milling force changes as teeth engage. Static bending alone does not predict chatter thresholds, surface roughness, fracture, safe loads or guaranteed finished-part error. The lab flags L/d below 4 and bending above 1% of L as prompts to question this simplification. Those are teaching checks, not certification or safety limits. Primary references MIT OpenCourseWare: review of cantilever beam theory, lecture 2 appendix, equations 10–11 on PDF page 5. Derives the end-loaded shape and tip displacement with fixed-clamp boundary conditions. Hyperion Materials & Technologies: Carbide vs. Steel. Its wear-grade comparison reports the modulus range; it does not specify the stiffness of a particular fluted cutter. Connect the setup to feeds, speeds and removal rate, and explore the path and tool access in Milling Operations."},
{"title":"The lips cut; the flutes give chips a way out","page":"Twist Drills and Drill Sizes","summary":"Identify the parts that cut and guide a twist drill, compare point shapes, and read the four common drill size systems.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/twist-drills.html#the-lips-cut-the-flutes-give-chips-a-way-out","text":"The lips cut; the flutes give chips a way out A twist drill makes a round hole with cutting edges at its point. The lips run from the center region toward the outside diameter. The helical grooves are flutes: they provide space for chips and a route for cutting fluid. The web is the central core between the flutes. The chisel edge crosses the center of a conventional point. It pushes material aside with substantial thrust. Narrow strips called margins run along the outside of the drill body and help guide it in the hole. The relieved body behind them reduces rubbing. The shank is the portion held by the machine. Schematic side and end views identify the lips, chisel edge, web, flutes, margins and shank; a side-view comparison shows 118 and 135 degree included point angles. The point does the cutting. The body supports it and provides room for chips. Views are schematic, not sharpening templates."},
{"title":"Point angle and split point describe different features","page":"Twist Drills and Drill Sizes","summary":"Identify the parts that cut and guide a twist drill, compare point shapes, and read the four common drill size systems.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/twist-drills.html#point-angle-and-split-point-describe-different-features","text":"Point angle and split point describe different features 118° and 135° are included point angles, measured across the point in an axial view. A 135° point is flatter and shorter than a 118° point of the same diameter. A 118° point is common on general-purpose drills; 135° points are common on drills intended for tougher materials. Select the complete tool for the material and setup, not by angle alone. A split point has extra grinding near the center to reduce the chisel edge and improve starting behavior. “135°” does not automatically mean “split point.” A split point is also not a guarantee that the hole will be located accurately without a suitable setup. Why a damaged or uneven point makes a poor hole Unequal lip lengths or unequal angles make the two lips remove different amounts of material. The drill can cut oversize or wander. A rounded edge can rub instead of producing a chip. Inspect the point and replace or correctly sharpen a damaged drill before trying to compensate with more force."},
{"title":"Read the diameter system before selecting the drill","page":"Twist Drills and Drill Sizes","summary":"Identify the parts that cut and guide a twist drill, compare point shapes, and read the four common drill size systems.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/twist-drills.html#read-the-diameter-system-before-selecting-the-drill","text":"Read the diameter system before selecting the drill System What the marking means Fractional inch A diameter in fractions of an inch, such as 1/4 in. Convert the fraction when a calculation needs a decimal. Number A size label, such as No. 7. In the common numbered series, a larger number means a smaller drill. The number itself is not a diameter. Letter A size label from A toward Z, increasing in diameter. Look up the actual diameter; letters are not equal increments. Metric A diameter in millimeters, such as 5.0 mm. It is not interchangeable with a nearby inch size just because both fit in the chuck. Read the marking and confirm the cutting diameter when identification is uncertain. A drill index is storage, not proof that someone returned the drill to the correct slot. Use the specified diameter and a checked chart when selecting a tap drill."},
{"title":"Match the shank to the holder and the hole to the job","page":"Twist Drills and Drill Sizes","summary":"Identify the parts that cut and guide a twist drill, compare point shapes, and read the four common drill size systems.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/twist-drills.html#match-the-shank-to-the-holder-and-the-hole-to-the-job","text":"Match the shank to the holder and the hole to the job A straight shank fits an appropriate chuck or collet. A reduced shank is smaller than the cutting diameter; it does not increase the machine's drilling capacity. A taper shank fits its matching taper socket or approved adapter, rather than being gripped as though it were a straight shank. Choose enough flute length and reach for the hole, with as little unnecessary projection as the setup permits. A drill's nominal diameter does not guarantee a finished hole's size, location or surface finish. Plan the drilling sequence, then choose a finishing operation if the drawing calls for it."},
{"title":"Ultrasonic impact machining removes material with abrasive particles","page":"Ultrasonic Machining","summary":"A vibrating tool drives abrasive particles against hard, brittle work without requiring electrical conductivity.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/ultrasonic-machining.html#ultrasonic-impact-machining-removes-material-with-abrasive-particles","text":"Ultrasonic impact machining removes material with abrasive particles A shaped tool vibrates rapidly with a small stroke, driving abrasive particles in a liquid slurry against the work. The removal energy at the work is mechanical, even though electricity powers the vibration system. Consider this process for holes or shaped cavities in hard, brittle materials such as glass and ceramics. The work need not conduct electricity. It is generally less effective for soft, ductile materials that deform instead of breaking away under particle impacts."},
{"title":"No thermal HAZ does not mean a perfect surface","page":"Ultrasonic Machining","summary":"A vibrating tool drives abrasive particles against hard, brittle work without requiring electrical conductivity.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/ultrasonic-machining.html#no-thermal-haz-does-not-mean-a-perfect-surface","text":"No thermal HAZ does not mean a perfect surface Ultrasonic impact machining normally avoids a thermal heat-affected zone. Abrasive size, tool wear, slurry access and feature depth affect dimensional control and finish. Fine abrasive can improve the surface, but removal may be slow; edge chipping and subsurface damage remain possible in brittle work. Distinguish this process from ultrasonic-assisted cutting, where vibration is added to another cutting or grinding process. The two names do not imply identical tooling or removal mechanisms. Why a shaped tool does not give an exact-size copy Abrasive particles occupy space around the tool and also wear it. The finished cavity therefore depends on the tool, abrasive action and process control, not simply on copying the tool's nominal dimensions. Bullen describes loose-abrasive ultrasonic machining. Compare Electrical Discharge Machining and Selecting a Material-Removal Process. Safety references: Machine Hazards and Housekeeping and Chemicals and Safety Data Sheets."},
{"title":"A vertical band follows a contour through the work","page":"Vertical Band Machining","summary":"Plan contour cuts around blade width and turning radius, and recognize the purpose of blade welding, band filing, and polishing attachments.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-band-machining.html#a-vertical-band-follows-a-contour-through-the-work","text":"A vertical band follows a contour through the work A vertical bandsaw supports the work on a table while a continuous band cuts through it. Moving the work along a marked path allows curved outlines as well as straight cuts. This can remove excess material before milling a profile. Leave the planned finishing allowance on the retained side of the line, including room for the kerf. Use Shop Layout and Angle Measurement for marking the path and Choosing Shop Measuring Tools for checking the result. Tooth pitch and blade material are covered in Saw Blade Selection; speed and coolant in Cutoff Sawing."},
{"title":"The tightest curve sets the blade-width limit","page":"Vertical Band Machining","summary":"Plan contour cuts around blade width and turning radius, and recognize the purpose of blade welding, band filing, and polishing attachments.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-band-machining.html#the-tightest-curve-sets-the-blade-width-limit","text":"The tightest curve sets the blade-width limit A narrow blade can turn a tighter curve; a wide blade better resists wandering in a straight cut. Find the smallest radius on the intended saw path, then use the blade maker's width-versus-minimum-radius chart. Choose a width that fits both that path and the machine. Radius is measured from the center of a curve to its edge; diameter is twice the radius. A chart's minimum radius is not a guarantee for every blade with that width: blade thickness and kerf clearance matter too. Do not force a wide blade around a tighter corner than its chart permits. Why relief cuts help with some outlines A relief cut runs in from a scrap edge toward the outline, allowing waste to separate in smaller pieces. Plan relief cuts on the waste side and preserve the finishing allowance. They can provide room to approach a contour from another direction; they do not make the blade itself capable of a smaller turning radius."},
{"title":"Blade welding closes the loop for some internal cuts","page":"Vertical Band Machining","summary":"Plan contour cuts around blade width and turning radius, and recognize the purpose of blade welding, band filing, and polishing attachments.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-band-machining.html#blade-welding-closes-the-loop-for-some-internal-cuts","text":"Blade welding closes the loop for some internal cuts Blades may arrive as ready-welded loops. On equipment designed for it, a suitable open band can pass through a starting hole in the work and then be joined, making an internal contour accessible without a slot from the outside edge. In general, blade joining involves preparing and aligning the ends, making a butt weld, heat-treating the joint as specified, finishing the weld flush, and inspecting it. The joint must flex around the wheels and pass through the guides. Follow the welder and blade maker's instructions for the exact sequence and settings; not every blade construction is suitable for a shop blade welder. Why joining includes more than making a weld Alignment keeps the band running straight. The prescribed annealing or tempering cycle restores suitable flexibility at the joint, and finishing removes raised weld material. A sound-looking joint alone is not proof that the loop is ready to run. DoALL's blade welder range is a starting point for equipment-specific documentation."},
{"title":"Filing and polishing require their own bands and guides","page":"Vertical Band Machining","summary":"Plan contour cuts around blade width and turning radius, and recognize the purpose of blade welding, band filing, and polishing attachments.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-band-machining.html#filing-and-polishing-require-their-own-bands-and-guides","text":"Filing and polishing require their own bands and guides Some band machines accept a file band to remove small amounts of material and refine a contour, or an abrasive band to smooth a surface. These are dedicated attachments with matching guides and operating instructions, not ordinary saw blades used differently. Confirm that the machine supports the attachment before selecting its band, speed, or lubricant. Hand finishing methods are in Bench Work with Hand Tools. For operating hazards and precautions, use Machine Hazards and Housekeeping, Personal Protective Equipment, and Metal Chips and Swarf."},
{"title":"The cutter rotates; feed changes its position relative to the work","page":"Vertical Mill Anatomy and Motion","summary":"Identify what moves on a knee mill or bed mill, and distinguish spindle rotation, quill travel, head tram and part coordinates.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-mill-anatomy.html#the-cutter-rotates-feed-changes-its-position-relative-to-the-work","text":"The cutter rotates; feed changes its position relative to the work A vertical mill has a spindle whose axis is vertical in its normal setup. The spindle rotates the tool. The head carries the spindle and its drive; the column supports and guides the machine assemblies. The table carries the workholding. Do not picture the workpiece as fixed in space. Depending on the machine, the table, head, or both move to feed the rotating cutter through the work. On a typical three-axis vertical mill, X is left–right, Y is front–back, and Z is along the vertical spindle axis. A CNC position describes the tool relative to the work. A table moving left can therefore produce tool motion to the right relative to the part. Confirm the machine's jog directions and readout before locating a feature. Continue with Milling Operations for what those movements cut."},
{"title":"A knee raises the table; a bed mill supports it without a rising knee","page":"Vertical Mill Anatomy and Motion","summary":"Identify what moves on a knee mill or bed mill, and distinguish spindle rotation, quill travel, head tram and part coordinates.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-mill-anatomy.html#a-knee-raises-the-table-a-bed-mill-supports-it-without-a-rising-knee","text":"A knee raises the table; a bed mill supports it without a rising knee Assembly Typical knee mill Typical bed mill Table Travels along X on the saddle. Supported by the bed and slides; often travels along X. Saddle Travels along Y on the knee. May provide Y travel beneath the table; layouts vary. Vertical positioning The knee raises or lowers the saddle and table. The head commonly travels vertically on the column. Quill A sliding sleeve gives the spindle a shorter axial feed, useful for drilling and fine depth adjustment. May be absent; the head's Z motion provides the feed. Identify the actual moving assemblies. “Bed mill” does not mean that every machine assigns Y travel to the head. CNC describes how motion is controlled, not whether the machine has a knee. The setup checklist follows a Tormach workflow. Use the manual for the installed machine to identify controls and travel; a familiar brand name does not establish its spindle interface, options or limits."},
{"title":"Head tram and vise squareness check two different alignments","page":"Vertical Mill Anatomy and Motion","summary":"Identify what moves on a knee mill or bed mill, and distinguish spindle rotation, quill travel, head tram and part coordinates.","collection":"Machining","url":"machining/vertical-mill-anatomy.html#head-tram-and-vise-squareness-check-two-different-alignments","text":"Head tram and vise squareness check two different alignments Tram is the spindle axis's perpendicular alignment to the table in both left–right and front–back directions. An indicator carried around the spindle axis compares points on the table at the same sweep radius. This is an alignment check with spindle power off; head adjustment belongs to the machine-specific procedure. Squaring the vise makes its fixed jaw parallel to an axis of table travel. It uses a straight sweep along the jaw, not a circular sweep over the table. A square vise does not prove the head is in tram, and a trammed head does not prove the vise is square. See Milling Vises and Strap Clamps. Why facing can reveal a tram problem A tilted spindle puts opposite sides of a face cutter at different heights. Adjacent passes may leave steps even with the vise correctly aligned. Surface finish alone does not identify the cause: check the geometry before changing cutting data. MIT: Introduction to the Mill illustrates the distinction between head tramming and vise alignment on a knee mill."},
{"title":"Why 5S sits in a safety module","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#why-5s-sits-in-a-safety-module","text":"Why 5S sits in a safety module 5S is a lean manufacturing method for organizing a workplace. It earns its place here because most of what it asks for is also what keeps people from getting hurt. A cluttered bench hides a hazard. An unlabeled container is an unknown chemical. A floor with chips on it is a slip. Every one of those is a 5S failure before it is an incident - which is why the shutdown steps in After operating any equipment are 5S in everything but name. This page is the basics. 5S will get its own expanded treatment later."},
{"title":"Sort - Seiri","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#sort-seiri","text":"Sort - Seiri Separate necessary items from unnecessary ones. Remove or relocate items that do not belong in the work area. Keep only what you need for the task in front of you."},
{"title":"Set in Order - Seiton","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#set-in-order-seiton","text":"Set in Order - Seiton Organize and label the remaining items clearly, for easy identification. A place for everything, and everything in its place. Create logical, efficient storage systems."},
{"title":"Shine - Seiso","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#shine-seiso","text":"Shine - Seiso Clean work areas to maintain high hygiene standards. Reduce accident risk through cleanliness and organization. Inspect while cleaning - that is how problems get found early. This is the step that overlaps most directly with safety - see the environment around the machine."},
{"title":"Standardize - Seiketsu","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#standardize-seiketsu","text":"Standardize - Seiketsu Establish consistent procedures for maintaining the first three S's. Create habits through regular practice and routine. Ensure everyone follows the same organizational systems."},
{"title":"Sustain - Shitsuke","page":"5S Workplace Organization","summary":"The five-step workplace organization method. It is a lean manufacturing practice, and it is also why a tidy shop is a safer one.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/5s-workplace-organization.html#sustain-shitsuke","text":"Sustain - Shitsuke Continuously review and maintain all 5S practices. Build the discipline to follow established procedures long-term. Regular audits, and improvements to the system itself."},
{"title":"Use TIMWOODS to name what you observe","page":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","summary":"Recognize eight patterns of wasted effort in manufacturing and daily life.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/eight-wastes-timwoods.html#use-timwoods-to-name-what-you-observe","text":"Use TIMWOODS to name what you observe Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Over-processing, Defects and Skills form TIMWOODS. Describe the observed problem before choosing a remedy. Eight-cell TIMWOODS grid: Transportation, Inventory, Motion, Waiting, Overproduction, Over-processing, Defects and Skills."},
{"title":"Compare shop examples with everyday examples","page":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","summary":"Recognize eight patterns of wasted effort in manufacturing and daily life.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/eight-wastes-timwoods.html#compare-shop-examples-with-everyday-examples","text":"Compare shop examples with everyday examples These are illustrative examples, not descriptions of a particular shop. Transportation: unnecessary material movement Shop: A part travels to a distant holding area and back. Daily life: Moving stored boxes between rooms without using them. Inventory: excess material or work Shop: Unneeded blanks fill a rack. Daily life: Buying food that expires before use. Motion: unnecessary movement by people Shop: An operator searches several drawers for a tool. Daily life: Searching for misplaced keys. Waiting: idle time before work can proceed Shop: A prepared job waits for missing information. Daily life: A completed form sits awaiting approval. Overproduction: too much or too early Shop: Extra parts are made without demand. Daily life: Printing copies nobody requested. Over-processing: work beyond requirements Shop: A hidden face receives an unnecessary cosmetic finish. Daily life: Entering the same information into duplicate lists. Defects: work that fails requirements Shop: An incorrect hole pattern requires a replacement part. Daily life: Retyping a form because of incorrect entries. Skills: unused talent Shop: An experienced operator's improvement idea is never considered. Daily life: Ignoring a team member's relevant skill when dividing work."},
{"title":"Distinguish similar wastes before proposing a change","page":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","summary":"Recognize eight patterns of wasted effort in manufacturing and daily life.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/eight-wastes-timwoods.html#distinguish-similar-wastes-before-proposing-a-change","text":"Distinguish similar wastes before proposing a change Transportation follows material; motion follows people. Overproduction creates unwanted output; inventory is the resulting excess stock. Several wastes can occur together. Inspection is not automatically a defect, and every machine cycle is not automatically wasted time. Check Lean Value and Flow. Buffers may be needed for supply variation; removing them without understanding demand and reliability can interrupt delivery. Use 5S Workplace Organization to address searching and Kaizen and Standard Work to test a change. Improve the system rather than blaming the person caught in it. Practice waste classification"},
{"title":"Improve a specific problem with the people doing the work","page":"Kaizen and Standard Work","summary":"Use small measured experiments to improve work and preserve what the team learns.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/kaizen-and-standard-work.html#improve-a-specific-problem-with-the-people-doing-the-work","text":"Improve a specific problem with the people doing the work Kaizen means continuous improvement. Start with an observed problem and a small change whose effect can be checked. Invite the people performing the task to explain what makes it difficult. For example, repeated searching for a shared measuring tool may suggest a labeled location. Measure search time and check whether the location also works for the next user. This is an illustrative experiment, not a promised productivity gain."},
{"title":"Use Plan–Do–Check–Act to learn from a change","page":"Kaizen and Standard Work","summary":"Use small measured experiments to improve work and preserve what the team learns.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/kaizen-and-standard-work.html#use-plan-do-check-act-to-learn-from-a-change","text":"Use Plan–Do–Check–Act to learn from a change Plan: define the problem, measure the starting condition and predict the effect of a proposed change. Do: try an authorized change on a manageable scale and record what happens. Check: compare results with the prediction, including quality and unintended effects. Act: adopt and document an improvement, revise an uncertain idea or undo an unsuccessful change; begin the next cycle. Plan leads to Do, then Check, then Act, with a return arrow to Plan for the next improvement cycle. A useful result may be that the idea did not help. Keep that evidence rather than declaring success because something changed."},
{"title":"Use standard work as a baseline that can improve","page":"Kaizen and Standard Work","summary":"Use small measured experiments to improve work and preserve what the team learns.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/kaizen-and-standard-work.html#use-standard-work-as-a-baseline-that-can-improve","text":"Use standard work as a baseline that can improve Standard work records the current agreed method so people can repeat it and recognize departures. State the sequence, relevant timing, checks and materials needed. In repetitive production, standardized work also relates the sequence and required in-process stock to the pace of customer demand. When an improvement is demonstrated, update the method and help everyone learn it. Do not bypass safety requirements or change controlled specifications as an informal experiment. Record the problem and starting measure. Describe one change and the expected result. Test within applicable authorization and safety requirements. Compare results and effects on other people or steps. Update the agreed method if the evidence supports the change. Use 5S Workplace Organization for workplace organization and Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS to name problems."},
{"title":"Start with what the customer needs","page":"Lean Value and Flow","summary":"Define customer value and improve the whole path from request to delivery.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/lean-value-and-flow.html#start-with-what-the-customer-needs","text":"Start with what the customer needs Lean thinking improves how work creates value while reducing waste and respecting the people doing it. Value is judged from the customer's need: the right function and quality, in the needed quantity, at the needed time. A busy machine is not proof of useful output. Making something early that nobody needs can increase storage and delay the work that is needed."},
{"title":"Separate value creation from necessary support and avoidable waste","page":"Lean Value and Flow","summary":"Define customer value and improve the whole path from request to delivery.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/lean-value-and-flow.html#separate-value-creation-from-necessary-support-and-avoidable-waste","text":"Separate value creation from necessary support and avoidable waste Value-added work changes the product or service toward the customer's requirement and is done correctly. Cutting a needed feature may add value; searching for a misplaced tool does not. Some non-value-added work is currently necessary: required checks, safety measures and records protect customers and workers. Improve how that work is done; do not delete a safeguard because it does not physically change the product. Process time is not automatically value-added time Rework takes processing time but corrects a failure. Inspection does not itself create the desired feature. A necessary machine cycle can transform the product even while the operator waits. Name whose time and which activity you are classifying."},
{"title":"Follow the work across handoffs","page":"Lean Value and Flow","summary":"Define customer value and improve the whole path from request to delivery.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/lean-value-and-flow.html#follow-the-work-across-handoffs","text":"Follow the work across handoffs Improving one station can build a queue at the next. Look at the whole path with Value Stream Mapping. Use Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS to describe problems and Kaizen and Standard Work to test improvements with the people doing the work. 5S Workplace Organization supports visible, repeatable work. It is one supporting method, not the whole of lean."},
{"title":"Map one product family from request to delivery","page":"Value Stream Mapping","summary":"Show material and information flow, then distinguish process time from elapsed lead time.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/value-stream-mapping.html#map-one-product-family-from-request-to-delivery","text":"Map one product family from request to delivery A value stream map (VSM) shows the steps and information that move a product or service toward the customer. Define the boundary and select work following a similar route. Walk the current process and record what happens, including queues and handoffs. Show who requests work, how instructions reach production, where material moves and where it waits. An operations-only map misses the information controlling those operations."},
{"title":"Read process boxes, inventory triangles and flow arrows","page":"Value Stream Mapping","summary":"Show material and information flow, then distinguish process time from elapsed lead time.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/value-stream-mapping.html#read-process-boxes-inventory-triangles-and-flow-arrows","text":"Read process boxes, inventory triangles and flow arrows Simplified map: customer order goes to planning, which instructs cutting. Material passes through cutting, inventory and assembly to the customer. Cutting takes 4 minutes, waiting 120 minutes and assembly 6 minutes. This original sketch uses common conventions: a rectangle for a process, a triangle marked I for inventory, attached data boxes for measurements and arrows for flow. The legend distinguishes material movement from information. This is a teaching sketch, not a complete symbol standard."},
{"title":"Measure elapsed lead time separately from process time","page":"Value Stream Mapping","summary":"Show material and information flow, then distinguish process time from elapsed lead time.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/value-stream-mapping.html#measure-elapsed-lead-time-separately-from-process-time","text":"Measure elapsed lead time separately from process time Lead time is elapsed time through the stated boundary, including waits. Process time is time actively spent doing the work within that boundary. Not all processing adds customer value. For one item in this simplified sequential example: cutting = 4 min, queue = 120 min, assembly = 6 min. Process time is 10 min; lead time is 130 min. No other delays or overlapping steps are included. Removing half the queue saves more elapsed time here than cutting one minute of processing. A queue suggests a question, not a proven cause A queue may reflect a capacity constraint, batching, missing information or an unreliable next step. Observe demand, timing and variation before calling one operation the bottleneck. A working day and a calendar day are not interchangeable time units. Practice lead time"},
{"title":"Draw a future state and test a specific change","page":"Value Stream Mapping","summary":"Show material and information flow, then distinguish process time from elapsed lead time.","collection":"Lean and Quality","url":"lean/value-stream-mapping.html#draw-a-future-state-and-test-a-specific-change","text":"Draw a future state and test a specific change A future-state map describes an improved flow to work toward. Choose a measurable change, assign responsibility and check the effect using Kaizen and Standard Work. Compare delivery, quality and workload across the whole stream."},
{"title":"Explore The Pace of AI Progress","page":"The Pace of AI Progress","summary":"How far has AI come, how would you know, and what can two points tell you about next year?","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-progress.html#explore-the-pace-of-ai-progress","text":"Explore The Pace of AI Progress Five sourced benchmark series to estimate and reveal, four benchmark families with the reasons each generation was retired, fourteen real achievements ranked by acceptance status and the AI's role, and three forecast rules that fit the same two points. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"The Pace of AI Progress","summary":"How far has AI come, how would you know, and what can two points tell you about next year?","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-progress.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path Five sourced benchmark series to estimate and reveal, four benchmark families with the reasons each generation was retired, fourteen real achievements ranked by acceptance status and the AI's role, and three forecast rules that fit the same two points."},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"The Pace of AI Progress","summary":"How far has AI come, how would you know, and what can two points tell you about next year?","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-progress.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide Demo 3 of 17 - Part two - 22&ndash;28 min full, 10 min cut The Pace of AI Progress - presenter guide One HTML file, offline, no AI at runtime. You need a screen to project or share and a mouse or touchscreen for the drawing in stage 1. Nothing else. Learning goals What the activity itself demonstrably shows, one per stage: On the tests the field cites most, the change since 2023 is measured in multiples, not points - and every series stops where its published record stops, conditions attached. A benchmark is retired when it stops measuring what it was meant to; being beaten is one of four routes, and the replacement often counts something new - so a rising line across generations is not one trend. An achievement is an event with an acceptance status and a stated AI role; read the rung before the headline - every rung from Nobel Prize to retracted has a real 2023&ndash;2026 occupant. A gap in published history can be estimated; a future cannot - three rules fit the same two points, and only the next measurement chooses between them. Where this sits on the path Demo 2 showed a language model being measured - a score is a measurement, not a property of a mind. This demo hands that forward to published measurements: read one with its conditions, and do not extend it past where it stops. Demo 4 takes the question this one raises - progress this fast invites \"is it a bubble?\" - and answers it with history rather than a script. One question for the session How far has AI actually come, and how would you know? The honest answer is large and specific: multiples, on named tests, under stated conditions, with the boundary on the same slide. The one sentence to land: the change is bigger than most rooms guess, and a number is only a result when it comes with its conditions. Both halves, in that order. The three facts worth a projector Ninety seconds each, no benchmark literacy needed. Fact The move Why this one GDPval - 1,320 real work deliverables, 44 occupations, graded blind by professionals from those occupations 12.3% &rarr; 70.9% in ~13 months Nurses, lawyers, accountants, engineers, journalists rated the machine's deliverable as good as or better than their own about seven times in ten. Vendor-built - say so - with the scores republished by Epoch AI. METR time horizon - the length of task, in human working time, a model finishes half the time 3.5 min &rarr; 320 min in 32 months The unit needs no explaining. An independent non-profit, same methodology both ends, and intervals nowhere near overlapping: 6.9 min upper bound then, 170 min lower bound now. Pair it with the doubling time - ~7 months over the whole history, 88.6 days since 2024. FrontierMath Tier 4 - 43 problems Epoch says take a researcher days 0% &rarr; 97.6% in 20 months And on FrontierMath-Erdős - 68 genuinely unsolved problems, released the same month - the same model gets 2 of 68. Pace and boundary in one image, and it pre-empts \"the tests just got easier\": the administrator built a harder one and the model fell off it. Fourth, if a room wants money: ARC-AGI-1 at 87.5% cost about $4,560 per task in December 2024; today 98.5% costs 28 cents, and ARC-AGI-2 - the harder successor - reaches 95% at $1.12. A harder test, a higher score, roughly four thousand times less money. The shape of the session Measure 10 min (two series drawn with the room, three offered) - Retire 6 (one family opened properly) - Achieve 6 (top rung, then bottom) - Forecast 4 (drag to year 8, then the boundary card). The ten-minute cut: stage 1 with the METR series only, stage 3 filtered to Nobel Prize and to contested, then the last paragraph of stage 4 - magnitude, acceptance and the anti-extrapolation point, without the benchmark-family detail. Stage 1 - Measure Question. How much did the most-cited AI measurements move, and could you have guessed the size of it? Predict prompt. \"GPT-4 managed tasks that take a person three and a half minutes. Where is that by late 2025 - ten minutes? An hour?\" Take numbers out loud, then draw the room's median, not your own. Activity. Do the GDPval series yourself so the room sees the mechanic, then hand over the METR series and take a show of hands before Reveal. Drag across the shaded region, or focus the chart and use Up/Down. Choose a series jumps between the five. Expected observation. Almost every room draws below the published line, on every series. Name that as a fact about the measurements, not a failing of the room - and do not let it become \"draw higher next time\", the error stage 4 exists to kill. The reveal's what would have broken this line list is the payload: a successor released inside the gap, a harness change, a problem set corrected in 42% of its items. Second half. Ask what the hatched gap between the last dot and the snapshot date means: nothing was measured there, by this page. Check-yourself answer key. \"Your line was below the published result. What does that show?\" - That the results moved faster than the room guessed, on these tests under these conditions. Not that the room can forecast by drawing further. Teaching limit. Five series are a teaching selection, not a survey. The ratio at reveal compares an endpoint with one selected report; it does not grade anyone's reasoning. Stage 2 - Retire Question. Every widely cited test gets replaced. Were they all simply beaten? Predict prompt. \"SWE-bench Verified was the standard AI coding benchmark, and it has been retired. What was the top score when its own co-author stopped reporting it?\" Let the room guess high: 76.80%, and the reason is not the score. Activity. Open Software engineering first; it breaks the tidy story. Read OpenAI's own words off the card: 59.4% of a hard 27.6% subset had flawed tests, and every frontier model could reproduce the human bug fix from memory. Then ARC-AGI, for the generation that changed the unit. Expected observation. Four routes, and only one is \"beaten\". Rooms assume contamination is rare; the Terminal-Bench leaderboard carries a canary string begging not to be trained on, which lands it better than any argument. Check-yourself answer key. \"Terminal-Bench went 2.0 &rarr; 3.0 &rarr; 4.0. What does the higher number tell you?\" - Only that the old version stopped measuring what it was meant to: 4.0 has 66 tasks to 2.1's 89, and one model scores higher on 4.0. Teaching limit. Four families, not the field. The status and route chips are labelled Reasoned because they are this page's reading of the quotation beside them - a reader is invited to disagree, which is why the quotation is shown. Stage 3 - Achieve Question. Which of these happened the way the headline says, and how would you tell? Predict prompt. \"The news said AI solved a Millennium Prize Problem. On a ladder from Nobel Prize down to retracted, where does that sit?\" Take answers before filtering. Activity. Filter to Nobel Prize - one card, and it took years. Then Announced, contested - one card, the biggest headline on the page; read its Who checked it line aloud, in full. Then Retracted: the same organisation nine months earlier. Expected observation. The rung ordering inverts the news ordering: the loudest claims sit at the bottom, and the one with a prize is two years old and not a language model. Say this about Navier&ndash;Stokes, precisely. An AI system produced a proof, checked in Lean, that closed the last gap in a strategy two human mathematicians had built - for one of the four statements Clay accepts as resolving the problem. Clay's page still reads \"Active\"; it is not peer-reviewed; Clay's rules need publication plus two years plus general acceptance, so no prize was possible before 2028 whatever anyone thinks of the proof; OpenAI states it does not intend to claim it; the priority dispute is unresolved; and statement (C) leaves (A), the unforced case everyone means, open. That long version is not hedging - it is a better story than the headline, and it survives the one person in the room who knows the field. If you shorten it, shorten it to: a real, checkable advance on one of the four statements Clay accepts, with AI closing a specific gap inside a human proof strategy. Never to \"AI solved a Millennium Prize Problem\". Check-yourself answer key. \"Which rung does the September 2026 Navier&ndash;Stokes result sit on?\" - Announced, contested. Teaching limit. Fourteen cases, weighted toward mathematics because that is where the strongest and the most contested recent claims are. The ladder ranks how thoroughly a claim has been checked, not how important it is. Stage 4 - Forecast Question. What can two measurements tell you about next year? Predict prompt. \"A score went 20 to 30 in a year. Eight years on, how far apart can three rules that fit those points be - a few points, or a factor of ten?\" Activity. Drag to year 8: the compound rule leaves the scale entirely, the saturation rule is still under 75. Then read the boundary card - five sentences, deliberately short. Expected observation. A room that has just watched five series move by multiples wants to extrapolate; this is the stage that refuses. Say the reconciliation out loud: stage 1 filled a gap in published history, where an answer existed. A forecast has no answer yet, and two points do not choose the shape. Check-yourself answer key. \"Which rule fits the two points?\" - All three, and by year 8 they disagree by a factor of ten. Teaching limit. The scenario is invented, ceiling included. It shows that two points underdetermine a curve; it says nothing about which curve AI is on. Misconceptions \"The benchmarks just got easier, so the numbers are meaningless.\" The opposite, mostly: ARC-AGI-2 was built to resist scale and went 6.5% to 95% in seventeen months, and on a harder mathematics set released this month the same model gets 2 of 68. Read the task count, not the version number (stages 1&ndash;2). \"A benchmark gets replaced because it was beaten.\" One of four. SWE-bench Verified was retired at 76.80% for flawed tests and contamination; Terminal-Bench 2.1 was an environment fix; multilingual variants exist for coverage (stage 2). \"A score is a property of the model.\" One model on one benchmark spans 18 points across harnesses, and 45 on ARC-AGI-3 at a fixed effort setting. Effort is not even monotone: 35.2% at \"none\" beats 17.5% at \"low\" (stages 1&ndash;2). \"If AI won a Nobel and solved a Millennium problem, it can do the work.\" The Nobel was for AlphaFold, not a chatbot, after years of checking; the Navier&ndash;Stokes result is unrecognised and contested. Meanwhile the one randomised trial of experienced developers measured them 19% slower, and the best score on real paid freelance projects is 16.1% (stages 3&ndash;4). \"Saturation means progress is slowing.\" Saturation is a property of bounded scores. METR's horizon has no ceiling, has forced no successor, and its doubling time is shortening (stages 1&ndash;2). Discussion questions Pick one series. What would you need to know to say whether it is still rising in two years - and could you have known it in February 2025? A vendor shows one number for its model on a benchmark. What five things do you now ask for before believing it? GDPval says professionals rate the machine's work as good as or better seven times in ten; the developer trial says experienced developers were 19% slower. Both careful. What question would distinguish them for your own work? Questions you will get \"So did AI solve a Millennium Prize Problem or not?\" Give the precise version in stage 3 and do not flinch from its length. Short form: a real, checkable advance on one of Clay's four statements, with AI closing a gap inside a human strategy; not peer-reviewed; not recognised; no prize; disputed on priority. Pushed for yes or no: \"not as the news said it, and more than a sceptic would expect.\" \"Aren't these numbers from the companies selling it?\" Some are, and the page labels them. GDPval is OpenAI's - but the graders are independent professionals judging blind, and Epoch AI republished the scores. METR is a non-profit with no product; the ARC Prize and Terminal-Bench boards are third-party and publish cost beside score. FrontierMath is the sharpest case: OpenAI funded it and holds exclusive access to part of it, and Epoch says so on its own page, and Epoch's own run reproduces OpenAI's 97.6% exactly. \"Why does that one card have two different numbers for GPQA?\" Because OpenAI published two. GPT-4o's GPQA Diamond pass@1 is 50.6% in the o1 post's table; the May 2024 launch chart, labelled plain \"GPQA\", shows 53.6% for the same model. Two runs, two pages, one vendor. Showing both rather than picking one is the whole lesson in a row. \"Where did you get this?\" Open Settings &rarr; Run data integrity checks in front of them. It re-derives live that every reference resolves to a source dated to the snapshot, that every point carries its conditions, that every series says where it stops, and that every label word is from its closed vocabulary. Then open Details on any stage: each lists its sources with the fetch date and how the page was read - fetched, rendered in a browser, or downloaded as a dataset. \"Is this current?\" Every source was opened on the snapshot date printed on the chart, and each series is drawn only as far as its published record goes - that is what the hatched stretch means. Re-check the sources each semester: a stale page here would teach the opposite of its own lesson. Presenter controls Settings &rarr; Presentation mode for projector-sized type; it also enlarges the chart and puts a Notes button in the header. Run the data integrity checks before the room arrives, so you know they are green. Between rooms, Settings &rarr; Reset restarts the demo without reloading - drawn lines cleared, first series, stage 1, filters and scenario back to their first state - and keeps Presentation mode as set. Nothing is stored, so an ordinary reload resets it too. No network is needed at any point. Top right:? is the Guide (the on-load how-to, not this document); &#9881; is Settings, holding this document, Presentation mode, Reset and the checks. The Pace of AI Progress - Bryant Harrison, Murray State University. Every source opened on the snapshot date shown on the chart; every figure on screen links to its origin, and the Details drawer says how each page was read. Claims this page deliberately does not make are listed in stage 4."},
{"title":"Explore AI Tool Guide","page":"AI Tool Guide","summary":"Four ways into the same models — and why reach and review burden rise together","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-tool-guide.html#explore-ai-tool-guide","text":"Explore AI Tool Guide The audience thinks “using AI” means a chat box. It is one surface out of four, and the least capable. A chat window, the agent already inside Docs or Word, a desktop coworker, a coding agent — set against 4 axes: what it can see, what it can do, whether it keeps going unwatched, and how much you must check. Then 7 real faculty jobs, and for each one the smallest door that finishes it and exactly what you hand over to get there. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"AI Tool Guide","summary":"Four ways into the same models — and why reach and review burden rise together","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-tool-guide.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path The audience thinks “using AI” means a chat box. It is one surface out of four, and the least capable. A chat window, the agent already inside Docs or Word, a desktop coworker, a coding agent — set against 4 axes: what it can see, what it can do, whether it keeps going unwatched, and how much you must check. Then 7 real faculty jobs, and for each one the smallest door that finishes it and exactly what you hand over to get there."},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"AI Tool Guide","summary":"Four ways into the same models — and why reach and review burden rise together","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-tool-guide.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide AI: From Zero to Takeoff - Demo 6 of 17 - Part three - about 12 minutes AI Tool Guide - notes for the presenter What to ask it with. Runs after The Stranger, which covered how to phrase the request. About 12 minutes, or 4 if you are behind. You need: the built page open, and nothing else — no network, no login, no account. Product claims checked 16 September 2026. Learning goals The goal for each stage, in plain words — what a learner should be able to say after doing the activity, not before it. These are the three stage Takeaways, verbatim. Stage 1, The four doors. Reach rises neatly because the four doors were chosen as a ladder — but review burden peaks at the desktop coworker (door 3), not the coding agent (door 4), because doors 2 and 4 hand you a change you can compare and take back — an edit inside a document with undo, a diff you can read — and door 3 hands you a finished thing. Stage 2, Pick a job. The smallest sufficient door changes by job — access is a cost you take on, not a feature to default to maximizing. Stage 3, The receipts. Two of four doors changed names in six months, and this page's own top uncertainty checked out unchanged when read directly — the doors are the stable unit here, not the names on them. Where this sits on the path Demo 5 (The Stranger) taught the room that how you phrase a request changes what a language model gives back. The obvious next conclusion — and the wrong one — is that prompting is the whole skill. This module is the correction: a perfectly phrased request to a browser tab still cannot rename a file on your laptop. Phrasing is one variable; the surface is the other. Demo 7 onward (Part four) hands forward a related discipline — a useful classroom tool starts from a learning goal, exposes its assumptions, and makes its evidence available for challenge — which is also the discipline this page holds itself to, out loud, in Stage 3. One question for the session The surface you use decides what the tool can reach - and the further it reaches, the more of its work you have to check. Everything on the page serves that sentence. If you only get one thing across, get that, and specifically the second half, because the first half is intuitive and the second is not. Stage 1 - The four doors 4 min Question. If the model behind the glass is often the same, what actually changes as you move from a chat window to a coding agent? Predict prompt. Rows 1&ndash;3 (Sees, Does, Keeps going) were built to rise neatly door 1 to door 4 — which one row will not? Activity, timed. Put the grid up and say nothing for a beat (30s). Let the room read the four questions down the left, then take the on-screen prediction (30s). Open door 1, row 2 (60s) — a chat window answers in the conversation. Then open door 2, row 4 (60s) — accept or reject, you get a diff; this is the cell that surprises people, because it is the least glamorous door and has the best review story in the table. Open Row 4's note last (60s). Expected observation. Rows 1–3 step up cleanly door by door; row 4 does not — door 3 (desktop coworker) reads higher than door 4 (coding agent). Check-yourself answer key. \"Which door is hardest to check?\" — Door 3, the desktop coworker; the coding agent's diff is what makes it *easier* to check than a door with less reach, which is the point of the refutation option. Teaching limit. The staircase in rows 1–3 is a property of which four doors were chosen, not a law of nature — say so if anyone calls it suspiciously tidy. \"Same model behind all four of these. What changes is how far it can reach - and every step of reach you hand it, you get back as review.\" Stage 2 - Pick a job 6 min Question. For one real faculty task, which door is the smallest one that finishes the whole thing? Predict prompt. Before the reveal, guess which door finishes the chosen job. Activity, timed. Do not walk all 7 jobs. Pick two: \"Rename four hundred scanned files\" (90s) — the clearest floor in the module, two doors say wrong door outright, and it makes the point that better prompting cannot buy reach; then \"Reformat a syllabus into the department's template\" (90s) — the answer is the boring door, the agent already inside Docs, the most useful answer of the day for most of the room. Read the last line of each card out loud: \"You hand over.\" That line is the whole argument. Expected observation. The prediction echo shows a guess that sometimes matches the computed smallest-sufficient-door and sometimes does not — both outcomes are useful; a mismatch is the more interesting one to talk through. Check-yourself answer key. \"Why is renaming four hundred files 'wrong door' at doors 1 and 2?\" — neither can touch the file system at all; the refutation option (lower answer quality) names the misconception that the gap is intelligence rather than reach. Teaching limit. The green box is computed, not written: the smallest-numbered door whose verdict is \"does the whole job.\" Say \"smallest\" deliberately — the module is not asking what the most capable tool is. Misconception: \"the biggest door is the safest choice.\" Bigger reach means more of the work you must check yourself, not less risk — the smallest sufficient door is the one that finishes the job with the least you have to verify afterward. Before you present: check door 2 actually works on your own account. Murray State runs Google Workspace, which is why this module leads door 2 with Docs and Sheets rather than Word and Excel — but running Workspace is not the same as having Gemini switched on in it. On a campus account that is licensed and enabled by an administrator; Google's Docs page (opened 16 September 2026) says the feature needs \"an eligible Google Workspace or Google AI plan,\" so a personal Google AI plan is the other route. Open a Doc and look for the pane the morning of the talk. If it is not there, say so from the stage and point at door 1. \"Notice what I did not ask. Not which is best. Which is smallest and still finishes the job.\" Stage 3 - The receipts 2 min Question. How much of a page about AI products should you expect to still be true in six months? Predict prompt. Guess how many of the four doors had a product renamed or retired in six months (0–4). Activity, timed. Show two entries under \"What already changed\" (20s each): \"Agent Mode\" → \"Edit with Copilot\", with Microsoft's own sentence under it — same capability, new name, inside six months; and \"ChatGPT agent\" → retired — OpenAI's help page still explains how to use the thing it opens by telling you is gone. Then show one entry under \"What was rechecked and held\" (20s): the page's own pre-build audit could not confirm whether OpenAI's Codex is still unselectable on web and mobile (automated fetch got HTTP 403); opened directly in a browser instead, and it is still true, quoted from the vendor's current FAQ. Expected observation. The predicted count is compared against the actual count (2 real vendor changes; the third CHANGED entry is the module's own drafting mistake, not a vendor change) — most rooms guess low. Check-yourself answer key. \"What does a 403 to an automated fetch mean?\" — the tool was blocked, not that the claim is false; the refutation option (guess instead) names exactly the failure mode this page's research-not-memory discipline exists to prevent. Teaching limit. A rechecked claim is confirmed as of the date shown, not permanently — that is why the page names a refresh cadence rather than declaring itself finished. If the room is engaged, the third CHANGED entry is worth ten seconds: the brief this module was built from called a product \"GPT Work,\" a name on none of the OpenAI pages the module opened; the product is ChatGPT Work. Somebody who does this for a living got it wrong six weeks out of date. That is the argument for the date at the top of the page, and it is more persuasive as a confession than as advice. \"Good - that is the module working. Which door was it? Because the door is still there.\" Misconceptions \"The biggest door is always the safest or best choice.\" Reach and review burden rise together; the smallest sufficient door is the one that finishes the job while asking you to hand over the least. \"The coding agent, having the most reach, must be the hardest to check.\" Instrumentation decides review cost, not reach: the coding agent hands you a diff and a test suite, while the desktop coworker hands you a finished artefact with nothing to compare it to — which is what makes door 3, not door 4, the hardest door in the table to check. \"An automated tool returning an error means the underlying claim is unverifiable.\" A 403 means the script was blocked; it says nothing about whether the page itself still says what it always said. This page's own top-ranked risk turned out to be exactly that gap, not a wrong claim. Discussion questions Pick a task you actually did this week with AI. Which door did you use, and was it the smallest one that would have finished it? Door 3 (desktop coworker) is the hardest door to check in this table. What would have to change about how it presents its work for that to stop being true? This page predicts what will rot and what will hold (Stage 3). Six months from now, which prediction do you expect to be wrong, and why? Presenter controls The header buttons.? is the Guide — the how-to panel that opens on first load and reopens from that button. &#9881; is Settings, and it offers Open Presenter Notes (this document, on screen), Presentation mode, Reset, and the self-test. Turn on &#9881; &rarr; Presentation mode for projector-sized type. Run the self-test once so you know it is green before anyone asks. Re-read Stage 3 the morning of. This is the one demo in the collection with a real shelf life. If a name on screen has changed, say so from the stage - it is a better moment than a correct page would have been. &#9881; &rarr; Reset puts the demo back to how it opened — stage 1, no job picked, no predictions made, no self-test on screen — in place, without a page reload, and without turning Presentation mode off. No network is needed at any point. The source links need one, but only if somebody asks you to follow one, and you can promise to send it instead. Questions you will get \"So which one should I buy?\" The module deliberately does not answer this and neither should you. The honest reply is: almost certainly none, because your institution already licenses one or two of these and that decides it. Send them to IT. \"Can it see my students' work?\" Only if you give it access, and whether you may give it access is a question for your registrar and your IT department rather than for a vendor page. Two of the jobs in Stage 2 carry that caution on screen for exactly this moment; open one and read it rather than improvising a policy answer. \"How do you know any of this?\" Open &#9881; &rarr; Run the self-test in front of them. It checks that every claim resolves to a declared source, that no source is cited and never used, that no price and no vendor ranking leaked into anything the page can display, and that the review row genuinely does not rise the way the other three do. Then tap any cell and follow the link. What the numbers on the page mean Figure What it is 16 cells 4 doors &times; 4 axes. Every one opens a reason, a label and a source. 13 verified Cells where a page belonging to the company that makes the product was opened on the check date and says it. 3 reasoned The ordering judgements. Labelled, because no vendor states them. 28 judgements 7 jobs &times; 4 doors in Stage 2. All reasoned from Stage 1, and each names the axis that decided it. 19 sources All first-party, each carrying the date it was last opened. Two of the OpenAI pages refuse automated retrieval and were read in an ordinary browser. 9 things cut Researched during the build and deliberately left off, each with the reason. AI Tool Guide (Front Doors) - Bryant Harrison, Murray State University. Product claims checked 16 September 2026 against first-party sources, opened in an ordinary browser where automated retrieval is blocked. Every claim on screen carries a confidence label and links to its origin; everything researched and left off is listed in Stage 3 under \"Researched, and deliberately not on the page.\""},
{"title":"Explore AI Winters: Boom and Bust","page":"AI Winters: Boom and Bust","summary":"Date ten AI claims, separate forecasts from assessments, and compare two contractions without forcing a cycle","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-winters.html#explore-ai-winters-boom-and-bust","text":"Explore AI Winters: Boom and Bust A curated selection of ten sourced claims: five time-bounded forecasts to score; four dated assessments or untimed ambitions to classify without grading against the future; and one warning that was testable and correct. A scrubbable timeline then compares claims, institutional bets, constraints, reassessment, and withdrawal across two historically different contractions. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"AI Winters: Boom and Bust","summary":"Date ten AI claims, separate forecasts from assessments, and compare two contractions without forcing a cycle","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-winters.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path A curated selection of ten sourced claims: five time-bounded forecasts to score; four dated assessments or untimed ambitions to classify without grading against the future; and one warning that was testable and correct. A scrubbable timeline then compares claims, institutional bets, constraints, reassessment, and withdrawal across two historically different contractions."},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"AI Winters: Boom and Bust","summary":"Date ten AI claims, separate forecasts from assessments, and compare two contractions without forcing a cycle","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/ai-winters.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide Demo 4 of 17 - Part two - 14&ndash;19 min full, 6 min cut AI Winters: Boom and Bust - presenter guide One HTML file, offline, no AI at runtime. You need: a screen to project or share; no other materials. Learning goals Not stated objectives - what the activity itself demonstrably shows, one per stage: A forecast needs an outcome and a deadline; an assessment needs evidence about its own moment - grading one as the other makes history look more certain than it is. The five lanes organize evidence for comparison; they do not prove that every AI contraction follows one fixed order. An open cell is evidence only that a limit or a withdrawal has not (yet) been established - not a prediction that one is coming. A historical rhyme is a reason to ask a question, not a reason to expect the same ending - each claim about today needs its own evidence. Where this sits on the path Demo 3 leaves the room reading a benchmark result under its own stated conditions rather than extrapolating from two points. Fast progress like that is exactly what raises the question this demo answers: is this a bubble? History answers with evidence, not a script - and this demo hands forward the habit of classifying a claim, forecast or assessment, before judging it. One question for the session Has this happened before? AI has contracted twice, through different combinations of technical limits, institutional choices and markets. The histories rhyme; neither is a script for the present. The one sentence to land: a prediction needs a deadline and an outcome; an assessment needs evidence about its own moment. Do not grade a historical claim until you know which kind it is. The shape of the session Stage What happens Time 1 - Claims Five time-bounded forecasts, four assessments or untimed ambitions, and one warning, with the speaker and date hidden. Score forecasts; classify the unscored context. 7&ndash;9 min 2 - History Scrub the timeline. Five lenses compare historical pathways without forcing a sequence. 3&ndash;4 min 3 - Compare Compare the two winters and the present under the same lenses. Two current questions stay open. 2&ndash;3 min 4 - Today Rhymes against differences. Close on the checklist. 2&ndash;3 min Stage 1 - Claims Question. Is this a forecast you can grade, or an assessment you can only judge by its own moment? Predict prompt. Guess the year, then say whether the claim is a scoreable forecast or an assessment to classify - before Lock it in reveals anything. Activity. Do the first two cards yourself, out loud, so the room sees the mechanic. Then hand it over - ask for a show of hands on the year before you drag the slider, and a verbal verdict or classification before you press Lock it in. For the four context cards, choose Assessment or untimed ambition when the claim supplies no scoreable future window. The room being wrong together is the experience; a silent room reading a screen is not. Expected observation. Three of the five time-bounded promises missed outright, one arrived thirty years late, and the deliberately unscored ones (the 1958 press claim, ALPAC, Perceptrons, Lighthill, and Amodei 2024) pull the room toward wanting to grade them anyway - that pull is the misconception to name. Check-yourself answer key. \"Should an open forecast be marked wrong?\" - No: an unresolved forecast stays unscored rather than counted as a failure. Teaching limit. This is a curated ten-claim deck, not a representative accuracy sample of optimists, pessimists, or the field. The deck, and what each card is for Year Card Type What it is for 1950 Turing - fifty years to the imitation game open forecast Its about-2000 bar was missed; the current status remains contested. Use it to separate a past deadline from an unresolved present judgment. 1958 \"walk, talk, see, write, reproduce itself\" untimed ambition An expansive press claim without a deadline. Classify it; do not pretend it supplies a scoreable outcome window. 1957 Simon & Newell - chess champion in ten years promise The most useful card in the deck. Right destination, wrong by a factor of four. Pause here. 1960 Simon - any work a man can do, in twenty years promise Twenty years lands in 1980, the bottom of the first winter. Also: it is usually misdated to 1965. 1966 ALPAC - machine translation is not near assessment Distinguish a near-term economic assessment from a forecast. The report also recommended continued computational-linguistics research. 1967 Minsky - substantially solved within a generation promise A reported quotation attributed to his textbook. Use it to explain why the app labels this source route reported. 1969 Perceptrons - neural nets read as a dead end assessment The proofs were right; the history of how the field received them is disputed. 1973 Lighthill - no major impact so far assessment A retrospective judgment on impact and constraints, not a permanent forecast. 1984 Schank & Minsky - a winter is coming warning The card that saves the demo from being anti-hype. They were right, three years early. 2024 Amodei - \"could come as early as 2026\" open Deliberately unscored. The year named is the year you are standing in. Short of time? Keep four: Simon & Newell 1957 (over-promise, right destination), ALPAC 1966 (assessment versus forecast), Schank & Minsky 1984 (a prediction that was right), and Amodei 2024 (still open). Skip to Stage 3 and close. Stage 2 - History Question. Do the two AI winters share one fixed cause-and-effect sequence, or different pathways that happen to rhyme? Predict prompt. Scrubbing from 1950, does an institutional bet or a named constraint fire first in each winter? Activity. Drag the slider slowly, or step with &lsquo; and ' to stop and read one event. Tap any dot for its full text and source. Use the rows as comparison lenses - claims, institutional bets, constraints, reassessment, withdrawal - and ask which causes matter in each episode and which do not carry across. Read the current entries as evidence about today, not a third historical run. Expected observation. The order is not identical between the two winters: the first winter's reassessment (ALPAC, then Lighthill) came from commissioned reports years apart, while the second winter's reassessment was the market itself - the LISP hardware collapse, with no equivalent report. Two histories, not one template. Check-yourself answer key. \"Does a named bet before a withdrawal in both winters prove a rule?\" - No: two episodes sharing an order is not a proven causal sequence. Teaching limit. No continuous, sourceable AI funding series exists across these years; four discrete funding examples with different currencies and accounting bases are shown instead of one invented total. Do not let the lenses turn into a prophecy. The correct line is: \"these comparisons tell us what to investigate. They do not establish that a withdrawal will occur.\" Stage 3 - Compare Question. Which of the five comparison lenses still shows a real similarity once you separate description from prediction? Predict prompt. On the \"Constraints\" lens, is \"Today\" an established limit, or an open question? Activity. Choose one of the five lens buttons; its navy fill marks the selection. Compare the three era headings, then open each era's Details for the full explanation and named sources. Escape closes the panel and returns focus to its button. Compare causes, not step numbers. Expected observation. Two cells in the \"Today\" column stay open - no single binding technical constraint is established, and no comparable broad contraction is established either - while the claims, money and reassessment lenses do resolve to sourced present-day evidence. Check-yourself answer key. \"Does an open cell mean a winter is currently impossible?\" - No: open means not yet established either way, not ruled out. Teaching limit. Calling a present-day cell \"open\" is this page's own reading of the evidence available at build time, not a citation that says \"unresolved.\" \"Hindsight makes old constraints easier to see. It does not prove that today has the same constraint or that it must end the same way. Ask what evidence would support the claim.\" Stage 4 - Today Question. Which rhyme still holds once you weigh it against a genuine difference? Predict prompt. Pick one rhyme and one difference - does the rhyme survive once you weigh the difference against it? Activity. Expand a similarity and a difference, giving both columns roughly equal time; the supporting statements start closed so the room investigates one claim at a time. Close on the checklist, not a verdict: is it a forecast or an assessment, what evidence supports it, and what evidence would move it. Expected observation. The strongest rhyme (an evaluation warning about SWE-bench Verified) and the strongest difference (measured scaling behaviour and reported consumer reach) do not cancel each other - each still needs its own evidence, which is the point. Check-yourself answer key. \"Does a bigger user base rule out a contraction?\" - No: user count answers a different question than whether funding, evaluation trust, or returns hold up. Teaching limit. Historical comparison is not a prediction; this curated set of near-term forecasts that missed does not establish a representative error rate for optimists, pessimists, or AI research generally. Misconceptions \"An unresolved forecast should count as a failure.\" No. Only a forecast whose window has closed one way or the other gets a verdict; an open claim stays unscored (Stage 1). \"The same order appearing in both winters proves a fixed sequence.\" No. Two historical cases sharing a pattern is a reason to look closer, not a general law about every contraction (Stage 2). \"An open cell in the 'Today' column means a winter cannot happen.\" No. Open means the evidence has not established a limit or withdrawal yet - not that one is ruled out (Stage 3). \"More users than any earlier boom means this one avoids a contraction.\" No. Scale of adoption is a genuine, sourced difference, but it answers a different question than whether funding, evaluation trust, or returns hold up (Stage 4). Discussion questions If someone states a confident AI timeline today, what would you need to see before you could score it - and by when could you score it? The first winter's reassessment came from a commissioned report; the second came from a market collapse with no report at all. Does the absence of a report today mean nothing is wrong, or only that the evidence would look different if something were? Pick one \"Today\" claim from Stage 4. What single piece of evidence would move your confidence in it the most, in either direction? Questions you will get \"So is it a bubble or not?\" Refuse the binary, in one sentence, then give the useful answer: current funding, reported usage, technical reliability, and returns are distinct questions. Earlier eras also had deployed systems and organisational users, though current consumer reach is much larger. A financial correction and a technological dead end are different claims and need different evidence. \"Aren't the winter dates made up?\" Partly, and the demo says so. The bands are conventions for broader contractions, not dates stamped by a single event. The 1987 hardware collapse is a useful second-winter anchor; the first has more distributed causes and withdrawals, including but not limited to ALPAC. The ends are also conventional and historians disagree; that is why the bands have soft edges and explanatory notes. \"Where did you get this?\" Open Settings &rarr; Run the self-test in front of them. It re-derives, live, that every claim resolves to a declared source, that no source is cited and never used, and that the counts quoted on screen match the data. Then open Stage 4, which lists every source under Every source and, separately, everything researched and cut for lack of one. Both supporting sections open on request. \"Why is that quote from a quotation-checking website?\" Because the Simon and Minsky lines are routed through a quotation-checking source that cites printed books and page references. This build has not inspected those original pages, so it labels the claims reported rather than primary. That distinction is part of the lesson: a plausible citation route is not the same as direct verification. Presenter controls Turn on Settings &rarr; Presentation mode for projector-sized type. Run the self-test once so you know it is green before anyone asks. Between rooms, Settings &rarr; Reset puts the demo back to its start without reloading the page - answers cleared, Stage 1, timeline rewound, opened cards closed - and keeps Presentation mode on so the projector does not change under you. The app saves nothing anywhere, so an ordinary reload resets it too. No network is needed at any point. If the room's wifi is down, nothing changes. The buttons, top right? - Guide: the short how-to panel that opens on first load, reopenable at any point. (Not this document.) &#9881; - Settings: Open Presenter Notes (this document, on screen), Presentation mode, Reset, and Run the self-test. AI Winters: Boom and Bust - Bryant Harrison, Murray State University. Sources checked September 2026. Every figure on screen links to its origin; everything that could not be sourced is listed in Stage 4 under \"What was cut, and why\"."},
{"title":"Explore How a Language Model Works","page":"How a Language Model Works","summary":"Train a tiny language model, test worked solutions, and explore a tool-using loop.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/how-language-models-work.html#explore-how-a-language-model-works","text":"Explore How a Language Model Works Train a tiny transformer and inspect its predictions. Compare direct answers with worked arithmetic, then explore an agent loop with real tools and a clearly labeled scripted policy. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"How a Language Model Works","summary":"Train a tiny language model, test worked solutions, and explore a tool-using loop.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/how-language-models-work.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path Train a tiny transformer and inspect its predictions. Compare direct answers with worked arithmetic, then explore an agent loop with real tools and a clearly labeled scripted policy."},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"How a Language Model Works","summary":"Train a tiny language model, test worked solutions, and explore a tool-using loop.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/how-language-models-work.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide Murray State University How a Language Model Works Demo 2 of 17 · Part one · 20 minutes You need: one browser and index.html. It runs offline. For projection use Settings → Presentation mode. Start fresh: the untrained model is useful evidence. Learning goals What a learner knows at the end of each stage — these are the three Takeaway cards, verbatim. LLM. Training changes the parameters; inference runs the frozen parameters forward to score every possible next token. A small model trained on a little text learns the shape of the language long before it learns to say anything true. Reasoning. Room to write intermediate steps lets the same machinery reach answers it cannot produce in one jump — a real, measurable gain on this task, and not the same thing as understanding arithmetic. Agents. Tools and a loop turn prediction into action, and the context window bounds what the loop can still see — a run can fail for want of memory, not for want of ability. Where this sits on the path Demo 1 showed models that play and handed forward one idea, in its own words: a model is a representation that can be trained, and architecture, training method and model type are three separate questions. Its last stage ended on the transformer; this demo trains one. It shows a model that predicts text, then what it takes to reason and to act. It hands forward two things: a model reads its context (Demo 5, Prompting) and a score is a measurement under conditions — the exam rows here — which is how Demo 3 asks you to read published benchmarks. One question for the session What changes when a text predictor is given more structure, more checking, or tools? This page shows three bounded mechanisms: a tiny language model learning next-character prediction, arithmetic experiments with written work and checking, and a tool loop with a scripted action selector. Use Details for the deeper architecture and implementation notes. “Keep asking what the program learned, what it is calculating now, and what part is a teaching fixture.” LLM · Language model — 6 minutes Question How can adjustable numbers turn recent text into a next-character prediction? Predict prompt After all four eras, can it do simple math, like 2 + 2? The answer is no: its vocabulary has no digits, so 2+2= reaches it as blank spaces. Have someone type 2+2= into the prompt box to see it. The reveal says so on the card once Era 4 finishes, and Stage 2 trains the same kind of model on sums. Activity 0:00 Open LLM. Use Tokenize text and Inspect parameters on an untrained prompt. The numbers begin as random values. 1:30 Choose Train the model. Read one untrained sample, then narrate the loss and samples as the first era completes. Use Try next-token prediction to compare likely next characters; change Temperature only after the audience sees the ranked choices. 4:30 Choose Trace attention. Select a character and show which earlier positions receive weight for that prediction. It is a live intermediate value, not evidence that the model has a mind or intent. Expected observation Training changes the weights, loss declines, and the model’s next-character distribution changes. Its samples can become more patterned while still being limited by one short, original story. Check-yourself answer key Era 4 writes more like the story than Era 0. What changed? Its parameters. Not a lookup of stored passages (nothing is stored to retrieve — the whole model is 42,458 numbers) and not a bigger vocabulary (Tokenize text shows the same 26 characters at every era). Teaching limit This is a character-level transformer trained from scratch on a tiny corpus. It demonstrates a transformer learning mechanism, not general language ability or a claim about every frontier system. Reasoning · Worked steps — 8 minutes Question How do written intermediate steps and checking change a small model’s behavior on addition? Predict prompt Same exam, which scores higher — answers-only or worked steps? Take the vote before pressing Run experiment; the card echoes each guess and settles it from the two scores on screen. Activity 6:00 Open Reasoning and choose Train and compare. Compare direct-answer training with worked column arithmetic. The models share core width, depth, and heads, but their contexts, seeds, and update counts differ to fit their different text formats. 8:30 Choose Compare exam answers. Read a live attempt and an exam row. The useful comparison is the presence of written intermediate work, not a universal performance promise. 10:30 Optional slow extension: Try more attempts. Run 1, 2, 4, and 8 attempts with the same frozen under-trained model. The arithmetic checker rejects malformed work and accepted chains vote. More attempts add inference-time computation, not training. Expected observation Written steps provide smaller targets for the model to predict. Sampling plus checking can improve the chosen answer even when weights stay fixed. Check-yourself answer key The worked-steps model scores higher. What does that show? That generating intermediate steps gives the same machinery more room to compute. The card’s two wrong options are wrong in opposite directions and both are common in a room: “it understands arithmetic” (too strong — both are next-token predictors, and the fine-tuning is supervised, not reinforcement learning) and “nothing changed, both just predict the next token” (too weak — the two exam scores on the page are the evidence against it). Give both corrections equal time. Teaching limit The checker validates column arithmetic from the addends. The optional accepted-example round also retains chains matching the generated answer, then uses ordinary next-token updates on those examples. That is rejection-selected supervised fine-tuning, not a policy-gradient reinforcement-learning update. Agents · Tool-using loop — 5 minutes Question What does a system need in addition to text prediction to take actions and use results? Predict prompt Same task with Visible context set to Tiny — does the run still finish? Take the vote before the tiny run; the card settles it when the run ends. Activity 14:00 Open Agents, then choose You choose the actions. The calculator, search, reading, notebook, observations, and distractor consequences all run locally. 16:00 Choose Watch the scripted policy. Point out the calculator’s deliberate bad syntax and its genuine error message, then the later corrected call. Open the raw context to see what the action selector can read. 18:00 Choose Tiny · last 450 characters, Restart run, and Watch the scripted policy again. The goal leaves the visible context and the automatic run stops. Expected observation Tool results become new text in the context. The small window changes what the selector can use, so the same task can fail when its goal is no longer visible. Check-yourself answer key On Tiny the same policy stops without finishing. Why? The goal scrolled out of the visible context. Not a weak policy (it is the same hand-written policy and it finishes on the full context) and not the calculator error (that error is real, deliberate, and recovered from in both runs). Teaching limit The action selector in this activity is a scripted policy, including the calculator mistake and retry; it is not a learned model. The loop, local tools, observations, and context truncation are the genuine mechanisms on display. Optional slow experiments and close Let Train the model and Train and compare continue while you discuss their live displays; each can take a few minutes. Try more attempts and Fine-tune on accepted examples are optional extensions. Do not promise a particular score or curve: the page uses seeded, bounded demonstrations. “A language model predicts. Reasoning experiments add written work, checking, or extra attempts. An agent adds a loop and tools; in this activity, its action choice is deliberately scripted.” Which claim came from a learned model, a fixed calculation, or a scripted policy? Why can extra attempts help without changing weights? What would need to change before this agent activity used a learned action selector? Misconceptions to expect It is just predicting the next token, so it cannot really compute anything. It does only predict the next token — and the worked-steps model still scores measurably higher on the identical exam. Prediction with room to write steps is computation. The two exam scores are the evidence; show them, do not argue it. The worked-steps model understands arithmetic. Same architecture, different training text. It reaches more right answers; nothing on the page supports comprehension, and the fine-tuning is ordinary supervised training on its own accepted examples, not reinforcement learning. Attention shows what the model is thinking. Attention weights are real intermediate values from this computation — a routing pattern for one prediction. They are not intent and not an explanation of why the answer came out as it did. Frontier models are this recipe plus scale. The shape of the computation is the same as a decoder-only transformer. The scale, the data, and much of the training pipeline are not, and scale is not a detail. This page demonstrates selected mechanisms, not a miniature of every production model. The agent got smarter or dumber between the two runs. It is the same scripted policy both times. What changed is what the loop could still see. A run can fail for want of memory, not for want of ability. Presenter controls Guide opens from the header and on entry. Settings contains Open Presenter Notes, Presentation mode, and Reset. Reset puts the whole demonstration back to its fresh state in place, without reloading the page: the transformer is untrained again, every era is gone, the Reasoning models and exam tables are cleared, and the Agents run is back at turn one on the full context. Presentation mode stays as you set it. Open printable guide opens the rendered copy of this same guide. Bryant Harrison · Murray State University · How a Language Model Works · Offline, single-file demonstration · no account or runtime network"},
{"title":"Explore Prompting Strategies","page":"Prompting Strategies","summary":"Why prompts work — three levers, sixty-four hand-written answers, zero network","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/prompting-strategies.html#explore-prompting-strategies","text":"Explore Prompting Strategies A question sits beside three toggleable levers, and the answer changes in exactly one dimension per lever. Sixty-four hand-written answers, each labelled as hand-written on the panel that shows it, a deck that decides your answer’s shape whenever you don’t, and a meter counting the expert vocabulary you got for free. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"Prompting Strategies","summary":"Why prompts work — three levers, sixty-four hand-written answers, zero network","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/prompting-strategies.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path Context. What changes when it knows who is asking? Success spec. If you don't say what finished looks like, who does? Expert keywords. What do you get for asking in the field's words?"},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"Prompting Strategies","summary":"Why prompts work — three levers, sixty-four hand-written answers, zero network","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/prompting-strategies.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide PROMPTING STRATEGIES The Stranger - presenter sheet - 12 minutes Learning goals The three stage Takeaways, verbatim from the app. Each is a plain claim the activity demonstrably shows, and each is what that stage's check-yourself item tests. CONTEXT With no context the answer is aimed at everybody, which is another way of saying nobody. Context changes who it is for - not its length, and not its vocabulary. SUCCESS SPEC An unspecified target does not stay unspecified - something else fills it in, and does not tell you. Saying what finished looks like takes that decision back. EXPERT KEYWORDS Asking in a field's vocabulary gets that vocabulary back - words nobody in the room supplied. That is real. It is also not the same thing as a better answer, and telling those apart is the skill. Where this sits on the path Demo 5 of 17 - Part three. Demo 2 showed that a model reads its context; here the room controls that context, one lever at a time. It hands forward to Demo 6: a perfect prompt in a browser tab still cannot touch your files, so the surface decides reach. One question for the session \"Who decided what a good answer to your question looks like?\" The roleplay - three acts You are the Stranger. A volunteer is the Asker. The app is on the projector behind you; run the same states on it as you say them out loud. ~3 min ACT I - CONTEXT \"I am a stranger on a street corner. I am not stupid. I know a great deal about almost everything. I have never met you.\" 1. The Asker says only: \"Help me get ready for my test.\" Answer out loud, as blandly and generally as you honestly can. Cover every case. Do not clown it - a real assistant is competent here, just aimed at nobody. 2. Ask the room: what would I have needed to know? Take three answers, put them on the board. 3. The Asker reads the context block. Answer again, aimed at that kid. \"I didn't get smarter. You got specific.\" ~4 min ACT II - THE TARGET DECK Hold the eight cards from page two face down. \"Somebody has to decide what a good answer looks like. If you don't decide, I do. And I never tell you what I picked.\" 1. Same question, context on, no success spec. Draw a card and do not show it. Answer to the card - if it says exhaustive, talk for forty-five seconds without stopping. 2. Now show the card. Wait for the room. 3. Draw a different card. Same question, answer again. Same asker, same words, different answer - and the difference was never theirs. 4. The Asker reads the success spec. Put the deck down and slide it across the table, away from you. On the app, the deck lives inside lever 2: flip it on and the three card names get struck out where they sit. Same gesture, same panel. \"You just took the deck out of my hands.\" On the app? is the Guide: what every control does. It opens itself on load. &#9881;&#xFE0E; beside it is Settings - Open Presenter Notes (this sheet, on screen; or press N ), Presentation mode (the projector build: big type, the 1&ndash;10 score strip, compare, and the card picker inside lever 2) and Reset. Reset is a fresh load without the reload: question 1, levers off, no answer on screen, scoreboard cleared. It deliberately leaves Presentation mode where you set it, so resetting between classes never drops you into student mode. ~4 min ACT III - THE FOLDERS Three labelled folders or sheets: BIOLOGY, BRIDGES, WRITING AT WORK. \"I have a folder on nearly everything. The words you use decide which one I open.\" 1. The Asker: \"Why do bridges stay up?\" Open nothing. Answer in plain words: the bottom gets stretched, the top gets squished. All true. All unusable. 2. The Asker adds the keyword block. Open BRIDGES and answer in the real vocabulary - load path, bending moment, neutral axis, section depth. 3. On the app, press H. Count aloud the expert words in the answer that the Asker never typed. \"You didn't know those words. You didn't have to. You only had to point at the folder.\" Say this before you move on - it matters. \"There are no folders. I don't open anything. Your words change which words are likely to come next - that is Demo 2's machine, doing this.\" The folders are a memory aid for the room. Left unsaid, the prop teaches a filing-cabinet model of a language model, which contradicts Demo 2 - this demo's own prerequisite - and a learner who does Demo 2 then Demo 5 is handed two incompatible machines. House rules The Stranger is never stupid. In this demo the bare answers are bland, not broken - we wrote them that way, because the demo dies the moment the bad answer looks rigged. Say it as a rule about the demo, not about models: bare prompts in the wild also produce confident errors, wrong lengths and refusals. No trick answers. If the prompt supports a good answer, give a good answer. Every answer gets a number. 1&ndash;10, from the room, out loud, on the board before the next one. Nobody says \"the AI is bad at this\" without naming which lever is off. The Asker types nothing they wouldn't really type. No fair inventing a great prompt to prove a point. Running order 0:00 Cold open. Act I, live, no screen. 3:00 App, scenario 1, all levers off. Draw the card. Score it. 4:30 One lever at a time. Score each. The curve argues for you. 7:00 Act III on the bridge scenario. Highlighter on, count the words. 9:30 \"Which lever would fix this?\" - two rounds, hands up. 11:00 Closing slide. Read the line. Stop talking. Per stage - question, predict prompt, expected observation, answer key, teaching limit 1 Question: What changes in the answer when it knows who you are? Predict: adding context changes one dimension - which one? (length / who it is aimed at / which words) The room commits before you press Ask. Expected observation: the answer becomes aimed at one asker. Length and vocabulary are not what this lever controls - they follow whichever card is dealt, so they can swing between runs. Answer key: who it is aimed at. The check item's correct option is Context; a room that picks Success spec is told the spec is already doing its job. Teaching limit: a bare prompt is not information-free. \"Help me get ready for my test\" already leaks topic, register and age. Do not claim it knows nothing. 2 Question: If you never say what a good answer looks like, who decides instead? Predict: lever 2 is off, so the target is unspecified - what happens to it? (nothing / something else fills it in / it asks you) Expected observation: a target card is dealt, face up, and the answer is written to it. Turn lever 2 on and the deck is struck out inside the lever. Answer key: something else fills it in. Correct option Success spec. Teaching limit: the deck is a teaching device, not a mechanism. A real model does not draw from three named cards; its defaults come from training, are more stable than a card draw, and vary continuously. Say so - it is in the Details drawer too. 3 Question: What do you actually get for asking in the vocabulary of the field? Predict: keywords off gives at most four expert terms - how many with keywords on? (under five / about ten / fifteen or more) Expected observation: 15&ndash;20 tagged terms, most of them absent from the prompt. Press H and count them aloud. Answer key: fifteen or more. Correct option Expert keywords. The stage also carries the accuracy-brief case (below), whose correct option is the prompt only asked for one kind of finding. Teaching limit: the counter measures vocabulary, not understanding, and the big number is a readout of the author's tag list, not a detector. Ask the room the demo's own question: did the answer get more useful, or just more impressive? Misconceptions this demo can leave - name them, refute them \"I watched the model get better as the prompt got better.\" No model ran. All 64 answers were written by hand before the file was saved, and the panel carries a permanent Scripted label saying so. What the room watched was an author's four answers, chosen to make one lever's effect legible. The claim the demo can honestly make is what a better prompt tends to buy you, never what this model did. \"The rising curve proves more levers make better answers.\" It cannot. The answers were written to improve as levers are pulled, the room scores them, and the mean rises - so the curve measures the room agreeing with the author and could not come out any other way. Keep it: making a room commit out loud is good teaching. Do not offer it as evidence, and say which it is. \"If it is not in the prompt box, the AI does not know it.\" False three ways. What it knows in general came from training and is not in the box; almost every real product prepends a system prompt the user never sees; and history, retrieved documents and tool output enter the context invisibly. The true and useful version: nothing about you and your situation reaches it unless you put it there. \"A bad answer means I left a lever unpulled.\" Sometimes. A lever fixes vague, mis-aimed or wrong-shaped. It does not fix confidently wrong, out of date, refusing, or past what the system can do - and on those, \"write a better prompt\" is the wrong response. The closing line says both halves; do not shorten it to the first. \"A careful prompt cannot mislead.\" The accuracy-brief case on stage 3 is a documented counter-example from building this very collection: a brief asked for 100% accuracy, every claim that came back was true, and the overall picture was still wrong - because the instruction only asked for one direction of finding. Symmetry is the repair, not more care. Discussion questions The first answer was accurate. Why was it useless? Who decided what a good answer looked like, and when? The expert answer used words you never typed. Where did they come from, and how did asking in plain words keep them hidden? Which of the three levers is hardest to pull for a subject you know nothing about? What do you do then? Name a time you blamed a tool for an answer you had underspecified. Success spec means saying what \"done\" looks like. When is that hard to say - and what does that tell you about the task itself? The app is one HTML file and touches no network. Every answer in it was written by hand before the file was saved: there is no model behind the glass. Keys: 1 2 3 levers - Enter ask - Space skip typing - H highlight - R replay card - P pin to compare. The stage tabs switch levers; Details on each stage carries that stage's assumptions, sources and boundary. Settings holds Open Presenter Notes, Presentation mode (projector type plus the class score strip, compare, scoreboard and the card picker) and Reset, which restores the demo in place without reloading the page. The target deck - cut along the dashed lines Eight editorial choices somebody has to make. When the Asker doesn't make them, the machine does, silently. Hold these face down; draw one whenever the success-spec lever is off. ▤ Length: exhaustive Cover everything that could possibly be relevant. Leave nothing out. turns up in: test · email ◐ Audience: someone who missed the whole semester Assume they know nothing. Define every term before using it. turns up in: test ▬ Format: dense paragraphs No headings. No bullets. Flowing prose, start to finish. turns up in: test · essay ▫ Length: short and safe Keep it brief. Don&#x27;t risk being wrong about the details. turns up in: bridge ☰ Format: numbered listicle Everything as a numbered list. Ten items. Equal weight to each. turns up in: bridge · email ◆ Audience: an expert peer Assume deep familiarity. Skip the basics, get to the interesting part. turns up in: bridge · essay ✦ Tone: encouraging coach Lead with affirmation. Soften every critique. Protect motivation. turns up in: essay ◇ Coverage: hedge every claim Present all sides. Avoid committing to one recommendation. turns up in: email"},
{"title":"Explore Types of AI","page":"Types of AI","summary":"Where does an AI's skill come from? Three architectures on one tic-tac-toe board, the methods that train them, and the model types the world sorts by.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/types-of-ai.html#explore-types-of-ai","text":"Explore Types of AI Compare written rules, a learned value table and a neural network on one tic-tac-toe board, then inspect the network's actual weights. Separate three questions people mix up: how a model is built, how it was trained, and what job it does — the last one linked to Hugging Face's own catalogue of model types. The activity runs in your browser without an AI service. Use its Guide button for instructions and Settings for presenter notes, presentation mode and reset. Open it full screen when you want more room."},
{"title":"Learning path","page":"Types of AI","summary":"Where does an AI's skill come from? Three architectures on one tic-tac-toe board, the methods that train them, and the model types the world sorts by.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/types-of-ai.html#learning-path","text":"Learning path Symbolic AI (GOFAI). Eight rules a person wrote. It never learns, and an exhaustive search still finds no losing line. Value table. One score per position, learned from game outcomes. Train it until the search can call it unbeatable. Neural Network. Shared weights fitted to the table's frozen scores, with error measured on positions kept out of training. Other architectures & training methods. The wider map: trees, ensembles, SVMs and the rest, and which of 1a–1c used which training method. Model types. What a model does rather than how it is built — Hugging Face's own by-task catalogue, with a link to real models of each type."},
{"title":"Presenter notes and learning guide","page":"Types of AI","summary":"Where does an AI's skill come from? Three architectures on one tic-tac-toe board, the methods that train them, and the model types the world sorts by.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/types-of-ai.html#presenter-notes-and-learning-guide","text":"Presenter notes and learning guide Murray State University · Presenter guide Types of AI Demo 1 of 17 · Part one · What AI is · 20 minutes You need: one browser and the demo's index.html; it works offline. Start from a fresh page or press Settings &rarr; Reset. Settings &rarr; Presentation mode scales the page for a projector; Open Presenter Notes shows this guide in the app. Learning goals Each stage's Takeaway, as the learner reads it: 1a. This opponent's skill came from a person: changing its rules changes its play, and playing more games changes nothing. 1b. Reinforcement learning stores experience as a separate score for each position; only an exhaustive search over every game line, not a good record, earns the word unbeatable. 1c. Shared weights give the network an answer for a board it never practised on - which a table cannot do - and training on other boards makes that answer better. The error readings say how close the answer is to the teacher's, not how well it plays. 1d. Architecture (how a model is built) and training method (how it learns) are separate choices: the same architecture can be trained more than one way, and the same method can train more than one architecture. Model types. A model type names the job - what goes in and what comes out - not the architecture inside. One neural-network family, the transformer, serves text, vision, audio, video and multimodal types; generating images, audio and video also leans on a different family, diffusion models, which Hugging Face keeps in a library of its own. Where this sits on the path Demo 1 of 17 opens Part one. Nothing comes before it. It hands forward one idea to Demo 2 (How a Language Model Works): a model is a representation that can be trained, and architecture, training method and model type are three separate questions to ask of any AI. One question for the session Where does an AI's skill come from? Three models play the same board and each got its skill a different way. The top lens sorts models two ways: Model architecture (how it is built and trained - 1a to 1d) and Model types (what it does). \"Architecture is how it is built. Training method is how it learns. Type is the job. Keep asking which one someone means.\" 1a · Symbolic AI (GOFAI) - 4 minutes Question Can a program play well without ever learning? Predict prompt Ask the room to press a prediction: with only the First 2 rules on, can you beat it? Take a show of hands before anyone plays. Activity 0:00 Press First 2 rules. Play one game; invite the room to find the fork. The Notice line and the panel name the rule that answered each move. 1:30 Press All 8 rules and play again. Point at the rule that fires each turn. 3:00 Open Details: the proof line reports the exhaustive search over every game playable against the ladder. Expected observation Two rules lose to a fork; eight rules have no losing line. The opponent explains every move by naming a rule, and nothing about it changes however many games are played. Check answer: a person wrote every rule and its priority; playing did not change them. The wrong option (\"it learned the rules by playing\") is refuted by First 2 losing the same fork forever. Teaching limit GOFAI means Good Old-Fashioned Artificial Intelligence. This decision list can be drawn as a tree; here people wrote the tests, whereas decision trees can also be learned. Say \"this small example\", not \"symbolic AI\". 1b · Value table - 5 minutes Question Can experience alone teach a blank table to play? Predict prompt After one burst of 5,000 games, will Era 1 be unbeatable or still beatable? Press a prediction. Activity 0:00 In Era 0 press Show move scores: every square reads 0.00. Play one short game; it moves at random. 1:00 Press Train the AI. Read the change report under the training controls: recorded scores before and after the burst. 2:30 Train again until the Notice line says verified unbeatable (usually burst 3 to 5). Switch the Opponent era back to Era 0 and forward again: the report follows the selection. 4:00 Open Details for the proof line. Expected observation Era 1 is still beatable; the scores moved but a losing line remains. A later era has no losing line in either role. Human play never changes the table; only the burst does. Check answer (\"Does playing the AI above train it?\"): no - here only the training burst updates the table; your games are scored, not learned from. Say the caveat out loud: that is this demo's choice, not a law. The same table could have learned from every game you played, and real assistants can use what people type to train later versions, usually behind a setting. Details cites two vendors' own pages (fetched 2026-09-16). Teaching limit A prediction of \"unbeatable\" is a claim about the table, settled by the exhaustive search, not by whether the room beat it: losing to a beatable era looks exactly like losing to an unbeatable one, and the reveal line says which one the search found. The table has one separate score per position, so it carries nothing to a different situation. Tic-tac-toe is small enough to tabulate; chess and language are not, which is why the next stage shares parameters. 1c · Neural Network - 6 minutes Question Can one set of shared weights score boards it never practised on? Predict prompt After training, will held-out error be about the same as practised error, or clearly higher? Press a prediction; the reveal quotes the measured numbers. Activity 0:00 Press Create learning examples. A fresh table learner plays 20,000 seeded games; its estimates freeze as targets. This is the one slow step. 1:30 In the network view, pick a connection layer and a neuron; read one weight and its initialized value. 2:30 Press Train network. Compare the weight with its initial value, then the practised and held-out errors, then the separate playing score. Show move scores shows predictions for legal moves. 5:00 Optional: press Ultimate tic-tac-toe above the board to show a representation that cannot see the whole game; Standard board brings the network back. Expected observation The network is a feedforward multilayer perceptron (29 inputs, 28 and 18 ReLU units, one output) fitted by supervised learning to targets another model produced. Backpropagation supplies gradients; updates are normalized gradient steps. Shared weights give an answer for a held-out position; the error readings say how close, and neither is playing strength. Check answers: error measures agreement with the frozen table's scores, which are themselves estimates; play is measured separately. 1b stores one number per position; 1c reuses 1,381 shared parameters for every board. Teaching limit Lower error means closer agreement with the teacher, not guaranteed playing strength; no automatic unbeatable claim is made. A position the fresh teacher never visited stays at zero - a limit of the examples, not proof the position is neutral. During play the network calculates its own scores without consulting the frozen table. Activations are responses to a board, not stored weights; a positive weight is not a good move. 1d · Other architectures & training methods - 3 minutes Question What else can a model be, and how else can it learn? Predict prompt None on this stage. 1d is a map, not an experiment; nothing on it runs a result a prediction could be checked against. If you want a show of hands anyway, ask which of 1a, 1b, 1c learned from inputs paired with target answers, then press the Supervised learning card. Activity 0:00 The stage has three parts behind three buttons. Under Training methods, press each card. The card faces do not say which model used them; the press does: supervised &rarr; 1c, reinforcement &rarr; 1b, no training &rarr; 1a; the other three light nothing. Point at the one-line \"used for\" notes: pretraining is self-supervised, fine-tuning and distillation are supervised, RLHF is reinforcement - stages, not extra methods. 1:30 Switch to Other architectures and press a card: the line beneath says what that model stores and what it computes - linear models, decision trees, ensembles, nearest neighbours, support-vector machines, probabilistic models. Search and planning are named separately as problem-solving approaches. 2:15 Switch to Learning with no weight change: in-context learning, retrieval, search at decision time. Tie it to 1b's check: a model can appear to learn in a session without changing. Press Search at decision time: 1a's fork rules look one move ahead every turn; the demo's exhaustive search checks players and never picks a move. Expected observation Architecture and training method are separate columns. 1c is a network trained by supervised learning; a network could equally be trained by reinforcement, and a table could be fitted to supervised targets. Check answer: no - the network is an architecture; supervised learning is how this one was trained. Teaching limit The cards are textbook categories, not a complete catalogue, and real systems combine them. Self-play is not self-supervision: 1b learns from rewards. Gradient training is not evolution. Model types - 3 minutes Question If \"type\" means the job, what goes in and what comes out? Predict prompt None on this stage: it is informational, and nothing on it runs a result a prediction could be checked against. Activity 0:00 Switch the top lens to Model types. Press three cards the room will recognise: text generation, text-to-speech, speech-to-text (automatic speech recognition). Each shows what goes in and comes out, then the catalogue's own definition. 1:30 Press Image-to-3D: the job people call image-to-mesh. Follow Browse models on one card if you are online; the demo itself never fetches. 2:00 Scroll to Beyond one catalogue: eight major types the Hub list does not show - world models (Genie 3), video with sound (Veo 3.1), robot control (Gemini Robotics), protein structure (AlphaFold), weather (GenCast), background removal (NVIDIA Broadcast), character animation, free-viewpoint video - each linked to the model's own page. 2:30 Scroll to the whole catalogue: every type Hugging Face lists, in its groups, with the model count on the fetch date. Expected observation A type is named by its input and output. Text generation and image-to-text are different types that can share one architecture family; tabular types are the visible exception, where the catalogue names tree and boosting libraries. The link back to 1c: a small network scoring boards is the same idea as the networks behind most of these types. Check answer: not necessarily - type is the job; one transformer may generate text and another caption images. Teaching limit The taxonomy and the counts are Hugging Face's, fetched on the date shown on the stage; counts move daily. Other catalogues cut the jobs differently. The stage does not run any of these models. Misconceptions \"A program that plays well must have learned.\" 1a plays unbeatable tic-tac-toe with no learning at all. Skill can be written down by a person; learning is one way to get it, not the definition of AI. \"A long run of draws proves it is unbeatable.\" Sampled games can miss a line. The app calls an era unbeatable only after searching every reachable game line in both roles. Open Details for the counts. \"Playing against the table trains it.\" Not here: human play is scored, not learned from, and every era is a frozen copy. But it is a design choice, not a rule - real systems can and sometimes do train on what users type. \"I lost, so it must be unbeatable.\" Beatable means a losing line exists, not that you found it. The search settles the verdict; your record only says how you played. \"Low error means it plays well.\" Error is agreement with a teacher whose estimates are imperfect. The playing score is a separate, fixed-seed measurement, and this tab never claims unbeatable. \"Neural network is a kind of learning.\" It is an architecture. 1c's network was fitted by supervised learning; the same weights could be trained by reinforcement. Press the method cards in 1d. \"Same architecture, same type.\" Type is the job. Hugging Face files transformers under text generation, image-to-text, speech recognition and more. Ask what goes in and what comes out. Discussion questions Which model stores separate values, and which shares weights? What does each choice make easy and hard? Why does lower held-out error not prove unbeatable play? What measurement would? Name an AI product you use. Which type is it, what might its architecture be, and how do you think it was trained? Which of the three can you actually tell from the outside? The Ultimate extension: what information does the inherited representation leave out, and why can more training not put it back? Presenter controls Guide (header) reopens the learner card. Settings has Open Presenter Notes, Presentation mode and Reset, then a playing preference, a rehearsal seed, the self-test and About. Reset is in place: stage 1a, First 2 rules, move scores hidden, Era 0, records and predictions cleared, type cards closed. Presentation mode and the playing preference stay. Rehearsal seed: type a seed and press Reset; training then unfolds identically, so a rehearsal matches the lesson. Run the full self-test re-runs the exhaustive search on the selected stage and prints it in Settings and the console. Deep links: #stage-1 to #stage-5; the older #rules, #learning, #neural still work. Bryant Harrison · Murray State University · Types of AI · single-file offline demo · no accounts or runtime network requests · the Model types stage links out to huggingface.co, fetched 2026-09-16"},
{"title":"AI: From Zero to Takeoff","page":"Understanding Artificial Intelligence","summary":"Start with a game small enough to inspect completely, then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and choose prompts and tools.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/understanding-artificial-intelligence.html#ai-from-zero-to-takeoff","text":"AI: From Zero to Takeoff Start with a game small enough to inspect completely. Then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and open classroom activities built for specific disciplines. These lessons are by Bryant Harrison, Murray State University. Each interactive activity includes its original Guide, presenter notes and printable guide. Measurements, scripted examples and sourced claims retain their original labels and snapshot dates. Start the learning track Print or save the complete track"},
{"title":"What AI is","page":"Understanding Artificial Intelligence","summary":"Start with a game small enough to inspect completely, then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and choose prompts and tools.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/understanding-artificial-intelligence.html#what-ai-is","text":"What AI is Two demonstrations make the machinery visible. The first compares three ways to choose a move on the same tic-tac-toe board. The second builds from a small language model to reasoning and tool use. Types of AI How a Language Model Works Artificial intelligence A broad label for machines performing tasks that call for judgment, prediction, planning, perception, or language. Machine learning A part of AI in which behavior improves from data or experience. Neural networks are one family of machine-learning models. Generative AI Systems that produce new text, images, audio, or other content. Modern examples often use neural networks, but “generative” describes what a system does. Models and agents A language model predicts sequences. Reasoning training can improve multi-step work; an agent adds tools, memory, and a loop around a model. These labels overlap; they are not one universal ladder. Rules-based systems, learned tables, neural networks, generative systems, reasoning methods, and agents describe different mechanisms or behaviors. Ask what was learned, what was written by people, and what the system can actually do."},
{"title":"Where AI is going","page":"Understanding Artificial Intelligence","summary":"Start with a game small enough to inspect completely, then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and choose prompts and tools.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/understanding-artificial-intelligence.html#where-ai-is-going","text":"Where AI is going One demo tests your intuition against measured progress. The other tests confident predictions against seventy years of history. Read together, they support urgency with skepticism. The Pace of AI Progress AI Winters: Boom and Bust Use the dates, sources, measurement conditions and qualifications inside each activity when weighing its evidence."},
{"title":"How to use AI","page":"Understanding Artificial Intelligence","summary":"Start with a game small enough to inspect completely, then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and choose prompts and tools.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/understanding-artificial-intelligence.html#how-to-use-ai","text":"How to use AI Practice writing a clear request, then choose the right tool for the job. These two activities show how your instructions and your choice of tools shape what AI can help you do. Prompting Strategies AI Tool Guide"},
{"title":"Three ideas to carry forward","page":"Understanding Artificial Intelligence","summary":"Start with a game small enough to inspect completely, then train a language model, test forecasts against evidence, and choose prompts and tools.","collection":"Artificial Intelligence","url":"artificial-intelligence/understanding-artificial-intelligence.html#three-ideas-to-carry-forward","text":"Three ideas to carry forward Ask what kind of system you are looking at. Human-written rules, learned values, neural networks, language models, and agents behave differently. Match the tool to the job and the review you can provide. Choose a workflow whose results you can inspect. Bring your discipline’s standards. A useful classroom tool starts from the learning goal, exposes its assumptions, and makes evidence available for challenge. Explore the classroom example projects. Each includes a guide to its model, assumptions, and teaching use. Start with a learning goal and inspect the evidence before adapting a project for your class."},
{"title":"Identify a radial load","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-load-direction","text":"Identify a radial load. A force acts vertically downward on a horizontal shaft. The shaft axis is horizontal. Which load is this? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Radial 2. Axial 3. Neither Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 1. The force is perpendicular to the shaft axis, so it is radial."},
{"title":"Recognize combined loading","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-combined-load","text":"Recognize combined loading. A shaft carries a sideways belt pull and a separate force along its axis. What must its complete support arrangement carry? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Axial only 2. Combined radial and axial 3. Radial only Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 2. The two perpendicular components make combined loading. They may be assigned to different bearings."},
{"title":"Select for repeated starts","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-speed-selection","text":"Select for repeated starts. A small shaft repeatedly starts and runs at moderate speed under radial load and modest thrust in either direction. Which is a sensible first catalog candidate if low starting resistance matters? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Unflanged plain journal alone 2. Pure thrust ball bearing alone 3. Deep-groove ball bearing within its ratings Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 3. A deep-groove ball bearing can carry radial load and thrust in both directions with generally low starting resistance. Final selection still needs speed, load, life and lubrication checks."},
{"title":"Avoid the plain-bearing speed myth","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-film-speed","text":"Avoid the plain-bearing speed myth. A continuously running high-speed shaft already has an engineered oil supply. Must all plain bearings be rejected for speed alone? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Yes, all plain bearings are low-speed only 2. No, an engineered hydrodynamic bearing can be a candidate 3. Yes, only tapered rollers work at high speed Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 2. Fluid-film bearings serve high-speed machinery. Their design and oil conditions matter; the category name is not a speed limit."},
{"title":"Find the locating support","page":"Practice","summary":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-fixed-end","text":"Find the locating support. Support A retains both rings axially and is rated for radial load and thrust both ways. Support B carries radial load while allowing axial travel. Which end locates the shaft? Choose one answer Choose… 1. A 2. B 3. Both Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 1. A provides axial location and carries the applied thrust. B accommodates relative axial growth."},
{"title":"Predict exhausted floating travel","page":"Practice","summary":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-thermal-travel","text":"Predict exhausted floating travel. The shaft wants more thermal growth than the floating seat has travel. What happens in the ideal model? Choose one answer Choose… 1. The extra growth disappears without load 2. The locating bearing stops rotating by definition 3. The unaccommodated growth creates axial restraint Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 3. Floating works only while sufficient travel remains. After the limit is reached, the support arrangement resists further expansion."},
{"title":"Count round-shaft freedoms","page":"Practice","summary":"Linear Bearing Guides","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-linear-dof","text":"Count round-shaft freedoms. In the ideal geometric model, an adequately supported round sleeve has no anti-rotation key or axial stop. How many rigid-body freedoms remain? This is not a rotary service rating. Choose one answer Choose… 1. One: translation only 2. Two: translation and rotation about the shaft 3. Six: all motions Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 2. Translation and rotation about the shaft remain: two freedoms. Four are restrained. Ball-bushing rotary service still requires a suitable product rating."},
{"title":"Count profile-guide freedoms","page":"Practice","summary":"Linear Bearing Guides","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-rail-dof","text":"Count profile-guide freedoms. A properly engaged profile carriage supports its rated transverse forces and moments. Which intended motion remains? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Rotation about the rail 2. Translation across the rail 3. Translation along the rail Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 3. The profile geometry restrains the other five ideal rigid-body motions."},
{"title":"Choose a rigid spacer","page":"Practice","summary":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-shim-choice","text":"Choose a rigid spacer. An assembly instruction specifies setting an end-cover position with a measured rigid spacer thickness. Which part performs that job? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Precision shim 2. Wave spring 3. Unspecified lock washer Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 1. A shim sets distance in the stack. The assembly geometry determines how its thickness changes end play."},
{"title":"Maintain preload over small movement","page":"Practice","summary":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-spring-choice","text":"Maintain preload over small movement. An arrangement calls for a specified preload while allowing small dimensional changes. Which approach is appropriate? Choose one answer Choose… 1. Tighten until rotation becomes difficult 2. Use a calibrated spring at its specified working deflection 3. Add arbitrary brass foil Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Answer 2. A properly selected wave or Belleville spring applies force over a working deflection range. The required preload is a specification, not a feel test."},
{"title":"Calculate free shaft growth","page":"Practice","summary":"Shaft Support and Axial Adjustment","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-thermal-growth","text":"Calculate free shaft growth. A 600 mm carbon-steel shaft warms uniformly by 40 °C and is free to expand. Use ΔL = α·L·ΔT, where L is the original length and ΔT is the temperature rise. For this estimate, use a constant α = 10.44 × 10 −6 per °C. How much does the shaft length increase, in mm? Keep at least four significant figures in calculations and report four significant figures. Length increase (mm) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution The DOE Fundamentals Handbook, Material Science, Volume 2, Table 1 (MS-03, page 2) lists carbon steel at 5.8 × 10 −6 per °F. A temperature rise of 1 °C equals a rise of 1.8 °F, so α = 5.8 × 10 −6 × 1.8 = 10.44 × 10 −6 per °C. ΔL = (10.44 × 10 −6 /°C)(600 mm)(40 °C) = 0.25056 mm ≈ 0.2506 mm. Using mm for L gives mm for ΔL. Four significant figures describe the arithmetic, not the accuracy of this approximate material coefficient."},
{"title":"Ball rating life","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-life-ball","text":"Ball rating life. For an example ball bearing, C = 14 kN and P = 2 kN. Find L10 = (C/P)³ in million revolutions. Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate values and report four significant figures. Answer (million rev) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution (14/2)³ = 7³ = 343.0 million revolutions."},
{"title":"Life in hours","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-life-hours","text":"Life in hours. An example ball bearing has L10 = 343 million revolutions and runs at n = 1500 r/min. Find L10h = L10 × 10⁶/(60n). Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate values and report four significant figures. Answer (h) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 343 × 10⁶/(60 × 1500) = 3811.111… ≈ 3811 h."},
{"title":"Double the load","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-life-double","text":"Double the load. An example ball bearing has L10 = 343 million revolutions. Double P while keeping C fixed. Find the new L10 in million revolutions. Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate values and report four significant figures. Answer (million rev) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution The load is in the denominator and is cubed: 343/2³ = 42.875 ≈ 42.88 million revolutions."},
{"title":"Roller rating life","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-life-roller","text":"Roller rating life. For an example roller bearing, C = 14 kN and P = 2 kN. Find L10 = (C/P)^(10/3) in million revolutions. Keep the exponent as 10/3. Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate values and report four significant figures. Answer (million rev) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 7^(10/3) = 656.1354… ≈ 656.1 million revolutions. Use 10/3, not 3, for the roller exponent."},
{"title":"Required dynamic rating","page":"Practice","summary":"Bearing Types and Loads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bearing-life-rating","text":"Required dynamic rating. An example ball bearing must have L10 = 500 million revolutions at P = 2 kN. Find the required C = P × L10^(1/3), in kN. This is an exercise, not a catalog selection. Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate values and report four significant figures. Answer (kN) Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution C = 2 × 500^(1/3) = 15.87401… ≈ 15.87 kN. L10 enters as 500 million revolutions, not 500 × 10⁶."},
{"title":"Synchronize separated shafts","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-select-timing","text":"1. Synchronize separated shafts: Two separated parallel shafts must stay synchronized in a clean area where oil at the drive contact is unwanted. Which ordinary drive is the best starting candidate? 1 = flat belt; 2 = timing belt; 3 = lubricated roller chain; 4 = a single compact gear pair. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2 — timing belt. Teeth maintain timing and the belt contact runs dry. A flat belt can slip; ordinary chain needs joint lubrication; a compact gear pair cannot bridge the stated gap. This is a shortlist choice, followed by load and environment checks."},
{"title":"Quiet fan drive on a budget","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt, Chain and Gear Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-select-friction","text":"2. Quiet fan drive on a budget: A sheltered fan has separated parallel shafts. Low initial cost and quiet running matter, and a little speed variation is acceptable. Which is the best starting candidate? 1 = V-belt; 2 = precision helical gearbox with extra shafts; 3 = exposed roller chain; 4 = precision timing drive solely to guarantee synchronization. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 — V-belt. The job accepts the speed variation of a friction drive and benefits from a simple, quiet flexible span. The other choices add requirements or costs the scenario does not call for. Check the actual power and pulley ratings before selection."},
{"title":"Match an existing chain interface","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-select-chain","text":"3. Match an existing chain interface: A sheltered low-speed conveyor has serviceable ANSI #40 sprockets, suitable lubrication access and no requirement for quiet precision positioning. Which replacement transmission fits these specified wheels? 1 = flat belt; 2 = V-belt; 3 = compatible #40 roller chain; 4 = 08B chain chosen only because its pitch is also 12.7 mm. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3 — compatible #40 roller chain. Match chain series and dimensions as well as pitch. Equal pitch alone does not make 08B interchangeable with #40. Confirm the strand count, load rating and connecting-link type too."},
{"title":"Quieter drive in a compact housing","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Drives and Tooth Geometry","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-select-helical","text":"4. Quieter drive in a compact housing: A compact lubricated gearbox has close parallel shafts and needs smoother high-speed engagement than a comparable spur pair. The bearing arrangement can carry axial thrust. Which candidate best addresses that requirement? 1 = a spur pair selected only for zero axial thrust; 2 = a slipping flat belt; 3 = a long exposed chain; 4 = a matched helical pair. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 4 — a matched helical pair. Progressive engagement generally improves smoothness and noise. The stated bearing capability addresses its axial thrust, but the tooth and bearing ratings still need checking."},
{"title":"Read #25 pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain25-pitch","text":"5. Read #25 pitch: What is the nominal pitch of ANSI #25 chain, in inches? Enter a decimal to three places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Remove the final digit of 25: 2/8 = 0.250 in. The answer is 0.250 in. The final 5 describes bush-chain construction; it does not mean a 5 in pitch."},
{"title":"Read #35 pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain35-pitch","text":"6. Read #35 pitch: What is the nominal pitch of ANSI #35 chain, in inches? Enter a decimal to three places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Remove the final digit: 3/8 = 0.375 in. The answer is 0.375 in, not 35/8 in and not 0.35 in."},
{"title":"Read #40-2 pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain40-pitch","text":"7. Read #40-2 pitch: An ANSI #40-2 chain has two parallel strands. What is its nominal pitch in inches? Enter a decimal to three places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The base number is 40: 4/8 = 0.500 in. The answer is 0.500 in. The -2 changes the strand count, not the pitch."},
{"title":"Look up 08B pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain08b-pitch","text":"8. Look up 08B pitch: A B-series lookup lists 05B-1 at 8.000 mm, 06B-1 at 9.525 mm, 08B-1 at 12.700 mm and 10B-1 at 15.875 mm. What pitch should a student record for 08B-1, to two decimal places in millimeters? Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The 08B-1 row gives 12.70 mm. For this size, 8/16 in is 0.5 in, and 0.5 × 25.4 = 12.70 mm. Do not read 08 as 8 mm, and do not generalize this fraction rule to small sizes such as 05B."},
{"title":"Write a sprocket reduction","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain-reduction","text":"9. Write a sprocket reduction: A 22-tooth driver turns a 55-tooth driven sprocket with compatible chain and normal engagement. Write the reduction as X:1, input speed over output speed. Enter only X, to two decimal places. Answer (X in X:1) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = N driven /N driver = 55/22 = 2.50. The reduction is 2.50:1. Enter 2.50. Reversing the tooth counts gives 0.40, the reciprocal, not this reduction. See Gear Ratios."},
{"title":"Find driven-sprocket speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Chain Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-chain-speed","text":"10. Find driven-sprocket speed: A 21-tooth sprocket at 980 rpm drives a 49-tooth sprocket through correctly engaged chain. What is the average driven speed? Round to the nearest rpm; ignore chordal speed ripple. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n out = 980 × 21/49 = 420 rpm. The answer is 420 rpm. A larger driven sprocket turns slower. The inverse calculation would give about 2287 rpm, which fails that direction check. See Gear Ratios."},
{"title":"Find timing-pulley speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-timing-speed","text":"11. Find timing-pulley speed: A timing belt connects a 28-tooth driver at 1440 rpm to a 60-tooth driven pulley. The belt and pulleys match, with no tooth jumping. What is the output speed, rounded to the nearest rpm? Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n out = 1440 × 28/60 = 672 rpm. The answer is 672 rpm. Teeth, not outside pulley diameters, establish this ratio. See Gear Ratios."},
{"title":"Estimate V-belt output speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drive-friction-speed","text":"12. Estimate V-belt output speed: A V-belt driver has an effective diameter of 90 mm and runs at 1680 rpm. The driven sheave has an effective diameter of 210 mm. Estimate output speed to the nearest rpm, explicitly assuming no slip. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n out = 1680 × 90/210 = 720 rpm. The no-slip estimate is 720 rpm. It is not a guarantee of actual friction-belt speed under load. Both dimensions use the same effective-diameter convention. See Gear Ratios."},
{"title":"Find an open-belt pitch length","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#belt-open-length","text":"Find an open-belt pitch length: An open belt has pitch diameters D = 200 mm and d = 100 mm, with C = 400 mm. Find the approximate pitch length L using the unequal-pulley correction. Round to 0.1 mm. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L ≈ 2(400) + π(200 + 100)/2 + (200 − 100)²/(4 × 400) = 800 + 471.2389 + 6.25 = 1277.5 mm. Omitting the correction gives 1271.24 mm."},
{"title":"Find spacing for a chosen belt length","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#belt-stock-center","text":"Find spacing for a chosen belt length: For a hypothetical stock choice with pitch length L = 1300 mm, use D = 200 mm and d = 100 mm. Find C from the inverse length approximation. This is a teaching value, not a catalog listing. Round to 0.1 mm. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution B = 4(1300) − 2π(200 + 100) = 3315.0444 mm. C = [B + √(B² − 32(100)²)]/16 ≈ 411.3 mm. Substitution into the forward equation returns 1300 mm."},
{"title":"Find large-pulley driven speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#belt-driven-speed","text":"Find large-pulley driven speed: The small driver has pitch diameter d = 100 mm and turns at 1200 r/min. The large driven pulley has D = 200 mm. Assuming no slip, find its speed to the nearest r/min. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in r/min Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n_small/n_large = D/d = 2. Therefore n_large = 1200/2 = 600 r/min. Multiplying by 2 gives 2400 r/min and reverses the reduction."},
{"title":"Find belt linear speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#belt-linear-speed","text":"Find belt linear speed: A small pulley with pitch diameter d = 100 mm turns at 1200 r/min without slip. Find belt speed in m/min. Round to 0.1 m/min. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in m/min Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution v = πd n_small / 1000 = π × 100 × 1200 / 1000 = 377.0 m/min. Use diameter, not the 50 mm radius. Using π = 3.14 gives 376.8 m/min."},
{"title":"Find small-pulley wrap","page":"Practice","summary":"Belt Drives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#belt-small-wrap","text":"Find small-pulley wrap: An open belt uses D = 200 mm, d = 100 mm and C = 400 mm. Find the small-pulley wrap θ = 180° − 2 asin((D − d)/(2C)). Express the inverse sine in degrees. Round to 0.1°. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in degrees Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution α = asin(100/800) = 7.180756°. θ = 180° − 2α = 165.6°. Subtracting the raw radian value from 180 gives about 179.75°, which mixes units."},
{"title":"A through hub keyway","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-hub-method","text":"A through hub keyway. A hub has a finished through bore and needs a straight internal keyway. A matching broach set and suitable press are available. Choose the direct method. End mill a slot on the outside of a shaft. Use a guided push keyway broach. Use a Woodruff cutter to make a curved shaft recess. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. The bushing guides the broach inside the existing bore; the feature is a hub keyway."},
{"title":"A keyseat ending within the shaft","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-shaft-method","text":"A keyseat ending within the shaft. A straight flat-bottom shaft keyseat ends before either shaft end. Rounded ends in plan view are allowed. Which tool suits this feature? An end mill with a permitted entry method. A conventional through-hole keyway broach and bore bushing. A drill alone. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. An end mill can enter and travel along this closed-end slot. Its diameter sets the end radius."},
{"title":"Repeated through hex holes","page":"Practice","summary":"Broaching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-hex-method","text":"Repeated through hex holes. Many identical parts need a through hex hole. A prepared starting hole, suitable press and dedicated hex hole broach are specified. Which method directly produces the profile? A round reamer alone. A Woodruff keyseat cutter. Conventional internal hole broaching. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. A dedicated hole broach generates the polygon from its specified starting hole. A keyway broach cuts only a slot, not the entire hex."},
{"title":"Between keyway passes","page":"Practice","summary":"Broaching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-shim-sequence","text":"Between keyway passes. A multi-pass keyway set calls for one added shim after its first pass. Choose the correct next sequence after that pass is complete. Add an arbitrary shim while the broach remains loaded. Remove and clean the broach; insert the specified shim behind it; reinsert, lubricate, check alignment and make the next pass. Repeat without a shim to obtain the specified extra depth. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. The shim offsets the tool for the next cut. The prescribed stack belongs behind the broach, and setup checks repeat between passes."},
{"title":"Count first and shimmed passes","page":"Practice","summary":"Broaching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-pass-count","text":"Count first and shimmed passes. Invented planning model: the unshimmed pass reaches 1.0 mm radial depth. Each later pass, with one additional shim increment, adds 0.5 mm. How many total passes reach 2.5 mm? Include the first pass; round a fractional added-pass count upward. These are not tool settings. Enter the whole number of passes Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1 + ceiling((2.5 − 1.0)/0.5) = 1 + 3 = 4 passes. Depths are 1.0, 1.5, 2.0 and 2.5 mm. Three counts only the added passes; five incorrectly divides the full target by the shim increment."},
{"title":"Round up, then check overshoot","page":"Practice","summary":"Broaching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#broach-pass-ceiling","text":"Round up, then check overshoot. Invented model: the first pass reaches 1.0 mm; each added shim increment gives 0.5 mm more depth on the next pass. What minimum total pass count reaches AT LEAST 2.2 mm? Round upward to a whole pass. This counting answer is not permission to exceed a drawing limit. Enter the whole number of passes Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1 + ceiling((2.2 − 1.0)/0.5) = 1 + ceiling(2.4) = 4. Three passes reach only 2.0 mm. Four reach 2.5 mm, so this schedule is unsuitable if 2.2 mm is the maximum allowed depth."},
{"title":"Equal cross-section dimensions","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#key-square","text":"Equal cross-section dimensions. A parallel key has equal width and height and a uniform section along its working length. Identify it. Square parallel key. Rectangular parallel key with unequal width and height. Woodruff key. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. Square describes equal width and height in cross-section, not the shape of the key seen from above."},
{"title":"Unequal cross-section dimensions","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#key-rectangular","text":"Unequal cross-section dimensions. A parallel key has unequal width and height, with no taper along its working length. Identify it. Woodruff key. Rectangular parallel key. Gib-head key. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. This is the rectangular parallel form; the dimensions alone do not specify the required fit."},
{"title":"A disk segment seated in a shaft","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#key-woodruff","text":"A disk segment seated in a shaft. A key has a curved bottom that sits in a circular recess cut into the shaft. Identify the key. Square parallel key. Gib-head key. Woodruff key. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. The Woodruff key is a disk segment with a matching curved shaft seat."},
{"title":"A tapered key with a removal head","page":"Practice","summary":"Keys, Keyways and Shaft Keyseats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#key-gib-head","text":"A tapered key with a removal head. A tapered key has a projecting head that can be engaged to remove it. Identify the key. Gib-head key. Woodruff key. Headless rectangular parallel key. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. The gib head supplies a place to engage the key for removal."},
{"title":"Recover a hinge's intended spin","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-hinge-diagnosis","text":"Recover a hinge's intended spin In the ideal axis-aligned lab, a grounded housing supports a shaft/lever. Its shoulder is seated at z = 0 and angular alignment holds θ = 0°. The goal is a hinge that turns without axial slide. Which change meets that goal? Choose one change Choose an answer Remove the shoulder condition; keep angular alignment Remove angular alignment; keep the shoulder seated Keep both conditions because 0 DOF is always best Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Remove angular alignment; keep the shoulder seated. The hinge needs one rotation and no axial translation. Removing only the angular condition restores spin; z = 0 still blocks slide. More constraints are not automatically better for a moving joint."},
{"title":"Preserve a slider's axial travel","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-slider-condition","text":"Preserve a slider's axial travel In the same bounded two-coordinate lab, axis alignment is already built in. The goal is axial sliding with no spin. Which additional condition matches it? Ignore contact, friction and collision; travel is within the declared lab range. Choose the condition Choose an answer Angular alignment at θ = 0° only Shoulder seating at z = 0 only Both shoulder seating and angular alignment Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Angular alignment only. It blocks spin and leaves z free to vary. Shoulder seating would remove the intended slide. One DOF alone does not identify a joint: a hinge and a slider each have one, but preserve different motions."},
{"title":"Apply a locating datum","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-datum-not-freeze","text":"Apply a locating datum The lab shaft is at z = 18 mm and θ = 70° with neither additional condition active. Select shoulder seating, explicitly defined as z = 0. What is the resulting z coordinate in millimeters? This condition leaves spin free. Resulting z (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 0 mm. The condition locates the shoulder at its stated datum, rather than freezing the existing 18 mm position. The angle stays 70° until another motion or condition changes it."},
{"title":"Count a free component’s motions","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-free-body","text":"Count a free component’s motions A rigid component floats freely in a three-dimensional assembly. It has no mates, contacts, or fixed state. How many independent degrees of freedom does it have relative to the assembly frame? Answer in DOF (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 6 DOF. Three translations and three rotations: 3 + 3 = 6. Three is the count of axes, not the count of independent rigid-body motions."},
{"title":"Seat a flat plate on a base","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-planar-dof","text":"Seat a flat plate on a base A free rigid plate receives one coincident mate between its flat underside and the top plane of a grounded base. No other mates or contact constraints exist. How many DOF remain? Answer in DOF (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 DOF. The plate can slide in two directions within the plane and rotate about the plane normal. The mate removes normal translation and two tilting motions: 6 − 3 = 3."},
{"title":"Align a shaft with a bore","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-shaft-dof","text":"Align a shaft with a bore A free shaft is mated concentrically to the cylindrical bore of a grounded housing. Rotation is not locked. There are no other mates or contacts. How many DOF remain? Answer in DOF (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 DOF. Translation along the common axis and rotation about that axis remain. The axis alignment removes four of the initial six independent motions: 6 − 4 = 2."},
{"title":"Seat the shaft shoulder","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-shoulder-dof","text":"Seat the shaft shoulder A shaft already has a concentric mate to a grounded bore, with rotation unlocked. Add a coincident mate between its shoulder face and a housing end face, both perpendicular to the common axis. No other constraints exist. How many DOF remain? Answer in DOF (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 DOF. Concentric alignment left axial slide and spin. Seating the shoulder removes the slide. Rotation about the common axis remains: 2 − 1 = 1."},
{"title":"Lock the remaining rotation","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-fixed-or-moving","text":"Lock the remaining rotation A shaft’s axis and shoulder are located as in the previous type of joint: only rotation about its axis remains. An independent angular constraint now fixes that remaining rotation. How many DOF remain? Assume compatible constraints. Answer in DOF (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 0 DOF. The final independent rotation is removed: 1 − 1 = 0. This is appropriate for a stationary part; a pivot meant to turn should retain that rotation."},
{"title":"Count component instances","page":"Practice","summary":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-bom-instances","text":"Count component instances One original teaching pivot assembly Item Part number Description Qty per assembly 1 PV-110 Support 1 2 PV-210 Lever 1 3 PV-310 Pivot pin 1 4 HW-410 Thrust washer 2 5 HW-510 Pin retainer 1 How many individual component instances are required for one assembly? Count both washers. Answer in components (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 6 components. Add the quantity column: 1 + 1 + 1 + 2 + 1 = 6. Five is the number of distinct rows, not the number of instances."},
{"title":"Count washers for six assemblies","page":"Practice","summary":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-bom-quantity","text":"Count washers for six assemblies One original teaching pivot assembly Item Part number Description Qty per assembly 1 PV-110 Support 1 2 PV-210 Lever 1 3 PV-310 Pivot pin 1 4 HW-410 Thrust washer 2 5 HW-510 Pin retainer 1 How many HW-410 washers are required for six complete assemblies, with no spares? Answer in washers (whole number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 12 washers. 2 washers/assembly × 6 assemblies = 12 washers. Item number 4 identifies the row; using 4 × 6 = 24 mistakes an identifier for a quantity."},
{"title":"Choose mates that fully locate a cover","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-locate-cover","text":"Choose mates that fully locate a cover A rigid rectangular cover must be stationary relative to a grounded rectangular housing. Their corresponding bottom, side, and end datum planes are mutually perpendicular and geometrically compatible. Choose A, B, or C and explain. A: Coincident bottom planes only. B: Coincident bottom planes, then a parallel mate between those same planes. C: Coincident bottom, corresponding side, and corresponding end planes. Your choice and reasoning Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing the explanation. This text is not automatically graded or saved. Show answer and reasoning C. Seating the bottom leaves two in-plane slides and one spin. The independent side-plane condition removes one slide and that spin. The end-plane condition removes the last slide. A leaves three DOF. B repeats an orientation already established by coincidence. This answer assumes matching perpendicular datum planes, not arbitrary selections."},
{"title":"Find the redundant relationship","page":"Practice","summary":"Checking CAD Assemblies","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-redundant-mate","text":"Find the redundant relationship A shaft has a concentric mate to a grounded housing bore, plus a seated shoulder mate. Which proposed addition repeats an existing geometric condition: A, a parallel relation between those same two axes; or B, an independent constraint fixing the remaining shaft rotation? Explain. Your choice and reasoning Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing the explanation. This text is not automatically graded or saved. Show answer and reasoning A is redundant. Coincident axes are already parallel. B removes the remaining spin; whether it is wanted depends on whether the shaft is meant to rotate. Do not add B merely to remove an under-defined indicator."},
{"title":"Read the washer balloon","page":"Practice","summary":"Exploded Views and Bills of Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-bom-balloon","text":"Read the washer balloon An original pivot diagram has two washer positions marked with balloon 4, referring to the same BOM row. One original teaching pivot assembly Item Part number Description Qty per assembly 1 PV-110 Support 1 2 PV-210 Lever 1 3 PV-310 Pivot pin 1 4 HW-410 Thrust washer 2 5 HW-510 Pin retainer 1 A leader ends at a washer and its balloon reads 4. State the part number and quantity per assembly. Does seeing balloon 4 twice double the listed quantity? Your choice and reasoning Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing the explanation. This text is not automatically graded or saved. Show answer and reasoning HW-410; two per assembly. The balloon maps to item 4. Two leaders identify two instances of the same part; they do not multiply the quantity column again."},
{"title":"Choose a meaningful motion check","page":"Practice","summary":"Checking CAD Assemblies","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-motion-check","text":"Choose a meaningful motion check A lever clears its housing at both travel endpoints. A mate animation moves, but you have not checked intermediate positions or collision settings. Can you conclude that it clears the housing throughout its travel? Describe the next check. Your choice and reasoning Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing the explanation. This text is not automatically graded or saved. Show answer and reasoning No. Inspect intermediate positions across the intended range and use appropriate interference or collision checks on the whole connected assembly. Endpoint clearance and a moving preview do not establish clearance everywhere. Check the scope of the tool: a joint preview may ignore other mates, and ordinary dragging may allow solids to pass through each other."},
{"title":"Recover a reference after renaming","page":"Practice","summary":"Assembly Files and Mates","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#assemblies-broken-reference","text":"Recover a reference after renaming A part was renamed in ordinary File Explorer. Its assembly now reports the old filename as missing. Choose the best response: A, suppress the missing component permanently; B, verify the intended file and repair the reference using the CAD workflow, then save and reopen affected documents; or C, insert an unrelated part with the old name. Your choice and reasoning Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing the explanation. This text is not automatically graded or saved. Show answer and reasoning B. Confirm the correct component identity before repairing its reference. Save the affected documents and check drawings as well as assemblies. Suppressing the component hides the symptom; substituting an unrelated file can make a model open while describing the wrong design."},
{"title":"Mass from volume and density","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Certification Preparation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-check-mass","text":"Mass from volume and density. For this original general skill check, assume a uniform aluminum solid has volume 12.0 cm³ and density 2.70 g/cm³. Calculate its mass in grams, rounded to the nearest 0.1 g. Mass in g Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution m = density × volume = 2.70 g/cm³ × 12.0 cm³ = 32.4 g. Cubic centimeters cancel. Dividing volume by density gives about 4.44 and does not give units of mass."},
{"title":"Convert a cubic measurement","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Certification Preparation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-check-volume-units","text":"Convert a cubic measurement. In this original general skill check, a solid's volume is 8,750 mm³. Express that same volume in cm³ to the nearest 0.01 cm³. Volume in cm³ Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 cm³ = (10 mm)³ = 1,000 mm³. Therefore 8,750 ÷ 1,000 = 8.75 cm³. Dividing by 10 only, as for length, would give 875, which is wrong for volume."},
{"title":"Change only the displayed units","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Certification Preparation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-check-display-units","text":"Change only the displayed units. In this original general skill check, you change a CAD measurement display from millimeters to centimeters without editing geometry or material. What happens to the physical part's mass? Choose a result Select one It becomes one tenth as large It stays the same It becomes one thousandth as large Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation It stays the same. A display conversion describes the same geometry using different units. Resizing the geometry or changing density would be a different operation."},
{"title":"Read a radius before entering a diameter","page":"Practice","summary":"Drawing Terminology Review","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-check-radius","text":"Read a radius before entering a diameter. This original general skill check describes a circle with radius 7.0 mm. A sketch tool asks for diameter. Enter the diameter in mm to the nearest 0.1 mm. Diameter in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution d = 2r = 2 × 7.0 mm = 14.0 mm. Entering 7.0 as the diameter makes the circle half the intended diameter."},
{"title":"Recognize an enlarged detail","page":"Practice","summary":"Drawing Terminology Review","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-check-view-purpose","text":"Recognize an enlarged detail. In this original general skill check, a drawing enlarges a small region solely to make its features easier to read. Which term describes that view? Choose a view Select one Section view Auxiliary view Detail view Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Detail view. Enlarging a selected region makes small features readable. A section reveals features at an imaginary cut; an auxiliary view establishes a viewing direction perpendicular to an inclined surface."},
{"title":"File references","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Files and Documents","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-file-references","text":"File references: A drawing shows a local SOLIDWORKS assembly made from separate part files. What should a transfer include? Only the drawing file. The drawing, assembly, and required component files. Only a screenshot of the assembly. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The drawing references its model, and the assembly references its components. Review a Pack and Go package rather than assuming one file holds everything."},
{"title":"Choosing an Onshape tab","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Files and Documents","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-part-studio","text":"Choosing an Onshape tab: You want to build two related parts from sketches and features in one Onshape modeling context. Where should you start? A Drawing tab. An Assembly tab that automatically creates their geometry. A Part Studio tab. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. A Part Studio can create several related parts. An Assembly arranges instances and defines their relationships."},
{"title":"Changing displayed units","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Files and Documents","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-unit-change","text":"Changing displayed units: A correctly modeled edge is 1 inch long. You change its displayed units to millimeters without scaling the model. What length should it report? 25.4 mm. 1 mm. 254 mm. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. One inch equals exactly 25.4 mm. A display-unit conversion preserves physical length."},
{"title":"Revealing hidden edges","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Model Views and Checks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-hidden-edges","text":"Revealing hidden edges: You want an unshaded view that distinguishes concealed edges from visible edges. Which style fits? Shaded without edges. Hidden edges removed. Hidden edges visible. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Hidden edges visible includes concealed boundaries. Hidden edges removed omits them; shading without edges does not provide those outlines."},
{"title":"Normal to a plane","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Model Views and Checks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-normal-view","text":"Normal to a plane: A planar face is tilted relative to all standard model planes. Which direction gives a normal view of that face? Along a direction perpendicular to the face. Always from the model front. Along an edge in the face. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Normal means perpendicular to the selected plane. It need not match a standard front, top, or side direction."},
{"title":"Section view or cut feature","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Model Views and Checks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-section-view","text":"Section view or cut feature: You turn on a section view in the model window, inspect a cavity, then turn the section off. What changed? Material was permanently removed. The display was clipped temporarily. A drawing section was automatically added. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The viewing section exposes the inside visually. It is not a material-removal feature or an automatically created drawing view."},
{"title":"Checking a mass result","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Model Views and Checks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-mass-density","text":"Checking a mass result: A uniform solid has volume 12 cm³ and assigned density 2.5 g/cm³. What mass should the model report? 4.8 g. 14.5 g. 30 g. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Mass = density × volume = 2.5 × 12 = 30 g. Dividing volume by density or adding the values gives the other choices, not mass."},
{"title":"Interpreting a measurement","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD Model Views and Checks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-measure-selection","text":"Interpreting a measurement: Measure reports the minimum gap between two cylindrical faces, but you need the distance between their axes. What should you do? Accept the number because every distance is equivalent. Check the selected entities and choose an appropriate center or axis measurement. Change the display color. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Minimum surface gap and axis-to-axis distance answer different questions. Read the measurement label and check the selection."},
{"title":"Version or branch","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-version-branch","text":"Version or branch: You need a fixed milestone and a separate place to explore a new design. Which Onshape workflow fits? Create a version, then branch from it to an editable workspace. Edit the captured design state of the version directly. Move the rollback bar and call that a separate branch. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. A version preserves a design state. Branching from it creates a separate workspace for further changes."},
{"title":"Feature order or edit history","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-rollback-history","text":"Feature order or edit history: An early sketch was edited today. You move the rollback bar just after that sketch. Does this recover its dimensions from last week? Yes; feature position is a timestamp. Yes; rollback always restores the previous save. No; rollback controls feature evaluation order, not which historical edit is used. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The current sketch can contain today's edits even near the start of the feature sequence. Use history or a preserved earlier state to inspect older geometry."},
{"title":"Checking a shared link","page":"Practice","summary":"CAD History, Versions, and Sharing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cad-sharing-access","text":"Checking a shared link: A reviewer needs to inspect a fixed Onshape design milestone. What should you check before sending the link? Only that the document has a short name. That the link targets the intended version and the recipient has appropriate access. That the reviewer has edit permission to every workspace. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. A fixed version identifies the milestone. Sharing permissions determine access; the URL alone does not grant it."},
{"title":"Place probes for a positive R1 reading","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#probe-place-r1-positive","text":"Place probes for a positive R1 reading. A 12 V source supplies 30 Ω R1 and 60 Ω R2 in series, with both switches closed. Which ordered placement gives the positive drop across R1? 1 = red after S1, black after R1; 2 = red after R1, black after S1; 3 = both probes after S1; 4 = red supply −, black supply +. Choice number Check answer Enter a choice, then check it. Show worked solution 1. Current leaves the positive source terminal, passes S1, then crosses R1. Red before R1 and black after R1 gives V(red) − V(black) = +4 V."},
{"title":"Reverse the probes across R1","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#probe-reverse-r1-sign","text":"Reverse the probes across R1. The same closed 12 V, 30 Ω / 60 Ω series circuit has 4 V across R1. Put red after R1 and black after S1. What signed reading appears? Signed reading in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution V(red) − V(black) = 8 V − 12 V = −4 V. A negative sign gives polarity information; it is not an error."},
{"title":"Measure across the only open switch","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#probe-open-s1-reading","text":"Measure across the only open switch. An ideal 12 V source supplies 30 Ω R1 and 60 Ω R2 in series. S1, immediately before R1, is open; S2, immediately before R2, is closed. Put red on supply + and black after S1. What signed voltage appears? Signed reading in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution No current flows, so neither resistor has a voltage drop. The downstream side is connected to supply − through the zero-current resistors. The sole open switch therefore has +12 V across it in this ideal circuit."},
{"title":"Recognize a same-node reading","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#probe-same-node-zero","text":"Recognize a same-node reading. Both probes touch the same uninterrupted conductor after S1. What voltage does an ideal meter report? Reading in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0 V. Both probes are at the same potential, whatever that potential is relative to supply −."},
{"title":"Do not invent a floating-node voltage","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#probe-two-opens-floating","text":"Do not invent a floating-node voltage. Both series switches are open, isolating a conductor between them. What should this bounded ideal model report for a probe from that floating conductor to supply −? 1 = always 0 V; 2 = always 12 V; 3 = undetermined without another connection; 4 = 6 V because the resistors divide the source. Choice number Check answer Enter a choice, then check it. Show worked solution 3. Two breaks isolate a floating region. The one-open teaching model does not add leakage, capacitance, meter loading, or another path that would establish its potential."},
{"title":"Point list: final X increment","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-point-list-dx","text":"Point list: final X increment. In mm, start at A=(3,7), then visit B=(18,7), C=(18,−2), D=(8,−2). Convert the complete list to signed increments on paper. What is ΔX for C→D? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The increments are A→B=(15,0), B→C=(0,−9), C→D=(−10,0), each found by next minus current. The requested value is −10 mm."},
{"title":"Point list: middle Y increment","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-point-list-dy","text":"Point list: middle Y increment. In mm, start at A=(3,7), then visit B=(18,7), C=(18,−2), D=(8,−2). Convert the complete list to signed increments on paper. What is ΔY for B→C? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The increments are A→B=(15,0), B→C=(0,−9), C→D=(−10,0), each found by next minus current. The requested value is −9 mm."},
{"title":"Increment list: final X","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-increments-x","text":"Increment list: final X. Start at (−4,6) mm. Apply increments (9,0), (0,−8), (−3,5). Write each absolute point. What is the final X? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Successive points are (5,6), (5,−2), (2,3): add each increment to the previous point. Final X is 2 mm."},
{"title":"Increment list: final Y","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-increments-y","text":"Increment list: final Y. Start at (−4,6) mm. Apply increments (9,0), (0,−8), (−3,5). Write each absolute point. What is the final Y? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Successive points are (5,6), (5,−2), (2,3): add each increment to the previous point. Final Y is 3 mm."},
{"title":"Program endpoint: X","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-trace-x","text":"Program endpoint: X. Paper trace only: start at (2,3,10) mm, no compensation, unchanged work offset. Common milling modes apply. G21 G90 G94 G01 X14 Y9 F100 G91 X-4 Y6 G90 X5 M30 What is final X? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Positions after the four moves are (14,9,10), (10,9,10), (10,15,10), (5,15,10). M30 ends the trace without a positioning command. X is 5 mm."},
{"title":"Program endpoint: Y","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-trace-y","text":"Program endpoint: Y. Paper trace only: start at (2,3,10) mm, no compensation, unchanged work offset. Common milling modes apply. G21 G90 G94 G01 X14 Y9 F100 G91 X-4 Y6 G90 X5 M30 What is final Y? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Positions after the four moves are (14,9,10), (10,9,10), (10,15,10), (5,15,10). M30 ends the trace without a positioning command. Y is 15 mm."},
{"title":"Program endpoint: Z","page":"Practice","summary":"Absolute and Incremental Positioning","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-trace-z","text":"Program endpoint: Z. Paper trace only: start at (2,3,10) mm, no compensation, unchanged work offset. Common milling modes apply. G21 G90 G94 G01 X14 Y9 F100 G91 X-4 Y6 G90 X5 M30 What is final Z? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Positions after the four moves are (14,9,10), (10,9,10), (10,15,10), (5,15,10). M30 ends the trace without a positioning command. No block moves Z; it remains 10 mm."},
{"title":"Quarter arc: I offset","page":"Practice","summary":"G-code and Toolpaths","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-arc-i","text":"Quarter arc: I offset. In G17, G90, millimetres, with I/J measured from the start: a quarter-circle G03 goes from (5,7) to (13,15) around centre (5,15). What is I? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = centre X − start X = 5−5 = 0 mm. J = 15−7 = 8 mm. Both endpoint radii are 8 mm."},
{"title":"Quarter arc: J offset","page":"Practice","summary":"G-code and Toolpaths","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-arc-j","text":"Quarter arc: J offset. In G17, G90, millimetres, with I/J measured from the start: a quarter-circle G03 goes from (5,7) to (13,15) around centre (5,15). What is J? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution J = centre Y − start Y = 15−7 = 8 mm. I = 0. Do not enter the absolute centre Y of 15."},
{"title":"Quarter arc: endpoint X","page":"Practice","summary":"G-code and Toolpaths","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-arc-end-x","text":"Quarter arc: endpoint X. In the XY plane, start at (16,9) mm, centre (10,9), and move one quarter turn counterclockwise (G03). What is the absolute endpoint X? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The start radius vector is (6,0). Rotating it 90° counterclockwise gives (0,6). The endpoint is (10,15); X is 10 mm."},
{"title":"Quarter arc: endpoint Y","page":"Practice","summary":"G-code and Toolpaths","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-arc-end-y","text":"Quarter arc: endpoint Y. In the XY plane, start at (16,9) mm, centre (10,9), and move one quarter turn counterclockwise (G03). What is the absolute endpoint Y? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The radius is 6 mm. The endpoint is 6 mm above the centre, (10,15); Y is 15 mm."},
{"title":"Reverse arc: centre offset","page":"Practice","summary":"G-code and Toolpaths","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-arc-reverse-i","text":"Reverse arc: centre offset. A G02 quarter arc returns from (13,15) to (5,7) mm around centre (5,15). I/J are measured from this new start. What is I? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = 5−13 = −8 mm; J = 15−15 = 0. Reversing direction also changes the point from which centre offsets are measured."},
{"title":"Diameter to radial distance","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Axes and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-diameter-radius","text":"Diameter to radial distance. A lathe uses diameter programming in mm. At X36, how far is the programmed surface from the spindle centreline? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Radial distance = diameter / 2 = 36/2 = 18 mm."},
{"title":"Radius to diameter command","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Axes and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-radius-diameter","text":"Radius to diameter command. A surface is 11 mm from the spindle centreline on the positive tool side. What absolute X value represents it in diameter programming? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution X = 2 × radius = 2 × 11 = 22 mm. Radius mode would use X11."},
{"title":"Diameter change versus tool travel","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Axes and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-radial-change","text":"Diameter change versus tool travel. A diameter-mode X coordinate changes from X42 to X34 mm. What is the magnitude of radial inward tool travel? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The diameter decreases by 42−34 = 8 mm, so radial travel is 8/2 = 4 mm inward."},
{"title":"Work coordinate to machine coordinate","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Axes and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-work-offset-x","text":"Work coordinate to machine coordinate. In a simplified XY translation with no tool offsets or other shifts, work zero is at machine (−80,−40) mm. A point is work (25,12). What is machine X? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Machine point = (−80+25,−40+12) = (−55,−28). Machine X is −55 mm."},
{"title":"Identify M05","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Program Structure","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-code-m05","text":"Identify M05. For the common milling meanings taught here, M05 means: 1 = stop spindle; 2 = end program; 3 = select work offset. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1: stop spindle. M30 ends the program; G54 selects a work coordinate system. Check the actual control manual."},
{"title":"Identify G01","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Program Structure","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-code-g01","text":"Identify G01. For common milling usage, G01 means: 1 = rapid positioning; 2 = straight-line feed motion; 3 = peck drilling. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: straight-line feed motion. G00 is rapid positioning; G83 commonly selects peck drilling."},
{"title":"Identify G54","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Program Structure","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-code-g54","text":"Identify G54. For common milling usage, G54: 1 = stores a new zero automatically; 2 = homes the machine; 3 = selects a stored work coordinate system. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3: selects a stored work coordinate system. Selection neither measures the work nor writes the offset."},
{"title":"Feed per revolution to feed per minute","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Program Structure","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#cnc-feed-per-rev","text":"Feed per revolution to feed per minute. Assume G21 G95, F0.2 mm/rev and a steady spindle speed of 700 rpm. What feed per minute does this represent? Answer (mm/min) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.2 mm/rev × 700 rev/min = 140 mm/min. This is arithmetic, not a cutting recommendation."},
{"title":"CSS RPM: inch-a","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-css-inch-a","text":"CSS RPM: inch-a. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G96 is constant surface speed (codes vary by control), and steady-state G96 at 240 SFM, diameter 2 in, and a clamp above the calculated RPM. What RPM is requested? Use π, not 3.82, and round to the nearest whole RPM. Answer (RPM) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution RPM = 240 × 12 / (π × 2) = 458.3662, or 458 RPM. The diameter, not the radius, belongs in the denominator."},
{"title":"CSS RPM: inch-b","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-css-inch-b","text":"CSS RPM: inch-b. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G96 is constant surface speed (codes vary by control), and steady-state G96 at 150 SFM, diameter 0.75 in, and a clamp above the calculated RPM. What RPM is requested? Use π, not 3.82, and round to the nearest whole RPM. Answer (RPM) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution RPM = 150 × 12 / (π × 0.75) = 763.9437, or 764 RPM. The diameter, not the radius, belongs in the denominator."},
{"title":"CSS RPM: metric-a","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-css-metric-a","text":"CSS RPM: metric-a. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G96 is constant surface speed (codes vary by control), and steady-state G96 at 120 m/min, diameter 40 mm, and a clamp above the calculated RPM. What RPM is requested? Use π, not 3.82, and round to the nearest whole RPM. Answer (RPM) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution RPM = 120 × 1000 / (π × 40) = 954.9297, or 955 RPM. The diameter, not the radius, belongs in the denominator."},
{"title":"CSS RPM: metric-b","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-css-metric-b","text":"CSS RPM: metric-b. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G96 is constant surface speed (codes vary by control), and steady-state G96 at 80 m/min, diameter 16 mm, and a clamp above the calculated RPM. What RPM is requested? Use π, not 3.82, and round to the nearest whole RPM. Answer (RPM) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution RPM = 80 × 1000 / (π × 16) = 1591.5494, or 1592 RPM. The diameter, not the radius, belongs in the denominator."},
{"title":"CSS with a clamp","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-css-limited","text":"CSS with a clamp. In metric CSS, S100 requests 100 m/min at a 10 mm diameter. The RPM ceiling is 2000. Ignoring acceleration and other limits, what RPM is commanded? Round to a whole RPM. Answer (RPM) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The unrestricted calculation is 100 × 1000 / (π × 10) = 3183.10 RPM. The lower of this and the ceiling is 2000 RPM."},
{"title":"Why the clamp matters","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-clamp-reason","text":"Why the clamp matters. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G96 is constant surface speed (codes vary by control). During facing under G96, why must the RPM ceiling be set before CSS? Choose 1: requested RPM falls toward zero; 2: requested RPM rises as diameter decreases; 3: G96 fixes RPM regardless of diameter. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. RPM is inversely proportional to diameter. The clamp limits that request. Below the crossover diameter, surface speed falls as diameter decreases."},
{"title":"Diameter-mode X command","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-x-diameter","text":"Diameter-mode X command. The required OD is 32 mm. In diameter programming with correctly set offsets, what absolute X value represents that cylindrical surface? Enter the number only. Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution X32. The tool reference is 16 mm radially from the spindle centreline, but X reports diameter."},
{"title":"Radius-mode X command","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-x-radius","text":"Radius-mode X command. The required OD is 32 mm. In radius programming with correctly set offsets, what absolute X value represents that cylindrical surface? Enter the number only. Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution X = 32 / 2 = 16 mm. Z is unaffected by this convention."},
{"title":"Diameter after an X change","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Setup and Offsets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-x-after-move","text":"Diameter after an X change. A tool starts at X30 mm in diameter mode. Its X coordinate decreases by 4 mm, then it turns a cylindrical surface along Z. With correct offsets and no deflection, what diameter results? Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Final X = 30 − 4 = 26 mm diameter. The radial inward travel was 2 mm, not 4 mm."},
{"title":"Feed per revolution to IPM","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-feed-inch","text":"Feed per revolution to IPM. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G99 is feed per revolution (codes vary by control), inch units, G99, F0.007 in/rev and a steady 900 RPM. What feed per minute results? Round to 0.1 in/min. Answer (in/min) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.007 × 900 = 6.3 in/min. Multiply advance per revolution by revolutions per minute."},
{"title":"Metric feed per minute","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-feed-metric","text":"Metric feed per minute. Assume a Fanuc-style lathe control, where G99 is feed per revolution (codes vary by control), metric units, G99, F0.18 mm/rev and a steady 750 RPM. What feed per minute results? Round to a whole mm/min. Answer (mm/min) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.18 × 750 = 135 mm/min."},
{"title":"Find the missing speed limit","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Spindle and Feed Modes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-error-clamp","text":"Find the missing speed limit. Paper review only; this Fanuc-style lathe has no verified RPM ceiling set. The snippet is G21 G99, then G96 S100 M03, then G01 X0 F0.10. Which missing setting must be resolved before CSS? 1: an appropriate maximum-RPM clamp; 2: mill G17; 3: G98 to hold feed per revolution. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1. Establish the setup-appropriate RPM ceiling before G96, using G50 S… on this assumed control. G98 is feed per minute here. This identifies one error; it does not approve the rest of the snippet."},
{"title":"Find the wrong feed mode","page":"Practice","summary":"CNC Turning Program Walkthrough","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#turning-error-feed","text":"Find the wrong feed mode. Paper review only, using this page’s Fanuc-style lathe meanings. Intended feed is 0.12 mm/rev. The snippet is G21 G98, G97 S800 M03, G01 Z-8 F0.12. What change matches the intended feed? 1: change G21 to G20; 2: change G98 to G99; 3: change G97 to G96. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. G98 interprets F0.12 as 0.12 mm/min. G99 interprets it as 0.12 mm/rev, giving 0.12 × 800 = 96 mm/min. The remaining snippet still needs a complete setup and prove-out."},
{"title":"Apply a factor of 1.7","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-brix-a","text":"Apply a factor of 1.7: A hypothetical fluid reads 2.6 Brix and its supplied factor is 1.7. Calculate concentration to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2.6 × 1.7 = 4.42%. Using 2.6% forgets the factor; dividing by 1.7 uses it backward."},
{"title":"Apply a factor of 1.25","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-brix-b","text":"Apply a factor of 1.25: A hypothetical fluid reads 3.8 Brix and its supplied factor is 1.25. Calculate concentration to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3.8 × 1.25 = 4.75%. Brix is the instrument reading, not automatically percent."},
{"title":"Apply a factor of 1.6","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-brix-c","text":"Apply a factor of 1.6: A hypothetical fluid reads 4.4 Brix and its supplied factor is 1.6. Calculate concentration to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 4.4 × 1.6 = 7.04%. Do not divide the reading by the factor."},
{"title":"Is the tank lean or rich?","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-lean","text":"Is the tank lean or rich?: A hypothetical tank reads 3.2 Brix; the supplied factor is 1.5 and target is 5.5%. Calculate measured concentration minus target to the nearest 0.01 percentage point. A negative result means lean; positive means rich. Decide which before revealing the solution. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in percentage points Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Measured concentration = 3.2 × 1.5 = 4.8%. Difference = 4.8 − 5.5 = −0.70 percentage points: lean relative to this assumed target. A real decision also checks the permitted range."},
{"title":"Compare measured concentration with the target","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-rich","text":"Compare measured concentration with the target: A hypothetical tank reads 4.2 Brix; the supplied factor is 1.8 and target is 6.0%. Calculate measured concentration minus target to the nearest 0.01 percentage point. A negative result means lean; positive means rich. Decide which before revealing the solution. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in percentage points Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 4.2 × 1.8 = 7.56%. Difference = 7.56 − 6.0 = +1.56 percentage points: rich. Comparing 4.2 directly with 6.0 would wrongly call it lean."},
{"title":"Find the missing tank volume","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-topup-volume","text":"Find the missing tank volume: A hypothetical tank contains 34 L of serviceable coolant. Its allowed final fill is 50 L. How much premix can be added? Give liters to the nearest 0.01 L. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Answer in L Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A = 50 − 34 = 16.00 L. This determines volume only; measure concentration before choosing the premix strength."},
{"title":"Restore a lean tank with premix","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-premix-lean","text":"Restore a lean tank with premix: A hypothetical tank contains 34 L at 4.0%. Bring it to 50 L at an assumed target of 6.0%. What premix concentration is required? Give percent to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A = 50 − 34 = 16 L. P = (50 × 6 − 34 × 4) ÷ 16 = 10.25%. Check: (34 × 4 + 16 × 10.25) ÷ 50 = 6%. Premix at 6% would leave the tank lean."},
{"title":"Restore a rich tank with weaker premix","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-premix-rich","text":"Restore a rich tank with weaker premix: A hypothetical tank contains 40 L at 7.0%. Bring it to 50 L at an assumed target of 6.0%. What premix concentration is required? Give percent to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A = 10 L. P = (50 × 6 − 40 × 7) ÷ 10 = 2.00%. Check: (280 + 20) ÷ 50 = 6%. Plain water would instead leave 5.6%."},
{"title":"Find volume from an available premix","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-premix-amount","text":"Find volume from an available premix: A hypothetical tank has 30 L at 4.0%. An approved teaching premix is 8.0%; the desired tank concentration is 5.0%. The allowed final volume is 45 L. How much premix reaches the target? Give liters to the nearest 0.01 L. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in L Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A = 30 × (5 − 4) ÷ (8 − 5) = 10.00 L. Final volume is 40 L, below the 45 L limit. Concentrate balance: (30 × 4 + 10 × 8) ÷ 40 = 5%."},
{"title":"Measure concentrate for the top-up batch","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-concentrate-volume","text":"Measure concentrate for the top-up batch: Prepare 16 L of hypothetical 10.25% premix. How many liters of concentrate are needed? Give liters to the nearest 0.01 L. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in L Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Concentrate = 16 × 10.25 ÷ 100 = 1.64 L. Water = 16 − 1.64 = 14.36 L. Put water in first, then add concentrate while mixing."},
{"title":"Convert a water-to-concentrate ratio","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-ratio-a","text":"Convert a water-to-concentrate ratio: A hypothetical mixing instruction says 19 parts water to 1 part concentrate, by volume. What percentage of the final mix is concentrate? Give percent to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Total parts = 19 + 1 = 20. Concentrate percentage = 100 × 1 ÷ 20 = 5.00%. Using 100 ÷ 19 gives 5.26%, which uses water rather than the whole mixture as the denominator. Reversing the named ingredients gives 95%, also wrong."},
{"title":"Keep all the parts in the denominator","page":"Practice","summary":"Coolant Concentration and Mixing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#coolant-ratio-b","text":"Keep all the parts in the denominator: A hypothetical mixing instruction says 31 parts water to 1 part concentrate, by volume. What percentage of the final mix is concentrate? Give percent to the nearest 0.01%. All values are hypothetical; use the actual product data sheet in practice. Assume additive liquid volumes, uniform mixing and no loss. Top-up examples assume serviceable fluid and supplier-approved premix. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Total parts = 32. Concentrate percentage = 100 ÷ 32 = 3.125%, or 3.13%. Using 100 ÷ 31 gives about 3.23%; reversing the named ingredients gives 96.875%. Neither is the concentrate fraction requested."},
{"title":"Orient a bar for tension","page":"Practice","summary":"3D Printing from CAD","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-orientation","text":"Orient a bar for tension A straight printed bar will be pulled along its length. Compare printing it standing on its end with printing its length parallel to the bed. Assume the same material and a sound print. Which is the better starting orientation, and why? Your choice and reason Self-check: write a reason before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Show reasoning Start with the length parallel to the bed. Printing upright puts the tension across the interfaces between layers, where separation is often easier. Laying the bar down allows paths within layers to run along its length. Actual strength still depends on material, paths, and bonding; test a representative part."},
{"title":"Separate an outline from a filled mark","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-cut-or-engrave","text":"Separate an outline from a filled mark An approved sheet must yield a rectangular label with a filled dark symbol on its face. Which operation belongs on the outside boundary, and which belongs on the filled symbol? Should a thin score line automatically use through-cut settings? Your operations and reason Self-check: write a reason before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Show reasoning Use a vector cutting path for the outside boundary and raster engraving for the filled symbol. A vector score follows a path but is intended to mark the surface, so it needs an appropriate scoring setup rather than through-cut settings. Verify the assignments in the job preview."},
{"title":"Compensate an outside width","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-kerf-outside","text":"Compensate an outside width A rectangular cutout must finish 30.00 mm wide. Assume a constant 0.18 mm kerf centered on the path, with no automatic compensation. What path-to-path width produces that finished outside width? Give millimeters to the nearest 0.01 mm. Path width in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Each path moves outward by 0.18 / 2 = 0.09 mm. Across both sides, 30.00 + 2 × 0.09 = 30.18 mm. Subtracting kerf would shrink the part further; adding only half the kerf would compensate only one edge."},
{"title":"Compensate an opening","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-kerf-opening","text":"Compensate an opening A rectangular opening must finish 12.00 mm wide. Assume a constant 0.22 mm kerf centered on the path and no automatic compensation. What path-to-path width is needed? Give millimeters to the nearest 0.01 mm. Path width in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The waste is inside the opening. Move each path inward by 0.22 / 2 = 0.11 mm. The path width is 12.00 − 0.22 = 11.78 mm. Cutting removes half the kerf beyond each path and brings the finished opening back to 12.00 mm."},
{"title":"Choose a process for two shapes","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Fabrication Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-process-choice","text":"Choose a process for two shapes Choose an initial process for (A) a flat cover of constant thickness with surface lettering and (B) a one-piece rounded grip whose cross section changes along its length. Approved sheet and printable materials both meet the stated service requirements. Explain a limitation of each choice. Your choices and limitations Self-check: write a reason before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Show reasoning A: laser-cut and engrave the sheet; account for kerf, available thickness, and edge quality. B: print the grip; choose orientation for load and surface quality and check support access. A laser could make a grip from stacked profiles, but that changes the one-piece construction requirement. Neither process is automatically faster without estimating the actual jobs."},
{"title":"Separate slot clearance from kerf","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Fabrication Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fabrication-slot-clearance","text":"Separate slot clearance from kerf A measured sheet edge is 3.02 mm thick. Its receiving slot needs 0.10 mm total clearance across the width. Assume a constant 0.20 mm kerf centered on the path and no automatic compensation. What path-to-path slot width produces the desired finished slot? Give millimeters to the nearest 0.01 mm. These are exercise values, not a recommended fit. Path width in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution First choose the finished opening: 3.02 + 0.10 = 3.12 mm. Then compensate the opening's kerf: 3.12 − 0.20 = 2.92 mm. The cut enlarges this path width to 3.12 mm. Clearance is added once because the question gives total clearance, not clearance per side. See Laser Cutting and Engraving from CAD for the path-offset method."},
{"title":"Identify the extension line","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-line-role","text":"Identify the extension line. Which description identifies an extension line? The line with arrows at both ends showing a measured span. The thin line projecting a feature endpoint toward a dimension. The heavier line outlining the part. Choice number Choose an answer 1. The line with arrows at both ends showing a measured span. 2. The thin line projecting a feature endpoint toward a dimension. 3. The heavier line outlining the part. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 2. The extension line carries a feature endpoint out to the dimension line. The dimension line carries the arrows that show the measurement span."},
{"title":"Place the short dimension","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-placement-choice","text":"Place the short dimension. A tab has a.75 width dimension and the part has a 3.60 overall width. Both dimensions are above the view. Which order is clearer? Put.75 nearer the part and 3.60 farther out. Put 3.60 nearer the part and.75 farther out. Put both dimension lines on the top edge. Choice number Choose an answer 1. Put.75 nearer the part and 3.60 farther out. 2. Put 3.60 nearer the part and.75 farther out. 3. Put both dimension lines on the top edge. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 1. Put the short dimension nearest the part so its extension lines need not cross the overall dimension line."},
{"title":"Separate paper spacing from part scale","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimension Anatomy and Placement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-spacing-sheet","text":"Separate paper spacing from part scale. A drawing is plotted at half size (1:2). Using the 10 mm first-dimension layout guide, how far should the first dimension line be from the outline on the final sheet? Enter a whole number in mm. Answer in mm Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 10 mm. This guide describes paper spacing, so do not halve it to 5 mm or double it to 20 mm."},
{"title":"Find the extra center-spacing requirement","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Dimensions","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-redundant-chain","text":"Find the extra center-spacing requirement. Two holes are located from the same left edge: the first at.70 in and the second at 2.55 in. The design requires those two edge-based locations. Which additional controlling dimension repeats information? Hole diameter ⌀.28. Plate thickness.45. Center-to-center distance 1.85. Choice number Choose an answer 1. Hole diameter ⌀.28. 2. Plate thickness.45. 3. Center-to-center distance 1.85. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 3. 2.55 −.70 = 1.85 in. That spacing closes the location chain. Leave it out or show (1.85) for reference if useful. The hole diameter and plate thickness supply different information."},
{"title":"Read the parentheses","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Dimensions","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-reference-reading","text":"Read the parentheses. A spacing is shown as (2.40). What does the pair of parentheses tell the reader? The actual spacing must be exactly 2.40 with no variation. The value is reference information. The value is in millimeters even on an inch drawing. Choice number Choose an answer 1. The actual spacing must be exactly 2.40 with no variation. 2. The value is reference information. 3. The value is in millimeters even on an inch drawing. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 2. It is reference information. It does not impose another acceptance requirement; the actual spacing is controlled by other requirements."},
{"title":"Count the simple plate dimensions","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Dimensions","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-count-plate","text":"Count the simple plate dimensions. A rectangular solid has one off-center circular through hole normal to its broad face. No special symmetry or other features are given. Count the independent numeric dimensions for nominal geometry: body sizes, hole size and hole location. Do not count THRU or tolerance specifications. Answer in dimensions Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 6 dimensions: body length, height and thickness (3); hole diameter (1); and two center coordinates (2). THRU states the end condition. This count is for the stated ideal geometry, not a universal count for every production drawing."},
{"title":"Count dimensions for two equal holes","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Dimensions","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-count-pair","text":"Count dimensions for two equal holes. A rectangular solid has two equal-diameter through holes at unrelated positions, normal to its broad face. No symmetry or pattern spacing is given. Count body dimensions, the shared hole-size value and the four center coordinates. Do not count 2X, THRU or tolerances. Answer in dimensions Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 8 dimensions: 3 for the body + 1 shared diameter + 2 coordinates for each of 2 centers = 8. A 2X note shares the size; it does not share the center location."},
{"title":"Choose the view for the hole diameter","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Dimensions","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-view-choice","text":"Choose the view for the hole diameter. A plate’s front view shows the round hole as a visible circle; its edge view shows the hole as two dashed hidden lines. Where should its diameter callout go? On the visible circle in the front view. Between the hidden lines in the edge view. On both views as separate controlling dimensions. Choice number Choose an answer 1. On the visible circle in the front view. 2. Between the hidden lines in the edge view. 3. On both views as separate controlling dimensions. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 1. The visible circular outline gives the reader the clearest connection between the leader and the round feature."},
{"title":"Separate the main hole from the recess","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-main-hole-size","text":"Separate the main hole from the recess. An inch drawing gives 3X ⌀.312 THRU ⌴ ⌀.625 ↧.187. What is the main hole diameter? Give three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning.312 in. The first diameter belongs to the main through hole..625 is the counterbore diameter;.187 is its depth. 3X counts the repeated holes."},
{"title":"Read the counterbore diameter","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-counterbore-size","text":"Read the counterbore diameter. An inch drawing gives 3X ⌀.312 THRU ⌴ ⌀.625 ↧.187. What is the counterbore diameter? Give three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning.625 in. Read the diameter immediately following the counterbore symbol. Do not use the.312 main-hole diameter."},
{"title":"Read the counterbore depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-counterbore-depth","text":"Read the counterbore depth. An inch drawing gives 3X ⌀.312 THRU ⌴ ⌀.625 ↧.187. How far below the entry surface is the counterbore floor? Give three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning.187 in. The depth symbol follows the counterbore diameter. The main hole continues through; the recess stops at this depth."},
{"title":"Find the full-diameter blind depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-blind-depth","text":"Find the full-diameter blind depth. A hole note reads ⌀.24 ↧.48 on an inch drawing. A pointed drill bottom is shown. What is the stated depth of the full-diameter portion? Give two decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning.48 in. The depth is to the end of the cylindrical, full-diameter portion. Do not add an assumed drill-point length to the stated value."},
{"title":"Read the included angle","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-countersink-angle","text":"Read the included angle. Read ⌀.22 THRU / ⌵ ⌀.46 × 82°. What is the included countersink angle? Enter whole degrees. Answer in degrees Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 82°. This is the angle across both sides of the cone. 41° would be the half-angle, not the specified included angle."},
{"title":"Read the number of places","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-repeat-count","text":"Read the number of places. A note reads 6X ⌀.18 THRU and points to the identified hole pattern. How many holes share the stated diameter and end condition? Answer in holes Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 6 holes. 6X is a count, not a multiplier applied to the.18 diameter. The drawing must also locate the pattern."},
{"title":"Calculate slot center spacing","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-slot-length","text":"Calculate slot center spacing. An original straight slot has semicircular ends,.50 in width and 1.90 in overall length. What is the distance between the end centers? Give two decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 1.40 in. Subtract the two end radii, or one full width: 1.90 −.50 = 1.40. Subtracting just one radius gives 1.65, which is incorrect. This is a geometry check, not a reason to add a third controlling dimension to the drawing."},
{"title":"Read a decimal-inch value","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-inch-decimal","text":"Read a decimal-inch value. The drawing states that dimensions are in inches. A feature is labeled.625. Enter its value in inches to three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning 0.625 in (also written.625 ). The omitted leading zero follows decimal-inch drawing convention. There is no conversion to millimeters in this question."},
{"title":"Keep the decimal places","page":"Practice","summary":"Hole and Feature Callouts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimension-trailing-zeros","text":"Keep the decimal places. The same nominal length appears as.40 in one drawing and.400 in another. What must you check before treating their requirements as equivalent? Whether.400 is ten times larger. Each drawing’s applicable tolerance block and local tolerance. Whether extra zeros make the actual part mathematically exact. Choice number Choose an answer 1. Whether.400 is ten times larger. 2. Each drawing’s applicable tolerance block and local tolerance. 3. Whether extra zeros make the actual part mathematically exact. Check answer Choose or enter an answer, then check it. Show the reasoning Choice 2. Their nominal lengths are equal, but the general tolerance blocks or local tolerances may assign different limits. No universal tolerance can be inferred from the number of decimal places alone."},
{"title":"A graphic for brainstorming","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-graphic-ideation","text":"A graphic for brainstorming: A team wants to compare three possible handle arrangements before choosing dimensions. Which graphic best supports a quick discussion? A fully released drawing set Labeled ideation sketches A completed inspection report Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Quick sketches let the team compare alternatives without pretending that dimensions and requirements are final."},
{"title":"A graphic for a fit review","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-graphic-fit","text":"A graphic for a fit review: You need to examine how several proposed components occupy the available space. Which is the most useful starting graphic? An exploded view of the assembly A detail drawing of the largest part A CAD assembly model Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. An assembly model represents component geometry and arrangement. It supports a fit review, but does not by itself prove every functional requirement. An exploded view separates the components on purpose, so it cannot show whether they clash in their assembled positions."},
{"title":"The governing convention","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineering Graphics in the Design Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-drawing-standard","text":"The governing convention: A drawing arrives from another country. How should you decide its projection convention? Read its stated projection method and governing requirements Assume every drawing from that country is identical Use whichever arrangement is familiar Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Country is not a substitute for the drawing requirements. Resolve a missing or conflicting declaration before using the drawing."},
{"title":"A drawing for manufacture","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-drawing-part","text":"A drawing for manufacture: A supplier needs to make and inspect one custom spacer. Which drawing provides the part requirements? An assembly overview with no part dimensions An unannotated pictorial image A complete detail (part) drawing Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The part drawing defines the spacer through the necessary geometry, dimensions, tolerances, material, and notes."},
{"title":"A drawing for assembly","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-drawing-assembly","text":"A drawing for assembly: A technician needs to identify components and see how they fit together. Which drawing should lead the package? An assembly drawing with the required component identification A material certificate alone Only an isolated part drawing Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The assembly drawing communicates relationships among components. Supporting part drawings or instructions may also be required."},
{"title":"Find the dimension units","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-title-units","text":"Find the dimension units: A drawing shows a length value of 40 without a unit beside it. Where should you first look for the drawing-wide unit declaration? The revision description The units field or general units note The sheet count Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The units declaration explains how unlabeled dimension values are interpreted. Check for any explicitly stated exceptions."},
{"title":"Interpret sheet scale","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-title-scale","text":"Interpret sheet scale: A view uses the stated sheet scale 1:2. Its dimension reads 60 mm. What is the required nominal length? 30 mm 120 mm 60 mm Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Scale changes the displayed size of the view, not the dimensioned part size. Use the dimension, not a ruler measurement of the image."},
{"title":"Locate the material requirement","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-title-material","text":"Locate the material requirement: Two parts look identical in shaded CAD images. Where should you look to determine the specified material? The material field and any referenced material specification The shading color alone The drawing scale Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Appearance does not establish material. Read the material requirement and its referenced specification."},
{"title":"Confirm the authorized revision","page":"Practice","summary":"Working Drawings and Title Blocks","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-title-revision","text":"Confirm the authorized revision: You find two PDFs with the same drawing number but different revisions. Which action determines the correct one for the job? Choose the file with the newest download date Match the revision to the authorized release record Choose the shorter file Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The authorized release record identifies the revision required for the job. A download date is not approval."},
{"title":"Derive a related view","page":"Practice","summary":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-base-view","text":"Derive a related view: A base view is already linked to the intended model. You need an aligned adjacent view. What is the sound next step? Insert a second base view from the model and line it up by eye Stretch a copy of the base view Create a projected view from the base Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Deriving the projected view preserves the model relationship and intended alignment. A second independent base view shows correct geometry but is not tied to its neighbour, so its alignment and scale can drift when the sheet changes. Check its placement against the projection method."},
{"title":"Change geometry deliberately","page":"Practice","summary":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-dimension-control","text":"Change geometry deliberately: A part must be longer. You are considering typing a larger number over a drawing dimension label. What should you do? Change the controlling model geometry, then update and inspect the drawing Override the label and assume the part changed Change only the sheet scale Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. A text override can misrepresent unchanged geometry. Use the model control and inspect the linked drawing dimensions after updating."},
{"title":"Review a model change","page":"Practice","summary":"Creating Drawings from CAD Models","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#design-model-update","text":"Review a model change: A referenced model changes after you exported a drawing PDF. What should happen before issuing the revised drawing? Send the old PDF because all exports update automatically Update the drawing as needed, review affected dimensions and notes, then export and check the approved revision Change only the PDF filename Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Associativity helps update the native drawing, subject to its references and settings. It does not review the drawing or update an already exported PDF."},
{"title":"Estimate an inch cutting-tap drill","page":"Practice","summary":"Tap Drills and Tapping","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-tap-inch","text":"Estimate an inch cutting-tap drill. Estimate the cutting-tap drill for a 5/16-18 thread using major diameter minus 1/TPI. Give the mathematical estimate to four decimal places in inches, before choosing a stock drill. Answer (in) Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation 5/16 = 0.3125 in; pitch = 1/18 in. Subtracting gives 0.256944… in, or 0.2569 in. The number 18 is TPI, not a length. Starting with a minor diameter would subtract thread depth twice."},
{"title":"Estimate a metric cutting-tap drill","page":"Practice","summary":"Tap Drills and Tapping","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-tap-metric","text":"Estimate a metric cutting-tap drill. Estimate the cutting-tap drill for M10 × 1.5 using major diameter minus pitch. Give the mathematical estimate to two decimal places in millimeters. Answer (mm) Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The nominal major diameter is 10 mm and pitch is 1.5 mm. 10 − 1.5 = 8.50 mm. Taking 1/1.5 treats a pitch as though it were TPI and gives the wrong dimension."},
{"title":"Reason about thread percentage","page":"Practice","summary":"Tap Drills and Tapping","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-thread-percent","text":"Reason about thread percentage. Two holes will receive the same cutting tap. Within the permitted hole-size range, what generally happens when the prepared hole diameter increases? Choose an answer Select one Thread height decreases and tapping torque generally decreases. Thread height increases because more room is available. The screw engagement length must increase by the same percentage. Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Thread height decreases and tapping torque generally decreases. The larger hole leaves less material for the tap to cut. Engagement length is an axial distance and is a separate design choice; thread percentage is not a strength percentage."},
{"title":"Choose the correct tap-drill reference","page":"Practice","summary":"Tap Drills and Tapping","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-form-tap","text":"Choose the correct tap-drill reference. A process changes from a cutting tap to a forming tap for the same thread. Which preparation is appropriate? Choose an answer Select one Keep the cutting-tap drill because the thread name is unchanged. Use a smaller hole so the forming tap has more metal to move. Use the forming-tap maker’s hole-size and material guidance. Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Use the forming-tap guidance. Forming displaces material rather than removing chips and generally needs a larger starting hole. The cutting-tap approximation is not a forming-tap specification."},
{"title":"Read an internal Unified thread","page":"Practice","summary":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-callout-inch","text":"Read an internal Unified thread. Which reading of 3/8-16 UNC-2B is correct? Choose an answer Select one 0.375 in nominal major diameter; 16 TPI; Unified coarse; class 2 internal. 0.375 in tap-drill diameter; 16 mm pitch; Unified fine; class 2 external. 0.375 in nominal major diameter; 16 TPI; Unified coarse; grade 2 bolt. Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation 0.375 in nominal major diameter, 16 TPI, Unified coarse, class 2 internal. B identifies an internal thread. The major diameter is not the tap-drill diameter, and thread class is not strength grade."},
{"title":"Read an external fine thread","page":"Practice","summary":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-callout-external","text":"Read an external fine thread. Which reading of 3/8-24 UNF-3A is correct? Choose an answer Select one 0.375 in; 24 TPI; Unified coarse; class 3 internal. 0.375 in; 24 TPI; Unified fine; class 3 external. 0.375 in; 24 mm pitch; Unified fine; class 3 external. Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation 0.375 in nominal major diameter, 24 TPI, Unified fine, class 3 external. A identifies the external thread. The class concerns dimensional fit; it is not the fastener material grade."},
{"title":"Read a metric thread pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-callout-metric","text":"Read a metric thread pitch. What does M8 × 1.25 tell you? Choose an answer Select one 8 mm drill diameter and 1.25 TPI. 8 in major diameter and 1.25 mm pitch. Metric thread, 8 mm nominal major diameter and 1.25 mm pitch; no tolerance class shown. Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Metric thread, 8 mm nominal major diameter and 1.25 mm pitch. The callout shown does not state a tolerance class. The pitch is a spacing, not a count per inch."},
{"title":"Convert TPI to pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Thread Callouts and Threaded Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-thread-pitch","text":"Convert TPI to pitch. A single-start thread has 20 threads per inch. What is its axial pitch? Give inches to three decimal places. Answer (in) Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Pitch = 1/20 = 0.050 in. Because this example is single-start, its lead is also 0.050 in per revolution. TPI itself is not a travel distance."},
{"title":"Calculate drill RPM from an inch diameter","page":"Practice","summary":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-rpm-inch","text":"Calculate drill RPM from an inch diameter. For a calculation exercise, assume a cutting speed of 60 ft/min and a drill diameter of 0.375 in. Use the exact-π RPM relationship in Feeds and Speeds, not the rounded shortcut. Give the theoretical speed to the nearest whole RPM. The assumed cutting speed is not a recommendation for an unidentified material. Answer (rpm) Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Using the surface-speed relationship, substitute 60 ft/min and 0.375 in with the inch-to-foot conversion. The result is 611.15498… rpm, or 611 rpm. Use the cutting diameter, not a thread minor diameter or the radius. A real setup still needs suitable cutting data and an available spindle setting."},
{"title":"Calculate drill RPM from a metric diameter","page":"Practice","summary":"Drilling on a Drill Press or Mill","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-rpm-metric","text":"Calculate drill RPM from a metric diameter. For a calculation exercise, assume 24 m/min surface speed and an 8 mm drill. Use the exact-π relationship in Feeds and Speeds. Give theoretical RPM to the nearest whole number. These inputs are assumed, not a material recommendation. Answer (rpm) Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Using the surface-speed relationship with consistent length units gives 954.92966… rpm, or 955 rpm. Forgetting the meter-to-millimeter conversion produces an answer a thousand times too small."},
{"title":"Recess a cylindrical screw head","page":"Practice","summary":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-operation-counterbore","text":"Recess a cylindrical screw head. The drawing calls for a cylindrical recess with a flat shoulder around a smaller through hole, so a screw head can sit below the surface. Which operation matches? Choose an answer Select one Ream Bore Counterbore Countersink Spot face Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Counterbore. Its cylindrical recess and flat shoulder provide the required head space. A countersink would create a cone."},
{"title":"Finish a nearly sized pin hole","page":"Practice","summary":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-operation-ream","text":"Finish a nearly sized pin hole. A hole is on location and has the recommended small finishing allowance. The goal is its final diameter and improved surface finish for a pin. Which operation best matches this plan? Choose an answer Select one Ream Bore Counterbore Countersink Spot face Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Ream. A reamer removes a small allowance to improve size and finish. It should not be expected to relocate a badly misplaced hole."},
{"title":"Make a local bearing seat","page":"Practice","summary":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-operation-spotface","text":"Make a local bearing seat. A rough surface around a hole needs a shallow flat area for a washer to bear on. No deep head recess is required. Which operation best describes the purpose? Choose an answer Select one Ream Bore Counterbore Countersink Spot face Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Spot face. It creates a local flat bearing surface. A counterbore may use similar tooling but serves a specified recess."},
{"title":"Correct an existing hole axis","page":"Practice","summary":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-operation-bore","text":"Correct an existing hole axis. An existing hole needs its axis corrected relative to the machine setup. Enough material remains for the intended corrected bore to clean up. Which operation offers control of axis and diameter? Choose an answer Select one Ream Bore Counterbore Countersink Spot face Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Bore. The boring tool path can be set from the machine axis. A reamer tends to follow the existing hole."},
{"title":"Make a conical screw seat","page":"Practice","summary":"Reaming, Boring and Hole Seats","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#drilling-operation-countersink","text":"Make a conical screw seat. The specified flat-head screw needs a matching conical seat. Which operation makes that feature? Choose an answer Select one Ream Bore Counterbore Countersink Spot face Check answer Enter or choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Countersink. Select its angle and diameter from the specified head and drawing; the thread size alone does not specify the seat."},
{"title":"Hold without feedback","page":"Practice","summary":"Electric Motor Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-open-loop-hold","text":"Hold without feedback: An energized mechanism must hold at commanded steps without a feedback sensor, within its load rating. Which is the best fit? 1 = ordinary brushed DC motor alone; 2 = stepper with driver; 3 = servo system. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: stepper with driver. It can hold a commanded step while energized. Excessive load can cause missed steps, and open-loop control cannot verify position."},
{"title":"Electronic commutation","page":"Practice","summary":"Electric Motor Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-no-brushes","text":"Electronic commutation: A fan motor has permanent magnets on its rotor, electronic phase switching, and no brushes to wear. Identify it: 1 = brushed DC; 2 = squirrel-cage induction; 3 = brushless permanent-magnet motor. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3: brushless permanent-magnet motor. The electronic drive switches stator current. No brushes alone would not uniquely identify it: induction motors and steppers can also be brushless."},
{"title":"Simple DC operation","page":"Practice","summary":"Electric Motor Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-simple-dc","text":"Simple DC operation: A low-cost motor must rotate from a DC supply without an external electronic commutation drive. Periodic brush wear is acceptable. Choose: 1 = permanent-magnet brushed DC; 2 = BLDC without a drive; 3 = open-loop stepper without a drive. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1: permanent-magnet brushed DC. Its commutator and brushes switch rotor current mechanically."},
{"title":"Correct position error","page":"Practice","summary":"Electric Motor Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-feedback","text":"Correct position error: A machine measures shaft position and adjusts motor drive to reduce the difference from the command. Identify the control: 1 = open-loop stepper; 2 = servo; 3 = fixed DC supply alone. Enter the choice number. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: servo. Feedback closes the loop. Servo describes the controlled system, not a single motor construction."},
{"title":"Speed at constant load","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-load-speed","text":"Speed at constant load: Assume a fixed-voltage linear torque-speed curve with stall torque 0.8 N·m and no-load speed 3,000 rpm. What steady speed results at a constant resisting load of 0.2 N·m? Give whole rpm. Answer (rpm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n = 3,000(1 − 0.2/0.8) = 2,250 rpm. One-quarter stall torque leaves three-quarters no-load speed."},
{"title":"Power at the load point","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-load-power","text":"Power at the load point: Assume a fixed-voltage linear torque-speed curve with stall torque 0.8 N·m and no-load speed 3,000 rpm. Find mechanical output power at a constant 0.2 N·m load. Give watts to two decimal places; carry intermediate values to at least four significant figures. Answer (W) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n = 3,000(1 − 0.2/0.8) = 2,250 rpm. ω = 2,250 × 2π/60 = 235.619… rad/s. P = 0.2 × 235.619… = 47.12 W. Using 235.6 rad/s also gives 47.12 W. Multiplying torque by rpm directly gives 450, which is not watts."},
{"title":"Speed of peak power","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-peak-speed","text":"Speed of peak power: An ideal linear motor curve has 1.2 N·m stall torque and 3,600 rpm no-load speed. At what speed is shaft output power greatest? Give whole rpm. Answer (rpm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Peak power occurs at half no-load speed: 3,600/2 = 1,800 rpm. At 3,600 rpm the external torque is zero, so shaft output power is zero."},
{"title":"Maximum shaft power","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-peak-power","text":"Maximum shaft power: An ideal linear motor curve has 1.2 N·m stall torque and 3,600 rpm no-load speed. What is its maximum mechanical output power? Give watts to one decimal place; carry intermediate values to at least four significant figures. Answer (W) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution At half speed and half torque: n = 1,800 rpm and T = 0.6 N·m. P = 0.6 × (1,800 × 2π/60) = 113.097… = 113.1 W. Stall torque × no-load angular speed would give 452.4 W: those endpoints cannot occur together."},
{"title":"Find the terminal connection","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-terminals","text":"Find the terminal connection. An assembler needs to connect a wire to the correct numbered terminal. Which drawing is the best starting point? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Block 2. Wiring 3. Riser Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Wiring drawings identify physical terminal connections."},
{"title":"Explain the circuit function","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-function","text":"Explain the circuit function. A technician wants to follow component connections to understand electrical operation. Which drawing is the best starting point? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Schematic 2. Pictorial 3. Floor plan Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A schematic shows electrical relationships using component symbols."},
{"title":"Recognize the parts","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-parts","text":"Recognize the parts. A beginner wants to match visible parts with their appearances in a drawing. Choose a view. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Riser 2. Logic 3. Pictorial Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A pictorial emphasizes recognizable appearances."},
{"title":"Explain system functions","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-block","text":"Explain system functions. A designer wants to explain source, control, current limiting, and light output without drawing each terminal. Choose a view. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Block 2. Wiring 3. PCB layout Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A block diagram groups functions and shows their relationships."},
{"title":"Route board copper","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-board","text":"Route board copper. A board designer needs to place footprints and route copper traces. Choose a drawing. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Schematic 2. PCB layout 3. Riser Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The PCB layout defines physical board placement and routing."},
{"title":"Trace a control rung","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-ladder","text":"Trace a control rung. A controls technician needs to follow conditions arranged in rungs between rails. Choose a drawing. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Pictorial 2. Floor plan 3. Ladder Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Ladder diagrams arrange control logic in rungs."},
{"title":"Trace Boolean operations","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-gates","text":"Trace Boolean operations. A designer wants to follow AND and OR operations between logical inputs and outputs. Choose a drawing. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Logic 2. Riser 3. Wiring Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A logic diagram emphasizes gates and logical relationships."},
{"title":"Locate room fixtures","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-floor","text":"Locate room fixtures. An installer wants to locate fixtures within a room and decode their marks using a legend. Choose a drawing. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Block 2. Electrical floor plan 3. IC layout Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation An electrical floor plan locates building devices and uses its legend to explain marks."},
{"title":"Follow distribution between floors","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Diagram Types","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-job-riser","text":"Follow distribution between floors. A designer wants to see how distribution equipment relates across building levels. Choose a drawing. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Pictorial 2. Logic 3. Riser Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A riser emphasizes distribution between levels."},
{"title":"Read an undotted crossing","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-crossing","text":"Read an undotted crossing. In the right-hand example below, the legend says only dotted crossings join. Are the horizontal and vertical wires connected at the crossing? Left drawing: dotted cross. Right drawing: undotted cross. Only dotted crossings join; the drawings are separate. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Yes 2. No Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation No. Under the stated convention, the undotted crossing keeps the conductors separate."},
{"title":"Read a dotted crossing","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-joined","text":"Read a dotted crossing. In the left-hand example below, the legend says a filled dot joins crossing conductors. Are the horizontal and vertical wires connected? Left drawing: dotted cross. Right drawing: undotted cross. Only dotted crossings join; the drawings are separate. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Yes 2. No Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Yes. The filled dot explicitly joins them."},
{"title":"Count nodes in a wire drawing","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-nodes","text":"Count nodes in a wire drawing. Count all nodes in the two separate wire drawings below, taken together. Only dotted crossings join, and there are no off-page connections. Left drawing: dotted cross. Right drawing: undotted cross. Only dotted crossings join; the drawings are separate. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. 2 2. 3 3. 5 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation There are 3 nodes: one in the dotted cross, plus two in the undotted cross. Bends and dots do not add nodes."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 1","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-battery","text":"Identify symbol 1. Which component or connection does this common form represent? + Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Battery 2. Capacitor 3. Fuse Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The repeated long and short plates represent cells in a battery. The long plate is positive."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 2","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-resistor-us","text":"Identify symbol 2. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Fuse 2. Resistor 3. Switch Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The zigzag is a widely taught US resistor form."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 3","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-resistor-iec","text":"Identify symbol 3. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Lamp 2. Capacitor 3. Resistor Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The rectangle is a widely taught international resistor form."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 4","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-switch","text":"Identify symbol 4. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Switch 2. Diode 3. Fuse Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The blade does not touch the second contact, so this switch is open."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 5","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-lamp","text":"Identify symbol 5. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Motor 2. Lamp 3. LED Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The circle with a cross is a common lamp symbol."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 6","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-led","text":"Identify symbol 6. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Diode 2. Lamp 3. LED Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The outward arrows distinguish a light-emitting diode from an ordinary diode."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 7","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-buzzer","text":"Identify symbol 7. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Buzzer 2. Motor 3. Capacitor Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation This common sounder form uses curved sound-wave marks. Check the drawing legend for the exact device."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 8","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-ground","text":"Identify symbol 8. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Battery 2. Ground / reference 3. Capacitor Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The descending bars mark a ground or reference connection. The drawing must establish its meaning; do not assume protective earth."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 9","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-fuse","text":"Identify symbol 9. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Resistor 2. Switch 3. Fuse Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation This common fuse form has a line through the rectangular body. Use the legend to distinguish variants."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 10","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-motor","text":"Identify symbol 10. Which component or connection does this common form represent? M Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Motor 2. Lamp 3. Buzzer Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The M identifies a motor in this common circular form."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 11","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-capacitor","text":"Identify symbol 11. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. Battery 2. Capacitor 3. Fuse Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Two equal parallel plates represent an unpolarized capacitor."},
{"title":"Identify symbol 12","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-symbol-diode","text":"Identify symbol 12. Which component or connection does this common form represent? Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. LED 2. Switch 3. Diode Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The bar identifies the cathode. Unlike the LED, this symbol has no light-emission arrows."},
{"title":"Count nodes with S1 open","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-component-nodes","text":"Count nodes with S1 open. Count the nodes with S1 open as drawn. Wires are ideal, and there are no off-page connections. B1 battery, open S1, R1 resistor and D1 LED in one loop. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. 2 2. 3 3. 4 4. 5 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Open S1 gives four conductor groups: B1+–S1, S1–R1, R1–D1 anode, and D1 cathode–B1−. The answer is 4; component bodies and wire bends do not add nodes."},
{"title":"Count nodes with S1 closed","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Electrical Connections","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#diagram-component-nodes-closed","text":"Count nodes with S1 closed. Count the nodes after replacing the open S1 gap with an ideal closed connection. Wires are ideal, and there are no off-page connections. B1 battery, open S1, R1 resistor and D1 LED in one loop. Choose the numbered answer Select one 1. 2 2. 3 3. 4 4. 5 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Open S1 gives four conductor groups: B1+–S1, S1–R1, R1–D1 anode, and D1 cathode–B1−. Closing S1 merges the first two groups, so the answer is 3."},
{"title":"Current in a control circuit","page":"Practice","summary":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#electrical-control-circuit-current","text":"1. Current in a control circuit: A resistor in a control circuit is 470 &Omega;. What current flows when 24 V is applied across it? Give the answer in milliamperes. Answer in mA Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Pick the form with the two quantities you have: I = V/R = 24 V &divide; 470 &Omega; = 0.051064 A. Then move the prefix: 0.051064 A &times; 1,000 mA 1 A = 51.06 mA The current is 51.1 mA. Check the units rather than trusting the arithmetic: an ohm is a volt per ampere, so volts divided by ohms leaves amperes. And mind the case - mA is a milliampere, while MA would be a megaampere, a billion times larger."},
{"title":"Voltage drop along a cord","page":"Practice","summary":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#electrical-cord-voltage-drop","text":"2. Voltage drop along a cord: A portable tool draws 12 A through an extension cord. Both of the cord's conductors are in the current path - out to the tool and back - and together they add 0.32 &Omega; to the circuit. What is the voltage drop across the cord? Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution V = IR = 12 A &times; 0.32 &Omega; = 3.84 V. The drop is 3.84 V, and that is 3.84 V that never reaches the tool: the supply is divided between the cord and the load, and the cord takes its share first. Read the resistance figure carefully before using it. A cord carries the current out on one conductor and back on the other, so what belongs in this calculation is the resistance of the whole path. A table that gives resistance per unit length gives it for a single conductor; use that figure alone for a cord and the answer comes out about half of what a meter across the ends would show."},
{"title":"Resistance from a nameplate","page":"Practice","summary":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#electrical-heater-resistance","text":"3. Resistance from a nameplate: A resistive heating element is rated 1,500 W at 120 V. What resistance does that rating imply? Answer in Ω Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The two quantities given are power and voltage, so use R = V 2 /P. R = (120 V) 2 &divide; 1,500 W = 14,400 &divide; 1,500 = 9.6 &Omega;. The rating implies 9.6 &Omega;. Two steps get to the same place if you prefer: I = P/V = 12.5 A, then R = V/I = 120 &divide; 12.5 = 9.6 &Omega;. This is the resistance at the rated operating point. A metal heating element is not the same resistance cold as it is hot, so a meter reading on an unpowered element will not match the nameplate figure exactly."},
{"title":"Heat lost in a cable run","page":"Practice","summary":"Current, Voltage, and Resistance","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#electrical-cable-heat-loss","text":"4. Heat lost in a cable run: A cable run carries 25 A and has a total resistance of 0.18 &Omega;. How much power is lost as heat in it? Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P = I 2 R = (25 A) 2 &times; 0.18 &Omega; = 625 &times; 0.18 = 112.5 W. The run loses 112.5 W as heat, spread along its whole length. Notice the squared current. Halve the load to 12.5 A and the loss does not halve, it quarters, to 28.1 W. That is why a drop that is harmless on a light load becomes a real problem on a heavy one, and why the length and size of a conductor matter more the more current the load draws."},
{"title":"Resistance of both wires","page":"Practice","summary":"Wire and Wire Gauge","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wire-loop-resistance","text":"Resistance of both wires: Use AWG 18 copper with a 50 ft one-way run and an equal return conductor. Find the loop resistance. Assume solid copper at 20 °C, DC/resistive behavior and no connection resistance. Use d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm, A = πd²/4, ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m, and 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly. Carry at least four significant figures through intermediate steps and report four significant figures (or an integer gauge). Answer in Ω Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Count both wires: 2 × 50 × 0.3048 = 30.48 m. For AWG 18, d = 1.024 mm and A = 0.8230 mm² = 8.230 × 10 −7 m² (values shown rounded from the formula). R = ρ(2ℓ)/A = (1.724 × 10⁻⁸)(30.48)/(8.230 × 10⁻⁷) = 0.6385 Ω. Answer: 0.6385 Ω."},
{"title":"Voltage lost along a run","page":"Practice","summary":"Wire and Wire Gauge","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wire-run-drop","text":"Voltage lost along a run: Use AWG 18 copper with a 50 ft one-way run and an equal return conductor. At 5 A, find the voltage drop. Assume solid copper at 20 °C, DC/resistive behavior and no connection resistance. Use d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm, A = πd²/4, ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m, and 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly. Carry at least four significant figures through intermediate steps and report four significant figures (or an integer gauge). Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Count both wires: 2 × 50 × 0.3048 = 30.48 m. For AWG 18, d = 1.024 mm and A = 0.8230 mm² = 8.230 × 10 −7 m² (values shown rounded from the formula). V = IR = 5 × 0.6385 = 3.192 V (using unrounded resistance). Answer: 3.192 V."},
{"title":"Percentage of source voltage lost","page":"Practice","summary":"Wire and Wire Gauge","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wire-percent-drop","text":"Percentage of source voltage lost: Use AWG 18 copper with a 50 ft one-way run and an equal return conductor. At 5 A with a 24 V source, find the percent voltage drop. Assume solid copper at 20 °C, DC/resistive behavior and no connection resistance. Use d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm, A = πd²/4, ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m, and 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly. Carry at least four significant figures through intermediate steps and report four significant figures (or an integer gauge). Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Count both wires: 2 × 50 × 0.3048 = 30.48 m. For AWG 18, d = 1.024 mm and A = 0.8230 mm² = 8.230 × 10 −7 m² (values shown rounded from the formula). Percent drop = 100 × 3.192/24 = 13.30%. Use source voltage as the denominator. Answer: 13.30 %."},
{"title":"Voltage remaining at the load","page":"Practice","summary":"Wire and Wire Gauge","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wire-load-voltage","text":"Voltage remaining at the load: Use AWG 18 copper with a 50 ft one-way run and an equal return conductor. At 5 A with a 24 V source, find the load voltage. Assume solid copper at 20 °C, DC/resistive behavior and no connection resistance. Use d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm, A = πd²/4, ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m, and 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly. Carry at least four significant figures through intermediate steps and report four significant figures (or an integer gauge). Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Count both wires: 2 × 50 × 0.3048 = 30.48 m. For AWG 18, d = 1.024 mm and A = 0.8230 mm² = 8.230 × 10 −7 m² (values shown rounded from the formula). Vload = 24 − 3.192 = 20.81 V. Answer: 20.81 V."},
{"title":"Smallest wire meeting a drop target","page":"Practice","summary":"Wire and Wire Gauge","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wire-smallest-gauge","text":"Smallest wire meeting a drop target: For a 24 V source, 5 A and a 50 ft one-way run, choose the smallest conductor from AWG 10, 12, 14, 16 and 18 that keeps voltage drop below 6%. Enter its AWG number. Assume solid copper at 20 °C, DC/resistive behavior and no connection resistance. Use d = 0.127 × 92 (36 − n)/39 mm, A = πd²/4, ρ = 1.724 × 10 −8 Ω·m, and 1 ft = 0.3048 m exactly. Carry at least four significant figures through intermediate steps and report four significant figures (or an integer gauge). Answer in AWG Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Count both wires: 2 × 50 × 0.3048 = 30.48 m. For AWG 18, d = 1.024 mm and A = 0.8230 mm² = 8.230 × 10 −7 m² (values shown rounded from the formula). The drops for AWG 10, 12, 14, 16 and 18 are 2.081%, 3.309%, 5.261%, 8.365% and 13.30%. AWG 14 passes; the next smaller conductor, AWG 16, fails. Smaller conductor means a larger AWG number. The 6% target is chosen for this exercise, not a code limit; ampacity still requires a separate check. Answer: 14 AWG."},
{"title":"Energy from capacity","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-energy-wh","text":"Energy from capacity: An ideal cell is rated 2.35 Ah at a constant 3.7 V. Find its energy in Wh. Round to four significant figures. Answer in Wh Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 2.35 Ah × 3.7 V = 8.695 Wh. Ah alone is charge, not energy."},
{"title":"Watt-hours to joules","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-energy-joules","text":"Watt-hours to joules: Convert 8.695 Wh to joules. Round to four significant figures. Answer in J Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 8.695 × 3600 = 31,302 J, or 31,300 J to four significant figures. A factor of 1000 converts kWh to Wh; it does not convert Wh to J."},
{"title":"Current from a discharge rating","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-c-rate","text":"Current from a discharge rating: A 2.35 Ah cell has a maximum continuous discharge rating of 4C. What is its rated current estimate? Answer in A Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 4 h⁻¹ × 2.35 Ah = 9.4 A. This is a rated limit, not a claim that the cell always supplies that current."},
{"title":"Ideal constant-current runtime","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-runtime","text":"Ideal constant-current runtime: An ideal 4.7 Ah pack supplies a constant 1.8 A. Find runtime in hours, to four significant figures. Assume all rated charge is usable. Answer in h Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 4.7 Ah ÷ 1.8 A = 2.61111… h, or 2.611 h. That is about 156.7 minutes; enter hours."},
{"title":"Voltage of a 4s3p pack","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-pack-voltage","text":"Voltage of a 4s3p pack: Matched 3.6 V, 2.5 Ah cells form a 4s3p ideal pack: four cells per series string and three strings in parallel. Find pack voltage. Answer in V Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 4 × 3.6 V = 14.4 V. The three parallel strings do not multiply the voltage."},
{"title":"Capacity of a 4s3p pack","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-pack-capacity","text":"Capacity of a 4s3p pack: Matched 3.6 V, 2.5 Ah cells form a 4s3p ideal pack. Find pack capacity in Ah. Answer in Ah Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 3 × 2.5 Ah = 7.5 Ah. Series adds voltage; parallel adds charge capacity."},
{"title":"Energy of a 4s3p pack","page":"Practice","summary":"Battery Capacity and Packs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-pack-energy","text":"Energy of a 4s3p pack: Matched 3.6 V, 2.5 Ah cells form a 4s3p ideal pack. Find nominal energy in Wh. Answer in Wh Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Pack voltage is 14.4 V and capacity is 7.5 Ah. Their product is 108 Wh. Check another way: 12 cells × 3.6 V × 2.5 Ah = 108 Wh."},
{"title":"Choose a primary cell","page":"Practice","summary":"Energy Storage Methods","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-primary-choice","text":"Choose a primary cell: A clock is designed for a replaceable, non-rechargeable cell. Which category matches that requirement? Choose an answer Select one Primary Secondary Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Primary cells are intended for replacement after discharge, not recharging."},
{"title":"Choose a rechargeable battery","page":"Practice","summary":"Energy Storage Methods","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#battery-secondary-choice","text":"Choose a rechargeable battery: A portable instrument must be recharged repeatedly with a compatible charger. Which category is required? Choose an answer Select one Primary Secondary Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Secondary batteries are designed for repeated charging and discharging."},
{"title":"Storage using two reservoirs","page":"Practice","summary":"Energy Storage Methods","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#storage-reservoir-choice","text":"Storage using two reservoirs: A stationary storage project already has suitable upper and lower reservoirs. It proposes pumping water uphill and recovering energy downhill. Which method is described? Choose an answer Select one Pumped hydro Battery storage Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Pumped hydro stores gravitational potential energy. This identifies the process; it does not establish a cost advantage at every site."},
{"title":"Storage for a portable instrument","page":"Practice","summary":"Energy Storage Methods","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#storage-portable-choice","text":"Storage for a portable instrument: A handheld instrument needs reusable electrical energy storage that travels with it. Which option fits? Choose an answer Select one Pumped hydro Rechargeable battery Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution A rechargeable battery fits the portable load. Pumped hydro needs reservoirs and elevation difference."},
{"title":"Find axial strain along the fibers","page":"Practice","summary":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fiber-axis-strain","text":"Find axial strain: The illustrative lamina has E 1 = 40 GPa. A 0° patch carries σ x = 10 MPa. What axial strain does the elastic model predict, expressed as a percent? Answer in percent Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Use consistent units: 40 GPa = 40,000 MPa. Then ε x = σ x /E 1 = 10/40,000 = 0.00025. Multiply by 100 to express strain as a percent: 0.025%. Entering 0.00025 would leave the result as a dimensionless ratio rather than the requested percent."},
{"title":"Find effective stiffness from stress and strain","page":"Practice","summary":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fiber-45-stiffness","text":"Find effective axial stiffness: At 45°, the same patch carries 10 MPa axial stress and has axial strain 0.000775. What is E x = σ x /ε x in GPa? Answer in GPa Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 10 MPa / 0.000775 = 12,903.23 MPa = 12.90 GPa. Using 0.0775 as the strain would confuse percent with the dimensionless strain ratio."},
{"title":"Compare plus and minus 45 degrees","page":"Practice","summary":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fiber-angle-symmetry","text":"Compare +45° and −45°: For the same unidirectional lamina and uniform axial stress, which statement matches this elastic model? 1 = both axial strain and shear sign reverse; 2 = axial stiffness and axial strain match, while shear strain reverses sign; 3 = +45° is always stronger; 4 = neither orientation develops shear strain. Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. The axial compliance contains even powers of sine and cosine, so opposite angles have the same axial stiffness. Their extension–shear coupling is mirrored, so engineering shear strain changes sign."},
{"title":"State what the elastic lab can conclude","page":"Practice","summary":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fiber-stiffness-not-strength","text":"Interpret the result: The 0° patch stretches least under the same 10 MPa stress. What can this lab conclude? 1 = 0° has the greatest failure strength; 2 = 0° is the safest laminate; 3 = 0° has the greatest modeled axial elastic stiffness for these illustrative constants; 4 = every real composite should use only 0° fibers. Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show worked solution 3. Stress divided by elastic strain gives stiffness. Failure strength, damage, safety and laminate design need evidence and models this one-lamina elastic lab does not contain."},
{"title":"Convert axial stress to applied force","page":"Practice","summary":"Fiber Direction and Elastic Stiffness","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#fiber-stress-force","text":"Convert stress to force: A patch has loaded cross-sectional area 200 mm² and uniform axial stress 10 MPa. Since 1 MPa = 1 N/mm², what total axial force corresponds to that stress? Answer in N Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution F = σA = 10 N/mm² × 200 mm² = 2,000 N. Equal stress means equal force per area; total force changes if loaded area changes."},
{"title":"Decode all five fields","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-marking-fields","text":"Decode all five fields: A supplier defines A 60 J 7 V as aluminum oxide, grit 60, grade J, structure 7 and vitrified bond. Which mapping is correct? A = abrasive; 60 = grit; J = grade; 7 = structure; V = bond. A = bond; 60 = RPM; J = abrasive; 7 = diameter; V = grade. A = abrasive; 60 = grade; J = grit; 7 = bond; V = structure. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The fields run abrasive, grit, grade, structure, bond. The supplier key defines the codes; no maximum RPM is given here."},
{"title":"Interpret wheel grade","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-marking-grade","text":"Interpret wheel grade: In that supplier's A 60 J 7 V specification, what does J describe? The hardness of the workpiece. The resistance to releasing abrasive grains. The diameter of each grain. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Grade describes grain retention by the wheel, not the abrasive mineral's hardness or the workpiece's hardness."},
{"title":"Interpret wheel structure","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-marking-structure","text":"Interpret wheel structure: A supplier labels the fourth field of A 60 J 7 V as structure 7. What property does this field describe? Maximum operating RPM. Grain diameter in millimeters. Relative spacing between grains. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Structure describes grain spacing. Interpret its value with the supplier's key."},
{"title":"Interpret the bond field","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-marking-bond","text":"Interpret the bond field: A supplier defines V as vitrified in the last field of A 60 J 7 V. What does V identify? A glasslike binder. A coarse grit size. A permissible side load. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The final field identifies the bond. It does not establish permitted grinding surfaces or speed."},
{"title":"Compare conventional grit numbers","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-grit-comparison","text":"Compare conventional grit numbers: Two otherwise comparable wheels use grit 36 and grit 80 in the same conventional system. Which statement is the better starting point? 80 is coarser and always removes more material. 36 and 80 are wheel grades. 80 is finer and generally favors smaller scratches. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. A larger conventional grit number means finer grains. Actual finish still depends on dressing, feeds and material."},
{"title":"Choose a conventional abrasive for HSS","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-abrasive-steel","text":"Choose a conventional abrasive for HSS: For an HSS bit, which conventional abrasive is the usual starting choice between aluminum oxide and silicon carbide? Aluminum oxide. Silicon carbide because all harder abrasives suit all metals. Either, without checking the wheel specification. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Aluminum oxide is a general starting choice for steels including HSS. The complete wheel must still suit the operation."},
{"title":"Choose a conventional abrasive for cast iron","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-abrasive-cast-iron","text":"Choose a conventional abrasive for cast iron: Which is a common conventional abrasive starting choice for gray cast iron? Diamond is compulsory for every cast iron. Silicon carbide, with a suitable wheel specification. No abrasive can cut cast iron. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Silicon carbide is commonly used for cast iron. This is a family choice, not a complete wheel recommendation."},
{"title":"Choose a superabrasive for hardened steel","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-abrasive-cbn","text":"Choose a superabrasive for hardened steel: Which superabrasive is the general starting choice for hardened steel? Diamond because hardness alone decides compatibility. CBN. Neither can grind hardened material. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. CBN is commonly used for hardened steels. Diamond is generally associated with carbide and other hard nonferrous materials."},
{"title":"Choose a superabrasive for cemented carbide","page":"Practice","summary":"Grinding Wheels and Abrasives","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-abrasive-diamond","text":"Choose a superabrasive for cemented carbide: Which superabrasive is a general starting choice for cemented-carbide tool grinding? Any steel bench wheel. CBN solely because the tool is hard. Diamond in a suitable wheel and process. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Diamond is commonly used for cemented carbide. Wheel bond, shape and grinding conditions still need selection."},
{"title":"Reject an exceeded RPM limit","page":"Practice","summary":"Offhand Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-rpm-limit","text":"Reject an exceeded RPM limit: A machine runs at 3,450 RPM. A proposed wheel is marked maximum 3,200 RPM. What does the speed comparison establish? It passes because the difference is small. It fails: machine speed exceeds the printed maximum. It passes if the wheel has worn smaller. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. 3,450 > 3,200 by 250 RPM, so this speed combination fails. A smaller diameter does not authorize exceeding the marked RPM limit."},
{"title":"Separate a speed check from full approval","page":"Practice","summary":"Offhand Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-rpm-below-limit","text":"Separate a speed check from full approval: A machine runs at 1,750 RPM and a wheel is marked maximum 3,000 RPM. What follows? The speed comparison passes; other compatibility checks remain. Every mounting and application is now approved. The machine must run at 3,000 RPM. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. 1,750 is below 3,000. A maximum is a ceiling, not a required operating speed or complete approval."},
{"title":"Calculate wheel rim speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Offhand Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-rim-speed","text":"Calculate wheel rim speed: An illustrative 8 in diameter wheel turns at 1,800 RPM. Calculate SFPM using π × D × RPM / 12 and round to the nearest whole SFPM. This is a calculation, not a recommended machine setting. Answer in SFPM Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution π × 8 × 1,800 / 12 = 3,769.911… SFPM, or 3,770 SFPM. Omitting the division by 12 gives inches per minute, not feet per minute."},
{"title":"Calculate rim speed after diameter loss","page":"Practice","summary":"Offhand Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-worn-wheel-speed","text":"Calculate rim speed after diameter loss: An illustrative wheel now measures 6.5 in diameter and turns at 1,725 RPM. Calculate its rim speed to the nearest whole SFPM. Do not change the wheel's printed maximum RPM. Answer in SFPM Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution π × 6.5 × 1,725 / 12 = 2,935.426… SFPM, or 2,935 SFPM. Use the current diameter; using radius would halve the result."},
{"title":"Count full-face downfeed increments","page":"Practice","summary":"Precision Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-downfeed-count","text":"Count full-face downfeed increments: An idealized plan removes 0.018 in from one face at 0.0015 in per full-face layer. How many downfeed increments are needed? Assume exact removal, no dressing allowance and no spark-out. One layer can require several table strokes; these are arithmetic values, not settings. Answer in increments Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.018 / 0.0015 = 12 increments. The units of length cancel. This is not a count of individual table strokes."},
{"title":"Plan a smaller final increment","page":"Practice","summary":"Precision Grinding","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#grinding-last-downfeed","text":"Plan a smaller final increment: An idealized plan must remove 0.017 in from one face. Eight full-face layers have each removed exactly 0.002 in. What thickness remains for the final layer? Give inches to three decimal places. Ignore wheel wear and elastic effects; these are arithmetic values, not settings. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.017 − (8 × 0.002) = 0.001 in. A ninth full 0.002 in layer would remove too much. Use the smaller remainder in this idealized plan."},
{"title":"Left reading after a midpoint zero","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#indicator-rezero-left","text":"Left reading after a midpoint zero. Across a 100 mm sweep, an ideal straight jaw rises smoothly from 0.000 mm at the left end to +0.060 mm at the right. Moving the jaw toward the indicator body was demonstrated to increase the reading. You move to 50 mm and zero the indicator there. What will the left-end reading be? Give millimetres to three decimal places. Left reading (mm) Check answer Enter a reading, then check it. Show reasoning The midpoint raw reading is +0.030 mm. Zeroing subtracts that value from every position, so the left becomes 0.000 − 0.030 = −0.030 mm. The right becomes +0.030 mm. Right minus left is still +0.060 mm."},
{"title":"Sweep span after re-zeroing","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#indicator-rezero-span","text":"Sweep span after re-zeroing. The same ideal jaw reads −0.030 mm at the left, 0.000 mm at midspan and +0.030 mm at the right after zeroing at midspan. What is right minus left in millimetres? Right minus left (mm) Check answer Enter a difference, then check it. Show reasoning +0.030 − (−0.030) = +0.060 mm. Re-zeroing changes every displayed value by the same constant, so pairwise differences stay unchanged."},
{"title":"Equal endpoints with a center high spot","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#indicator-hidden-midspan","text":"Equal endpoints with a center high spot. The indicator reads 0.000 mm at both ends of a jaw and +0.080 mm at the center. Which conclusion is supported? The jaw is aligned because its endpoints match. The full sweep reveals a local high spot; inspect and clean the contact surface and check indicator stability before adjusting alignment. Tap one end until the center alone reads zero. Choice number (1–3) Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 2. Equal endpoints describe only two positions. A center departure is not the smooth linear trend corrected by moving one end. Inspect the local surface and setup, then repeat the full sweep."},
{"title":"What zeroing changes","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#indicator-zero-meaning","text":"What zeroing changes. Which statement describes zeroing an indicator at one position during a comparison sweep? It physically straightens the jaw at that position. It calibrates the instrument across its full range. It stores a reference reading that is subtracted from subsequent displayed readings. Choice number (1–3) Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 3. Zeroing establishes the displayed reference. It does not move the jaw, prove calibration or set the job's acceptance requirement."},
{"title":"GO plug will not enter","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-plug-go","text":"GO plug will not enter A clean, identified plain GO plug will not enter the specified inspection length of a hole using the approved method. Has this check passed? What should happen next? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning No. The GO plug should enter. Hold the part for investigation; an undersize hole is one possible cause, but form, burrs or alignment can also obstruct entry. Do not force the gauge or accept the part because the NO-GO end also stays out."},
{"title":"Both plug ends enter","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-plug-no-go","text":"Both plug ends enter The GO plug enters, and the NO-GO plug also enters beyond the engagement allowed by the plain-hole gauging procedure. Is the hole acceptable on this check? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning No. GO entry is required, but the NO-GO end must not enter beyond the permitted engagement. This is a failed upper-limit check; investigate and contain the affected work."},
{"title":"Which ring should pass?","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-ring","text":"Which ring should pass? For a plain shaft checked by an appropriate GO/NO-GO ring pair, which ring should pass over the specified length? What does a NO-GO ring passing mean? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning The GO ring should pass; it checks the large-shaft limit. The NO-GO ring should not pass under the specified procedure. If it passes, the shaft fails that lower-limit check. Correct gauge identity, condition and use still matter."},
{"title":"Bilateral tolerance decision","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-bilateral","text":"Bilateral tolerance decision An invented size is 18.00 ±0.03 mm. A reading is 18.025 mm. Treat the numbers as exact and include the limits. Enter 1 if within limits, 0 if outside. Answer (0 or 1) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Limits are 17.97 to 18.03 mm. 18.025 lies inside, so enter 1."},
{"title":"Unilateral tolerance decision","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-unilateral","text":"Unilateral tolerance decision An invented size is 12.000 +0.020/−0.000 mm. A reading is 11.995 mm. Treat the numbers as exact and include the limits. Enter 1 if within limits, 0 if outside. Answer (0 or 1) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Limits are 12.000 to 12.020 mm. 11.995 is below the lower limit, so enter 0. Do not incorrectly apply ±0.020."},
{"title":"Limit-dimension endpoint","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Inspection and Quality Control","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-limits","text":"Limit-dimension endpoint An invented drawing gives limits of 31.96 and 32.04 mm. A reading is 32.04 mm. Treat the numbers as exact and include the limits. Enter 1 if within limits, 0 if outside. Answer (0 or 1) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The upper endpoint is included, so enter 1. The exercise treats the given value as exact; real acceptance uses its specified uncertainty decision rule."},
{"title":"Mean of five readings","page":"Practice","summary":"Process Capability and Variation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-sample-mean","text":"Mean of five readings Five invented readings are 19.98, 20.00, 20.00, 20.03 and 20.04 mm. Find the mean to 0.01 mm. Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The sum is 100.05 mm. Divide by five: 100.05 / 5 = 20.01 mm. The middle observation alone is not the definition of mean."},
{"title":"Range of five readings","page":"Practice","summary":"Process Capability and Variation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-sample-range","text":"Range of five readings Five invented readings are 19.98, 20.00, 20.00, 20.03 and 20.04 mm. Find the range to 0.01 mm. Answer (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Largest minus smallest: 20.04 − 19.98 = 0.06 mm. Do not halve this difference; half-range is a different quantity."},
{"title":"Is the sample centred?","page":"Practice","summary":"Process Capability and Variation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-sample-centre","text":"Is the sample centred? Five invented readings are 19.98, 20.00, 20.00, 20.03 and 20.04 mm. The limits are 19.95 to 20.05 mm. Compare the sample mean with the limit midpoint. Does this prove capability? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning The mean is 20.01 mm; the midpoint is (19.95 + 20.05) / 2 = 20.00 mm. This sample is 0.01 mm above the midpoint, not exactly centred. Its readings all pass, but five readings cannot establish long-term capability."},
{"title":"Three primary supports","page":"Practice","summary":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-locators-primary","text":"Three primary supports A rigid block stays seated on three non-collinear underside supports in the XY plane. How many independent degrees of freedom are constrained by this primary arrangement? Give an exact count. Answer (count) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3: Z translation and rotations about X and Y. The block can still slide along X and Y and rotate about Z until the side and end locators establish the remaining constraints."},
{"title":"Add two side locators","page":"Practice","summary":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-locators-secondary","text":"Add two side locators After the primary supports, two separated side contacts locate the block against the XZ plane. How many additional independent degrees of freedom do these side contacts constrain while seated? Give an exact count. Answer (count) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: Y translation and rotation about Z. Five independent constraints are now established in total."},
{"title":"Complete the 3-2-1 arrangement","page":"Practice","summary":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-locators-total","text":"Complete the 3-2-1 arrangement A block is seated on three primary supports, against two secondary contacts and one tertiary end stop, all appropriately arranged. How many independent degrees of freedom are constrained in total? Give an exact count. Answer (count) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3 + 2 + 1 = 6. The final stop establishes X position. Clamps maintain seating; do not double the count for positive and negative directions."},
{"title":"Bushing-guided drilling","page":"Practice","summary":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-jig","text":"Bushing-guided drilling A device locates and clamps a plate, and a hardened bushing guides the drill into the hole position. Is it a jig or a fixture? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning It is a jig because it guides the cutting tool as well as holding and locating the part."},
{"title":"Program-guided milling","page":"Practice","summary":"Jigs, Fixtures and Repeatable Location","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#inspection-fixture","text":"Program-guided milling A plate and clamps locate a casting. The CNC axes guide the milling cutter; the workholding device has no tool-guiding feature. Is the device a jig or a fixture? Your decision and reason Self-check: write your reasoning before revealing. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal reasoning It is a fixture. The device locates and holds the work, while the machine controls the cutting tool path."},
{"title":"Diameter change per inch","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-taper-rate","text":"Diameter change per inch. A taper changes from 1.250 to 0.950 in over an axial length of 4.000 in. Find taper per inch to three decimal places. Answer in in/in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution (1.250 − 0.950)/4.000 = 0.075 in/in. Use diameter change, not radius change. Rounded answer: 0.075 in/in."},
{"title":"Convert taper per inch to per foot","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-taper-per-foot","text":"Convert taper per inch to per foot. A taper rate is 0.075 in/in. Express it in in/ft to three decimal places. Answer in in/ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 12 × 0.075 = 0.900 in/ft. A foot contains twelve inches. Rounded answer: 0.900 in/ft."},
{"title":"Convert taper per foot to per inch","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-taper-per-inch","text":"Convert taper per foot to per inch. A taper is specified as 0.600 in/ft. Find taper per inch to three decimal places. Answer in in/in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.600/12 = 0.050 in/in. Do not substitute 0.600 directly into a formula that requires in/in. Rounded answer: 0.050 in/in."},
{"title":"Compound taper half-angle","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-compound-angle","text":"Compound taper half-angle. A taper has a diameter change of 0.080 in per inch of axial length. Find the compound travel angle from the spindle axis, to three decimal places in degrees. Answer in degrees Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution α = atan(0.080/2) = 2.291 degrees. The included angle is about 4.581 degrees; that is not the travel angle. Rounded answer: 2.291 degrees."},
{"title":"Offset with unequal total and taper lengths","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-tailstock-offset","text":"Offset with unequal total and taper lengths. A shallow external taper changes from 1.200 to 1.000 in over l = 3.000 in. Total effective center-support length L is 9.000 in. Use the small-angle offset estimate and round to three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Taper rate = 0.200/3.000 in/in. Offset ≈ (0.200/3.000) × 9.000/2 = 0.300 in. Using only the taper length gives 0.100 in and is wrong. Rounded answer: 0.300 in."},
{"title":"Offset from a per-foot specification","page":"Practice","summary":"Turning Tapers","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-offset-from-foot","text":"Offset from a per-foot specification. A taper is 0.720 in/ft and the effective center-support length is 8.000 in. Find the small-angle tailstock offset estimate, to three decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution TPI = 0.720/12 = 0.060 in/in. Offset ≈ 0.060 × 8.000/2 = 0.240 in. Treating TPF as TPI would give 2.880 in. Rounded answer: 0.240 in."},
{"title":"Pitch from threads per inch","page":"Practice","summary":"Single-Point Threading","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-thread-pitch","text":"Pitch from threads per inch. A single-start thread has 16 threads per inch. Find its axial pitch to four decimal places. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P = 1/16 = 0.0625 in. Lead equals pitch for this single-start thread. Rounded answer: 0.0625 in."},
{"title":"Lead of a two-start thread","page":"Practice","summary":"Single-Point Threading","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-thread-lead","text":"Lead of a two-start thread. A two-start thread has axial pitch P = 0.075 in. Find the required axial travel along one helix per revolution, to three decimal places. Answer in in/rev Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Lead = 2P = 2 × 0.075 = 0.150 in/rev. Using pitch alone gives half the required travel. Rounded answer: 0.150 in/rev."},
{"title":"Ideal sharp-V radial depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Single-Point Threading","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-thread-depth","text":"Ideal sharp-V radial depth. For an ideal sharp-V 60-degree teaching profile with 20 threads per inch, calculate total radial depth H = (√3/2)P. Round to five decimal places. This is a geometric model, not a finished standard thread or a per-pass setting. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P = 1/20 = 0.050 in. H = (√3/2) × 0.050 = 0.04330 in. Diameter reduction would be 2H, not H. Real thread flats and root shape change the depth. Rounded answer: 0.04330 in."},
{"title":"Ideal compound travel from radial depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Single-Point Threading","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lathe-thread-compound-travel","text":"Ideal compound travel from radial depth. For the ideal sharp-V 60-degree, 20-per-inch teaching profile, H = (√3/2)/20 in. Find total actual compound travel at 29 degrees from radial using c = H/cos(29°). Round to five decimal places. Ignore tip truncation and backlash; this is not a production setting. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution H = 0.04330127 in. c = H/cos(29°) = 0.04951 in. Compound travel is longer than its radial component; do not double H or use H alone. Rounded answer: 0.04951 in."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 1","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-transportation","text":"Classify a work observation 1. A tote of parts is moved to an unnecessary distant staging area and then returned. Classify the needless movement of the tote. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Transportation follows material movement. The observation concerns the tote, rather than the worker’s reaching or searching."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 2","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-inventory","text":"Classify a work observation 2. Unused spare blanks beyond current needs occupy a storage rack. Classify the excess stock itself. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Inventory describes the excess stock. Making it could have been overproduction, but this observation is about what is stored."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 3","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-motion","text":"Classify a work observation 3. A worker repeatedly searches several drawers for a measuring tool. Classify the searching. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Motion follows unnecessary movement by a person. A labeled location may help; check the effect before adopting it."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 4","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-waiting","text":"Classify a work observation 4. A job is ready, but cannot start until missing specifications arrive. Classify the idle delay. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Waiting is idle time before work can proceed. The missing information should be investigated."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 5","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-overproduction","text":"Classify a work observation 5. A station makes 40 extra pieces with no order or replenishment need. Classify the act of making extra pieces. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Overproduction means making too much or too early. It can create inventory afterward."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 6","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-over-processing","text":"Classify a work observation 6. A part meets every functional and appearance requirement, but receives another unnecessary polishing pass. Classify that pass. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Over-processing adds work beyond requirements. Required finishing would be a different case."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 7","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-defects","text":"Classify a work observation 7. A part has the wrong hole pattern and must be scrapped. Classify the failed part and its replacement work. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Defects are failures to meet requirements, leading to scrap or rework. Inspection alone is not a defective part."},
{"title":"Classify a work observation 8","page":"Practice","summary":"Eight Wastes: TIMWOODS","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#waste-skills","text":"Classify a work observation 8. A team never considers a trained assembler’s relevant suggestions for improving the work. Classify the unused contribution. Choose the best classification Choose a waste Transportation Inventory Motion Waiting Overproduction Over-processing Defects Skills / unused talent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Skills waste means failing to use or develop people’s abilities and ideas."},
{"title":"Elapsed time through a sequence","page":"Practice","summary":"Value Stream Mapping","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#lean-lead-time","text":"Elapsed time through a sequence. One item takes 8 minutes in operation A, waits 75 minutes, then takes 12 minutes in operation B. There are no other delays or overlapping steps. Find elapsed lead time in whole minutes. Answer in minutes Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Lead time = 8 + 75 + 12 = 95 minutes. Process time is only 8 + 12 = 20 minutes."},
{"title":"Order a supported drilling route","page":"Practice","summary":"Operation Method Sheets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-operation-order","text":"Order a supported drilling route A small wooden tag will be drilled while attached to a larger strip that provides the required holding area. Its final cut preserves the reference edge. Put these operations in order: A — final sanding and inspection; B — cut the tag free; C — establish the reference edge and mark the hole; D — drill and inspect the hole while the strip is held in the qualified setup. Your order and reason Self-check: write your answer, then compare below. This response is not automatically graded or saved. Show a sensible order C → D → B → A. Establish the reference before locating the hole. The problem specifies holding on the larger strip, so drill before cutting free. Finish and inspect after the cuts. This order depends on the stated setup; it is not a universal rule for drilling."},
{"title":"Find the missing acceptance criterion","page":"Practice","summary":"Operation Method Sheets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-missing-criterion","text":"Find the missing acceptance criterion A row says: “Drill two through holes using a drill press, the specified bit, backing board and clamps. Use the qualified setup and its recorded speed. Make the holes look good.” What inspection information is missing? Your revision Self-check; your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show what must be added “Look good” is not an acceptance criterion. Add the drawing revision, hole-diameter and location limits, any required edge condition, and inspection tools or a defined comparison method. Do not invent the limits; take them from the part requirements."},
{"title":"Find the missing equipment and setup","page":"Practice","summary":"Operation Method Sheets","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-missing-equipment","text":"Find the missing equipment and setup A row says: “Make a 30 ±0.5 mm-wide strip. Measure width with calipers at both ends.” It identifies material and drawing revision elsewhere. Why can another person still not repeat the operation from this row? What would you add? Self-check; your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show the missing fields The row gives a result and an inspection method, but no equipment or route. Add the operation, equipment, tool, reference edge, support and holding method, and the required process settings with units or a qualified setup reference. Equipment selection depends on the material and available approved process."},
{"title":"Choose a dimension from function","page":"Practice","summary":"Critical Dimensions for the Shop","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-critical-feature","text":"Choose a dimension from function A cover must slide over two fixed locating pins. Its decorative outer border has plenty of clearance. Which features would you investigate first when choosing critical dimensions, and why? Features and reason Self-check; your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show the functional choice Start with the hole diameters and their locations relative to the pins and each other. Those control whether the cover fits. The border still needs dimensions, but a tighter border tolerance does not fix misaligned holes. Derive limits from the mating geometry instead of simply choosing a small number."},
{"title":"Allocate sheet material cost","page":"Practice","summary":"Bills of Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-stock-cost","text":"Allocate sheet material cost A sheet costs $35. A part is allocated 180 × 100 mm of a 1000 × 400 mm sheet of the same material and thickness. This allocation already includes cutting waste. What is the material cost per part? Report dollars to the nearest cent. Cost in dollars Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Share = (180 × 100) / (1000 × 400) = 0.045. Cost = 0.045 × $35 = $1.575, or $1.58 to the nearest cent. Using the length fraction alone gives $6.30 and ignores the width fraction."},
{"title":"Extend BOM quantities once","page":"Practice","summary":"Bills of Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-bom-total","text":"Extend BOM quantities once An invented small stand uses two made side pieces at $0.85 in allocated material each and four purchased pads at $0.30 each. What is the material and component total for one stand, to the nearest cent? Exclude labor and other costs. Total in dollars Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2 × $0.85 + 4 × $0.30 = $1.70 + $1.20 = $2.90. Adding only the two unit prices gives $1.15 and misses the quantities."},
{"title":"Split a total silicone mass","page":"Practice","summary":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-silicone-total","text":"Split a total silicone mass For this arithmetic exercise, a silicone's stated A:B ratio is 10:1 by weight. You need 330 g total of A and B combined. How much B is needed? Report grams to one decimal place. This ratio is not a recommendation for another product. Mass of B in grams Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution There are 10 + 1 = 11 ratio parts. B = 330 / 11 = 30.0 g; A = 300.0 g. Check: 300 + 30 = 330 g. Dividing the total by 10 gives 33 g, which treats the total as A alone."},
{"title":"Add B to a measured mass of A","page":"Practice","summary":"Making Silicone Molds from a Printed Pattern","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#documentation-silicone-base","text":"Add B to a measured mass of A An arithmetic example specifies A:B = 10:1 by weight. You have measured 275 g of A, excluding the cup. What mass of B is needed? Report grams to one decimal place. Follow the actual product's ratio for real mixing. Mass of B in grams Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution B = 275 / 10 = 27.5 g. Total mixed mass is 302.5 g. Dividing by 11 gives 25 g and incorrectly treats the given A mass as the total."},
{"title":"Equal process costs","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-break-even","text":"Equal process costs. For a batch of acceptable parts, A costs $180 fixed plus $9/part; B costs $930 fixed plus $4/part. Both meet the specification. At what quantity are total costs equal? Give a whole number. Answer in parts Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 180 + 9Q = 930 + 4Q; 5Q = 750; Q = 150 parts. Each total is $1,530. Dividing the fixed-cost difference by a sum of variable costs would not equate the totals."},
{"title":"Average unit cost","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Cost and Volume","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-unit-cost","text":"Average unit cost. A batch has a $350 setup and $7.375 variable cost per acceptable part. All 80 parts are acceptable. Find average unit cost to the nearest cent. Answer in dollars/part Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution C = 350 + 7.375 × 80 = $940. Unit cost = 940/80 = $11.75/part. The $7.375 variable charge alone omits setup."},
{"title":"Share of a purchased sheet","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Utilization and Cost","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-stock-share","text":"Share of a purchased sheet. A $60 sheet yields eight identical acceptable blanks. Charge the whole sheet, including waste, equally to the eight blanks. No other costs or remnant credits apply. Find material cost per blank to the nearest cent. Answer in dollars Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Each blank is charged 1/8 of the sheet: (1/8) × $60 = $7.50. Do not charge the whole $60 to each blank."},
{"title":"Count blanks with cutting allowance","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Utilization and Cost","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-kerf","text":"Count blanks with cutting allowance. A 1,000 mm bar supplies 100 mm blanks. Each blank needs one 2 mm separating cut, including the last blank. Assume no end trim or other allowances. What is the maximum whole number of blanks? Answer in blanks Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Each blank consumes 102 mm. Nine consume 918 mm; ten need 1,020 mm and will not fit. Answer: 9 blanks. There are 82 mm left over, plus 18 mm lost to nine kerfs. Ignoring kerf incorrectly predicts ten blanks."},
{"title":"Useful length as a percentage","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Utilization and Cost","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-utilization","text":"Useful length as a percentage. Use a 1,020 mm bar to make nine acceptable 110 mm blanks of the same cross-section. Include all purchased stock in the denominator. What is utilization to one decimal place? Answer in percent Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution Useful length = 9 × 110 = 990 mm. Utilization = 990/1,020 × 100 = 97.0588…%, or 97.1%. 1,020 − 9 × 110 = 30 mm of the bar lies outside the finished blanks (kerf plus offcut together)."},
{"title":"Material BOM roll-up","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Utilization and Cost","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#economics-bom","text":"Material BOM roll-up. One assembly needs three plates at $4.80 each and six fasteners at $0.30 each. Those prices already include material waste. Find the material-only BOM total to the nearest cent. Answer in dollars Check answer Enter an answer, then check it. Show worked solution 3 × $4.80 + 6 × $0.30 = $14.40 + $1.80 = $16.20. Processing and assembly costs are not included."},
{"title":"Cut a board to length","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-sawing","text":"Cut a board to length A saw cuts a long wooden board into two shorter pieces. Which category describes this operation? Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Separating. The cut removes material along a narrow path and divides the board."},
{"title":"Make a hole in plastic","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-drilling","text":"Make a hole in plastic A drill removes chips from a solid plastic plate to make a mounting hole. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Separating. The intended change is a hole made by material removal. The plastic family does not change that classification."},
{"title":"Cut a sheet-metal outline","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-laser-cutting","text":"Cut a sheet-metal outline A laser cuts an outline through thin metal sheet. Material leaves the narrow cut. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Separating. Heat is the means of cutting; removing material to produce the outline is the goal. This is not conditioning merely because heat is involved."},
{"title":"Build a spacer in layers","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-printing","text":"Build a spacer in layers A printer deposits plastic layer by layer to build a spacer. Use the five-category model taught here. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Forming. This course includes additive manufacturing within forming. Removing supports afterward would be a separate separating operation."},
{"title":"Make a cast planter","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-casting","text":"Make a cast planter A wet concrete mixture fills a mold that establishes a planter’s shape. Classify the shape-making step, before considering later property development. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Forming. The mold establishes the shape. Later curing develops properties and is conditioning."},
{"title":"Bend an aluminum strip","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-bending","text":"Bend an aluminum strip A press bends an aluminum strip into an L-shaped bracket without cutting it. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Forming. The operation changes shape through deformation. A change in properties may accompany it, but the stated goal is the bend."},
{"title":"Bond a shelf edge","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-adhesive-joint","text":"Bond a shelf edge An adhesive bonds a wooden edge strip to a shelf panel. Classify the assembly operation as a whole. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Fabricating. Separate parts become one assembly. Curing of the adhesive is a conditioning mechanism within that joining operation."},
{"title":"Attach a metal handle","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-fasteners","text":"Attach a metal handle Bolts secure a metal handle to a plastic housing through holes that already exist. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Fabricating. The operation joins parts. The presence of holes does not make this step drilling or separating."},
{"title":"Dry lumber before cutting","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-drying","text":"Dry lumber before cutting Lumber is dried to reduce its moisture content before parts are cut from it. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Conditioning. Moisture content and resulting behavior change. Shrinkage may accompany drying, but making a new outline is not the purpose."},
{"title":"Relieve stress in glass","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-annealing","text":"Relieve stress in glass A formed glass piece receives a controlled thermal cycle to reduce residual internal stress. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Conditioning. The goal is to change the internal stress condition. A thermal process is not automatically forming."},
{"title":"Coat a metal housing","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-painting","text":"Coat a metal housing Paint is applied to a metal housing for surface protection and color. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Finishing. The desired result is a protective and decorative surface layer."},
{"title":"Apply a metal surface layer","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-plating","text":"Apply a metal surface layer A thin metal coating is deposited onto an existing part to improve its surface performance. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Finishing. Adding metal does not automatically mean forming; the stated goal is a surface treatment, not building the main shape."},
{"title":"Sand to a required thickness","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-sanding-size","text":"Sand to a required thickness A wooden block is too thick. Sanding continues until it reaches the required thickness. Which category is primary, and what other category might seem plausible? Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Separating is primary because the acceptance goal is a dimension reached by material removal. The surface may also become smoother, an effect associated with finishing."},
{"title":"Remove visible scratches","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-sanding-surface","text":"Remove visible scratches A correctly sized plastic panel is polished with a fine abrasive to remove visible scratches. Dimensions are not the acceptance goal. Pick the primary category and explain the overlap. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Finishing is primary because surface appearance is the goal. Abrasion also removes some material, so separating describes part of the mechanism."},
{"title":"Make a curved laminated rail","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-curved-lamination","text":"Make a curved laminated rail Thin wooden strips are bonded around a form. The operation is described as making a specified curve. Pick the primary category and name the second category involved. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Forming is primary under the stated shape goal; fabricating describes bonding the layers. If a question focused instead on achieving the bond, fabricating would be a defensible primary category."},
{"title":"Develop a resin part’s properties","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-resin-cure","text":"Develop a resin part’s properties An already shaped resin part is cured to develop its intended material properties. It is not being attached to another part. Pick the primary category. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Conditioning. The chemical state and properties develop through curing. Without a joining goal, fabricating is not the primary category."},
{"title":"Join frame members with a weld","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-welded-frame","text":"Join frame members with a weld A weld joins two metal frame members. Heating also changes material near the joint. Pick the primary category and explain why the side effect does not control it. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Fabricating is primary because the intended result is a joint. Local property changes are real, but they are secondary to the assembly goal in this description."},
{"title":"Harden the surface of a gear","page":"Practice","summary":"Manufacturing Process Categories","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#process-surface-hardening","text":"Harden the surface of a gear A treatment changes the material near a metal gear’s surface to increase hardness. No decorative or protective coating is deposited. Use the course model. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Separating Forming Fabricating Conditioning Finishing Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Conditioning. The main goal is a change in material properties, even though the change is concentrated near the surface. Surface location alone does not make it finishing."},
{"title":"Copper wire core","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-copper-wire","text":"Copper wire core Classify only the copper core of an insulated wire. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Metals. Copper belongs to the metal family; the surrounding polymer insulation is a separate part with a different classification."},
{"title":"Steel screw","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-steel-screw","text":"Steel screw A screw is specified as steel. Which of the four material families contains the screw material? Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Metals. Steel is an iron-based alloy. Its shape or coating does not change the base material’s family."},
{"title":"Transparent acrylic panel","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-acrylic-panel","text":"Transparent acrylic panel A transparent panel is identified as acrylic, not glass. Pick its material family. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Plastics (polymers). Transparency is a property shared by materials in different families; acrylic is a polymer."},
{"title":"Unreinforced nylon gear","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-nylon-gear","text":"Unreinforced nylon gear A small gear is made from unreinforced nylon. Classify its material, not the job the gear performs. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Plastics (polymers). A gear can be made from several families; the stated composition determines this answer."},
{"title":"Plywood shelf panel","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-plywood-shelf","text":"Plywood shelf panel A shelf panel consists of thin wood layers bonded together. Use the course’s four-family model. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Wood & Composites. Plywood is an engineered wood composite. There is no separate fifth composites family here."},
{"title":"Glass-fiber-reinforced panel","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-reinforced-panel","text":"Glass-fiber-reinforced panel A panel consists of glass fibers held in a cured polymer matrix. Classify the complete reinforced material, not either ingredient alone. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Wood & Composites. This engineered composite belongs in that combined course family even though it contains no wood. The glass fibers alone and the polymer alone would have different family labels."},
{"title":"Porcelain floor tile","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-porcelain-tile","text":"Porcelain floor tile A tile is made of fired porcelain. Pick its material family. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Ceramics & Glass. Porcelain is a ceramic. A hard surface does not make it a metal."},
{"title":"Glass jar body","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Families","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#family-glass-jar","text":"Glass jar body Classify the glass body of a jar, leaving its metal lid out of the question. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Ceramics & Glass. A product can contain several families; this prompt names only the glass body."},
{"title":"Rain-exposed equipment cover","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-outdoor-cover","text":"Rain-exposed equipment cover Choose a candidate family for a light, nonstructural outdoor cover that must resist rain without rusting. Explain why and name one further check. More than one family can be defended. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning One suitable starting point is Plastics (polymers): a suitable outdoor grade can offer low density and will not rust. Check sunlight resistance, temperature, impact and chemical exposure. A corrosion-resistant metal with suitable protection can also be defensible; “metal” alone is not enough. No family label guarantees outdoor life."},
{"title":"Reduce a handheld enclosure’s mass","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-light-enclosure","text":"Reduce a handheld enclosure’s mass A handheld enclosure should be light, moldable and electrically insulating. Choose a candidate family and explain the tradeoff. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Plastics (polymers) is a reasonable starting point because many unfilled grades have low density, mold readily and are electrical insulators. Check impact resistance, temperature and the grade’s actual electrical properties. A thin metal enclosure might be light, but would need a separate strategy for insulation."},
{"title":"Carry heat away from a warm component","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-heat-spreader","text":"Carry heat away from a warm component A plate should carry heat away from a warm component. Electrical insulation is not required. Choose a candidate family, say why, and name a next check. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Metals is a reasonable starting point: aluminum or copper can conduct heat well. Check the actual thermal conductivity, operating temperature, dimensions, environment and cost. Some engineering ceramics can also be suitable; the family tendency is a starting point, not an exclusive answer."},
{"title":"Conduct electric current","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-wire-core","text":"Conduct electric current A flexible wire core must conduct electric current well and be drawable into a long, thin shape. Choose a family and name two relevant properties. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Metals. High electrical conductivity and ductility support the requirement; copper is a common candidate. The complete wire also needs suitable insulation and a size matched to its service requirements."},
{"title":"Hard decorative floor surface","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-wearing-tile","text":"Hard decorative floor surface A decorative floor surface needs scratch resistance and low sensitivity to routine water exposure. Choose a candidate family and explain one limitation. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Ceramics & Glass is a reasonable starting family, with a suitable ceramic tile as a candidate. Hardness helps resist scratching. Brittleness means impact and support conditions still matter, and water exposure must be considered for the complete tile-and-joint system."},
{"title":"A stiff panel with limited mass","page":"Practice","summary":"Material Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#requirement-stiff-light-panel","text":"A stiff panel with limited mass A large panel must be stiff without excessive mass. A layered or fiber-reinforced construction is acceptable. Choose a candidate family and explain what must be checked. Your classification or candidate family Choose before revealing Metals Plastics (polymers) Wood & Composites Ceramics & Glass Your reason Self-check: make a choice and explain it, then compare with the reasoning below. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Reveal the classification and reasoning Wood & Composites is a reasonable starting point: plywood or a suitably designed fiber composite can provide useful stiffness for its mass. Check loading direction, layer arrangement, thickness, joints and moisture or temperature exposure. A shaped metal panel can also be defensible; geometry affects part stiffness."},
{"title":"Inch micrometer reading A","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-micrometer-368","text":"Inch micrometer reading A. A 0–1 in micrometer has 0.025 in sleeve spaces and 0.001 in thimble spaces. The last reached sleeve line is 0.350 in. Thimble line 18 aligns with the datum. What is the reading in inches? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Sleeve 0.350 + thimble 0.018 = 0.368 in. At thimble zero, do not add a full extra turn."},
{"title":"Inch micrometer reading B","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-micrometer-399","text":"Inch micrometer reading B. A 0–1 in micrometer has 0.025 in sleeve spaces and 0.001 in thimble spaces. The last reached sleeve line is 0.375 in. Thimble line 24 aligns with the datum. What is the reading in inches? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Sleeve 0.375 + thimble 0.024 = 0.399 in. At thimble zero, do not add a full extra turn."},
{"title":"Inch micrometer reading C","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-micrometer-400","text":"Inch micrometer reading C. A 0–1 in micrometer has 0.025 in sleeve spaces and 0.001 in thimble spaces. The last reached sleeve line is 0.400 in. Thimble line 0 aligns with the datum. What is the reading in inches? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Sleeve 0.400 + thimble 0.000 = 0.400 in. At thimble zero, do not add a full extra turn."},
{"title":"Inch micrometer reading D","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-micrometer-zero","text":"Inch micrometer reading D. A 0–1 in micrometer has 0.025 in sleeve spaces and 0.001 in thimble spaces. The last reached sleeve line is 0.000 in. Thimble line 0 aligns with the datum. What is the reading in inches? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Sleeve 0.000 + thimble 0.000 = 0.000 in. At thimble zero, do not add a full extra turn."},
{"title":"Inch micrometer reading E","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-micrometer-999","text":"Inch micrometer reading E. A 0–1 in micrometer has 0.025 in sleeve spaces and 0.001 in thimble spaces. The last reached sleeve line is 0.975 in. Thimble line 24 aligns with the datum. What is the reading in inches? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Sleeve 0.975 + thimble 0.024 = 0.999 in. At thimble zero, do not add a full extra turn."},
{"title":"Vernier micrometer reading A","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-vernier-micrometer-4627","text":"Vernier micrometer reading A. On a 0–1 in vernier micrometer, the last reached sleeve line is 0.450 in. The datum is between thimble 12 and 13. Sleeve vernier line 7 aligns with a thimble line. Each sleeve vernier division adds 0.0001 in. Read the instrument. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.4500 + 0.0120 + 0.0007 = 0.4627 in. Keep the completed thousandth; then add the vernier fraction."},
{"title":"Vernier micrometer reading B","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-vernier-micrometer-2999","text":"Vernier micrometer reading B. On a 0–1 in vernier micrometer, the last reached sleeve line is 0.275 in. The datum is between thimble 24 and 0. Sleeve vernier line 9 aligns with a thimble line. Each sleeve vernier division adds 0.0001 in. Read the instrument. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.2750 + 0.0240 + 0.0009 = 0.2999 in. Keep the completed thousandth; then add the vernier fraction."},
{"title":"Vernier micrometer reading C","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-vernier-micrometer-3000","text":"Vernier micrometer reading C. On a 0–1 in vernier micrometer, the last reached sleeve line is 0.300 in. The datum is exactly at thimble 0. Sleeve vernier line 0 aligns with a thimble line. Each sleeve vernier division adds 0.0001 in. Read the instrument. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.3000 + 0.0000 + 0.0000 = 0.3000 in. Keep the completed thousandth; then add the vernier fraction."},
{"title":"Vernier micrometer reading D","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-vernier-micrometer-0001","text":"Vernier micrometer reading D. On a 0–1 in vernier micrometer, the last reached sleeve line is 0.000 in. The datum is between thimble 0 and 1. Sleeve vernier line 1 aligns with a thimble line. Each sleeve vernier division adds 0.0001 in. Read the instrument. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.0000 + 0.0000 + 0.0001 = 0.0001 in. Keep the completed thousandth; then add the vernier fraction."},
{"title":"Vernier micrometer reading E","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-vernier-micrometer-9999","text":"Vernier micrometer reading E. On a 0–1 in vernier micrometer, the last reached sleeve line is 0.975 in. The datum is between thimble 24 and 0. Sleeve vernier line 9 aligns with a thimble line. Each sleeve vernier division adds 0.0001 in. Read the instrument. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.9750 + 0.0240 + 0.0009 = 0.9999 in. Keep the completed thousandth; then add the vernier fraction."},
{"title":"Vernier caliper reading A","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-caliper-342","text":"Vernier caliper reading A. A 25-division inch vernier caliper has 0.025 in main-scale spaces and a 0.001 in least count. Its zero is just past the 1.325 in main-scale line. Vernier division 17 aligns. What is the reading? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Main scale 1.325 + 17 × 0.001 = 1.342 in. Read the main scale at the vernier zero, not at the matching line."},
{"title":"Vernier caliper reading B","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-caliper-349","text":"Vernier caliper reading B. A 25-division inch vernier caliper has 0.025 in main-scale spaces and a 0.001 in least count. Its zero is just past the 1.325 in main-scale line. Vernier division 24 aligns. What is the reading? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Main scale 1.325 + 24 × 0.001 = 1.349 in. Read the main scale at the vernier zero, not at the matching line."},
{"title":"Vernier caliper reading C","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-caliper-350","text":"Vernier caliper reading C. A 25-division inch vernier caliper has 0.025 in main-scale spaces and a 0.001 in least count. Its zero is exactly on the 1.350 in main-scale line. Vernier division 0 aligns. What is the reading? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Main scale 1.350 + 0 × 0.001 = 1.350 in. Read the main scale at the vernier zero, not at the matching line."},
{"title":"Vernier caliper reading D","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-caliper-zero","text":"Vernier caliper reading D. A 25-division inch vernier caliper has 0.025 in main-scale spaces and a 0.001 in least count. Its zero is exactly on the 0.000 in main-scale line. Vernier division 0 aligns. What is the reading? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Main scale 0.000 + 0 × 0.001 = 0.000 in. Read the main scale at the vernier zero, not at the matching line."},
{"title":"Vernier caliper reading E","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-caliper-024","text":"Vernier caliper reading E. A 25-division inch vernier caliper has 0.025 in main-scale spaces and a 0.001 in least count. Its zero is just past the 0.000 in main-scale line. Vernier division 24 aligns. What is the reading? Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Main scale 0.000 + 24 × 0.001 = 0.024 in. Read the main scale at the vernier zero, not at the matching line."},
{"title":"Metric half-millimeter mark","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-metric-micrometer","text":"Metric half-millimeter mark. A metric micrometer has 0.5 mm sleeve spaces and 0.01 mm thimble spaces. The 11 mm line and the next half-millimeter mark are exposed. Thimble 36 aligns. Read the tool in mm. Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 11 + 0.5 + 0.36 = 11.86 mm. Missing the half-millimeter line would give 11.36 mm."},
{"title":"Beam plus dial","page":"Practice","summary":"Reading Measuring Scales","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-dial-caliper","text":"Beam plus dial. An inch dial caliper is marked 0.100 in per revolution with 100 dial spaces. The completed beam reading is 2.400 in and the dial indicates 63. Read the caliper. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2.400 + 63 × 0.001 = 2.463 in. The beam already accounts for completed dial turns."},
{"title":"Tool for a tight outside size","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-tool-od","text":"Tool for a tight outside size. An accessible outside diameter is 0.750 ±0.0005 in. Use the rule of thumb that resolution should be about one tenth of the TOTAL tolerance. Which candidate meets that resolution target? Enter its number: 1 = 0.01 in steel rule; 2 = 0.001 in caliper; 3 = 0.0001 in outside micrometer. Assume the tools have adequate range and are in good condition. Answer in choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Total tolerance = 0.001 in; target increment = 0.0001 in. Choose 3, the outside micrometer. Its accuracy and method still need verification; this resolution rule is not an acceptance standard."},
{"title":"Tool geometry for a depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-tool-depth","text":"Tool geometry for a depth. A wide flat-bottom recess is 0.500 ±0.001 in deep. The reference rim is wide enough to support a depth-micrometer base. Which candidate meets the one-tenth-of-total-tolerance resolution target AND reaches this feature? Enter 1 = outside micrometer, 0.0001 in; 2 = depth micrometer, 0.0001 in; 3 = caliper depth rod, 0.001 in. Assume adequate range and verified condition. Answer in choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Total tolerance = 0.002 in; target = 0.0002 in. Choose 2. The depth micrometer has both suitable geometry and a fine enough increment. The outside micrometer cannot make this depth measurement."},
{"title":"Resolution target from bilateral limits","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Shop Measuring Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-tool-tolerance","text":"Resolution target from bilateral limits. A shaft size is 1.250 ±0.002 in. Using the one-tenth-of-TOTAL-tolerance rule of thumb, calculate the target reading increment in inches. This asks for an increment, not an accuracy guarantee. Answer in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Total tolerance = 0.004 in. Divide by 10: 0.0004 in. Dividing only the ± amount by 10 would use half the stated tolerance width."},
{"title":"Bevel protractor reading A","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-bevel-35","text":"Bevel protractor reading A. A 5-minute vernier bevel protractor shows 26 completed degrees read to the right of zero. Division 7 on the same-side vernier aligns. Express the complete scale reading in TOTAL arcminutes (60 arcminutes = 1 degree). Answer in arcmin Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 26° + 7 × 5′ = 26°35′. In total minutes: 26 × 60 + 35 = 1595 arcmin. Read the vernier in the same direction as the main scale."},
{"title":"Bevel protractor reading B","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-bevel-55","text":"Bevel protractor reading B. A 5-minute vernier bevel protractor shows 48 completed degrees read to the left of zero. Division 11 on the same-side vernier aligns. Express the complete scale reading in TOTAL arcminutes (60 arcminutes = 1 degree). Answer in arcmin Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 48° + 11 × 5′ = 48°55′. In total minutes: 48 × 60 + 55 = 2935 arcmin. Read the vernier in the same direction as the main scale."},
{"title":"Bevel protractor reading C","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-bevel-20","text":"Bevel protractor reading C. A 5-minute vernier bevel protractor shows 17 completed degrees read to the left of zero. Division 4 on the same-side vernier aligns. Express the complete scale reading in TOTAL arcminutes (60 arcminutes = 1 degree). Answer in arcmin Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 17° + 4 × 5′ = 17°20′. In total minutes: 17 × 60 + 20 = 1040 arcmin. Read the vernier in the same direction as the main scale."},
{"title":"Obtuse angle from an acute supplement","page":"Practice","summary":"Shop Layout and Angle Measurement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-bevel-supplement","text":"Obtuse angle from an acute supplement. The contact arrangement measures the acute supplement of the required obtuse angle. The scale shows 41 completed degrees and vernier division 9 aligns on a 5-minute instrument. Report the REQUIRED obtuse angle in total arcminutes. Answer in arcmin Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Scale = 41°45′ = 2505 arcmin. Required angle = 180° − 41°45′ = 138°15′. Thus 10800 − 2505 = 8295 arcmin. Subtract only because the problem explicitly identifies the measured supplement."},
{"title":"A small batch of large housings","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-sand-batch","text":"A small batch of large housings A large hollow iron housing is needed in a small batch. Which route is the strongest starting candidate among these? Pressure die casting in an aluminum die-casting cell Sand casting with a core Wire drawing Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Sand casting can accommodate large castings and internal cavities using cores, with tooling suited to varied batch sizes. Confirm alloy, wall design and later machining."},
{"title":"Repeated small housings","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-die-run","text":"Repeated small housings A stable design needs a long run of detailed zinc housings that can eject from a metal die. Which route deserves investigation? Pressure die casting Open-die forging Wire drawing Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Pressure die casting repeats detailed shapes in suitable alloys. A long run can spread the die cost, but quantity alone does not prove an economic crossover."},
{"title":"A long constant profile","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-rail","text":"A long constant profile A rail has the same intricate cross-section along its length. Local holes will be machined later. Which starting route fits? Deep drawing Sand casting each short length Extrusion Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Extrusion creates a long profile through a shaped die. The later holes do not invalidate that choice; they are separate operations."},
{"title":"Twenty bent brackets","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-bracket","text":"Twenty bent brackets Twenty simple sheet brackets are needed, and their design may change. Choose a flexible forming route after the blanks have been cut. Dedicated high-volume progressive tooling Press-brake bending with suitable tooling Wire drawing Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Press-brake bending suits this simple shape and small, changeable batch. Dedicated tooling needs an economic justification; drawing wire makes a different geometry."},
{"title":"Classify a stamping operation","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-blanking","text":"Classify a stamping operation A press cuts the required flat outline out of sheet, without bending it. What is this operation? Blanking: Separating Blanking: Conditioning Deep drawing: Forming Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The wanted cut-out is the blank. Cutting separates material even if the machine is part of a stamping line."},
{"title":"Casting size versus shrinkage voids","page":"Practice","summary":"Forming Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-riser","text":"Casting size versus shrinkage voids Which feature supplies liquid metal to a casting as it solidifies? Draft The core A properly designed riser Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The riser feeds contraction during solidification. Draft aids release and the core creates an internal opening; neither replaces feeding."},
{"title":"Join repeated overlapping sheets","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-spot","text":"Join repeated overlapping sheets Many overlapping steel-sheet assemblies need discrete permanent joints. Both sides are accessible. Which process fits these conditions? Resistance spot welding Soldering simply because it uses less heat Extrusion Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Resistance spot welding forms local nuggets at overlapping sheets and suits repeated production. Separate spots are not a continuous leak-tight seam."},
{"title":"A removable service cover","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-cover","text":"A removable service cover A cover must be removed repeatedly for maintenance. Tool access exists. Which starting joint makes most sense? A continuous fusion weld Compatible threaded fasteners Solid rivets chosen for permanent retention Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Threaded fasteners support disassembly. The design still needs suitable engagement, strength, access and environmental compatibility."},
{"title":"Join without melting the base parts","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-braze","text":"Join without melting the base parts A suitable dissimilar-metal joint uses a molten filler drawn into a narrow gap while both base parts remain solid. Which process family matches? Fusion welding only Rolling Brazing or soldering Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Brazing and soldering melt filler rather than the base parts. Filler temperature regime, compatibility and service requirements distinguish the choice."},
{"title":"Spot the galvanic risk","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-wet-pair","text":"Spot the galvanic risk Assume two metals have sufficiently different electrochemical behavior in salt water. Which arrangement completes a galvanic cell? They share salt water and electrical contact They are dry and electrically isolated They are in separate sealed dry boxes Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The first arrangement supplies both electrolyte and electrical connection. Dissimilar names alone do not complete a cell."},
{"title":"A washer does not isolate the whole joint","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-sleeve","text":"A washer does not isolate the whole joint An insulating washer is under a bolt head, but the bare bolt shank touches the dissimilar plate in a wet hole. What should the designer conclude? The washer proves isolation An electrical path remains; review complete isolation and drainage All corrosion has been prevented Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The shank contact bypasses the washer. A compatible sleeve and washers may be part of a complete design, subject to load and environmental requirements."},
{"title":"Decode an inch thread","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-inch-thread","text":"Decode an inch thread What does the thread-size designation 1/4-20 UNC communicate? Nominal 1/4-inch thread diameter, 20 threads per inch, Unified coarse series 1/4-inch bolt length and a 20-inch pitch 6 mm nominal diameter and 1 mm pitch Choice number Check answer Choose a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The first value is nominal diameter; 20 is a count of threads over one inch, so pitch is 1/20 inch. UNC names the Unified coarse series. Bolt length and the remaining specification are separate."},
{"title":"Decode a metric thread","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-metric-thread","text":"Decode a metric thread What does M6 × 1 mean, and does it match 1/4-20 UNC? 6 mm wrench size and one thread per inch; it matches 6 mm bolt length and 1 mm diameter; it matches 6 mm nominal thread diameter and 1 mm pitch; it does not match Choice number Check answer Choose a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. M6 identifies nominal 6 mm thread diameter; 1 is the axial spacing in millimeters. An inch designation uses threads per inch instead. These two sizes differ in diameter and spacing; neither designation specifies wrench size or bolt length."},
{"title":"Classify the filler temperature","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Metal","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-braze-temperature","text":"Classify the filler temperature A close-fitting joint is filled by capillary action. The filler has a liquidus of 600°C and both base parts remain solid. Which process matches? Soldering because the base parts stay solid Brazing because the filler liquidus is above 450°C Fusion welding because some metal becomes liquid Choice number Check answer Choose a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The filler liquidus, not the flame temperature, is compared with 450°C. At 600°C it is in the brazing regime. Both brazing and soldering keep the base parts solid; fusion welding melts base metal at the joint."},
{"title":"Compare wetted galvanic areas","page":"Practice","summary":"Fasteners and Galvanic Corrosion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metal-process-area-ratio","text":"Compare wetted galvanic areas Compare the same active/noble metal pair in the same electrolyte with electrical contact. Which arrangement can concentrate galvanic attack on the active metal most strongly? A large wetted active area coupled to a small wetted noble area A dry, fully isolated pair A small wetted active area coupled to a large wetted noble area Choice number Check answer Choose a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The small active anode receives the attack associated with the larger wetted cathode. A coating defect can create that unfavorable area ratio. The dry isolated pair lacks the conditions stated for the working cell; area ratio alone does not predict service life."},
{"title":"Choose a property for impact","page":"Practice","summary":"Metals and Their Properties","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-impact-property","text":"Choose a property for impact: A part must absorb energy before fracturing during an impact. Which property most directly describes that requirement? Density Toughness Electrical conductivity Answer (Choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2: toughness. Hardness and strength alone do not establish resistance to impact fracture."},
{"title":"Choose a conductor","page":"Practice","summary":"Nonferrous Metals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-conductor-choice","text":"Choose a conductor: Choose a starting material for a compact electrical conductor when conductivity is the main requirement and mass is secondary. Copper Tool steel Gray cast iron Answer (Choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1: copper. The final choice still requires a suitable grade and conductor design."},
{"title":"Read an incomplete order","page":"Practice","summary":"Types of Steel","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-grade-condition","text":"Read an incomplete order: An order says only “4140 round bar, 30 mm diameter.” What is still needed to establish the intended mechanical condition? The color of the packaging A magnet test The delivery condition and required properties Answer (Choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The grade alone does not establish heat treatment or hardness. Length, quantity, tolerances and the governing specification also belong on a complete order."},
{"title":"Choose the purpose of tempering","page":"Practice","summary":"Heat Treating Metals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-temper-goal","text":"Choose the purpose of tempering: A suitable steel has been quench hardened. What is the usual reason for tempering it? Make it molten Adjust hardness and toughness after hardening Turn it into a nonferrous metal Answer (Choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Tempering adjusts the properties of hardened steel, commonly sacrificing some hardness to improve toughness."},
{"title":"Restore formability","page":"Practice","summary":"Heat Treating Metals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-anneal-goal","text":"Restore formability: A cold-worked metal needs restored ductility before further forming. Which process is the appropriate starting point for a material-specific treatment plan? Annealing More cold work Painting Answer (Choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. A suitable anneal can restore ductility. More cold work generally uses up remaining ductility; painting changes the surface coating."},
{"title":"Calculate solid round bar mass","page":"Practice","summary":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-round-bar-mass","text":"Calculate solid round bar mass: A hypothetical stock estimate specifies density = 2,700 kg/m³. Use that supplied assumption, not a certified alloy property. A solid round bar has diameter 20 mm and length 500 mm. Find its mass in kg, rounded to three decimal places. Answer (kg) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Radius = 0.010 m and length = 0.500 m. Volume = π × 0.010² × 0.500 = 0.000157080 m³. Mass = volume × 2,700 = 0.424115 kg, or 0.424 kg. Using the diameter as the radius gives 1.69646 kg, four times too much. This is mass, not weight force."},
{"title":"Calculate tube material volume","page":"Practice","summary":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-tube-volume","text":"Calculate tube material volume: Round tube has outside diameter 30 mm, wall thickness 3 mm and length 200 mm. Find the volume of metal in cm³, rounded to two decimal places. Ignore end features and manufacturing tolerances. Answer (cm³) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Inside diameter = 30 − 2 × 3 = 24 mm. Volume = π(30² − 24²) × 200 / 4 = 50,893.801 mm³. Divide by 1,000 to obtain 50.89 cm³. Treating the tube as solid gives 141.37 cm³; subtracting only one wall gives 26.86 cm³."},
{"title":"Estimate cost from supplied mass","page":"Practice","summary":"Metal Stock Forms and Purchasing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#metals-stock-cost","text":"Estimate cost from supplied mass: A supplier quote for an invented example charges $6.50 per kg plus one $4.25 cut charge. The supplied billable mass is 2.90 kg. Find the total before tax and shipping, rounded to cents. Answer (USD) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2.90 × 6.50 + 4.25 = $23.10. Omitting the cut charge gives $18.85. These are supplied practice values, not current market prices."},
{"title":"ADC code from voltage","page":"Practice","summary":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-adc-code","text":"ADC code from voltage: Use an ideal 10-bit ADC, exact 5.000 V reference and the floor-bin model. What code represents 3.000 V? Round down after multiplying by 1024/5. Answer (code) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution floor(3.000 × 1024 / 5) = floor(614.4) = 614. Using 1023 here would give 613 after rounding down."},
{"title":"Voltage from ADC code","page":"Practice","summary":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-adc-volts","text":"Voltage from ADC code: With an exact 5.000 V reference, find the lower-bin-edge voltage for code 800. Use 1024 steps and give four significant figures. Answer (V) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 800 × 5 / 1024 = 3.90625 V, or 3.906 V. Dividing by 1023 gives about 3.910 V, a different endpoint scaling."},
{"title":"ADC step size","page":"Practice","summary":"Analog-to-Digital Conversion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-adc-step","text":"ADC step size: For 10 bits and an exact 5.000 V reference, what is the nominal ADC step in mV? Give four significant figures. Answer (mV) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 5 / 1024 × 1000 = 4.8828125 mV, or 4.883 mV."},
{"title":"PWM period from frequency","page":"Practice","summary":"Pulse-Width Modulation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-pwm-period","text":"PWM period from frequency: An ideal PWM signal repeats at 490 Hz. Find its period in milliseconds, to four significant figures. Answer (ms) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution T = 1/490 s × 1000 = 2.041 ms. Frequency is cycles per second; period is seconds per cycle."},
{"title":"PWM on-time","page":"Practice","summary":"Pulse-Width Modulation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-pwm-on-time","text":"PWM on-time: An ideal PWM signal is 490 Hz with 25% duty. Find its on-time in microseconds. Keep at least four significant figures in intermediate results. Answer (µs) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution T = 1/490 = 0.002040816 s. On-time = 0.25 × T × 1000000 = 510.2 µs. Using a rounded T of 2.041 ms gives 510.25 µs."},
{"title":"Average PWM voltage","page":"Practice","summary":"Pulse-Width Modulation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-pwm-average","text":"Average PWM voltage: An ideal output switches between 0 and 5.000 V at 35% duty. What is its average voltage? Answer (V) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution D = 35/100 = 0.35. Average = 0.35 × 5 = 1.750 V. The pin is not steadily at that voltage."},
{"title":"Nominal analogWrite value","page":"Practice","summary":"Pulse-Width Modulation","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-pwm-write","text":"Nominal analogWrite value: Use the nominal mapping round(255 × D). What analogWrite value requests 35% duty on an Uno R3 PWM pin? Round to the nearest integer. Answer (value) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution round(255 × 0.35) = round(89.25) = 89. This is a nominal request, not an exact timer-duty guarantee."},
{"title":"Pin mode for a button to ground","page":"Practice","summary":"Microcontroller Basics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-button-mode","text":"Pin mode for a button to ground: A button connects pin 2 to ground when pressed, with no external resistor. Choose the pinMode: enter 1 for INPUT, 2 for OUTPUT, or 3 for INPUT_PULLUP. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3: INPUT_PULLUP enables an internal pull-up so the open input has a defined HIGH state."},
{"title":"Open pull-up button","page":"Practice","summary":"Microcontroller Basics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-button-open","text":"Open pull-up button: Pin 2 is INPUT_PULLUP and its button to ground is open. Enter 1 for HIGH or 0 for LOW. Answer (state) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1: HIGH. The pull-up holds the open input high."},
{"title":"Closed pull-up button","page":"Practice","summary":"Microcontroller Basics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-button-closed","text":"Closed pull-up button: Pin 2 is INPUT_PULLUP and its button is closed to ground. Enter 1 for HIGH or 0 for LOW. Answer (state) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0: LOW. The closed switch pulls the input to ground."},
{"title":"Ultrasonic Echo signal","page":"Practice","summary":"Digital and Analog Signals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-ultrasonic-signal","text":"Ultrasonic Echo signal: Classify the Echo output of an HC-SR04 module: enter 1 for a timed digital pulse or 2 for an analog voltage proportional to distance. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1: timed digital pulse. Measure pulse duration to obtain range."},
{"title":"Phototransistor signal","page":"Practice","summary":"Digital and Analog Signals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-phototransistor-signal","text":"Phototransistor signal: A phototransistor drives a load resistor without a comparator or threshold stage. Enter 1 for an inherently digital output or 2 for an analog light response. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: analog response. A threshold circuit can turn that response into a digital signal."},
{"title":"Potentiometer wiper signal","page":"Practice","summary":"Digital and Analog Signals","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#micro-potentiometer-signal","text":"Potentiometer wiper signal: A potentiometer has its outer terminals at 0 V and 5 V. Classify the wiper voltage: enter 1 for digital or 2 for analog. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: analog. The wiper voltage varies continuously with position in the ideal model."},
{"title":"Higher reading at the right end","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-vise-positive","text":"Higher reading at the right end. The indicator body is on the operator side of the fixed jaw. You verified that moving the jaw toward the body increases the reading. The left mounting point is the pivot; only the right end is adjusted. A left-to-right sweep reads 0.00 mm at the left and +0.08 mm at the right. Which way should the first small correction move the right end? Toward the indicator. Away from the indicator. Along the jaw toward the left end. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. The right end is closer to the indicator than the left. Move it away, then repeat the sweep and recheck after final tightening. The sign is defined by the response test, not a remembered clockwise dial rule."},
{"title":"Lower reading at the right end","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-vise-negative","text":"Lower reading at the right end. The indicator body is on the operator side of the fixed jaw. You verified that moving the jaw toward the body increases the reading. The left mounting point is the pivot; only the right end is adjusted. The left reading is +0.02 mm and the right reading is −0.03 mm. Choose the correction. Move the right end toward the indicator. Move the right end away from the indicator. Accept it because neither reading is large. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. Right minus left is −0.03 − 0.02 = −0.05 mm. The right end is farther from the indicator. Bring it toward the indicator and resweep. Alignment is judged against the specified job tolerance, which this problem does not supply."},
{"title":"Reversed indicator response","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-vise-reversed-response","text":"Reversed indicator response. The left mounting point is the pivot. In this new indicator arrangement you verify that moving the jaw toward the body makes the reading DECREASE. Left reads 0.00 mm; right reads +0.04 mm. Which correction should move the right end? Away from the indicator body. No correction can be inferred. Toward the indicator body. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. With this reversed response, the positive change means the right end is farther away. Move it toward the body. You have enough information because the response was demonstrated; reversing the contact arrangement invalidates a memorized sign rule."},
{"title":"A jump halfway across the jaw","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-vise-local-jump","text":"A jump halfway across the jaw. The indicator body is on the operator side of the fixed jaw. You verified that moving the jaw toward the body increases the reading. The left mounting point is the pivot; only the right end is adjusted. The reading starts at 0.00 mm, rises abruptly to +0.12 mm at one small spot, returns to 0.00 mm and stays there to the far end. What is the best next action? Tap the right end away until the +0.12 mm spot reads zero. Inspect and clean the local contact surface and check indicator stability. Accept the setup without checking the middle. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. A local jump is not the smooth trend expected from simple jaw rotation. Check for debris, damage or unstable contact, then repeat the entire sweep. Equal endpoints alone do not explain the jump."},
{"title":"Stud near the part","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-clamp-near-part","text":"Stud near the part. A strap receives P = 7,200 N. The part-contact-to-stud distance a is 18 mm; the stud-to-heel distance b is 54 mm. What downward force does the strap apply to the part? Assume an ideal weightless strap with only part and heel contacts and a vertical stud load. Round to the nearest newton. Answer in N, rounded to the nearest newton Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning F part = P × b / (a + b) = 7,200 × 54 / (18 + 54) = 5,400 N. The heel receives 1,800 N. The two shares sum to 7,200 N. Using a in the numerator gives the heel share, not the requested part share."},
{"title":"Stud near the heel","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-clamp-near-heel","text":"Stud near the heel. A strap receives P = 7,200 N. The part-contact-to-stud distance a is 54 mm; the stud-to-heel distance b is 18 mm. What downward force does the strap apply to the part? Assume an ideal weightless strap with only part and heel contacts and a vertical stud load. Round to the nearest newton. Answer in N, rounded to the nearest newton Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning F part = P × b / (a + b) = 7,200 × 18 / (54 + 18) = 1,800 N. The heel receives 5,400 N. The two shares sum to 7,200 N. Using a in the numerator gives the heel share, not the requested part share."},
{"title":"Unequal clamp arms","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-clamp-unequal-arms","text":"Unequal clamp arms. A strap receives P = 5,200 N. The part-contact-to-stud distance a is 27 mm; the stud-to-heel distance b is 63 mm. What downward force does the strap apply to the part? Assume an ideal weightless strap with only part and heel contacts and a vertical stud load. Round to the nearest newton. Answer in N, rounded to the nearest newton Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning F part = P × b / (a + b) = 5,200 × 63 / (27 + 63) = 3,640 N. The heel receives 1,560 N. The two shares sum to 5,200 N. Using a in the numerator gives the heel share, not the requested part share."},
{"title":"Clamp-force rounding","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Vises and Strap Clamps","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-clamp-rounding","text":"Clamp-force rounding. A strap receives P = 4,100 N. The part-contact-to-stud distance a is 23 mm; the stud-to-heel distance b is 48 mm. What downward force does the strap apply to the part? Assume an ideal weightless strap with only part and heel contacts and a vertical stud load. Round to the nearest newton. Answer in N, rounded to the nearest newton Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning F part = P × b / (a + b) = 4,100 × 48 / (23 + 48) = 2,772 N. The heel receives 1,328 N. The two shares sum to 4,100 N. Using a in the numerator gives the heel share, not the requested part share."},
{"title":"Choose an aluminum-slot cutter","page":"Practice","summary":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-tool-aluminum-slot","text":"Choose an aluminum-slot cutter. A rigid setup must cut a full-width slot in aluminum. All tools listed fit the feature and holder. Which is the strongest starting choice? A four-flute steel finishing tool with little chip space, selected only because it has more teeth. A two-flute aluminum-rated tool with open chip space and published slotting data. Any ball end mill, because end shape does not matter. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. The material-specific geometry and slotting data address the actual cut. A suitable three-flute aluminum tool could also work, but it is not one of these choices. Tool material, reach, speed and feed still need checking."},
{"title":"Choose a steel side-milling cutter","page":"Practice","summary":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-tool-steel-side","text":"Choose a steel side-milling cutter. A rigid machine and fixture will side-mill steel. Each candidate has enough reach and fits the holder. Which starting choice is best justified? A steel-rated four-flute carbide tool with data for the intended engagement. An aluminum-specific tool chosen only for its polished appearance. A drill held in a drill chuck because its diameter matches. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. It matches both material and side-milling duty. Four flutes and carbide are not guarantees by themselves; the steel rating, setup and application data are what make this choice justified."},
{"title":"When HSS is a reasonable choice","page":"Practice","summary":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-tool-hss-choice","text":"When HSS is a reasonable choice. A modest-speed machine will make a light interrupted cut. The fixture is secure, and the tool maker permits this operation with either listed milling tool. Which reasoning is sound? Carbide is always best because hardness alone determines the result. Choose a drill chuck to absorb the interruptions. A suitable HSS end mill may be economical and more tolerant of impact; use its lower-speed data. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. Toughness, available speed and setup matter alongside hot hardness. This does not license a loose setup or justify using HSS outside its cutting-data range."},
{"title":"Flutes do not prove plunge capability","page":"Practice","summary":"End Mill Anatomy and Cutter Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-tool-center-cutting","text":"Flutes do not prove plunge capability. A pocket needs an entry from above. You have a two-flute end mill, but its entry capability is unknown. What should decide whether to plunge? Two flutes automatically mean it can drill straight down. Confirm center-cutting geometry and the manufacturer’s permitted entry method and limits. Any end mill can plunge if the feed is slow enough. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. Flute count alone does not establish center-cutting geometry. Choose a permitted ramp, plunge or prepared entry as the tool and operation require."},
{"title":"Chatter after a longer tool","page":"Practice","summary":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-chatter-reach","text":"Chatter after a longer tool. A formerly clean cut chatters after changing to a much longer end mill. Seating, runout and workholding have been checked. The feature does not need the extra reach. What is the first useful change? Shorten the unsupported tool reach while retaining the required shank engagement. Increase the overhang further. Change speed, feed and depth together so the cause is impossible to isolate. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 1. The changed reach is a clear loss of stiffness. Correct it before tuning around it. Use the shortest reach that still clears the work and holder."},
{"title":"Chatter only inside corners","page":"Practice","summary":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-chatter-corner","text":"Chatter only inside corners. A correctly seated, rigid setup cuts straight pocket walls cleanly but chatters in tight inside corners. What should you review first? Whether halving feed everywhere will hide the symptom without changing engagement. Whether doubling the axial depth will always damp the vibration. The increase in cutter engagement and the programmed corner path. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. More of the circumference can become engaged in a corner. Smooth or reduce engagement using a suitable path and cutting data, then test the change."},
{"title":"A stable setup at an unstable speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-chatter-speed","text":"A stable setup at an unstable speed. Seating, rigidity and engagement have been reviewed. Chatter appears at the current speed. Which next experiment is useful? Always halve feed while leaving speed unchanged. Try a different permitted spindle speed and review feed to preserve the intended chip load. Assume that a higher speed can never be stable. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 2. Speed changes the timing of tooth engagement. A different permitted speed may help, but the accompanying feed must still produce a suitable chip load. Slowing feed alone can cause rubbing."},
{"title":"The part lifted during a cut","page":"Practice","summary":"Diagnosing Milling Chatter","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#mill-chatter-part-lift","text":"The part lifted during a cut. After ending a noisy cut, you find a gap between the part and one parallel. What takes priority before another trial? Raise spindle speed to move away from resonance. Lower feed until the sound is hidden. Correct seating and grip, then recheck location before cutting. Enter the option number (1–3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Option 3. A moved part is a setup failure. Restore support and clamping and verify the part reference; do not try to tune speed around it."},
{"title":"Reflect load through a reduction","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-match-reflected-torque","text":"Reflect load through a reduction: A 20-tooth motor gear drives a 40-tooth output gear through one mesh modeled at 90% efficiency. The output load torque is 0.600 N·m. What motor torque is required at a steady running point? Give four significant figures. Answer in N·m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The reduction is i = 40/20 = 2. With stated running mesh efficiency η = 0.90, T motor = T load /(iη) = 0.600/(2 × 0.90) = 0.3333 N·m. Using 0.300 N·m ignores the modeled loss."},
{"title":"Find geared output speed under load","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-match-output-speed","text":"Find geared output speed: The motor has 0.800 N·m stall torque and 120 rpm no-load speed. It supplies the 0.3333 N·m reflected torque from the previous problem. Using the ideal linear motor curve and the 2:1 reduction, what is output speed? Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution n motor = 120(1 − 0.3333/0.800) = 70.0 rpm. The 2:1 reduction gives n output = 70.0/2 = 35.0 rpm. Dividing the 120 rpm no-load endpoint by two would ignore the loaded motor curve."},
{"title":"Find gearbox output power","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-match-output-power","text":"Find gearbox output power: The motor operates at 0.3333 N·m and 70.0 rpm. Find motor shaft power, then apply the stated 90% running mesh efficiency. Give output power in watts. You may use π = 3.14 and four-significant-figure intermediate values. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P motor = Tω = 0.3333 × 70.0 × 2π/60 = 2.443 W. Then P output = 0.90 × 2.443 = 2.199 W. Multiplying torque by rpm without converting to rad/s gives the wrong units and value."},
{"title":"Explain the changed output power","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-match-power-explanation","text":"Explain the comparison: In the lesson example, direct output power is 1.885 W while geared output power is 2.199 W. Which explanation matches the model? 1 = the gearbox created power; 2 = both cases had equal motor shaft power; 3 = reflected torque moved the motor to a different speed and shaft-power point before mesh loss; 4 = the mesh was more than 100% efficient. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3. The reduction changes the motor torque required by the same output load. The motor therefore settles at a different speed and produces different shaft power. The stated mesh loss is then applied. This is not free power and does not claim equal electrical input."},
{"title":"Compare direct overload with geared running","page":"Practice","summary":"DC Motor Torque, Speed, and Power","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motor-match-direct-overload","text":"Compare direct overload with geared running: Keep the 0.800 N·m, 120 rpm motor and the 2:1, 90% reduction, but raise output load torque to 0.900 N·m. Direct drive has no forward steady operating point in this model. What geared output speed does the model give? Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The reflected motor torque is 0.900/(2 × 0.90) = 0.500 N·m. The motor curve gives 120(1 − 0.500/0.800) = 45.0 rpm. After the 2:1 reduction, output speed is 22.5 rpm. This steady-running point does not prove the system starts or runs continuously."},
{"title":"A small-radius die opening","page":"Practice","summary":"Selecting a Material-Removal Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-hardened-opening","text":"A small-radius die opening A hardened steel die needs a through opening with small internal radii. A start hole is available. Which candidate best fits? Wire EDM Pure waterjet Ordinary drilling Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Wire EDM can trace the conductive hardened part with low cutting force. Check the required radius against wire radius plus gap; perfectly sharp corners are not possible."},
{"title":"A thick glass profile","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-glass-profile","text":"A thick glass profile A thick, non-tempered glass plate needs a through profile without a thermal HAZ. Which candidate fits? Plasma Abrasive waterjet Wire EDM Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Abrasive waterjet can cut nonconductive glass without a thermal HAZ. Thickness, piercing and edge quality still require checks."},
{"title":"A nonconductive ceramic","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Discharge Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-ceramic-edm","text":"A nonconductive ceramic Why is ordinary EDM unsuitable for an electrically insulating ceramic? The ceramic is too hard for every machining process EDM requires electrical conduction through the work EDM only makes round holes Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. EDM needs a discharge circuit through the workpiece. Hardness alone is not the deciding property."},
{"title":"A blind die cavity","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Discharge Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-blind-cavity","text":"A blind die cavity Which is the stronger EDM candidate for a blind cavity in hardened conductive steel? Wire EDM threaded through the cavity floor Sinker EDM with a shaped electrode Neither, because hardened steel cannot be EDMed Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. A sinker electrode can approach a blind cavity. Wire EDM requires a through path for the wire."},
{"title":"EDM removal energy","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Discharge Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-edm-energy","text":"EDM removal energy What removes material at the work in EDM? A sharp edge shearing chips Heat from electrical discharges Electrochemical dissolution without sparks Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Electrical power produces discharges whose local heat melts and vaporizes material."},
{"title":"ECM removal mechanism","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrochemical Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-ecm-energy","text":"ECM removal mechanism Which mechanism describes ECM? Anodic dissolution through a conductive electrolyte Laser heating through insulating oil Abrasive impacts Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The work is the anode. Current through the electrolyte supports dissolution; ECM is not spark erosion."},
{"title":"Laser removal energy","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-laser-energy","text":"Laser removal energy Which supplies the concentrated cutting energy in laser cutting? Moving abrasive grit An arc through a wire electrode Focused light Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The beam supplies thermal energy. The machine and wavelength must suit the material."},
{"title":"Plasma removal energy","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-plasma-energy","text":"Plasma removal energy Which describes ordinary plasma cutting? An electric arc in ionized gas melts conductive work A chemical bath dissolves glass Water alone cuts a hard steel die Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The arc supplies heat and the gas carries molten material away. Conductive metal is the candidate material."},
{"title":"Waterjet removal energy","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-waterjet-energy","text":"Waterjet removal energy What supplies removal energy in abrasive waterjet cutting? Electrical discharges through the work Mechanical energy of moving water and grit Light absorbed by the work Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The jet carries particles that erode material. Electricity may power the pump, but the removal mechanism is mechanical."},
{"title":"Ultrasonic removal energy","page":"Practice","summary":"Ultrasonic Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-ultrasonic-energy","text":"Ultrasonic removal energy In ultrasonic impact machining, what acts on the hard, brittle work? A shaped tool driving abrasive particles by vibration An ionized gas arc Electroplating Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The vibrating tool drives abrasive impacts through a slurry. This is distinct from adding vibration to conventional cutting."},
{"title":"Chemical removal mechanism","page":"Practice","summary":"Chemical Milling and Etching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-chemical-energy","text":"Chemical removal mechanism What removes exposed metal in ordinary chemical milling? A laser A rotating tool Reaction with an etchant Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. A mask protects selected areas while the etchant dissolves exposed metal. There is no external machining current as in ECM."},
{"title":"A soft foam gasket","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-pure-water","text":"A soft foam gasket Which is a reasonable first candidate for a soft foam gasket? Pure waterjet Plasma Wire EDM Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. Soft material may be separated with water alone. Abrasive is useful for harder materials; the gasket does not need to conduct electricity."},
{"title":"Explain a no-HAZ choice","page":"Practice","summary":"Selecting a Material-Removal Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-haz-reason","text":"Explain a no-HAZ choice A conductive plate must retain an edge without thermal alteration. Explain why abrasive waterjet is a candidate and why EDM is not automatically a substitute. Your reasoning Self-check: write first, then compare. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show a model answer Abrasive waterjet removes by mechanical erosion and normally produces no thermal HAZ. EDM removes by local melting and vaporization, so recast and thermal surface effects remain possible despite low cutting force. Waterjet edge quality and mechanical damage still need evaluation."},
{"title":"Distinguish the two liquids","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrochemical Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-ecm-fluid","text":"Distinguish the two liquids Explain the difference between ECM electrolyte and EDM dielectric, including why “ECM makes no heat” is misleading. Your reasoning Self-check: write first, then compare. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show a model answer ECM electrolyte conducts machining current and carries reaction products. EDM dielectric normally insulates until local breakdown permits a discharge, then helps cool and flush the gap. ECM does not melt the work, but current heats the electrolyte."},
{"title":"Thickness and finish","page":"Practice","summary":"Laser, Waterjet and Plasma Cutting","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-thickness-finish","text":"Thickness and finish A waterjet profile has acceptable dimensions at the top but an unacceptable lower edge. Name two process effects to investigate. Your reasoning Self-check: write first, then compare. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show a model answer Check taper (different cut widths through the thickness) and jet lag or striations. Thickness, travel speed and the cutting setup affect both. A no-HAZ process does not guarantee straight walls or the required surface finish."},
{"title":"Etching a narrow land","page":"Practice","summary":"Chemical Milling and Etching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-etch-undercut","text":"Etching a narrow land Why might a narrow strip of protected metal end up narrower than its mask pattern? Your reasoning Self-check: write first, then compare. Your response is not automatically graded or saved. Show a model answer The etchant can attack sideways beneath the mask edge as well as downward. This undercut must be allowed for in mask design and limits small features, especially for deeper etches."},
{"title":"Wire center-path offset","page":"Practice","summary":"Electrical Discharge Machining","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#nt-wire-offset","text":"Wire center-path offset For this teaching example, the wire radius is 0.125 mm and the one-sided spark gap is 0.025 mm. What is the offset magnitude from the finished wall to the wire center path? Give mm rounded to the nearest 0.001 mm. Offset in mm Check answer Enter an offset in mm. Show answer and reasoning Offset = radius + gap = 0.125 + 0.025 = 0.150 mm. The center path is on the removed-material side of the wall. 0.125 mm ignores the gap; 0.175 mm adds two gaps; 0.275 mm uses the wire diameter. The full kerf is 0.300 mm, not the one-sided offset."},
{"title":"Choose a base feature","page":"Practice","summary":"Extrude and Revolve","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-base","text":"Choose a base feature. A round knob has several coaxial outside steps and a central bore. Which base best keeps those radial and axial dimensions in one section? Choose 1 = revolve, 2 = shell, 3 = linear pattern. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1: Revolve. A section can describe the steps and bore together. This is a design-intent choice; extrusions could also construct the shape."},
{"title":"Keep a cut through","page":"Practice","summary":"Extrude and Revolve","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-end-condition","text":"Keep a cut through. A hole must pass through a single plate even when the plate thickness increases. Choose 1 = blind at the current thickness, 2 = through all in the cutting direction, 3 = blind at half the thickness. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 2: Through all expresses the requirement. A blind cut tied only to the old thickness may leave a floor."},
{"title":"Split a total depth","page":"Practice","summary":"Extrude and Revolve","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-midplane","text":"Split a total depth. A SOLIDWORKS Mid Plane extrusion has a total depth of 18 mm. How far does it extend from the sketch plane on each side? Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 18 ÷ 2 = 9.00 mm on each side. Using 18 mm on each side would double the total depth."},
{"title":"Count a minimum with stated limits","page":"Practice","summary":"Extrude and Revolve","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-minimum","text":"Count a minimum with stated limits. Starting with no solid, make a plain rectangular block with one circular through hole. Allow only ordinary straight boss extrusions from closed sketch regions, including nested loops. Count solid features only, not sketches. What is the minimum number of solid features? Answer in features Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1 feature. Extrude a rectangular region with a circular region excluded all the way through. A separate boss and cut uses 2 features and may express independent design intent better, but is not the minimum under these rules."},
{"title":"Find a lost reference","page":"Practice","summary":"Feature-based Modeling","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-parent","text":"Find a lost reference. The tree is: plate extrusion; hole using a sketch on its top face; pattern using that hole; an unrelated edge chamfer. Delete only the hole while retaining the plate. Which feature definitely loses its stated input? Choose 1 = plate extrusion, 2 = hole pattern, 3 = every earlier feature. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 2: The hole pattern loses its seed. Deletion dialogs may offer dependent deletions. Do not infer that every later feature must fail merely because it appears later."},
{"title":"Estimate mass from volume","page":"Practice","summary":"Feature-based Modeling","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-mass","text":"Estimate mass from volume. A uniform solid spacer is 24 × 15 × 10 mm with no holes. Use the assumed density 2.50 g/cm³. What is its mass in grams? Give two decimal places. Answer in g Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning Volume = 24 × 15 × 10 = 3,600 mm³ = 3.6 cm³. Mass = 3.6 × 2.50 = 9.00 g. There are 1,000 mm³ in 1 cm³; multiplying 3,600 directly by the density would give a result 1,000 times too large."},
{"title":"Recognize a profile and path","page":"Practice","summary":"Sweeps and Lofts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-sweep","text":"Recognize a profile and path. You have a circular section and a smooth bent centerline. You want a solid rod of constant circular size along that line. Choose the simplest starting feature: 1 = chamfer, 2 = sweep, 3 = shell. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 2: Sweep the circular profile along the path. Verify profile alignment and enough bend radius to avoid self-intersection."},
{"title":"Connect changing sections","page":"Practice","summary":"Sweeps and Lofts","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-loft","text":"Connect changing sections. You have a large oval section, a medium oval section, and a small oval section on three separated planes. Which feature directly transitions through these ordered sections? Choose 1 = linear pattern, 2 = fillet, 3 = loft. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 3: Loft. Select the profiles in spatial order and inspect the connections. Add guides only when needed to control the shape between sections."},
{"title":"Count the spaces in a row","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-linear-span","text":"Count the spaces in a row. A single-direction linear pattern has 5 total hole instances including the seed, with 12 mm between adjacent centers. No instances are skipped. What is the first-to-last center distance? Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning There are 5 − 1 = 4 intervals. Span = 4 × 12 = 48.00 mm. Multiplying by 5 counts one interval too many."},
{"title":"Space a full-circle pattern","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-circular-pitch","text":"Space a full-circle pattern. Eight total holes, including the seed, are evenly spaced around a complete circle, with no duplicate at 360°. What is the angular pitch between adjacent holes? Answer in degrees Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 360° ÷ 8 = 45.00°. The last-to-first gap is also an interval; dividing by 7 is for a different endpoint-inclusive arc construction."},
{"title":"Choose symmetry","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-mirror","text":"Choose symmetry. A tab must have an oppositely handed counterpart at equal distance across a center plane. Choose 1 = mirror, 2 = translate with a linear pattern, 3 = shell. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1: Mirror reflects the tab. Translation preserves handedness and does not encode plane symmetry."},
{"title":"Hollow a solid","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-shell","text":"Hollow a solid. A solid block must become an open container with a controlled wall and floor thickness. Choose 1 = fillet, 2 = shell with the top face removed, 3 = circular pattern. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 2: Shell. Select the opening face, thickness, and inward or outward direction, then inspect the result in section."},
{"title":"Add a support web","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-rib","text":"Add a support web. You have a sketch line spanning two supporting walls and need a thin reinforcing web with controlled thickness. Choose 1 = chamfer, 2 = shell, 3 = rib. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 3: Rib. The sketch, thickness, direction, and target part define the support. Check that the web joins the walls."},
{"title":"Tilt faces for release","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-draft","text":"Tilt faces for release. Side faces must taper relative to a pull direction while the neutral-plane section stays fixed. Choose 1 = draft, 2 = mirror, 3 = circular pattern. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1: Draft. Check which end grows or shrinks. The required angle depends on the design and process; it is not supplied by this question."},
{"title":"Choose a cylindrical recess","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-hole-type","text":"Choose a cylindrical recess. A hole needs a larger cylindrical recess with a flat shoulder above its smaller bore. Choose 1 = countersink, 2 = counterbore, 3 = plain hole with no recess. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 2: Counterbore. A countersink has a conical recess. Define the type and size separately from the center position."},
{"title":"Distinguish bevel from radius","page":"Practice","summary":"Patterns, Mirror, and Applied Features","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#part-features-edge-treatment","text":"Distinguish bevel from radius. A sharp outside edge must become a straight bevel in section, rather than a rounded transition. Choose 1 = chamfer, 2 = fillet, 3 = rib. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show reasoning 1: Chamfer. A fillet gives a curved transition; a rib adds a support web."},
{"title":"Identify the oblique pictorial","page":"Practice","summary":"Pictorial Sketching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-type","text":"Identify the oblique pictorial. In the comparison, which numbered panel uses oblique sketching? Panel 1 is an isometric block. Panel 2 has a rectangular front face and parallel diagonal receding edges. Choose an answer Select one Panel 1 Panel 2 Both panels Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Panel 2 preserves the rectangular front face and sends parallel edges along one receding direction. Panel 1 uses isometric directions."},
{"title":"Locate a non-isometric line","page":"Practice","summary":"Pictorial Sketching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-non-isometric","text":"Locate a non-isometric line. Which line on the beveled block is non-isometric? The beveled block has a diagonal edge PQ across its front face, as well as vertical and depth-parallel edges. Choose an answer Select one A vertical side edge An edge running in the depth direction The sloping line PQ Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation PQ is parallel to none of the three isometric axes. Locate P and Q using axis-parallel offsets, then join them."},
{"title":"Sketch a circle on an isometric top face","page":"Practice","summary":"Pictorial Sketching","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-circle","text":"Sketch a circle on an isometric top face. A circular feature lies on the horizontal top face of an isometric block. What should its sketch look like? Choose an answer Select one A circle drawn with a compass An ellipse within its isometric enclosing square A diamond joining the four midpoints Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The face is tilted to the picture plane, so the circle appears elliptical. The four midpoints locate tangencies; joining them with straight lines does not form the curve."},
{"title":"Find the edge views of face A","page":"Practice","summary":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-surface-edge","text":"Find the edge views of face A. In the surface diagram, which principal views show face A as an edge? A labels the front face of a beveled block; B labels its sloping face. Front, top, and right views show the corresponding areas and edges. Choose an answer Select one Front only Top and right All three Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A is parallel to the front viewing plane, so front shows its true shape. Top and right look along that face and collapse it to a line."},
{"title":"Match a beveled block to its top view","page":"Practice","summary":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-top-match","text":"Match a beveled block to its top view. Which candidate is the top view? The front of the part is at the bottom of every candidate. A block has an upper-right corner removed through its full depth. Candidate 1 has a horizontal divider, 2 a divider near the right, 3 a divider near the left. Choose an answer Select one Candidate 1 Candidate 2 Candidate 3 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Candidate 2. The bevel runs through the entire depth and begins two-thirds of the way across the width from the left. Its boundary therefore runs front to back near the right side. Candidate 1 changes along depth; candidate 3 puts the boundary on the wrong side."},
{"title":"Classify sloping face B","page":"Practice","summary":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-inclined","text":"Classify sloping face B. B appears as an edge in front and a foreshortened area in top and right. What type of flat surface is it? A labels the front face of a beveled block; B labels its sloping face. Front, top, and right views show the corresponding areas and edges. Choose an answer Select one Normal Inclined Oblique Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Inclined. B is perpendicular to the front projection plane and tilted to the other two. The sloping line in front is its edge view."},
{"title":"Recognize an oblique surface","page":"Practice","summary":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-oblique-surface","text":"Recognize an oblique surface. A flat face is tilted to all three principal planes, parallel to none and perpendicular to none. Which statement is correct? Choose an answer Select one It shows true shape in top It disappears to an edge in right It is foreshortened in all three principal views Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation An oblique surface shows an area in all three principal views, but none is its true shape. This describes surface orientation, not the pictorial method."},
{"title":"Check before extruding a profile","page":"Practice","summary":"Visualizing Parts from Multiview Drawings","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#pictorial-depth-check","text":"Check before extruding a profile. You have recognized the front profile. What must you check before carrying it unchanged through the full depth? Choose an answer Select one Whether top and side support a constant cross-section Whether the front outline matches the overall width and height Whether every line is already dark Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Top and side must support a constant cross-section. A pocket or step may end partway through the depth; the front outline alone cannot establish that. A matching overall width and height confirms only the enclosing box."},
{"title":"Monomers and polymers","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-polymer-building-blocks","text":"Monomers and polymers: Which statement correctly connects monomers and polymers? A monomer is a finished plastic part. Monomers react to build polymer molecules. Polymer means any small metal particle. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Small starting molecules react to build the much larger polymer molecules. Polymer chains may also branch or become chemically linked."},
{"title":"Reheating cured epoxy","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-cured-epoxy","text":"Reheating cured epoxy: A fully cured epoxy casting must be reshaped. Which prediction fits its material type? It is a thermoplastic and can simply be remelted. It is a thermoset and cannot be remelted into a new shape. It becomes a monomer automatically when warmed. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. Cured epoxy is a thermoset. Its chemically linked network does not become a reusable melt. Heating is not a way to return it to the uncured liquid."},
{"title":"Reheating polypropylene","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-pp-reheating","text":"Reheating polypropylene: A clean PP part can soften and flow again under appropriate processing conditions. What does that tell you? It is a thermoplastic; local recycling acceptance is a separate question. It is a thermoset because it started as a solid. Every local recycling program must accept it. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. PP is a thermoplastic. Remeltability is useful for recycling, but the actual item still needs a compatible collection and processing route."},
{"title":"A long run of clips","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-clips","text":"A long run of clips: A manufacturer needs 250,000 identical PP clips with small ribs and a controlled mating shape. The design can release from a mold. Which process is the strongest starting candidate? Hand layup Rotational molding Injection molding Extrusion Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3: Injection molding. Injection molding repeats detailed discrete parts. The quantity can spread the mold cost. Extrusion cannot directly vary the shape along each clip."},
{"title":"Thin-walled bottles","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-bottles","text":"Thin-walled bottles: A packaging line needs 600,000 hollow HDPE detergent bottles with narrow necks. Which process best fits the shape and quantity? Blow molding Compression molding Hand layup Casting Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1: Blow molding. Blow molding expands hot plastic into hollow containers. HDPE bottles commonly use extrusion blow molding. It does not need a removable core filling the entire bottle interior."},
{"title":"Large hollow tanks","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-tank","text":"Large hollow tanks: An order calls for 240 large hollow PE water-storage tanks. Fine surface detail is unnecessary and longer molding cycles are acceptable. Which process deserves the first investigation? Extrusion Injection molding Single-sheet vacuum forming Rotational molding Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 4: Rotational molding. Rotational molding suits large hollow PE shells and modest quantities. Its slower heating and cooling cycle is acceptable here. Confirm wall control and delivery rate before final selection."},
{"title":"A short run of trays","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-trays","text":"A short run of trays: A supplier needs 180 shallow PETG trays, each open on top, using available sheet. Which process fits the starting stock and geometry? Blow molding Vacuum forming Rotational molding Compression molding of thermoset compound Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2: Vacuum forming. Vacuum forming reshapes hot thermoplastic sheet against a tool. A simple tool can suit a short run. Check draw depth, thinning and draft."},
{"title":"Continuous channel","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-profile","text":"Continuous channel: A building product needs thousands of meters of PVC channel with the same cross-section throughout. Which process fits? Extrusion Casting Hand layup Blow molding Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1: Extrusion. Extrusion produces a continuous profile through a die. End features or isolated side holes would need later operations."},
{"title":"Repeated thermoset parts","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-compression","text":"Repeated thermoset parts: A producer needs 40,000 electrical components from a thermosetting molding compound. Both faces need shaped detail, and the charge can spread in matched heated tools. Which process fits? Single-sheet vacuum forming Rotational molding Compression molding Extrusion of thermoplastic pipe Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3: Compression molding. Compression molding presses the charge between matched tools and allows the thermoset to cure. A cooling-only explanation would miss the chemical change."},
{"title":"Twenty detailed replicas","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-cast","text":"Twenty detailed replicas: A designer needs 20 small detailed solid replicas in a casting-grade two-part polyurethane. Flexible molds are available and cure time is acceptable. Which process fits? Extrusion Blow molding Vacuum forming Casting Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 4: Casting. Casting matches the liquid thermosetting resin and short run. Flexible tooling can reproduce detail, but mold life and trapped air still matter."},
{"title":"Custom reinforced shells","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-process-layup","text":"Custom reinforced shells: A maker needs eight broad glass-fiber/epoxy equipment covers. One smooth tool-side face is sufficient and manual work is acceptable. Which process fits? Blow molding Wet hand layup Extrusion Injection molding of unfilled PP Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2: Wet hand layup. Wet hand layup builds reinforcement and thermosetting resin against a mold. The resulting material belongs to Wood & Composites in the course model. A one-sided mold does not precisely control both faces."},
{"title":"A dimple opposite a thick rib","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastic Forming Processes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-design-sink","text":"A dimple opposite a thick rib: An injection-molded cover has a shallow depression on the visible face directly opposite a heavy rib. Which design change should be investigated first? Make every rib as thick as possible. Reduce the heavy rib or core out the local mass. Remove all draft so the part stays in place. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The thick region can shrink differently and pull the surface inward, producing sink. Reduce the local mass and check packing and cooling; filling every region thicker is not a general cure."},
{"title":"A part scrapes during ejection","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastic Forming Processes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-design-draft","text":"A part scrapes during ejection: A deep molded wall is parallel to the opening direction and scrapes the tool as the part is pushed out. Which feature is missing or insufficient? Draft Resin identification code A higher cavity count Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1: draft. A slight release taper reduces contact as the mold opens. Required draft depends on depth, texture, material and tooling."},
{"title":"A sudden thick-to-thin wall change","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastic Forming Processes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-design-walls","text":"A sudden thick-to-thin wall change: A housing changes abruptly from a thin wall to a bulky solid region. Which redesign best addresses the geometry problem? Make the bulky region larger. Add a recycling code to the thick region. Core out unnecessary material and use gradual transitions. Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. A more uniform section and gradual transitions help manage filling and shrinkage. The goal is a manufacturable shape, not merely a larger mass of plastic."},
{"title":"PET bottle resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-pet","text":"PET bottle resin: Which resin identification code matches PET (also called PETE)? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1. Code 1 identifies polyethylene terephthalate. Do not generalize that code to every material with PET in its name."},
{"title":"HDPE container resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-hdpe","text":"HDPE container resin: Which resin identification code matches HDPE? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2. Code 2 identifies high-density polyethylene."},
{"title":"PVC resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-pvc","text":"PVC resin: Which resin identification code matches PVC (vinyl)? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3. Code 3 identifies polyvinyl chloride."},
{"title":"LDPE film resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-ldpe","text":"LDPE film resin: Which resin identification code matches LDPE? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 4. Code 4 identifies low-density polyethylene."},
{"title":"PP lid resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-pp","text":"PP lid resin: Which resin identification code matches PP? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 5. Code 5 identifies polypropylene. A code does not guarantee that a local program accepts that lid."},
{"title":"Polystyrene resin","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-ps","text":"Polystyrene resin: Which resin identification code matches PS? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 6. Code 6 identifies polystyrene, including familiar rigid and foamed products."},
{"title":"The OTHER resin category","page":"Practice","summary":"Plastics as Materials","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-code-other","text":"The OTHER resin category: Which resin identification code matches a resin outside categories 1–6, such as polycarbonate? Enter a number from 1 to 7. This question identifies resin, not local recycling acceptance. Resin code number (1–7) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 7. Code 7 is OTHER. It does not identify one resin, and it does not mean all code-7 items can be recycled together."},
{"title":"Parts per hour from four cavities","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-cavity-output","text":"Parts per hour from four cavities: An ideal molding cycle takes 48 seconds and produces one part in each of four identical cavities. Assume no downtime or rejects. How many parts are produced per hour? Report to the nearest whole part per hour. Answer in parts/h Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3600 s/h ÷ 48 s/cycle = 75 cycles/h. Then 75 × 4 = 300 parts/h. Reporting 75 ignores the four cavities. Multiplying cycle time by cavity count does not give an hourly rate."},
{"title":"Tooling cost per part","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-tooling-share","text":"Tooling cost per part: An illustrative mold costs $7,500 and will make 2,400 acceptable parts with no replacement tooling. What is the tooling cost alone per part? Ignore material and operating costs. Round to the nearest cent. Answer in USD/part Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning $7500 ÷ 2400 = $3.125 per part, or $3.13 per part to the nearest cent. Dividing quantity by tooling cost gives 0.32 parts per dollar, the inverse quantity."},
{"title":"When two routes cost the same","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Plastic Forming Process","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-cost-crossover","text":"When two routes cost the same: Two hypothetical routes meet the same specification. A costs $1,200 for tooling plus $9 per part. B costs $13,200 for tooling plus $3 per part. Assume constant variable costs, no scrap and no tooling replacement. At what quantity do total costs match? Report to the nearest whole part. Answer in parts Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Set 1200 + 9N = 13200 + 3N. Thus 6N = 12000, and N = 2,000 parts. Each route then costs $19,200. Using $13,200 without subtracting A's tooling cost gives 2,200, which is wrong."},
{"title":"A melted routed edge","page":"Practice","summary":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-chip-welding","text":"A melted routed edge. An acrylic slot has softened chips stuck to its edge. The tool is rubbing at a very low feed per tooth. Which is the best next investigation? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Reduce feed further without changing RPM. 2. Check sharpness and chip clearance, then correct feed and RPM together using suitable cutting data. 3. Use a dull cutter to make smaller chips. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 2. Rubbing and trapped chips generate heat. A lower feed by itself can worsen the problem; the tool, feed, RPM and chip path need to work together."},
{"title":"Cutting a clear polycarbonate profile","page":"Practice","summary":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-clear-sheet","text":"Cutting a clear polycarbonate profile. A clear polycarbonate sheet needs a curved profile with a clean edge. Which is the best starting plan? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Use acrylic CO2 laser settings because both sheets are clear. 2. Assume all clear plastics release the same products under heat. 3. Evaluate a router or saw with tooling and cutting data for the polycarbonate grade. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 3. Polycarbonate can discolor and char under CO2 cutting. A clear appearance does not establish either the resin or its processing behavior."},
{"title":"Preserving the molded wall","page":"Practice","summary":"Cutting and Machining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-degating","text":"Preserving the molded wall. A molded part has a feed connection still attached at its gate. Which result should degating achieve? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Remove the connection while preserving the specified part contour. 2. Cut below the wall surface to ensure no trace remains. 3. Leave a sharp notch to show where the gate was. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 1. The connection is excess material, but the wall is part of the finished product. A notch can weaken it."},
{"title":"A permanent clear corner","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-acrylic-joint","text":"A permanent clear corner. Two identified acrylic pieces have closely fitting edges and need a permanent clear corner. Which candidate deserves evaluation first? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Any hot-melt stick, without a material check. 2. A solvent cement specified for that acrylic grade and joint fit. 3. A solvent selected only by its smell. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 2. Compatible solvent cement can soften the mating surfaces and develop a joint as solvent leaves. Fit, residual stress and the full strength-development time still matter."},
{"title":"An adhesive peels from polypropylene","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-pp-bond","text":"An adhesive peels from polypropylene. A fully cured general-purpose adhesive peels cleanly from a clean PP sample. What material issue should be investigated? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. PP is always impossible to bond. 2. The joint must need a thicker layer of the same adhesive. 3. Low surface energy and whether the adhesive or surface treatment is specified for PP. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 3. PP resists wetting by many ordinary adhesives. Specialized systems can work, so neither impossible nor just add more glue is a sound conclusion."},
{"title":"A cover that opens repeatedly","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-removable-cover","text":"A cover that opens repeatedly. A thermoplastic cover must be removed regularly for inspection. Which candidate best addresses that requirement? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Screws with a suitable threaded-insert arrangement and adequate surrounding plastic. 2. A continuous solvent-cemented seam. 3. A permanent fusion weld around every edge. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 1. A mechanical joint permits disassembly. Insert geometry and the plastic supporting the insert still govern pullout and cracking; a metal thread alone is not enough."},
{"title":"A weld that looks continuous","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-weld-seam","text":"A weld that looks continuous. An extrusion weld looks smooth but leaks during the specified inspection. Which conclusion is justified? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Smooth beads are hermetic, so the inspection must be wrong. 2. Appearance does not prove fusion or sealing; review material compatibility, joint preparation and heating. 3. Add paint and call the weld accepted. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 2. Compatible filler must bond to adequately prepared and heated base material. A continuous-looking bead is not proof of a leak-tight joint."},
{"title":"A joint creeps in warmth","page":"Practice","summary":"Joining Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-hot-melt","text":"A joint creeps in warmth. A part bonded with an ordinary hot-melt adhesive holds at room temperature but slips under sustained load when warm. What is the likely selection issue? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. The adhesive must have fusion-welded both parts. 2. Warm conditions guarantee stronger adhesive bonds. 3. The adhesive service-temperature and creep limits may not suit the load. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 3. Hot-melt adhesive forms its own bond layer. Fast handling strength does not establish resistance to a sustained load at a higher temperature."},
{"title":"A printed bar in tension","page":"Practice","summary":"FDM 3D Printing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-orientation","text":"A printed bar in tension. A rectangular FDM test bar will be pulled along its length. With comparable material and settings, which orientation is the better starting candidate? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Length lying in the build plane, with paths carrying load along the bar. 2. Length standing vertically, so tension pulls across stacked layers. 3. Either orientation must have identical strength at 100% infill. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 1. Deposited roads can carry the main load along the layers. Across-layer tension relies on interlayer bonds. This is a starting choice to test, not a guaranteed strength value."},
{"title":"An inaccessible supported roof","page":"Practice","summary":"FDM 3D Printing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-support-choice","text":"An inaccessible supported roof. The slicer places ordinary breakaway support under a long roof inside a closed cavity. Which change should be evaluated? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Leave the support because all support disappears on cooling. 2. Reorient or redesign the part so the underside is printable and any support can be removed. 3. Reduce layer height and assume all overhangs become self-supporting. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 2. Support must be removable. Reorientation, splitting the part for assembly or redesigning the roof may help; finer layers alone do not solve an inaccessible support."},
{"title":"Painting a detailed PLA part","page":"Practice","summary":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-finish-selection","text":"Painting a detailed PLA part. A detailed PLA part needs an opaque painted finish while preserving fit. Which candidate is most appropriate to trial? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Treat acetone vapor smoothing as a universal plastic process. 2. Flame-polish it using an acrylic edge procedure. 3. Controlled sanding and a compatible filler/primer and paint system, keeping fit surfaces within tolerance. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 3. Mechanical preparation and a material-compatible coating are appropriate candidates. ABS acetone smoothing and acrylic flame polishing are not transferable recipes for PLA."},
{"title":"A print changes size after annealing","page":"Practice","summary":"Conditioning and Finishing Plastics","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-annealing-fit","text":"A print changes size after annealing. A PLA part has better heat resistance after a validated annealing trial, but no longer fits its mating part. What should be concluded? Choose one answer Select a choice 1. Conditioning can change both properties and dimensions; fit must be evaluated as well. 2. Improved heat resistance proves every dimension is acceptable. 3. All PLA should instead be heated nearly to molding temperature. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show reasoning Choice 1. Annealing can change crystalline structure and dimensions. Its usefulness depends on the complete requirement, including the fit and the grade-specific schedule."},
{"title":"Count equal-height layers","page":"Practice","summary":"FDM 3D Printing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-layer-count","text":"Count equal-height layers. A simplified print is 30 mm tall. Every layer, including the first, is 0.20 mm thick. How many layers does it have? Give a whole number. Answer in layers Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 30 mm ÷ 0.20 mm/layer = 150 layers. This assumes constant layer height and says nothing about travel or heating time."},
{"title":"Estimate deposited filament mass","page":"Practice","summary":"FDM 3D Printing","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#plastics-filament-mass","text":"Estimate deposited filament mass. Use a simplified volume model: walls and skins total 6 cm³, and the remaining interior is 30 cm³ at 20% infill. Assume a density of 1.24 g/cm³. Ignore supports, purge, brim and other waste. Estimate mass in grams to the nearest 0.1 g. Answer in g Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Deposited volume = 6 + (30 × 0.20) = 12 cm³. Mass = 12 × 1.24 = 14.88 g, or 14.9 g to the nearest 0.1 g. Applying 20% to the entire 36 cm³ would incorrectly reduce the solid walls and give 8.928 g. Real slicer paths need not match this ideal volume model exactly."},
{"title":"Recognize a concealed edge","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-hidden","text":"Recognize a concealed edge. An edge is blocked by material in this view. Which line represents it? Choose an answer Select one Thin, evenly spaced short dashes Thin long–short pattern Thick continuous line Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Thin, evenly spaced short dashes. Short dashes identify a hidden edge. A center line locates an axis; a visible line shows an edge you can see."},
{"title":"Recognize a visible edge","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-visible","text":"Recognize a visible edge. Which line would you read as an edge directly visible from this viewing direction? Choose an answer Select one Thin diagonal hatching Thick continuous line Thin long–short–short pattern Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Thick continuous line. A relatively thick continuous line is a visible or object line."},
{"title":"Distinguish an axis from an edge","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-center","text":"Distinguish an axis from an edge. A thin long–short line passes through the center of a circular hole. What does it identify? Choose an answer Select one A crack A second bore wall The hole center or axis Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The hole center or axis. The center line locates the hole center or axis. It is not a physical edge."},
{"title":"Recognize an alternate position","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-phantom","text":"Recognize an alternate position. A lever outline is repeated in a thin long–short–short pattern beside its solid outline. What is the likely meaning? Choose an answer Select one The lever has a hidden hole An alternate lever position A cutting plane Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation An alternate lever position. Phantom lines can show an alternate position. Use nearby notes to confirm what the alternate outline represents."},
{"title":"Read a dimension line","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-dimension","text":"Read a dimension line. A thin line has arrowheads at both ends and the value 42 between them. What kind of line is it? Choose an answer Select one Dimension Center Break Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Dimension. The dimension line identifies the extent of the stated measurement. Read the value and the drawing units rather than measuring the image."},
{"title":"Read an extension line","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-extension","text":"Read an extension line. Thin lines start just outside two part edges and extend to the ends of a dimension line. What are they? Choose an answer Select one Hidden edges Extension lines Phantom lines Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Extension lines. Extension lines carry feature locations to the dimension line. The small gap helps distinguish them from part edges."},
{"title":"Follow a note to its feature","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-leader","text":"Follow a note to its feature. A thin bent line joins a hole note to an arrow touching the hole outline. What is the line? Choose an answer Select one Section hatch Break line Leader Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Leader. A leader connects the note to the feature it describes. Follow its endpoint before applying the note."},
{"title":"Read cutting-plane arrows","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-cut","text":"Read cutting-plane arrows. A prominent line across a view has arrows and the letter B at both ends. A separate view is labeled SECTION B–B. What do the arrows tell you? Choose an answer Select one The direction to look into the section The drilling feed direction The direction to measure the length Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The direction to look into the section. The arrows set the viewing direction for the imaginary cut. B–B connects the cut location to its section view."},
{"title":"Read section hatching","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-hatch","text":"Read section hatching. Thin diagonal strokes fill a region in a section view. What do they ordinarily show? Choose an answer Select one Surface scratches Material crossed by the imaginary cut Hidden outlines of another part Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Material crossed by the imaginary cut. Hatching identifies cut material. It does not instruct someone to machine diagonal grooves."},
{"title":"Read a shortened view","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-break","text":"Read a shortened view. A long uniform bar is drawn with zigzag break lines and an overall length dimension. How should you interpret it? Choose an answer Select one The bar must be supplied in two pieces The zigzags are teeth on its surface Part of the view is omitted; read the full length dimension Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Part of the view is omitted; read the full length dimension. A break can shorten a long view on the sheet. It does not by itself mean the physical bar is fractured."},
{"title":"Resolve coincident line types","page":"Practice","summary":"The Alphabet of Lines","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-lines-overlap","text":"Resolve coincident line types. A visible edge and a hidden edge project to exactly the same segment. Which line should you expect there? Choose an answer Select one Visible line Hidden line Both patterns superimposed Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Visible line. The visible line takes precedence over the hidden line at the overlap. Consult another view to recover the concealed geometry."},
{"title":"Identify the front view","page":"Practice","summary":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-front","text":"Identify the front view. Which candidate is the front view of the pictured part? The numbered candidates are laid out as choices, not as a projection arrangement. A tall-left stepped block with front, top, and right viewing directions. Candidate 1 is a rectangle with a horizontal line; candidate 2 has a tall left step in its outline; candidate 3 is a rectangle with a vertical line near its left edge. Choose an answer Select one View 1 View 2 View 3 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation View 2. View 2 follows the tall-left stepped profile. Width is horizontal and height is vertical."},
{"title":"Identify the top view","page":"Practice","summary":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-top","text":"Identify the top view. Which candidate is the top view of the pictured part? The numbered candidates are laid out as choices, not as a projection arrangement. A tall-left stepped block with front, top, and right viewing directions. Candidate 1 is a rectangle with a horizontal line; candidate 2 has a tall left step in its outline; candidate 3 is a rectangle with a vertical line near its left edge. Choose an answer Select one View 1 View 2 View 3 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation View 3. View 3 shows the rectangular footprint, split at the width where the height changes. Looking down removes height from the outline."},
{"title":"Identify the right-side view","page":"Practice","summary":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-right","text":"Identify the right-side view. Which candidate is the right view of the pictured part? The numbered candidates are laid out as choices, not as a projection arrangement. A tall-left stepped block with front, top, and right viewing directions. Candidate 1 is a rectangle with a horizontal line; candidate 2 has a tall left step in its outline; candidate 3 is a rectangle with a vertical line near its left edge. Choose an answer Select one View 1 View 2 View 3 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation View 1. View 1 shows depth and height. The near low shelf gives the visible horizontal edge; the tall strip behind it reaches the top outline."},
{"title":"Follow a dimension between views","page":"Practice","summary":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-shared","text":"Follow a dimension between views. Which dimension is shared by the front and top views at the same scale? Choose an answer Select one Height Depth Width Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Width. Front shows width and height; top shows width and depth. Width is their shared direction."},
{"title":"Recognize a face seen edge-on","page":"Practice","summary":"Orthographic Projection and the Glass Box","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-face","text":"Recognize a face seen edge-on. You look exactly along a flat face so that the face is edge-on to you. How does that face project? Choose an answer Select one As a line As its true rectangular shape As a larger rectangle Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation As a line. An edge-on face collapses to a line. Its full area appears only when the viewing direction exposes the face."},
{"title":"Decide whether a plate needs another view","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Drawing Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-plate","text":"Decide whether a plate needs another view. A flat plate has a fully specified face outline and holes. A note states its uniform thickness; there are no other features. Must you add two more views just to reach three? Choose an answer Select one Yes, every part requires three views No, a complete one-view description can be enough Yes, because thickness cannot be stated in a note Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation No, a complete one-view description can be enough. The target is an unambiguous definition. Under the stated assumptions, a face view plus thickness information can define the shape."},
{"title":"Find information missing from one view","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing Drawing Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-views-depth","text":"Find information missing from one view. Two solid blocks have identical front outlines but different depths. Which added view directly distinguishes their depths? Choose an answer Select one Another copy of the front view A larger front view A top view at the same scale Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A top view at the same scale. The top view shows depth. Repeating or enlarging the front view does not reveal it."},
{"title":"Place a right view in first-angle","page":"Practice","summary":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-angle-right","text":"Place a right view in first-angle. The drawing declares first-angle projection. Where is the right-side view normally placed relative to the front? Choose an answer Select one To the left To the right Always above Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation To the left. First-angle puts the right-side view to the left of the front. The name still identifies the direction from which you look."},
{"title":"Place a top view in third-angle","page":"Practice","summary":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-angle-top","text":"Place a top view in third-angle. The drawing declares third-angle projection. Where is the top view normally placed relative to the front? Choose an answer Select one Below Above To the left Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Above. Third-angle places the top above the front. Front and top remain aligned by width."},
{"title":"Confirm the drawing convention","page":"Practice","summary":"First-Angle and Third-Angle Projection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#reading-angle-check","text":"Confirm the drawing convention. A drawing from a U.S. supplier has a clearly marked first-angle symbol. What should you do when interpreting the layout? Choose an answer Select one Assume third-angle because of the supplier location Use either layout; they are interchangeable Use the declared first-angle convention and check view consistency Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Use the declared first-angle convention and check view consistency. The explicit convention controls interpretation. A country or unit system is not a substitute for the symbol."},
{"title":"Index table in radians","page":"Practice","summary":"Angular Motion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#angular-degrees-to-radians","text":"1. Index table in radians: A rotary index table advances 40&deg; between one station and the next. How many radians is one advance? Answer in rad Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Put degrees on the bottom so they cancel: 40 &deg; &times; 0.01745329 rad 1 &deg; = 0.6981317&hellip; rad or 0.6981 rad. The same thing exactly: 40&deg; &times; &pi;/180&deg; = 2&pi;/9 rad. A quick check on the size of the answer - one radian is about 57.3&deg;, so 40&deg; should come out a little under 1 rad, and it does."},
{"title":"Nameplate speed into the formula","page":"Practice","summary":"Angular Motion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#angular-nameplate-to-rad-s","text":"2. Nameplate speed into the formula: A gearbox output shaft turns at 340 r/min. What is its angular velocity in rad/s? Answer in rad/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 340 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s 1 r/min = 35.60472&hellip; rad/s or 35.60 rad/s. The factor is not measured, it is the two definitions divided: 2&pi; rad per revolution &divide; 60 s per minute = 0.104719755&hellip;"},
{"title":"Spindle slowing down","page":"Practice","summary":"Angular Motion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#angular-spindle-slowdown","text":"3. Spindle slowing down: A spindle is running at 1,800 r/min. The drive brings it down to 600 r/min in 4.0 s. What is its average angular acceleration in rad/s 2, taking the running direction as positive? Answer in rad/s² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Only the change in speed matters, so take it first: &Delta;&omega; = 600 r/min &minus; 1,800 r/min = &minus;1,200 r/min &minus;1,200 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s 1 r/min = &minus;125.6637 rad/s Then &alpha; = &Delta;&omega; / &Delta;t = &minus;125.6637 rad/s &divide; 4.0 s = &minus;31.4159 rad/s 2, or &minus;31.42 rad/s². The sign is the answer as much as the number is: it says the angular acceleration points opposite the direction of rotation, which is what slowing down means. This is the average over the 4.0 s; a drive that ramps its braking has a changing instantaneous value inside the same interval."},
{"title":"Back to the nameplate","page":"Practice","summary":"Angular Motion","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#angular-back-to-nameplate","text":"4. Back to the nameplate: A calculation has produced an angular velocity of 52 rad/s, and the machine has to be set in r/min. What speed is that? Answer in r/min Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Turn the factor over so that rad/s cancels: 52 rad/s &times; 9.549297 r/min 1 rad/s = 496.5634&hellip; r/min or 497 r/min. Carry the factor to at least five figures - the exact value is 60/(2&pi;) = 9.5492966&hellip;, and rounding it to 9.54 puts the answer about 0.1% low, which is a real error on a speed that has to be dialled in."},
{"title":"Belt speed from a drive roller","page":"Practice","summary":"Rotation and Linear Speed","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#rotation-belt-speed-from-roller","text":"5. Belt speed from a drive roller: A conveyor drive roller is 160 mm in diameter and turns at 60 r/min. What is the belt speed in m/s? Answer in m/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Halve the diameter: r = 80 mm = 0.080 m. Convert the speed: 60 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 6.283185 rad/s. v = &omega;r = 6.283185 rad/s &times; 0.080 m = 0.5026548 m/s. The belt runs at 0.503 m/s. Worth a sanity check: 60 r/min is one turn per second, and one turn of a 160 mm roller lays out &pi; &times; 0.160 m = 0.5027 m of belt - the same number, reached without the formula. The answer assumes the belt does not slip on the roller."},
{"title":"Setting the roller speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Rotation and Linear Speed","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#rotation-roller-speed-from-web","text":"6. Setting the roller speed: Material has to feed past a station at 1.25 m/s. The drive roller is 250 mm in diameter. What speed must the roller be set to, in r/min? Answer in r/min Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution r = 125 mm = 0.125 m. Rearrange: &omega; = v/r = 1.25 m/s &divide; 0.125 m = 10 rad/s. The machine is set in r/min, so convert back: 10 rad/s &times; 9.549297 r/min per rad/s = 95.49297 r/min. Set the roller to 95.5 r/min. Two chances to go wrong here, and both are worth a deliberate check: using 0.250 m instead of the radius halves the answer, and stopping at step 2 leaves it in rad/s, which is not what a drive is dialled in."},
{"title":"Wheel to road speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Rotation and Linear Speed","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#rotation-wheel-to-road-speed","text":"7. Wheel to road speed: A wheel 24 in in diameter turns at 6.0 rad/s. How fast is the vehicle travelling, in mi/h? Answer in mi/h Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution r = 12 in, which is exactly 1 ft, or 12 in &times; 0.0254 m/in = 0.3048 m. v = &omega;r = 6.0 rad/s &times; 0.3048 m = 1.8288 m/s. Then convert to mi/h: 1.8288 m/s &times; 1 mi/h 0.44704 m/s = 4.0909&hellip; mi/h or 4.09 mi/h. Both factors used here are exact by definition: 1 in = 0.0254 m and 1 mi/h = 0.44704 m/s."},
{"title":"Wheel angular acceleration","page":"Practice","summary":"Rotation and Linear Speed","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#rotation-wheel-angular-acceleration","text":"8. Wheel angular acceleration: A vehicle accelerates at 3.2 m/s 2 on wheels 0.62 m in diameter. What is the angular acceleration of one wheel, in rad/s 2? Answer in rad/s² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution r = 0.31 m. Rearrange a = &alpha;r into &alpha; = a/r = 3.2 m/s 2 &divide; 0.31 m = 10.3226 rad/s 2. The answer is 10.3 rad/s². Check the unit as well as the number: dividing m/s 2 by m leaves 1/s 2, and because the radian is a length over a length, rad/s 2 is that same thing named for what it describes. The result assumes the wheel rolls without slipping."},
{"title":"Torque at a pulley","page":"Practice","summary":"Torque","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#torque-pulley-tangential-force","text":"9. Torque at a pulley: A belt applies 240 N of tangential force to a pulley 300 mm in diameter. What torque does that put on the shaft, in N-m? Answer in N·m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The pulley is given by diameter, so halve it: r = 150 mm = 0.150 m. &tau; = rF = 0.150 m &times; 240 N = 36 N-m. The answer is 36 N-m - lower-case m, because N-M is not a valid SI torque unit; a prefix cannot stand alone. Using the 300 mm diameter instead of the radius gives 72 N-m, exactly twice too much."},
{"title":"Reading a torque rating","page":"Practice","summary":"Torque","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#torque-unit-conversion","text":"10. Reading a torque rating: A motor is rated 42 lbf-ft. What is that rating in N-m? Answer in N·m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 42 lbf-ft &times; 1.355818 N-m 1 lbf-ft = 56.944&hellip; N-m The rating is 56.9 N-m. Read the given unit carefully before reaching for the factor: this motor rating is a torque and converts to the newton metre. This site writes energy as ft-lbf, converting to joules, but word order is only a convention; the quantity and context determine which is meant. The numerical factor happens to be the same 1.355818 either way, so the arithmetic will not warn you if you have read the wrong quantity."},
{"title":"Force from a torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Torque","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#torque-drum-cable-force","text":"11. Force from a torque: A winch drum is 400 mm in diameter and its drive supplies 95 N-m. What tangential force does that produce at the drum surface? Answer in N Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution r = 200 mm = 0.200 m. Rearrange &tau; = rF into F = &tau;/r = 95 N-m &divide; 0.200 m = 475 N. The drive produces 475 N at the drum surface. Notice what the radius is doing: wind more cable onto the drum, the effective radius grows, and the same torque pulls with less force. That single rearrangement is the whole story of a longer wrench, a bigger steering wheel, and a larger pulley."},
{"title":"Single-stage reduction ratio","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-single-ratio","text":"12. Single-stage reduction ratio: An 18-tooth driver turns a 54-tooth driven gear. Ignore losses (η = 1.00). Find i and enter X for X:1. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in ratio Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = driven/driver = 54/18 = 3:1. Enter 3.00 for 3.00:1."},
{"title":"Single-stage output speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-single-speed","text":"13. Single-stage output speed: An 18-tooth driver at ω_in = 1350 rpm turns a 54-tooth gear. Ignore losses (η = 1.00). Find the output speed magnitude. Round to 1 decimal place. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 54/18 = 3; ω_out = 1350/3 = 450 rpm. Enter 450.0 rpm."},
{"title":"Single-stage ideal torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-single-torque","text":"14. Single-stage ideal torque: An 18-tooth driver supplies 6.4 lbf·ft to a 54-tooth gear. Ignore losses (η = 1.00). Find the output torque magnitude at steady load. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 3; τ_out = 6.4 × 3 = 19.2 lbf·ft. Enter 19.20 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Speed increase with a torque loss","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-step-up-torque","text":"15. Speed increase with a torque loss: A 48-tooth driver turns a 16-tooth output at steady load. Input torque is 15 lbf·ft. The single mesh is 94% efficient; there are no other losses. Find output torque. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 16/48 = 1/3, a speed increase. τ_out = 15 × (1/3) × 0.94 = 4.7 lbf·ft. Enter 4.70 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Multiply compound ratios","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-compound-ratio","text":"16. Multiply compound ratios: A20 → B50; B shares a shaft with C24 → D60. Numbers are tooth counts. Ignore losses (η = 1.00). Find overall i and enter X for X:1. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in ratio Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution B and C turn together. i = (50/20) × (60/24) = 2.5 × 2.5 = 6.25:1. Enter 6.25 for 6.25:1."},
{"title":"Speed on a compound shaft","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-shaft-speed","text":"17. Speed on a compound shaft: A20 → B50; B is fixed to C24, which drives D60. Input ω_A = 1500 rpm. Each of the two stages is 97% efficient; no other losses. Find the speed magnitude of C. Round to 1 decimal place. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution ω_B = 1500 × 20/50 = 600 rpm. C shares B’s shaft, so ω_C has the same magnitude, 600 rpm. Losses do not change this speed. Enter 600.0 rpm."},
{"title":"Torque reaching the second stage","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-shaft-torque","text":"18. Torque reaching the second stage: A20 → B50; B shares a shaft with C24 → D60. Input torque is 8 lbf·ft at steady rotation. Each stage is 97% efficient; no other losses or intermediate loads. Find the torque delivered by the first stage to the shared shaft. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Stage 1 gives τ_B = 8 × (50/20) × 0.97 = 19.4 lbf·ft. This is the torque available to drive C. Enter 19.40 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Losses do not slow the compound output","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-compound-speed","text":"19. Losses do not slow the compound output: A20 → B50; B shares a shaft with C24 → D60. Input ω_A = 1500 rpm. Each stage is 97% efficient; no other losses. Find output speed magnitude. Round to 1 decimal place. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 2.5 × 2.5 = 6.25. ω_D = 1500/6.25 = 240 rpm. Do not multiply speed by 0.97². Enter 240.0 rpm."},
{"title":"Compound output torque with losses","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-compound-torque","text":"20. Compound output torque with losses: A20 → B50; B shares a shaft with C24 → D60. Input torque is 8 lbf·ft at steady rotation. Each stage is 97% efficient; no other losses. Find output torque. Round to 3 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 6.25; η_total = 0.97² = 0.9409. τ_out = 8 × 6.25 × 0.9409 = 47.045 lbf·ft. Keep both efficiency factors. Enter 47.045 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"One idler: signed output speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-idler-direction","text":"21. One idler: signed output speed: External gears A22 → I33 → B55 turn from an input of 1100 rpm clockwise. I is an idler. Ignore all losses, including both idler meshes (η = 1.00). Find signed output speed: clockwise positive, viewed from the same end of every shaft. Round to the nearest whole rpm. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The idler count cancels: i = 55/22 = 2.5. Two meshes reverse twice, so output is clockwise: +440 rpm. Enter 440 rpm."},
{"title":"Two idlers: signed output speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-two-idlers-direction","text":"22. Two idlers: signed output speed: External gears A18 → I30 → J42 → B54 turn from an input of 900 rpm clockwise. I and J are idlers. Ignore all three mesh losses (η = 1.00). Find signed output speed, clockwise positive from the same end of every shaft. Round to the nearest whole rpm. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 54/18 = 3. Three external meshes reverse direction three times: output is counterclockwise, so ω_out = −900/3 = −300 rpm. Enter -300 rpm."},
{"title":"Two stages, one idler, three losses","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-idler-efficiency","text":"23. Two stages, one idler, three losses: A20 → I30 → B60; B shares a shaft with C24 → D48. I is an idler. Every actual mesh is 95% efficient, including both meshes around I; there are no other losses. What is total efficiency as a percentage? Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution There are two reduction stages but three actual meshes. η_total = 0.95 × 0.95 × 0.95 = 0.857375 = 85.7375%. Enter 85.74 %."},
{"title":"An idler adds a specified mesh loss","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-idler-torque","text":"24. An idler adds a specified mesh loss: A20 → I30 → B60; B shares a shaft with C24 → D48. I is an idler. Input torque is 7 lbf·ft at steady rotation. Every actual mesh is 95% efficient, including both meshes around I; no other losses. Find output torque. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = (60/20) × (48/24) = 6. Three meshes give η_total = 0.95³. τ_out = 7 × 6 × 0.857375 = 36.00975 lbf·ft. The idler adds no reduction factor. Enter 36.01 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Three stage efficiencies multiply","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-three-stage-efficiency","text":"25. Three stage efficiencies multiply: Three compound reduction stages each have one mesh and are each 96% efficient. There are no idlers or other losses. What is total efficiency as a percentage? Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution η_total = 0.96 × 0.96 × 0.96 = 0.884736 = 88.4736%. Neither 96% nor 288% is the total efficiency. Enter 88.47 %."},
{"title":"Total loss from three efficient stages","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-three-stage-loss","text":"26. Total loss from three efficient stages: Three compound stages each have one mesh and are each 98% efficient. There are no idlers or other losses. What percentage of input power is lost overall? Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution η_total = 0.98³ = 0.941192. Total loss = (1 − 0.941192) × 100 = 5.8808%. Adding three 2% losses gives 6%, which is only an approximation. Enter 5.88 %."},
{"title":"Unequal efficiencies still multiply","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-mixed-efficiency","text":"27. Unequal efficiencies still multiply: Three stages have efficiencies 94%, 97% and 99%, respectively, with one mesh each. There are no idlers or other losses. Find total efficiency as a percentage. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution η_total = 0.94 × 0.97 × 0.99 = 0.902682 = 90.2682%. Do not average the three percentages. Enter 90.27 %."},
{"title":"Three-stage torque versus ideal torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-three-stage-torque","text":"28. Three-stage torque versus ideal torque: A compound train has stage reductions 2:1, 3:1 and 2:1. Input torque is 5 lbf·ft at steady rotation. Each stage has one mesh at 96% efficiency; no idlers or other losses. Find output torque with losses. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 2 × 3 × 2 = 12. Ideal output torque is 60 lbf·ft; with losses, τ_out = 60 × 0.96³ = 53.08416 lbf·ft. Enter 53.08 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Three-stage speed stays tied to teeth","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-three-stage-speed","text":"29. Three-stage speed stays tied to teeth: A compound train has stage reductions 2:1, 3:1 and 2:1, with input ω_in = 1800 rpm. Each stage has one mesh at 96% efficiency; no idlers or other losses. Find output speed magnitude. Round to 1 decimal place. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 12; ω_out = 1800/12 = 150 rpm. Efficiency changes the delivered torque and power, not the positive-drive speed ratio. Enter 150.0 rpm."},
{"title":"Power remaining after three stages","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-three-stage-power","text":"30. Power remaining after three stages: A gear train receives 1600 W. Three stages each have one mesh at 96% efficiency; no idlers or other losses. Find delivered output power. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P_out = 1600 × 0.96³ = 1415.5776 W. The stage ratios do not create power. Enter 1415.58 W. Keep at least 4 significant figures in η_total: 0.8847 gives 1415.52 W. The absolute tolerance is 0.057600000001 W: the larger of the printed-answer error (0.0024 W) and the 4-significant-figure answer error (0.0576 W), plus 0.000000000001 W to protect the numerical boundary."},
{"title":"Work backward through the losses","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-required-input-torque","text":"31. Work backward through the losses: A two-stage train has overall i = 10:1 and must deliver 80 lbf·ft at steady rotation. Each stage has one mesh at 95% efficiency; no idlers or other losses. What input torque is required? Round to 3 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Rearrange τ_out = τ_in × i × η_total: τ_in = 80/(10 × 0.95²) = 8.8642659… lbf·ft. Divide by efficiency when finding the required input. Enter 8.864 lbf·ft."},
{"title":"Minimum stages for a winch","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-stage-count","text":"32. Minimum stages for a winch: A motor supplies 1920 rpm and 18 lbf·ft. A winch needs at most 40 rpm and at least 800 lbf·ft. Gears may have any integer count from 10–60, with unlimited copies; every reduction stage is at most 6:1 and 97% efficient. No idlers or other losses. Find the minimum feasible number of stages. Enter a whole number. Answer in stages Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Speed requires i ≥ 1920/40 = 48. Two stages reach at most 6² = 36, so they fail. Three stages can work: 12 → 48, 15 → 60, 16 → 50 give i = 50, 38.4 rpm and 821.4057 lbf·ft. Thus three stages are sufficient as well as necessary. Enter 3 stages."},
{"title":"Choose a whole-tooth final gear","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-teeth","text":"33. Choose a whole-tooth final gear: A winch design uses three compound stages: 12 → 48, 15 → 60, then a 16-tooth driver → an unknown output gear. Choose the final driven tooth count for overall i = 50:1. Available teeth: integers 10–60, unlimited copies; each stage ≤6:1 and 97% efficient, with no idlers or other losses. Enter a whole number. Answer in teeth Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The first two ratios multiply to 16. The last ratio must be 50/16 = 3.125, so driven teeth = 16 × 3.125 = 50. All teeth are in range and the last stage is below 6:1. Efficiency does not alter the tooth ratio. Enter 50 teeth."},
{"title":"Check the winch speed ceiling","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-speed","text":"34. Check the winch speed ceiling: A motor at 1920 rpm drives compound stages 12 → 48, 15 → 60 and 16 → 50 teeth. Each stage is 97% efficient; no idlers or other losses. Find output speed to check a 40 rpm ceiling. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in rpm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 4 × 4 × 3.125 = 50. ω_out = 1920/50 = 38.4 rpm, which passes the 40 rpm ceiling. Do not apply efficiency to speed. Enter 38.40 rpm."},
{"title":"Check the winch torque floor","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-torque","text":"35. Check the winch torque floor: A motor supplies 18 lbf·ft at steady rotation to compound stages 12 → 48, 15 → 60 and 16 → 50 teeth. Each stage is 97% efficient; no idlers or other losses. Find output torque to check an 800 lbf·ft floor. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in lbf·ft Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i = 50; η_total = 0.97³ = 0.912673. τ_out = 18 × 50 × 0.912673 = 821.4057 lbf·ft, which passes the 800 lbf·ft floor. Ideal torque alone (900 lbf·ft) is not the check. Enter 821.41 lbf·ft. Keep at least 4 significant figures in η_total: 0.9127 gives 821.43 lbf·ft. The absolute tolerance is 0.024300000001 lbf·ft: the larger of the printed-answer error (0.0043 lbf·ft) and the 4-significant-figure answer error (0.0243 lbf·ft), plus 0.000000000001 lbf·ft to protect the numerical boundary."},
{"title":"Find the ratio required by torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-torque-bound","text":"36. Find the ratio required by torque: For a three-stage winch train, input torque is 18 lbf·ft and required output torque is at least 800 lbf·ft. Each stage has one mesh at 97% efficiency; no idlers or other losses. Find the minimum overall ratio allowed by torque alone; enter X for X:1. Keep at least 4 significant figures in η_total until the final answer. Round to 3 decimal places. Answer in ratio Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution i ≥ 800/(18 × 0.97³) = 48.6970081…. For a 1920 rpm input and 40 rpm ceiling, speed requires only i ≥ 48, so torque is the tighter requirement. Select actual tooth pairs at or above the unrounded bound. Enter 48.697 for 48.697:1. Keep at least 4 significant figures in η_total: 800/(18 × 0.9127) = 48.695567… rounds to 48.696. The absolute tolerance is 0.001008068: the larger of the printed-answer error (about 0.000008068) and the 4-significant-figure answer error (about 0.001008068), rounded upward at the numerical boundary. Full precision is preferred and is needed when checking actual tooth choices against the torque floor; 4 significant figures in η_total are accepted for this answer rounded to 3 decimal places."},
{"title":"Choose the fastest feasible candidate","page":"Practice","summary":"Gear Ratios","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#gear-winch-design-choice","text":"37. Choose the fastest feasible candidate: A winch needs ≤40 rpm and ≥800 lbf·ft from a 1920 rpm, 18 lbf·ft motor. All three-stage candidates use 10–60 teeth, ≤6:1 per stage and 97% per stage, with no idlers or other losses. A: 12 → 48, 15 → 60, 16 → 48 (48:1). B: 12 → 48, 15 → 60, 16 → 50 (50:1). C: 12 → 36, 10 → 60, 20 → 60 (54:1). Enter X for the X:1 ratio of the fastest candidate that meets both limits. Round to 2 decimal places. Answer in ratio Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A gives 40 rpm but only 788.549472 lbf·ft: it fails torque. B gives 38.4 rpm and 821.4057 lbf·ft: both pass. C gives 35.5556… rpm and 887.118156 lbf·ft: both pass, but it is slower. Choose B, 50:1. This compares the three supplied candidates, not every possible train. Enter 50.00 for 50.00:1."},
{"title":"Teeth across a quarter inch","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-teeth-quarter","text":"Teeth across a quarter inch: A uniform 24 TPI blade crosses 0.250 in of continuous material along the tooth line. Estimate teeth engaged. Give one decimal place. Answer (teeth) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.250 in × 24 teeth/in = 6.0 teeth. This is a spacing estimate; actual engagement changes as the blade moves."},
{"title":"Teeth across a decimal thickness","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-teeth-decimal","text":"Teeth across a decimal thickness: A uniform 18 TPI blade crosses 0.250 in of continuous material along the tooth line. Estimate teeth engaged to one decimal place. Answer (teeth) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.250 × 18 = 4.5 teeth. Do not divide thickness by TPI; that does not count tooth spacings."},
{"title":"Convert thickness before using TPI","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-teeth-metric","text":"Convert thickness before using TPI: A uniform 12 TPI blade crosses 6.35 mm of continuous material. Use 25.4 mm = 1 in. Estimate teeth engaged to one decimal place. Answer (teeth) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 6.35 ÷ 25.4 = 0.250 in. Then 0.250 × 12 = 3.0 teeth. Multiplying millimeters directly by teeth per inch mixes units."},
{"title":"Minimum calculated pitch","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-minimum-pitch","text":"Minimum calculated pitch: For 0.125 in of continuous contact, calculate the minimum TPI for an estimated three teeth. Give one decimal place. This asks for the mathematical threshold, not a catalog selection. Answer (TPI) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3 ÷ 0.125 = 24.0 TPI. Check: 0.125 × 24 = 3 teeth."},
{"title":"Select from available pitches","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-pitch-round-up","text":"Select from available pitches: For this simplified exercise, the candidate uniform pitches are 10, 14, and 18 TPI. A continuous contact length is 0.200 in. Which is the coarsest candidate meeting the three-tooth estimate? Enter the TPI. Actual selection also needs the maker's chart. Answer (TPI) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3 ÷ 0.200 = 15 TPI minimum. The available choice is 18 TPI: 0.200 × 18 = 3.6 teeth. The tempting 14 TPI choice gives only 2.8."},
{"title":"Thin-wall tube versus solid bar","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-tube-pitch","text":"Thin-wall tube versus solid bar: A thin-wall tube and a solid bar have the same outside diameter. Which selection approach fits the tube? Enter 1 or 2. 1: Count the whole outside diameter as solid engagement. 2: Use a tube/structural chart with wall thickness and overall size; expect finer teeth than for the solid bar. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. The hollow center is not material. Engagement changes around the tube, so neither outside diameter alone nor a fixed two-wall sum describes the whole cut."},
{"title":"Chip space in soft material","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-skip-form","text":"Chip space in soft material: A blade specification calls for widely spaced teeth, generous gullets, and a zero-rake form for chip clearance in a softer material. Which named form fits? Enter 1, 2, or 3. 1: Fine regular. 2: Skip. 3: Positive-rake hook. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: skip. The generous chip space and zero-rake description identify the form. Final pitch still depends on section size."},
{"title":"Identify a positive-rake form","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-hook-form","text":"Identify a positive-rake form: A maker recommends an aggressive positive-rake tooth form for a supported production cut. Which form does that describe? Enter 1, 2, or 3. 1: Regular. 2: Skip. 3: Hook. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3: hook. Positive rake describes the face angle. It does not establish pitch, set, or one universal rake angle."},
{"title":"Fine-pitch set for thin sections","page":"Practice","summary":"Saw Blade Selection","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#saw-wavy-set","text":"Fine-pitch set for thin sections: For a thin section, a fine-pitch blade specification calls for groups of teeth that gradually change sideways displacement. Which set matches? Enter 1, 2, or 3. 1: Left-right-straight raker. 2: Wavy. 3: Left-right alternate. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2: wavy. Groups create the wave of set. Raker includes an unset tooth; alternate switches sides at each tooth. Set alone does not determine suitable TPI."},
{"title":"Hand hacksaw cutting direction","page":"Practice","summary":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bench-hacksaw-direction","text":"Hand hacksaw cutting direction: For a conventional push-cut hand hacksaw, which setup is correct? Enter 1 or 2. 1: Teeth away from the handle; pressure on the forward stroke, eased on return. 2: Teeth toward the handle; heavy pressure in both directions. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1. The teeth face the cutting stroke. This describes a conventional hand hacksaw, not a universal power-hacksaw stroke direction."},
{"title":"Choose a file for draw filing","page":"Practice","summary":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bench-file-finish","text":"Choose a file for draw filing: A flat face needs light finishing by draw filing after rough shaping. Which is the better starting choice? Enter 1 or 2. 1: A coarse double-cut file chosen for rapid stock removal. 2: A suitable fine single-cut file, kept clean and flat. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. A fine single-cut file suits controlled draw filing. Keep the file crosswise to the travel and avoid rocking across the face."},
{"title":"Choose a file for stock removal","page":"Practice","summary":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bench-file-roughing","text":"Choose a file for stock removal: An accessible flat steel edge needs substantial hand shaping before finishing. Which file is the better starting choice? Enter 1 or 2. 1: A suitable bastard-grade double-cut flat file. 2: A fine round file chosen for finishing a small round opening. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1. Match both shape and removal rate. Bastard describes coarseness; double-cut describes the crossing tooth pattern."},
{"title":"Respond to chips lodged in a file","page":"Practice","summary":"Bench Work with Hand Tools","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#bench-file-pinning","text":"Respond to chips lodged in a file: A file begins leaving deep scratches, and chips are lodged between its teeth. What should happen next? Enter 1 or 2. 1: Increase pressure so the file forces the chips through. 2: Clear the teeth with a file card, then inspect the work and resume controlled filing. Answer (choice) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2. This is pinning. Trapped chips can score the work; extra force does not restore a clean cutting surface."},
{"title":"Lead from three starts","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-triple-lead","text":"1. Lead: A triple-start screw has pitch 1.5 mm. What is its lead in mm per revolution? Lead in mm/rev Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = pitch × starts = 1.5 × 3 = 4.5 mm/rev. The 1.5 mm spacing is pitch; it is not the travel per turn for this screw."},
{"title":"ACME actuator travel speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-acme-speed","text":"2. Actuator speed: A single-start ACME actuator has 0.20 in pitch and turns at an actual 900 rpm. Its given efficiency is 60%. Find linear speed in in/min during steady engaged travel; the nut cannot rotate. Speed in in/min Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = 0.20 × 1 = 0.20 in/rev. Then v = 900 × 0.20 = 180 in/min (3 in/s). Applying 60% to speed would give 108 in/min; efficiency affects force, not the engaged motion ratio."},
{"title":"ACME actuator useful force","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-acme-force","text":"3. Actuator force: A single-start ACME actuator with 0.20 in pitch receives 12 lb-in (lbf·in) of torque. Given overall efficiency 60%, find actual axial force during steady motion against a resisting load. Round to the nearest lbf. Force in lbf Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution F = 0.60 × 2π × 12 / 0.20 = 226.1947 ≈ 226 lbf. The theoretical force is 376.9911 lbf. Omitting 2π would give only 36 lbf."},
{"title":"Vise handle input torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-vise-torque","text":"4. Handle torque: A 120 N hand force acts perpendicular to a vise handle, 180 mm from the screw axis. Find screw input torque in N·m. Torque in N·m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution From Torque, T = rF = 0.180 × 120 = 21.6 N·m. The supplied 180 mm is already the axis-to-hand radius; do not halve it."},
{"title":"Square-thread vise clamping estimate","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-vise-force","text":"5. Clamping estimate: An illustrative single-start square-thread vise has 4 mm pitch and receives 21.6 N·m. Assume a given overall tightening efficiency of 30%, not a measured typical vise value. Estimate force while tightening, in kN, to two decimal places. Force in kN Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = 0.004 m/rev. F = 0.30 × 2π × 21.6 / 0.004 = 10,178.7602 N = 10.18 kN. Using π = 3.14 gives 10.1736 kN, or 10.17 kN rounded; both are accepted. This is not a rated vise capacity or a residual static-force prediction. Keeping 4 mm beside N·m without conversion makes the result 1,000 times too small."},
{"title":"Free jaw travel in mm/s","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-vise-speed","text":"6. Free jaw travel: Before contacting the work, a vise's single-start 4 mm pitch screw turns at 24 rpm. Find jaw speed in mm/s. The nut is restrained from rotation and backlash has been taken up. Speed in mm/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution v = 24 × 4 = 96 mm/min. Divide by 60: 1.6 mm/s. The jaw cannot keep travelling at that speed through a rigid clamped workpiece."},
{"title":"Triple-start CNC table feed","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-cnc-speed","text":"7. Table feed: A CNC positioning slide uses a triple-start screw of 1.5 mm pitch, rotating at an actual 480 rpm. Given efficiency is 55%. Find steady feed in mm/s with the nut restrained from rotating and clearance taken up. Feed in mm/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = 1.5 × 3 = 4.5 mm/rev. v = 480 × 4.5 / 60 = 36 mm/s. Pitch instead of lead gives 12 mm/s; applying efficiency gives 19.8 mm/s; leaving minutes unconverted gives 2,160 with the wrong unit."},
{"title":"Triple-start screw thrust","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-cnc-force","text":"8. Available thrust: A triple-start screw of 1.5 mm pitch receives 0.75 N·m at steady speed against a resisting load. Given assembly efficiency is 55%. Find actual axial force to the nearest N. This is available screw thrust, before allocating force to guide friction or other loads; it is not a cutting-capacity rating. Force in N Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = 1.5 × 3 / 1,000 = 0.0045 m/rev. F = 0.55 × 2π × 0.75 / 0.0045 = 575.9587 ≈ 576 N. Using pitch instead of lead gives 1,727.876 N; omitting 2π gives 91.667 N."},
{"title":"Torque needed for target force","page":"Practice","summary":"Lead Screws","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-required-torque","text":"9. Required torque: A two-start screw with 2.5 mm pitch must deliver 800 N at steady speed. Its given efficiency is 50%. Find the input torque in N·m, to two decimal places. Torque in N·m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution L = 2.5 × 2 = 5 mm = 0.005 m. T = FL/(2πη) = 800 × 0.005/(2π × 0.50) = 1.27324 ≈ 1.27 N·m. Divide by efficiency when solving for the required input."},
{"title":"Read 1/4-20 UNC","page":"Practice","summary":"Screw Threads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-unc-pitch","text":"10. Inch coarse callout: What pitch in inches is specified by 1/4-20 UNC? This checked designation appears in NBS Circular 479; the source entry and link are in Screw Threads. Pitch in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 20 is threads per inch: pitch = 1/20 = 0.050 in. The 1/4 is nominal diameter. UNC identifies the coarse series."},
{"title":"Read 1/4-28 UNF","page":"Practice","summary":"Screw Threads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-unf-pitch","text":"11. Inch fine callout: Find the pitch of 1/4-28 UNF in inches, to four decimal places. The NBS Circular 479 designation is checked and linked in Screw Threads. Pitch in in Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution pitch = 1/28 = 0.035714… ≈ 0.0357 in. At the same nominal diameter, the fine series has a smaller pitch than the coarse series."},
{"title":"Read M6 × 1.0","page":"Practice","summary":"Screw Threads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-metric-pitch","text":"12. Metric callout: What pitch in mm does M6 × 1.0 specify? The NAAMS standard entry is quoted and linked in Screw Threads. Pitch in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The pitch is written directly: 1.0 mm. M6 gives the metric family and nominal 6 mm diameter. Do not invert the pitch as though it were a threads-per-inch count."},
{"title":"Choose a form for one-way thrust","page":"Practice","summary":"Screw Threads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-buttress-choice","text":"13. One-way thrust: Which form deliberately uses an asymmetric working flank to favor heavy axial thrust in one direction? Choose the form, not a rated capacity: 1 = V, 2 = square, 3 = ACME/trapezoidal, 4 = buttress. Thread form Choose a form 1 — V 2 — square 3 — ACME / trapezoidal 4 — buttress Check answer Choose a form, then check it. Show worked solution 4 — buttress. Its working flank and sloping back are intentionally different. Reverse motion is possible, but heavy loading on the other flank does not keep the same strength and efficiency advantages."},
{"title":"Choose a form for an adjustable lead nut","page":"Practice","summary":"Screw Threads","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#screw-acme-choice","text":"14. Repeated travel: A slide needs a power-screw form suited to repeated travel in both directions, easier machining than square, and a split-nut arrangement to take up wear clearance. Which conventional form best matches? 1 = V, 2 = square, 3 = ACME/trapezoidal, 4 = buttress. Thread form Choose a form 1 — V 2 — square 3 — ACME / trapezoidal 4 — buttress Check answer Choose a form, then check it. Show worked solution 3 — ACME/trapezoidal. This trades some square-thread efficiency for practical manufacturing and nut-adjustment advantages. Actual wear life, efficiency and load rating still require assembly data."},
{"title":"Identify a straight complete cut","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-full-identify","text":"Identify a straight complete cut. Which section type uses the straight cut through the entire cylinder shown here? Straight cut across a hollow cylinder and the resulting pair of hatched walls. Choose an answer Select one Full Half Broken-out Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Full. One straight plane passes through the whole part. Both walls are sectioned."},
{"title":"Identify a quarter removal","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-half-identify","text":"Identify a quarter removal. Which section type produces this combined exterior and interior view? Two perpendicular cutting-plane portions and a result with exterior left and section right. Choose an answer Select one Offset Half Removed Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Half. Removing one quarter of a symmetric part produces a view with half exterior and half section."},
{"title":"Identify a jogged cut","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-offset-identify","text":"Identify a jogged cut. Which section type follows the jogged path through all three holes? A cutting path jogs through three staggered holes; its result shows all three openings. Choose an answer Select one Aligned Broken-out Offset Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Offset. The parallel cutting portions are offset to reach staggered holes. The jogs are not edges on the manufactured plate."},
{"title":"Identify a local cutaway","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-local-identify","text":"Identify a local cutaway. Which section type exposes this passage within an irregular boundary? An irregular local boundary exposes a passage in a housing. Choose an answer Select one Broken-out Full Revolved Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Broken-out. A broken-out section removes only a local area to a selected depth in the existing view."},
{"title":"Identify an overlaid slice","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-revolved-identify","text":"Identify an overlaid slice. The rectangular slice is turned into the drawing plane and placed on the parent bar. What is it? A rectangular cross section is overlaid at the cutting location on a bar. Choose an answer Select one Removed section Revolved section Detail view Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Revolved section. A revolved section places the rotated cross section on the parent view."},
{"title":"Identify a separate slice","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-removed-identify","text":"Identify a separate slice. The slice is shown clear of the parent bar and identified by its cut letters. What is it? A bar has a transverse A–A cut and a separate rectangular section. Choose an answer Select one Half section Broken view Removed section Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Removed section. A removed section is placed apart from the parent view and linked to its location by identification."},
{"title":"Identify a rotated radial cut","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-aligned-identify","text":"Identify a rotated radial cut. One radial cutting portion is rotated into the other before drawing the result. Which section type is used? Two intersecting radial cutting planes through a disk, with an arc showing rotation into alignment. Choose an answer Select one Aligned Offset Broken-out Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Aligned. An aligned section rotates an angled cutting portion into alignment; the top view retains the actual angular locations."},
{"title":"Choose the rib hatch convention","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-rib-hatch","text":"Choose the rib hatch convention. A section plane passes lengthwise through a thin reinforcing rib. Which treatment usually avoids making the rib look like a solid wall? Choose an answer Select one Hatch every region inside the outline Leave the lengthwise rib unhatched Delete the rib entirely Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Leave the lengthwise rib unhatched. A rib cut along its length is conventionally left unhatched; a transverse cut through its thickness is hatched."},
{"title":"Distinguish shaft cut directions","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-shaft-hatch","text":"Distinguish shaft cut directions. Which statement is appropriate for a solid shaft in an assembly drawing? Choose an answer Select one Never hatch a shaft in any direction Hatch a longitudinal shaft but never a transverse cut A longitudinal shaft is commonly unsectioned; a transverse section can be hatched Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation A longitudinal shaft is commonly unsectioned; a transverse section can be hatched. The convention depends on cut direction and context. It does not make all shafts immune to sectioning."},
{"title":"Match a cutting plane to its result","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-match-cut","text":"Match a cutting plane to its result. Follow the X–X arrows. Which candidate matches the section through this plate? A plate has one hole on cut X–X and another below it; arrows look upward. Candidate A has one opening and candidate B has two. Choose an answer Select one Result A Result B Neither result Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Result A. Result A. The straight plane crosses the left hole. The right hole lies in the portion removed toward the viewer, so it does not appear in this section."},
{"title":"Interpret the offset jog","page":"Practice","summary":"Section Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#section-jog-edge","text":"Interpret the offset jog. The cutting plane jogs across solid material between two holes. Should that jog appear as a new physical edge in the resulting section? Choose an answer Select one Yes, the part now has a step No, the jog changes the imaginary cut path Yes, every construction line is a visible edge Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation No, the jog changes the imaginary cut path. The offset cutting path is a drawing device, not a step machined into the part."},
{"title":"Choose a true-shape view","page":"Practice","summary":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#auxiliary-true-shape","text":"Choose a true-shape view. An inclined face appears as a sloping line in the front view. Which view shows the complete face in true shape? Choose an answer Select one A larger copy of the front view Any view with hidden lines An auxiliary view looking perpendicular to that face Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation An auxiliary view looking perpendicular to that face. The projection plane must be parallel to the face. Enlargement does not remove foreshortening."},
{"title":"Choose the projector direction","page":"Practice","summary":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#auxiliary-projectors","text":"Choose the projector direction. For a primary auxiliary construction, the fold line is parallel to the edge view of the inclined face. How do you draw the projectors? Choose an answer Select one Perpendicular to the fold line Parallel to the fold line Always vertical on the sheet Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation Perpendicular to the fold line. Projectors are normal to the auxiliary projection plane as represented by its fold line; their direction follows the chosen face."},
{"title":"Read a partial auxiliary","page":"Practice","summary":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#auxiliary-partial","text":"Read a partial auxiliary. A partial auxiliary shows only the sloping face and nearby identifying geometry. What has changed from a complete auxiliary? Choose an answer Select one The direction is now perspective The coverage is limited; the projection direction is unchanged All dimensions must be halved Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The coverage is limited; the projection direction is unchanged. Partial describes which region is shown, not a new projection system."},
{"title":"Read a detail scale","page":"Practice","summary":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#view-detail-scale","text":"Read a detail scale. A detail is marked 2:1. What does that tell you? Choose an answer Select one The model feature is twice its specified size The viewing direction has changed The drawn lengths are twice actual lengths at the intended print scale Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation The drawn lengths are twice actual lengths at the intended print scale. A 2:1 view is an enlargement. The actual feature size remains the stated dimension; resized screens or prints are not measuring tools."},
{"title":"Read a broken view","page":"Practice","summary":"Auxiliary and Detail Views","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#view-break-length","text":"Read a broken view. A continuous rod is drawn with two break lines and an overall dimension of 360 mm. What is its specified overall length? Choose an answer Select one 360 mm The visible end lengths added together 360 mm minus the drawn gap Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show explanation 360 mm. The dimension gives the full length. The break saves sheet space and does not remove length from the manufactured rod."},
{"title":"Sine-wave peak","page":"Practice","summary":"AC and DC","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-rms-to-peak","text":"Sine-wave peak. A sine wave is 72 V RMS. What is its positive peak? Give one decimal place. Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 72 × √2 = 101.8 V. RMS and peak are not equal."},
{"title":"Sine-wave RMS","page":"Practice","summary":"AC and DC","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-peak-to-rms","text":"Sine-wave RMS. A sine wave peaks at ±210 V. What is its RMS voltage? Give one decimal place. Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 210 / √2 = 148.5 V. Do not use peak-to-peak voltage."},
{"title":"Line loss at the lower voltage","page":"Practice","summary":"AC and DC","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-loss-low-voltage","text":"Line loss at the lower voltage. An ideal DC comparison transmits 6,000 W at the sending end at 300 V through a total line resistance of 0.8 Ω. What is the line heating loss? Give one decimal place. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = 6000 / 300 = 20 A; loss = 20² × 0.8 = 320.0 W. The line resistance includes the complete current path."},
{"title":"Line loss at the higher voltage","page":"Practice","summary":"AC and DC","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-loss-high-voltage","text":"Line loss at the higher voltage. Repeat the ideal DC comparison at 3,000 V, still transmitting 6,000 W at the sending end with total line resistance 0.8 Ω. What is the line heating loss? Give two decimal places. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = 6000 / 3000 = 2 A; loss = 2² × 0.8 = 3.20 W. Ten times the voltage produces one hundredth the loss: compare 320 W at 300 V."},
{"title":"Series current","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-series-current","text":"Series current. An ideal 18 V DC source drives 40 Ω and 80 Ω in series with both switches closed. What current passes through the 80 Ω resistor? Give three decimal places. Answer in A Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution R_eq = 40 + 80 = 120 Ω. The current through each resistor is 18 / 120 = 0.150 A; it does not divide."},
{"title":"Series voltage drop across 40 ohms","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-series-drop-small","text":"Series voltage drop across 40 ohms. An ideal 18 V DC source drives 40 Ω and 80 Ω in series. What is the drop across 40 Ω? Give two decimal places. Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = 18 / 120 = 0.15 A. The drop is 0.15 × 40 = 6.00 V. The remaining 12 V drops across 80 Ω."},
{"title":"Series voltage drop across 80 ohms","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-series-drop-large","text":"Series voltage drop across 80 ohms. An ideal 18 V DC source drives 40 Ω and 80 Ω in series. What is the drop across 80 Ω? Give two decimal places. Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution I = 0.15 A; drop = 0.15 × 80 = 12.00 V. The two drops add to 18 V."},
{"title":"Parallel branch current","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-parallel-branch","text":"Parallel branch current. An ideal 18 V DC source supplies parallel 45 Ω and 75 Ω resistors. What current passes through 75 Ω? Give three decimal places. Answer in A Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Each branch has 18 V: I = 18 / 75 = 0.240 A. The other branch draws 0.400 A."},
{"title":"Parallel supply current","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-parallel-total","text":"Parallel supply current. An ideal 18 V DC source supplies parallel 45 Ω and 75 Ω resistors. What is the total supply current? Give three decimal places. Answer in A Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Add branch currents: 0.400 + 0.240 = 0.640 A."},
{"title":"Parallel equivalent resistance","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-parallel-equivalent","text":"Parallel equivalent resistance. Find the equivalent resistance of 45 Ω and 75 Ω in parallel. Give two decimal places. Answer in Ω Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution R_eq = (45 × 75) / (45 + 75) = 28.125 Ω, or 28.13 Ω. It is less than either resistor."},
{"title":"Which series resistor dissipates more?","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-series-power","text":"Which series resistor dissipates more?. An ideal 18 V DC source drives 40 Ω and 80 Ω in series. Identify the resistor dissipating more power, then enter that power in watts to two decimal places. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The 80 Ω resistor dissipates more: 0.15² × 80 = 1.80 W. The 40 Ω resistor dissipates 0.90 W."},
{"title":"Which parallel resistor dissipates more?","page":"Practice","summary":"Series and Parallel Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-parallel-power","text":"Which parallel resistor dissipates more?. An ideal 18 V DC source supplies parallel 45 Ω and 75 Ω resistors. Identify the resistor dissipating more power, then enter that power in watts to two decimal places. Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The 45 Ω resistor dissipates more: 18² / 45 = 7.20 W. The 75 Ω resistor dissipates 4.32 W."},
{"title":"Voltmeter connection and reading","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-meter-voltage","text":"Voltmeter connection and reading. An ideal 18 V DC source drives 56 Ω and 91 Ω in series. Where must an ideal voltmeter connect to read the voltage of the 56 Ω resistor? State the connection, then enter its reading to two decimal places. Answer in V Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Connect across the 56 Ω resistor, in parallel. I = 18 / (56 + 91) = 18 / 147 ≈ 0.1224 A, so the reading is 18 × 56 / 147 ≈ 6.86 V. The numeric checker checks the reading; compare your connection with this explanation."},
{"title":"Ammeter connection and reading","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-meter-current","text":"Ammeter connection and reading. An ideal 18 V DC source supplies parallel 45 Ω and 75 Ω branches. Where must an ideal ammeter connect to read only the 75 Ω branch current? State the connection, then enter its reading to three decimal places. Answer in A Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Insert it in series within the 75 Ω branch. It reads 18 / 75 = 0.240 A. In the supply lead it would read 0.640 A. The numeric checker checks the reading; compare your connection with this explanation."},
{"title":"Ohmmeter reads the unpowered network","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-meter-resistance","text":"Ohmmeter reads the unpowered network. The source is disconnected and stored energy discharged from a parallel network of 68 Ω and 120 Ω. An ideal ohmmeter is connected across the two network terminals. What does it read? Give two decimal places. Answer in Ω Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Both paths remain connected. The meter reads their equivalent resistance: (68 × 120) / (68 + 120) = 43.404255… Ω, or 43.40 Ω, not the resistance of either part alone."},
{"title":"Current from a shunt reading","page":"Practice","summary":"Measuring Electric Circuits","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#circuits-meter-shunt","text":"Current from a shunt reading. A voltmeter reads 18 mV across a 0.060 Ω shunt installed in series with a DC load. Find the load current to three decimal places. Answer in A Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 18 mV = 0.018 V. I = 0.018 / 0.060 = 0.300 A. The shunt carries the load current; the voltmeter connects across the shunt."},
{"title":"Choose a machine for resawing","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-resaw-machine","text":"Choose a machine for resawing A straight board must be divided through its width into two thinner boards. Which machine is the best starting choice, assuming adequate capacity? Miter saw Band saw Thickness planer Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. A band saw resaws the thickness into separate boards. A planer removes thickness as chips; it does not recover a second board."},
{"title":"Choose a machine for an enclosed opening","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-interior-cut","text":"Choose a machine for an enclosed opening A thin panel needs a delicate enclosed curved opening, with no access cut from its outside edge. Which route fits? Band saw entering from the edge Miter saw Scroll saw with its blade threaded through a starter hole Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. The scroll-saw blade can enter the enclosed region through a starter hole. A continuous band cannot be threaded through an ordinary starter hole."},
{"title":"Repeat an edge profile","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-template-router","text":"Repeat an edge profile Several flat blanks need the same curved outside outline copied from a template. Which machine and tooling best fit? Router with suitable template-guided tooling Drill press with twist bit Thickness planer Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. A template supplies the path for suitable router tooling. The bit removes the wood to that path."},
{"title":"Choose a bit for a blind recess","page":"Practice","summary":"Choosing a Woodworking Machine","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-blind-recess","text":"Choose a bit for a blind recess A wood part needs a clean-sided blind circular recess with a largely flat bottom. A small center mark is acceptable. Which bit best fits? Hole saw Twist bit Forstner bit Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. A Forstner bit removes the recess interior and leaves a largely flat bottom. A hole saw leaves a core, and an ordinary twist bit leaves a conical bottom."},
{"title":"Order the rough-stock milling steps","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Rough Lumber Square","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-milling-order","text":"Order the rough-stock milling steps Choose the final-sizing sequence that first establishes references, then controls thickness, width and length. Any preliminary breakdown has already left the blank oversized. Plane → rip → face-joint → crosscut → edge-joint Face-joint → edge-joint → plane → rip → crosscut Crosscut to final length → rip → plane → edge-joint → face-joint Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 2. The flat face supports thickness control; the straight square edge supports width control. Final end cuts follow the established face and edge geometry."},
{"title":"Recognize a planer reference","page":"Practice","summary":"Milling Rough Lumber Square","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-planer-reference","text":"Recognize a planer reference A bowed board goes through a thickness planer without a flattening fixture or a previously flattened face. What is the best prediction? It must emerge flat because thickness is uniform The planer automatically makes one edge square It may retain a bow after the rollers release it Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 3. Roller pressure can temporarily flatten the board. Uniform thickness does not prove flatness; establish a reference face first."},
{"title":"Recognize bent lamination","page":"Practice","summary":"Bending and Laminating Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-bent-lamination","text":"Recognize bent lamination A curved rail is made from thin strips with grain along their length, bonded together over a form. Which description fits? Bent lamination; springback can still occur Veneering; the face layer alone supplies all support Steam bending; adhesive removes the need for a form Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Choice 1. The strips bend separately and the cured bonds retain the assembled curve. The bend may still open after release, so form compensation needs a trial."},
{"title":"Count strips with kerf","page":"Practice","summary":"How Saws Cut Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-strip-yield","text":"Count strips with kerf A board has exactly 12 in of usable width. Each 3 in strip is removed with its own cut from a remaining offcut. Kerf is 0.125 in per cut. Ignore other allowances. What is the maximum whole number of strips? Whole strips Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 strips. floor(12 / (3 + 0.125)) = 3. Three strips and three kerfs use 9.375 in; four would need 12.5 in. Ignoring kerf gives 4, which does not fit."},
{"title":"Calculate the remaining offcut","page":"Practice","summary":"How Saws Cut Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-offcut-width","text":"Calculate the remaining offcut From a 10 in usable width, remove three strips, each 2 in wide, using three cuts with a 0.125 in kerf. No other material is removed. What offcut width remains? Enter inches to three decimal places. Remaining width (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3.625 in. 10 − 3(2) − 3(0.125) = 3.625. The kerfs remove 0.375 in. Ignoring them would give 4 in."},
{"title":"Divide a board into equal final strips","page":"Practice","summary":"How Saws Cut Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-equal-strips","text":"Divide a board into equal final strips An exactly 12 in wide board is divided into four equal strips with three cuts, each with a 0.125 in kerf. Both outside edges are already finished, no offcut remains, and no further allowance is needed. How wide is each strip? Enter inches to three decimal places. Strip width (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2.906 in to three decimal places. (12 − 3 × 0.125) / 4 = 2.90625 in. Four pieces have three intervening cuts. Ignoring kerf gives 3 in; counting four cuts gives 2.875 in. Both are outside the answer tolerance."},
{"title":"Predict centered rectangle growth","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-centered-growth","text":"Predict centered rectangle growth. An axis-aligned 64 × 22 mm rectangle has its center at the origin, and both sizes are driving. Increase width to 80 mm while preserving that intent. What is the new left-edge x coordinate in mm? Use a negative value for left of the origin. Left-edge x (mm) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning −40 mm. Centering keeps the left and right coordinates opposite: −80/2 and +80/2. Each edge moves 8 mm outward from its original ±32 mm position. The new driving width gives a new fully defined rectangle; no free motion remains."},
{"title":"Measure a rectangle that can slide","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-reference-freedom","text":"Measure a rectangle that can slide. Use the lab's axis-aligned rectangle model with four variables: left x, right x, bottom y and top y. Width and height drive size; bottom y is anchored at 0, but there is no horizontal anchor. Add a top-edge reference width measurement. How many DOF remain? Whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 DOF. The three independent conditions fix width, height and vertical position. Horizontal translation remains. The reference dimension measures the width; it adds no constraint."},
{"title":"Distinguish repetition from contradiction","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-conflicting-widths","text":"Distinguish repetition from contradiction. In the axis-aligned rectangle lab, a driving dimension sets bottom-edge width to 64 mm. Add a driving top-edge width of 74 mm without removing the first. What does this mean? One more independent condition makes the rectangle more fully defined The widths contradict; no rectangle satisfies both The second dimension is an agreeing redundant condition Choose an answer Choose… 1 2 3 Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2. Both edges measure right x − left x, so they cannot be 64 and 74 mm simultaneously. A second 64 mm condition would agree but add no independent rank. A reference width would only measure the result. This mathematical distinction does not prescribe a particular CAD application's error handling."},
{"title":"Two circles keep one center","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-common-center","text":"Two circles keep one center. The larger circle can change diameter without changing the smaller circle. Which single relation keeps their centers together? Two unequal circles surround the same marked center. Equal Concentric Tangent Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — Concentric. It shares the center while leaving the two radii independent. Equal would control size instead."},
{"title":"Separated edges on one line","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-same-line","text":"Separated edges on one line. Two nonzero straight edges must stay on the same extended line, with a gap allowed between their ends. What is this geometric relation called? Two separated segments sit on one dashed diagonal guide. Parallel Coincident endpoints Collinear Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 — Collinear. Parallel alone allows an offset between the lines; coincident endpoints would close the gap. In Onshape, point-on-line constraints can express collinearity."},
{"title":"A line touches a round boundary","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-smooth-contact","text":"A line touches a round boundary. A straight edge must keep the shown contact with the circle without crossing it. Which relation controls that contact? A horizontal line touches the bottom of a circle; its radius to contact is vertical. Tangent Equal Concentric Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — Tangent. The radius to contact is perpendicular to the line. Equal does not control contact, and concentric applies to centers."},
{"title":"A locator point stays halfway","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-halfway","text":"A locator point stays halfway. The point must remain halfway between the segment endpoints when the segment grows. Which relation captures that rule? A dot lies in the middle of a sloping segment. Matching ticks mark the two halves. Coincident with line Midpoint Fix Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — Midpoint. A point merely coincident with the line may slide along it. Fix would hold its current location rather than follow the moving midpoint."},
{"title":"Separated arcs share a supporting circle","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-same-circle","text":"Separated arcs share a supporting circle. These two circular arcs must share both center and radius, but their endpoints stay separate. What is the SOLIDWORKS relation name? Two separated arc segments lie on the same dashed supporting circle. Concentric Equal Coradial Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 — Coradial. Concentric alone leaves radius free; equal alone leaves center position free. In Onshape, combine concentric and equal radius."},
{"title":"Two ribs change length together","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometric Relations in Sketches","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-name-matching-lengths","text":"Two ribs change length together. Two separate straight segments represent layout ribs. Their lengths must stay equal, while their locations and directions may differ. Which relation should you use? Two equal-length segments appear at different positions and angles, with matching length ticks. Equal Parallel Collinear Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — Equal. It links the lengths. Parallel links directions, and collinear puts the segments on the same supporting line."},
{"title":"An isolated sketch point","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-free-point","text":"An isolated sketch point. A single point lies in a 2D sketch. It has no relations, dimensions, or automatic constraints. How many degrees of freedom remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — Its x and y coordinates can vary independently."},
{"title":"A sized circle that can move","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-circle-size","text":"A sized circle that can move. An isolated circle has one driving diameter of 16 mm. Its center has no constraints. How many DOF remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — The diameter removes the radius freedom from the original three. Center x and center y remain."},
{"title":"A circle centered at the origin","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-origin-circle","text":"A circle centered at the origin. An isolated circle center is coincident with the origin. There is no radius or diameter dimension and no other constraint. How many DOF remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — The center is located in both directions. Only radius remains free."},
{"title":"A free horizontal segment","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-horizontal-line","text":"A free horizontal segment. A nonzero line segment has only a horizontal relation. Neither endpoint is anchored and there is no length dimension. How many DOF remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 — Starting x, starting y, and length remain. Equivalently, four endpoint coordinates minus one independent horizontal condition gives three."},
{"title":"A horizontal segment starts at the origin","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-anchored-line","text":"A horizontal segment starts at the origin. A nonzero horizontal segment has its left endpoint coincident with the origin. No length dimension or other relation is present. How many DOF remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — The free endpoint can move horizontally. The origin coincidence removes two coordinate freedoms and horizontal removes one more from the original four."},
{"title":"A rectangle with location and width","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-dof-located-rectangle","text":"A rectangle with location and width. A rectangle has joined corners and horizontal top/bottom and vertical sides. Its lower-left corner is coincident with the origin and a 52 mm driving dimension sets its width. Height is positive and undimensioned. There are no other constraints. How many DOF remain? Enter the whole-number DOF count Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — Only height remains. The stated relations already control orientation, closure, and rectangular shape; the origin fixes location and the width dimension fixes size in x."},
{"title":"Finish locating a sized circle","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-define-circle","text":"Finish locating a sized circle. A circle has a driving diameter and a vertical relation between its center and the origin. It should be centered 14 mm above the origin. Which single addition fully defines it? A radius dimension A 14 mm vertical center-to-origin dimension A horizontal relation between the center and origin Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — A vertical center-to-origin distance of 14 mm removes the remaining vertical motion. Radius repeats size control. A horizontal alignment would put the center at the origin, against the stated intent."},
{"title":"Finish the length of an anchored edge","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-define-line","text":"Finish the length of an anchored edge. A horizontal line starts at the origin and extends right. It should be 37 mm long. Which addition removes the remaining freedom? A 37 mm driving length A second horizontal relation A vertical relation on the line Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — Length is the only free variable. Horizontal is already present. Vertical would conflict with a nonzero horizontal line."},
{"title":"Match a second circle to a known size","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-define-second-circle","text":"Match a second circle to a known size. Circle A is fully defined with a 12 mm diameter. Circle B has its center fully located elsewhere and must always match A in size. Which single relation fully defines B and captures that intent? Concentric Tangent Equal Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 — Equal removes B&#x27;s radius freedom and keeps both circles the same size when A changes. Concentric conflicts with the intended separate centers. Tangent adds a different geometric requirement."},
{"title":"Finish a located label blank","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-define-rectangle","text":"Finish a located label blank. An axis-aligned rectangle has joined corners, its lower-left corner at the origin, and a 68 mm driving width. It should be 26 mm tall. Which addition completes it? A second 68 mm driving dimension on the top edge A 26 mm driving height Fix the upper-right corner at its current position Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — The height is the remaining independent size. The top edge is already 68 mm because of the rectangle&#x27;s existing shape constraints. Fixing the upper-right corner would freeze an arbitrary current height rather than specify 26 mm."},
{"title":"Spot duplicate circle size control","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-over-radius","text":"Spot duplicate circle size control. A circle is fully defined by its center at the origin and a driving diameter of 28 mm. Which proposed driving dimension duplicates its existing size control? A 14 mm driving radius A driven diameter measurement A driven radius measurement Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 1 — The 14 mm radius is already determined by the 28 mm diameter. Keep it driven if you only need to report it. A driven diameter or radius is a measurement, not another driving constraint."},
{"title":"Spot a conflicting angle","page":"Practice","summary":"Fully Defined Sketches and Design Intent","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-over-angle","text":"Spot a conflicting angle. A nonzero line has a horizontal relation. Which addition contradicts that relation? A driving length of 42 mm A driving vertical location of one endpoint A driving 25-degree angle to the sketch horizontal axis Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 3 — A 25-degree driving angle to the horizontal axis conflicts with horizontal. A length and a vertical position of an endpoint can coexist with horizontal, assuming no other constraints."},
{"title":"Choose a sketch reference","page":"Practice","summary":"Sketch Entities and Planes","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#sketch-origin-choice","text":"Choose a sketch reference. You are starting a 2D profile for a new part with no existing faces. Which choice gives the sketch a plane and a useful position reference? Select only the origin and treat it as a plane Choose a standard plane and intentionally relate a sketch point to its origin Draw at an arbitrary screen location and assume that anchors the sketch Enter the option number (1, 2, or 3) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2 — A standard plane supplies orientation and placement in 3D; coincidence with its origin can anchor a point in that plane. The origin alone is not a plane, and screen appearance does not define a stable reference."},
{"title":"Identify poor wetting","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-cold-joint","text":"Identify poor wetting. Solder sits in a bead beside the lead and does not bond to it. Which diagnosis fits? Enter the choice number. A sound joint because solder is present A poorly wetted joint A properly insulated splice Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. A poorly wetted joint. The bead has not wetted both surfaces. Correct heat transfer and surface preparation before remaking the joint."},
{"title":"Judge a dull joint","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-dull-joint","text":"Judge a dull joint. A lead-free joint is dull but has a continuous wetted fillet and no bridge or crack. What follows from dullness alone? Enter the choice number. It must be cold It must be reheated until shiny Dullness alone does not prove a defect Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Dullness alone does not prove a defect. Some lead-free alloys have a dull finish. Inspect wetting, shape, clearance, and damage."},
{"title":"Find an unintended connection","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-bridge","text":"Find an unintended connection. A ribbon of solder joins two pads that the circuit keeps separate. What is it? Enter the choice number. A solder bridge A Western Union splice A correct fillet Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. A solder bridge. A bridge makes an unintended electrical connection. Remove it before powering the circuit."},
{"title":"Identify movement during cooling","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-disturbed","text":"Identify movement during cooling. A lead shifted while the solder solidified, leaving a cracked joint. What is the defect? Enter the choice number. Too little insulation A disturbed joint Normal lead-free dullness Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. A disturbed joint. Movement during solidification can disrupt the joint. Secure the parts before reworking it."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 1","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-1","text":"Joint sequence: step 1. The bench and iron are ready. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 1? Enter the choice number. Heat both parts Strip and clean Remove solder, then iron Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. Strip and clean. Step 1 is: Strip and clean. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 2","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-2","text":"Joint sequence: step 2. You have completed step 1: strip and clean. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 2? Enter the choice number. Support the parts Hold still while cooling Feed solder into the heated joint Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Support the parts. Step 2 is: Support the parts. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 3","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-3","text":"Joint sequence: step 3. You have completed step 2: support the parts. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 3? Enter the choice number. Inspect the joint Remove solder, then iron Flux if needed Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Flux if needed. Step 3 is: Flux if needed. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 4","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-4","text":"Joint sequence: step 4. You have completed step 3: flux if needed. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 4? Enter the choice number. Hold still while cooling Heat both parts Strip and clean Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. Heat both parts. Step 4 is: Heat both parts. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 5","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-5","text":"Joint sequence: step 5. You have completed step 4: heat both parts. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 5? Enter the choice number. Feed solder into the heated joint Support the parts Inspect the joint Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Feed solder into the heated joint. Step 5 is: Feed solder into the heated joint. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 6","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-6","text":"Joint sequence: step 6. You have completed step 5: feed solder into the heated joint. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 6? Enter the choice number. Flux if needed Strip and clean Remove solder, then iron Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Remove solder, then iron. Step 6 is: Remove solder, then iron. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 7","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-7","text":"Joint sequence: step 7. You have completed step 6: remove solder, then iron. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 7? Enter the choice number. Support the parts Hold still while cooling Heat both parts Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. Hold still while cooling. Step 7 is: Hold still while cooling. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Joint sequence: step 8","page":"Practice","summary":"Making and Inspecting Solder Joints","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-order-8","text":"Joint sequence: step 8. You have completed step 7: hold still while cooling. In the eight-step joint checklist, what is step 8? Enter the choice number. Inspect the joint Feed solder into the heated joint Flux if needed Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Inspect the joint. Step 8 is: Inspect the joint. Follow this order so preparation comes before heat, and cooling before inspection."},
{"title":"Meet a lead-free requirement","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-alloy-constraint","text":"Meet a lead-free requirement. An assembly specification requires lead-free solder. Which spool fits that constraint? Enter the choice number. Tin-lead solder because it melts easily Any spool with rosin core The specified lead-free alloy with compatible electronics flux Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. The specified lead-free alloy with compatible electronics flux. Flux core does not identify the alloy. Match both the alloy requirement and the flux system."},
{"title":"Compare common alloy melting behavior","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-leaded-choice","text":"Compare common alloy melting behavior. A repair specification permits tin-lead solder. Compared with common tin-copper or tin-silver-copper alloys, which generally melts at a lower temperature? Enter the choice number. Common tin-lead solder Every lead-free solder They always melt at the same temperature Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Common tin-lead solder. The comparison is between these common alloy families, not every alloy sold as lead-free. Follow the repair specification."},
{"title":"Name an inline wrapped splice","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-western-union","text":"Name an inline wrapped splice. Each free wire end wraps around the opposite standing wire, making an inline connection. Name it. Enter the choice number. Lap splice Twist pigtail Western Union splice Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Western Union splice. Western Union uses opposing wraps. A lap overlaps straight ends; a pigtail twists ends together on the same side."},
{"title":"Name an overlapping splice","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-lap","text":"Name an overlapping splice. Two stripped ends overlap side by side without wrapping. Name the arrangement. Enter the choice number. Lap splice Western Union splice Twist pigtail Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Lap splice. This is a lap or lay-over splice. It needs support during soldering and strain relief in use."},
{"title":"Name a pigtail splice","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-twist","text":"Name a pigtail splice. Two stripped ends twist together into a joined tail on the same side. Name it. Enter the choice number. Lap splice Twist or pigtail splice Western Union splice Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. Twist or pigtail splice. The joined tail distinguishes this arrangement from an inline Western Union splice."},
{"title":"Apply the battery shop rule","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-battery-rule","text":"Apply the battery shop rule. A connection would require putting a soldering iron directly on a battery. What should you do? Enter the choice number. Work faster Use more flux Do not solder the battery; use its intended holder or connector Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Do not solder the battery; use its intended holder or connector. NEVER SOLDER BATTERIES. Heat can damage a cell; the rule is not conditional on speed or flux."},
{"title":"Keep flux fume out of the breathing zone","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-fume-action","text":"Keep flux fume out of the breathing zone. Flux fume rises toward your face. What action fits the setup guidance? Enter the choice number. Use effective extraction and keep your head out of the plume Switch to lead-free and ignore the fume Blow it toward the next person Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Use effective extraction and keep your head out of the plume. Lead-free solder still uses flux. Arrange ventilation or extraction so fume is kept away from people."},
{"title":"Handle leaded solder cleanly","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Equipment and Setup","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-lead-hygiene","text":"Handle leaded solder cleanly. You have handled leaded solder and are leaving for a snack. Choose the correct action. Enter the choice number. Eat at the soldering bench Wash your hands and eat away from the bench Wipe your hands on your clothing Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 2. Wash your hands and eat away from the bench. Wash after handling leaded solder, and keep food and drink away from the bench."},
{"title":"Heat a through-hole connection","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-pad-and-lead","text":"Heat a through-hole connection. Where should the iron make contact for a through-hole PCB joint? Enter the choice number. Only the component lead Only the board surface beside the pad Both the copper pad and component lead Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 3. Both the copper pad and component lead. Both surfaces must get hot enough for solder to wet them. Use a tinned working face that fits the joint."},
{"title":"Prepare insulation before soldering","page":"Practice","summary":"Soldering Wire Splices and Through-Hole PCBs","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#soldering-heat-shrink","text":"Prepare insulation before soldering. Both ends of an inline cable will be inaccessible after the splice is closed. When should heat-shrink be slipped onto a wire? Enter the choice number. Before joining, kept away from heat until the joint cools and is inspected While solder is molten Only after both ends become inaccessible Choice number Check answer Enter a choice number, then check it. Show explanation 1. Before joining, kept away from heat until the joint cools and is inspected. Put the tubing on first, away from soldering heat. Cover and shrink it after cooling and inspection."},
{"title":"Assembly-line travel time","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-assembly-line-travel-time","text":"1. Assembly-line travel time: Two stations are 10 m apart. Parts enter a conveyor 1 ft apart, and the line processes 2 parts every 15 s at a steady rate. How long does one part take to travel from the first station to the second? Answer in s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The spacing supplies 1 part / 1 ft, and the throughput supplies 15 s / 2 parts. Therefore 10 m &times; 1 ft 0.3048 m &times; 1 part 1 ft &times; 15 s 2 parts = 246.063 s or 246 s (about 4.10 min). This assumes constant belt speed and steady spacing."},
{"title":"Recycling-line loading","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-recycling-line-loading","text":"2. Recycling-line loading: A recycling conveyor carries 72 lb of material per linear foot. It moves continuously at 0.23 m/s. How many lb of material pass a point in one hour? Answer in lb/h Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Carry the line loading, speed, exact foot factor, and hour conversion together: 0.23 m / s &times; 1 ft 0.3048 m &times; 72 lb 1 ft &times; 3,600 s 1 h = 195,590.551&hellip; lb/h The result is 1.96 &times; 10 5 lb/h (about 195,600 lb/h)."},
{"title":"Typing count rate","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-typing-count-rate","text":"3. Typing count rate: A typist produces 150 words/min. The average word has 5.1 letters, and one spacebar press follows each word, for 6.1 key presses/word. What is the typist's key rate in keys/s? Answer in keys/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 150 words / min &times; 6.1 keys 1 word &times; 1 min 60 s = 15.25 keys/s Words and minutes cancel, leaving 15.25 keys/s."},
{"title":"Average running speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Motion Quantities","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motion-average-running-speed","text":"4. Average running speed: A runner completes 5 km in 12 min 37 s. What is the runner's average speed in mi/h? Answer in mph Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution First convert the distance and time: 5 km &times; 1 mi 1.609344 km = 3.10685596 mi 12 min 37 s = 757 s Then 3.10685596 mi / 757 s &times; 3,600 s 1 h = 14.775&hellip; mi/h or 14.8 mph."},
{"title":"Conveyor setting","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-conveyor-setting","text":"5. Conveyor setting: A packaging conveyor must cover 20 m in 12 s. What speed setting is required in ft/s? Answer in ft/s Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 20 m / 12 s &times; 1 ft 0.3048 m = 5.468066&hellip; ft/s The required speed is 5.47 ft/s."},
{"title":"Reverse exact-factor check","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-reverse-exact-factor-check","text":"6. Reverse exact-factor check: A guard is specified as 18 ft long. What is its length in meters? Answer in m Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Put feet on the bottom so they cancel: 18 ft &times; 0.3048 m 1 ft = 5.4864 m The exact relationship gives 5.4864 m."},
{"title":"Prefix scale","page":"Practice","summary":"Measurement Language","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-prefix-scale","text":"7. Prefix scale: A feature is 0.0048 m long. What is its length in mm? Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.0048 m &times; 1,000 mm 1 m = 4.8 mm The answer is 4.8 mm."},
{"title":"Squared conversion","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#geometry-squared-conversion","text":"8. Squared conversion: A panel has an area of 2.75 ft 2. What is its area in in 2? Answer in in² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Because 1 ft = 12 in, square the relationship: 2.75 ft 2 &times; 144 in 2 1 ft 2 = 2.75 &times; 144 in 2 = 396 in 2 The answer is 396 in²."},
{"title":"Cubed conversion","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#geometry-cubed-conversion","text":"9. Cubed conversion: A fixture has a volume of 0.50 ft 3. What is its volume in in 3? Answer in in³ Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Cube the 12-in-per-foot relationship: 0.50 ft 3 &times; 1,728 in 3 1 ft 3 = 0.50 &times; 1,728 in 3 = 864 in 3 The answer is 864 in³."},
{"title":"Conveyor acceleration","page":"Practice","summary":"Acceleration and Squared Time","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#acceleration-conveyor-acceleration","text":"10. Conveyor acceleration: A conveyor accelerates from rest (0 m/s) to 3 m/s in 5 s. What is its average acceleration? Answer in m/s² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution a avg = (v f &minus; v i ) / &Delta;t = (3 m/s &minus; 0 m/s) / 5 s = 0.6 m/s 2. The answer is +0.6 m/s² in the selected positive direction."},
{"title":"Direction reversal","page":"Practice","summary":"Acceleration and Squared Time","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#acceleration-direction-reversal","text":"11. Direction reversal: Take motion to the right as positive. A cart changes from +2 m/s to &minus;1 m/s in 0.75 s. What is its average acceleration? Answer in m/s² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution a avg = (&minus;1 m/s &minus; (+2 m/s)) / 0.75 s = &minus;3 m/s / 0.75 s = &minus;4 m/s 2. The negative sign means the acceleration component points left, opposite the chosen positive direction."},
{"title":"Complete-part schedule","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-complete-part-schedule","text":"12. Complete-part schedule: A cell runs for 7.5 h. Setup takes 20 min, and each repeatable cycle takes 3.6 min per part. How many complete parts fit in the run? Answer in complete parts Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The running window is 7.5 h &times; 60 min 1 h &minus; 20 min = 430 min Then 430 min &times; 1 part 3.6 min = 119.444&hellip; parts Only 119 complete parts fit; the fraction is time into the next cycle."},
{"title":"Micrometers to millimeters","page":"Practice","summary":"Measurement Language","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#measurement-prefix-conversion","text":"Practice: A feature is specified as 3,200 &micro;m. What is that length in millimeters? Answer in mm Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Micro means 10 &minus;6 and milli means 10 &minus;3. Moving from micrometers to millimeters divides by 1,000: 3,200 &micro;m &times; 1 mm 1,000 &micro;m = 3.2 mm"},
{"title":"SolidWorks volume conversion","page":"Practice","summary":"Dimensional Analysis","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#dimensional-solidworks-volume","text":"Practice: A SolidWorks model reports 400 in 3. What is the volume in ft 3? Answer in ft³ Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution There are 12 in in 1 ft, so there are 12 3 = 1,728 in 3 in 1 ft 3. Therefore 400 in 3 &times; 1 ft 3 1,728 in 3 = 0.23148148 ft 3 Rounded sensibly: 0.2315 ft 3."},
{"title":"Cart round-trip rate","page":"Practice","summary":"Motion Quantities","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#motion-round-trip-rate","text":"Practice: A cart moves at a constant 4.5 ft/s between two areas 0.25 mi apart. Ignoring loading and turnaround time, what is its idealized round-trip rate per hour? Answer in round trips per hour Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution One round trip is 2 &times; 0.25 mi = 0.50 mi. 0.50 mi &times; 5,280 ft 1 mi = 2,640 ft At 4.5 ft/s, one trip takes 2,640 ft / 4.5 ft 1 s = 586.667 s Therefore 3,600 s / 586.667 s = 6.13636 round trips per hour, or about 6.14 round trips per hour. A plan that requires complete trips would schedule 6 and leave time for only part of a seventh."},
{"title":"Machine-arm acceleration","page":"Practice","summary":"Acceleration and Squared Time","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#acceleration-machine-arm","text":"Practice: A machine arm starts from rest and reaches 3 m/s in 4 s. What is its average acceleration, assuming the stated interval? Answer in m/s² Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution a avg = (v f &minus; v i ) / &Delta;t = (3 m/s &minus; 0 m/s) / 4 s = 0.75 m/s 2. The assumption is that the requested value is the average over the complete four-second interval."},
{"title":"Enclosure paint coverage","page":"Practice","summary":"Geometry, Mass, and Force","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#geometry-paint-coverage","text":"Practice: A sheet-metal enclosure has 1,250 in 2 of external surface area. A U.S. gallon of paint covers 350 ft 2. Ignoring overspray, waste, unpainted areas, and extra coats, how many complete enclosures can one gallon cover? Answer in complete enclosures Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Convert the enclosure area using the squared relationship: 1,250 in 2 &times; 1 ft 2 144 in 2 = 8.68056 ft 2 Then 350 ft 2 / 8.68056 ft 2 per enclosure = 40.32 enclosures Only 40 complete enclosures can be painted. The answer assumes the stated coverage is for the required coat and all paint reaches the enclosure."},
{"title":"A supported shelf","page":"Practice","summary":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-shelf-joint","text":"A supported shelf: A fixed shelf must be located across the grain of a solid side panel and have a shoulder beneath it. Which joint directly provides that seat? A plain end-to-face butt joint without reinforcement A fitted dado with enough side-panel thickness remaining A miter cut only on the shelf end Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 2. A dado provides a bearing surface under the shelf and shoulders for location. It still needs suitable proportions and a way to resist withdrawal."},
{"title":"A moving tabletop","page":"Practice","summary":"Wood Joinery and Fasteners","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-moving-top","text":"A moving tabletop: A wide solid-wood top crosses a narrow support rail. Which attachment can allow seasonal movement across the top’s grain? Glue the full width of the top to the rail Install rigid screws across the width with no travel Use suitable tabletop clips with the required movement clearance Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 3. Clips can hold the top while allowing travel. The movement direction and clearance must match the design; a finish does not remove that need."},
{"title":"A small gap in a repair","page":"Practice","summary":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-adhesive-gap","text":"A small gap in a repair: For a noncritical wood repair with a small gap that cannot be refitted, which candidate should be checked first for a structural gap-filling specification? Ordinary PVA with sawdust added An epoxy system with its specified bonding filler Foaming polyurethane chosen only because it expands Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 2. Investigate the epoxy system and its gap limit, substrate preparation and cure instructions. A structural bonding filler differs from an easily sanded fairing filler. Foam expansion alone is not evidence of strength."},
{"title":"Adhesive for damp service","page":"Practice","summary":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-adhesive-exposure","text":"Adhesive for damp service: A close-fitting wood joint will experience occasional wetting. What establishes whether the adhesive is suitable? The exact product’s exposure rating and application limits Its yellow color The fact that all wood glues are waterproof Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 1. Use the product’s stated service conditions, including limits on immersion, temperature and structural use. Adhesive family and color are not exposure ratings."},
{"title":"Unclamping versus loading","page":"Practice","summary":"Gluing and Clamping Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-clamp-cure","text":"Unclamping versus loading: A product’s minimum clamp interval has elapsed, but its stated interval before loading has not. What follows from those instructions? The joint is ready for its full service load The glue must have failed Removing restraint and applying service loads are different milestones Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 3. Clamp time and cure/loading time are separate. Follow both instructions under the specified conditions rather than assuming that an unclamped joint has full cured properties."},
{"title":"Order the preparation grits","page":"Practice","summary":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-sanding-order","text":"Order the preparation grits: A sample calls for all four grits: 80, 120, 150 and 180. Which sequence progressively removes coarser scratches? 180 → 150 → 120 → 80 80 → 120 → 150 → 180 80 → 180 → 120 → 150 Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 2. Use 80 → 120 → 150 → 180, inspecting for removal of the previous scratches at each step. This exercise specifies the grits; a real finishing system may call for a different start or endpoint."},
{"title":"Finish for frequent wiping","page":"Practice","summary":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-finish-choice","text":"Finish for frequent wiping: A surface will receive frequent wiping and light abrasion. The owner wants visible grain and accepts a film appearance. Which is the best starting candidate? A clear film finish explicitly rated for that service Pigment stain alone Wax alone chosen for maximum wear resistance Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 1. A suitable clear film can provide wear and spill protection while leaving grain visible. Check the exact formulation and complete preparation/cure schedule. Stain primarily supplies color; wax alone provides limited protection."},
{"title":"A level open-pore surface","page":"Practice","summary":"Preparing and Finishing Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-pore-filler","text":"A level open-pore surface: A sample has open pores but no dents. A level high-gloss surface is wanted. Which preparation product directly addresses those pores? A screw-hole plug Pre-stain conditioner alone A compatible grain/pore filler Choice number Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Choice 3. Pore filler levels the open texture. Conditioner addresses uneven stain absorption; it does not perform the same job. Test filler order and color in the complete finishing system."},
{"title":"Moisture from sample masses","page":"Practice","summary":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-moisture-mass","text":"Moisture from sample masses: A sample initially weighs 144 g. Its oven-dry mass is 120 g. Calculate MC on the oven-dry basis, to one decimal place. Moisture content (%) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Water mass = 144 − 120 = 24 g. MC = 24 / 120 × 100 = 20.0%. Dividing by the initial 144 g gives 16.7%, which is a different basis."},
{"title":"Round a moisture result","page":"Practice","summary":"Drying and Conditioning Wood","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-moisture-rounding","text":"Round a moisture result: A sample initially weighs 125 g. Its oven-dry mass is 110 g. Calculate MC on the oven-dry basis, to one decimal place. Moisture content (%) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show explanation Water mass = 125 − 110 = 15 g. MC = 15 / 110 × 100 = 13.636…%, or 13.6%. Dividing by the initial 125 g gives 12.0%, which is incorrect for the requested basis."},
{"title":"Explicit sales tally","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-board-feet-tally","text":"Explicit sales tally: A seller tallies a board using nominal thickness 1 in, measured width 7 in, and length 9 ft. Use exactly those dimensions. How many board feet? Give two decimals. Answer (BF) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 × 7 × 9 / 12 = 5.25 BF. This is the stated tally basis, not a measurement of the finished volume."},
{"title":"Finished physical volume","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-board-feet-actual","text":"Finished physical volume: A finished board actually measures 3/4 in thick, 7 in wide, and 9 ft long. Express its physical volume in board feet to two decimals. Answer (BF) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 0.75 × 7 × 9 / 12 = 3.9375 BF, or 3.94 BF. Substituting nominal 1 in thickness would answer a different question."},
{"title":"Three boards","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-board-feet-batch","text":"Three boards: Three boards each actually measure 1.25 in × 5.5 in × 8 ft. Find their combined physical volume in board feet to two decimals. Answer (BF) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Each board is 1.25 × 5.5 × 8 / 12 = 4.583333… BF. Multiply the unrounded value by 3: 13.75 BF."},
{"title":"Material allowance","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-board-feet-allowance","text":"Material allowance: Your parts need 30 BF of physical volume. You expect 20% of the material you buy to be lost to saw kerf, defects, flattening and layout. Using that convention, how many board feet of physical volume should you buy? Give two decimals. Answer (BF) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The 20% is a share of what you buy, so only 80% of the purchase becomes parts: 30 ÷ (1 − 0.20) = 30 ÷ 0.80 = 37.50 BF. Adding 20% to the need (30 × 1.20 = 36 BF) uses the other convention and leaves you short under this one."},
{"title":"All dimensions in inches","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-board-feet-inches","text":"All dimensions in inches: A piece actually measures 2 in × 9 in × 40 in. Find its physical volume in board feet to two decimals. Answer (BF) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution All dimensions are inches, so divide cubic inches by 144: 2 × 9 × 40 / 144 = 5.00 BF."},
{"title":"Nominal and actual width","page":"Practice","summary":"Buying Lumber and Board Feet","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-actual-width","text":"Nominal and actual width: A dry surfaced nominal 2 × 4 is specified to have an actual cross-section of 1-1/2 × 3-1/2 in. What width belongs in a physical-volume calculation? Give two decimals. Answer (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Use the supplied actual width, 3.50 in. The nominal width 4 in is its sales name."},
{"title":"Classify balsa","page":"Practice","summary":"Wood Structure and Species","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-balsa-classification","text":"Classify balsa: Balsa dents easily. Is it botanically a hardwood or softwood? Enter 1 for hardwood or 2 for softwood. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 — hardwood. Balsa comes from an angiosperm tree. Hardness is not the classification test."},
{"title":"Classify yellow pine","page":"Practice","summary":"Wood Structure and Species","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-yellow-pine-classification","text":"Classify yellow pine: Southern yellow pine can be harder than balsa. Enter 1 if this makes it a hardwood, or 2 if it remains a softwood. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2 — softwood. Pine is a gymnosperm; relative hardness does not change its botanical group."},
{"title":"Identify cup","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-identify-cup","text":"Identify cup: A board is trough-shaped across its width. Identify the defect: 1 = cup, 2 = bow, 3 = crook, 4 = twist, 5 = check. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 — Cup. Cup is curvature across the width."},
{"title":"Identify bow","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-identify-bow","text":"Identify bow: The face curves along its length, while both long edges bend together out of the face plane. Identify the defect: 1 = cup, 2 = bow, 3 = crook, 4 = twist, 5 = check. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2 — Bow. Bow is lengthwise curvature out of the broad face plane."},
{"title":"Identify crook","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-identify-crook","text":"Identify crook: Viewed from above its broad face, a board bends sideways along its length. Identify the defect: 1 = cup, 2 = bow, 3 = crook, 4 = twist, 5 = check. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 3 — Crook. Crook is edgewise curvature within the face plane."},
{"title":"Identify twist","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-identify-twist","text":"Identify twist: The two ends rotate relative to each other so the four corners do not lie in one plane. Identify the defect: 1 = cup, 2 = bow, 3 = crook, 4 = twist, 5 = check. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 4 — Twist. Twist changes the relative orientation of the ends."},
{"title":"Identify check","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-identify-check","text":"Identify check: A narrow separation follows the grain on a face but does not pass through the board. Identify the defect: 1 = cup, 2 = bow, 3 = crook, 4 = twist, 5 = check. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 5 — Check. A check is a separation that does not extend through the piece; a through separation is a split."},
{"title":"Painted interior panel","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineered Wood Products","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-pick-mdf","text":"Painted interior panel: Choose a smooth panel for a nonstructural painted interior decorative door with a routed profile, in dry service. 1 = ordinary MDF, 2 = rough structural OSB. Enter the better starting choice. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 — MDF. Fine fibers provide a smooth surface and consistent profiles. Plan suitable fastening; this does not make ordinary MDF a wet-service product."},
{"title":"Rated wall sheathing","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineered Wood Products","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-pick-osb","text":"Rated wall sheathing: A wall design calls for rated structural sheathing. Which is the relevant starting product? 1 = unrated furniture particleboard, 2 = OSB with the specified structural rating and thickness. Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 2 — the specified rated OSB. The product rating, orientation, and installation details matter; a generic sheet name is not a design specification."},
{"title":"Laminate substrate","page":"Practice","summary":"Engineered Wood Products","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-pick-particleboard","text":"Laminate substrate: A dry interior furniture panel needs an economical substrate under decorative laminate, and its design already accommodates the substrate strength and fasteners. 1 = particleboard, 2 = glulam beam. Which is the suitable product form? Answer (choice number) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution 1 — particleboard. It supplies a panel substrate. Glulam supplies structural members, not an interchangeable sheet surface."},
{"title":"Center-fixed edge travel","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-movement-center-travel","text":"Center-fixed edge travel. A flat-sawn white fir board is 9.15 in wide when installed at 8% MC and reaches 11% MC. Use C T = 0.00245 per percentage point MC. Assume uniform free width change on a stationary support, with the center as the only fixed reference. How far does the right edge move outward? Give inches to four decimal places. Outward travel (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Total width change = 9.15 × 0.00245 × (11 − 8) = 0.0672525 in. The right edge is half a width from the reference, so it moves 0.0672525 ÷ 2 = 0.0336 in outward. The total width change is shared by two edges."},
{"title":"Left-edge-fixed travel","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-movement-edge-travel","text":"Left-edge-fixed travel. A flat-sawn white fir board is installed 9.15 in wide at 8% MC. It reaches 11% MC; C T = 0.00245 per percentage point MC. Assume uniform free width change and a stationary support with the left edge as the only fixed reference. How far does the right edge move outward? Give inches to four decimal places. Outward travel (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning The reference is one full width from the right edge. Travel = 9.15 × 0.00245 × 3 = 0.0673 in outward. Dividing by two would incorrectly use a center reference."},
{"title":"Drying reverses travel","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-movement-inward-travel","text":"Drying reverses travel. A flat-sawn white fir board is installed 9.15 in wide at 8% MC, then dries to 6% MC. Use C T = 0.00245 and assume uniform free width change with only the center fixed to a stationary support. Find signed right-edge travel: positive means outward, negative means inward toward the fixed reference. Give inches to four decimal places, including the sign. Signed travel (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning ΔW = 9.15 × 0.00245 × (6 − 8) = −0.044835 in. Right-edge travel = ΔW ÷ 2 = −0.0224 in. This uses the inward allowance, even if ample outward allowance exists."},
{"title":"Check the correct travel direction","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-movement-directional-window","text":"Check the correct travel direction. An ideal center-fixed board's right edge needs 0.0224 in inward travel from its installed position. The stationary support allows 0.020 in inward and 0.040 in outward clear travel at that point. Which conclusion follows from this geometric comparison? Choose a conclusion Choose… The total 0.060 in allowance covers the movement. Inward demand exceeds inward travel by about 0.0024 in. The board will crack by exactly 0.0024 in. Check answer Choose an answer, then check it. Show answer and reasoning 2. Compare 0.0224 with 0.020, not with their sum or the outward allowance. The excess is about 0.0024 in. The model estimates free displacement; it does not calculate stress, cracking or joint strength."},
{"title":"A point inside the edge","page":"Practice","summary":"Grain and Moisture Movement","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#wood-movement-interior-point","text":"A point inside the edge. A 9.15 in wide board has a calculated free width increase of 0.0672525 in. Assume uniform strain and a stationary support with only the center fixed. A tracking point is one quarter of the installed full width to the right of the center. How far does this point move outward? Give inches to four decimal places. Outward travel (in) Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show answer and reasoning Point travel = (distance from reference ÷ installed width) × ΔW = 0.25 × 0.0672525 = 0.0168 in outward. The edge is farther from the reference and travels 0.0336 in; this point is halfway to that edge."},
{"title":"Steady lift in SI","page":"Practice","summary":"Work and Energy","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#work-steady-lift-si","text":"1. Steady lift in SI: A hoist raises a 320 kg load 4.5 m at a steady speed. How much work is done against gravity? Use standard gravity, g 0 = 9.80665 m/s 2. Answer in J Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The load is a mass, so turn it into the force needed to lift it: F = mg = 320 kg &times; 9.80665 m/s 2 = 3,138.128 N. W = Fs = 3,138.128 N &times; 4.5 m = 14,121.576 J. The work done is 14.1 kJ. Two assumptions are doing real work in that answer: the lift is steady, so no extra force goes into accelerating the load, and the force acts along the direction of travel for the whole 4.5 m."},
{"title":"A lift in foot pounds-force","page":"Practice","summary":"Work and Energy","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#work-lift-in-foot-pounds","text":"2. A lift in foot pounds-force: How much work is required to lift a 750 lb load 22 ft? Give the answer in joules. Answer in J Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The force needed to lift steadily is the load's weight, 750 lbf, acting along the 22 ft of travel: W = 750 lbf &times; 22 ft = 16,500 ft-lbf. Then convert: 16,500 ft-lbf &times; 1.355818 J 1 ft-lbf = 22,370.997 J or 22.4 kJ. Note the word order in the working unit: energy is ft-lbf, and it converts to the joule. The 750 lb figure is treated as a weight in pound-force, which is what the formula needs - not a mass in pound-mass."},
{"title":"A meter reading in megajoules","page":"Practice","summary":"Work and Energy","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#work-kilowatt-hour-to-megajoules","text":"3. A meter reading in megajoules: An electricity meter records 8.4 kW-h over one shift. How many megajoules of energy is that? Answer in MJ Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution A kilowatt hour is a power multiplied by a time and nothing more: one kilowatt sustained for one hour. So 1 kW-h = 1,000 W &times; 3,600 s = 3,600,000 J exactly, which is 3.6 MJ. 8.4 kW-h &times; 3.6 MJ 1 kW-h = 30.24 MJ The shift used 30.24 MJ. The kilowatt hour is not itself an SI unit - NIST lists it among the units to be converted into SI - but the factor is exact, so nothing is lost in the conversion."},
{"title":"Work from a torque","page":"Practice","summary":"Work and Energy","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#work-torque-through-an-angle","text":"4. Work from a torque: A fastener is run down with a steady 45 N-m through 2.5 revolutions. How much work is done, in joules? Answer in J Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The angle has to be in radians before it can multiply a torque: 2.5 r &times; 6.283185 rad per r = 15.70796 rad. W = &tau;&theta; = 45 N-m &times; 15.70796 rad = 706.858 J. The work done is 707 J. Nothing was converted between N-m and J along the way, and nothing needed to be: the radian is a length over a length, value 1, so multiplying by an angle in radians leaves the units alone. Feed the same formula 2.5 revolutions or 900 degrees directly and the answer is wrong, because both of those carry a chosen count inside them. The steady 45 N-m is an idealization - a real run-down torque climbs as the joint seats."},
{"title":"Horsepower for a lift","page":"Practice","summary":"Power and Efficiency","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#power-lift-horsepower","text":"5. Horsepower for a lift: A 750 lb load is raised 22 ft in 9 s. How much power does that take, in hp? Answer in hp Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution W = 750 lbf &times; 22 ft = 16,500 ft-lbf. P = W/t = 16,500 ft-lbf &divide; 9 s = 1,833.33 ft-lbf/s. One horsepower is defined as 550 ft-lbf/s, so dividing by 550 is a unit conversion, not a fudge: 1,833.33 &divide; 550 = 3.3333 hp. The lift needs 3.33 hp delivered to the load. A motor for the job needs more than that, because no drive is perfectly efficient. Cross-check in SI if you like: 16,500 ft-lbf &times; 1.355818 = 22,371 J, and 22,371 J &divide; 9 s = 2,486 W, which is 2,486 &divide; 745.6999 = 3.33 hp."},
{"title":"Power from torque and speed","page":"Practice","summary":"Power and Efficiency","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#power-torque-and-speed","text":"6. Power from torque and speed: A motor delivers 12 N-m at 1,750 r/min. What is its mechanical power output, in W? Answer in W Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution P = &tau;&omega; needs the speed in rad/s: 1,750 r/min &times; 0.1047198 rad/s per r/min = 183.2596 rad/s. P = 12 N-m &times; 183.2596 rad/s = 2,199.1 W. The output is 2,199 W, or about 2.20 kW. Substituting 1,750 straight into the formula gives 21,000 - wrong by the factor of about 9.55 that separates r/min from rad/s. In coherent SI units the relationship needs no constant at all, which is exactly why the speed has to arrive in rad/s."},
{"title":"The torque-and-rpm shortcut","page":"Practice","summary":"Power and Efficiency","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#power-shortcut-63025","text":"7. The torque-and-rpm shortcut: A drive delivers 250 lbf-in at 1,200 r/min. What is the power, in hp? Answer in hp Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution The shortcut takes the torque in pound-force inches and the speed in r/min: hp = (250 lbf-in &times; 1,200 r/min) &divide; 63,025 = 300,000 &divide; 63,025 = 4.760 hp The answer is 4.76 hp. Check it the long way, since the constant is only a folded-up set of conversions: 250 lbf-in = 20.833 lbf-ft, 1,200 r/min = 125.664 rad/s, so P = 20.833 &times; 125.664 = 2,618.0 ft-lbf/s, and 2,618.0 &divide; 550 = 4.760 hp. The two routes agree to four figures. Give the shortcut a torque in lbf-ft instead and the answer comes out twelve times too small - the constant already contains the inch-to-foot step."},
{"title":"The volts-and-amps shortcut","page":"Practice","summary":"Power and Efficiency","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#power-electrical-shortcut-746","text":"8. The volts-and-amps shortcut: A direct-current motor draws 14 A at 24 V. What is that electrical input power, in hp? Answer in hp Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Electrical power first: P = VI = 24 V &times; 14 A = 336 W. Then convert: 336 W &divide; 746 W per hp = 0.4504 hp. The input is 0.450 hp. The 746 is exact because NIST defines a separate unit, horsepower (electric), as 746 W. The condition sits in step 1, and this problem satisfies it: volts times amps gives real power in watts on direct current or a purely resistive load. On alternating current it gives apparent power in volt-amperes, and the real power is that multiplied by the power factor - so the same shortcut used on an AC motor without its power factor reads high. This is also the motor's input; its mechanical output is less."},
{"title":"Hoist efficiency","page":"Practice","summary":"Power and Efficiency","collection":"Practice","url":"practice.html#power-hoist-efficiency","text":"9. Hoist efficiency: A hoist motor draws 2.2 kW and raises a 900 N load at a steady 2.0 m/s. What is the efficiency of the system, as a percentage? Answer in % Check answer Enter a number, then check it. Show worked solution Useful output, using P = Fv: 900 N &times; 2.0 m/s = 1,800 W. &eta; = P out /P in = 1,800 W &divide; 2,200 W = 0.8182. The system is 81.8% efficient. The ratio is two like quantities, so the watts cancel and the answer is a plain number. The missing 400 W has not vanished - it is heat in the windings, the gearbox, and the bearings. An answer above 100% would mean an error in the numbers, not a remarkable machine. This is a system efficiency, measured from the electrical input to the load. The motor alone would quote a higher figure, over a different boundary."}
];
